const MG={"QZ":[{"t":"buit","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Buitelaar borrows an old football analogy (going back to Locke) to explain the Free City. What does it show?","o":["A result is unfair whenever one team ends up far ahead of the other","The referee should make sure both teams score about equally often","The fans' total enjoyment of the match decides whether it was fair","Only specific actions in the game can be unfair, never the result"],"a":3,"e":"In the Free City only conduct can be unjust (a serious foul, a goal wrongly disallowed), never the resulting score, just as a spatial pattern is not unjust if it came about by legitimate actions. Judging by the gap between teams is the Equal City's logic, and judging by total enjoyment is the Happy City's."},{"t":"buit","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Buitelaar treats the city mainly as a provider of basic services for basic human activities. Which three?","o":["Housing, green space and safety","Housing, jobs and transportation","Education, health care and housing","Transport, energy and water supply"],"a":1,"e":"He focuses on housing (residing), jobs (working) and transportation (travelling). Green space and safety appear in Moroni's list of spatial primary goods, which is a different list; energy justice is explicitly left aside."},{"t":"buit","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Within the 'three-tenet framework' from the energy-justice literature, which kind of justice does Buitelaar position his article in?","o":["Procedural justice: how fairly decisions are made","Recognition justice: respect for group identities","Environmental justice: fair shares of nature","Distributional justice: who gets which goods"],"a":3,"e":"He positions himself within distributional justice, as distinct from procedural and recognition justice, and leaves environmental and energy justice aside. Procedural justice is tempting because the Free City judges rules, but the article's framing is distributional."},{"t":"buit","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"Buitelaar distinguishes the 'ethical level' from the 'executive level' of justice. What is the difference?","o":["Ethical: where justice should be achieved; executive: who brings it about","Ethical: what values people hold; executive: which laws the state passes","Ethical: justice for individuals; executive: justice for the whole nation","Ethical: the basic structure of society; executive: daily city decisions"],"a":0,"e":"The city is treated as the ethical level of concern (the level at which justice should be achieved), not necessarily the executive level that brings it about, which could be the nation, Europe or the neighbourhood. The last option confuses this with background versus allocative justice."},{"t":"buit","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Buitelaar quotes Young's definition of justice (conditions for all to use skills, take part in decisions and express their experience). How does he classify it?","o":["As the concept of justice that every conception has to share","As a conception: one possible way of specifying the concept","As a material of justice, namely the ground of a conception","As a procedural rule rather than a theory of justice at all"],"a":1,"e":"Young's definition is 'a conception, not the concept', because it is only one way of specifying justice (after Moroni). It illustrates the second problem: defining justice narrowly, as if no alternative conceptions existed."},{"t":"buit","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"In the HC1 lecture the '3-30-300' rule for urban green appeared in the section on which conception of the Just City?","o":["Equal City: the same amount of green in every neighbourhood","Happy City: green where willingness to pay for it is highest","Supportive City: an absolute minimum of green for everyone","Free City: green provided by landowners under general rules"],"a":2,"e":"The rule (3 trees visible, 30% canopy, 300 m to a green space) sets an absolute 'enough', a sufficiency threshold, so it fits the Supportive City and Moroni's spatial primary good of proximity to greenery. An equal share would be the Equal City's relative logic."},{"t":"buit","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A region defines 'transport poor' households as those who cannot reach a hospital, school or job cluster within a set travel time at a reasonable price. In Buitelaar's terms, what is the key policy task here?","o":["Setting and measuring a threshold of accessibility","Maximising total travel-time savings across the region","Reducing the gap between the best and worst connected","Removing rules that stop private bus operators"],"a":0,"e":"Transport poverty is a Supportive City notion: everyone should have enough accessibility and affordability, so determining the height of the threshold is the key activity. Reducing the gap between best and worst is the Equal City's relative logic."},{"t":"buit","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the three problems Buitelaar sees in debates on The Just City?","o":["Justice is kept vague, which creates superficial consensus","Justice is defined narrowly, as if one conception existed","Competing conceptions are pursued at once, inconsistently","Justice is reduced to procedures, ignoring distribution"],"a":3,"e":"His three problems are vagueness, narrowness (e.g. Fainstein's anti-neoliberal framing) and inconsistency when incompatible conceptions are pursued together. He does not complain that justice is reduced to procedure; he himself chooses to focus on distributional justice."},{"t":"buit","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which rule would pass Hayek's 'negative test of universality', as Buitelaar uses it for the Free City?","o":["A ban on refusing mortgages because of the applicant's postcode","A quota setting the share of low incomes in every neighbourhood","A subsidy that lifts every household above an income threshold","A levy on land values to fund an equal spread of public parks"],"a":0,"e":"Just rules must be general, concern interaction between individuals and be negative: they forbid unjust conduct instead of enforcing outcomes. A ban on redlining does exactly that; quotas, threshold subsidies and equalising levies all steer the resulting pattern."},{"t":"buit","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Buitelaar distinguishes instrumental and intrinsic grounds for the Equal City. What is the difference?","o":["Instrumental: inequality is wrong in itself; intrinsic: it harms health and growth","Instrumental: equality of rights; intrinsic: equality of incomes and of wealth","Instrumental: inequality harms other things; intrinsic: it is unjust in itself","Instrumental: aims at a threshold; intrinsic: aims at a ceiling on top wealth"],"a":2,"e":"Instrumental grounds point to effects of inequality or segregation on health, the economy or neighbourhood outcomes; intrinsic grounds hold inequality to be unjust in itself. The first option simply swaps the two, and the threshold/ceiling contrast belongs to sufficientarianism and limitarianism."},{"t":"buit","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"What does Buitelaar mean by 'the city' in The Just City?","o":["The target territory where goods, duties or rights are allocated","The municipal government, which must deliver justice on its own","The main cause of injustice through the way capital builds space","A neutral container whose form plays no role in creating injustice"],"a":0,"e":"Following Moroni and De Franco, the city is the 'target territory' to which a conception applies. He engages less with the city as a cause of injustice (Harvey, Marcuse), yet explicitly denies treating it as a neutral container, and the city is not necessarily the executive level."},{"t":"buit","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A council proposes capping how many homes one investor may own, arguing that concentrated property wealth turns into political power. Which position in Buitelaar matches this argument?","o":["Limitarianism: a ceiling on wealth, defended on instrumental grounds","Prioritarianism: help those furthest below the threshold first","Equal City: inequality of property is unjust in itself, whatever its effects","Free City: entitlement theory judges how the homes were acquired"],"a":0,"e":"Robeyns' limitarianism adds a ceiling to the sufficiency threshold and defends it instrumentally: wealth becomes political power and resources are needed elsewhere. The Equal City option is tempting, but the council argues from effects, not intrinsic wrongness."},{"t":"buit","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which of these policies would a Free City advocate NOT support?","o":["Selling public land to households who develop it under general rules","Abolishing rent control so owners can set rents in free contracts","Requiring a fixed share of social rent in every new housing project","Banning landlords from refusing tenants because of their background"],"a":2,"e":"Buitelaar lists refraining from growth controls, inclusionary housing and rent control as Free City (non-)policies, along with Oosterwold and anti-discrimination rules. An inclusionary quota restricts property rights to produce a pattern, which is what the Free City rejects."},{"t":"buit","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"What is the ground of the Happy City, according to Buitelaar?","o":["The more preferences are satisfied, the better for the collective","Each person has a right to a minimum of welfare whatever the total","Inequality of welfare is harmful and should be reduced over time","Only voluntary exchange can produce a legitimate pattern of welfare"],"a":0,"e":"The ground is that more preference satisfaction is better for the collective, so the individual is 'subordinate to the sum or the average'. A guaranteed minimum is the Supportive City and voluntary exchange the Free City."},{"t":"buit","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Buitelaar's account of the Free City, which statements are true? I. Distributive or social justice loses relevance, because no designer of the distribution exists. II. A buy-to-let investor who legitimately acquires many homes is a sign of injustice. III. Just rules forbid unjust behaviour rather than enforce fair outcomes. IV. A positive-freedom variant of the Free City builds on the capability approach.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV F"],"a":2,"e":"I, III and IV are in the text: in a spontaneous order there is no 'divider', rules must pass the negative test, and 'The Free City as a city of capabilities' adds positive freedom. II is false: eyebrows may be raised, but for liberalism legitimate acquisition is no injustice. The option with IV false is tempting if you forget the capability section."},{"t":"buit","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Buitelaar on the Supportive City and its variants, which statements are true? I. Differences above the sufficiency threshold are of no moral consequence. II. Prioritarianism gives priority to those just below the threshold, since lifting them is most efficient. III. Robeyns holds that great wealth is intrinsically immoral, whatever its effects. IV. Moroni's list of spatial primary goods can be adapted to social and political consensus.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":1,"e":"I paraphrases Frankfurt and IV is Moroni's own caveat. II is false: lifting those just below may be most efficient from the sufficiency view, but prioritarianism (Crisp) favours those furthest below. III is false: Robeyns defends limitarianism instrumentally (political power, global needs), not from intrinsic immorality."},{"t":"buit","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Buitelaar treats the city mainly as a 'target territory' where goods, duties or rights are allocated. How would a Marxist political economist critique this?","o":["The city's distribution was never designed by anyone, so judging allocations at city level is already a category mistake","Allocation choices are made by self-interested officials and lobbies, so declared justice goals will be captured in practice","Capitalist property relations produce injustice in how the city is built; allocating goods afterwards leaves that intact","Any definition of the city frames the object of study, so the researcher should state which values this framing carries"],"a":2,"e":"A Marxist locates injustice in the relations of production and private property that make the city, so a distributive focus treats symptoms. Buitelaar admits he engages less with the city as a factor behind injustice. The other critiques belong to a Hayekian, a rational choice theorist and Rydin (2021)."},{"t":"buit","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"The Equal City supports social mixing so that income groups are spread more evenly across the city. How would Hayek (1944) critique this?","o":["Mixing leaves private ownership of land intact, so it treats the symptoms of class division","Equality is not what matters morally; what matters is that every household has enough","The segregation data could support several narratives, so the framing should be explicit","Designing who lives where needs one scale of values nobody shares, imposed by planners"],"a":3,"e":"For Hayek a plan aimed at a definite social pattern presupposes a 'complete ethical code' that does not exist, so experts end up imposing their own preferences and coercion grows. The sufficiency objection is Frankfurt's (Supportive City), the class critique is Marxist and the framing point is Rydin's."},{"t":"buit","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a party programme promises to judge every urban project by its contribution to total welfare (via CBA) and, in the same section, to guarantee that no individual's property rights may ever be overridden for collective gains. Which of Buitelaar's concepts fits this tension most precisely?","o":["External incongruence: two separate conceptions clash with each other","Internal incongruence: the materials inside one conception do not fit","Superficial consensus: justice is kept vague to avoid a controversy","Formal versus material equality: equal rights produce unequal incomes"],"a":1,"e":"The programme combines the utilitarian principle of maximum collective welfare with individual rights as a value inside one framework; Buitelaar gives exactly this as an example of internal incongruence. External incongruence would concern two whole conceptions, such as Free versus Equal City."},{"t":"buit","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A housing alderman states: 'We do not mind that some districts are richer than others. Our task is to make sure that no household's housing costs leave too little to run a household, and to help those furthest below that line first.' Which conception grounds this passage?","o":["Supportive City, limitarian variant: a floor plus a ceiling on top wealth","Equal City, instrumental ground: narrowing gaps to avoid neighbourhood effects","Free City, positive freedom: real capabilities to choose one's way of living","Supportive City, prioritarian variant: a floor, the worst off helped first"],"a":3,"e":"Indifference to differences above the line plus a budget-style threshold is sufficientarian; helping those furthest below first is Crisp's prioritarianism. The limitarian option is tempting because it is also a Supportive City variant, but the alderman proposes no ceiling."},{"t":"buit","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Buitelaar argues that, strictly speaking, a city cannot be just or unjust. Statement II: Price rises, demographic growth and investment patterns play no role in debates about urban justice.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is his first premise: only institutions (formal and informal rules) can be just. II is false: such developments have an important signalling function, raising concern or outrage that starts the discussion about whether institutions are just."},{"t":"buit","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Buitelaar himself labels policies that promote agglomeration economies as 'neoliberal'. Statement II: Sager lists fourteen planning-related policies, such as city marketing and attracting the creative class, that share the aim of raising property values, welfare and agglomeration effects.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"II is in the text. I is false: Buitelaar reports that the planning literature commonly '(dis)qualifies' such policies as neoliberal, while he classifies them neutrally as Happy City policies; indeed he criticises Fainstein for assuming that every conception of justice targets neoliberalism."},{"t":"buit","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs Buitelaar's account of why the Happy City still justifies planning?","o":["Perfect markets reveal all preferences in prices → equilibrium is Pareto-optimal → so market outcomes need no correction at all","Markets fail with public goods and externalities → prices miss some preferences → hedonic pricing and CBA guide intervention","Markets fail with public goods and externalities → the worst off fall below a threshold → planners must provide primary goods","Markets fail with public goods and externalities → prices miss some preferences → planners must equalise incomes across areas"],"a":1,"e":"Following Pigou, markets fail especially for the public goods and externalities that make up cities; hedonic price analysis can price these and CBA evaluates projects, while market failure remains a justification for planning. The threshold and equalising chains swap in the Supportive and Equal Cities' principles."},{"t":"buit","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"A study rates Amsterdam's districts as 'just' or 'unjust' by how evenly income groups are spread, and presents the measure as a neutral fact. Combining Rydin (2021) and Buitelaar, what is the strongest critique?","o":["The measure is sound, since evenness is the concept of justice that every conception shares","The measure confuses ethical and executive level; per Rydin only phronesis can judge justice","The measure adopts the Equal City conception; per Rydin this value choice must be made explicit","The measure is a Supportive City threshold; per Rydin the data should then speak for themselves"],"a":2,"e":"Evenness of spread is the Equal City's principle, one conception among four, so calling it neutral hides a value choice; Rydin adds that theory choice carries values that rigorous research makes explicit. Evenness is not the shared concept, and Rydin rejects letting data speak for themselves."},{"t":"buit","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Buitelaar's Free City draws on Hayek. Which pairing of a Hayekian idea with its role in the Free City is correct?","o":["Negative test of universality → rules must secure every household a minimum of primary goods","Dispersed knowledge → CBA should replace market prices to add up all individual preferences","Price of democracy → majorities may legitimately vote for any distributive pattern they choose","Cosmos versus taxis → cities order spontaneously, so no designer owns the distribution"],"a":3,"e":"Buitelaar uses Hayek's spontaneous order (cosmos) to argue that distributive justice loses relevance when nobody designs the distribution. The negative test concerns forbidding unjust conduct, not guaranteeing minimums, and Hayek (1944) argues for limiting, not widening, what majorities decide."},{"t":"fw","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Next to Rydin's definition, the HC1 lecture showed a definition of theory by Føllesdal et al. (1990). What did it say?","o":["A law that connects variables and is tested by searching for refuting evidence","A body of assumptions, of which the mutual relations have been made explicit","A desirable end state for a town, drawn as a diagram of ideal urban form","Practical wisdom about how to act in one setting, aware of power relations"],"a":1,"e":"The slide set Føllesdal et al.'s 'body of assumptions, of which the mutual relations have been made explicit' beside Rydin's heuristic definition. The law-like option is the natural-science model that Rydin rejects, and the end-state option is Keeble's older use of 'theory'."},{"t":"fw","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"The course uses M.C. Escher's Relativity as its cover image. Which central idea of the course does it capture?","o":["Planning theories replace one another in a clean upward sequence over time","Every perspective on the city is equally valid, so choosing one is arbitrary","The planner stands above the city and sees how all of its parts connect","One spatial world can appear coherent in very different ways by perspective"],"a":3,"e":"The course text says the image shows that 'the same spatial world can appear coherent in radically different ways depending on the perspective from which it is viewed'. It does not claim that all perspectives are equally valid, and the lecture explicitly says theories do not replace one another cleanly."},{"t":"fw","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"In Rydin's Table 1.1, what does new institutionalism say the governmental model misses?","o":["A cultural dimension that drives action, including path dependency","State failure and the influence of state actors' own self-interests","The importance of stakeholder involvement in formulation and delivery","The taken-for-granted dimension of discourses that circulate in society"],"a":0,"e":"Table 1.1 links new institutionalism (ch. 4) to culture and path dependency. State failure and self-interest belong to rational choice, stakeholder involvement to governance theory, and taken-for-granted discourses to the Foucauldian approach."},{"t":"fw","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Rydin refuses a generic definition of planning, but names what the diverse cases have in common. What?","o":["A legal mandate, a trained planner in charge and a statutory land-use plan to follow","Goal-directed public administration that implements what elected politicians decide","Future focus, natural and built environment, mixed political and technical work","A public-interest mission defined by expert analysis of market failures and needs"],"a":2,"e":"Rydin lists a future orientation, a focus on natural and built environments, and a mix of political and technical work, and leaves the rest to theory. The public administration option is just one of the definitions she shows to be a theoretical framing."},{"t":"fw","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"In the HC4 excursion quiz, 'Merwede is being developed amid high demand for housing and scarcity of well-located urban land' was matched to which kind of explanation?","o":["Explanations focused on what actors want, decide and do in the project","Explanations focused on conditions and forces beyond individual actors","Explanations focused on the normative commitments held by the planners","Explanations focused on outcomes of communicative action between parties"],"a":1,"e":"Market conditions such as demand and land scarcity were sorted under 'conditions and forces beyond individual actors', the structure side. Statements about negotiations and developers' strategic choices went to the agency side."},{"t":"fw","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Besides universal ↔ situated and monist ↔ pluralist, which pair did the knowledge lecture add to the knowledge dimension?","o":["Reproduction ↔ transformation","Deliberate ↔ emergent","Structure ↔ agency","Realism ↔ relativism"],"a":3,"e":"The knowledge lecture added realism ↔ relativism to dimension 3. Reproduction ↔ transformation and deliberate ↔ emergent are the two axes of dimension 2, and structure ↔ agency is dimension 1."},{"t":"fw","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which research question asks for an evaluative claim, rather than an explanatory or a prescriptive one?","o":["Why did housing output in Utrecht fall after 2022?","Was Utrecht's allocation of social homes fair to newcomers?","What should Utrecht planners do to speed up housing output?","How do developers decide when to start a housing project?"],"a":1,"e":"Evaluative claims judge processes or outcomes ('which processes are fair'), which is where the lecture highlighted normativity. The 'why' and 'how' questions are explanatory; 'what should planners do' is prescriptive."},{"t":"fw","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the caveats Rydin (2021) gives about her own book?","o":["It is analytic: it studies how planning works rather than how it should be done","It concerns empirical, data-based social science rather than other research","It is not a textbook on research methods for students of planning research","It ends each chapter with a limitations section critiquing that perspective"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin deliberately adds no limitations section per chapter: the chapters already criticise each other and example studies are presented, not critiqued. Being analytic, empirical and not a methods textbook are all caveats she does state."},{"t":"fw","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Apart from hidden theory, what is Rydin's other objection to Flyvbjerg et al.'s phronesis?","o":["It ignores power, although every research question is shaped by power relations","It treats good research as a virtue of character, not as a skill that can be learnt","It relies on local and tacit knowledge, which she says cannot be used in research","It aims to change practice, while she holds that research should never seek change"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin says phronesis attacks a straw man of theory and treats good research as a matter of the researcher's character; she prefers to see research as a skill that can be learnt. Phronesis does not ignore power: its four questions put 'mechanisms of power' at the centre."},{"t":"fw","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Rydin says theories are not 'arbitrary windows' on the world. What does she mean?","o":["Each theory is tested against data until only one valid window on the world remains","Each theory must be derived from the facts rather than chosen by the researcher","Each theory carries assumptions about how the world is, ought to be and can be known","Each theory is equally useful, so the choice makes little difference for the findings"],"a":2,"e":"For Rydin every theory bundles an ontology, normative positions and an epistemology, so the terrain of theory is contested and choosing one is a value choice. Deriving theory from the facts is the empiricism she rejects."},{"t":"fw","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Rydin says research moves 'in both directions' between general understanding and a specific case. What does she mean?","o":["The case improves the general picture, and the general picture guides the case","Theory is tested on the case and dropped as soon as one finding contradicts it","Cases only illustrate, while general understanding comes from theory alone","General understanding emerges only after many cases are described without theory"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin describes research as combining a general understanding of how planning works with knowledge of one case, each informing the other. Dropping a theory after one refutation is the natural-science model she does not adopt."},{"t":"fw","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A study argues that Utrecht's car-free Merwede district exists because aldermen and planners deliberately chose it and pushed it through negotiations. Where does this explanation sit?","o":["Agency side, with change produced deliberately by actors","Structure side, with change emerging from wider processes","Agency side, with change emerging from uncoordinated action","Structure side, with change deliberately steered by the state"],"a":0,"e":"The explanation gives weight to what actors want and decide (agency) and treats the outcome as intended (deliberate). An emergent reading would see the district as the unintended result of many interacting actions."},{"t":"fw","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A municipality accepts only its engineers' traffic models as valid input and dismisses residents' local experience. On the knowledge dimension this is closest to…","o":["Pluralist: several forms of knowledge are admitted as input","Monist: one form of knowledge, produced by experts, counts","Situated: knowledge is tied to the local context of residents","Relativist: no knowledge claim can be judged better than another"],"a":1,"e":"Monist ↔ pluralist asks who can produce knowledge and which forms count; admitting only the experts' models is monist. Situated would be the residents' local knowledge, which is exactly what is excluded here."},{"t":"fw","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Rydin says pure empiricism produces a 'disparate' field of research. What does she mean?","o":["Researchers from different schools disagree on which facts even count as data","Data are collected with so many methods that they can no longer be compared","Each situation is studied in detail, but the findings are hard to connect","Findings are generalised far too quickly from one case to the whole field"],"a":2,"e":"Rydin's second pragmatic objection is that empiricism leaves a disparate field: detailed accounts that do not add up to a broader picture, which theory can provide. Over-quick generalisation is not her complaint; empiricism suffers from the opposite."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: after a ministry report calls municipal rules a 'barrier' to housebuilding, aldermen start describing their own policies in those terms in interviews. A researcher notices she is interpreting people who already interpret their world with categories from earlier policy and research. Which concept fits this most precisely?","o":["Empiricism: the facts about barriers speak for themselves once collected","Phronesis: practical wisdom about acting within relations of power","Double hermeneutic: researchers interpret people who interpret the world","Modernist settlement: the world lies out there waiting to be observed"],"a":2,"e":"The double hermeneutic (Giddens, cited by Rydin) means social scientists interpret how people themselves interpret the world, one reason Rydin rejects law-like theory. Empiricism and the modernist settlement assume the opposite: that data simply reveal the world."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Rydin's point about defining 'planning' most precisely?","o":["Every definition of planning frames the research object, so it has theoretical implications","Any definition of planning is arbitrary, so researchers may simply pick whichever suits them","Only politicised definitions of planning carry theoretical implications for research","A definition of planning fully determines which findings a researcher will eventually reach"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin writes that any definition 'implies a framing of that object of research and that has theoretical implications'. Her point applies to the instrumental definition too, not only politicised ones, and framing shapes what is studied without fixing the findings."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A research proposal states: 'Our study asks where Utrecht's housing policy is going, who gains and who loses and by which mechanisms of power, whether this is desirable, and what should be done. We work from inside the case and need no theoretical framework.' Which approach grounds this passage, and how would Rydin assess it?","o":["Empiricism; she would answer that data never speak for themselves","Phronesis; she would say it still makes theoretical choices unawares","Normative planning theory; she would place it outside analytic research","Political economy; she would say it neglects the agency of actors"],"a":1,"e":"The four questions are Flyvbjerg et al.'s phronetic research. Rydin's reply is that phronesis relies on unacknowledged theory (the double hermeneutic, assumptions about how change happens) and should come with an explicit framing. The empiricism option is tempting but the passage is about values and power, not letting data speak."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about the course's four dimensions is NOT accurate?","o":["Dimension 1 asks how much weight goes to individual action versus wider conditions","Dimension 2 has two axes: reproduction ↔ transformation and deliberate ↔ emergent","The monist ↔ pluralist axis asks what knowledge is, rather than who can produce it","Dimension 4 asks which values are embedded, explicitly or implicitly, in theory"],"a":2,"e":"On the slide, 'what is knowledge?' belongs to universal ↔ situated, while monist ↔ pluralist asks who can produce knowledge and what role different forms should play. The other three statements match the lecture's table."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which author would endorse this claim? 'Practitioners may find academic theory unintelligible and too sweeping, but academics should first do the research task as well as they can; translation can follow.'","o":["Hall (2014)","Rydin (2021)","Buitelaar (2026)","Hayek (1944)"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin admits the language and tone problem but insists 'it is the role of the academy to do the best job it can of the research task first', optimistic that research can be translated. Hall is the tempting option, but he laments the divorce and wants theory to serve the job of planning real places."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin (2021), which statements are true? I. Theory is a heuristic: it helps you find things out without guaranteeing a complete answer. II. Once enough data have been collected, they speak for themselves. III. Every theory bundles assumptions about how the world is, ought to be and can be known. IV. A rigorous researcher avoids normative topics to keep the analysis value-free.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"I and III are Rydin's view of theory (heuristic; ontology, normative positions and epistemology). II is the empiricism she rejects: data must be interpreted into a narrative. IV is false: rigour means making the value-ladenness explicit, not avoiding values. The option with II true is the tempting one if you equate rigour with lots of data."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to the course framework (HC1, with the HC8 clarifications), which statements are true? I. Agency includes choices made within existing structures, not only acting against them. II. Monist ↔ pluralist asks how broadly a knowledge claim applies across contexts. III. A theory that stresses structural conditions can logically be critiqued from a more agency-focused theory. IV. Dimension 4 asks which values are embedded, explicitly or implicitly, in a theory.","o":["I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":2,"e":"HC8 stressed that people exercise agency when they choose within structures (I) and used political economy critiqued from rational choice institutionalism as the example of III; IV matches the HC1 table. II is false: scope across contexts is universal ↔ situated, while monist ↔ pluralist asks how many legitimate ways of knowing exist."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Rydin treats theory as a heuristic, partial narrative and accepts that several theories can illuminate one case. How would a positivist critique this view?","o":["Treating theories as interchangeable narratives hides the economic base that shapes them all","Heuristics for researchers do little for planners who must make plans for real places","Theory-led research misses the context-dependent practical wisdom that guides action","Without law-like hypotheses tested against data, competing theories can never be ranked"],"a":3,"e":"A positivist wants theory as tested, law-like statements between variables, so a plurality of heuristic narratives looks unfalsifiable. The other critiques come from a Marxist, from Hall (theory should serve the job of planning places) and from Flyvbjerg's phronesis."},{"t":"fw","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rydin argues that empiricism cannot avoid value judgements. Statement II: Researchers cannot collect all data, so choosing which data to collect, and how, already involves values.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are Rydin's: her first pragmatic objection to empiricism is precisely that selecting data (why this data, collected this way) involves value judgements, so II gives the reason for I."},{"t":"fw","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rydin sees phronetic research as incompatible with the use of theory. Statement II: Phronesis relies on Giddens' double hermeneutic, which is itself a theoretical choice.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"II is Rydin's argument, but I is false: she says phronesis can be combined with theory, it should simply go with an explicit theoretical framing. Her point is that phronesis already uses theory without admitting it."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims match Rydin's discussion of defining planning? I. Defining planning as goal-directed decision-making frames it as instrumental and downplays politics. II. Defining it as the state ordering the environment frames it as correcting imbalances of market-led change. III. Rydin ends by offering one generic definition for all planning research.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are two of her 'small shifts' (pp. 4–5). III is wrong: she refuses a generic definition beyond a few shared features, because one purpose of theory is to supply a specific definition for a given study."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Rydin lets researchers choose a theory according to their own value judgements, provided they make these explicit. Which criticism fits the logic of the perspective it is attributed to?","o":["Positivist: treating theory as a heuristic leaves too much room for testing hypotheses","Hayekian: allowing many theories concentrates knowledge in one central authority","Communicative: making values explicit would close down dialogue between stakeholders","Marxist: treating theory choice as personal values hides the social forces behind theory"],"a":3,"e":"A Marxist would argue, as Hall reports, that planning theory is itself a product of the social forces behind planning, so treating it as a researcher's value preference hides that. The other critiques contradict their own perspective: positivists want more testing, Hayekians favour dispersed knowledge, communicative theorists welcome openness."},{"t":"fw","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs Rydin's discussion of Næss and Saglie's definition of planning?","o":["Planning as goal-directed decision-making → framed as political struggle → research judges planning by who benefits","Planning as goal-directed decision-making → framed as instrumental administration → research takes intentions at face value","Planning as state ordering of the environment → framed as instrumental administration → research ignores market-led change","Planning as engagement of diverse interests → framed as instrumental administration → research aligns with professed values"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin argues that a goal-directed definition frames planning as instrumental public administration and so aligns research with the professed values of practice, taking intentions at face value. Judging by who benefits belongs to her politicised definition, not to this one."},{"t":"hall","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Hall opens with a paradox about planning around 1955. What is it?","o":["It became legitimate as a discipline and so began to sow the seeds of its own destruction","It lost its legal powers just as the public began to demand more planning of cities and regions","It produced more plans than ever, while the cities themselves were changing less than before","It gained a theory of the planning process, yet this theory was ignored by the planning schools"],"a":0,"e":"Planning 'at last became legitimate; but in doing so, it began to sow the seeds of its own destruction', splitting into theory-obsessed schools and practice-minded offices. The last option reverses who embraced the theory: the schools were the ones obsessed with it."},{"t":"hall","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Hall cites Abercrombie's triad of 'beauty, health and convenience'. What does it illustrate about pre-1955 planning?","o":["Goals were set through public debate and written into binding plans","Goals were derived from models that forecast future land-use needs","Goals stayed implicit; planners drew them from their own 'expert' values","Goals were set by politicians, and planners only translated them into maps"],"a":2,"e":"Apart from such general statements, goals were left implicit and the planner developed them intuitively from his own values, which were by definition 'expert' and apolitical. Model-based goal-setting belongs to the 1960s systems era."},{"t":"hall","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Hall mentions Aaron Wildavsky's paper 'If Planning Is Everything, Maybe It's Nothing'. What point does it support?","o":["Planning should cover every policy field, since everything in a city is connected","Planning was so marginal by 1975 that few governments still employed any planners","Planning had become so technical that no citizen could follow its computer models","Planning had spread so thinly over so wide an area that it was almost meaningless"],"a":3,"e":"Hall uses the title to show that, by 1975, planning had extended so thinly that it lost its claim to unique expertise. The second option inverts Wildavsky's ironic point into an endorsement of comprehensive planning."},{"t":"hall","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Around 1975, how did many young graduating planners see their role, according to Hall?","o":["As engineers who calibrate traffic models for the regional transport authority","As 'barefoot doctors' helping the poor on the streets of the inner city","As designers who draw the ideal land-use plan for each growing town","As neutral mediators who only manage the process of a public debate"],"a":1,"e":"Young planners 'saw their roles as rather like barefoot doctors, helping the poor down on the streets of the inner city', working for acceptable councils or for community groups. Model calibration fits the 1965 graduate and the drawing board the 1955 graduate."},{"t":"hall","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Hall quotes Tewdwr-Jones on planning practice in the 1980s and 1990s. How is it described?","o":["As a visionary profession leading the redevelopment of inner cities","As a bureaucratic regulatory process: 'rampant technocracy'","As a social movement that organised tenants against developers","As a branch of regional science driven by computer forecasting"],"a":1,"e":"Tewdwr-Jones says the 'vision thing' was lost and planning became 'rampant technocracy'; Hall adds the 'New Proceduralism' of codified, bureaucratised practice. The regional-science option describes the 1960s, not the 1980s."},{"t":"hall","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"In the knowledge lecture, the Project Cybersyn operations room (Chile, early 1970s) and a SimCity screenshot illustrated which figure from Hall's history?","o":["The craft planner at the drawing board before about 1955","The advocate planner working late for community groups","The communicative planner hosting an open public dialogue","The systems planner steering a city from a control room"],"a":3,"e":"Both images show the city as a system steered from a control room or a screen, the cybernetic ideal of 1960s systems planning. The craft planner is illustrated instead by blueprint plans such as Berlage's Plan Zuid."},{"t":"hall","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How did American planning before the systems era differ from the British 1947 system, according to Hall?","o":["It spoke of 'end-directions' adjusted over time, yet still sought an optimal land-use plan","It rejected any land-use plan and relied entirely on market forces and private deed covenants","It had already adopted computer models of traffic and land use before the war had ended","It gave planners the final say on values, while British councils only advised on them"],"a":0,"e":"Kent's 1964 text spoke of 'end-directions which are continually adjusted' and left final value judgements to the council, yet still assumed an optimal land-use plan was possible. The last option reverses Kent's point: councils, not planners, made the final value judgements."},{"t":"hall","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"What 'logical paradox' does Hall see at the core of the 1960s systems approach?","o":["Systems planners claimed to be neutral scientists but were paid by developers with interests","In a weapons system the controls sit inside it; the city sits inside its own system of control","The more data the systems planners collected, the less able they were to decide anything at all","Systems models predicted growth correctly, but the plans based on them always came too late"],"a":1,"e":"Hall writes that, unlike a weapons system where 'the controls were inside the system', 'the urban-regional system was inside its own system of control', so planners cannot steer the city from outside. The first option sounds like a left critique but is not the paradox Hall names."},{"t":"hall","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which was NOT one of the three themes that, according to Hall, fuelled the left-wing attack on systems planning?","o":["A widespread distrust of expert, top-down planning in general","Suspicion that systems jargon was a smokescreen for bad policies","Urban riots suggesting systems planning had not helped the cities","A belief that only bargaining between groups could ever work"],"a":3,"e":"The left's three themes were distrust of top-down expertise, paranoia about pseudo-scientific jargon, and the riots of 1964 to 1967. Incremental bargaining is the pluralist critique from the right (Lindblom, Altshuler)."},{"t":"hall","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Hall says later Marxist explanations 'come close to being pluralist'. Why?","o":["Because they gave up the idea that capital shapes the city at all","Because they adopted Forester's model of communicative action","Because capital is split into fractions with divergent interests","Because they accepted that planners can redirect capitalism"],"a":2,"e":"The capitalist class is not homogeneous: fractions with divergent, even contradictory interests form complex alliances, so explanations come close to pluralism, 'albeit with a strong structural element'. They did not drop the structural role of capital."},{"t":"hall","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A city cuts its planning department and hands planning to neighbourhood initiatives, celebrating each area's own culture and way of doing things. Which warning that Hall reports (via Storper) fits best?","o":["Localism can legitimise the retreat of the state from its tasks","Neighbourhood plans will drift back into blueprint land-use design","Community planning always hides the noir side of official planning","Local initiatives prove that cities are complex adaptive systems"],"a":0,"e":"Storper's critique of the cultural turn, reported by Hall, is that celebrating difference and localism can legitimise the state's retreat. The noir-side option is Sandercock's claim about official planning, which Hall rejects."},{"t":"hall","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which was NOT among the developments that, according to Hall, overwhelmed the static planning system in the 1950s?","o":["A baby boom and a long economic boom","Mass consumption and car ownership","Land-hungry suburban homes and roads","A collapse of post-war land values"],"a":3,"e":"Hall names the baby boom, the economic boom, mass consumption and especially land-hungry homes and cars, which a system geared to slow change could not handle. A collapse of land values is not part of his account."},{"t":"hall","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How did 1990s communicative and radical theory differ from 1970s political economy, according to Hall?","o":["It wanted to replace the system, while political economy sought reform","It wanted to work with the market rather than replace the whole system","It returned to physical design, while political economy studied capital","It rejected community involvement, while political economy supported it"],"a":1,"e":"Unlike 1970s political economy, which wanted to replace the system, the new theory wanted to work with the market; some radicals even rejected the welfare state, 'a strange reflection of Thatcherite libertarian logic'. The first option simply swaps the two positions."},{"t":"hall","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Hall cites Faludi's distinction between functional and normative planning. What is it?","o":["Functional: plans for one sector; normative: comprehensive plans for the whole city","Functional: goals are given; normative: the goals themselves are rationally chosen","Functional: planning by experts; normative: planning by elected politicians alone","Functional: physical design of space; normative: social and economic programmes"],"a":1,"e":"In Faludi's text of 1973 planning is functional when goals and objectives are taken as given, and normative when they are themselves the object of rational choice. Hall uses it to ask whether planning could really choose its own goals."},{"t":"hall","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hall, which statements are true? I. Postmodern planning theory is 'infuriatingly aspatial'. II. The systems revolution was a clean break with every form of blueprint thinking. III. Marxists call conventional planning theory 'vacuous' because it depoliticises and legitimates planning. IV. Many academics still tried to teach real-life planning in the 1980s and 1990s.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I, III and IV are in the chapter; Hall even admits the divorce was not total. II is false: many 'systems' plans had 'a distinctly blueprint tint' and resulted in fixed proposals such as freeway systems. The pattern with IV false is tempting if you read Hall's lament as total."},{"t":"hall","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hall's account of the attacks on planning between 1965 and 1975, which statements are true? I. Lindblom's critique came from the left, fuelled by the civil rights movement. II. Davidoff's advocate planners made goal-setting explicit and informed the public of alternatives. III. Hall calls advocacy planning democratic and pluralistic, but legalistic. IV. Altshuler found that planners beat the highway engineers by stressing broad goals.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III F · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"II and III describe Davidoff's advocacy planning and Hall's verdict. I is false: Lindblom belongs to the pluralist critique from the right. IV reverses Altshuler: the engineers won, by stressing their expertise and narrow goals, while the planner had no clout."},{"t":"hall","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Hall's 1960s systems planners treat the city as a system to be modelled, monitored and controlled by experts. How would Hayek (1944) critique this?","o":["The models treat the city as neutral and hide that capital's pursuit of profit produces its form","No central planner can rank everyone's ends in one scale of values, so experts impose their own","The single expert plan silences unheard groups; each group needs its own planner and own plan","Expert models distort communication; planners should reveal alternatives in dialogue instead"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek argues that steering society towards one plan needs a 'complete ethical code' that nobody has, so the plan is handed to experts who impose their own preferences. The other critiques are Marxist, Davidoff's advocacy planning and Forester's communicative planning."},{"t":"hall","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Hall writes planning history mainly as a sequence of ideas developed by academics and professionals. How would a Marxist critique this way of writing it?","o":["Every history uses a theoretical framing that should be made explicit and argued for","The 'official story' of heroic, state-led reason leaves out excluded groups' planning","Ideas about planning are products of social forces; a history of ideas misses the cause","Urban order emerges from many individual plans, so planners' ideas matter rather little"],"a":2,"e":"For a Marxist, planning theory is itself 'a creation of the social forces' that produce planning (as Hall reports), so tracing ideas mistakes the superstructure for the cause. The other options are Rydin (2021), Sandercock's insurgent histories and a Hayekian view."},{"t":"hall","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a province adopts a ten-year programme whose measures are adjusted every year as new monitoring data arrive. Its planners insist that the same cycle of goals, forecasts, appraisal and monitoring applies whether the plan concerns housing, water or energy. Which concept fits most precisely?","o":["Systems planning: a theory of the object, the region as a set of interrelated activities","Blueprint planning: a fixed end-state plan for the region, updated every five years","Planning as Webber's 'special way of deciding and acting', whatever is being planned","Advocacy planning: separate plans for each group, which compete before the authorities"],"a":2,"e":"The stress on a recycled sequence of steps that is the same for any object is the rational process view (Faludi, Webber): a theory of the process. Systems planning is tempting, but it is the theory of the object; here the case insists the object does not matter."},{"t":"hall","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A professional journal argues: 'Planning has become a regulatory routine in which the political is downplayed for efficiency. The vision that gave birth to the profession has gone, and nobody in the field can say why planning matters.' Which phase of Hall's history does this describe, and what is his diagnosis?","o":["Pre-1955 craft: planners sure of their expertise, untouched by any politics","1960s systems era: the city treated as a machine steered by expert controls","Mid-1970s paradigm crisis: process cut loose from the substance of cities","1980s to 1990s practice: proceduralism without the intellect to defend itself"],"a":3,"e":"This is Tewdwr-Jones' picture of 'New Proceduralism', and Hall concludes that planning slipped into insignificance because 'it lacked the intellectual wherewithal to defend itself'. The paradigm crisis option is tempting, but it concerned theory losing substance, not practice losing vision."},{"t":"hall","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims match Hall's view of postmodern and cultural-turn theory? I. Because it denies meta-theory, it cannot explain socio-economic relationships. II. Following Storper, celebrating difference and localism can legitimise the state's retreat. III. Its strength is its close attention to what happens where, and why.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are Hall's own critique and Storper's. III is the opposite of Hall's view: postmodern theory is 'entirely uninterested in the question of what happens where, and why', which makes it 'infuriatingly aspatial'."},{"t":"hall","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Hall reports that Marxists call conventional planning theory 'vacuous'. Statement II: It defines what planning ideally ought to be, devoid of all context, and so depoliticises and legitimates planning.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are in Hall's account (p. 403), and II is the Marxian reason for I: by ignoring the capitalist basis of planning, conventional theory is 'by definition vacuous', serving to depoliticise and legitimate it."},{"t":"hall","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Hall notes that advocacy planning paradoxically gave planners some of the powers of elected officials. Statement II: In advocacy planning, planners set the goals for the groups they served and kept the alternatives from the public.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is Hall's observation. II is false: Davidoff's advocates worked bottom-up, made goal-setting explicit and informed the public about alternatives, forcing agencies to compete."},{"t":"hall","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs how, in Hall's account, the systems revolution came about?","o":["Booms in babies, incomes and cars overwhelm the static plan → location theory and cybernetics offer tools → planning as continuous monitoring and control","Booms in babies, incomes and cars overwhelm the static plan → urban riots discredit expert planners and their tools → planning as continuous monitoring and control","Booms in babies, incomes and cars overwhelm the static plan → location theory and cybernetics offer tools → planning as a fixed blueprint end-state","A slowdown of growth makes urban change slow and predictable → location theory and cybernetics offer tools → planning as continuous monitoring and control"],"a":0,"e":"Rapid 1950s change broke a system geared to stability, while location theory, regional science and Wiener's cybernetics supplied the tools for planning as a continuous process. The riots came later and fuelled the attack on systems planning, not its rise."},{"t":"hall","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Hall and Rydin (2021) both discuss the gap between academic theory and practice. Which comparison is accurate?","o":["Hall blames only practitioners for ignoring theory; Rydin blames only the academics for their jargon","Hall says theory should stay away from practice; Rydin says it should become practitioners' handbook","Hall sees no gap before the 1990s; Rydin argues that the gap was already present before the 1950s","Hall blames both sides for a divorce; Rydin: academics should do the research first, then translate"],"a":3,"e":"Hall blames inward, incomprehensible theory and unreflective practice alike; Rydin admits the problem of language and tone but says 'it is the role of the academy to do the best job it can of the research task first'. Neither puts all the blame on one side."},{"t":"hall","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Hall's story runs from the expert designer, via the scientific modeller, to pluralist and relativist positions. How does Rydin (2007) position herself in that story?","o":["She returns to the modeller: only expert, model-based claims count as knowledge","She embraces relativism: every voice is knowledge, so none can be tested","She accepts many knowledges, but holds that knowledge claims must be tested","She returns to the designer: intuition and judgement are the best knowledge"],"a":2,"e":"Rydin (2007) welcomes multiple knowledges yet insists that knowledge claims (causal claims) be distinguished from other claims and tested, a realist constructivism between modernist and postmodern planning. 'Engaging different knowledges is fundamentally different to engaging different voices', so the relativist option is wrong."},{"t":"marx","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Marx says that labour does not only produce commodities. What else does it produce, according to 'Estranged Labour'?","o":["A surplus that the worker can save up to buy capital later","Itself and the worker, both turned into commodities for sale","A shared culture that binds the worker to the employer","Free time in which the worker can develop his talents"],"a":1,"e":"Marx writes that labour produces not only commodities: it produces itself and the worker as a commodity. The saving and free-time options describe exactly what estranged labour denies the worker."},{"t":"marx","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Marx rejects the economists' habit of explaining things from a fictitious 'primordial condition'. To whom does he compare them?","o":["Theologians who explain the origin of evil by the Fall of man","Philosophers who derive history from the progress of the Spirit","Lawyers who justify property by pointing to an ancient contract","Natural scientists who infer general laws from a single experiment"],"a":0,"e":"Marx says such a primordial condition explains nothing and only pushes the question back, just as theology explains evil by the Fall. The Spirit option is tempting because the lecture links Marx to Hegel, but that is not the comparison he makes here."},{"t":"marx","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Marx calls the second aspect of estranged labour, estrangement from one's own activity, by a specific name. Which?","o":["Objectification, as opposed to appropriation of nature","Species-estrangement, as opposed to alienation of property","Self-estrangement, as opposed to estrangement of the thing","Commodification, as opposed to direct use of the thing"],"a":2,"e":"Marx contrasts self-estrangement (in the act of production) with the estrangement of the thing (the product) discussed before. Objectification is a broader term: the embodiment of labour in an object, which only under estrangement appears as loss."},{"t":"marx","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which example did the HC4 lecture use to show estrangement from the product and from the act of production?","o":["Peasants who kept part of their harvest in exchange for paying tithes","Craft guilds that limited the number of masters allowed in a town","Colonial traders who bought raw materials cheaply and sold goods abroad","Industrial workers who sold their bodies' labour for a small wage"],"a":3,"e":"The slides say that with the shift from an agricultural to an industrial economy workers no longer transformed matter for their own use but sold their bodies for a small wage. The other options are historical situations the lecture did not use."},{"t":"marx","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Quoting Lenin, the HC4 lecture showed a sequence of states on the road to Marx's goal. Which order is correct?","o":["Socialist state → capitalist state → stateless society","Capitalist state → socialist state → stateless society","Capitalist state → stateless society → socialist state","Stateless society → capitalist state → socialist state"],"a":1,"e":"The slide runs from the capitalist state via a transitional socialist state to a stateless society; Lenin says the state must persist until capitalist resistance is crushed and classes are gone. The goal is a society built on material equality."},{"t":"marx","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"On the progressive-planner slide, Madden and Marcuse (2016) stress a 'world of difference' between which two things?","o":["Economic demand and social need","Use value and exchange value","Market failure and state failure","Formal rights and real freedom"],"a":0,"e":"The quote says many poor and working-class households need more housing than they can afford, but 'this form of need does not register with purely profit-oriented developers'. Use versus exchange value is the related idea behind commodification, but not the pair in this quote."},{"t":"marx","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which statement is NOT how Marx describes the worker's relation to his own labour?","o":["He feels at home only when he is not working","His labour is a means to satisfy needs outside it","His labour belongs to another, not to himself","He freely develops his energies while working"],"a":3,"e":"Marx says the worker does not freely develop his physical and mental energy in work; he denies rather than affirms himself in it. Feeling at home only outside work, labour as a mere means, and labour belonging to another are all in the text."},{"t":"marx","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which situation best illustrates the fourth form of estrangement: of man from man?","o":["A welder never sees the ship he helps to build, which belongs to a shipping firm","A cleaner feels like herself only at home, never during her long night shifts","A designer reduced to repeating one task sees her creativity only as a wage source","Pickers ranked against each other see colleagues as rivals and the owner as master"],"a":3,"e":"The estrangement of man from man means each relates to others through his estranged position as worker, and the product belongs to another man, the owner. The ship is estrangement from the product, the night shift from the activity, the designer from species-being."},{"t":"marx","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Marx says that under estrangement the worker loses nature as a means of life 'in a double sense'. What are the two senses?","o":["Nature is used up by industry, and its beauty is no longer visible in the city","Nature becomes private property, and the worker may no longer live on the land","Nature stops being the object of his own labour and stops directly feeding him","Nature turns into a commodity, and its price rises faster than the worker's wage"],"a":2,"e":"The sensuous external world supplies both the material for labour and the means of physical subsistence; estranged, it ceases to be either for the worker. Ecological damage and land enclosure are later or other arguments, not the double sense in this text."},{"t":"marx","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A planner argues that housing markets work efficiently when left alone and that zoning rules should be eased so that supply can respond. Which column of the HC4 planner table does this match?","o":["The progressive planner, Madden and Marcuse","The liberal planner, Glaeser and Gyourko","The governmental planner, Lichfield's steps","The communicative planner, Forester's view"],"a":1,"e":"The liberal column says markets 'aren't bad per se' but are often distorted by land use regulation, so planning should ensure a level field rather than limit supply. The progressive planner holds the reverse: markets produce material inequality by design."},{"t":"marx","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"On realism ↔ relativism, what did the knowledge lecture's exit ticket say about Marx and Hayek?","o":["Both are ontological realists, though they differ epistemologically","Marx is a realist and Hayek a relativist, since Hayek doubts knowledge","Both are relativists, because each treats knowledge as situated in class","Hayek is a realist and Marx a relativist, since ideology shapes all ideas"],"a":0,"e":"The exit ticket asks why both sit in the ontological realist camp: each assumes a real world (material relations; a real dispersed economy) that exists independently. Hayek's point about dispersed knowledge concerns what one mind can know, not whether reality exists."},{"t":"marx","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"If the product of labour is alien to the worker, to whom does Marx say it belongs?","o":["To the gods, as the ancient temple builders believed of their work","To nature, from which all the materials of labour are taken","To another man, outside of labour, who becomes its master","To the state, which holds it in trust for society as a whole"],"a":2,"e":"Marx rules out the gods and nature: the alien being to whom labour belongs 'can only be man himself', someone outside production for whom the worker's torment is pleasure. The state option reads later socialist ideas into this text."},{"t":"marx","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Why does Marx say that 'wages and private property are identical'?","o":["Both are fair rewards for the effort that each class contributes","Both flow directly from estranged labour, so both express it","Both are protected by the same law, so they must stand or fall together","Both rise in good times and fall in bad times in the same proportion"],"a":1,"e":"Wages are a direct consequence of estranged labour, as private property is; in wages labour appears as a mere means. That is why an enforced rise in wages is only 'better payment for the slave'. The legal option confuses base and superstructure."},{"t":"marx","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Marx admits that eating, drinking and procreating are genuinely human functions. When do they become 'animal'?","o":["When they are performed out of habit rather than as conscious choices","When they serve only to restore the body's strength for more labour","When they are shared with other people instead of done in private","When cut off from other activity and turned into sole, final ends"],"a":3,"e":"The lecture quote reads: 'when abstracted from other aspects of human activity, and turned into final and exclusive ends, they are animal'. The restoring-strength option sounds Marxist but is not the condition Marx names."},{"t":"marx","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: meal couriers in Amsterdam are ranked against each other by the platform's algorithm. They start to see colleagues only as rivals for orders, and the platform's owners as distant masters who profit from their effort. Which form of estrangement does this foreground most precisely?","o":["Estrangement from the product, which confronts them as an alien power","Estrangement from the activity, which they feel as forced, not voluntary","Estrangement from species-being, as free activity becomes only survival","Estrangement of man from man, through their position as workers"],"a":3,"e":"The case is about how the couriers relate to other people, colleagues and owners, through their estranged position: Marx's fourth form. Coerced activity is tempting, but the case says nothing about how the work itself feels."},{"t":"marx","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about the base and superstructure model in the HC4 lecture is NOT accurate?","o":["The base consists of the means and the relations of production","Planning, law and ideology belong to the superstructure","The superstructure has no influence of its own on the base","Change in history requires change in the underlying base"],"a":2,"e":"The slide draws a two-way relation: the base shapes the superstructure and the superstructure maintains the base, 'in a spiral pattern', although the base is generally dominant. The other three statements match the lecture."},{"t":"marx","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to 'Estranged Labour', which statements are true? I. Political economy explains how private property comes about. II. The worker feels at home when he is not working. III. An enforced rise in wages would restore the worker's human dignity. IV. Private property becomes, in turn, the means by which labour alienates itself.","o":["I F · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"II and IV are in the text: the worker is at home only outside work, and private property later becomes the means of alienation as well as its product. I is false: political economy 'starts with the fact of private property; it does not explain it'. III is false: higher wages are 'better payment for the slave'."},{"t":"marx","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to the HC4 lecture's account of Marxism, which statements are true? I. History is driven by class struggles between owners and workers. II. Humans are actors rather than subjects of broad societal change. III. Planners play a role in redistributing resources and ensuring material equality. IV. The goal is a society built upon material equality.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":1,"e":"I, III and IV come from the take-home slide and the goal on the political-ideology slide. II is the reverse: the deterministic view the lecture attributes to Hegel and Marx treats humans as 'subject to rather than actors of' broad change."},{"t":"marx","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"The HC4 lecture presents Marx's goal as abolishing private property and passing through a socialist state that redistributes resources. How would a Hayekian critique this?","o":["Class is not one actor: individuals free-ride, so a revolutionary class rarely acts together","A state directing all production needs a complete ethical code and ends in arbitrary power","Shared ownership ignores cultural scripts that make people accept existing property rules","Abolition by decree is fine in principle, but only with strong coalitions and resources"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek argues that central direction requires a complete ethical code nobody has, so decisions pass to experts and discretion and finally to dictatorship. The free-rider critique is rational choice (Olson), the cultural-scripts critique sociological institutionalism."},{"t":"marx","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Marxist theory explains history as a struggle between classes of owners and workers. How would a rational choice theorist critique this?","o":["Class position is a cultural script that people internalise, not a material condition","Society is a spontaneous order, so no class can direct history towards a chosen end","A large class is a latent group: each member free-rides unless selective incentives exist","The planner can enable dialogue so that both classes reach a shared understanding"],"a":2,"e":"Following Olson, rational individuals in a large group will not bear the costs of collective action for a shared good, so class struggle cannot simply be assumed. The spontaneous-order critique is Hayek's, the cultural-script one sociological institutionalist, the dialogue one communicative."},{"t":"marx","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims belong to the progressive planner on the HC4 slide (Madden and Marcuse 2016)? I. Markets do not deliver what they promise; they lead to material inequality. II. Planning should ensure a level field rather than limit supply. III. Housing problems result from the structural logic of the housing system, not from greed.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and III are on the progressive side of the slide. II belongs to the liberal planner (Glaeser and Gyourko), who sees markets as efficient but distorted by land use regulation."},{"t":"marx","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim? 'The more the worker appropriates the external world through his labour, the more he deprives himself of the means of life.'","o":["Hayek (1944)","Cozzolino (2020)","Marx (1844)","Scott (1998)"],"a":2,"e":"This is Marx's argument about nature as the material and the means of subsistence that the estranged worker loses in a double sense. Cozzolino also links ownership to action, but sees ownership as distributing design control, not as dispossession."},{"t":"marx","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Marx holds that equal wages for all workers would end the estrangement of labour. Statement II: Marx treats wages as a direct consequence of estranged labour.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"II is Marx's claim. I is false, and II is exactly why: if wages follow from estranged labour, equalising them would only turn every person's relation to labour into that of the worker, not end estrangement."},{"t":"marx","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Marx says the worker becomes a servant of his object. Statement II: The worker receives work, and through work the means of subsistence, so he exists first as a worker and only then as a physical being.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are in the text, and II is the reason Marx gives: because work and the means to survive come to the worker only through the object, he is its servant. The servitude is that he can maintain himself as a physical subject only as a worker."},{"t":"marx","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs how estranged labour inverts species-being?","o":["Humans produce consciously and freely → estranged labour turns free activity into a mere means → species-life serves only physical existence","Humans produce consciously and freely → estranged labour makes free activity the goal of life → species-life replaces physical existence","Humans produce only from physical need → estranged labour turns this need into a means → species-life serves only physical existence","Humans produce consciously and freely → private property creates estranged labour → species-life becomes the purpose of physical existence"],"a":0,"e":"Marx: man produces even when free from physical need, but estranged labour, 'in degrading spontaneous, free activity to a means', makes species-life a means to physical existence. The third chain describes the animal, and the fourth reverses both the causal order of private property and the end-means relation."},{"t":"marx","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combining the HC4 lecture with Buitelaar's four just cities, which pairing is correct?","o":["Marx's material equality → the Free City; Hayek's equal rights → the Equal City","Marx's material equality → the Happy City; Hayek's equal rights → the Supportive City","Marx's material equality → the Supportive City; Hayek's equal rights → the Happy City","Marx's material equality → the Equal City; Hayek's equal rights → the Free City"],"a":3,"e":"Marx's goal of material equality through shared ownership is closest to the goal-based Equal City, while Buitelaar builds the rights-based Free City on Hayek. The Happy City is utilitarian and the Supportive City is about guaranteed minimums, neither of which fits either thinker."},{"t":"marx","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Which statement correctly combines the lecture's base and superstructure model with Hall's account of 1970s Marxist planning theory?","o":["Planning sits in the base; Hall's Marxists saw planners as owners who direct accumulation","Planning sits in the superstructure; Hall's Marxists saw it as serving capital accumulation","Planning sits in the superstructure; Hall's Marxists saw it as a neutral tool for any class","Planning sits in the base; Hall's Marxists saw it as the motor that drives class struggle"],"a":1,"e":"Planning, like law and ideology, belongs to the superstructure, which maintains the base. Hall reports that Marxists saw planning as a function of the capitalist state, securing accumulation and the reproduction of labour. A neutral tool is precisely what they denied."},{"t":"cozz","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Cozzolino opens with an epigraph saying that 'as in all Utopias, the right to have plans of any significance belonged only to the planners in charge'. Who wrote it?","o":["Jane Jacobs","John Habraken","Stewart Brand","Christopher Alexander"],"a":0,"e":"The first epigraph is from Jacobs (1961); the second is Habraken's line that a built environment is a complex organism 'by virtue of human actions'. Brand is cited later for 'small groups adapt more quickly than large groups'."},{"t":"cozz","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which Dutch case does Cozzolino call an 'interesting experience' of letting people use locality knowledge while avoiding undesirable externalities?","o":["Bijlmermeer in Amsterdam","Voorhof in Delft","Oosterwold in Almere","Leidsche Rijn in Utrecht"],"a":2,"e":"Cozzolino points to the ongoing case of Oosterwold (Almere) after discussing locality knowledge. The Bijlmermeer and Voorhof appear in note 1 as examples of AANs, the opposite of what is asked."},{"t":"cozz","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Cozzolino quotes one author: 'everything depends on the pattern of ownership'. Who?","o":["Akbar","Moudon","Hakim","Pirenne"],"a":1,"e":"The line is from Moudon (1986), quoted when Cozzolino argues for multiple independent ownerships. Akbar is cited for the question why we analyse only the end-product, and Hakim for the old building codes."},{"t":"cozz","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Akbar asks why, instead of investigating the societal process that produced the traditional environment, we only analyse the end-product. Which principle does Cozzolino support with this?","o":["Welcome multiple independent ownerships","Abandon paternalistic regeneration","Treat public space as a robust skeleton","Include time in the planning discourse"],"a":3,"e":"Akbar's question supports the principle of including time: the main difference between modern and traditional environments lies in the role time played in forming them. Multiple ownerships is a separate principle, supported with Moudon and Habraken."},{"t":"cozz","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Following Pirenne, what was the essential condition for being recognised as a citizen in the 11th-century civitas?","o":["Owning a dwelling in the city","Paying the municipal building tax","Membership of a craft guild","Being born within the city walls"],"a":0,"e":"Pirenne says the possession of a dwelling was the condition for citizenship, so citizens expressing themselves through their houses drove the city's growth. Taxes were collected by municipalities for public works, but they were not the condition for citizenship."},{"t":"cozz","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"Cozzolino uses Jacobs' term 'intricacy', 'a variety of reasons for which people come to the neighbourhoods', as a signal of which AAN characteristic?","o":["Scale of design","Ownership system","Attractiveness","Construction time"],"a":2,"e":"Under attractiveness, Cozzolino says the continuous mingling of people for different purposes is a good signal, and he calls this Jacobs' intricacy. AANs are attractive at first but lose this over time."},{"t":"cozz","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A 1990s district has a zoning plan that fixes exactly the heights, densities, distances and uses that were built. Which AAN characteristic is this?","o":["Planning rules coincide with the configuration","Scale of design is comprehensive and large","Ownership system is communal and centralised","Functions are limited to residential dormitories"],"a":0,"e":"A major AAN characteristic is that the physical configuration coincides with the planning rules, freezing density, heights, distances and uses. Mono-functionality is a separate characteristic: the case is about the rules, not about which uses are present."},{"t":"cozz","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT a tactic Cozzolino proposes for regenerating an existing AAN?","o":["Opening opportunities for demolition and rebuilding","Granting additional floor area for densification","Drawing up a new comprehensive plan for the estate","Improving its accessibility and links to other areas"],"a":2,"e":"Cozzolino wants to break the paternalistic circle by enlarging room for local experimentation (demolition and rebuilding, extra FAR, mixed uses) and better connections. A new comprehensive plan is exactly the centrally coordinated intervention he says is not the main need."},{"t":"cozz","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Why does Cozzolino say planning can only INDIRECTLY address the mono-functional, dormitory character of AANs?","o":["Because functions are set once by the original master plan","Because zoning law forbids any mix of homes and businesses","Because residents in AANs have no interest in local services","Because functions come from market processes it cannot force"],"a":3,"e":"Most functions that make a neighbourhood come alive result from market processes; planning cannot force people to run businesses there, and the state cannot provide every activity. It can only create conditions that welcome them. Zoning is not described as forbidding all mixing."},{"t":"cozz","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"In Cozzolino's terms, what kind of order does a SIMPLE urban configuration possess?","o":["An emergent order from many spontaneous actions","A prevalently artificial order that is designed","A mixed order produced by proscriptive codes","An evolving order produced by many small owners"],"a":1,"e":"A simple configuration has a prevalently artificial order that is, by definition, designed; a complex one is emergent, the result of spontaneous action. The first option describes complexity, the opposite pole."},{"t":"cozz","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"Cozzolino presents self-building as an example of what?","o":["Demand-driven development spreading design control","A nostalgic return to the medieval civitas and its codes","Participatory design that yields a complex master plan","A supply-driven scheme that favours large developers"],"a":0,"e":"Self-building is his example of demand-driven development, which can distribute design control and reach variety as a natural result. Participatory design is something he treats separately and more sceptically: it still yields designs fixed in space."},{"t":"cozz","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Cozzolino quotes Brand: 'small groups adapt more quickly than large groups'. Which AAN characteristic does this support?","o":["The large scale of design over a whole area","The single designer or team at the start","The construction of everything all at once","The communal ownership of big condominiums"],"a":3,"e":"Brand's line supports the ownership point: adaptive areas consist of many independent owners with decentralised responsibilities, while AANs rely on large assemblies. The single-designer point is about design intelligence at the start, for which he quotes a different line of Brand."},{"t":"cozz","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Why, according to Cozzolino, does emulating the physical form and aesthetics of the traditional neighbourhood fail to reproduce its complexity?","o":["Because modern materials age faster than traditional ones","Because its complexity came from a process, not the form","Because residents today prefer large public open spaces","Because building codes now forbid small plots and lanes"],"a":1,"e":"Complexity emerged from many independent actions over time within a framework of rules; copying the end-product gives a more complex design but no room for self-organising complexity. Standards do hinder copying old patterns, but that is a different point from why copied forms stay simple."},{"t":"cozz","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Cozzolino says that when an AAN's planning rules coincide with its initial design, this is 'a great loss of knowledge'. Why?","o":["The original architects' drawings are no longer used","Residents forget how the estate was first designed","No one can learn from neighbours' adaptations","Planners stop collecting data on how residents live"],"a":2,"e":"In adaptive areas knowledge is embedded, stratified and evolving through continual neighbouring adaptation; frozen rules remove the chance to experiment and learn from neighbours' innovations. It is not about planners' data collection, which would be a governmental-model reading."},{"t":"cozz","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Cozzolino (2020), which statements are true? I. Social and economic change still occurs inside AANs, even though they rarely re-adapt physically. II. A neighbourhood is either adaptive or anti-adaptive in absolute terms. III. Revising planning rules alone may be ineffective when the ownership system stifles change. IV. The traditional city grew in a legal vacuum, without codes.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I and III are true: change happens inside AANs, and rule changes can fail because of communal ownership. II is false: adaptability is 'a relative concept'. IV is false: growth was governed by codes protecting neighbours (Hakim)."},{"t":"cozz","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Cozzolino (2020), which statements are true? I. Public spaces should be created only when strictly necessary. II. Top-down design may be indispensable for certain common spaces. III. Participatory design and city gaming have solved the problem of new AANs. IV. Self-building can distribute design control and produce variety as a natural result.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"I, II and IV are true: public space should be questioned for its concrete utility, a top-down skeleton may be indispensable, and self-building distributes design control. III is false: such processes still tend to produce rational, mechanical designs fixed in space."},{"t":"cozz","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Cozzolino argues that many independent owners, acting incrementally within proscriptive rules, make neighbourhoods adaptive. How would a Marxist political economist critique this?","o":["Adaptation depends on shared norms and scripts, so new rules alone will not change how residents act","Uncoordinated plot-level changes lack the comprehensive synthesis needed to serve the public interest","Local claims about needs should first be tested in hybrid forums before they shape the neighbourhood","Markets are power structures, so what emerges serves owners' exchange value more than residents' use"],"a":3,"e":"Tutorial 1 gives this Marx-inspired critique: markets are a power structure and 'spontaneous' outcomes may raise exchange value for owners rather than use value for residents. The other options are sociological institutionalist, governmental and Rydin (2007) critiques."},{"t":"cozz","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"How would a planner working in Rydin's governmental model most plausibly critique Cozzolino's preference for incremental, owner-led change?","o":["Owner-led change raises land values and so mainly serves capital accumulation, not residents","Incremental changes cannot be integrated into a reasoned, balanced view that serves set goals","Owners will free ride on others' improvements, so adaptation stays far below the social optimum","Adaptation is shaped by habits and cultural scripts that rules and ownership cannot easily change"],"a":1,"e":"The governmental model draws legitimacy from a comprehensive synthesis by planners serving politically set goals; dispersed incremental change escapes that integration. The free-rider option is a rational choice critique, and the capital option is Marxist."},{"t":"cozz","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: two Dutch districts both consist of 400 individually owned houses. In District A the zoning plan fixes footprints, heights and uses exactly as built; in District B the rules only forbid nuisance and set a height limit well above the existing homes. After 20 years, B shows many extensions and home businesses; A is unchanged. Which concept explains the difference most precisely?","o":["Distribution of design control through ownership","Locality knowledge held by the residents","Rules that leave room beyond the built form","Avoiding synchronous processes of decay"],"a":2,"e":"Both districts have many independent owners, so ownership and locality knowledge are held constant; the difference is whether the rules coincide with the built configuration or leave room for further change. The tempting answer, design control through ownership, does not vary between the two cases."},{"t":"cozz","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A planning report argues: 'Our task is not to design the perfect neighbourhood, but to set the few rules under which thousands of households can keep changing their homes for decades without harming their neighbours.' Which reading grounds this, and why?","o":["Cozzolino (2020): action, ownership and time make areas adaptive","Hayek (1944): a democracy cannot agree on the ends of a single plan","Rydin (2007): many knowledge claims must be heard and then tested","Rydin (2021): planners integrate all claims into one balanced view"],"a":0,"e":"The passage combines action (households changing homes), time (decades) and proscriptive rules (no harm to neighbours) in the built environment: Cozzolino's argument. Hayek also prefers general rules, but his 1944 chapter is about agreement on the ends of national planning, not incremental change in neighbourhoods."},{"t":"cozz","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which features does Cozzolino attribute to ADAPTIVE built environments? I. Buildings with different values, states of maintenance and users. II. Large open spaces managed by one public agency. III. As many designers as there are users over time.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and III describe adaptive environments: incremental building gives varied values and users, and design responsibility is spread over all users. II is an AAN characteristic: large open spaces managed by one agency, with Jacobs' 'streets without eyes'."},{"t":"cozz","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Cozzolino warns that changing the planning rules of an AAN may, on its own, fail to make it self-regenerating. Statement II: In communal ownership, significant changes require the consent of large majorities, which discourages change.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true, and II explains I: Cozzolino says the ownership system can be so important that revising rules is completely ineffective, because communal ownership needs large majorities and stifles self-regeneration."},{"t":"cozz","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Cozzolino argues that public spaces should be venerated as the core of urban quality and maximised in new districts. Statement II: Cozzolino sees public spaces as a robust skeleton, designed top-down, on which small private transformations can occur over time.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"I is false: he says public spaces 'should not be venerated in an uncritical fashion' and should be created only if strictly necessary. II is true: public space as 'a robust skeleton' with bottom-up design on individual lots."},{"t":"cozz","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs why Cozzolino links adaptability to innovation?","o":["Planners collect dispersed knowledge, so one optimal design anticipates future needs and avoids costly trial and error","Many users act on dispersed local knowledge, so trial and error renews the fabric in ways nobody could predict","Many users act on dispersed local knowledge, so a consensus emerges on one plan that experts then implement","Communal owners pool their local knowledge, so large majorities can agree on innovations for the whole estate"],"a":1,"e":"Adaptability makes efficient use of dispersed local knowledge: people renew the urban fabric in unpredictable ways, and through continuous trial and error society achieves innovations otherwise unachievable. The third chain breaks the last link: knowledge is used through many actions, not through a consensus plan."},{"t":"cozz","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combine Hayek (1944) and Cozzolino (2020). Which statement links them correctly?","o":["Both say democracy works only once residents agree on a single plan for their neighbourhood","Hayek's general rules and Cozzolino's proscriptive rules limit power without fixing outcomes","Both hold that experts should set the ends of planning while citizens choose the means locally","Cozzolino's prescriptive standards apply Hayek's call to judge each case on its particular merits"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek accepts delegating general rule-making but not discretion over particular cases; Cozzolino prefers proscriptive rules that only prevent externalities over prescriptions fixing spatial outcomes. The last option inverts both: Hayek warns against judging cases 'on their merits', and Cozzolino criticises prescriptions."},{"t":"cozz","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Compare Cozzolino (2020) with Rydin's governmental model (2021). Which statement is accurate for BOTH readings?","o":["Both locate legitimacy in a rational synthesis made by expert planners","Both see local knowledge as claims that must be tested in public forums","Both accept that some common structures need deliberate top-down design","Both treat the ownership pattern as the decisive cause of planning failure"],"a":2,"e":"The governmental model is deliberate throughout, and Cozzolino also accepts top-down design for a public skeleton and certain common spaces. Rational synthesis as the source of legitimacy fits only the governmental model; Cozzolino wants dispersed, incremental decisions instead."},{"t":"gov","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Rydin explains 'missing markets': without a pricing mechanism to ration use, resources get over-exploited. Which example does she give?","o":["Beautiful views","Street lighting","Good housing","Clean drinking water"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin says beautiful views can often be subject to this mechanism of over-exploitation. Street lighting is her example of a public good, and good housing of a merit good."},{"t":"gov","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Rydin names one author as a key proponent of planning based in systems thinking. Who?","o":["Nathaniel Lichfield","Brian McLoughlin","Charles Lindblom","Ian Sanderson"],"a":1,"e":"McLoughlin (1969) proposed planning as based in systems thinking, as a way to integrate dimensions. Lichfield supplies the eleven-step general planning process, and Lindblom disjointed incrementalism."},{"t":"gov","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whose phrase does Rydin quote for evidence-based planning: 'the assumption that we can make policies work better if we understand how policy mechanisms bring about change'?","o":["Kontokosta (2018)","Davoudi (2006)","Sanderson (2002)","Smith (2018)"],"a":2,"e":"The definition of evidence-based planning is quoted from Sanderson (2002). Kontokosta is cited for big data and computational methods; Davoudi for the suggestion that policy often leads to the choice of evidence."},{"t":"gov","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"Smith (2018), cited by Rydin, distinguishes 'plan implementation' from 'planning implementation'. What is plan implementation?","o":["How planners negotiate with developers over a permit","Whether different policy fields are integrated in practice","How plans frame debates and offer a narrative resource","Whether a plan's stated goals and objectives were achieved"],"a":3,"e":"Plan implementation evaluates whether the stated goals or objectives were achieved; planning implementation is closer to integration and needs separate process-based evaluation. Framing debates is Kaza's point about other purposes of plans."},{"t":"gov","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which study in Rydin's chapter built a standardised climate change vulnerability index, weighting exposure, sensitivity and adaptive capacity, and mapped it by ward?","o":["Kumar et al. on Bangalore","Hettinga et al. on Escamp","Zhou et al. on Yulin City","Ma et al. on Chongming"],"a":0,"e":"Kumar et al. (2016) developed the vulnerability assessment tool for Bangalore; Rydin calls it 'definitely expert led'. Hettinga et al. piloted a Geodesign decision support system for energy planning in Escamp, The Hague."},{"t":"gov","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What does Kaza (2019) argue, according to Rydin?","o":["Plans fail because politicians set unclear goals","Every plan should be monitored with indicators","Not all plans should be judged by implementation","Planners should never negotiate over their plans"],"a":2,"e":"Kaza argues that some plans have other purposes, such as framing debates, spreading information or offering a narrative resource, so they should not all be judged by implementation. He even notes authorities adopting mutually inconsistent plans."},{"t":"gov","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A municipality buys a former industrial site and develops a business park on it itself. Which policy instrument from Rydin's list is this?","o":["Fiscal measures such as taxes and subsidies","Direct public investment, incl. landownership","Regulation and the granting of authorisations","Strategic coordination through spatial planning"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin lists direct public investment, including landownership and procurement, as one of the instruments that together measure the power of the state planner. Regulation would be granting or refusing permits for someone else's project."},{"t":"gov","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the three research themes Rydin uses to show research in the governmental frame?","o":["Supporting decision-making","Implementation and evaluation","Assessing integration","Mapping power and conflict"],"a":3,"e":"The three themes are supporting decision-making, implementation including evaluation, and analysing and promoting integration. Power and conflict are exactly what the frame leaves out; they come in later chapters (e.g. urban politics)."},{"t":"gov","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Furlong et al. revised Lichfield's process for water infrastructure, adding stakeholder input and circular feedback. Why does Rydin still place this research in the governmental frame?","o":["Because it rejects any role for technical experts","Because it treats water as a pure market commodity","Because it keeps decision analysis at its core","Because it lets communities take the decisions"],"a":2,"e":"Despite more engagement and circularity, the key elements remain decision analysis and decision-taking, with planners reshaping their own practice and improving procedures at the margin. It does not hand decisions to communities."},{"t":"gov","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Ma et al.'s evaluation of Chongming Eco Island used a broader matrix than the official indicators. What does Rydin say the governmental framing emphasises in this study?","o":["The plan's assumed agency in producing outcomes","The power of national elites over local residents","The way residents construct the meaning of 'eco'","The rent that landowners capture from the policy"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin says the framing emphasises the assumed agency of the plan and judging outcomes in relation to the plan; the evaluation matrix is treated as sufficient, with no further conceptualisation needed. Power and rent are outside this frame."},{"t":"gov","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"How do Zhou et al. use the governmental model in their study of Yulin City, China?","o":["As an outdated ideal they replace with markets","As a benchmark, then design a better version","As a critique of state power over local land","As a method for testing residents' claims"],"a":1,"e":"The ideal governmental model is the benchmark for diagnosing fragmentation; they then propose an alternative governmental model, the 'multi-planning link model' with a 'One Map'. They do not turn to markets."},{"t":"gov","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT a reason Rydin gives for why the governmental vision is so attractive to planners?","o":["It makes planners the main actors","It suggests planning is under their control","It shows it is rational, so hard to argue with","It gives residents the final say on plans"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin says the vision makes planners the main actors, suggests planning is under their control, demonstrates their knowledge and is rational and hence difficult to argue with. Residents having the final say runs against the model, where stakeholders are involved only instrumentally."},{"t":"gov","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Why, according to Rydin, may the effectiveness of planning be 'taken for granted to some degree'?","o":["Because politicians forbid planners to evaluate their plans","Because many confounding factors make effects hard to prove","Because outcomes are always measured before plans are made","Because welfare economics proves that planning is efficient"],"a":1,"e":"Market and social dynamics as well as planning actions shape urban change, so it is hard to establish how planning achieves its goals; assumptions about planning's effect fill the gap. Welfare economics justifies planning but does not prove its effects."},{"t":"gov","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Rydin notes the state can struggle with both too little knowledge (of the right kind) and too much (to process). Which approach does she say this led to?","o":["Lindblom's disjointed incrementalism","Davidoff's advocacy planning","McLoughlin's systems approach","Lichfield's general planning process"],"a":0,"e":"The limits of generating and absorbing knowledge led to more pragmatic approaches such as Lindblom's disjointed incrementalism: incremental steps towards the comprehensive vision. McLoughlin's systems approach is a way of achieving integration, not a response to its limits."},{"t":"gov","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to the governmental model as Rydin (2021) describes it, which statements are true? I. Goals are set in the political realm and stand outside the planning process. II. Research should question the goals that politicians have set. III. Tools such as CBA and MCA offer information rather than make decisions. IV. Problems with planning are located within the planning process.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I, III and IV are true: goals come from politics, tools inform the locus of decision, and problems are sought inside the process. II is false: research in this frame takes the goals as given, as the rational planner does."},{"t":"gov","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to implementation studies and evaluation as Rydin presents them, which statements are true? I. Outcomes depend on which tools are used, not on how they are used. II. Planners exercise discretion and negotiate with developers, communities and other departments. III. Policy goals can be reformulated during implementation. IV. Kaza holds that every plan should be judged by whether its stated goals were achieved.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III F · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"II and III are true: discretion, negotiation and goal reformulation blur the politics/administration line. I is false: a key insight is that it matters how tools are used. IV is false: Kaza argues that not all plans should be judged by implementation."},{"t":"gov","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"In the governmental model, politicians set the goals and expert planners choose the best means. How would Hayek (1944) critique this?","o":["Planners and politicians pursue their own interests, so state action fails just as markets do","The state planner is not neutral, since planning serves the reproduction of capital and landed interests","Assemblies agree there should be a plan but not on its ends, so experts end up imposing their own values","Planners' choices follow taken-for-granted norms and cognitive scripts, so means are never neutral"],"a":2,"e":"Hayek argues that democratic assemblies can agree on planning but not on its ends, so planning is handed to experts, who must impose their own scale of preferences. The first option is a rational choice critique, the second Marxist, the fourth sociological institutionalist."},{"t":"gov","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"The governmental model justifies planning by market failure and defines the public interest as the greatest net benefit to society. How would a Marxist political economist critique this?","o":["The public interest should emerge from deliberation among all stakeholders, not from calculation","The idea of a neutral public interest hides that planning serves capital and property interests","No central body can rank everyone's ends, so 'net benefit' is a superstition about majorities","Politicians and officials maximise budgets, so government failure is as likely as market failure"],"a":1,"e":"A Marxist reading sees the state and planning as embedded in capitalist relations, so a neutral 'public interest' masks class and property interests. The fourth option is public choice (rational choice); the first is a communicative critique."},{"t":"gov","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a ministry publishes an annual dashboard of every municipality's housing output so parliament can hold them to account. Separately, it funds research into which instruments actually speed up construction and why. Which activity is evidence-based planning in Sanderson's sense, as quoted by Rydin?","o":["The dashboard, since data on outputs are the evidence base","Both equally, since both produce public information on plans","The research: it studies how policy mechanisms bring change","Neither, since evidence-based planning concerns forecasting only"],"a":2,"e":"Sanderson's evidence-based planning rests on understanding how policy mechanisms bring about change; he distinguishes this from providing information about public action to improve accountability, which is what the dashboard does. Forecasting is part of evidence, but not the definition."},{"t":"gov","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A municipal policy note states: 'The council has set the target of 10,000 new homes by 2035. It is now for the department to assess alternative locations, forecast their effects and recommend the option that meets this target at the lowest cost.' Which perspective grounds this note?","o":["Rydin (2021) governmental model: politically set ends, expert means","Rydin (2007): many knowledge claims are opened up and closed down","Hayek (1944): agreement on a plan is limited to fields of consensus","Cozzolino (2020): many owners adapt the city incrementally over time"],"a":0,"e":"The council sets the goal and the department applies expertise to forecasting and choosing the most efficient means: the Weberian separation and instrumental rationality of the governmental model. Rydin (2007) would ask which knowledge claims are being made and how they are tested."},{"t":"gov","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which are among the policy instruments that, according to Rydin, together measure the power of the state planner? I. Tradeable market-based permits. II. Information and persuasion, including nudges. III. Binding referendums held among residents.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin lists direct investment, strategic coordination, regulation, fiscal measures, tradeable permits, and information and persuasion including nudges. Referendums are not on the list and do not fit a model in which stakeholders are involved only instrumentally."},{"t":"gov","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rydin presents Tajima and Fischer's study of impact assessments in English SEA as framed by the public administration approach. Statement II: Their results suggested that full integration can downgrade certain sustainability values.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both are true, but II does not explain I: the study is in the frame because it assumes the value of integration and studies procedures; its finding that integration can subordinate values is what lets it critique the approach from within."},{"t":"gov","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rydin suggests the smart city might be seen as the latest exemplar of the governmental approach. Statement II: The governmental approach looks outward at markets and power as the main causes of planning failure.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: the smart city integrates technological rationality into the heart of urban planning. II is false: the approach locates problems within the planning process and does not stray far into its context."},{"t":"gov","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs what implementation studies showed about the Weberian divide?","o":["Planners apply impersonal rules, so goals stay fixed, so politics and administration remain clearly separated","Planners use discretion and negotiate, so goals are reformulated, so planners become inevitably political","Planners use discretion and negotiate, so goals stay fixed, so politicians must take over implementation","Politicians reformulate goals after elections, so planners lose discretion, so administration turns neutral"],"a":1,"e":"Discretion and negotiation in using policy tools mean goals are reformulated through implementation, which questions the clear divide and makes planners inevitably political. The third chain breaks the middle link: goals do not stay fixed."},{"t":"gov","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combine Rydin (2021) and Rydin (2007). What would the 2007 article add to the governmental model's view of evidence?","o":["Knowledge is a mirror of nature, so experts should gather even more data","Knowledge is just storytelling, so evidence should be dropped from plans","Knowledge claims from many actors must be heard and tested in arenas","Knowledge should be replaced by ethical claims settled by majority vote"],"a":2,"e":"The governmental model assumes knowledge can be gathered 'in a positive way' by experts; Rydin (2007) treats knowledge as socially constructed claims from lay and expert actors, to be opened up and then tested and closed down. She rejects both the mirror view and pure storytelling."},{"t":"gov","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Compare Rydin's governmental model (2021) with Cozzolino (2020) on where urban order should come from.","o":["Governmental: from emergent action; Cozzolino: from expert synthesis","Both: from a comprehensive plan made with intensive participation","Both: from market processes, with the state only fixing failures","Governmental: from expert synthesis; Cozzolino: from many owners"],"a":3,"e":"The governmental model derives order and legitimacy from a deliberate synthesis by planners serving politically set goals; Cozzolino wants order to emerge from many owners' incremental actions under proscriptive rules. The first option swaps the two positions."},{"t":"hayek","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Hayek opens 'Planning and democracy' with a warning that a statesman who directs how private people employ their capital assumes an authority no council could safely be trusted with. Whose warning?","o":["John Stuart Mill","Lord Acton","Adam Smith","Edmund Burke"],"a":2,"e":"The chapter's epigraph is Adam Smith's warning against a statesman directing private capital. Acton is mentioned much later, when Hayek contrasts his view of liberty as the highest end with democracy as a means."},{"t":"hayek","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to Hayek, what has happened to moral rules as civilisation has developed?","o":["They have become more complete, covering more and more choices","They have become fewer and more general, marking out a free sphere","They have been replaced by the law of the state in most fields","They have become more collective as societies grew more complex"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek notes a steady shrinking of the sphere governed by fixed rules: morals became fewer, more general and increasingly mere limits around a sphere where individuals may act as they like. A comprehensive code for a unitary plan would reverse this trend."},{"t":"hayek","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"Hayek says the refusal to recognise autonomous spheres in which individuals' ends are supreme is what one word truly means. Which word?","o":["Authoritarian","Paternalist","Corporatist","Totalitarian"],"a":3,"e":"Hayek writes that organising the whole of society for one unitary end, refusing autonomous spheres for individual ends, is what 'totalitarian' truly means. Authoritarian is tempting but is not his term here."},{"t":"hayek","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which British committee's call for 'elasticity' in delegated powers does Hayek read as the conferment of arbitrary power?","o":["The Donoughmore Committee","The Beveridge Committee","The Barlow Commission","The Uthwatt Committee"],"a":0,"e":"A footnote reads the Donoughmore Committee's demand for 'elasticity' as conferring arbitrary power. Barlow and Uthwatt are real British planning reports of the period, which makes them plausible but wrong."},{"t":"hayek","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"How, according to Hayek, are the arbitrary decisions of an authority with discretionary power usually described?","o":["As 'acting in the public interest'","As 'judging the case on its merits'","As 'serving the general welfare'","As 'applying sound expert advice'"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek writes that such authorities make arbitrary decisions 'usually described as judging the case on its merits'. 'General welfare' is a term he discusses elsewhere, as a vague label hiding the absence of agreement on ends."},{"t":"hayek","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"In the HC4 cartoon 'Liberalism enters the chat', Marx says abolishing private property will change the social structure. How does Hayek reply?","o":["'Property is the root of all freedom, Marx'","'Only the market can set the right prices'","'We might try, Marx: but who knows what happens then?'","'History will prove that you are wrong'"],"a":2,"e":"The cartoon has Hayek answer 'We might try, Marx: but who knows what happens then?', framing his objection as uncertainty about outcomes (indeterminism), not as a defence of the status quo."},{"t":"hayek","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Hayek writes that the goal of planning is often described by a term like 'common welfare'. What does such a term do, in his view?","o":["It provides a clear criterion against which experts can test a plan","It conceals the absence of real agreement on the ends of planning","It expresses the identical ends that all citizens share in a nation","It ensures that the plan serves the poor rather than the wealthy"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek says a term like 'common welfare' 'only conceals the absence of real agreement on the ends of planning': agreement exists only on the mechanism. He denies that such a term gives experts a usable criterion."},{"t":"hayek","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT among Hayek's reasons why parliaments fail once they embark on central planning?","o":["They are asked to produce agreement on everything, not to act where they agree","A plan needs a unitary conception and cannot be voted on clause by clause","Majority voting only suits choices between limited sets of alternatives","They are captured by the capitalist class, whose interests they defend"],"a":3,"e":"Hayek blames the task, not the institution: assemblies must produce agreement on everything, a plan needs a unitary conception, and majorities suit limited alternatives. Capture by the capitalist class is a Marxist explanation, not his."},{"t":"hayek","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Why, according to Hayek, do experts who receive delegated planning powers inevitably impose their own scale of preferences?","o":["Because they are personally ambitious and seek power for themselves","Because parliament instructs them to follow the majority party","Because they must choose between conflicting ends of different people","Because their technical training makes them value efficiency above all"],"a":2,"e":"Unlike a general with one supreme goal, the economic planner must choose between the competing ends of different people; only those who know all the facts can see which ends conflict, so they decide. Hayek does not blame experts' motives or ambition."},{"t":"hayek","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"How does Hayek define 'social ends'?","o":["The ends that a democratic majority imposes on all citizens","The identical ends of many individuals, or ends they will help others with","The ends that experts derive from a complete ranking of all values","The ends of the state as a person separate from its own citizens"],"a":1,"e":"For Hayek social ends are simply identical ends of many individuals, or ends to which people are willing to contribute in return for help with their own. They do not come from a majority imposition or from an expert ranking, which is exactly what he says is impossible."},{"t":"hayek","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"The HC4 take-home slide summarises what a Hayekian planner does. What?","o":["Sets the right framework conditions for spontaneous order to emerge","Designs the optimal land-use pattern from a full set of relevant data","Redistributes land to secure material equality between all residents","Mediates between stakeholders until they agree on one common vision"],"a":0,"e":"The slide: 'A Hayekian planner focuses setting the right framework conditions to allow for spontaneous order to guide urban development', which emerges if there is equality of rights. Material equality is Marx's value on the HC4 slides."},{"t":"hayek","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Hayek says that in a centrally planned society the will of a small minority is often imposed on the people. Why?","o":["Because elites always control the state under any economic system","Because voters delegate their votes to the most competent minority","Because minorities are better informed about the economy's needs","Because the plan cannot wait for a majority; the largest group able to agree decides"],"a":3,"e":"A planned society cannot make control depend on a majority being able to agree, so often the largest group able to agree among themselves, possibly a small minority, imposes its will. Hayek does not make a general elite-theory claim, which is closer to Marxist reasoning."},{"t":"hayek","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which claim about democracy does Hayek NOT make in this chapter?","o":["Democracy is a means, not the highest political end","Democracy is not infallible and can be oppressive","A homogeneous majority can be as oppressive as a dictator","Democratic origin of power keeps it from being arbitrary"],"a":3,"e":"Hayek's conclusion is the reverse: 'it is not the source but the limitation of power which prevents it from being arbitrary'. The other three are claims he makes when he refuses to make a fetish of democracy."},{"t":"hayek","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A council agrees that a district needs more homes, but members split over many incompatible layouts and none gains a majority. Which claim of Hayek's does this illustrate?","o":["The individual is the ultimate judge of his own ends","It is a superstition that there must be a majority view on everything","Democracy is a utilitarian device for internal peace","Delegating general rule-making to local bodies is acceptable"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek says majority decision works for choices between limited alternatives, but 'it is a superstition to believe that there must be a majority view on everything'. The ultimate-judge principle concerns individual values, not the failure to find a majority among many options."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: frustrated by slow debates, a new cabinet proposes an enabling act that lets the housing minister issue decrees on where, what and for whom to build without further votes, saying the crisis is too urgent for a 'talking shop'. Which concept from Hayek fits most precisely?","o":["Acceptable delegation of general rule-making to a competent authority","Delegation of discretion, a step towards plebiscitarian dictatorship","Democracy as a means, which justifies any efficient decision procedure","The price of democracy, which the cabinet is rightly prepared to pay"],"a":1,"e":"Delegating case-by-case decisions because parliament cannot agree on ends is the delegation of discretion that Hayek sees as a step on the road to dictatorship, as with Laski's enabling acts. It is not delegation of general rules, which he accepts."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Hayek's final lesson about power most precisely?","o":["Power is arbitrary unless a democratic majority has conferred it","Power stays democratic as long as parliament keeps ultimate control","Power is kept from being arbitrary by its limits, not by its source","Any limit on state power makes democratic government impossible"],"a":2,"e":"Hayek: 'it is not the source but the limitation of power which prevents it from being arbitrary'. The second option is Mannheim's position, which Hayek rejects: a parliament that can only accept or reject a plan as a whole cannot direct it."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A pamphlet argues: 'We all agree the economy must be planned. Let us end the endless debates in parliament, give the planning board full powers, and let the experts decide what is best for the nation.' Which reading most directly diagnoses the danger here, and why?","o":["Rydin (2021): politicians must first set the goals before experts choose means","Hayek: agreement is only on the mechanism, so experts impose their own values","Marx: the board will serve the owners of the means of production, not workers","Rydin (2007): the board's knowledge claims must first be tested in open arenas"],"a":1,"e":"Agreement that 'the economy must be planned' without agreement on ends, followed by delegation to experts, is Hayek's mechanism: the experts must rank competing ends and impose their scale of preferences. The governmental model would merely ask for goals first; it does not see delegation itself as the danger."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with 'Planning and democracy'?","o":["In 1928, German public authorities controlled about 53% of national income","Hitler took advantage of a democracy that had already decayed","Parliament can control execution when it agrees on the aim of a plan","Dictatorship must always destroy all cultural and personal freedom"],"a":3,"e":"Hayek explicitly does not claim that dictatorship must extirpate freedom; he even notes more cultural freedom under some autocracies. His claim is that planning leads to dictatorship because dictatorship is the most effective instrument of coercion."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim: 'Because no one can survey more than a limited field of needs, scales of value exist only in individual minds, so people should follow their own values within defined limits'?","o":["Marx (1844)","Hayek (1944)","Rydin (2007)","Scott (1998)"],"a":1,"e":"This is Hayek's foundation of individualism: the limits of any mind, not egoism, make the individual the ultimate judge of his ends. Rydin (2007) is pluralist about knowledge holders, but she argues about testing knowledge claims, not about individual scales of value."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which does Hayek present as features or stages of democratic planning sliding into dictatorship? I. Separate sectoral plans fail to add up to a planned whole. II. A cry arises for an economic dictator. III. Parliament is reduced to periodically confirming a leader with practically absolute power.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"All three are in the chapter: 'Many separate plans do not make a planned whole', the 'cry for an economic dictator' (Halévy's composite photograph), and 'plebiscitarian dictatorship', where parliament can only choose persons with practically absolute power."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of Hayek's chapter would a Marxist most plausibly make?","o":["Agreement on ends can be built through deliberation, not only found","Experts can rank goals rigorously with cost-benefit analysis","Freedom based on private property is formal for those without it","Value pluralism does not remove the need to test knowledge claims"],"a":2,"e":"A Marxist would say Hayek's freedom, grounded in a competitive system of free disposal over private property, is formal: it protects owners and ignores material inequality. The first option is the communicative critique and the second the governmental model's reply."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hayek (1944), which statements are true? I. A single economic plan presupposes a complete ethical code. II. As civilisation grew, moral rules became more numerous and detailed. III. Democracy is the highest end, above individual freedom. IV. Agreement on the ends of state action grows as the scope of that action widens.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":1,"e":"Only I is true. Moral rules became fewer and more general (II false); democracy is 'essentially a means, a utilitarian device' (III false); and the probability of agreement falls as the scope of state action widens (IV false)."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hayek (1944), which statements are true? I. People may agree that there should be planning without agreeing on its ends. II. Individualism means that people should act out of egoism. III. Once the state controls a large enough share of resources, it indirectly controls almost everything. IV. A plan needs a unitary conception and cannot be voted on clause by clause.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV F"],"a":2,"e":"I, III and IV are true: agreement on planning is not agreement on ends, state control of resources spreads to almost everything, and majority voting cannot settle a plan's unitary conception. II is false: for Hayek individualism is not egoism but the recognition that no mind can survey all needs."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"How would a planner working in Rydin's governmental model (2021) critique Hayek's 'Planning and democracy'?","o":["Formal equal rights hide material inequality, so liberal freedom mainly serves the owners","Shared ends need not pre-exist: stakeholders can build them through deliberation together","Preferences are shaped by institutions, so individuals are not the final judges of fixed ends","Markets fail through externalities and public goods, so politically set goals justify planning"],"a":3,"e":"The governmental model justifies planning by market failure: politicians set goals in the public interest and experts choose the means, which is what Hayek doubts. The first option is Marxist, the second communicative, the third sociological institutionalist."},{"t":"hayek","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Hayek argues that a single economic plan presupposes a complete ethical code. Statement II: The welfare of millions cannot be measured on one scale of more and less, so a plan needs every need of every person ranked in one hierarchy of ends.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Hayek reasons that welfare depends on endless combinations of things and can only be expressed as one hierarchy of ends; hence a plan 'presupposes, in short, the existence of a complete ethical code'. II gives the reason for I."},{"t":"hayek","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Hayek holds that parliament can keep real control over a comprehensive economic plan by delegating only its details. Statement II: When delegation happens because there is no agreement on ends, parliament can only accept or reject the plan as a whole.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"I is the planners' (Mannheim's) view, which Hayek rejects for comprehensive plans; parliament controls execution only when it has agreed on the aim. II is Hayek's own claim, and it is precisely why I is false."},{"t":"hayek","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs why, for Hayek, delegated planning power becomes arbitrary?","o":["The matter cannot be governed by general rules, so the body decides cases by discretion, so it ranks ends without rules","Delegation is undemocratic as such, so any delegated body lacks a mandate, so all its decisions are arbitrary","Experts pursue their own interests, so they bend the rules, so their decisions favour themselves over citizens","The task is too technical for parliament, so it is delegated, so the experts' rules are too complex to follow"],"a":0,"e":"Hayek objects to delegation when a matter 'cannot be regulated by general rules but only by the exercise of discretion in the decision of particular cases'; the body then makes in effect arbitrary decisions. He explicitly accepts delegation of general rule-making, so delegation as such is not the problem."},{"t":"hayek","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combine Hayek (1944) and Rydin (2007). Which statement fits both readings best?","o":["Both argue experts should rank society's ends, since lay people lack the knowledge","Hayek says ends can be tested scientifically; Rydin says ends are private tastes","Hayek's problem is agreement on ends; Rydin separates ethical from knowledge claims","Both hold that a majority vote is the right way to settle claims about causal links"],"a":2,"e":"Hayek's argument turns on the absence of a complete ethical code, i.e. on ends; Rydin distinguishes ethical claims from knowledge claims about causal relations, which can be tested. Neither wants experts to rank ends, and Rydin explicitly rejects settling knowledge by popularity."},{"t":"know","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Rydin (2007) starts from what one science-studies scholar calls the breakdown of the 'modernist consensus'. Who?","o":["Bruno Latour","Jürgen Habermas","Brian Wynne","Sheila Jasanoff"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin opens with Latour's 'breakdown of the modernist consensus': knowledge is no longer a mirror of nature produced by neutral experts. Wynne and Jasanoff appear later in the article (realist constructivism, co-production), and Habermas supplies the validity claims, not the starting point."},{"t":"know","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Rydin borrows a term from Rip for the arenas in which different knowledge claims are engaged and tested. Which term?","o":["Citizen juries","Epistemic communities","Hybrid forums","Policy networks"],"a":2,"e":"Rip speaks of 'hybrid forums' for 'agonistic, collective learning', and Rydin adopts this for her testing arenas. Epistemic communities (Haas) and communities of practice (Wenger) are cited earlier as examples of knowledge being embedded in social networks, not as testing arenas."},{"t":"know","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Rydin sees knowledge as socially constructed yet engaged with material reality. Which label (after Wynne) fits this position?","o":["Naive empiricism","Strong cultural relativism","Positivist objectivism","Realist constructivism"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin links her view to Wynne's 'realist constructivism': knowledge is constructed, but claims engage a real world and can be tested. Strong relativism is the tempting wrong answer: it denies any neutral way to compare claims, which Rydin rejects."},{"t":"know","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which policy field is one of the three stylised examples in Rydin's Table 1?","o":["Sustainable construction","Regional railway corridors","Brownfield remediation","Coastal flood defence"],"a":0,"e":"Table 1 applies the typology to sustainable construction, the housing market and landscape values. The other options are plausible planning fields but do not appear in the table."},{"t":"know","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Rydin says it makes a difference whether an area's affluence is described by market prices or by child poverty. What does this example show?","o":["Empirical claims are less reliable than predictive ones","Lay knowledge should replace expert statistics","Even descriptive knowledge involves value-laden framing","Normative claims can be reduced to empirical data"],"a":2,"e":"The example supports her point that none of the knowledge types is value-free: choosing what to describe already involves value judgements and carries a causal story. It does not say lay knowledge should replace statistics; that confuses value-ladenness with a ranking of knowledge holders."},{"t":"know","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Rydin follows two authors who argue that stakeholder rights differ from rights based on expertise. Who are they?","o":["Evans and Marvin","Collins and Evans","Gibbons and Nowotny","Innes and Booher"],"a":1,"e":"Collins and Evans distinguish stakeholder rights from expertise rights, which Rydin uses to separate knowledge claims from other claims. Evans and Marvin are cited for a different point: knowledges are not additive."},{"t":"know","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Rydin cites Nowotny's criteria for 'socially robust knowledge'. Which is NOT one of them?","o":["Tested for its validity","Involving an extended group of experts","Repeatedly tested, expanded and modified","Endorsed by most affected residents"],"a":3,"e":"Nowotny's criteria are validity testing, an extended group of experts, and repeated testing, expansion and modification. Majority endorsement by residents is not a criterion: robustness comes from testing, not from popularity, which is exactly Rydin's point about voices versus knowledges."},{"t":"know","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A neighbourhood group says a planned incinerator is unfair because their district already hosts most of the city's polluting facilities. In Rydin's terms, the core of this claim is…","o":["an ethical claim about fairness, to be heard but not tested as knowledge","a process claim, because it explains how pollution reaches residents","experiential knowledge that counts automatically because they live there","a predictive claim about the incinerator's future emissions levels"],"a":0,"e":"The core of the claim is about justice, so for Rydin it is an ethical claim: legitimate and important, but different in kind from a knowledge claim. Treating it as automatically valid experiential knowledge is the trap: experience is 'not of itself a sufficient condition' for knowledge."},{"t":"know","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A municipality joins an action-research project in which its own planners are studied while they trial a new parking policy. Which knowledge type is mainly being produced, according to Rydin's Table 2?","o":["Empirical knowledge of the current state","Predictive knowledge of the future state","Process knowledge of how change happens","Normative knowledge of a desired future"],"a":2,"e":"Table 2 links process claims to theoretically framed research and even action research, with planners as object of research or co-researchers. It is not predictive knowledge, which Rydin describes as mostly expert-led and focused on forecasting State B."},{"t":"know","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Why does Rydin describe predictive knowledge claims as mostly expert-led?","o":["Because only experts are entitled to stakeholder rights","Because lay knowledge is rooted in past and present experience","Because forecasts are value-free and need no public testing","Because Habermas assigns forecasts to the validity claim of truth"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin argues lay knowledge is grounded in past and present experience, so forecasting is mostly expert-led, with the planner as client alert to lay input. She does not say forecasts are value-free: she insists no knowledge type is, and adds debate on prediction techniques to the testing."},{"t":"know","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which statement would Rydin (2007) REJECT?","o":["Knowledge claims and ethical claims differ but neither ranks above the other","Lay participants can only offer experiential stories and emotions","Even a purely descriptive claim carries an embedded causal story","Forums may need to support actors who lack resources to take part"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin calls it patronising to assume that lay discourses must be limited to experiential stories or emotional realms; knowledge debates can be held in many languages. The other three are her own claims, including support for actors without resources."},{"t":"know","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Rydin follows Evans and Marvin in saying that knowledges are 'not additive'. What does this imply for deliberative planning?","o":["Combining more knowledges always yields a more complete picture","Expert knowledge prevails once lay claims are properly translated","A shared lingua franca alone will not resolve their disagreements","Deliberation should be replaced by committees of expert advisers"],"a":2,"e":"Because knowledges are not additive, translating them into a common language does not by itself settle disagreements rooted in different experiences. The opposite reading (more knowledges simply add up) is the tempting mistake; Rydin does not conclude that experts should take over either."},{"t":"know","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which task does Rydin NOT give the planner in the arena for normative claims about desired futures?","o":["Managing an informed public debate","Checking that a future is genuinely possible","Preventing possible futures from being ruled out","Selecting the best future from expert forecasts"],"a":3,"e":"For normative claims the planner manages informed debate, checks that proposed futures are warranted as possible and keeps all possible futures on the table, adding 'an ethical voice'. Choosing the future from expert forecasts would collapse normative debate into predictive expertise."},{"t":"know","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"The lecture links the Grenfell Tower fire to Rydin (2007). What does the case illustrate?","o":["That lay warnings were heard but then tested and found wrong","That residents' claims should have replaced fire-safety evidence","That local warnings were never opened up, let alone tested","That fire safety is a normative claim for public-sphere debate"],"a":2,"e":"The Grenfell Action Group's warnings were ignored: opening up would have heard them and closing down would have tested them against fire-safety evidence. Rydin would not let residents' claims simply replace evidence; she wants both voice and testing."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a university heat-stress map and a residents' 'heat walk' disagree on which streets of an old district are hottest. The municipality brings both groups together to inspect each other's measurements and agree a procedure to settle the differences. Which concept from Rydin (2007) fits most precisely?","o":["A public-sphere arena for normative claims about desired futures","A lay-expert forum for testing competing empirical claims","Consensus building through collective storytelling, as in Innes","Mode 2 action research producing process knowledge with planners"],"a":1,"e":"The dispute is about the current state (State A), so these are empirical claims; Table 2 asks for forums where lay and expert examine each other's claims with procedures to resolve them. Innes's storytelling is tempting, but Rydin says consensus building suits value disputes, not contested knowledge."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Rydin's knowledge-specific concern about deliberative processes most precisely?","o":["Deliberation fails mainly because most planners lack facilitation skills","Deliberation always produces weaker knowledge than expert analysis","Deliberation suits values, so it should be dropped from planning altogether","A focus on agreement may not ensure the best knowledge shapes decisions"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin's specific worry is that processes geared to agreement and trust may not ensure that the most appropriate knowledge shapes decisions. Planner skills is one of the general, well-known concerns she lists, not her knowledge-specific point, and she never says to drop deliberation."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A planning report argues: 'Our traffic and air-quality models are not the last word. Residents' observations must be heard, and then both sides' methods must be scrutinised in a joint forum, so that the plan rests on claims that have survived testing.' Which reading grounds this, and why?","o":["Rydin (2007): planners open up to claims and then close them down by testing","Rydin (2021): evidence is gathered positively by experts to support decisions","Healey: consensus is inherent in communication, so all voices count equally","Scott: practical mētis of residents should override the state's formal models"],"a":0,"e":"Hearing residents and then testing both sides in a forum is Rydin's opening up plus closing down. The governmental model is tempting because the report values evidence, but it treats knowledge as gathered by experts, not as lay and expert claims tested against each other."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about Rydin's Figure 1 and her knowledge types is NOT consistent with the article?","o":["Knowledge of State A serves as a benchmark for later comparison","Knowledge of State B matters because B1 must be judged against B","Process knowledge covers how A turns into B, into B1 and into B2","Knowledge of B2 can be dropped once the goal B1 is clearly set"],"a":3,"e":"Because outcomes differ from intentions, Rydin needs knowledge of B2 and of how A became B2; a clear goal does not make outcome knowledge redundant. The other statements are accurate: A is the benchmark, B1 is judged against B, and process knowledge covers all three transitions."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim: 'Planners should give voice to many claims and then actively recognise some of them as knowledge after testing: knowledge is co-produced'?","o":["Davoudi (2006)","Scott (1998)","Rydin (2007)","Cozzolino (2020)"],"a":2,"e":"Co-production with two parts, giving voice and testing and recognising, is Rydin's (2007) proposal. Cozzolino values locality knowledge but wants people to use it in their own adaptations, not to have it tested and recognised by planners in arenas."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which ideas does Rydin take from Habermas? I. Only speech acts that carry criticisable validity claims constitute communicative action. II. A speech act must be both understandable and warranted. III. Consensus arises from collective storytelling rather than the force of the better argument.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin uses Habermas to argue that communicative action requires criticisable validity claims and that a speech act must be understandable and warranted, so claims must be tested. III is Innes's description of how consensus building works, which Rydin uses to show its limits."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of Rydin's testing arenas would a Hayekian liberal most plausibly make?","o":["Arenas ignore that landed capital decides who can afford to fund expertise","Closing down restores the expert monopoly the communicative turn overcame","Much knowledge is dispersed local knowledge no forum can gather and test","Forums add little: experts should gather evidence and synthesise it rationally"],"a":2,"e":"The Hayekian line (dispersed local knowledge, Hayek 1945, cited by Cozzolino) doubts that any central arena can collect and test the knowledge of particular circumstances. The first option is a Marxist critique and the last a governmental-model reply; neither follows Hayek's logic."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin (2007), which statements are true? I. Knowledge differs from information and data because it specifies a causal relationship. II. Knowledge claims are more important than ethical claims. III. The experience of local stakeholders is of itself a sufficient condition for knowledge. IV. Planning theory has been better at discussing opening-up than closing-down.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":1,"e":"I and IV are true: causal specification defines knowledge, and Rydin says theory is better at opening-up than closing-down. II is false: knowledge claims are different from ethical claims, not more important. III is false: experience 'is not of itself a sufficient condition' for knowledge."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin (2007), which statements are true? I. Predictive knowledge claims are mostly expert-led. II. Normative knowledge of a desired future (B1) belongs in public-sphere debate. III. Empirical knowledge of the current state is value-free. IV. Planning has real effects, even when these differ from its stated intentions.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"I, II and IV are true: forecasting is mostly expert-led, normative claims about B1 go to the public sphere, and the actual outcome B2 shows planning has real (if unintended) effects. III is false: none of the knowledge types is value-free, as the market prices versus child poverty example shows."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Rydin (2007) wants arenas in which planners give voice to knowledge claims and then test them. How would a sociological institutionalist critique this?","o":["Actors will misrepresent their claims strategically, so arenas need incentives to reveal the truth","Arenas mask class conflict, since capital's interests decide in the end which claims count as robust","Knowledge should be gathered by experts in a positive way, so testing by lay actors only adds delay","The criteria for testing are embedded in taken-for-granted norms, so arenas reproduce dominant frames"],"a":3,"e":"A sociological institutionalist stresses that cognitive scripts and norms shape what counts as valid and appropriate, including the testing criteria themselves. The first option is rational choice, the second Marxist, the third the governmental model."},{"t":"know","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rydin calls planning theory's habit of labelling all community claims as 'knowledge' politically understandable. Statement II: Labelling a claim as knowledge raises the status of that claim.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin writes that labelling all community claims as knowledge is politically understandable because it raises their status; II gives the reason for I. She still rejects the practice: 'Not all claims within planning can be recast as knowledge claims just to promote the status of the claim-maker'."},{"t":"know","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rydin locates the testing of predictive claims mainly in open debate in the public sphere. Statement II: Rydin holds that lay knowledge is rooted in past and present experience.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Public-sphere debate is Rydin's arena for normative claims; predictive claims are mostly expert-led, tested by challenging causal models and prediction methods. II is true and is in fact her reason why prediction is expert-led, which makes I false."},{"t":"know","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly explains why Rydin needs knowledge of State B2?","o":["Planning is part of societal processes with real effects, so outcomes differ from intentions, so outcome knowledge is needed","Planning stands outside societal processes, so its effects are predictable, so B2 knowledge confirms that B1 was reached","Planning is purely symbolic and has no real effects, so B2 equals B, so B2 knowledge shows what happens without plans","Planning outcomes always match intentions, so B2 equals B1, so B2 knowledge is mainly needed to legitimise the plan"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin treats planning as part of the processes that turn A into B, with real effects even when B1 is not reached; hence B2 differs from both B and B1 and must be known. She explicitly rejects both the 'outside' view and the view that planning is purely symbolic."},{"t":"know","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combine Rydin (2007) with Rydin's governmental model (2021). Which statement is accurate?","o":["Both locate the legitimacy of plans in the rationality of expert analysis","2007 treats every stakeholder claim as knowledge; 2021 treats none as such","2021 involves stakeholders instrumentally; 2007 sees lay knowledge claims","2021 says goals must be tested in arenas; 2007 says goals stand outside"],"a":2,"e":"The governmental model involves stakeholders only instrumentally and gathers knowledge 'in a positive way'; Rydin (2007) accepts that lay actors can hold knowledge claims, which must be heard and tested. She does not treat every claim as knowledge: experience alone is not sufficient."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which 1999 UK government paper does Davoudi name as the starting point of the British push for evidence-based policy?","o":["The White Paper on Modernising Government","Planning Policy Statement 12 (PPS12)","The Barker Review of Housing Supply","The Schuster Report on planning education"],"a":0,"e":"Davoudi opens with New Labour's 1999 White Paper on Modernising Government, which asked for 'better use of evidence and research in policy-making'. PPS12 (2004) and the Barker Review (2004) come later in her article; the Schuster Committee dates from 1948."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"What does Davoudi call the kind of policy that the enlightenment model produces?","o":["Evidence-based policy","Evidence-informed policy","Evidence-driven policy","Policy-based evidence"],"a":1,"e":"Under the enlightenment model the emphasis is on evidence-informed rather than evidence-based policy: research clarifies the context and informs debate instead of dictating decisions. Evidence-based and evidence-driven policy belong to the instrumental model."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whose 'four I's' does Davoudi use to show what evidence has to compete with in policy-making?","o":["Nancy Shulock","Bent Flyvbjerg","Carol Weiss","Charles Lindblom"],"a":2,"e":"Davoudi takes the four I's (ideology, interests, institutional norms and practices, prior information) from Carol Weiss. Shulock is cited for the 'paradox of policy analysis', Flyvbjerg for power and knowledge, Lindblom for incrementalism."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which early planner's rule 'survey before plan' does Davoudi discuss?","o":["Ebenezer Howard","Brian McLoughlin","Patrick Abercrombie","Patrick Geddes"],"a":3,"e":"Geddes's 'survey before plan', later 'survey-analysis-plan', was every planning student's rule for decades. McLoughlin belongs to the 1960s systems view; Abercrombie is cited only for his triangle of objectives."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What was the name of the 2004 English test that asked whether plans rest on a 'robust and credible evidence base'?","o":["The sequential test","The viability test","The test of soundness","The public interest test"],"a":2,"e":"The 2004 reform (PPS12) introduced an independent examination of a plan's 'soundness', including whether strategies are founded on a robust and credible evidence base. The other tests are not discussed in the article."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"How does the instrumental model see the relation between research and policy?","o":["As indirect, slow and diffuse","As linear, direct and unproblematic","As two-way and set through interaction","As irrelevant, since policy ignores research"],"a":1,"e":"The instrumental view assumes the relation between evidence and policy is 'unproblematic, linear and direct', in either direction. Indirect and slow influence describes the enlightenment model; a two-way relationship is what Davoudi recommends."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A national research council sets its own research agenda, and ministries are expected to adopt whatever its findings show. Which of Davoudi's models fits this best?","o":["Instrumental: expert on tap","Enlightenment model","Political/tactical model","Instrumental: expert on top"],"a":3,"e":"When research leads and policy follows, policy is evidence-driven: the expert is 'on top', with 'an element of scientific inevitability'. Expert on tap is the reverse direction, where research serves questions set by policy."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which of Davoudi's examples illustrates Weiss's 'ideology' as a competitor of evidence?","o":["Urban containment kept on values despite Hall's study","DEFRA defining evidence by its strategic priorities","Commissioned research left unread on the shelf","Inconclusive studies on high-density housing"],"a":0,"e":"Urban containment and the Green Belt were sustained by strong values even after Peter Hall's 1973 study showed their regressive effects: ideology. DEFRA's definition illustrates interests, unread research illustrates institutions, and inconclusive density studies concern the nature of evidence (prior information)."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Davoudi quotes Popper that 'Science does not rest upon solid bedrock'. Which point does she use this for?","o":["Social science is less reliable than natural science","Even science is uncertain, so it cannot settle policy alone","Planners should go back to testing falsifiable hypotheses","Research should be left to universities, not ministries"],"a":1,"e":"Davoudi notes that uncertainty is not exclusive to social science: even natural science, as Popper says, rises above a swamp, so evidence cannot simply settle policy. The first option is tempting but she explicitly says such uncertainties are not limited to the social sciences."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"What does Weiss's 'problem of little effect', cited by Davoudi, refer to?","o":["Planning policies rarely change actual land-use outcomes","Small research budgets produce only marginal findings","Research, even when commissioned, often goes unused","Evidence has little effect on how citizens vote"],"a":2,"e":"Practical, institutional and political factors mean much research, even commissioned and paid for by government, sits on the shelf; a Cabinet Office audit found little of it was used by policy-makers. The other options are not what the phrase refers to."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Davoudi widens Weiss's 'prior information' into a view of what evidence is. Which claim is NOT part of that view?","o":["Facts become evidence only when used to support or refute a claim","Expert and experiential, formal and tacit knowledge can all count","What counts is what is available and accessible at a time and place","Evidence is valid only when produced by systematic research methods"],"a":3,"e":"Davoudi explicitly says evidence is not limited to research findings: it includes informal, experiential and tacit knowledge. The other three are her points, building on the dictionary definition of evidence."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"What did the 1970s structure plans lack, according to Davoudi, despite their massive statistical reports of survey?","o":["Policy innovation and imagination","Reliable data on population and jobs","A legal basis in national planning law","Approval by the national government"],"a":0,"e":"Davoudi concludes that the structure plans had huge reports of survey but lacked policy innovation and 'that elusive ingredient called imagination' that their predecessors had. Lack of data is the opposite of her point."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"The Cabinet Office report cited by Davoudi found a shortage of 'intelligent users' of research. What did it recommend?","o":["Build policy-makers' skills to find, commission and use evidence","Fund fewer but larger studies at universities and research centres","Let independent researchers set the policy agenda for each ministry","Replace long written reports by short oral briefings for ministers"],"a":0,"e":"The report recommended enhancing policy-makers' skills in dealing with evidence (knowing where to find it, how to commission it and how to use it) and improving its accessibility. It did not hand the agenda to researchers."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Davoudi says evidence, once part of criminal conviction, now seems part of political conviction too, 'or so we are led to believe'. What does this last phrase signal?","o":["Agreement that evidence now drives most British policy","A claim that planning has become a legal procedure","Concern that researchers are now openly partisan","Doubt that policy really follows evidence as claimed"],"a":3,"e":"The phrase introduces her scepticism: the article explores the gap between the rhetoric of evidence-based policy and the reality of how policy is made. It is not an endorsement of the evidence drive."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Davoudi (2006), which statements are true? I: More and better information always makes policy choices easier. II: Under the enlightenment model, research mainly clarifies the context and informs public debate. III: Davoudi concludes that planning needs less evidence and more political judgement. IV: What counts as evidence depends on what is available and accessible at a given time and place.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"II and IV are Davoudi's claims. I is false: more information can confuse choices that are easier 'under conditions of relative ignorance' (Keynes). III is false: she wants less emphasis on evidence-based policy but more on an evidence-informed society, not less evidence."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Davoudi's history of evidence in British planning, which statements are true? I: Booth's 1887 survey of London helped trigger the public health and housing acts. II: The 1960s systems view of planning came from cybernetics, via McLoughlin and Chadwick. III: Rational planning sought analytical evidence and hypotheses that could be tested, following Popper. IV: The rational process matched the disjointed, incremental way decisions were actually made.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I, II and III are in Davoudi's account. IV is false: citing Lindblom, she says the ideal rational process hardly matched the 'disjointed, incremental' way decisions were really made."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Davoudi recommends a shift from evidence-based policy to an 'evidence-informed society'. How would a historical institutionalist most plausibly critique this recommendation?","o":["Officials and researchers act on self-interest, so evidence will be used strategically whichever model is preferred","Evidence use is locked into routines set long ago, so a real shift needs a critical juncture, not just a change of mind","Research agendas reflect the interests of capital, so an informed society cannot be neutral while class relations remain","No central body can gather the dispersed knowledge needed, so policy should rely on general rules and market signals"],"a":1,"e":"Historical institutionalists stress path dependence: Davoudi herself shows positivist assumptions persisting from the 1960s into the 2004 soundness test, which suggests change needs a critical juncture. The self-interest critique is rational choice, the capital critique Marxist, the dispersed-knowledge critique Hayekian."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Davoudi explains why evidence is often ignored with Weiss's four I's. How would a Marxist (Marx, Harvey) most plausibly critique this explanation?","o":["It overlooks that internalised norms and scripts shape what officials even recognise as evidence","It overlooks that officials weigh the costs and benefits of gathering information before using it","It lists ideology and interests as rival factors but not whose class interests structure them","It overlooks that policy evolves by trial and error, so no model of research use can be designed"],"a":2,"e":"A Marxist would push the analysis to structure: ideology and interests are not neutral competitors but shaped by class relations and capital, which decide which research is funded and heard. The internalised-norms critique is sociological institutionalism, the cost-benefit critique rational choice, the trial-and-error critique evolutionary (Moroni, Hayek)."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"A hypothetical Dutch province checks municipal housing visions. Its inspectors tick off whether the required studies are attached and the procedure was followed, but never ask whether the chosen growth strategy is better than the alternatives. Which idea from Davoudi fits most precisely?","o":["The political/tactical model: evidence picked to back a policy already chosen","Evidence used in a procedural, quasi-judicial sense, as in the soundness test","The expert-on-top model: research findings directly lead the policy choice","Weiss's 'ideology': values that no amount of data can shift in the short term"],"a":1,"e":"Davoudi argues that early practice turned the 2004 soundness test into a largely procedural, tick-box exercise about the process followed rather than the merits of strategies. Nothing in the case shows evidence being cherry-picked for a prior decision, so the political/tactical model does not fit as precisely."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Davoudi argues that today's instrumental view of evidence-based policy repeats the assumptions of 1960s and 1970s rational planning. Statement II: Both treat policy-making and planning as sciences that use value-free techniques to solve well-defined, neatly structured problems.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are Davoudi's claims, and II states exactly what the resemblance consists of: the shared conception of policy as a value-free science solving well-defined problems is why she says the two 'bear a strong resemblance'."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Davoudi argues that research for policy's sake is selective, so fields not defined as policy problems attract little research. Statement II: Davoudi cites Keynes to show that governments are eager to be fully informed before they decide.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: strategic waste planning is her example of a field neglected because it was not seen as a policy problem. II is false: Keynes said 'there is nothing a government hates more than to be well-informed', because it makes decisions harder."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Davoudi's core argument?","o":["Evidence is assumed to be value-free, so policy is expected to follow research directly, but real policy is shaped by power and values, so she calls for less use of evidence","Evidence is assumed to be value-free, so policy is expected to follow research directly, but real policy is shaped by power and values, so she calls for evidence-informed policy","Evidence is assumed to be biased, so policy is expected to ignore research entirely, but real policy is shaped by power and values, so she calls for evidence-informed policy","Evidence is assumed to be value-free, so policy is expected to follow research directly, but real policy lacks enough data and statistics, so she calls for evidence-informed policy"],"a":1,"e":"The instrumental view rests on positivist assumptions and expects a linear transfer; in reality evidence enters a non-pristine environment of power, values and the four I's, so Davoudi argues for an enlightenment, evidence-informed relationship. She does not call for less evidence, and lack of data is not her diagnosis."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"A city's evidence on short-stay rentals comes mostly from studies funded by hosts' associations, while the neighbours who suffer the nuisance commission none. Combining Olson (Hindmoor) and Davoudi, which explanation fits best?","o":["Neighbours form a privileged group that relies on hosts to pay, and the hosts' studies are enlightenment research","Large groups tend to organise more easily than small ones, and Weiss's 'ideology' decides which studies get read","Both groups organise equally well, and the evidence gap shows the expert-on-top model of research at work","Hosts are a small group with concentrated stakes that organises easily, and its studies serve its interests"],"a":3,"e":"Olson: small groups with concentrated benefits tend to overcome free-riding, while many neighbours with diffuse costs stay latent. Davoudi: evidence produced this way serves interests, the political/tactical use of research. Large groups organising more easily reverses Olson."},{"t":"davoudi","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"In Rydin's governmental model, politicians set the goals and research acts as a 'critical friend' that evaluates the steps, taking goals as given. Which of Davoudi's models is closest, and what is her objection?","o":["Expert on top; she objects that experts in this model have far too little power","Enlightenment; she objects that this model is too slow to help any government","Political/tactical; she endorses it as a realistic account of how policy works","Expert on tap; she objects that a linear view ignores what evidence competes with"],"a":3,"e":"Research that follows goals set by policy is Davoudi's expert 'on tap, but not on top'. Her objection to both instrumental variants is that the linear view ignores ideology, interests, institutions and prior information. She does not endorse the political/tactical model; she criticises it as cherry-picking."},{"t":"scott","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which city does Scott call 'about the closest thing we have to a high-modernist city'?","o":["Chandigarh","Brasília","Canberra","Marseilles"],"a":1,"e":"Scott says Brasília, built more or less along the lines set out by Le Corbusier and CIAM, is about the closest thing we have to a high-modernist city. Chandigarh is Le Corbusier's only built city, but Scott treats it as a second case that repeats Brasília's pattern."},{"t":"scott","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which architect-planner is Scott's main figure for high-modernist urban doctrine in chapter 4?","o":["Le Corbusier","Robert Moses","Ebenezer Howard","Lucio Costa"],"a":0,"e":"Scott uses Le Corbusier (Charles-Edouard Jeanneret) as the embodiment of high-modernist urban design because his views were extreme, consistent and influential. Lucio Costa designed Brasília's plan, but within doctrines that Le Corbusier had shaped."},{"t":"scott","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whose 1961 book provides the street-level critique of high-modernist planning in Scott's chapter?","o":["Lewis Mumford","James Holston","Jane Jacobs","Kevin Lynch"],"a":2,"e":"Scott turns to Jane Jacobs's The Death and Life of Great American Cities (1961), which he calls the most carefully observed critique of high-modernist urbanism. Holston is Scott's source on Brasília, and Mumford only supplies the chapter's epigraph."},{"t":"scott","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What was the first of Le Corbusier's 'principles of urbanism', according to Scott?","o":["The death of the street","The house is a machine","Form follows function","The Plan: Dictator"],"a":3,"e":"Scott writes that the first principle, before even 'the death of the street', was the dictum 'The Plan: Dictator'. The death of the street was the second principle, about separating pedestrians from traffic."},{"t":"scott","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Who designed Brasília, according to Scott?","o":["Le Corbusier and his brother Pierre","Lucio Costa and Oscar Niemeyer","Matthew Nowicki and Albert Mayer","Jane Jacobs and James Holston"],"a":1,"e":"Costa won the 1957 design competition and Niemeyer was chief architect, both working within CIAM and Le Corbusier's doctrines. Nowicki and Mayer drew the original plan for Chandigarh, which Le Corbusier later took over."},{"t":"scott","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"By 1980, roughly what share of Brasília's population lived in settlements the plan had never anticipated?","o":["About 25 percent","About 50 percent","About 90 percent","About 75 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Scott reports that by 1980, 75 percent of Brasília's population lived in unplanned settlements, while the planned city had reached less than half of its projected population. This is the core of his 'unplanned Brasília' argument."},{"t":"scott","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Brasília's first residents coined the mock illness 'brasilite'. What did the term express?","o":["Frustration at long daily commutes from the satellite towns","Pride in being the pioneers who conquered the interior","Rejection of a uniform, anonymous life without any street life","Complaints about the dust and noise of permanent construction"],"a":2,"e":"Brasilite was a mock clinical condition expressing residents' rejection of standardisation and anonymity: daily life without the distractions and small rituals of outdoor life in other Brazilian cities. Long commutes from the periphery are part of the unplanned Brasília story, not what brasilite named."},{"t":"scott","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Le Corbusier claimed his straight lines and right angles had the authority of the machine. Which point does Scott use to undercut this claim?","o":["The machines he admired, such as cars and airplanes, have rounded shapes","Engineers of his day preferred curved roads for faster traffic flows","His own factory designs never used a single straight line or right angle","Most residents of his buildings asked for curved and irregular rooms"],"a":0,"e":"Scott notes that the locomotive, the airplane and the automobile embody rounder or elliptical shapes (the teardrop is most streamlined), so the machine cannot justify the right angle; what remains is an aesthetic ideology. The other options are invented and not in the chapter."},{"t":"scott","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"Jacobs describes a neat lawn in an East Harlem housing project that residents despised. What does the example show?","o":["Public green space in projects was simply too small to be useful","Tidy visual order does not mean the place meets daily needs","Relocated residents preferred private gardens to shared lawns","Projects lacked police, so the lawn was vandalised by outsiders"],"a":1,"e":"The lawn looked orderly, but residents living among strangers could not even get a newspaper or a cup of coffee: visual order did not correspond to a system that meets daily needs. Size, gardens and policing are not the point Jacobs makes with it."},{"t":"scott","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which of these was NOT one of the conditions that made Brasília ideal ground for high modernism, according to Scott?","o":["A state agency owned all the land, so no owners had to be bought out","The city was built on an empty plateau far from existing cities","Residents helped draw the plan through neighbourhood councils","It was a single-function administrative capital, easier to plan"],"a":2,"e":"Scott lists state ownership of all land, an empty site (a 'clean tablecloth') and a single-function administrative capital as ideal conditions. The plan made 'not the slightest concession' to residents' desires; there was no participatory design."},{"t":"scott","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"Jacobs left her apartment key with the local deli owner for visiting friends. What does Scott use this to illustrate?","o":["Informal services that come from being on 'sidewalk terms' with others","The deep friendships that only form in small and stable neighbourhoods","The need for formal neighbourhood watch schemes on busy mixed streets","How private businesses replace public services once the state retreats"],"a":0,"e":"Such services are not the outgrowth of deep friendship but of people being on 'sidewalk terms' with each other, and no public institution could plausibly provide them. Scott stresses that this order has no formal organisations such as a neighbourhood watch."},{"t":"scott","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Why does Scott compare planning a road with planning a home?","o":["To show that roads need more public consultation than homes do","To argue that homes should be planned with the same efficiency tools","To show that roads and homes should always be planned in separate zones","To show narrow efficiency criteria fit a road but do violence to a home"],"a":3,"e":"A road judged only on moving cars from A to B can be compared on efficiency, but a home serves work, sociability, cooking, gossip, politics and more, so single-value criteria 'do considerably greater violence to human practice'. Scott criticises applying road logic to homes, not recommends it."},{"t":"scott","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"In the Plan Voisin, the business elite live in towers at the core and the subaltern classes in garden flats at the edge. What does Scott say this shows?","o":["That Le Corbusier aimed to mix social classes in every district","That one's status can be read from one's distance from the centre","That land prices, not the plan, decided where each class would live","That the elite demanded towers to escape the noise of the workers"],"a":1,"e":"Scott says the place of each individual in the urban hierarchy is spatially coded in the Plan Voisin: status can be read directly from distance to the centre. Land prices are a market explanation; here the plan itself assigns places."},{"t":"scott","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Apart from his aesthetic objections, what second danger did Le Corbusier see in crowded slums, according to Scott?","o":["They pushed land values down across the whole of the city centre","They blocked the circulation of cars between business and housing","They harboured revolt and made police work extremely difficult","They kept rural migrants from returning to their home villages"],"a":2,"e":"Scott writes that the slums 'harbored a potential revolutionary menace' and, as Haussmann knew, obstructed efficient police work, so a legible geometric city would also facilitate control. Traffic mattered to Le Corbusier, but it is not the second danger Scott names for the slums."},{"t":"scott","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Scott's account of Le Corbusier, which statements are true? I: His plans referred to the history and tastes of the city they were drawn for. II: The Plan was to be drawn up away from the electorate and existing regulations. III: He admired the factory as a place where one rational purpose ordered space and people. IV: He treated residents' plural, self-defined needs as the starting point of design.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III F · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":3,"e":"II and III are true: 'The despot is not a man. It is the Plan', drawn up away from the cries of the electorate and ignoring regulations; and the factory was his model of rational order. I is false (his plans 'could be anywhere at all') and IV is false: he stipulated an abstract, schematic subject and never admitted residents might have something valuable to say."},{"t":"scott","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Jacobs, as presented by Scott, which statements are true? I: Complex systems of functional order can look like chaos when seen without understanding. II: Mixed uses create a confusion that single-use zoning rightly resolves. III: Formal institutions of public order work only when backed by a rich informal public life. IV: Busy shops add eyes on the street because shopkeepers have an interest in watching it.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I, III and IV are Jacobs's claims (falling leaves and an airplane engine look chaotic without comprehension; police alone make a dangerous place; shopkeepers watch the street because their business depends on it). II is false: it is the planners' view she attacks, since their aesthetic idea of order drove them to segregate uses."},{"t":"scott","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Scott explains the failures of Brasília mainly through the high-modernist faith of planners and a state that could override residents. How would a Marxist political economist most plausibly critique this explanation?","o":["It forgets that planners and squatters were rational actors, so the result reflects the sum of their own strategic choices","It neglects how class and capital shape cities: the poor periphery reflects property and labour, not only planners' ideas","It forgets that a plan's symbols can still be absorbed into residents' mental maps, so new meanings may take root over time","It neglects that choices made at the founding moment locked the city into a path that later reformers found very hard to reverse"],"a":1,"e":"A Marxist would shift weight from planners' ideology to structure: who owns land and who sells labour explains the class-segregated periphery. The rational-actor critique belongs to rational choice theory, the symbols critique to sociological institutionalism (Dembski and Salet) and the founding-path critique to historical institutionalism."},{"t":"scott","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Scott shows that Le Corbusier assumed citizens would embrace his plan once they understood its scientific logic. How would a sociological institutionalist most plausibly critique this assumption?","o":["Citizens are self-interested, so they will accept the plan only if its benefits exceed their own private costs","No planner can gather the dispersed knowledge of what citizens want, so any total plan will be presumptuous","Acceptance depends on class position and on the property relations that the new plan leaves intact","People act on deeply internalised norms and images of home, so a plan cannot simply re-educate them"],"a":3,"e":"Sociological institutionalists see institutions as internalised in mindsets and habits, so behaviour cannot be redesigned from above; Holston's children who all drew a traditional house fit this. The self-interest critique is rational choice, the dispersed-knowledge critique is Hayekian, the class critique is Marxist."},{"t":"scott","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Scott's verdict on Brasília's success most precisely?","o":["It was a failure by every yardstick, since the planned centre never worked","It was a success, as it became healthy and egalitarian for all its residents","It departed from old Brazilian cities but failed to transform Brazil","It failed only because its builders lacked the money to complete the plan"],"a":2,"e":"Scott judges Brasília by two yardsticks: its success was considerable in how far it departed from older Brazilian cities, but minimal in transforming the rest of Brazil or inspiring love for its way of life. He does not say it failed on every yardstick, nor that money was the cause; the real city was marked by resistance and political calculation."},{"t":"scott","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Scott holds that what seemed rational and legible to Brasília's administrators was experienced as disorder by ordinary residents. Statement II: Brasília's repetitive sectors had few landmarks and were named by acronyms that could be mastered only from the global logic of the centre.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are in the chapter, and II is the reason for I: uniform clusters and acronyms make sense from the centre's overview but leave residents unable to find their way, Holston's 'macro-order and micro-confusion'. Option two would fit only if the disorientation had another cause."},{"t":"scott","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Scott attributes the poor's foothold in Brasília solely to the generosity of Kubitschek and his wife. Statement II: Squatters could mobilise and be heard because a reasonably competitive political system made politicians court them as a voting bloc.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Scott explicitly says the foothold was 'not just a result of the beneficence of Kubitschek and his wife'; political structure played a key role, so I is false. II is his own explanation: competitive politics let squatters protest, and politicians cultivated them as a bloc."},{"t":"scott","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Jacobs's argument, as Scott presents it?","o":["Planners read order from visual regularity, so mixed uses looked like disorder, so they zoned single uses, which removed the steady use that puts eyes on the street","Planners read order from visual regularity, so mixed uses looked like disorder, so they zoned single uses, which raised land prices and pushed out the police","Planners read order from the purpose a thing serves, so mixed uses looked inefficient, so they zoned single uses, which removed the eyes on the street","Planners read order from visual regularity, so mixed uses looked like disorder, so they mixed uses further, which overloaded the street with traffic"],"a":0,"e":"For Jacobs the planners' fundamental error was an aesthetic view of order, which drove the further error of segregating functions; single-use areas lack the continual use that supplies eyes on the street. Reading order from purpose is Jacobs's own position, not the planners', so the third chain has its first link wrong."},{"t":"scott","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Hayek (1944) and Scott both distrust comprehensive central plans. Which statement captures the difference in their reasoning most accurately?","o":["Hayek objects that plans ignore the lived order of streets; Scott that democracy cannot agree on a complete scale of ends","Hayek objects that no democracy can agree on a complete scale of ends; Scott that plans simplify away the lived order of cities","Both object mainly that the state lacks the money and technology to carry out such plans in large and diverse countries","Hayek objects that plans serve capital; Scott that they serve the state, so both explain planning failure by class interests"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek's argument runs through values and democracy: a unitary plan needs agreement on ends that does not exist, which pushes decisions to experts and arbitrary power. Scott's runs through knowledge and legibility: high modernism simplifies cities to a few functions and an abstract citizen. The first option swaps the two."},{"t":"scott","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Cozzolino's anti-adaptive neighbourhoods and Scott's Brasília are both built all at once from a single plan. Which combination of their arguments is accurate?","o":["Scott blames the ownership of land by many small owners; Cozzolino blames a state that held all the land and imposed one design","Both mainly blame a lack of public money, which kept the plans from being completed and maintained in later decades","Scott stresses order imposed from above that ignores practice; Cozzolino adds that single ownership blocks later adaptation","Both conclude that more detailed central plans are needed to correct the mistakes made by the first round of designers"],"a":2,"e":"Scott shows a legible order imposed from above at the cost of street life and residents' practices; Cozzolino adds the institutional mechanism: single ownership and prescriptive rules freeze incremental adaptation. Both reject more central planning as the cure, and the first option reverses who blames what."},{"t":"needham","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to Needham, what does achieving the goals of land-use planning depend on?","o":["Who owns the land, so public ownership is the surest route","How the land in the area is actually used, whoever happens to own it","How many permits the authority issues each year for building activity","How precisely the land-use plan maps every parcel in the area"],"a":1,"e":"Needham writes that achieving planning goals 'depends on how land is used, not on who owns it' (p. 24); that is why planning must influence owners' voluntary decisions. Public ownership is only one tool (taking the decision away), not the condition for success."},{"t":"needham","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"With which image does Needham illustrate that an owner may not use land in a way that greatly harms others?","o":["A dog may not sleep in its own manger if the horse has no other source of food","A neighbour may paint her front door bright pink, whatever the street thinks of it","A factory polluting the air of hundreds of people is restrained through its permit","A farmer renting land cannot be evicted when the owner wants to sell to a developer"],"a":0,"e":"The dog-in-the-manger image (p. 26) shows that ownership is never absolute. The pink door is his example of an interest protected neither way, and the evicted farmer illustrates that courts protect partial rights, not the harm principle."},{"t":"needham","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"What does Needham call 'regulation', the usual way of carrying out land-use planning?","o":["Buying land and developing it through a municipal land development company","Fixing maximum land prices so that owners cannot profit from rezoning","Making development depend on a permit granted only if it conforms to a plan","Creating tradable rights that owners exchange among themselves in a market"],"a":2,"e":"Regulation means building and development need a permission, given only if the initiative conforms to a plan or policy (p. 24). Tradable rights are the alternative he investigates, not regulation."},{"t":"needham","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"In Needham's 'private law' way of protecting an interest, who takes the initiative to enforce it?","o":["The municipality, which inspects sites and fines owners who break the rules","The court itself, which acts on its own initiative once a right exists","A national inspectorate that monitors all property rights on the state's behalf","The harmed private person, who takes the wrongdoer to a civil court"],"a":3,"e":"Under private law the holder protects her right herself: 'the initiative to enforce the restriction is taken by a private person, not by the state' (p. 27). The court is a public institution, but it only adjudicates; enforcement by an authority is the public-law route."},{"t":"needham","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which 'well-known example' does Needham give of property rights used as a policy variable?","o":["Inheritance laws designed to prevent an unhealthy accumulation of wealth","Rent controls that cap what landlords may charge sitting tenants each year","Expropriation of farmland when a municipality needs land for a new road","Tradable emission permits that let factories buy extra rights to pollute"],"a":0,"e":"Needham cites inheritance laws as the familiar case of rights being 'purposefully changed in order to achieve a particular outcome' (p. 28). Tradable permits appear in Hindmoor's Box 5.3, not in Needham's chapter."},{"t":"needham","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which advantage for citizens does Needham expect if planning goals could be reached through markets in property rights?","o":["They would no longer pay local taxes for parks, streets and other public spaces","They would gain a guaranteed vote on every new municipal land-use plan in town","They would be relieved of responsibility for maintaining their surroundings","They could use rights to protect themselves against those with excessive power"],"a":3,"e":"Needham says people would be freer, could use rights 'to protect themselves against those who have excessive power', and would have MORE responsibility for their surroundings (p. 24). The 'relieved of responsibility' option reverses his point."},{"t":"needham","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which interest would Needham's 'technical' criterion most likely assign to private-law protection through a property right?","o":["Thousands of residents' interest in less noise from a nearby busy motorway","A region's interest in keeping its open landscape free of scattered housing","One neighbour's interest that digging next door does not crack her walls","A town's interest in the continued vitality of its central shopping street"],"a":2,"e":"A right suits cases with a clear cause, a clear culprit and few parties, like the 'right of support' for foundations (p. 28). Motorway noise, landscape and shop vitality involve diffuse effects and many people, which Needham assigns to public law."},{"t":"needham","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of Needham's reasons for rejecting 'the public interest' as the justification of planning?","o":["It is exceedingly hard to substantiate that one land use is more in the public interest","Economic arguments about distortions such as external effects no longer convince","It turns the people affected into an amorphous, unidentifiable 'public'","Planning goals such as affordable housing are not legitimate aims for a state"],"a":3,"e":"Needham explicitly does not question 'the legitimacy nor the value of the state trying to influence land use' (p. 23); he questions the methods and the public-interest framing. The other three are his stated objections (p. 25)."},{"t":"needham","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"Why does Needham say that exercising a property right is itself an action 'in the market'?","o":["Because every property right carries a price that is set on the land market","Because property rights set a framework for exchanging interests in land","Because courts that protect rights are themselves private market institutions","Because the state normally sells new property rights at a public auction"],"a":1,"e":"For Needham, 'property rights set a framework for exchanging interests in land' (p. 29), so using them is acting in the market in his broad sense of voluntary exchange. Courts are public institutions in his account, which rules out that distractor."},{"t":"needham","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Using Hodgson's conditions as Needham does, which situation is NOT a market?","o":["Households regularly buying and selling homes from one another in a town","Firms on an estate regularly leasing units from different landlords","Allotment holders regularly renting plots from whichever owner they choose","A municipality compulsorily acquiring a farm to build a new ring road"],"a":3,"e":"A market needs something wanted, voluntary exchange with free choice of partner, and regular exchange among more people (p. 29). Compulsory purchase is not voluntary; Needham notes the decision 'ceases to be free if ownership is taken away'."},{"t":"needham","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Why, according to Needham, might the state have to pay compensation when it protects interests through public law?","o":["Because public law always lowers land values and the state must offset every single loss","Private and public law must be consistent, so 'taking' rights may require compensation","Because courts treat any planning restriction as a criminal act against the owner","Because the European Convention forbids states from restricting the use of land"],"a":1,"e":"Needham says the state must keep the two routes consistent, so 'if the state allows someone's property rights to be taken by public law, it might be necessary to compensate' (p. 28). The ECHR protects 'peaceful enjoyment' and limits changes to rights; it does not forbid planning restrictions."},{"t":"needham","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why, according to Needham, do property rights change only slowly even though they are a policy variable?","o":["Because they are natural rights that no state may alter, as Locke once argued","Because only the European Union has the power to redefine national rights","Because rights carry high ideological content and treaties can restrict change","Because land markets would collapse if any single right were altered at all"],"a":2,"e":"Rights 'often have a high ideological content' and agreements such as the ECHR may restrict changes (p. 28). The Locke option is exactly the view Needham rejects: rights are created by the state."},{"t":"needham","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How does Needham's English industrial-estate solution differ from the US housing-estate covenant?","o":["Industrial estates: one landlord holds the rights for all firms; covenants: each resident's interest becomes a right an association enforces","On industrial estates the municipality enforces the rules; with covenants the national government enforces them on behalf of all residents","On industrial estates firms trade their leases freely; with covenants residents cannot leave the estate until their contract has fully expired","On industrial estates rights are natural; with covenants rights are created by the state, so only the second is an example of planning"],"a":0,"e":"Firms' interests cannot be turned into individual rights, so they 'are taken over by one landlord' who holds the necessary right (p. 32); in housing estates the residents' interest in amenity 'has been transformed into a property right' enforced by an association (p. 31). Both are private-law routes."},{"t":"needham","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"In 2024 the municipality of Ede bought the nitrogen deposition space of a closing livestock farm, retired part of it and used the rest for local projects. Read through Needham, this shows...","o":["that planning has been fully replaced by a free market in which the state plays no further role","a public-law permit acting partly like a tradable right: a mix of planning and the market","the libertarian view that nature protection is unnecessary once rights are clearly defined","that property rights are natural, since the farmer's permit existed before the state recognised it"],"a":1,"e":"Emission space protected through public law becomes partly tradable, with the municipality as buyer and the authority shaping the market (65% retired). That is the public/private mix Needham describes; the permit still transfers only with public approval, so it is not a full free market."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a developer in Amsterdam builds a courtyard block. In variant A the residents own the courtyard, but permit conditions keep it open to everyone and oblige them to maintain it. In variant B the residents close it with gates for their own use. Which reading fits Needham most precisely?","o":["A is a market in rights that replaces planning; B is planning, because a gate needs a permit","A is regulation through a zoning plan; B is a property right, so only B is a market solution","A: rights created to provide a type of urban space; B: his warning about exclusion and fragmentation","A and B are both libertarian attacks on planning, since both rely on private ownership of the space"],"a":2,"e":"Privately owned, publicly accessible spaces under permit conditions are Needham's case of rights 'created deliberately so as to provide a certain type of urban environment' (p. 33); he also warns that rights-based developments 'can be exclusive, such as gated communities' and fragment the city (p. 34). A still combines rights with planning conditions, so it does not replace planning."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Needham's view on 'the public interest' most precisely?","o":["Planning has no legitimate basis at all, because there is no such thing as a public interest","Authorities protect many identifiable private interests more than some general public interest","The public interest is simply whatever outcome the land market produces without interference","Only landowners have interests in land use, so planning should protect owners rather than the public"],"a":1,"e":"Needham writes that the authority acts 'not so much to uphold some general public interest, as to uphold the identifiable interests of many identifiable private persons' (p. 26). The first option overstates (he does not deny legitimacy); the last is wrong because one has interests 'whether or not I own land'."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A policy note argues: 'Instead of asking whether the province or the land market should decide on rural land, we should ask which combination of the two works best for each goal, since owners' voluntary decisions will shape land use anyway.' Which reading grounds this, and why?","o":["Needham: the market cannot be excluded, so what matters is the mix of planning and market","Hayek: central plans fail for lack of knowledge, so the market should simply take over rural land","Rydin's governmental model: market failure justifies a plan drawn up by expert public planners","Marx: the land market expresses class power, so the question of its mix with planning is secondary"],"a":0,"e":"Needham (with Lindblom and Buitelaar) says 'planning or the market?' is not useful because 'the market cannot be excluded'; the only interesting question is the mix (p. 29). Hayek would not ask for a mix per goal but warn against planning displacing the market's coordination."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about Needham's four situations is NOT accurate?","o":["In the US, buyers sign covenants that a residents' association enforces on their behalf","In the Netherlands, industrial estates decay partly because unexpired leases cannot be sold on","Shopping malls are privately owned spaces whose owner may restrict access, e.g. when shops close","For regional coherence, a market in rights is the most promising route because many people gain"],"a":3,"e":"For coherent regional development Needham finds a market in rights much harder: effects are so dispersed over so many people that it 'is not realistic' (p. 34); only private-law tools used by public bodies, like conservation agreements, appear. The other three statements match pp. 31-33."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would most clearly endorse this claim: 'Property rights are neither natural nor inalienable; a way of using land becomes a right because the courts protect it, so different states create different rights'?","o":["Hayek (1944)","Needham (2012)","Taylor (2016)","Hindmoor (2006)"],"a":1,"e":"Needham defines a right as a way of using land 'recognized by, and protected by, the courts', rejects Locke's natural rights and quotes Bromley (p. 27). Taylor notes that perceived property rights gain political traction, but she does not theorise rights as state-created."},{"t":"needham","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Needham argues that US-style covenant arrangements for housing estates are not found in the Netherlands. Statement II: Dutch property law does not allow owners to be obliged to become members of a residents' association.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Needham asks why similar arrangements are absent in the Netherlands and answers that they are not legally binding there because owners cannot be obliged to join (p. 31). So II is his explanation of I; changing Dutch property law would create such a market."},{"t":"needham","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Needham concludes that, wherever they are feasible, markets in property rights are more desirable than regulation. Statement II: Needham suggests rights-based protection could be less inflexible than planning and would avoid public costs of compensating 'takings'.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Needham stresses that 'demonstrating feasibility is not the same as demonstrating desirability' and that the chapter did not compare advantages and disadvantages (p. 34), so I is false. II is true: he lists flexibility and avoided takings costs, alongside exclusion and fragmentation risks."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims match Needham's closing political argument? I. Influencing land use by creating property rights is neither more right-wing nor more left-wing than regulation. II. Because the rich hold most property, the left should avoid rights-based instruments. III. The power of poorer people can be strengthened by giving them rights in land that need not be full ownership.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"Needham calls the rights route politically neither right nor left and supports the 'rather old-fashioned' idea of empowering the poor with rights, quoting Blomley that 'the Left should re-appropriate property rights' (pp. 34-35). II reverses that conclusion."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Which critique of Needham would a communicative planning theorist most plausibly make?","o":["He treats interests in land as given before dialogue, while interests are shaped in deliberation","He ignores that rights evolve along paths set at critical junctures, so they cannot be redesigned at will","He overlooks that property relations reflect class power, so rights for the poor cannot challenge capital","He gives the state too much scope to design what should be a spontaneous order of evolved rules"],"a":0,"e":"Communicative planning sees interests and preferences as formed through interaction; Needham starts from interests people already 'have' and asks how the state protects them. The path-dependence critique belongs to historical institutionalism, the class critique to Marxism, the spontaneous-order critique to Hayek or Moroni."},{"t":"needham","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Needham's route from 'rights are created' to 'markets in property rights'?","o":["Rights are created by the state, so the private/public-law split can be revised; shifting protection to rights creates new exchange structures: markets","Rights are natural and prior to the state, so the private/public-law split is fixed; shifting protection to rights then creates markets automatically","Rights are created by the state, so the private/public-law split can be revised; shifting protection to public law creates new exchange structures: markets","Rights are created by the state, so planners may abolish all rights; once rights are abolished, owners exchange land freely and a market in property rights appears"],"a":0,"e":"Because rights are state-created and never absolute, the allocation 'can always be revised'; shifting protection from public to private law 'is tantamount to creating markets in property rights', since it creates new exchange structures (pp. 28-29). The third chain reverses the direction of the shift; the second contradicts his rejection of Locke."},{"t":"ni","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"According to the lecture, when does Hardin's 'tragedy of the commons' occur?","o":["When a state sells shared land to private owners who then build on all of it","When individuals acting in rational self-interest overuse and deplete a shared resource","When a community's norms about a shared resource erode under outside cultural influence","When users of a shared resource cannot agree on how to vote about its future use"],"a":1,"e":"Hardin (1968): the tragedy occurs when individuals, acting in rational self-interest, overuse and deplete a shared resource such as a pasture, a fish stock or the atmosphere (HC7). Privatisation is one classic answer to the tragedy, not its definition."},{"t":"ni","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whose definition does rational choice institutionalism use: institutions as 'humanly devised constraints that shape human interaction'?","o":["Anthony Giddens (1984)","March and Olsen (2010)","Douglass North (1990)","Hall and Taylor (1996)"],"a":2,"e":"The rational choice slide quotes North (1990): 'the formal and informal rules of the game ... humanly devised constraints that shape human interaction'. Hall and Taylor are cited for the sociological and historical definitions."},{"t":"ni","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"The lecture quotes March and Olsen (2010) on how institutions influence behaviour. What do institutions do, in their words?","o":["They define what is 'appropriate for actors to do within a given setting'","They are 'humanly devised constraints that shape human interaction'","They 'establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences'","They are 'both the context and object of planning practices'"],"a":0,"e":"March and Olsen: institutions define what is 'appropriate for actors to do within a given setting'. The other phrases belong to North, the 1991 sociological quote and Savini (2019)."},{"t":"ni","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which example does the lecture use to illustrate sociological institutionalism?","o":["Holsen (2021) on why participation in Norwegian land-use planning stayed weak","Tennekes, Harbers and Buitelaar (2015) on residential morphology in three regions","Ostrom's fishing cooperative, with its membership rules, quotas and reported catches","Savini and Dembski (2016) on symbols linking Amsterdam-Noord's past and future"],"a":3,"e":"Savini and Dembski (2016) show that a set of symbols linking the shipyard past to a creative-economy future enabled Amsterdam-Noord's new agenda: planning as creating meaning. Holsen is the historical institutionalist example, Ostrom the rational choice one."},{"t":"ni","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"In Ostrom's IAD fishing example, 'fishers must report catches to the cooperative' illustrates which type of rule?","o":["A payoff rule: it assigns rewards or sanctions","A boundary rule: it governs entry into a position","An information rule: it governs what is shared","A choice rule: it states which actions are allowed"],"a":2,"e":"Information rules regulate what information must, may or must not be shared; reporting catches is the slide's example. Payoff rules assign rewards and sanctions (fines for breaking quotas), choice rules what actions are allowed (certain gear)."},{"t":"ni","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What does the lecture call the 'double life' of institutions?","o":["They exist both on paper as formal law and in people's minds as informal norms","They constrain actors, but they are also the outcome of human action","They serve both the planners who design them and the citizens who obey them","They are made at national level but carried out by local organisations"],"a":1,"e":"The double life: institutions constrain actors, but are also the outcome of human action, which is why Savini calls them both the context and the object of planning. The formal/informal distinction is real but is not what 'double life' refers to."},{"t":"ni","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality lets developers build extra floors if at least 30 percent of the new homes are social housing. In Ostrom's IAD terms, this is mainly a…","o":["Boundary rule, because it decides who may enter the position of developer","Payoff rule, because it attaches a reward to a particular action","Aggregation rule, because it combines choices into a collective decision","Scope rule, because it defines which areas are off-limits to building"],"a":1,"e":"Payoff rules assign rewards or sanctions to actions and outcomes; extra floors are a reward for including social housing. It does not regulate who may become a developer (boundary) or how individual choices are combined (aggregation)."},{"t":"ni","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"In the lecture's sense, which of these is an institution rather than an organisation?","o":["Rijkswaterstaat, the national agency that manages the roads and waterways","A housing corporation that owns and lets social rented homes in a city","The unwritten norm that neighbours are consulted before a building plan","The municipal council that adopts the land-use plan for its territory"],"a":2,"e":"Institutions are established rules and norms, formal or informal, that guide and constrain behaviour; organisations are concrete entities with a specific purpose. The agency, the corporation and the council are organisations; the consultation norm is an informal institution."},{"t":"ni","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the things the lecture says characterises new institutionalism?","o":["An explanatory theory of how institutions shape behaviour","An understanding of how institutions arise and transform","A way of reconciling structure and agency (after Giddens)","A claim that markets allocate land better than planning"],"a":3,"e":"The slide lists an explanatory theory of how institutions shape behaviour, an understanding of institutional genesis and transformation, and a way of reconciling structure and agency (Giddens). A pro-market claim is not part of the definition; strands differ widely on markets."},{"t":"ni","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How do rational choice and historical institutionalism, as presented on the map, differ on institutional change?","o":["RC: change comes through new shared symbols; HI: change comes through new incentives","RC: self-interested actors create rules for cooperation; HI: junctures, then paths","RC: institutions cannot be changed on purpose; HI: planners can redesign at will","RC: change comes from slow evolution; HI: change comes from rational redesign"],"a":1,"e":"Rational choice institutionalism: institutions change when rational, self-interested actors create rules to enhance cooperation. Historical institutionalism stresses path dependency and critical junctures. Symbols are sociological and slow evolution is the evolutionary strand."},{"t":"ni","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Which planning strategy would a sociological institutionalist see as the most promising way to change an institution?","o":["Raising fines and bonuses so that actors' payoffs favour the desired behaviour","Waiting for a crisis that opens a short window in which the rules can be reset","Working with symbols and practices so that new meanings become taken for granted","Simplifying general rules and letting new arrangements emerge by trial and error"],"a":2,"e":"Sociological institutionalism sees institutions as symbol systems, cognitive scripts and moral templates; change means new meanings embedded in practice (e.g. Amsterdam-Noord). Payoffs are rational choice, crisis windows historical, and trial and error evolutionary."},{"t":"ni","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"For the homework problem 'Housing is becoming unaffordable', which problem definition and solution fit an evolutionary institutionalist?","o":["Path-dependent rules such as tax relief for owners; use a crisis to reform them","Norms that present homeownership as normal; change the meanings around housing","Weak payoff rules for builders; redesign incentives so building pays off again","Rules that block adaptation; simplify and open general rules step by step"],"a":3,"e":"The evolutionary view sees institutions as evolved orders; the problem is rules that block adaptation, and the remedy modest, gradual simplification rather than grand design (Moroni's kosmos approach). The first two options are the historical and sociological answers."},{"t":"ni","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Sociological institutionalism is said to hold an 'internalised rather than externalised' understanding of institutions. What does this mean?","o":["Institutions are part of how actors think and see, not only rules outside them","Institutions are made inside organisations and are not imposed from the outside","Institutions apply within one country and are not copied from other countries","Institutions are enforced by actors themselves, not by an outside authority"],"a":0,"e":"Internalised means institutions work through actors' cognition and identity (scripts, templates, frames of meaning) rather than as external constraints that rational actors calculate around. Self-enforcement is about who sanctions, which is a different question."},{"t":"ni","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A planner who sees institutions as evolved, largely unintended orders would most likely see her own role as…","o":["A designer who rewrites the rules so that actors' incentives line up with goals","A meaning-maker who seeks niches where new practices can take root and spread","A modest keeper of general rules who intervenes, but not in a taxis-style way","An actor who waits for crises and then uses them to push particular reforms"],"a":2,"e":"'How we conceptualize institutions shapes how we conceptualize planning agency': if institutions are evolved orders, Moroni's point is that intervention is still possible but calls for an approach different from the taxis-oriented one. The niche-seeker is Dembski and Salet, the crisis-user Sorensen."},{"t":"ni","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to rational choice institutionalism as presented in the lecture, which statements are true? I. Institutions are humanly devised constraints that shape human interaction. II. Institutions establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences. III. Institutions coordinate and provide certainty, information and credible commitment. IV. Actors follow rules mainly because they feel appropriate, not because they pay off.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV F"],"a":3,"e":"I (North) and III are the rational choice slide. II is the sociological quote, and IV describes the sociological logic of appropriateness: rational choice actors follow rules because of the incentives and certainty they provide."},{"t":"ni","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to the lecture's account of historical institutionalism, which statements are true? I. Institutions are procedures, routines, norms and conventions embedded in the structure of the polity. II. It places the emphasis on structural elements. III. Path dependency and critical junctures are its key concepts. IV. Most institutions are the unintended outcome of slow evolution, so they cannot be designed like organisations.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":0,"e":"I (Hall and Taylor 1996), II and III are on the historical institutionalism slide. IV is Moroni's evolutionary claim from the exercise: it concerns unintended evolution, not path dependence and junctures."},{"t":"ni","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Rational choice institutionalists explain Ostrom's fishery rules as deliberately crafted by self-interested users to secure credible commitment. How would a sociological institutionalist critique this explanation?","o":["Such rules are the unintended outcome of slow trial and error over generations, so presenting them as deliberate crafting is a misreading","Such rules persist because they were set at a founding moment and then reproduced along a path, so present calculation explains little","Such rules reflect the power of boat owners over hired crews, so the language of mutual commitment hides an unequal class relation","Such rules also shape what fishers want and see as proper, so compliance rests on legitimacy and shared meaning, not on calculation"],"a":3,"e":"Sociological institutionalism rejects given preferences: institutions 'establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences', and rules hold because they are legitimate and internalised. The other critiques are evolutionary (Moroni), historical institutionalist and Marxist."},{"t":"ni","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Dembski and Salet argue that planners can help institute new meaning through symbolic markers in under-institutionalised niches. How would a rational choice institutionalist critique this?","o":["Niches are rare outside crises; most of the time change is confined to a pathway set by earlier choices","Symbols matter only if they change payoffs and information; lasting change needs rules with incentives","Even niche meanings cannot be steered, because institutions emerge unintentionally from countless acts","Symbolic markers serve to sell places to mobile capital, hiding the interests that drive redevelopment"],"a":1,"e":"For rational choice institutionalism institutions are rules that coordinate behaviour by providing certainty, information and credible commitment; symbols are only relevant if they alter incentives. The other options are historical institutionalist, evolutionary and Marxist (Harvey-style) critiques."},{"t":"ni","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a tax relief for homeowners has survived decades of criticism, while more households planned their lives around it. After a deep financial crisis, a new coalition phases it out within two years. Which concept fits this turn most precisely?","o":["Goodness-of-fit: an arrangement that is reproduced because it matches legitimate templates","A critical juncture: a short period in which actors' choices weigh unusually heavily","A payoff rule: a reward attached to an action, adjusted to change the incentives","Unintended evolution: a rule that slowly adapts through trial and error over time"],"a":1,"e":"A crisis that suddenly opens room for reform of a long-reproduced rule is a critical juncture: 'relatively short periods of time during which there is a substantially heightened probability that agents' choices will affect the outcome' (Sorensen on the slide). Goodness-of-fit explains the earlier persistence, not the sudden turn."},{"t":"ni","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A planning report argues: 'Homeownership has come to be seen as the normal, responsible way to live. Policies that treat renting as equal struggle, not because they are costly, but because they seem out of place.' Which strand grounds this, and why?","o":["Rational choice: households weigh costs and benefits, and owning simply pays off better","Historical: policies follow a path set long ago, which only a critical juncture can change","Sociological: norms define what is appropriate and normal, so rivals seem illegitimate","Evolutionary: tenure forms evolved unintentionally, so no policy should try to change them"],"a":2,"e":"The passage locates the obstacle in taken-for-granted meanings and appropriateness ('normal', 'out of place'), the hegemonic character of norms that sociological institutionalism stresses; the lecture's homework example names norms of homeownership as 'normal'. It explicitly rejects a cost explanation, which rules out rational choice."},{"t":"ni","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which statements fit sociological institutionalism as presented on the map? I. Institutions are symbol systems, cognitive scripts and moral templates that provide frames of meaning. II. Arrangements are reproduced because they fit established templates and are seen as legitimate. III. Institutions change when self-interested actors redesign rules to enhance cooperation.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I (Hall and Taylor 1996) and II (goodness-of-fit, DiMaggio and Powell) are on the sociological slide. III is the rational choice account of change."},{"t":"ni","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: In new institutionalism, institutions are not the same as organisations. Statement II: Institutions are systems of established rules and norms, while organisations are concrete entities with a specific purpose.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are take-home messages, and II gives the reason for I: the distinction rests on rules and norms versus purposive entities (the EBU versus the rules of the Eurovision contest)."},{"t":"ni","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Evolutionary institutionalism holds that planners cannot intervene in institutions at all. Statement II: Moroni argues that complex, largely unintentional institutions cannot be designed in the same way as organisations.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"II is the quote the lecturer selected (p. 282). I is false: Moroni adds that acknowledging the evolutionary nature of institutions 'does not prevent us from intervening upon them', but calls for an approach different from the taxis-oriented one (p. 287)."},{"t":"ni","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain best captures the historical institutionalist reading of the lecture's chart on the US mortgage interest deduction?","o":["The rule makes owning and borrowing attractive, so more households build their plans on it, so reform gets costlier and the rule persists","The rule makes owning and borrowing attractive, so more households build their plans on it, so reform gets easier and the rule soon comes to an end","The rule makes renting more attractive than owning, so more households rent, so landlords lobby for it and the rule persists","The rule makes owning and borrowing attractive, so more households build their plans on it, so the cost falls and the rule persists"],"a":0,"e":"Historical institutionalism explains persistence through path dependence: an established rule creates beneficiaries and expectations, raising the cost of change, so it keeps reproducing itself (the cost grew from about $60bn to $150bn a year). The other chains break a link: reform gets harder, not easier; the rule favours owning; and its cost rises."},{"t":"ni","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Placing Taylor (2016) on the lecture's map: which reading of Melbourne's growth boundary and the GAIC fits the strand the lecture assigns her to?","o":["Owners re-enact taken-for-granted meanings of rural land, which the charge offended and the redesign restored","The boundary and charge create rents and incentives, and self-interested owners lobby to change the rules","The charge followed a long path in Victoria's land policy, which only a crisis could have bent in a new direction","The boundary is an evolved order of property rights, and the charge was a taxis-style intervention that failed"],"a":1,"e":"The lecture's table lists Needham and Taylor under rational choice: institutions are rules of the game that create value and incentives, and self-interested actors use political pressure to change them. The other readings place her in the sociological, historical and evolutionary strands."},{"t":"ni","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combining the lecture's Hardin and Ostrom with Hindmoor's Olson: which statement is faithful to all three?","o":["Hardin and Olson both show that rational individuals always cooperate once they see the shared gain; Ostrom confirms it","Hardin and Olson show that groups solve shared problems through cultural norms; Ostrom shows that only the state can","Hardin and Olson show that markets solve shared problems; Ostrom shows that self-organised rules usually fail in practice","Hardin and Olson show individually rational choices can produce collective harm; Ostrom shows users can craft rules"],"a":3,"e":"The tragedy of the commons and the logic of collective action share one puzzle: individual rationality producing collective irrationality (overuse; under-provision through free-riding). Ostrom asks how self-interested people self-organise rules that overcome the 'problem of commitment'. The other options misstate at least one author."},{"t":"olson","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"In Box 5.1, why is the beam of a lighthouse the standard example of a non-excludable good?","o":["Because any passing ship can benefit from it, whether or not it has paid","Because one ship's use of the light leaves less light for the next ship","Because the owner can easily switch the light off for ships that do not pay","Because only ships registered in the owner's country may legally use it"],"a":0,"e":"A good is non-excludable if its benefits are available to all; 'any passing ship can potentially benefit' from the beam (Box 5.1). The second option describes rivalness, the other dimension of the classification."},{"t":"olson","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"In game theory as used by Hindmoor, what is a 'dominant' strategy?","o":["The strategy that gives the highest joint pay-off to all players taken together","The strategy a player chooses after first seeing what the other player has done","The strategy that is best for a player no matter what the other player does","The strategy that the strongest player can force on all the other players"],"a":2,"e":"A dominant strategy is rational 'no matter what their opponent does' (Box 5.2); in the prisoners' dilemma that is confessing. The highest joint pay-off is mutual cooperation, which is exactly what a dominant strategy of defection fails to reach."},{"t":"olson","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which example does Hindmoor use for hegemonic stability theory, an application of the privileged group?","o":["European countries jointly cutting sulphur dioxide emissions to stop acid rain","Britain and later the US bearing the costs of an open trading order and reserve currency","Democracies that, according to the evidence, have never once gone to war with one another","Mafia families in Southern Italy enforcing honest exchange in return for a cut"],"a":1,"e":"A hegemon gains so much from a liberal international order that it bears the costs of maintaining it (pp. 115–116), like the one member of a privileged group who pays for the whole good. The Mafia example illustrates coercion by non-state actors."},{"t":"olson","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"In the N-person prisoners' dilemma about a street protest, what does it mean that everyone cooperating is 'Pareto-superior' to everyone defecting?","o":["Everyone is better off if all join the protest than if all stay at home","Most people, but not all of them, gain when everyone joins the protest","Joining the protest is the best choice for each person whatever others do","The total number of protesters is larger than the number staying at home"],"a":0,"e":"Pareto-superior means better for everyone (3,3 > 2,2 in Figure 5.2). It does not mean cooperation is individually best: staying at home remains the dominant strategy, which is the dilemma."},{"t":"olson","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which example of a 'focal point' does Hindmoor give in Box 5.2?","o":["Driving on the same side of the road as every other motorist in the whole country","A cop telling two suspects that he will put exactly the same deal to each","Two farmers agreeing to take turns repairing the dam in alternate years","Meeting a friend at Grand Central Station because you always met there before"],"a":3,"e":"Without communication, a past meeting place becomes an obvious 'focal point' around which to coordinate (Schelling, Box 5.2). The road example in the same box illustrates interdependent choice in general, not a focal point."},{"t":"olson","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to Hindmoor, who first offered a general analysis of why and when collective action problems arise?","o":["Thomas Hobbes, in his case for a leviathan","Garrett Hardin, in his tragedy of the commons essay","Mancur Olson, in The Logic of Collective Action","Elinor Ostrom, in Governing the Commons"],"a":2,"e":"Hobbes, Hume and Rousseau gave specific examples, but 'Olson was the first person to offer a general analysis' (p. 127). Hardin's analysis, Hindmoor notes, 'strangely, contains no references to Olson's work'."},{"t":"olson","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"In a street of 25 households, a shared rota for the communal garden is posted on the fence, so everyone can see who did their turn. In Olson's terms this group is best described as...","o":["privileged, because at least one household values the garden enough to tend it alone","latent, because nobody will bear their share and the garden will go unmaintained","intermediate, because members can notice whether any other member is helping or not","large, because any group of more than twenty members is by definition latent"],"a":2,"e":"An intermediate group is one whose members can 'notice whether any other member is or is not helping' (Olson 1965: 50, p. 118), which makes conditional cooperation possible. Size does not define the type; Hindmoor calls the size-latency link 'at most, an empirical tendency'."},{"t":"olson","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the standard game-theory assumptions Hindmoor lists in Box 5.2?","o":["Actors are instrumentally rational in pursuing their goals","There is common knowledge of rationality among all players","Actors know the rules and the pay-offs","Players can sign agreements a third party enforces"],"a":3,"e":"Box 5.2 lists instrumental rationality, common knowledge of rationality, choice between strategies, known rules and pay-offs, and ordinal pay-offs. Enforceable agreements are absent, which is why a one-shot prisoners' dilemma ends in mutual defection."},{"t":"olson","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which good is a common-pool good in the classification of Box 5.1?","o":["A groundwater aquifer that many farmers pump from without any meters","A television signal that anyone with an aerial can receive at full quality","A toll tunnel that only drivers who pay can enter, however busy it is","A sandwich bought at a station kiosk and eaten by the person who paid"],"a":0,"e":"Common-pool goods are non-excludable but rivalrous, like fishing grounds: water one farmer pumps is not available to others, and stopping pumping is hard. The TV signal is a public good, the tunnel a toll good, the sandwich a private good."},{"t":"olson","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"In Box 5.4, why might a consumer buy from a second-hand car dealer after all, although a one-shot game predicts no sale?","o":["Because the state forces every dealer to give buyers a full money-back guarantee","Because the dealer has a reputation for good cars that he risks losing with a lemon","Because consumers enjoy the bargaining process itself, which counts as a process benefit","Because the consumer is a privileged member who values the car above its cost"],"a":1,"e":"Without reputation the consumer expects a 'lemon' and does not buy, a Pareto-inefficient outcome; a dealer with a good name can be trusted because selling a lemon would cost him that reputation (Box 5.4). Coercion by the state is a different route Hindmoor discusses elsewhere."},{"t":"olson","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"According to Hindmoor, what may happen when the chicken game about repairing the dam is played year after year?","o":["The game turns into an assurance game, because both farmers now need each other","Both farmers defect every single year, since defection is the dominant strategy in chicken","A norm of sharing or turn-taking may emerge, or one farmer ends up doing all the work","The state must step in, since repeated chicken games can never produce cooperation"],"a":2,"e":"In iterated chicken the farmers may develop 'a norm of either sharing or taking it in turns', but the farmer who works first may also be trapped into doing it every year (p. 121). Chicken has no dominant strategy, which rules out the second option."},{"t":"olson","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Why does Hindmoor mention traditional Marxist expectations of revolution in his list of collective action problems?","o":["To show that workers never share interests, so a revolution could not benefit them at all","To show that even if workers gain from revolution, no single worker gains from risking the barricades","To show that revolutions happen automatically once objective economic conditions are right","To show that revolutions are privileged groups, because the leaders pay for the whole revolt"],"a":1,"e":"Marxists assume revolution will 'somehow simply happen' when conditions are right, but 'why would any one person risk their neck on the barricades when they could stay at home and free ride?' (p. 104). The third option is the Marxist assumption Hindmoor questions."},{"t":"olson","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"In Box 5.2, when do game theorists speak of a 'Nash equilibrium'?","o":["When each player picks its best strategy, given that the other also plays its best strategy","When both players reach the outcome that maximises the sum of their two pay-offs taken together","When a third party enforces an agreement that both players signed before the game","When one player has a dominant strategy and the other has to accept the result"],"a":0,"e":"Where there is no dominant strategy, rational actors 'choose the best possible strategy on the assumption that their opponent will play their best possible strategy'; the resulting outcome is a Nash equilibrium (Box 5.2). It need not maximise joint pay-offs, as the prisoners' dilemma shows."},{"t":"olson","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which example does NOT illustrate a privileged group in Olson's sense?","o":["A flatmate who hates squalor so much that she cleans the flat alone","A hegemonic power that bears the cost of an open trading order","A landowner who pays for the whole dike repair because he gains most","A worker who joins the union to receive free legal representation"],"a":3,"e":"Free legal aid is a positive selective incentive: an excludable reward for contributors (p. 116). In a privileged group at least one member values the good so much that she pays the entire cost and tolerates others' free-riding, as in the other three."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: at an allotment complex with 40 plots, members clean the shared tool shed in turns. A board shows who did their turn; members keep doing it as long as others do, and nobody fines or shuns anyone. Which concept from Hindmoor fits most precisely?","o":["A negative selective incentive: members only clean because they fear being ostracised","A privileged group: one member values the shed enough to bear the whole cost","Conditional cooperation: visible contributions let people cooperate while others do","Coercion: the complex works as a leviathan that forces members to do their share"],"a":2,"e":"Members can see who contributes and cooperate 'so long as everyone else ... cooperates as well' (p. 118): conditional cooperation in an intermediate group, made easier by cheap monitoring. With no sanctions applied, the selective-incentive reading does not fit."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase of Hindmoor's second limitation of Olson is most precise?","o":["Olson claimed a full theory of collective action, but his theory turned out to be wrong","Rational choice theorists cannot study group identity, so only psychologists should do so","Olson offers no full theory of collective action and never claimed one; others can help fill it","Olson's theory explains how groups form identities, but not how groups win policy changes"],"a":2,"e":"Olson's book 'does not (and does not claim to) provide a comprehensive theory', for example of how people come to identify with a group; rational choice theorists can address these topics, but 'it would be a mistake to think that only rational choice theorists can do so' (p. 128)."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A report on a business estate argues: 'Each of the three large firms could easily fund the new cycle bridge alone, and each would rather have it than not, yet each waits for another to pay, so the bridge may never be built.' Which idea from Hindmoor grounds this, and why?","o":["A privileged group with one keen member: the bridge is certain to be built by the keenest firm","Several members able to pay alone: the situation resembles chicken and the good may not appear","A prisoners' dilemma: each firm's dominant strategy is to block the bridge whatever others do","Process benefits: the firms enjoy negotiating so much that the outcome no longer matters"],"a":1,"e":"If several members are each prepared to bear the whole cost, Olson recognises the good 'may not be provided', because 'each individual might try to free-ride in the expectation that one of the others will eventually provide all of the good': a game of chicken (p. 116). With only one such member the group would be safely privileged."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about the games in Hindmoor's chapter is NOT accurate?","o":["In the prisoners' dilemma, confessing (defection) is the dominant strategy for both","In the assurance game, defection is the dominant strategy, so cooperation is rare","In chicken, there is no dominant strategy and in a single play anything can happen","Olson himself used no game theory; Hindmoor uses it to clarify Olson's argument"],"a":1,"e":"In the assurance game there is no dominant strategy and, given common knowledge of rationality, 'collective action should be easily achieved' (p. 111). The other three statements are accurate (Box 5.2, pp. 105–112)."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would most clearly endorse this claim: 'Many people contribute because a norm says they ought to, which a self-interest model cannot explain; yet a few such unconditional cooperators may be what gets collective action past its threshold'?","o":["Taylor (2016)","Needham (2012)","Hindmoor (2006)","Moroni (2010)"],"a":2,"e":"Hindmoor names the self-interest assumption as a limit of Olson and argues, with Figure 5.5, that 'a few unconditional cooperators might make a significant difference' in reaching the threshold (pp. 122–127). Taylor and Needham work with self-interest and rights, and Moroni with evolved institutions."},{"t":"olson","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Hindmoor presents Hardin's tragedy of the commons as building explicitly on Olson's analysis. Statement II: The resources in Hardin's analysis, such as common pasture, are rivalrous but non-excludable.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Hindmoor notes that Hardin's argument 'strangely, contains no references to Olson's work' (Box 5.3), so I is false. II is true: common land is rivalrous (grass eaten by one herd is gone) and non-excludable (no farmer can stop another grazing)."},{"t":"olson","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: According to Hindmoor, selective incentives cannot by themselves explain every instance of collective action. Statement II: Applying informal sanctions such as ostracism is itself a collective good, which creates a second-order collective action problem.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Selective incentives presuppose that someone already overcame a collective action problem, and applying them is itself a collective good that threatens 'an infinite regress' (pp. 116–117). That second-order problem is precisely why selective incentives alone cannot explain all collective action."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about entrepreneurs match Hindmoor's account? I. Entrepreneurs can provide selective incentives, such as promised rewards for participants and enmity for free-riders. II. They may themselves be motivated by, for example, the prospect of an attractive job in a future regime. III. Entrepreneurship necessarily traps a group in an infinite regress of sanctions.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"Entrepreneurs such as Trotsky or Martin Luther King can offer rewards and threats, motivated perhaps by a future job, and so solve the second-order problem 'without the threat of an infinite regress' (p. 117). III reverses that point."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Which critique of Olson's logic of collective action would a Marxist political economist most plausibly make?","o":["It stresses incentives and group size, but who can organise reflects class position and resources","It ignores that people contribute because norms and identities tell them what is appropriate","It treats interests as fixed, while groups discover shared interests by deliberating together","It ignores that rules for the commons evolve slowly and unintentionally, far beyond anyone's design"],"a":0,"e":"A Marxist reading would link the capacity to organise to structural position: owners of capital have resources and stakes that workers or tenants lack, which group size and incentives do not capture. Norms and appropriateness are sociological institutionalism, deliberation is communicative planning, and evolved rules are Moroni."},{"t":"olson","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly explains why kibbutz members work hard, according to Hindmoor?","o":["Everyone is paid regardless of work, so shirking pays; shirkers face gossip, which members enjoy, so there is no second-order problem; leaving is hard, so the sanction bites","Everyone is paid according to work, so shirking does not pay; shirkers face gossip, which members enjoy, so there is no second-order problem; leaving is hard, so the sanction bites","Everyone is paid regardless of work, so shirking pays; shirkers face gossip, which members dislike, so a second-order problem remains; leaving is easy, so the sanction bites","Everyone is paid regardless of work, so shirking pays; shirkers face fines from the state, which members fear, so there is no second-order problem; leaving is hard"],"a":0,"e":"Kibbutzim pay members regardless of effort, creating an incentive to shirk; shirkers become targets of gossip, and 'gossiping is an enjoyable activity', so no second-order problem arises; and because it is very difficult to leave, the sanction works (pp. 118, 120). Each other chain breaks one link."},{"t":"taylor","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What material does Taylor (2016) analyse to show rent-seeking by Melbourne's fringe landowners?","o":["Interviews with forty developers and state planners about the 2010 boundary shift","Records of every land sale on the urban fringe between 2002 and the 2010 expansion","A sample of 264 public submissions to the inquiry on the boundary and the GAIC","Transcripts of the state parliament's debates on the first version of the GAIC bill"],"a":2,"e":"Taylor critically interprets 264 public submissions (218 individuals, 46 organisations) to the 2010 inquiry on the UGB expansion and the GAIC (p. 265). Price data and other studies are cited as background, but her own evidence is what submitters claimed."},{"t":"taylor","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"What was 'Taxed Out!' in Taylor's Melbourne case?","o":["A landowner group that campaigned against the GAIC throughout 2009 and early 2010","The state government's slogan for the new charge on value from boundary expansion","A developers' lobby that asked for the charge to be paid at the point of development","An environmental coalition that objected to the expansion near the western grasslands"],"a":0,"e":"Taxed Out! was a landowner group whose campaign cast the GAIC as a tax on existing land value and wanted it applied only at subdivision or development (pp. 262, 270, 272). Developers were not the drivers: Taylor finds opposition was led by landowners."},{"t":"taylor","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"Taylor stresses the disputed 'incidence' of a betterment charge. What does incidence mean here?","o":["How often the charge is levied over the lifetime of a single parcel of rezoned land","Who ends up actually bearing the cost of the charge, whoever formally has to pay it","The share of the land value increase that the state takes through the charge","The moment at which the charge becomes payable, at sale or at subdivision"],"a":1,"e":"Incidence is who really bears a tax; research suggests landowners get slightly lower prices but homebuyers bear most of the cost (p. 264). That is why Taylor focuses on what landowners claimed about who would pay. The timing of payment is a design feature, not incidence."},{"t":"taylor","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"How, according to Taylor, had development gains in Victoria been treated before the GAIC?","o":["They were taxed at a fixed share of every sale since the first metropolitan plan","They were captured through compulsory purchase of fringe land by the state","They were never directly taxed; the 1971 plan discussed and rejected such a tax","They were taxed only when farmland was sold to developers with a building permit"],"a":2,"e":"Taylor notes that development gains in Victoria had never been directly taxed, and that the 1971 metropolitan plan had discussed and rejected a betterment tax (p. 262). That history makes the GAIC a new and politically exposed instrument."},{"t":"taylor","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Who, in a study Taylor cites, said that fringe owners 'believe their land is liquid gold'?","o":["A state minister defending the new infrastructure charge in parliament","A lifestyle landowner explaining in a submission why she wanted to stay rural","A spokesperson for Taxed Out! complaining about speculation on the fringe","A developer describing farmers and owners whose land was inside the boundary"],"a":3,"e":"A developer interviewed in another study said 'People that are inside the UGB, the farmers and the owners, believe their land is liquid gold' (p. 261). Planners and developers agreed land inside the boundary had become much more expensive, though they blamed each other."},{"t":"taylor","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why, following Evans' 'insider-outsider' problem, does Taylor expect planning participation to favour existing owners?","o":["Future residents who will buy the new homes are not yet there and have no voice","Existing owners are legally entitled to a veto over any change to their zoning","The inquiry only accepted submissions from owners of land inside the new boundary","Developers paid for existing owners' submissions to secure the boundary expansion"],"a":0,"e":"Evans' insider-outsider problem: participation is biased towards existing property owners, while future residents have no voice (p. 260). No veto or restricted access is involved; the bias comes from who is present to take part."},{"t":"taylor","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A hobby farmer sincerely writes that the boundary would destroy the birdlife and quiet she loves; her argument also protects her land's value. Which of Clingermayer's routes does Taylor call this?","o":["Heresthetics: the intentional manipulation of an issue in order to win a political contest","Happenstance: altruistic goals that unintentionally line up with rent-seeking ones","Premium-seeking: lobbying to be included and harvest the expected betterment","Dual predictability: certainty about where development may and may not happen"],"a":1,"e":"Happenstance is when altruistic goals unintentionally align with rent-seeking ones (p. 260); Taylor stresses such owners may argue in good faith. Heresthetics would require intentional manipulation, which the sincerity in the case rules out."},{"t":"taylor","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which claim does Taylor NOT make in her article?","o":["Landowners managed to discredit the idea that the boundary shift created windfalls","Opposition to the GAIC was driven by landowners rather than by housing developers","The redesign moved formal liability for the charge in most cases to purchasers","Her data show that homebuyers in fact bore the full cost of the redesigned charge"],"a":3,"e":"Taylor analyses claims in submissions; she does not measure who bore the GAIC and calls the 'purchaser pays' incidence an assumption (p. 272). The other three are her findings on discrediting betterment, the role of landowners and the shift of formal liability (pp. 271-273)."},{"t":"taylor","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How did objections from fringe landowners differ from those of non-landowners in Taylor's sample?","o":["Landowners stressed urban consolidation; others stressed their own rural lifestyle","Landowners cited local amenity and rural lifestyle; others cited consolidation and nature","Landowners cited property law and a right to compensation; others cited land values and tax","Landowners objected to nothing but the charge; others objected to the expansion itself"],"a":1,"e":"Non-landowners, often inner-city residents and environmental groups, referred to urban consolidation and valued areas such as Merri Creek; objecting landowners talked about local amenity and their rural lifestyle, fitting Hubbard's self/other contrast (p. 266). The first option reverses the two groups."},{"t":"taylor","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Taylor calls impact fees and inclusionary zoning 'related forms' of what?","o":["Growth containment, since both limit how much land may be developed","Rent-seeking, since both let developers secure income they did not earn","Land value taxation: capturing part of value created by public action","Insider protection, since both shield existing owners from new arrivals"],"a":2,"e":"Taylor lists impact fees and inclusionary zoning as related forms of land value taxation (p. 263): ways of capturing part of the unearned gain that arises from community or government action. They are instruments of capture, not of containment or rent-seeking."},{"t":"taylor","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"What role does the later sale of a 64-hectare fringe farm for 36 million dollars play in Taylor's argument?","o":["It proves that the GAIC was set far too low to capture a fair share of the gain","It shows that developers, not landowners, finally captured most of the betterment","It is evidence that the boundary had raised land prices right across the metropolitan area","It contrasts with owners' claims that inclusion in the boundary would not raise values"],"a":3,"e":"Taylor sets the press reports of huge sales (a 64-hectare farm for $36 million) against landowners' successful claims that no windfall would result (p. 273). She does not use them to recalculate the right charge or to argue that developers captured the gain."},{"t":"taylor","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Taylor links landowners' reactions to the power of 'perceived property rights'. What does she say about them?","o":["They gain political traction even when they contradict the legal basis of property","They were rejected by the inquiry because they had no basis in the planning law","They are the only rights that the planning system of Victoria formally recognises","They mattered mainly to developers who had bought options on land outside the line"],"a":0,"e":"Perceived property rights can gain political traction even when they contradict the legal basis of property (p. 270); together with aversion to taxing owner-occupation, this helped landowners' framing succeed. The inquiry did not reject them: policy moved in their direction."},{"t":"taylor","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which feature of the GAIC redesign best illustrates what Taylor means by costs being shifted to 'outsiders'?","o":["Exempting sales of large broadacre farms from the charge altogether","Raising the per-hectare rate to fund more infrastructure in growth areas","Making purchasers liable for the charge rather than the current owners","Letting the opposition block the first version of the bill in parliament"],"a":2,"e":"Moving liability to purchasers (30 percent at purchase, the rest at development) means the cost is likely passed on to future homebuyers, the outsiders (pp. 272-274). The exemptions concerned small parcels (0.41 to 5 hectares), not large farms, and the rate was not raised."},{"t":"taylor","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Taylor writes that 70 percent of the sampled submissions came from directly affected fringe landowners. How does she interpret this?","o":["As a sign that the inquiry was badly advertised to the wider Melbourne public","As what a rent-seeking perspective would predict: those with a stake take part","As proof that most fringe owners supported the expansion and wanted inclusion","As evidence that environmental groups had stayed out of the planning debate altogether"],"a":1,"e":"Taylor notes that, 'as a rent-seeking perspective would predict', 186 of 264 submissions (70 percent) came from directly affected fringe landowners (p. 265). Many of them objected, so the share does not show support; environmental groups did take part."},{"t":"taylor","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Taylor (2016), which statements are true? I. Opposition to the GAIC was led by housing developers. II. Owners who mentioned plans to develop rarely objected to the GAIC. III. Objectors mostly treated the GAIC as a tax on existing land value, not on a future windfall. IV. Landowner pressure led to the GAIC being abolished.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":1,"e":"II and III are true: only 4 of 49 owners with development plans objected (p. 271), and objectors treated the charge as a tax on existing value (p. 269). I is false (opposition was driven by landowners, not developers) and IV is false: the GAIC survived in modified form, which Taylor reads as a limit on stakeholder influence (p. 273)."},{"t":"taylor","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Taylor's rent-seeking analysis, which statements are true? I. A binding boundary splits land prices at the line, and the gap is betterment. II. Homevoters and premium-seekers take opposite stances on growth controls, yet both follow property interests. III. Landowners who invoked rural amenity were insincere. IV. Policies survive or fail depending on conflict with groups that have an existing economic stake.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I, II and IV restate Figure 1 (p. 263), the two kinds of rent-seeking (p. 264) and her broader lesson (p. 274). III is false: Taylor stresses that owners may argue in good faith, but their altruistic arguments unavoidably align with their property interests (p. 266)."},{"t":"taylor","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Taylor explains the watering down of the GAIC as rent-seeking by self-interested landowners. How would a sociological institutionalist critique this explanation?","o":["Owners' claims reflect internalised meanings of property and home, not only calculation, so reading them as strategy treats preferences as given","The episode is one moment in a long pathway since the 1971 rejection of a betterment tax, so real change could only come at a critical juncture","Secure property rights are an evolved institution that stores dispersed knowledge, so a new charge on land is itself the real problem here","Lobbying is only a surface effect: the deeper cause is the contradiction between socially produced land value and its private appropriation"],"a":0,"e":"Sociological institutionalism holds that institutions shape the very criteria by which people discover their preferences, so owners' sense that the charge was 'robbery' expresses taken-for-granted meanings of ownership, not just strategy. The pathway critique is historical institutionalist, the evolved-rights critique evolutionary (Moroni), and the contradiction critique Marxist."},{"t":"taylor","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"How would a Marxist reading, such as Foglesong's property contradiction, most plausibly critique Taylor's account of the GAIC fight?","o":["It overlooks that owners' arguments are shaped by cognitive scripts about home and land that the planners themselves also share","It focuses on one moment of lobbying and misses the long, path-dependent route by which value capture develops over decades","It explains the outcome by interest groups and leaves the structural tension between social and private land value unexplained","It treats a deliberately designed charge as legitimate, while lasting rules can only evolve gradually through trial and error"],"a":2,"e":"Foglesong's property contradiction sees a structural tension between the social character of land (its value produced by public action and growth) and its private ownership; a Marxist would say Taylor's actor-level rent-seeking story describes symptoms of that tension. The other options are sociological, historical and evolutionary institutionalist critiques."},{"t":"taylor","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch landowners' association, knowing most affected parcels are farmland awaiting rezoning, deliberately recasts a new betterment levy as 'a tax on pensioners' homes'. In Taylor's terms, the association is using…","o":["Happenstance: altruistic concerns that just happen to align with members' property interests","Dual predictability: certainty about which land may be developed, which fuels speculation","Heresthetics: the intentional manipulation of an issue to secure or protect unearned rents","Homevoting: owner-occupiers defending growth restrictions to protect their home values"],"a":2,"e":"The deliberate reframing of an issue is Clingermayer's heresthetics (p. 260). Happenstance is the tempting distractor, but it describes unintended alignment of sincere altruistic goals with rent-seeking; here the framing is knowingly chosen."},{"t":"taylor","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A planning report argues: 'The evenings on the new urban extension were full of nearby owners, who secured lower densities and a later start. The young families who will one day buy there, and pay through the price, were absent.' Which reading grounds this, and why?","o":["Olson: large latent groups fail to organise unless they offer selective incentives to members","Needham: interests should be protected through rights, because 'the public interest' hides people","Davoudi: evidence is used selectively, so the claims made at the evenings need critical checking","Taylor: participation favours insiders, while outsiders who later bear the costs have no voice"],"a":3,"e":"Present owners shaping the plan while future buyers, who bear costs through prices, are absent is Taylor's insider-outsider problem (via Evans, p. 260). Olson is tempting, but his argument concerns a large group's failure to organise, not a group that does not yet exist."},{"t":"taylor","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which of these support Taylor's reading of the GAIC episode as rent-seeking rather than mere misunderstanding? I. Opposition came mainly from owners without development plans, who would face the charge on a sale. II. Owners' denials that inclusion raised values clashed with soon-reported sales at huge prices. III. Taylor checked each submitter's later land sale to prove that their claims were insincere.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I (Table 4, p. 271) and II (p. 273) support the reading. III is false: Taylor only analyses what submissions claimed, tests the assumption that part of the claims reflected self-interest, and does not trace individual sales."},{"t":"taylor","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: In Taylor's sample, owners in the west whose land was to become protected grassland called the rules unfair and wanted to be included. Statement II: Taylor reads premium-seeking as objection to planning restrictions that limit the future development value of one's land.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true (pp. 264, 268), and Taylor explicitly interprets the 21 grassland objections as premium-seeking objection to restrictions, in contrast to non-landowners who supported grassland protection. So II is the reason she gives for I."},{"t":"taylor","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Taylor shows that landowner pressure led to the GAIC being abolished in 2011. Statement II: In June 2011 a new minister deferred the charge fully until development, saying this protected hobby farmers and lifestyle owners.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"II is accurate (pp. 272-273) and Taylor reads it as a homevoter effect. I is false: the GAIC was not abolished but modified and deferred; Taylor even notes that its survival shows stakeholder influence has limits (p. 273)."},{"t":"taylor","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain matches Taylor's account of why some fringe owners lobby to be brought inside the boundary?","o":["Containment makes clear which land may be developed and that it is scarce, so a premium is expected inside, so owners outside lobby to be included","Containment lowers land prices inside the boundary through extra supply, so owners outside fear losses, so they lobby to be included","Containment makes clear which land may be developed and that it is scarce, so prices outside rise above those inside, so owners lobby to be included","Containment makes clear which land may be developed and that it is scarce, so a premium is expected inside, so owners outside lobby to stay rural"],"a":0,"e":"Dual predictability (via Nelson) makes it certain which land can be developed and that land inside is exhaustible, which gives owners outside a strong incentive to lobby for inclusion: premium-seeking (p. 264). The other chains break a link: containment raises, not lowers, prices inside; land outside is worth less; and the expected premium motivates inclusion, not staying rural."},{"t":"taylor","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combining Hindmoor's Olson and Taylor: why could fringe landowners shape the GAIC while future homebuyers did not?","o":["Olson: large groups organise best because their numbers bring political weight; Taylor: buyers stayed silent because they gained from the charge","Olson: groups organise only when the state funds them; Taylor: the inquiry was paid for by the landowners' campaign and served them","Olson: a smaller group with concentrated stakes organises more easily; Taylor: buyers are outsiders not yet present to take part","Olson: people act from duty, not interest; Taylor: owners' sense of duty to the land made them speak up while buyers felt no such duty"],"a":2,"e":"Olson's logic says a small group with concentrated stakes overcomes collective action problems more easily than a large, diffuse one, and Taylor adds the insider-outsider problem: future buyers have no voice because they are not yet there (pp. 260, 274). The first option reverses Olson, for whom large groups are the hard case."},{"t":"taylor","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"A student uses Taylor and Sorensen together to explain why a Dutch value capture levy was watered down. Which combination is faithful to both authors?","o":["Taylor: the levy failed because the planners lacked evidence of betterment; Sorensen: it failed because Dutch planning is locked in for good","Taylor: insiders' pressure at the decisive moment shaped the levy; Sorensen: such instruments develop through contestation along a constrained pathway","Taylor: landowners accepted the levy once it was made transparent; Sorensen: change happens gradually through symbols embedded in local practice","Taylor: the outcome reflects the long history of Dutch land policy; Sorensen: one group of owners with a stake simply outlobbied all the others"],"a":1,"e":"Taylor zooms in on interest-group pressure by insiders at one moment; Sorensen (historical institutionalism) sees land value capture as a long pathway in which change is contested within a relatively constrained range of options, with critical junctures. The last option swaps the two authors, and 'locked in for good' contradicts Sorensen."},{"t":"dembski","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"In Bourdieu's card-game image, as used by Dembski & Salet, what do the cards stand for?","o":["Individual skill, which decides how well each player uses the rules","Material capital, whose value is set by the rules of the game","Symbolic markers, which planners deal out to their audience","Formal laws, which the state hands to each player in advance"],"a":1,"e":"The cards are material capital whose values the rules fix, which limits the strategies available; individual capacity to play comes in on top of that. Skill is the second element of the image, not what the cards represent."},{"t":"dembski","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whose idea of 'banal nationalism', the everyday display of the flag rather than big moments, do Dembski & Salet use to explain how regions stay meaningful?","o":["Anssi Paasi","Clifford Geertz","Michael Billig","Maarten Hajer"],"a":2,"e":"Billig (1995) shows that everyday, largely unconscious reminders keep a collective identity alive; Dembski & Salet apply this to regions. Paasi is the tempting option: he supplies the internal and external sides of regional identity, not banal nationalism."},{"t":"dembski","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whose essay 'Deciphering a meal' do Dembski & Salet cite to show that social codes are expressed and reproduced in daily practices?","o":["Mary Douglas","Pierre Bourdieu","Clifford Geertz","Peter Berger"],"a":0,"e":"Douglas analyses the preparation of food as a set of precoded messages expressing patterns of social relations. Geertz is tempting because he also reads culture as codes, but his example in the article is the Balinese cockfight."},{"t":"dembski","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Dembski & Salet say the 'institutional void' offers an outstanding chance for new cultural practices. Whose term is it?","o":["Patsy Healey","Pierre Bourdieu","Peter Hall","Maarten Hajer"],"a":3,"e":"The authors explicitly credit Hajer (2003) with the term 'institutional void'. Bourdieu supplies habitus and rites of institution, not this term."},{"t":"dembski","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"How, according to Dembski & Salet, are institutions usually perceived in planning practice and research, a view their paper sets out to challenge?","o":["As neutral tools that planners can redesign at will","As rusty old fixtures that hinder solving planning problems","As symbolic systems that mobilise people for change","As cooperative games that cut the costs of exchange"],"a":1,"e":"They note that institutions are seen as 'rusty old fixtures' that limit action, and ask why institutions should explain dynamic change. The 'symbolic systems' option is their own conclusion, not the common view they challenge."},{"t":"dembski","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"Which term do Dembski & Salet take from Berger & Luckmann for institutionalisation as an ongoing process of normalisation?","o":["Inculcation","Consecration","Habitualisation","Articulation"],"a":2,"e":"Institutionalisation is 'an ongoing process of normalisation, or habitualisation' (Berger & Luckmann). Inculcation is also in the paper, but it names the audience absorbing a symbol's meaning, linked to Bourdieu."},{"t":"dembski","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which landmark do Dembski & Salet cite as not having (yet) turned its city into what it was meant to symbolise?","o":["The Erasmus Bridge, meant to make Rotterdam a world port city","The Guggenheim museum, meant to make Bilbao a cultural city","The Sydney Opera House, meant to make Sydney a capital of arts","The Eiffel Tower, meant to make Paris a city of modern industry"],"a":1,"e":"The paper says the Guggenheim has not yet turned industrial Bilbao into a cultural city (and the Futuroscope has not made Poitiers a technology centre). The other landmarks are not discussed in the article."},{"t":"dembski","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to Dembski & Salet (after Berger & Luckmann), those who impose norms must be institutionally recognised as what?","o":["'Claim-makers' who articulate new frames","'Norm entrepreneurs' with formal authority","'Symbolisers' elected by the audience","'Knowers' who transmit institutional knowledge"],"a":3,"e":"Those who impose norms 'have to be institutionally recognised as knowers and thus transmitters of institutional knowledge'. Claim-makers appear in step 6 of the research model, but they are not the legitimacy condition meant here."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which situation best illustrates what Dembski & Salet, after Bourdieu, call producing 'discontinuity out of continuity'?","o":["A slow rise in prices that gradually changes who can live in a district","A graduation that turns a student into a 'master' though little changed overnight","A new zoning plan that replaces the old one on a fixed date set by law","A demographic shift in which an area's population ages year by year"],"a":1,"e":"A rite of institution consecrates an arbitrary boundary in a continuous process, so others treat the person differently and the person changes their self-image. A new plan on a fixed date is a formal switch, not a consecrated boundary produced by belief."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"In Dembski & Salet's Table 1, what do rational choice institutionalism and political or actor-centred institutionalism have in common?","o":["Both treat institutions as validated prior to action","Both treat institutions as internalised in actors' minds","Both treat institutions as external constraints on choice","Both treat institutions as symbolic systems of meaning"],"a":2,"e":"Both sit on the 'external' side of the first dimension; they differ on the second (prior to action versus in action). 'Validated prior to action' fits rational choice but not actor-centred institutionalism, which is placed 'in action'."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"Which example do Dembski & Salet (via Friedland & Alford) give of a ritual that only makes sense to those who believe in the institution behind it?","o":["Queuing, which makes sense only if one fears social sanctions","Voting, which makes sense only if one believes in democracy","Bargaining, which makes sense only if one calculates payoffs","Saving, which makes sense only if one trusts the banking system"],"a":1,"e":"Voting is a symbol of democracy and makes sense only to those who believe in it, while also signalling that the institution functions. The bargaining option describes strategic calculation, which is exactly what their internalised approach sets aside."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: a ministry launches a brand for a border region, but local councils see the ministry as an outsider with no feel for the area and ignore the label. Which step of Dembski & Salet's research model is most directly at stake?","o":["Step 2, the encoded message: which social pattern the marker symbolises","Step 3, the subjective context: whether the symboliser is legitimate","Step 6, articulation: how the marker's meaning shifts over time","Step 8, sanctions: who punishes misrecognition of the message"],"a":1,"e":"Step 3 asks who symbolises and whether they are authoritative; some persons or groups are better placed than others to act as plausible symbolisers. Step 6 is tempting because councils contest the label, but the core problem here is the transmitter's legitimacy."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which claim about formal legislation fits Dembski & Salet's account of institutionalisation?","o":["A law is an institution from the moment a legitimate body decrees it","A law takes shape gradually and must be validated in practice after it is enacted","A law is an external constraint, so it falls outside sociological analysis","A law changes its meaning only at critical junctures opened by a crisis"],"a":1,"e":"Even formal legislation 'takes shape gradually' within evolving expectations, and its formal commencement must be followed by validation in social interaction. The first option treats a decree as automatically meaningful, which is the voluntarism they reject."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which of these is NOT among the new urban forms Dembski & Salet cite as prompting a quest for new symbols and identities?","o":["Garreau's 'Edge City'","Sieverts' 'Zwischenstadt'","Ascher's 'métapolis'","The European 'compact city'"],"a":3,"e":"The compact city with density falling outward is the classic pattern that began to crack; edge city, Zwischenstadt and métapolis name the dispersed regions that lack crystallised identity. That old pattern is what new symbols move away from."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"What, according to Dembski & Salet, is the 'Achilles heel' of the social magic of symbols?","o":["The symbol's design may be too weak to attract investors or visitors","Planners may lack the formal powers needed to enforce the symbol","Rival regions may copy the symbol and so dilute its distinct meaning","The essential condition, inculcation by the audience, may not be met"],"a":3,"e":"Social magic works only when the meaning is inculcated in and guaranteed by the audience; if that condition is not met, mobilisation through symbols stays powerless. Weak design is exactly the explanation the authors reject."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Dembski & Salet share communicative planning's interest in metaphors and narratives. Where do they part ways with it?","o":["They reject metaphors as irrelevant and study formal legal rules instead","They study only material landmarks, not planning discourse or stories","They see consensus-building dialogue as the key mechanism of meaning","They locate success not in the message itself but in institutional embedding"],"a":3,"e":"Communicative work studies the symbol and its expression; Dembski & Salet look for the institutional conditions that make audiences believe it. They do not reject metaphors; metaphors are one of their main kinds of symbolic marker."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"A researcher asks: 'Do these symbols represent the real situation and people's judgments about their own position in social space?' Which step of Dembski & Salet's Box 1 is this?","o":["Step 3, the subjective context","Step 4, the objective context","Step 5, the audience","Step 7, internalisation"],"a":1,"e":"Step 4 asks what social structure is reproduced (for example via habitus) and whether the symbols are genuine. Step 3 is the tempting neighbour, but it concerns who symbolises and their authority."},{"t":"dembski","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"How does Geertz, as quoted by Dembski & Salet, define culture?","o":["A set of external rules that reduce uncertainty in exchange","A historically transmitted pattern of meanings embodied in symbols","A durable set of dispositions shaped by one's class position","A system of ideas imposed on society by the ruling class"],"a":1,"e":"Geertz defines culture as a 'historically transmitted pattern of meanings embodied in symbols'. Durable dispositions describe Bourdieu's habitus, also used in the paper but not as a definition of culture."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a municipality near Utrecht renames a motorway business park 'Science Corridor'. The label appears in council documents, but firms and staff see it as a planners' invention unrelated to what they do there. Which concept fits this failure most precisely?","o":["Path dependence: past investment locks the park into its old role","Free-riding: firms enjoy the brand without paying for its upkeep","Failed inculcation: the audience does not absorb or guarantee the meaning","Rent-seeking: owners use the label to lobby for profitable rezoning"],"a":2,"e":"For Dembski & Salet a marker works only if its meaning is inculcated by the audience; here the symbol is detached from practices and falls on fallow ground. Path dependence is the historical-institutionalist reading and says nothing about whether the audience believes the symbol."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Dembski & Salet's view of the relation between symbols and institutions most precisely?","o":["Symbols always fail when they aim at change, because institutions only reproduce what exists","Symbols create institutions by themselves once they are well designed and widely communicated","Symbols work only if the meaning they refer to is inculcated, so embedding matters more than design","Symbols matter little, since embedding is achieved by formal rules that planners enact and enforce"],"a":2,"e":"The paper argues symbols can institute new meaning, but only when their reference is inculcated in the audience. The first option overstates: the authors explicitly want to explain transformation, not only reproduction."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A policy memo argues: 'Residents did not sort their waste because of fines or savings; they did it because, in this neighbourhood, that is simply what decent people do.' Which perspective grounds this argument, and why?","o":["Sociological institutionalism: action follows a logic of appropriateness","Rational choice institutionalism: rules cut the costs of cooperation","Evolutionary institutionalism: rules survive selection by trial and error","Historical institutionalism: early choices lock in a path of behaviour"],"a":0,"e":"Following an assumed imperative of what is normal and fitting, rather than calculation, is March & Olsen's logic of appropriateness, which Dembski & Salet adopt. Rational choice is tempting because waste rules solve a collective action problem, but the memo explicitly denies that costs and benefits drive behaviour."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about Dembski & Salet's use of Bourdieu is NOT consistent with the text?","o":["Habitus lets them avoid both objectivism and subjectivism","Practice is informed by structure but is not a mechanical reaction","The verb 'instituting' stresses that meaning is settled again and again","Actors' judgments of their place float free of their position in space"],"a":3,"e":"Bourdieu, as used here, holds that actors' ideas about their position in objective space depend on that position; this is what keeps the account from being purely subjectivist. The other three statements are close to the text."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course author(s) would most clearly endorse this claim: 'A new name for a region gains force only when those who launch it are accepted as legitimate knowers by the people addressed.'","o":["Moroni","Dembski & Salet","Sorensen","Davoudi"],"a":1,"e":"Legitimate 'knowers' who transmit institutional knowledge (after Berger & Luckmann) is Dembski & Salet's condition for symbolic efficacy. Moroni also stresses that meaning cannot be decreed, but he speaks of evolved rules, not of the legitimacy of symbolisers."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: in a city where few norms are shared across groups, a planner builds a new park programme on the strong sports culture of one large local community, instead of on a city-wide identity. Which idea of Dembski & Salet fits most precisely?","o":["Building on accepted practices of particular groups in a fragmented order","Finding an institutional void where no group holds any settled norms","Using a rite of institution to consecrate a new arbitrary boundary","Reaffirming regional identity through banal, everyday reminders"],"a":0,"e":"Where few institutions are shared, the authors suggest planning can be strengthened by referring to the accepted practices of particular groups. The institutional void is close but different: there no group holds settled norms, whereas here the planner builds on a group's existing ones."},{"t":"dembski","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Dembski & Salet hold that planners cannot create whatever social reality they desire. Statement II: Dembski & Salet deny that social reality is socially constructed.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement I is their position. Statement II is false: they write that 'the fact that reality is socially constructed does not by any means imply' planners can create any reality; they accept social construction but deny it is voluntaristic."},{"t":"dembski","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: In Dembski & Salet's account, sanctions are the primary way institutions control behaviour. Statement II: Following Berger & Luckmann, they state that institutional knowledge is everyday knowledge.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"The primary controlling role lies in the reciprocal recognition of social codes; sanctions are reserved for those who fail to recognise the norm, so I is false. II is a near-literal statement from the text."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims fit Dembski & Salet's account of how a region is instituted? I. A high-tech region must be believed inside and outside, not only developed technologically. II. Regional meaning is secured once a founding event has consecrated the new boundary. III. Unconscious, everyday reaffirmation matters even more than conscious remembrance.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I follows Paasi's two-sided identity and III follows Billig and Paasi on (especially unconscious) remembrance. II is wrong: regions are a continuous process in need of reproduction, so one founding rite does not secure meaning."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Dembski & Salet explain the success or failure of place symbols by whether the audience inculcates their meaning. Which pairing of perspective and critique is correct?","o":["Moroni: the model forgets that rules need the belief of those who follow them","Harvey: it ignores whose interests branding serves in the competition for capital","Sorensen: the model overstates power and contestation in institutional change","Rational choice: it relies too much on actors' strategic calculation of payoffs"],"a":1,"e":"A Marxist reading asks whom place symbols serve; for Harvey, image-making is part of entrepreneurial competition between cities for mobile capital, a question of interests that inculcation leaves aside. The Moroni option misfires: his sense-making principle agrees that meaning cannot be decreed."},{"t":"dembski","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly describes why, for Dembski & Salet, changing city-regions prompt planners to look for new symbolic markers?","o":["Dispersed urbanisation crosses borders, so the landscape gains strong local identities, so symbols are sought to protect them","Dispersed urbanisation crosses borders, so the landscape feels like a nonplace, so symbols are sought to build identity and legitimacy","Compact cities grow denser, so the landscape feels like a nonplace, so symbols are sought to build identity and legitimacy","Dispersed urbanisation crosses borders, so the landscape feels like a nonplace, so symbols are sought to replace regional governments"],"a":1,"e":"Sprawl ignores administrative and cultural boundaries, the new landscape lacks rooted meaning, and the fragmented archipelago needs identity and legitimacy for city-regional arrangements. The last chain breaks at the final link: symbols are meant to support, not replace, regional arrangements."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims fit Dembski & Salet's choice of internalised institutions? I. Institutions shape not only behaviour but also basic preferences. II. People follow institutions out of an assumed imperative rather than strategic calculation. III. The internalised approach is analytically easier, because rules are clearly separated from choices.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are stated in their defence of the internalised view. III reverses the text: the clear separation of rules and choices is the advantage of the external approach, and the authors admit the internalised one is analytically harder."},{"t":"moroni","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Moroni quotes the line that institutions are 'the result of human action, but not the execution of any human design'. Who wrote it?","o":["David Hume","Adam Smith","Adam Ferguson","Friedrich Hayek"],"a":2,"e":"The line is Adam Ferguson's (1767), quoted by Moroni as the core of the Scottish Enlightenment view. Hume is tempting because Moroni also quotes him, but on the gradual emergence of property conventions."},{"t":"moroni","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whose image of institutions as 'the rules of the game' and organisations as 'its players' does Moroni adopt?","o":["Ronald Coase","Mancur Olson","Herbert A. Simon","Douglass North"],"a":3,"e":"Moroni follows North (1990): both give structure to interaction, but institutions define how the game is played while organisations try to win it. Coase is tempting as an institutional economist, but the rules-versus-players image is North's."},{"t":"moroni","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"In classical Greek, as Moroni explains in a note, what does 'taxis' mean?","o":["A grown order that arises from within","A made order imposed from outside","An order of nature without human action","An order based on shared convention"],"a":1,"e":"'Taxis' is a made, exogenous order; 'kosmos' is a grown, endogenous order, terms revived by Hayek. The first option describes kosmos, the opposite term."},{"t":"moroni","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to Moroni, which school mainly took up the Scottish Enlightenment's evolutionary approach to institutions?","o":["The Austrian School of Economics","The old American institutionalism","The Chicago School of Economics","The Frankfurt School of social theory"],"a":0,"e":"Moroni names the Austrian School (Menger, Hayek) and the Neo-Austrians. He mentions the old American institutionalists (Veblen, Commons) only to say they are less pertinent to his argument."},{"t":"moroni","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Moroni quotes Bastiat marvelling that a city of a million people is fed every day without anyone directing it. Which principle does this support?","o":["Robustness: stop changing rules often","Modesty: recognise rules that evolved","Knowability: publish rules in advance","Openness: avoid case-specific rules"],"a":1,"e":"Under the principle of modesty Moroni argues that institutions such as freedom of contract and ownership have proved beneficial; Bastiat's 'freedom in transactions' illustrates this. Robustness concerns the frequency of rule changes, not which institutions to keep."},{"t":"moroni","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"What does Moroni's principle of robustness say?","o":["Rules must hold even if politicians are not virtuous","Rules should be clear and known before people act","Frequent rule changes make their information useless","Rules must be changed gradually, defect by defect"],"a":2,"e":"Institutions provide information for deciding and acting; a frequent shake-up of the rules creates confusion for developers and citizens. Gradualism is the tempting distractor: it concerns how change is made, not how often rules are shaken up."},{"t":"moroni","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which of Moroni's principles says that institutions must work even when people and politicians behave in non-virtuous ways?","o":["Motivational realism","Principle of peculiarity","Principle of sense-making","Non-tabula-rasa principle"],"a":0,"e":"Motivational realism asks for a middle path between assuming everyone is a saint and everyone is a knave, and warns against undue optimism about urban actors and politicians. Sense-making is about whether rules carry meaning for actors."},{"t":"moroni","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Moroni writes that 'reformed institutions' are almost always 'successor institutions'. Which principle is this?","o":["Principle of openness","Principle of gradualism","Non-tabula-rasa principle","Motivational realism principle"],"a":2,"e":"We never intervene on a blank slate but always within a long-term, consolidated framework, so reformed institutions succeed earlier ones. Gradualism is related, but it concerns the pace and method of change, not the impossibility of starting from zero."},{"t":"moroni","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which of these is, in Moroni's terms, an artificial phenomenon of the third kind: the unintended result of many intentional actions?","o":["A water board set up by statute with a budget","The seasonal flooding of an unregulated river","A zoning plan drafted by a municipal office","A language that grew through everyday use"],"a":3,"e":"Language is Hume's example of a convention that arises gradually without any promise, like most institutions. The water board is tempting because it is an institution in everyday speech, but in Moroni's terms it is an organisation: artificial by deliberate design."},{"t":"moroni","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which statement does Moroni NOT make about organisations?","o":["They are built for a specific end by someone","They are governed by directives from a centre","They are relatively simple since they are made","Their complexity exceeds what one mind grasps"],"a":3,"e":"Complexity beyond what a human mind can master is how Moroni describes institutions, under the principle of peculiarity. The other three statements are his description of organisations."},{"t":"moroni","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"What does Moroni concede to Alexander, despite rejecting the term 'institutional design'?","o":["His micro- and meso-level work is very useful","Institutional evolution also involves design","Macro-level design works if it is slow enough","Planning is best understood as institutional design"],"a":0,"e":"Moroni calls Alexander's contribution on the micro and meso levels 'exceptionally insightful and useful', but calls for a different approach at the macro level. The second option is Alexander's own reply, which Moroni rejects."},{"t":"moroni","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: a council writes a special zoning rule that applies only to one named tower project on one plot. Which of Moroni's principles does this most directly violate?","o":["Openness: rules for many future cases","Robustness: rules that change rarely","Motivational realism: no virtue needed","Sense-making: rules that carry meaning"],"a":0,"e":"Open institutions are universal rules for an indeterminate number of future cases; case-specific rules are to be avoided as much as possible. Robustness is tempting but concerns how often rules change, not how general they are."},{"t":"moroni","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Moroni combines evolutionary theory with 'radical ignorance'. What does he mean by this?","o":["Ignorance planners can reduce with better data and models","Ignorance that is ineradicable in a complex social world","Ignorance of citizens about the rules that bind them","Ignorance of past rules that reformers fail to study"],"a":1,"e":"He contrasts radical ignorance with mere limited rationality: in a complex world individual ignorance cannot be overcome, which is why a kosmos approach is a stance against the 'pretence of knowledge'. The first option describes bounded rationality that better tools could fix."},{"t":"moroni","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"How does Moroni position invisible-hand explanations of institutions with respect to methodological individualism?","o":["They must always be strictly individualist","They must always be strictly collectivist","He prefers an intermediate position","He rejects both and favours psychologism"],"a":2,"e":"Invisible-hand explanations can be individualist or not; Moroni prefers an intermediate position between methodological individualism and collectivism. He explicitly says social sciences must avoid psychologism."},{"t":"moroni","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Moroni says evolution produces sub-optimal results. Why does he still value evolved institutions?","o":["Because selection always keeps the best of all possible rules","Because they succeed where other mechanisms would have failed","Because they were chosen by the wisest legislators of the past","Because they are fixed once and for all, so nobody must decide"],"a":1,"e":"Evolution acts on chance variations, not on all possible variants, so it cannot be optimal; yet it achieves important results where other mechanisms would have failed (Hayek). The first option contradicts his point about sub-optimality."},{"t":"moroni","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Is Moroni's kosmos-oriented approach simply a conservative position, according to his conclusion?","o":["Yes: existing institutions should be kept exactly as they are","No: he wants radical reform once the evolved rules are understood","No: the answer lies in between; they need a different approach","Yes: any reform is a form of taxis and should therefore be avoided"],"a":2,"e":"He writes that the issue is not simply being conservative or not; the answer 'lies somewhere in between', and the point is that institutions need a wholly different approach. He explicitly allows intervention, so the last option overstates."},{"t":"moroni","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"In a note, Moroni admits something about the link between his evolutionary theory and his normative proposals. What?","o":["The description proves his normative conclusions logically","The description does not imply them, but makes some more plausible","The normative part is independent and needs no description at all","The description is drawn from Toronto, so it holds only for cities"],"a":1,"e":"Quoting Schubert, he concedes that the features of an evolutionary view do not logically imply normative conclusions, but render some more or less plausible. The first option overstates the link he claims."},{"t":"moroni","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a ministry wants to replace the whole national expropriation law in one stroke, with a new code that takes effect next year. The old law is not changed often; the plan is simply to rewrite it at once. Which of Moroni's principles fits the objection most precisely?","o":["Robustness: frequent changes waste the rules' information","Gradualism: repair recognisable defects step by step","Knowability: rules must be known before people act","Peculiarity: institutions must not serve set outcomes"],"a":1,"e":"The problem is not frequency but the leap: gradualism asks reformers to reconstruct how rules developed and remedy recognisable defects through gradual improvement. Robustness is the tempting option, but the case says the law is rarely changed; one big rewrite violates gradualism first."},{"t":"moroni","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states the aim of invisible-hand explanations, as Moroni presents them, most precisely?","o":["To show that no one ever designs anything in social life","To prove that every social pattern is efficient and fair","To find the responsible actor behind each social pattern","To downsize visible-hand and conspiratorial explanations"],"a":3,"e":"Their aim is to 'downsize' the role of visible-hand or conspiratorial explanations that always look for a responsible element. The first option overstates: Moroni accepts that organisations are designed, and evolution works on intentional trial and error."},{"t":"moroni","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A policy note argues: 'Before scrapping the old tenancy rules, the ministry should ask how they came about. No single legislator wrote them; they carry lessons from generations of disputes, and that knowledge is held by no expert.' Which perspective grounds this argument, and why?","o":["Historical institutionalism: early choices lock in a path","Sociological institutionalism: rules are cognitive scripts","Evolutionary institutionalism: rules hold dispersed knowledge","Rational choice institutionalism: rules cut transaction costs"],"a":2,"e":"Rules as an undesigned 'distillate' of successful experimentation that no individual mind could hold is Moroni's evolutionary core. Historical institutionalism is tempting because the note looks back in time, but it explains persistence through power and path dependence, not stored knowledge."},{"t":"moroni","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about Moroni's distinction between institutions and organisations is NOT consistent with the text?","o":["Rules exist inside organisations too, but of a very different kind","Institutions define how the game is played; teams try to win it","Organisations can subordinate commitments to preferred outcomes","Both are best changed by design, only at a different scale each"],"a":3,"e":"Moroni's point is precisely that they cannot be changed in the same way: there is 'a breach along the line', so he separates organisational design from institutional intervention. The other three statements are in the text."},{"t":"moroni","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Moroni's evolutionary theory of institutions, which statements are true? I. Cultural evolution, unlike biological evolution, passes on acquired characteristics as learnt rules. II. Evolved institutions are optimal, because selection keeps only the best possible rules. III. An evolutionary explanation explains the principle, not the detail. IV. Institutions let individually ignorant people use more knowledge than any of them holds.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I, III and IV are in the text; II is false because evolution acts on chance variations and leads to sub-optimal results. The second pattern is the tempting one: it accepts II, mixing up 'successful' with 'optimal'."},{"t":"moroni","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Moroni's kosmos-oriented principles, which statements are true? I. Institutions should be designed on the assumption that politicians act virtuously. II. Institutions should be universal rules for an indeterminate number of future cases. III. Reformers should invent new optimal institutions rather than rely on existing ones. IV. Rules should be updated often to keep them relevant to changing needs.","o":["I F · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III F · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F"],"a":3,"e":"Only II (openness) is true. I contradicts motivational realism, III contradicts modesty ('recognize' rather than 'invent') and IV contradicts robustness. The first pattern is tempting for readers who confuse reform with invention."},{"t":"moroni","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Moroni argues that most institutions evolve unintentionally and that reform should therefore be modest and gradual. How would a historical institutionalist critique this view?","o":["Evolved rules are not a neutral selection of what works: they reflect power, and who wins at critical junctures","Rules are embedded in cognitive scripts and meanings, so even gradual reform fails unless people internalise it","Rules can be deliberately designed by planners at every level, as long as political goals are clearly defined","Rules persist because they lower transaction costs, so any reform should be judged on its efficiency gains"],"a":0,"e":"Historical institutionalists see institutions as unequally distributive and contested, so 'what survived' reflects power at junctures rather than benign selection (Sorensen). The second option is a sociological critique: it concerns meaning, not power."},{"t":"moroni","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Moroni concludes that 'planning strictu senso cannot be applied to institutions'. How would a sociological institutionalist such as Dembski & Salet critique this conclusion?","o":["Change comes at critical junctures, when a crisis opens a short window in which choices set a path","Planners can institute new meaning if they build on accepted norms in under-institutionalised niches","Planners can foster cooperation under new rules by lowering the transaction costs of collective action","Institutions at all levels, including whole societies, can be deliberately created by skilled planners"],"a":1,"e":"Dembski & Salet share Moroni's doubts about voluntarism, but argue that deliberate change of meaning is possible when it connects to existing norms and uses niches of under-institutionalised space. The last option is Alexander's design view, which is not a sociological position."},{"t":"moroni","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims fit Moroni's principle of adaptive efficiency? I. Rules should support a continuous process of social and economic discovery. II. The set of rules and the order of actions within it differ: rules can favour the order but not shape it. III. The aim is the efficient allocation of known resources to identifiable end-users.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are in Moroni's account of adaptive efficiency (after North). III describes allocative efficiency, which he says must be abandoned for institutions, since resources, preferences and end-users are not given in advance."},{"t":"moroni","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Moroni rejects Alexander's claim that the evolution of institutions also involves institutional design. Statement II: Moroni distinguishes what is artificial because it was designed from what is artificial because it is the unintended result of interaction.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Moroni counters Alexander's reply with exactly this distinction: institutions are mostly artificial in the second sense, so their evolution is not design even if it involves intentional acts. II is therefore the reason for I."},{"t":"moroni","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Moroni defends a first-order taxis-oriented approach for land-use planning while rejecting it for institutions. Statement II: Moroni argues that the taxis-oriented approach of orthodox land-use planning can resurface higher up, at the level of institutions.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Moroni criticises first-order taxis (detailed end-state coordination of activities) as well; his new point is that the same mindset reappears as a second-order taxis. So I is false and II is true."},{"t":"moroni","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly describes why, for Moroni, a second-order taxis approach risks perverse effects?","o":["Institutions are simple, so designers can grasp them, so redesign to an end-state model rarely misfires","Institutions hold dispersed knowledge, so redesign to an end-state model gathers it, so reform succeeds","Institutions hold dispersed knowledge, so redesign to an end-state model loses it, so reform misfires","Institutions hold dispersed knowledge, so redesign is blocked by vested interests, so nothing changes"],"a":2,"e":"Evolved institutions crystallise knowledge no single mind can hold; rebuilding them around someone's end-state model throws that knowledge away, which is why a taxis approach to institutions risks perverse effects. The last chain swaps in a historical-institutionalist link about vested interests."},{"t":"moroni","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Hypothetical: a province rewrites its rules on developer contributions every two years, as municipalities and developers keep lobbying. Which pairing of Moroni's and Sorensen's readings is correct?","o":["Moroni: normal contestation in a pathway; Sorensen: it undermines robustness","Moroni: it undermines robustness; Sorensen: normal contestation in a pathway","Moroni: a sign of adaptive efficiency; Sorensen: proof of lock-in by 1946 rules","Moroni: a critical juncture every time; Sorensen: spontaneous order by trial"],"a":1,"e":"For Moroni frequent shake-ups destroy the information rules provide (robustness); for Sorensen continuous contestation and incremental change within a developmental pathway are normal. The first option swaps the two authors."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Sorensen contrasts Toronto with a Canadian city that routinely captures about 70 percent of the land value uplift from rezoning. Which city?","o":["Montreal","Vancouver","Calgary","Ottawa"],"a":1,"e":"Vancouver's Community Benefits Agreements capture about 70 percent of rezoning uplift, based on developers' own pro forma data; Toronto planners aimed for 10 to 15 percent. The contrast shows how locally contingent value capture institutions are."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"In whose work does conjunctural analysis originate, before Stuart Hall used it to analyse the turn to neoliberal hegemony in 1980s Britain?","o":["Karl Polanyi","Max Weber","Antonio Gramsci","Michel Foucault"],"a":2,"e":"Sorensen traces conjunctural analysis from Gramsci through Stuart Hall to Peck. Polanyi is tempting as another critic of market society, but he is not part of this lineage in the article."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"From whom does Sorensen take the distinction between 'permissive' and 'productive' conditions of a critical juncture?","o":["Paul Pierson","Fligstein & McAdam","Capoccia & Kelemen","Hillel Soifer"],"a":3,"e":"Soifer (2012) defines permissive conditions as those that raise the causal power of agency, and productive conditions as those that shape the outcome. Capoccia & Kelemen supply the definition of a critical juncture itself."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"In Sorensen's Toronto history, what were 'lot levies'?","o":["Per-lot charges on subdividers for off-site infrastructure","Annual property taxes on vacant lots held for speculation","Fees for extra density granted above the existing zoning","Provincial grants per lot to help suburbs pay their debts"],"a":0,"e":"Lot levies were per-lot charges to pay for off-site infrastructure such as sewage plants, demanded through subdivision approval powers. Fees for extra density describe Section 37 agreements, a later instrument."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which new body, created as an upper tier of regional government, made developers provide on-site infrastructure and pay levies to secure suburban finance?","o":["The Ontario Municipal Board","Metropolitan Toronto","The Urban Development Institute","The Ontario Home Builders Association"],"a":1,"e":"Metro Toronto was set up partly to prevent a repeat of the 1930s suburban debt crisis, using the city's tax base, on-site infrastructure requirements and lot levies. The Ontario Municipal Board was the tribunal that heard appeals against levies."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which principle, long argued in Ontario, sums up the case for land value capture in Sorensen's article?","o":["The polluter should pay","Growth should pay for growth","Housing should come first","The user should pay the cost"],"a":1,"e":"'Growth should pay for growth': new residents are on average wealthier, so if development contributes nothing, lower-income residents subsidise newcomers or developers' profits. 'The polluter should pay' is an environmental principle, not the one Sorensen cites."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"In the 1980s conflict over lot levies, which organisation lobbied in favour of development charges and presented draft legislation?","o":["The Urban Development Institute","The Ontario Home Builders Association","The Association of Municipalities of Ontario","The Ontario Ministry of Treasury and Economics"],"a":2,"e":"The AMO lobbied for development charges, while developers, represented by the UDI and the OHBA, opposed them. The ministry convened a working group but the parties could not agree."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What did Ontario's Bill 197 (2020) do, according to Sorensen?","o":["It walked back several Bill 108 changes after wide opposition","It created the Development Charges Act and its formula","It merged Metro Toronto into a single new City of Toronto","It raised the cap on community benefits to 15 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Opposition to Bill 108 was so wide, even in Conservative-held municipalities, that Bill 197 reversed several of its changes not yet in force, including those to the DC Act. It shows continuing contestation in the reactive sequence."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which Toronto episode best illustrates incremental change within a developmental pathway, rather than a critical juncture?","o":["The passage of the Planning Act in 1946","The Development Charges Act of 1989","The spread of lot levies and their legalisation in 1959","The rewriting of value capture rules from 2019 onwards"],"a":2,"e":"Lot levies spread through subdivision powers, were contested in courts and the OMB, and were then confirmed retroactively: meaningful incremental change in the reactive sequence after 1946. The 1946 and 1989 laws are the two critical junctures."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the things critical junctures typically establish, according to Sorensen's conclusion?","o":["The terrain of debate over the new institutions","Which institutions are contested and which not","A lock-in that stops contest until a new crisis","How much ambiguity there is in reading the rules"],"a":2,"e":"Sorensen rejects lock-in: contestation continues and incremental change is constant, within a developmental pathway. The terrain of debate, which institutions are contested, the rules for change and the degree of ambiguity are all things he lists."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: after a major river flood, a national government passes new rules on building in flood-prone areas, and municipalities create new local bodies in response. Why would Sorensen not be surprised?","o":["Disasters and multilevel governance make urban institutions prone to critical junctures","Disasters show that urban rules evolve by trial and error without any human design","Disasters make residents internalise new norms of appropriate behaviour more quickly","Disasters let small, concentrated groups organise more easily than large diffuse ones"],"a":0,"e":"Sorensen argues that urban institutions are especially prone to critical junctures: senior governments can trigger change by law, local governments respond with new institutions, and disasters prompt new ones. The second option is Moroni's evolutionary view."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Why does Sorensen recommend focusing on formal rules such as land value capture when studying urban institutions?","o":["They are the only rules that actors actually follow in practice","They shift costs and benefits between actors and are contentious","They are easy to measure, since they never change after adoption","They express the shared meaning that a community gives to place"],"a":1,"e":"Rules that shift the costs and benefits of development strongly affect land values and profits, and are therefore politically contentious. The third option contradicts his whole account of constant contestation."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"What does historical institutionalism assume about who defends and who challenges an institution?","o":["Those who gain from it resist change; those disadvantaged seek it","Everyone defends it, as long as it lowers their transaction costs","Nobody defends it consciously, because rules are taken for granted","Those who designed it defend it; everyone else is indifferent to it"],"a":0,"e":"Following Mahoney & Thelen, HI assumes institutions are the product of competition among interested actors: winners resist weakening changes, losers push for change. The third option is a sociological view of taken-for-granted rules."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"Why does Sorensen call the 1946 Planning Act 'contingent'?","o":["The province copied the Curtis report's proposals in full","The courts, not the legislature, wrote most of its content","The government chose a new, limited law no one had proposed","The suburbs drafted it to secure their own infrastructure"],"a":2,"e":"Several detailed proposals were on the table, but the new majority government chose none of them and passed a much more limited law that no one expected. Copying the Curtis report is wrong: the Act rejected making subdividers pay for services."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Sorensen discusses Leitner & Sheppard's conjunctural analysis of Jakarta and Bangalore. What does he say it lacks?","o":["Any attention to land development in the current conjuncture","Any spatial dimension, since it remains purely historical","Anything about specific processes of institutional change","Any link to global economic and ideological currents"],"a":2,"e":"Leitner & Sheppard 'spatialise' conjunctural analysis through land assetisation, but say nothing about specific institutional change processes, which is the gap HI fills. The second option is wrong: spatialisation is exactly their contribution."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Why does Sorensen suggest that Toronto's rate of value capture was probably already too low before recent cuts?","o":["Developers' own pro forma data showed losses on most towers","The construction boom continued even as charges increased","Vancouver captured less uplift and built more housing","Courts had ruled the charges illegal in many suburbs"],"a":1,"e":"The accelerating construction boom of the last two decades suggests the industry stayed profitable even with rising Section 37 payments and development charges. Vancouver is the opposite case: it captured far more, about 70 percent."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"What, for Sorensen, does the intense and continual contestation of Toronto's value capture rules show?","o":["That the rules are badly designed and should be replaced","That the rules matter and do shift costs and benefits","That the rules are locked in and cannot really be changed","That developers have always captured the planning system"],"a":1,"e":"He reads continual contestation as evidence that these institutions are substantively important and do, in some measure, shift the distribution of costs and benefits. Lock-in is the tempting misreading his conclusion rejects."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch province faces a sharp housing affordability crisis, and everyone agrees land policy must change. Yet no coalition has a proposal ready, the legal tools are lacking and the debate splits along old lines; five years later the rules are unchanged. Which concept fits most precisely?","o":["Lock-in: past investments make any change too costly to make","Permissive conditions without productive ones: no outcome follows","Collective action failure: large diffuse groups fail to organise","Failed inculcation: the audience did not absorb the new meaning"],"a":1,"e":"Following Soifer, a crisis can create permissive conditions, but without productive conditions (engaged actors, ready mechanisms, legal structures) no meaningful change results; both are needed. Lock-in is tempting, but Sorensen rejects it as the usual form of path dependence."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Sorensen's view of the role of global ideological currents most precisely?","o":["They matter, but their local impact is mediated by local institutions","They directly determine the content of local planning legislation","They are irrelevant, since local institutions explain every outcome","They shape planning only in the Global South, not in North America"],"a":0,"e":"He accepts that hegemonic global currents and conjunctures are important, but argues their impact on the ground is mediated through local institutions and contests over specific rules. The second option overstates the global side and the third understates it."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A housing review argues: 'Who keeps the uplift from rezoning is not a technical matter. Each rule on developer contributions was won by some coalition during a crisis and has been fought over ever since, in courts and in parliament.' Which perspective grounds this argument, and why?","o":["Rational choice: actors calculate costs and lobby to capture rents","Historical institutionalism: contested rules set at critical junctures","Evolutionary institutionalism: rules survive selection over time","Sociological institutionalism: rules are taken-for-granted norms"],"a":1,"e":"Rules won by coalitions during crises and contested in a long sequence afterwards is Sorensen's critical-juncture and reactive-sequence logic. Rational choice is tempting because lobbying and rents appear, but it does not explain the timing (crisis) and the long sequence of contest."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about Sorensen's Toronto history is NOT consistent with the text?","o":["The 1946 Act was the first to give real powers over subdivision","The 1946 Act explicitly allowed municipalities to charge lot levies","The 1989 Act linked development charges to building permits","Bill 197 reversed several of Bill 108's changes not yet in force"],"a":1,"e":"Lot levies were not mentioned in the 1946 Act; suburbs used their subdivision approval powers to demand them, and the province confirmed them only in 1959. The other three statements are in the text."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to historical institutionalism as Sorensen uses it, which statements are true? I. Institutions are unequally distributive, produced by unequal power relations, and contested. II. Path dependence usually means lock-in until an external shock arrives. III. A crisis is by itself sufficient to produce meaningful institutional change. IV. Actors who gain from an institution tend to resist changes that weaken their advantage.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"I and IV are HI assumptions in the text. II is false because path dependence is usually not lock-in; III is false because permissive conditions need productive conditions as well. The first pattern is tempting for readers who equate path dependence with lock-in."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Sorensen's account of Toronto, which statements are true? I. The 1946 Planning Act was more limited than any proposal debated in the 1945 election. II. The City of Toronto collected development charges throughout the 1990s. III. Amalgamation in 1997 was a critical juncture for Toronto's governance. IV. Recent legislation kept density bonuses and development charges, in reduced form.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I, III and IV are in the text. II is false: Toronto first approved a policy to collect development charges in January 2001. Amalgamation was a juncture for governance, though not for value capture, which makes III a trap for careful readers."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Sorensen explains Toronto's value capture rules through critical junctures and continuous contest among organised actors. How would an evolutionary institutionalist such as Moroni critique this account?","o":["Frequent political rewriting of the rules destroys the information they provide; good rules evolve slowly","Formal statutes alone do not shape practice; rules work only once actors internalise their meaning","It underplays how each developer and council calculates payoffs and free-rides on collective goods","It treats local rules as autonomous, when they mainly serve capital accumulation and class power"],"a":0,"e":"Moroni's robustness and gradualism principles make constant rule changes a problem, and he sees good institutions as evolved rather than won in battles. The second option is a sociological critique, the third a rational choice and the fourth a Marxist one."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Sorensen focuses on formal rules, such as statutes on development charges, to explain institutional change. How would a sociological institutionalist critique this focus?","o":["Most rules are undesigned products of evolution, not outcomes of political battles at junctures","Taken-for-granted norms and meanings shape what planners and developers do, beyond any statute","The analysis should start from individual actors' strategic cost-benefit calculation instead","Local rules are a surface effect of global capital flows, so studying statutes misses the point"],"a":1,"e":"Sociological institutionalists (like Dembski & Salet) see institutions as internalised norms and cognitive scripts, so a focus on formal statutes misses how practices are shaped and reproduced. The first option is an evolutionary critique."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims fit Sorensen's use of Fligstein & McAdam's 'Strategic Action Field' for Toronto? I. The 1946 Planning Act established such a field and its rules of engagement. II. A growing set of actors pursued their goals while working within the existing rules. III. Even the radical recent changes kept the basic structure of density bonuses and development charges.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"All three are in Sorensen's conclusion: 1946 created the field, actors increasingly advocated within its rules, and recent changes altered amounts, timing and names but kept the basic tools. This is his evidence for a developmental pathway without lock-in."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Sorensen finds that each of Toronto's critical junctures for value capture coincided with a housing crisis. Statement II: Sorensen argues that the type of housing crisis decides whether value capture is strengthened or weakened.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: the postwar shortage, the late-1980s bubble and the current affordability crisis. II is false: the crises supplied permissive conditions, but differing productive conditions determined the outcome."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Sorensen argues that land value capture rules in Toronto have been continuously contested. Statement II: Sorensen holds that such rules shift the distribution of the costs and benefits of development between actors.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Because value capture rules redistribute costs and benefits, actors who gain or lose fight over them; Sorensen even reads the contest as evidence that the rules matter. II therefore explains I."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly describes how lot levies became part of Ontario's planning system after 1946?","o":["The Act gave discretion over subdivision, so suburbs demanded levies, so court cases led to a 1959 amendment","The Act required developers to pay levies, so suburbs collected them, so court cases led to a 1959 amendment","The Act gave discretion over subdivision, so suburbs demanded levies, so the courts banned them for good","The Act gave discretion over subdivision, so developers offered levies, so the province abolished them"],"a":0,"e":"The 1946 Act was ambiguous: it gave real discretion over subdivision but did not require subdividers to pay; suburbs used that leverage to demand levies, and after court challenges and OMB appeals the province confirmed them in 1959. The second chain breaks at the first link."},{"t":"sorensen","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Dembski & Salet criticise historical institutionalism for explaining continuity better than change. Which reply can be built from Sorensen's article?","o":["HI accepts this: institutions are rusty fixtures that change only by evolution","Critical junctures open windows for choice, and incremental change goes on between them","Change happens once audiences inculcate new symbolic meanings in their daily practice","Change is the sum of individual cost-benefit decisions to defect from existing rules"],"a":1,"e":"Sorensen's juncture-plus-reactive-sequence model explains change at junctures and constant incremental change in between, since path dependence is rarely lock-in. The third option is Dembski & Salet's own account of change, not a reply drawn from Sorensen."},{"t":"fog","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which theoretical tradition grounds Foglesong's account of why planning exists in capitalist cities?","o":["Evolutionary institutionalism in the line of Hayek","Marxist political economy and theory of the state","Communicative planning and deliberative democracy","Rational choice theory and public choice analysis"],"a":1,"e":"The course places Foglesong in Marxist political economy, with a structuralist theory of the capitalist state. Evolutionary institutionalism (Moroni) is the opposite view of property: it sees private property as a beneficial evolved institution, not a source of contradiction."},{"t":"fog","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which period of US planning history does Foglesong's 1986 book Planning the Capitalist City cover?","o":["The post-war suburban boom up to about 1970","The New Deal and the federal housing acts","From the colonial era up to the 1920s","The 1970s urban crisis up to the 1990s"],"a":2,"e":"The subtitle is 'The Colonial Era to the 1920s'; the course reading is an excerpt from that book. The New Deal option is tempting because it is also early federal planning, but it falls after the period the book covers."},{"t":"fog","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"What does an instrumentalist Marxist theory of the state claim, in the terms of the course material on Foglesong?","o":["The state is a tool that the capitalist class directly controls","The state serves the long-term collective interests of capital","The state is a neutral arbiter between all competing groups","The state is a self-interested actor that maximises its budget"],"a":0,"e":"Instrumentalism sees the state, and planning, as a tool directly controlled by capitalists. Serving capital's long-term collective interest, even against individual capitalists, is the structuralist (relative autonomy) view that Foglesong leans towards."},{"t":"fog","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"In the course comparison table, how does the liberal/pluralist view explain why planning exists, in contrast to Foglesong?","o":["Capitalists use the state as their tool","Contradictions of capitalist urbanisation","Class struggle over the means of production","The public interest and market failure"],"a":3,"e":"The table lists 'public interest; market failure' for the liberal/pluralist view, 'capitalists use the state as a tool' for instrumentalist Marxism, and 'contradictions of capitalist urbanisation' for Foglesong. The contradictions option is Foglesong's own answer, not the liberal one."},{"t":"fog","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Foglesong explains planning through two contradictions of capitalist urbanisation. Which pair is it?","o":["The town–country and the centre–periphery contradiction","The property and the capitalism–democracy contradiction","The use value–exchange value and the capital–labour one","The growth–equity and the equity–environment conflicts"],"a":1,"e":"The course material names the property contradiction (social character of land versus private ownership) and the capitalism–democracy contradiction. Growth–equity is the property conflict in Campbell's planner's triangle (PSC), which only borrows Foglesong's term."},{"t":"fog","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"The course illustrates Foglesong with US zoning in the 1910s–20s. Which feature of that zoning shows the capitalism–democracy contradiction?","o":["It limited what single owners could build on their land","It protected aggregate property values in whole districts","It was run by expert commissions, not popular bodies","It separated incompatible land uses like factories and homes"],"a":2,"e":"Running zoning through expert commissions rather than popular bodies kept land-use control away from democratic pressure. Restricting single owners to protect aggregate values shows the other contradiction, the property contradiction."},{"t":"fog","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"In 2021 a majority of Berliners voted to expropriate large landlords; the government then routed the issue through an expert commission and a planned framework law that delayed it. Which idea does the course link to this delay?","o":["The property contradiction, as land's social value is privately owned","The capitalism–democracy contradiction, contained through procedure","The instrumentalist view, as landlords directly ordered the delay","The legitimation function, as planning claims a neutral public interest"],"a":1,"e":"A democratic majority went further than private property allows and the state slowed it through procedure (a commission, a framework law): the capitalism–democracy contradiction. The property contradiction explains the pressure to socialise housing, not the delay; the course also notes the limit that this is about housing stock, not land-use planning."},{"t":"fog","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which situation best illustrates what Foglesong means by the 'social character' of urban land?","o":["An owner raises her plot's value by adding two floors with her own money","A plot gains value because the city opens a metro station right next to it","A buyer pays extra for a flat because she personally likes its quiet view","A landlord and a tenant freely agree on a rent in their private contract"],"a":1,"e":"Social character means land's value and use depend on collective investments and surrounding uses, not only on the owner's actions; a public metro station is a clear case. Adding floors is value created by the owner's own action, which is exactly what the concept contrasts with."},{"t":"fog","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which of the following is NOT a limit on planning in Foglesong's structuralist account, as summarised in the course table?","o":["Individual owners resist socialised control over their own land","Planning must not undermine private property rights as such","Capital fears that democratic majorities will seize control of land","Planners lack the technical knowledge to steer land use well"],"a":3,"e":"The table gives 'property rights; fear of democratic control' as the limits in Foglesong's view; resistance of individual owners is part of the property contradiction. Technical and knowledge limits are what the liberal/pluralist view stresses."},{"t":"fog","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How does Foglesong's view of agency differ from instrumentalist Marxism, according to the course comparison table?","o":["He leaves some room: class struggle shapes planning outcomes","He sees high agency, as many groups compete on roughly equal terms","He leaves no room at all, as the capitalists decide everything","He grants agency only to the planners acting as neutral experts"],"a":0,"e":"The table gives instrumentalist Marxism 'low' agency for non-capitalists and Foglesong 'some: class struggle shapes outcomes'. High agency with many competing groups is the liberal/pluralist position."},{"t":"fog","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Which pairing of a view and its role for the state matches the course comparison table?","o":["Liberal/pluralist view: a relatively autonomous servant of capital's needs","Instrumentalist Marxism: a neutral arbiter among all the social groups","Foglesong: relatively autonomous, serving capital's collective interest","Foglesong: a tool directly in the hands of the capitalist class"],"a":2,"e":"For Foglesong the state is relatively autonomous and serves capital's collective interest. The last option is the instrumentalist view, which Foglesong rejects because it cannot explain why capitalists often resisted planning."},{"t":"fog","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Critics say Foglesong's account risks 'functionalism'. What flaw does this charge point to?","o":["Ignoring how different the planning systems of countries are","Treating planning as a neutral, technical public-interest task","Reducing planning to the self-interest of planners themselves","Explaining planning simply by its usefulness to capital"],"a":3,"e":"Functionalism means explaining why something exists by the function it serves, here planning's usefulness to capital. Ignoring national differences is a separate (neo-institutionalist) critique, not what 'functionalism' refers to."},{"t":"fog","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which normative stance does the course material attribute to Foglesong?","o":["Defending private property as a beneficial institution that evolved","Critiquing planning's claim to neutrality and whose interests it serves","Calling for evidence-informed policy instead of evidence-based policy alone","Asking planners to develop 'justice literacy' about conceptions of the Just City"],"a":1,"e":"The course says Foglesong critiques planning's claim to neutrality, exposes whose interests it serves and implicitly sympathises with democratic and working-class control over land. Defending private property as an evolved institution is Moroni's position."},{"t":"fog","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"The PSC lecture on Campbell's planner's triangle borrows Foglesong's property contradiction. How is the contradiction phrased there?","o":["Growth and environmental protection can never be reconciled","Planners mediate between economy, environment and equity","The private sector both resists and needs social intervention","Property values always rise when environmental quality improves"],"a":2,"e":"The PSC material says that, following Foglesong, capitalist democracies treat property as a private commodity but rely on government to secure its social benefits, so the private sector both resists and needs social intervention. Mediating between the three corners is Campbell's planner role, not Foglesong's contradiction."},{"t":"fog","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Foglesong (as summarised in the course), which statements are true? I. The value of urban land depends partly on collective investments such as streets, sewers and transit. II. Capitalists and landowners uniformly welcomed planning because it served their interests. III. Early planning institutions were often insulated from direct democratic control. IV. Planning outcomes are fully determined by the needs of capital, leaving no role for political struggle.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV T"],"a":0,"e":"I and III are core claims (social character of land; insulation through commissions and boards). II is false: Foglesong rejects instrumentalism precisely because capitalists and landowners often resisted planning; IV is false because he leaves room for class struggle and contingency."},{"t":"fog","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Foglesong (as summarised in the course), which statements are true? I. The property contradiction is, at its core, the conflict between capital and labour in the workplace. II. Democratic control over land is a risk for capital because it could go further than capital wants. III. The state can serve capital's long-term collective interest even against individual capitalists. IV. Planning also helps legitimise capitalist urban development by presenting it as the public interest.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"II, III and IV match the capitalism–democracy contradiction, the structuralist view and the legitimation function. I is the classic trap: the property contradiction is between the social character of land and its private ownership and control, not between capital and labour."},{"t":"fog","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"How would a public choice theorist critique Foglesong's explanation of why planning exists?","o":["Planners and politicians pursue their own interests, such as budgets and careers, so planning reflects officials' goals.","Planning systems follow national pathways set at critical junctures, so a general theory of capitalism misses why they differ so much.","The social character of land gives no reason for central control, since prices and property rights coordinate dispersed knowledge better.","Planning outcomes come from deliberation among many competing groups, so reducing them to the interests of capital is one-sided."],"a":0,"e":"Public choice treats officials as self-interested (bureau-maximising), so it would explain planning by planners' and politicians' interests rather than capital's. The other options are the critiques of historical institutionalism, Hayekian liberalism and liberal pluralism or communicative theory."},{"t":"fog","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"How would a liberal pluralist or communicative planning theorist critique Foglesong?","o":["Because land is better coordinated by prices and secure property rights, the very idea of socialising control over land is mistaken.","Because officials maximise their own budgets and careers, it is the self-interest of planners, not capital, that drives planning.","Because many groups compete and deliberate over plans, explaining planning by its usefulness to capital risks functionalism.","Because public land ownership differs so much between countries, the form of planning cannot follow from capitalism in general."],"a":2,"e":"The course names this as the first critique: outcomes reflect many competing groups and deliberation, and Foglesong risks functionalism. The national-differences option is the neo-institutionalist critique (for example Dutch or Nordic public landownership), not the pluralist one."},{"t":"fog","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a port authority's plan for a logistics zone mainly serves shipping firms, but the plan's brochure frames it entirely as 'the public interest of all residents: jobs, clean air and safety'. The elected council approved it after open debate. Which concept from the course reading of Foglesong fits most precisely?","o":["The capitalism–democracy contradiction, as control is kept from voters","Legitimation, as development for capital is cast as public interest","The property contradiction, as land's social value is privately owned","The instrumentalist state, as shipping firms directly control the plan"],"a":1,"e":"Legitimation means planning helps legitimise capitalist development by presenting it as the public interest, which is what the brochure does. The capitalism–democracy option is tempting, but here the elected council debated and approved the plan, so nothing was insulated from democratic control."},{"t":"fog","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: In Foglesong's account, capitalists and landowners often resisted planning. Statement II: Socialising control over land threatens the rights and profits of individual owners and developers.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are in the course summary, and II gives the reason for I: the same socialisation that capital as a whole needs threatens individual owners, who therefore resist it. This is why planning is both necessary for and constrained by capitalism."},{"t":"fog","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Foglesong's capitalism–democracy contradiction concerns workers' lack of control over the production process. Statement II: Foglesong leans towards a structural, contradiction-based view of the state while leaving room for class struggle.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"I is false: the contradiction concerns the risk that democratic control over land, once handed to the state, goes further than capital accepts; workers' lack of control over production is closer to Marx's estranged labour. II correctly describes his position."},{"t":"fog","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reproduces Foglesong's capitalism–democracy contradiction?","o":["Land control is socialised via the state → in a democracy the state is open to popular pressure → majorities could push control beyond what capital accepts → planning is insulated in expert bodies","Land control is socialised via the state → capitalists directly control that state → majorities could push control beyond what capital accepts → planning is insulated in expert bodies","Land control is socialised via the state → in a democracy the state is open to popular pressure → experts know the public interest better than voters → planning is insulated in expert bodies","Land control is socialised via the state → in a democracy the state is open to popular pressure → majorities could push control beyond what capital accepts → planning is handed to elected assemblies"],"a":0,"e":"The danger for capital comes from democratic openness, so planning is shielded through commissions, technical procedures and independent boards. The second chain inserts instrumentalism (direct control), which would remove the danger; the third swaps the class logic for a technocratic justification; the fourth reverses the outcome."},{"t":"fog","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combining Foglesong and Taylor (2016): Melbourne landowners discredited expected windfalls and got the betterment charge (GAIC) shifted to purchasers. Which reading joins the two correctly?","o":["Landowners lost the struggle over betterment (property contradiction) as homebuyers organised as insiders through rent-seeking.","The state, run directly by landowners, adopted the GAIC as their tool (instrumentalism) and then passed it on to homebuyers.","Voters pushed control over land too far (capitalism–democracy contradiction), so landowners captured the expert planning bodies.","Individual owners resisted socialising land value (property contradiction) and won as organised insiders through rent-seeking."],"a":3,"e":"The course links Taylor's case to the property contradiction: a public claim on socially produced land value met resistance from private owners, and Taylor shows how they succeeded as rent-seeking insiders at the expense of outsider homebuyers. The option in which landowners lose reverses who the insiders were and who won."},{"t":"fog","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"The course reads the Falck site (Savini & Aalbers) through Foglesong as well. Which combination of the two readings is accurate?","o":["Falck disproves Foglesong, because financial owners welcomed every planning restriction that Sesto San Giovanni imposed on the site.","Falck shows the instrumentalist state: the municipality of Sesto San Giovanni was directly run by the banks that held the land deeds.","Falck's value hinged on public approval and infrastructure, yet distant financial owners decided over it: social land met financial ownership.","Falck shows the capitalism–democracy contradiction: residents' referendums blocked the masterplan, so planning was handed to experts."],"a":2,"e":"The course example says that in Falck the social character of land (public development rights, value capture) collides with private and financial ownership. Nothing in Savini & Aalbers shows banks running the municipality or referendums blocking the plan."},{"t":"harvey","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which city is the main case that underpins Harvey's three broad assertions about the new urban entrepreneurialism?","o":["Sheffield","New Orleans","Baltimore","Bologna"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey says his three assertions are true for Baltimore, the case underpinning the argument, and perhaps more general (p. 7). Sheffield is his British example of a Labour council endorsing local entrepreneurship, not the underlying case."},{"t":"harvey","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"According to Harvey, what did Baltimore's narrowly passed 1978 referendum make possible?","o":["A city income tax surcharge to pay for a new convention centre","Use of city land for the private development that became Harborplace","Transfer of the old port to a regional public development authority","A rent freeze for inner-city tenants affected by harbour renewal"],"a":1,"e":"The 1978 referendum sanctioned the use of city land for the private development that became Harborplace, giving public-private partnership a popular mandate (p. 7). The convention-centre tax is invented; Harvey uses the referendum to date the turn to partnership, not a new tax."},{"t":"harvey","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What did the cut in US federal redistribution after 1972 revive, according to Harvey?","o":["A strong tradition of local boosterism","Federally funded public housing programmes","Regional planning across city-suburb lines","Municipal ownership of urban building land"],"a":0,"e":"When federal redistribution was cut after 1972, boosterism revived so strongly that state and local governments were called 'the last entrepreneurs' (p. 4). Public housing is the opposite: it belongs to the redistributive 1960s that was being wound down."},{"t":"harvey","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"Harvey says that image-led urban entrepreneurialism opens up mechanisms of social control. Which Roman formula does he say is being reinvented?","o":["Divide and rule","Pax Romana","Patronage and clientelism","Bread and circuses"],"a":3,"e":"Harvey writes that 'bread and circuses' is the Roman formula that now stands to be reinvented and revived: spectacle and civic pride bind residents to place (p. 14). 'Divide and rule' sounds plausible but is not his term; his point is integration through image, not division."},{"t":"harvey","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Harvey suggests that urban entrepreneurialism may have played a role in a broader transition in capitalism. Which one?","o":["From flexible accumulation back to a Fordist-Keynesian regime","From a Fordist-Keynesian regime to flexible accumulation","From mercantile capitalism to industrial Fordist production","From state socialism to a neoliberal market economy"],"a":1,"e":"Harvey links the shift to a transition from a Fordist-Keynesian regime of accumulation to 'flexible accumulation' (p. 5) and later argues it facilitated that move (p. 12). The first option reverses the direction of the change."},{"t":"harvey","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Harvey notes that coalition-building leaves room for individuals with vision. Whom does he name as the charismatic mayor who stamped his mark on Baltimore?","o":["Mayor Koch of New York","Council leader Blunkett of Sheffield","Mayor Schaeffer of Baltimore","Mayor Daley of Chicago"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey names Mayor Schaeffer in Baltimore (and private entrepreneurs in Halifax or Gateshead) as individuals who could put their stamp on governance (pp. 6–7). Blunkett appears in the article, but as the Labour leader in Sheffield who endorsed local entrepreneurship."},{"t":"harvey","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Harvey warns against language that makes 'cities' seem like active agents. How does he want urbanisation to be understood instead?","o":["As the outcome of one coherent plan drawn up and carried out by city hall","As a spatially grounded social process in which many different actors interact","As a physical container that passively reflects national economic policy","As a land market that allocates sites to their best use without conflict"],"a":1,"e":"For Harvey, treating cities as agents reifies 'mere things'; urbanisation is 'a spatially grounded social process' in which actors with different agendas interact through spatial practices (p. 5). The city-hall option is exactly the reification he warns against: it treats one actor as 'the city'."},{"t":"harvey","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: a port city's waterfront strategy is drafted by the chamber of commerce, a university, two developers and a trade union, while the council mainly coordinates and facilitates. Which idea from Harvey does this illustrate?","o":["Urban managerialism: the council provides local services to its residents","Serial reproduction: the city copies a waterfront model from elsewhere","Urban governance: power lies in a coalition broader than government itself","Progressive urban corporatism: an alliance of cities against competition"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey insists that urban 'governance' means much more than 'government': the power to reorganise urban life lies in a broader coalition in which local government facilitates and coordinates (p. 6). Nothing in the case says the model was copied, so serial reproduction does not fit."},{"t":"harvey","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: a region's economy is kept afloat mainly by large defence contracts that its politicians win from the national government. Which of Harvey's four basic options does this region rely on?","o":["Competition for redistributions from central government","Competition within the spatial division of consumption","Competition for control and command functions","Competition within the international division of labour"],"a":0,"e":"The fourth option is competition for surplus redistributed by central (or US state) governments, such as military contracts that sustained Bristol and Long Beach-San Diego (p. 10). Defence firms produce goods, but the region's lever here is winning public money, not its production advantages."},{"t":"harvey","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Harvey contrasts 'territory' with 'place'. Which project is the clearest example of a territorial rather than a place-based intervention?","o":["A new civic centre and concert hall on the old harbour front","An industrial park on the edge of town with tax breaks for firms","A citywide programme improving schools and housing conditions","A festival marketplace that draws visitors from the whole region"],"a":2,"e":"Territorial projects such as housing and education are designed to improve conditions within a jurisdiction; place projects (a civic centre, an industrial park, a marketplace) have indirect benefits that may be wider or narrower than the territory (pp. 7–8). The industrial park is tempting because it targets jobs, but Harvey lists it as a place project."},{"t":"harvey","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why, according to Harvey, is entrepreneurial urban development speculative?","o":["Because developers deliberately inflate land prices before each sale","Because nobody can predict which package of projects will succeed","Because cities must borrow on stock markets to fund every project","Because national governments refuse to approve local plans in time"],"a":1,"e":"Harvey argues that speculation follows because no one knows in advance which package will succeed in inter-urban competition, producing up and down spirals (p. 11). Inflated land prices across repeated sales is Savini & Aalbers' Falck story, not Harvey's reason."},{"t":"harvey","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to Harvey, who mainly benefits from much of the US public-private partnership?","o":["The working class and poor, through better collective consumption","Small local firms, which gain access to cheap public land","Affluent consumers, corporations and command functions in town","Suburban homeowners, whose property taxes fall as a result"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey calls much of the partnership a subsidy to affluent consumers, corporations and command functions to stay in town, at the expense of collective consumption for the working class and poor (p. 12). The first option reverses his distributive verdict."},{"t":"harvey","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Harvey argues that localities become sites for regulating labour relations. Which mechanism does he point to?","o":["National unions gain power as cities compete for skilled workers","Federal minimum wages are raised to stop competition on labour costs","Cities recruit migrant labour through national quota programmes","Local replaces national bargaining, so local costs are easier to control"],"a":3,"e":"Local costs 'can most easily be controlled when local replaces national collective bargaining', and public institutions can lead with wage cuts, as in Baltimore in the 1970s (p. 9). The national-union option runs the other way: Harvey sees labour regulation moving down to the local scale."},{"t":"harvey","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which statement about the 1985 colloquium that Harvey uses as evidence is NOT correct?","o":["It brought together people from eight large cities in seven countries","Participants agreed that urban governments had to become more entrepreneurial","It was held in New Orleans, the US city Harvey calls a ward of the state","Participants disagreed mainly about how to be entrepreneurial, not whether"],"a":2,"e":"The colloquium was held in Orleans (France), not New Orleans; New Orleans appears separately as a city that remained a 'ward' of the federal government (pp. 4–5). The other three statements match Harvey's description of a broad consensus with disagreement only about the means."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a municipality subsidises a science park on its border. Most new jobs go to commuters from neighbouring towns, and the council's attention and budget shift away from deprived districts elsewhere in the region. Which idea from Harvey fits most precisely?","o":["Serial reproduction: the science park copies a model that cities everywhere adopt","Place over territory: benefits leak beyond the jurisdiction and divert attention","Collective consumption: the subsidy improves services that residents use together","Command functions: the city competes for headquarters that need costly infrastructure"],"a":1,"e":"Place-specific projects make benefits indirect and 'potentially either wider or smaller in scope than the jurisdiction', and they divert concern and resources from the region's broader problems (p. 8). Science parks do appear in Harvey's list of serially reproduced forms, but nothing in the case is about copying."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which statement most precisely captures Harvey's view of inter-urban competition early in the article?","o":["Inter-urban competition is always zero-sum, so progressive urban outcomes are impossible","Inter-urban competition is a beneficial hidden hand that raises welfare provision everywhere","If competition is zero-sum it disciplines even socialists, but it may open a non zero-sum path","Inter-urban competition stops mattering once a progressive coalition takes power locally"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey warns that zero-sum competition makes even avant-garde municipal socialists play the capitalist game, but adds that under the right circumstances competition 'may open the way to a non zero-sum pattern of development' (p. 5). 'Always zero-sum' overstates him and ignores this opening."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A policy analyst writes: 'Nobody ordered our city to build a casino and marina; we chose them ourselves. But once five rival cities did the same, the market decided which of us survived.' Which idea in Harvey (1989) grounds this passage?","o":["The local state is simply captive of capital, which dictates every project in advance","Competition is a beneficial hidden hand that rewards cities offering the best services","Coalitions build a stable 'power to' govern through resource interdependence with business","The coercive laws of competition discipline cities after the event, not before it"],"a":3,"e":"Harvey follows Marx: competition is the 'bearer' of capitalist relations, but 'the forcing occurs after rather than before' the action, so cities experiment and are sorted afterwards (p. 15). The captive-state option is the view he explicitly distances himself from."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with Harvey's conceptual groundwork on urbanisation?","o":["Built forms and institutions produced by urbanisation constrain later social action","Urban governance is wider than government: coalitions hold the power to reorganise space","Because capitalism is class-bound, every spatial practice must be read in class terms","Under capitalism, class practices tied to the circulation of capital remain hegemonic"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey says spatial practices acquire a class content, 'which is not to say that all spatial practices be so interpreted': they also have gender, racial and bureaucratic contents, although class practices remain hegemonic (p. 5). The other statements match his process-thing dialectic and his governance concept."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim? 'A harbour festival gives residents civic pride and a sense of place-bound identity, but that image also works as a mechanism of social control, even when living conditions decline.'","o":["Dembski & Salet (2010)","Savini & Aalbers (2016)","Stefano Moroni (2010)","David Harvey (1989)"],"a":3,"e":"Harvey argues that urban image creates solidarity and civic pride and opens 'a range of mechanisms for social control'; in Baltimore 'the circus succeeds even if the bread is lacking' (pp. 13–14). Dembski & Salet also study symbols, but as markers that can institute new meaning, not as a critique of social control."},{"t":"harvey","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Harvey does not claim that entrepreneurial urban governance is automatically captive to capitalist class interests. Statement II: In his view, competition disciplines cities only after the event, so they can experiment speculatively before winners and losers are sorted.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Harvey grants local governance real room to explore different paths, and explains how this autonomy is consistent with capital accumulation: the coercive laws of competition force agents 'after the event rather than before' (pp. 14–15). So II is the reason why autonomy (I) does not contradict his Marxist theory."},{"t":"harvey","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Harvey argues that inter-urban competition tends to push down social responsibility and welfare provision. Statement II: Harvey sees inter-urban competition as a beneficial hidden hand that rewards efficient cities.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"Competition operates 'not as a beneficial hidden hand, but as an external coercive law forcing the lowest common denominator of social responsibility and welfare provision' (p. 12). So I is true and II is precisely the reading Harvey rejects."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about Harvey's four basic options are correct? I. The options are not mutually exclusive, and cities combine them. II. Uneven urban growth can come from synergy between options, as in Los Angeles. III. Command functions require expensive infrastructure such as airports and teleports, and established centres hold strong monopoly power.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I, II and III","I and III only"],"a":2,"e":"All three match pp. 8–10: the options are combinable, Los Angeles grew through interaction of defence, command and consumption, and command functions need costly infrastructure where New York and London have monopoly power. Students who think command functions are open to any city miss the monopoly point in III."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of Harvey's argument would a Foucauldian governmentality scholar such as Raco & Imrie (2000) most plausibly make?","o":["Competition is less an external law than a rationality made real through discourses, programmes and techniques that shape how councils think","National land, fiscal and legal institutions create path-dependent varieties of urban governance that a general theory of capital overlooks","Competition between cities usefully disciplines wasteful local governments, since residents and firms can vote with their feet","Local actors build a stable 'power to' govern through resource interdependence, so coalitions have more room than Harvey admits"],"a":0,"e":"Governmentality treats 'the competitive city' as a constructed rationality, made thinkable and governable through programmes and technologies, rather than as an objective coercive law. The path-dependence critique is plausible too, but it belongs to historical institutionalism, not to governmentality."},{"t":"harvey","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reproduces Harvey's argument about serial reproduction?","o":["Falling transport costs make small place differences matter less, so cities stop competing and copy each other, which stabilises the urban system","Cities copy successful projects, and because every city then has one, benefits spread evenly and instability declines across the urban system","Mobile capital sharpens competition, so cities copy successful projects, so any competitive advantage becomes ephemeral and the system unstable","Competition pushes cities to invent unique projects that no rival can copy, so their competitive advantage becomes durable over time"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey argues that competition forces 'serial reproduction' of trade centres, waterfronts and malls; imitation makes advantage ephemeral and the urban system more unstable (pp. 10–13). The first chain gets the first link wrong: lower transport costs make small differences between places matter more, not less."},{"t":"hh","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Roughly what share of its land area does the City of Helsinki own, according to Hyötyläinen & Haila?","o":["About 25%","About 40%","About 60%","About 85%"],"a":2,"e":"Helsinki owns 60% of its land area (p. 138, fn 3), which is why the shift from leasing to selling matters so much. The other figures are invented; the point is that Finnish cities are very large landowners."},{"t":"hh","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"What was the Eiranranta area used for before it was rezoned as residential in 2001?","o":["A naval harbour with military barracks and storage depots","Light industry, waste water management and a city depot","A public beach and park used by the old working-class district","A 1960s social housing estate due for demolition and renewal"],"a":1,"e":"Eiranranta was a rocky shore that from the 1930s to the early 2000s served light industry, waste water management and a city depot, and was rezoned residential in 2001 (p. 140). The public beach and park lie next to the area today; they are not what was built over."},{"t":"hh","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Who was the Deputy Mayor, an economist, who promoted selling Helsinki's shoreline land to maximise revenue?","o":["Juha Sipilä, the Prime Minister","Pekka Korpinen","The director of the Premises Centre","A board member of the developer Skanska"],"a":1,"e":"Pekka Korpinen, Deputy Mayor from 1991 to 2007 and an economist, brought an economist's mindset and even renamed the area with marketing in mind (p. 140). Sipilä appears in the article, but as the Prime Minister whose slogan was that public assets must be put to work."},{"t":"hh","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which politically elected body decided to sell the Eiranranta sites, and whose members did the authors interview?","o":["The Premises Centre's board","The Real Estate Committee (REC)","The Ministry of the Interior","The National Board of Public Building"],"a":1,"e":"The Real Estate Committee, elected by the City Council, decided in 2004 to sell the sites; the authors interviewed five of its nine members (pp. 137–138, 140). The Premises Centre, founded in 2005, manages city real estate and charges market rent, but it did not take the sale decision."},{"t":"hh","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which slogan did Prime Minister Sipilä add to 'the necessity to save'?","o":["There is no alternative to the market","Public assets have to be put to work","Every euro of public land counts twice","Land belongs to those who build on it"],"a":1,"e":"'The necessity to save' got a new formulation, 'public assets have to be put to work', meaning state land and real estate should be assessed by market value (p. 139). 'There is no alternative' is a familiar neoliberal slogan, but it is not the one the authors quote."},{"t":"hh","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why were Helsinki's politicians so keen to attract 'good taxpayers' from Espoo and Kauniainen?","o":["Corporate tax is the main source of municipal revenue in Finland","Property tax on luxury homes funds most social services in Finland","Income tax is the main source of revenue for Finnish municipalities","State subsidies are allocated according to residents' average income"],"a":2,"e":"Income tax is the main source of revenue for Finnish municipalities, so attracting high earners pays off directly (p. 140, fn 12). Corporate tax is the trap: the state redistributes it, so Helsinki even loses corporate tax revenue to less successful municipalities (fn 13)."},{"t":"hh","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why, according to the authors, had leasing land served Finnish cities well?","o":["It kept planning power with town planners and helped avoid glaring inequalities","It raised more revenue than selling, because ground rents rose with land values","It let developers capture value increases, which kept new housing cheap to buy","It allowed the state to ratify every local plan, so conflicts were settled nationally"],"a":0,"e":"Leasing ensured cities' control over land use, and keeping planning power with town planners helped create a socially sustainable, heterogeneous urban fabric without glaring inequalities (p. 138). The revenue option misreads the argument: the case for leasing is control, not income."},{"t":"hh","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"When Helsinki adopted a 'strategic' approach in the 2000s, what was land policy expected to do?","o":["Guarantee social housing in every neighbourhood of the metropolitan area","Enhance the 'innovative' capacity of firms, for example via science parks","Return land to the state so that national mediation could be restored","Keep land prices low so that household developers could build cheaply"],"a":1,"e":"Land policy became a strategic tool to enhance the 'innovative' capacity of firms; the city was keen on science parks for Nokia spin-offs (p. 140). Social housing everywhere was the metropolitan social-mix goal that Eiranranta later contradicted, not the aim of the strategic approach."},{"t":"hh","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to one REC member, why did developers and firms push the city to sell rather than lease land?","o":["Leaseholds could not legally be used for owner-occupied flats in Finland","EU competition rules banned cities from leasing land below market value","Anglo-American companies will not move to a city that does not let them own land","Banks refused to lend to developers who built on municipal leasehold land"],"a":2,"e":"Developers insisted there was demand for luxury housing, and an REC member said Anglo-American companies will not move to a city if they are not allowed to own the land (p. 141). The EU and bank options sound technical but appear nowhere in the article."},{"t":"hh","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which kind of concentration did REC members regard as a problem for Helsinki's social-mix policy?","o":["Concentrations of social housing tenants, as in Jakomäki","Concentrations of wealthy owners in shoreline enclaves","Concentrations of offices that empty the centre at night","Concentrations of foreign investors in the housing market"],"a":0,"e":"REC members supported mixing when it meant fighting concentrations of social housing (Kontula, Mellunmäki, Jakomäki), but not concentrations of wealth: 'Not every place needs to be mixed' (pp. 141–142). The wealthy-enclave option is what the authors see as a problem, not the REC members."},{"t":"hh","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which statement about Eiranranta is NOT consistent with Hyötyläinen & Haila?","o":["The master plan required 1.2 m fences and gated entrances","The area was built without any social housing at all","Adverts stressed that no common laundries were needed","It is a large-scale flagship project with striking architecture"],"a":3,"e":"The authors stress that, unlike typical neoliberal projects, Eiranranta is not a large-scale urban development project and has no eye-catching architecture; it even seems designed to be inconspicuous (pp. 142–143). Fences, no social housing and 'no common laundries' are all in the text."},{"t":"hh","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How do the authors contrast the Finnish state with the British state?","o":["The UK state sold its land to Finnish investors, while Finland kept all state land","In the UK the state enabled others to treat land financially; in Finland the state did so itself","The UK state kept planning power central, while Finland abolished planning altogether","Both states treated land as a financial asset in the same way and at the same time"],"a":1,"e":"Unlike the UK, where the state did not itself treat its land as a financial asset but enabled it by selling to actors who do, the Finnish state became an actor treating land 'according to the rent it yields' (p. 143). The 'same way' option erases exactly the contrast the authors call unique."},{"t":"hh","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"What risk do the authors see in letting an investment company such as Apollo into Helsinki's development market?","o":["Established household developers could form a cartel and refuse to bid again","The city could lose its planning monopoly to the state real estate companies","More investment firms and REITs could follow, making real estate a pure financial asset","Foreign buyers could leave flats empty, which would lower the city's tax income"],"a":2,"e":"If one investment company is followed by more and by REITs, this may facilitate 'creating liquidity out of spatial fixity' (Gotham) and real estate treated 'as a pure financial asset' (Harvey) (p. 144). The cartel concern is real but concerns the established developers before Apollo, not the risk the authors draw."},{"t":"hh","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"The newspaper Helsingin Sanomat asked why land should be given at a discount to developers of rental flats. How do the authors read this argument?","o":["As proof that leasing costs the city more than it earns","As a popular prejudice against the public sector","As an accurate call for transparency in land policy","As a sign that the media opposed selling public land"],"a":1,"e":"The paper argued that selling at the highest price would be smarter for taxpayers; the authors call this 'the popular prejudice against the public sector' and note the media supported selling off public land (p. 139). The last option reverses the media's stance."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a city keeps ownership of its land but announces that ground rents for existing housing leaseholds will rise step by step to full market level, to close a budget gap. Which concept from Hyötyläinen & Haila fits most precisely?","o":["Privatisation of development rights, because private owners gain the plan's value","Entrepreneurial public real estate policy, in its form of charging market rent","A development contract between the municipality and a private landowner","The welfare city, because the city now funds social services from its budget"],"a":1,"e":"Entrepreneurial public real estate policy takes two forms: selling public land or charging market rent when leasing (p. 137); the authors note that announcements to raise rents intervene in tenants' everyday life (p. 143). Privatising development rights requires selling sites, which does not happen here."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which statement most precisely reflects how the authors explain why REC members did not see Eiranranta as a warning sign of segregation?","o":["REC members deliberately designed Eiranranta to push poor households out of the centre","REC members rejected Helsinki's social-mix policy and wanted it abolished altogether","A plausible answer is their focus on real estate issues, which left them ignorant or negligent","A survey of residents proved that REC members had personal interests in the luxury flats"],"a":2,"e":"The authors offer 'a plausible answer': REC members' focus on real estate issues; they were 'either ignorant of, or negligent about, the dangers of segregation' (p. 142). The first option overstates intent, and the second contradicts the fact that members still backed mixing against social housing concentrations."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A ministry circular argues: 'From now on we will no longer finance all municipal services. Cities will draw up their own urban programmes, compete for selective funding and, ideally, work with private partners.' Which change described by Hyötyläinen & Haila does this passage express?","o":["The new state urban policy that made cities compete for subsidies","The Land Use and Building Act that ended state ratification of plans","The founding of state real estate companies such as Senaatti","The Premises Centre charging city departments market rent"],"a":0,"e":"From the mid-1990s the Ministry of the Interior asked cities to draw up urban programmes and funded only some, making them compete and encouraging public-private partnerships (p. 139). The MRL is another of the three scaling-down changes, but it concerns plan ratification and development contracts, not subsidies."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about the Finnish state's role is NOT consistent with Hyötyläinen & Haila?","o":["The state introduced entrepreneurial real estate policy before the City of Helsinki","In 1995 state real estate companies replaced the National Board of Public Building","The MRL removed the obligation for cities to have their plans ratified by the state","Its real estate companies kept the state an impartial mediator between city interests"],"a":3,"e":"The authors argue the opposite: by founding real estate companies and becoming 'just one player in the real estate market, the state lost its impartiality' (p. 143). The other three statements match pp. 138–139."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course author(s) would endorse this claim? 'When advertisements boast that residents need no common laundries, one more piece of common, collective property is being converted into private space.'","o":["Hyötyläinen & Haila (2018)","Bossuyt & D'Ottaviano (2025)","Savini & Aalbers (2016)","Pruijt (2003)"],"a":0,"e":"Hyötyläinen & Haila note that common laundries historically mattered in Finnish urban culture and read their replacement by private housekeeping space as another conversion of common rights into private property (p. 143). Bossuyt & D'Ottaviano also write about commons and enclosure, but about occupied housing in São Paulo, not this Helsinki detail."},{"t":"hh","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Hyötyläinen & Haila conclude that Eiranranta is a gated community. Statement II: Eiranranta's residents pay city taxes, use public services, and the fences still allow right of passage.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"The authors call Eiranranta an exclusive compound and possibly a 'privatopia', but explicitly do not regard it as a gated community, precisely because of the facts in II (p. 143). II is therefore true, and it is their reason for rejecting I."},{"t":"hh","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: According to Hyötyläinen & Haila, selling the Eiranranta sites also meant privatising development rights. Statement II: In Finland the right to assign development rights belongs by law to the city, so the value increase from development belongs to it.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Because development rights and the resulting value increase belong to the city (the planning monopoly, p. 138), selling sites with those rights to developers privatises them; 'privatising the municipal land can also mean privatising development rights' (p. 143). II explains why I follows."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"How did the four justifications fare in Eiranranta, according to the authors? I. Transparency: bid prices were published, but the key issue of who captures land rent was sidelined. II. Diversity: the sale produced a homogeneous enclave for high net-worth residents. III. Efficiency: it meant a social cost-benefit comparison of alternative uses for the site.","o":["I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III","I and II only"],"a":3,"e":"I and II match the authors' account (pp. 141–142). III is wrong: in practice 'efficiency' meant that land should go to its highest paying alternative, as the tender's highest-offer rule shows (p. 140); no cost-benefit comparison of uses was made."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of Hyötyläinen & Haila would a historical institutionalist such as Sorensen most plausibly make?","o":["Market prices reveal the opportunity cost of land, so selling simply makes hidden public subsidies visible to voters","The shift is path-dependent: the 1990s recession opened a critical juncture, and Finland's land regime shaped what followed","The REC failed mainly as a deliberative forum: excluded groups had no voice, so the remedy is better communicative design","Property is an evolved institution that coordinates dispersed knowledge, so public landownership itself is the anomaly"],"a":1,"e":"A historical institutionalist would stress sequences, critical junctures and how national land institutions mediate neoliberalism, rather than a general capitalist logic. The price-signal critique is the rational choice/public choice view (the 'apologists' the authors cite), not historical institutionalism."},{"t":"hh","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reproduces how the authors link the MRL to the Eiranranta enclave?","o":["MRL required state ratification of all plans, so the state blocked the sale and the city had to lease the land instead","MRL ended state ratification and legalised disconnected development, so the sale could create an enclave against social-mix policy","MRL imposed social housing quotas on all new plans, so developers protested and the city sold the land to an investor instead","MRL gave tenants a veto over land sales, so the REC had to hold a referendum, which narrowly approved the shoreline sale"],"a":1,"e":"The MRL (1999) ended state ratification and legalised development areas and contracts; Eiranranta was treated as a disconnected project, and 'MRL made it possible to develop separate enclaves', which the sale realised (pp. 139, 142–143). The first chain reverses what the law did."},{"t":"savini","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Under which municipality's jurisdiction does the Falck site in Savini & Aalbers fall?","o":["Monza, north of Milan","Rho, west of Milan","Sesto San Giovanni","Bicocca, in Milan"],"a":2,"e":"The 150 ha Falck area lies in the middle of Sesto San Giovanni, a city of over 80,000 inhabitants in Milan's industrial belt (p. 7). Bicocca is tempting because it is mentioned, but it is the adjacent area developed by Pirelli, not the site's municipality."},{"t":"savini","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which architectural firm coordinated the masterplan that Risanamento proposed for the Falck site in 2006–07?","o":["Renzo Piano Architects","Zaha Hadid Architects","Stefano Boeri Architetti","OMA (Rem Koolhaas)"],"a":0,"e":"The Risanamento plan was coordinated by Renzo Piano Architects and almost doubled the built surface compared with the previous plan (pp. 10–11). The other firms are well-known names in Milan or elsewhere but do not appear in the article."},{"t":"savini","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"In Savini & Aalbers, what is the 'generator company' of a special-purpose vehicle?","o":["The bank that lends the SPV the money to buy the land","The development consortium or holding behind the SPV","The municipal agency that negotiates the SPV's plan","The architectural firm that draws the SPV's masterplan"],"a":1,"e":"SPVs are connected to a generator company, which can be a development consortium or a holding; the SPV links the project's gains back to it (p. 6). Banks finance SPVs, for example Banca Intesa, but they are not the generator."},{"t":"savini","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Savini & Aalbers say they are 'more skeptical' than one scholar that municipalities can use financial markets for their own agenda. Who?","o":["Harvey (1982)","Fainstein (2001)","Gotham (2006)","Weber (2010)"],"a":3,"e":"Weber (2010) argues that local governments are not passive recipients of market directives and can push their own agenda, as in Chicago; the authors fear Chicago may be the exception (pp. 2, 15). Harvey is their main theoretical source, not the scholar they are sceptical of."},{"t":"savini","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which Milan project do Savini & Aalbers give as an example of a corporation NOT specialised in real estate turning its land into development?","o":["Hines at the Garibaldi station area","Pirelli at the Bicocca area","Risanamento at Santa Giulia","EuroMilano at the Bovisa area"],"a":1,"e":"Pirelli, an industrial group, developed its own land at Bicocca, part of the trend of non-real-estate corporations owning land in key locations (p. 9). Hines is a large investment group, so it belongs to the specialised financial sector rather than this trend."},{"t":"savini","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What condition was attached to Sesto Immobiliare's purchase of the Falck land in 2010?","o":["The region would first pay for a new campus for health care","At least 30% of the total floor space would be social housing","The municipality would refund the full soil remediation costs","Approval of a plan of at least 962,000 m² within two years"],"a":3,"e":"The €405 million purchase was made on the condition that the municipality would approve the masterplan within two years with at least 962,000 m² of built surface (p. 12). The regional campus investment (€323 million) came later, as part of the 2011 approval."},{"t":"savini","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which data source did Savini & Aalbers NOT use for the Falck case?","o":["City council decisions on the Falck project","Twenty interviews with involved stakeholders","Economic sections of the proposed masterplans","A household survey among Sesto's residents"],"a":3,"e":"The authors combine council decisions, 20 stakeholder interviews, discussion groups, designers' and managers' documents, the economic prospects of masterplans, attorney general documents and legal appeals (p. 8). No resident survey is mentioned."},{"t":"savini","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: a developer fixes the number and type of apartments from a yield model before any study of local housing needs, builds them and only then looks for buyers. Which feature described by Savini & Aalbers does this show?","o":["Investment relocation towards riskier peripheral brownfields","Supply-driven programming: calculate, build and then sell","Value capture through stringent municipal land regulation","Clustering of several projects into one larger package"],"a":1,"e":"The authors note that programmes tend to be supply-driven, following a logic of 'calculate-build and then sell' that reduces the variety of local markets and social demands (p. 6). Nothing in the case concerns the site's location or bundling, so relocation and clustering do not fit."},{"t":"savini","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which situation is an example of what Savini & Aalbers call instrumental land use planning?","o":["A holding sells its project company to a new buyer along with its debt","An investor moves from downtown renewal to cheaper peripheral land","A council allows extra volume and new uses to get a stalled project going","A firm bundles several sites into one package to spread its demand risk"],"a":2,"e":"Instrumental planning means planning geared to facilitating investment; in Falck public planning aimed to activate the project 'as soon as possible' by allowing higher volumes and different functions (p. 14). Selling an SPV with its debt is financial layering, not a planning choice."},{"t":"savini","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why do smaller, economically weaker municipalities tend to rely on regulatory tools to capture land value, according to Savini & Aalbers?","o":["They have only a shallow knowledge of land markets","National law forbids them to own building land","Investors prefer to deal with the regional level","Regulation is the cheapest way to raise more taxes"],"a":0,"e":"Local governments, especially smaller and weaker ones, 'have a shallow knowledge of land markets' and rely on regulation to ensure part of the added value goes to public investment (p. 7). Nothing in the text says national law forbids municipal land ownership; the authors even list public-led land readjustment as an option."},{"t":"savini","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"What made the 2000–2005 construction for the Falck site a 'classic' one, in contrast to later phases?","o":["Many small shareholders held the SPV, with land deeds owned by creditor banks","One real estate firm fully controlled the SPV, with a mortgage loan and local ties","A public-private agency led by the municipality financed it with EU programmes","A national holding clustered it with other projects and funded it by issuing stock"],"a":1,"e":"The 'classic' construction was an SPV fully controlled by one subject specialised in real estate, drawing on mortgage banking, with a significant down payment and strong local connections (p. 10). Clustering with other projects and issuing stock describes Risanamento after 2005."},{"t":"savini","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why, according to Savini & Aalbers, did Risanamento's 2006 plan propose a much denser programme?","o":["Sesto demanded far more social housing and public facilities in return","Higher profits were needed to cover larger financial burdens","The region required a denser plan near the railway line","The housing market had collapsed and prices had dropped"],"a":1,"e":"The denser plan was expected to ensure higher profits, which were needed to cover larger financial burdens; the expected gain was 15% on about €5 billion (p. 11). Public facilities were part of the plan, but the authors explain the density by financial needs, not municipal demands."},{"t":"savini","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"Savini & Aalbers say the 2009 bankruptcy procedure against Risanamento 'clearly marks' something. What?","o":["The start of the city's proactive public-private partnership","The end of the clustering trend in the wider Milan region","The first time a foreign investor entered the Falck project","A detachment between land markets and planning negotiations"],"a":3,"e":"The event marks a point of detachment between land market dynamics and planning negotiations, with planning becoming dependent on decisions made in financial markets (p. 12). The city never chose a proactive partnership in the later phases; it regulated and waited."},{"t":"savini","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the three trends of the Milan real estate market that Savini & Aalbers take from Pasqui (2007)?","o":["A specialised financial sector in large-scale redevelopment","Non-real-estate firms owning land in key urban locations","Institutional actors such as universities activating sites","Foreign sovereign funds buying up downtown office towers"],"a":3,"e":"The three trends are a specialised financial sector, an active role of corporations not specialised in real estate, and a larger role for institutional actors such as universities and health care companies (p. 9). Sovereign funds buying offices are not mentioned."},{"t":"savini","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Savini & Aalbers, which statements are true? I. Land is increasingly used as collateral to create credit that is invested elsewhere. II. Most municipalities can mobilise financial markets to their benefit, as Weber showed for Chicago. III. Overregulation might disengage investors, generate long-term vacancies or promote speculation. IV. Programmes in the SPV logic tend to be supply-driven: calculate, build and then sell.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"I (pp. 6, 14–15), III (p. 7) and IV (p. 6) are in the text. II is false: the authors are more sceptical than Weber and fear that Chicago may be the exception, since many local governments, including Sesto, cannot use financialisation to their benefit (p. 15)."},{"t":"savini","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Savini & Aalbers' account of the Falck case, which statements are true? I. In the 1990s the site was handled by a development agency in which the municipality held the majority share. II. In the later phases Sesto San Giovanni chose a proactive public-private partnership. III. The expected return on investment rose with every change of owner. IV. The 2010 buyer paid the €405 million mostly in cash.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":1,"e":"Only I is true: PSTA was owned 80% by the municipality (p. 10). The city regulated and waited instead (p. 14); expected returns went from 9% to 15% and then down to 12% (Table 1); and a large share of the €405 million was a transfer of land ownership to the banks, not cash (p. 12)."},{"t":"savini","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"How would a neo-institutionalist most plausibly critique Savini & Aalbers' explanation of the Falck gridlock?","o":["The gridlock may owe more to Italy's negotiation regime, weak municipal capacity and alleged bribery than to finance as such.","A long vacancy signals rules that block price signals; with fewer municipal conditions the market would have found a viable use.","Value capture is a bargaining game, so the outcome follows from each player's pay-offs and the credibility of their commitments.","Other cities such as Chicago steer financial markets, so the decisive factor is whether a strong local coalition takes the lead."],"a":0,"e":"The course's commentary angle: a neo-institutionalist would point to Italian institutions (the PII negotiation regime, events under bribery investigation, weak municipal capacity) and to the single extreme case. The game-theoretic option is the rational choice critique, which explains outcomes by pay-offs rather than by institutions."},{"t":"savini","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"How would a rational choice theorist most plausibly critique Savini & Aalbers' account of value capture in the Falck case?","o":["Rules that block price signals are the real cause; less regulation would have let the market find a use for the brownfield.","Embedded national institutions and practices, not finance, explain why Sesto and its developers could not reach agreement.","It is a bargaining game: the outcome follows from the actors' pay-offs and whether threats and promises were credible.","Urban regimes differ: in cities with a strong local coalition, financial markets are steered to serve the city's own agenda."],"a":2,"e":"Rational choice and game theory would model value capture as strategic bargaining (the paper itself cites Samsura and van der Krabben) and explain the gridlock by pay-offs and credible commitments. The first option is the Hayekian liberal critique, which the authors partly anticipate when they admit that overregulation may disengage investors (p. 7)."},{"t":"savini","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a medium-sized Dutch city faces a large brownfield owned by an investment vehicle. Instead of only setting conditions and waiting, it buys part of the land through a public-private development company and services it itself, while keeping strict zoning. Which idea from Savini & Aalbers does this embody?","o":["Instrumental planning that facilitates the influx of finance","Value capture by issuing project bonds backed by future tax gains","Strong regulation combined with proactive public intervention","Clustering of public and private investments to spread risk"],"a":2,"e":"The authors conclude that governing property-led development requires strong regulation combined with proactive capacity to intervene in land development, the opposite of Sesto's 'regulate and wait'. Instrumental planning is what they criticise: planning bent to unlock capital rather than steer it."},{"t":"savini","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Savini & Aalbers present Falck as one particular extreme case in the Milan region. Statement II: The authors argue that a gridlocked negotiation is the inevitable outcome under financial capitalism.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is in the conclusion ('one particular extreme case', p. 14). II is false: 'We do not suggest that this is an inevitable outcome under financial capitalism'; the complexity of financial constructions and the regulatory position of public authorities differentiate outcomes (p. 15)."},{"t":"savini","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Risanamento's 2006 plan for the Falck site aimed at a higher expected gain than the 2002 plan. Statement II: According to the authors, higher profits were needed to cover larger financial burdens.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"The 2002 plan expected 9%, the 2006 plan 15% (Table 1), and the authors explain the denser, more profitable plan by the need to cover larger financial burdens (p. 11). So II gives the reason for I."},{"t":"savini","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reproduces why value capture becomes harder under financialisation, according to Savini & Aalbers?","o":["Investment decisions detach from land use decisions → cities poorly understand the economic calculations → they cannot judge what programme is viable → they lean on regulation in a weak bargaining position","Investment decisions detach from land use decisions → cities fully understand the economic calculations → they cannot judge what programme is viable → they lean on regulation in a weak bargaining position","Investment decisions detach from land use decisions → cities poorly understand the economic calculations → they cannot judge what programme is viable → they take over the projects as active developers","Investment decisions merge with land use decisions → cities poorly understand the economic calculations → they cannot judge what programme is viable → they lean on regulation in a weak bargaining position"],"a":0,"e":"When investment is detached from land use, cities have a poor understanding of the calculations and cannot define a viable programme; weaker municipalities therefore rely on regulation (pp. 6–7). The chain ending in active development reverses Sesto's actual response, which was to regulate and wait."},{"t":"savini","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combining Harvey (1989) and Savini & Aalbers: who carries the risk of large urban projects?","o":["In both, the local public sector carries the risk because it acts as the main developer and investor of the projects","In Harvey the private side carries the risk; in Falck the small city absorbed it through an active partnership","In both, the risk is spread evenly among public and private partners through formal public-private contracts","In Harvey the public sector absorbs the risk; in Falck the small city avoided it but lost its bargaining power"],"a":3,"e":"Harvey's speculative public-private partnership lets the public sector absorb risk while the private sector takes the benefits; in Falck a public partnership was not viable for a small city, so it regulated and waited while risk sat in layered SPVs, and its bargaining power weakened (p. 14). The second option reverses both readings."},{"t":"savini","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Hyötyläinen & Haila and Savini & Aalbers both show urban land becoming a financial asset. Which statement combines the two cases correctly?","o":["In Helsinki the city itself sells land to maximise revenue; in Sesto the city owns no site but faces distant layered investors","In Helsinki distant layered investors own the land; in Sesto the city itself sells public land to the highest bidder","In both cases the city acts as a proactive developer that steers financial investors towards its own local agenda","In both cases national law forces the city to sell its land, which ends municipal control over the planning process"],"a":0,"e":"Hyötyläinen & Haila show Helsinki as a public landowner adopting entrepreneurial real estate policy, while in Falck the land was privately owned and sold four times among financial owners, with the municipality only regulating. The second option swaps the two cases."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which movement organised the Dandara and Marisa Letícia occupations?","o":["The ULC, a tenement movement within the UMM umbrella","A municipal housing company acting for São Paulo's council","An international NGO campaigning for the right to housing","A landless rural workers' movement active outside the city"],"a":0,"e":"The ULC (founded 1991) is part of the Union of Housing Movements (UMM, 1987); it is less confrontational and frames occupation through citizenship and social rights. The cases are urban, central-city occupations, not rural land struggles."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"What was the Dandara building before it was occupied?","o":["A former state court building in the central Sé district","A former textile factory that once housed 2,000 occupiers","A warehouse site of the national social security institute","A former hotel close to the main railway station of the city"],"a":0,"e":"Dandara is a retrofit of a vacant former state court on Avenida Ipiranga in Sé. The INSS warehouse site is Marisa Letícia; the textile factory is the Prestes Maia building."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"From whom do the authors take the term 'ideation'?","o":["De Angelis","Stavrides","Varvarousis","Dardot and Laval"],"a":2,"e":"Ideation is Varvarousis' term for commons ideas formed during a liminal occupation that materialise later elsewhere. De Angelis supplies the tripartite commons and 'structural coupling', not ideation."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"What did the 2018 fire in the occupied Wilton Paes de Almeida building lead the ULC to do?","o":["Stop occupying buildings and lobby only through formal channels","Demand that the state buy every occupied building in the centre","Hand its occupied buildings over to the city's fire department","Shift mostly to shorter, political occupations to force talks"],"a":3,"e":"The fire showed how precarious long occupations are, so the ULC shifted mostly to shorter, political occupations aimed at forcing negotiations, though these can still last years. It did not abandon occupation altogether."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"What did social workers do in the cases shortly before residents moved in?","o":["They screened families for income to decide who could stay","They negotiated the building's purchase with the federal state","They ran the points system that allocated the housing units","They taught courses on how to live in a condominium"],"a":3,"e":"Social workers taught courses on condominium life, alongside gates and doormen that controlled access. The points system was run by the entidades (residents' organisations), not by social workers."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What did the federal SPU Ordinance 179 do in the Dandara case?","o":["It made the building a public-interest asset, opening negotiation","It transferred full ownership of the building to the ULC movement","It ordered the eviction of the occupiers within a fixed period","It set the federal cap per unit for the MCMV-Entidades programme"],"a":0,"e":"The ordinance designated the vacant court building as a public-interest asset, which opened it to negotiation for social housing. The per-unit cap belongs to MCMV-Entidades, a separate federal programme."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A self-managed housing project refuses new members sent by the movement and keeps vacant units for friends of current residents. Which form of enclosure is this?","o":["Commodification, because units are sold individually for profit","Cooptation, because the state undermines self-governance","Marginalization, through exclusive access towards outsiders","Regularization, because tenure is made legally secure"],"a":2,"e":"Marginalization is a self-selecting commons shutting out outsiders through exclusive membership or access. Commodification would require selling for profit; regularization is a pattern of protection, not a form of enclosure."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"The authors follow both cases through four stages. Which is NOT one of them?","o":["Appropriation of a vacant building","Regularization with the state","Federation into a national network","Renovation and moving in"],"a":2,"e":"The stages are appropriation, regularization, renovation and moving in. Federation is one of the five patterns of protection, operating at the interface between movements and the state, not a stage of the cases."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"What do the authors mean by a 'relational concept of property'?","o":["Property as a fixed thing owned outright by one person or entity","Property as a bundle of claims, rights and duties shaped by law","Property as a purely informal norm with no legal standing at all","Property as an asset whose value depends on nearby land prices"],"a":1,"e":"Following a relational view, property is a bundle of claims, rights and obligations shaped by legal frameworks; it can shield a commons or enable enclosure. The 'fixed thing owned by one entity' is the ownership model they warn against."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which observation illustrates the pattern of 'restoration'?","o":["Families receive a legal title that protects them from eviction","Residents attend the movement's marches to earn allocation points","Residents guard the building at night and make it habitable and safe","Federations lobby to amend the rules of a national housing programme"],"a":2,"e":"Restoration is the material side of protection: habitability, safety, night vigils and barriers. Legal titles are regularization, the other pattern at the level of the housing development."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How did the property outcomes of the two cases differ?","o":["Dandara became a rental block of the city, Marisa Letícia a cooperative","Both became collective cooperatives owned by the ULC movement itself","Dandara got a use-right concession, Marisa Letícia individual titles","Both remained state property rented out to residents for low rents"],"a":2,"e":"Marisa Letícia became a condominium with individual titles and mortgages; Dandara received an unusual use-right concession. Neither became collectively owned, because Brazil offers few legal options for collective property."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"In the three dimensions of a housing commons, what does 'decommodification' refer to?","o":["Prioritising use value over the exchange value of housing","Removing all market prices from the economy of the city","Transferring housing from private owners to the state","Banning residents from earning any income at home"],"a":0,"e":"Decommodification means housing is held for living in rather than for profit: use value over exchange value. It does not mean state ownership; the authors stress self-governance as a separate dimension."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which pair of patterns operates at the level between the housing development and the movement?","o":["Regularization and restoration","Federation and integration","Restoration and ideation","Integration and ideation"],"a":3,"e":"Integration (embedding residents in the wider struggle) and ideation (ideas stored in movements) link development and movement. Regularization and restoration operate at the level of the development itself."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Why do the authors warn that Ostrom's design principles risk depoliticising housing commons?","o":["They were made for natural resources at risk of overuse, so protection is seen only materially","They assume the state must own every commons, so movements and residents' claims are left out","They are based on urban housing in the US, so they do not fit Brazil's legal system or culture","They reject rules altogether, so they cannot explain any points system or residents' committee"],"a":0,"e":"Ostrom's principles were designed for natural resources threatened by overuse; applied to housing they risk treating protection purely in material terms and missing political claims. Ostrom's work is about self-governance without relying on state or market, so the state-ownership option misreads her."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: residents of a self-managed project in Recife must attend the wider movement's demonstrations to earn points, and newcomers are drawn into its housing struggle from day one. Which pattern of protection fits most precisely?","o":["Federation, since commoning is layered from floor committees up to national networks","Integration, since residents are embedded in the movement's wider political struggle","Ideation, since commons ideas stored in the movement materialise later in other places","Regularization, since legal recognition secures tenure for the families against eviction"],"a":1,"e":"Integration embeds residents in the movement's struggle, with the points system cultivating contribution and new members countering marginalization. Federation is about layered organisation linking movements to the state, not about individual residents' participation."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase most precisely states the authors' view of institutionalist approaches to housing commons?","o":["They are useless, because housing commons are political and not institutional","They are of partial use, because they overlook the political dimension of such projects","They fully explain stability once political claims are added as one more design rule","They are of partial use, because they neglect material rules such as access and boundaries"],"a":1,"e":"The authors write that institutionalist approaches are 'only of partial use because they overlook the political dimension'. Calling them useless overstates this, and the last option shifts the gap from politics to material rules, which institutionalists cover well."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A cooperative's annual report states: 'On our own we are fragile. We survive because we sit in a network of cooperatives that shares know-how and lobbies the state jointly, so that no project becomes an island in commodified waters.' Which idea in Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano grounds this?","o":["Harvey's view that some degree of closure is needed to protect a commons","Ostrom's boundary rules that define who may use a common-pool resource","Federation and nesting, linking single commons into wider networks of commons","Regularization, which secures each project's legal tenure against eviction"],"a":2,"e":"Nesting and federating (Savini), echoing de Angelis' 'common ecologies', is what the authors develop as the federation pattern; 'islands in commodified waters' is Vidal's phrase they cite. Harvey's closure is about protecting a commons by limiting access, not linking it to others."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about the study is NOT consistent with the article?","o":["One team member worked as an architect on Dandara's retrofit plans","The two cases were selected precisely because they had succeeded","Interviews covered residents, movement leaders and policymakers alike","Marisa Letícia got a use-right concession and Dandara individual titles"],"a":3,"e":"It is the other way round: Dandara received the use-right concession and Marisa Letícia became a condominium with individual titles. The architect role (participatory action research via extensão), the success-based selection and the interview mix are all accurate."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course reading would endorse this claim? 'Know-how about self-management formed in one occupation or exchange can lie dormant in movements and materialise years later in another housing project.'","o":["Pruijt (2003), on squatters' movements","Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano (2025)","Sorensen, on critical junctures","Rydin (2021), on urban regimes"],"a":1,"e":"This is the ideation pattern, illustrated by Uruguayan cooperative know-how that later shaped Brazilian projects. Sorensen's critical junctures concern moments of heightened agency in institutional change, not stored commons knowledge."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: The authors see regularization as the main defence of housing commons against eviction. Statement II: Because Brazil offers few options for collective property, regularization usually takes the form of individual condominium titles.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both statements are in the article, but II does not explain why regularization protects against eviction; it explains why that protection is only partial, since individual titles open routes to enclosure."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about MCMV-Entidades are consistent with the article? I. It gave movements direct access to federal housing funds for the first time. II. Its per-unit cap was enough to retrofit buildings in central São Paulo. III. Federation helped insert self-management (autogestão) into this programme.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I, II and III","I and III only"],"a":3,"e":"I and III are correct: MCMV-E channelled federal funds to movements, and federation inserted autogestão into a market-led programme. II is false: the federal cap was insufficient in the expensive centre, so state and municipal top-ups were needed."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano would a governmentality scholar in the line of Raco and Imrie most plausibly make?","o":["Secure private titles are what give residents freedom, so occupations wrongly break the rule of law","The points system is a selective incentive, so solidarity is a by-product of private rewards","Brazil's ownership culture is path dependent, so the condominium outcome was locked in early","Condominium courses and individual mortgages produce responsible, indebted, self-governing subjects"],"a":3,"e":"A governmentality reading treats the coupling to state programmes, condominium training and mortgages as techniques that shape residents into responsible, self-governing subjects. The selective-incentive critique is Olson's logic and the path-dependence one is historical institutionalism."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs 'structural coupling' in the cases?","o":["Movements lack funds, so they align with state programmes, which stabilises claims but shapes property outcomes","Movements hold enough funds, so they refuse state programmes, which keeps the buildings outside all property law","The state wants to evict occupiers, so it offers funds, which dissolves the movements into municipal agencies","Movements win in court, so the state must fund them, which gives them collective ownership of all the buildings"],"a":0,"e":"Lacking money, movements align with state systems (SPU ordinance, MCMV-E) to stabilise their claims and safeguard autonomy, but the programmes' rules channel outcomes into condominium titles. No collective ownership resulted, and movements were not dissolved into agencies."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs how occupations legitimise themselves in São Paulo?","o":["Private property law allows squatting after a year, so occupiers simply wait, so eviction becomes impossible","The social function of property is enshrined, so vacant buildings fail it, so occupying them gains legitimacy","Owners support occupations to cut costs, so they invite movements in, so legitimacy rests on owners' consent","Courts always side with occupiers, so the state stops resisting, so occupation becomes a normal tenure form"],"a":1,"e":"The 1988 Constitution and 2001 City Statute enshrine the social function of property; targeting vacant, tax-defaulting or public buildings lets movements frame occupation as enforcing that principle, and legitimacy is a resource against eviction. Occupations still clash with the right to private property; they are not legal by default."},{"t":"gurran","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which factors do Gurran and Ruming name behind Australia's rapid house price growth between 1996 and 2004?","o":["Planning red tape, slow approvals and high developer contributions","Mortgage deregulation, falling interest rates and investor incentives","Immigration quotas, new land taxes and rising construction wages","Public housing sell-offs, rent controls and mining-boom incomes"],"a":1,"e":"Prices grew after the 1980s financial deregulation of mortgage markets, falling interest rates and government incentives for property investment, and with demand concentrated in established suburbs prices rather than output rose. Red tape is the industry's explanation, which the authors question."},{"t":"gurran","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What, according to the authors, made NSW's Environmental Planning and Assessment Act 1979 stand out in Australia?","o":["It gave the Planning Minister absolute discretion over major projects","It required inclusionary zoning in all new urban renewal areas","It was the first to promote wide environmental and social equity goals","It standardised all local plans into one state-wide template"],"a":2,"e":"The 1979 Act was Australia's first to promote wide environmental and social equity goals in plan making and assessment. Ministerial discretion over major projects came with the 2005 reforms, part of the later winding back of protections."},{"t":"gurran","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"How did the Better Planning Network stage its best-known protest against Planning Minister Brad Hazzard?","o":["It occupied the parliament building in Sydney for a day","It wrapped him in bright yellow 'Hazzard' tape","It delivered 1,200 objection letters to his office","It took his department to court over consultation"],"a":1,"e":"In a widely publicised stunt the Network wrapped the minister in yellow 'Hazzard' tape. The roughly 1,200 letters are a confusion with the number of Green Paper submissions, which came through the formal process."},{"t":"gurran","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whom do Gurran and Ruming quote for the view that 'politics is an arena in which different interest groups seek to establish a particular narrative or version of events'?","o":["Hastings, on discourse and urban change","Clapham, on housing pathways","Foucault, on power and knowledge","Jacobs, on discourse analysis in urban policy"],"a":3,"e":"The quote is from Jacobs (2006). Hastings is cited for language 'producing or constructing reality', Clapham for the 'objective truth' phrase, and Foucault for the 'regime of truth'."},{"t":"gurran","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"What did the Property Council propose for controls that 'unreasonably constrain' development outcomes 'desirable to the market'?","o":["That they should be 'outlawed'","That councils compensate developers","That they be phased out over ten years","That an expert panel review them first"],"a":0,"e":"The PCA wanted such controls 'outlawed', and the draft Bill indeed barred local plans from 'onerous' controls affecting financial viability. Compensation and phasing out were not part of the PCA submission as reported."},{"t":"gurran","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why did the authors select the resident group Friends of Ku-ring-gai Environment (FOKE) for their analysis?","o":["It was the largest environmental charity in New South Wales","It represented developers in the Ku-ring-gai council area","It typified homeowner opposition, in the then Premier's electorate","It had been set up by the state to run the Green Paper consultation"],"a":2,"e":"FOKE was 'emblematic' of the long dialectic between the state and homeowner action groups since urban consolidation, and Ku-ring-gai lay in Premier O'Farrell's electorate. The environmental peak bodies in the sample were the EDO and NCC/TEC."},{"t":"gurran","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A minister says: 'More approvals mean more choice for buyers and downward pressure on prices.' Which finding of Gurran and Ruming most directly challenges this?","o":["Community groups made most submissions but had the least influence","Approvals almost doubled in 2011–2014, yet Sydney prices rose sharply","The government resisted industry pressure on infrastructure charges","The draft Bill made economic growth the main purpose of planning"],"a":1,"e":"NSW Planning Minister Goward made exactly this claim, yet approvals reached a 20-year high while the median house price rose from $535,000 to $735,000: 'increased supply did not ease affordability pressures'. The other options are true but do not test the supply claim."},{"t":"gurran","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the limitations of neoliberal housing approaches that Gurran and Ruming list?","o":["Ownership schemes for low earners were questioned after the GFC","Rent subsidies expose vulnerable tenants to insecurity","Inclusionary models depend on buoyant markets and funding","Inclusionary zoning always raises the price of new housing"],"a":3,"e":"The authors list the GFC doubts about low-income ownership, insecurity under rent subsidies, and the dependence of inclusionary models on buoyant markets. That inclusionary zoning raises prices is the fear of industry and governments in Australia, not a limitation the authors endorse."},{"t":"gurran","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How do Gurran and Ruming contrast the use of planning tools for affordable housing in the UK and the US?","o":["UK: s106 kept land flowing to social housing; US: tools to overcome exclusionary zoning","UK: planning tools sold council homes to tenants; US: tools to raise suburban land values","UK: inclusionary zoning in all suburbs; US: public rental housing built directly by planners","UK: tax credits for low-income housing; US: value capture under section 106 agreements"],"a":0,"e":"In the UK, councils used section 106 to keep social housing's access to land after Thatcherite transfers; in the US, inclusionary tools try to overcome suburban exclusionary zoning and offset gentrification. Tax credits are a US funding tool, not a UK one."},{"t":"gurran","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"What do Gurran and Ruming mean when they say regimes of truth become 'rather static representations of reality'?","o":["They never change, since language cannot alter material conditions","They are neutral summaries of evidence that all groups agree on","They fix which formulations of problems and solutions are acceptable","They are statements that lobbyists know to be untrue but repeat anyway"],"a":2,"e":"Once dominant, such discourses 'work to determine the acceptable formulations of problems and their solutions'. They are not unchangeable: the authors stress that dominant regimes of truth can be challenged, so discourse is a site of power struggle."},{"t":"gurran","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to the authors, what has Australian central government policy mostly focused on in response to affordability problems?","o":["Building large volumes of public rental housing through the states","Stimulating demand from first home buyers and investors, and planning reform","Taxing land value uplift to fund affordable homes across the country","Introducing national rent controls for low-income private tenants"],"a":1,"e":"Policy energy went to stimulating demand among first home buyers and investors and pushing states to dismantle perceived planning barriers. Public housing investment declined instead, apart from a short GFC stimulus boost."},{"t":"gurran","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Which situation best illustrates the 'contradictory roles' of planning that Gurran and Ruming describe?","o":["A council must apply national and EU environmental rules to one project","A planner must serve both the elected council and a professional code","A ministry must cut its budget while expanding its planning workforce","A council must speed up major development yet protect suburban owners"],"a":3,"e":"Planning is expected to support growth by facilitating major development, while preserving the property rights and interests vested in established suburbs; wider public goals 'jostle between these interests'. The other tensions are real but not the one the authors name."},{"t":"gurran","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"Why do the authors mention that developers were implicit funders of the conservative government's election campaign?","o":["To show the Bill was drafted by developers' own lawyers","To explain why the Network refused to make submissions","To show the government's dilemma over resident opposition","To show resident groups were funded by opposition parties"],"a":2,"e":"The government was ideologically and financially aligned with developers, yet faced resident opposition to density in the Premier's own seat, a 'dilemma'. Nothing in the article says developers drafted the Bill."},{"t":"gurran","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"What position did the environmental groups (NCC and TEC) take on faster planning decisions?","o":["They accepted simplification, but not at the cost of proper assessment","They rejected any simplification as a developer-driven agenda","They wanted fixed deadlines with deemed approval after expiry","They wanted assessment moved from councils to expert panels"],"a":0,"e":"They recognised the system needed simplifying but warned that 'Fast approvals that deliver poor quality, high risk, or unsustainable development are not in the public interest'. They did not reject simplification outright."},{"t":"gurran","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Gurran and Ruming, which statements are true? I. Industry submissions cast local residents as part of the problem. II. The government adopted Shelter NSW's inclusionary zoning proposal. III. The draft Bill barred local plans from 'onerous' controls that affect financial viability. IV. Distrust, partly fed by corruption inquiries, was a major community theme.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"I, III and IV are in the text (UDIA on residents opposing growth; s3.3 Principle 10; distrust linked to ICAC investigations). II is false: Shelter's proposal gained no traction and existing affordable housing contribution provisions were even wound back."},{"t":"gurran","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Gurran and Ruming, which statements are true? I. Planning systems have proved remarkably resilient to deregulation agendas. II. Most new Australian homes are now apartments built under urban consolidation. III. Developers and established homeowners both criticised urban consolidation, for different reasons. IV. The authors directly analyse the NIMBY phenomenon in Ku-ring-gai.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV T"],"a":1,"e":"I and III are true: planning persists as a key intervention, and consolidation was attacked by industry (less fringe land) and by homeowners (fear of density). II is false: most new homes are still detached on greenfield land. IV is false: the authors say they do not directly address NIMBYism."},{"t":"gurran","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Gurran and Ruming explain the NSW reform through the industry's regime of truth. How would a historical institutionalist most plausibly critique their account of the outcome?","o":["The authors never test whether regulation raises prices, so the industry's 'truth' may simply be true","Text analysis cannot explain why developer capital holds such discursive power in the first place","The 1979 Act survived through veto points and path dependence, not because one discourse lost out","Influence follows concentrated, well-funded interests, so discourse is a mere by-product of group power"],"a":2,"e":"A historical institutionalist would stress that the old Act persisted because the government lacked a majority in parliament and reform met institutional veto points, which is path dependence rather than discursive victory or defeat. The untested-prices critique is Hayekian or Glaeser-style economics; the capital critique is Marxist."},{"t":"gurran","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"How would a rational choice theorist in the line of Olson and Taylor most plausibly critique Gurran and Ruming?","o":["Discourse is a by-product: small, organised interests win, and resident groups also rent-seek","It treats discourse as a lobby tool and misses how policy forms self-governing subjects","It ignores that central planners lack the dispersed knowledge only markets can use","It stays at the level of text and never explains why capital dominates the state"],"a":0,"e":"Rational choice explains influence by concentrated interests that organise easily, and would add that homeowner groups defending property values are rent-seekers too, which the authors bracket. The self-governing-subjects critique is Raco and Imrie's governmentality; the knowledge critique is Hayek's."},{"t":"gurran","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a national builders' federation claims that procedures add '30 per cent' to building costs. No calculation is ever published, yet ministers and newspapers repeat the figure as fact, and a bill to 'remove barriers' follows. Which concept from Gurran and Ruming fits most precisely?","o":["Rhetorical relegation of social goals to subordinate clauses","Spot rezoning that hands landholders a large windfall","A regime of truth that fixes the acceptable solution","Value capture of uplift created by planning decisions"],"a":2,"e":"Like the PCA's unevidenced '40%' claim, the figure makes deregulation's benefits 'appear obvious' and becomes accepted as objective truth, fixing which solution counts. Rhetorical relegation concerns wording like 'while' and 'having regard to' in legislation, which the case does not show."},{"t":"gurran","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A housing advocate argues: 'Where a rezoning lifts land values, part of that uplift should be levied to fund affordable homes near jobs and services.' Where does this position fit in Gurran and Ruming's case, and what happened to it?","o":["The PCA's red-tape agenda, which the White Paper adopted as a key objective","UDIA's broad-based infrastructure tax, which the government partly accepted","Shelter NSW's inclusionary proposal, ignored while existing provisions were cut","FOKE's heritage demand, which the Bill met by adding heritage to its objects"],"a":2,"e":"Shelter NSW proposed 'levying of developers for affordable housing in situations of value uplift'; the draft Bill instead wound back existing affordable housing contribution provisions. UDIA's broad-based tax concerned infrastructure, and the government kept developer contributions."},{"t":"gurran","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about Gurran and Ruming's conclusion are correct? I. They warn that ideology and economic interests are not the only drivers of reform. II. Residents' influence worked mainly inside the formal consultation process. III. They expect the same discourses to return in the next economic downturn.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and III are in the conclusion. II is wrong: the Better Planning Network's influence 'was exerted outside the government's contained and formal policy-making processes', where community submissions seemed impotent."},{"t":"gurran","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Gurran and Ruming argue that housing affordability served as a semantic justification for a wider deregulatory agenda. Statement II: Despite the heavy emphasis on affordability, the draft Planning Bill wound back existing provisions for affordable housing contributions.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are in the text, and II is the evidence the authors use for I: if affordability were the real aim, affordable housing provisions would not be cut, so 'what is really meant is residential development as a form of economic growth'."},{"t":"gurran","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs how the Property Council's '40%' claim worked rhetorically, according to the authors?","o":["A figure is verified by Treasury, so deregulation is costed, so the Bill targets the worst rules","A precise figure is stated without evidence, so deregulation looks obvious, so viability rules enter","A figure is disputed by councils, so the claim is withdrawn, so the Bill keeps all local controls","A figure is stated without evidence, so residents reject it, so the Bill adds consultation rights"],"a":1,"e":"By claiming red tape costs '40%', 'the benefits of deregulation appear obvious', although 'no evidence of actual costs or savings is ever provided'; the draft Bill then barred 'onerous' controls affecting viability. The Bill added no consultation rights, so the last chain fails."},{"t":"gurran","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs the authors' account of why Australian prices, rather than output, rose between 1996 and 2004?","o":["Red tape blocked approvals, so supply stalled, so prices rose although demand stayed flat","Public housing grew, so private builders withdrew, so supply fell and prices rose sharply","Credit and investor incentives lifted demand, met inherent supply constraints, so prices rose","Credit lifted demand, so builders expanded output fast, so prices rose with construction costs"],"a":2,"e":"Mortgage deregulation, falling rates and investor incentives raised demand, which was concentrated in established suburbs with inherent supply constraints, so 'prices, rather than housing production, rose'. The red-tape chain is the industry narrative the authors treat as a regime of truth."},{"t":"gurran","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combining Harvey (1989) with Gurran and Ruming, which reading of the Property Council's call for a planning system that delivers 'an economic dividend' is most accurate?","o":["Harvey sees managerial welfare provision; Gurran and Ruming see neutral, evidence-based reform","Harvey sees an entrepreneurial growth turn; Gurran and Ruming show how it became policy via discourse","Harvey sees free choice by residents; Gurran and Ruming see a community-led drive for more housing","Harvey sees market failure needing more planning; Gurran and Ruming see a natural consensus on growth"],"a":1,"e":"Harvey would place the growth imperative in the shift from managerialism to entrepreneurialism under inter-urban competition; Gurran and Ruming show how the industry's framing was adopted in the White Paper ('main purpose ... economic growth'). Managerialism is what Harvey contrasts entrepreneurialism with."},{"t":"gurran","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"A reformer cites both Moroni and the NSW development industry to argue for fewer planning rules. Combining Moroni with Gurran and Ruming, which assessment is most accurate?","o":["Both argue for discretionary, case-by-case deals that let developers bypass local plans","Moroni defends detailed end-state plans; the industry wants plans written by developers","Moroni wants few, general rules on principle; Gurran and Ruming show such talk can serve interests","Both readings show that deregulation reliably lowers prices for low-income renters and buyers"],"a":2,"e":"Moroni argues for a limited set of general, abstract rules (kosmos rather than taxis) as a matter of principle; Gurran and Ruming show deregulatory discourse in NSW served development interests, while the industry also wanted easier spot rezoning and public infrastructure funding. Neither shows that deregulation lowers prices."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What did the 1971 Dutch Supreme Court ruling do, according to Pruijt?","o":["It made squatting a criminal offence for buildings empty under a year","It extended 'house right' to squatters, so owners could not simply evict","It obliged the city to buy every squatted building for social housing","It gave owners the right to cut off water and power to squatted homes"],"a":1,"e":"The ruling applied the protection of the home ('house right') to squatters, making quick evictions impossible and long-lived squats feasible. The one-year rule came later (1994) and eroded this protection."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"How does Soja, cited by Pruijt, label Amsterdam's policy of buying squats and handing them to housing associations?","o":["'Zero tolerance'","'Benign neglect'","'Slightly repressive tolerance'","'Managed decline'"],"a":2,"e":"Soja calls it 'slightly repressive tolerance': legalization through purchase and leases, combined with containment. 'Zero tolerance' would describe repression, which is not how Amsterdam handled most squats."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Where in New York did a squatters' movement proper emerge after 1983?","o":["In the South Bronx","On the Lower East Side","In central Harlem","In downtown Brooklyn"],"a":1,"e":"The Lower East Side squatters' movement was neither co-opted nor repressed out of existence until legalization in 2002. Bronx groups such as Banana Kelly were housing-movement organisations that became service providers."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What name did New York squatters give to non-profits accused of channelling money into high executive salaries?","o":["'Slumlords'","'Milkers'","'Speculators'","'Poverty pimps'"],"a":3,"e":"Squatters called them 'poverty pimps', part of the opaque 'poverty industry'. 'Milkers' are owners who extract rent while neglecting maintenance, a different actor in the disinvestment story."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"In New York's official homesteading programmes, what was 'sweat equity'?","o":["Participants' own renovation work in return for eventual ownership","A tax credit for landlords who kept rents below the market level","A city loan that squatters repaid out of their monthly earnings","A share in the building given to the contractors who renovated it"],"a":0,"e":"Homesteaders put in their own work ('sweat equity') to renovate abandoned city buildings, with contractors doing skilled tasks, in return for eventual ownership. It was not a landlord tax credit."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"What did about 5,000 squatters in Greater London Council property receive, according to Pruijt?","o":["Criminal charges after a large police operation","An 'amnesty' that entitled most to rehousing","Ownership titles to the buildings they occupied","Municipal jobs managing short-life housing"],"a":1,"e":"The London 'amnesty' entitled most of them to rehousing before a crackdown: an example of institutionalization as legalization. Managing short-life housing describes UK 'licensed squatting', a different arrangement."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which situation illustrates cooptation rather than institutionalization, in Pruijt's terms?","o":["Squatters receive leases on their buildings yet keep defending other squats","A city legalizes the last squats and the movement stops all disruption","A group gets buildings and funds if it stops squatting and provides services","Police evict every squat in the city and close the legal loopholes as well"],"a":2,"e":"Cooptation means the co-opting organisation embraces some movement goals while redefining the problem so that solving it does not threaten its stability, giving activists a service role. Leases plus continued disruption is flexible institutionalization."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT part of the opportunity structure that Pruijt compares between the two cities?","o":["The city's market-oriented or redistributive regime","The legal protection of property against squatters","The technical condition of the vacant buildings","The squatters' own oppositional identity and ideology"],"a":3,"e":"Opportunity structure is the context: regime, property protection, access to officials, building condition. The oppositional identity belongs to the movement itself; Pruijt shows it can partly override the opportunity structure."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How did the technical condition of squattable buildings differ between the two cities?","o":["Amsterdam's were worse, because urban renewal was delayed for decades","Both were similar, as each city had large stocks of new vacant offices","New York's were far worse, after a long cycle of disinvestment","New York's were better, because the city maintained its own stock"],"a":2,"e":"New York's tenements had gone through milking, redlining, arson and abandonment, so squatters' manuals covered holes in roofs and missing stairs; Amsterdam's state-led renewal intervened early. The city-maintenance option reverses this."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Why did New York squatters' focus on city-owned buildings leave speculators untouched?","o":["Speculators had bribed officials to protect their own empty buildings","Lacking rights, squatters picked low-risk city buildings, as private owners evict more easily","Federal law exempted privately owned buildings from any form of squatting","Squatters had agreed with speculators to divide the vacant buildings between them"],"a":1,"e":"Without legal protection, squatting turned covert and concentrated on city-owned buildings, where risk was lower and eviction harder than for private owners; this left speculators in peace. No bribery or deal with speculators is described."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"Why did Amsterdam squatters in old working-class districts usually leave voluntarily when renewal was scheduled?","o":["A broad consensus promised affordable new homes for the neighbourhood and rehousing","The squatters' leases forbade any resistance to demolition or renewal plans","Police routinely evicted squatters before any renewal work began there","Housing associations paid squatters large sums of money to leave the buildings"],"a":0,"e":"Pruijt explains it through a broad consensus: building for the neighbourhood at subsidised rents, a right to rehousing, reliable plans, democratic planning and recognition of squatters' needs. Nothing suggests squatters were paid off."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How did Pruijt's data differ between the two cities?","o":["Both cities were studied through the same survey of current squatters","Amsterdam relied on news articles; New York on his own 1980s archive work","Both cities were studied only through official municipal statistics","New York drew on news articles and 2000-01 interviews; Amsterdam on his own 1980-84 research"],"a":3,"e":"For New York he used news articles, squatters' web documents, interviews in 2000 and 2001 and community board archives; for Amsterdam his own interviews and archive work from 1980 to 1984 plus existing literature. The second option swaps the sources."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"What does the Berlin experience of 1981 to 1984 illustrate in Pruijt's article?","o":["Squatting thrives wherever the city refuses any form of negotiation","Squatting can be ended through a mix of repression and legalization","Squatting disappears once house right is granted by the highest court","Squatting grows fastest when movement groups become service providers"],"a":1,"e":"Berlin authorities eliminated squatting through a combination of repression and legalization, showing that the two state responses are often combined. House right worked the other way in the Netherlands: it enabled long-lived squats."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"What infrastructure did Amsterdam's squatters' movement build at its height?","o":["A network of advice desks, media, bars, workshops and a bureau on speculators","A national political party that won seats in parliament and on the council","A federation of housing cooperatives that later ran the city's social housing","A private security firm that protected squats against eviction squads"],"a":0,"e":"Amsterdam's movement had a rich infrastructure including advice desks, media, bars, workshops and even a bureau investigating speculators. Pruijt stresses that the housing associations did not originate in the movement."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a US city hands tax-foreclosed buildings to tenant groups to self-manage, with less than half the usual subsidy. Organisers who once led rent strikes now spend all their energy keeping the buildings afloat. Which concept fits most precisely?","o":["Flexible institutionalization, because convention complements disruption","A squatters' movement proper, because squatting is both means and end","The privatization of poverty, with self-help as a cheaper state substitute","House right, because tenants gain legal protection against eviction"],"a":2,"e":"This mirrors the Tenant Interim Lease programme: self-help replaces state provision at lower cost and absorbs activists' energy, which Pruijt calls the privatization of poverty. Flexible institutionalization would require continued disruption, which has stopped here."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase most precisely states Pruijt's stance on the value of cooptation for movements?","o":["Cooptation is always a sell-out that destroys a movement's identity","Cooptation always benefits the poor, as groups gain resources and roles","Cooptation's costs and benefits have to be judged case by case","Cooptation is harmless, since it only changes a movement's tactics"],"a":2,"e":"Pruijt takes a neutral stance: whether cooptation costs or benefits a movement must be judged per case (Selznick allowed for both possibilities). The 'always a sell-out' option overstates his position."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A former activist writes: 'Once we took the city's renovation money we stopped occupying buildings. Today we manage forty buildings, but our board talks more about contracts and salaries than about tenants.' Which idea from Pruijt grounds this passage?","o":["Cooptation followed by the Weber-Michels drift towards managerial self-interest","Flexible institutionalization, since the group still disrupts while it manages","A squatters' movement proper, immune because squatting remains its main goal","Erosion of house right, which forced the group into covert action and isolation"],"a":0,"e":"Trading squatting for funds and a service role is cooptation, and the drift from movement goals to organisational self-interest is the 'Weber-Michels' scenario. There is no continued disruption, so flexible institutionalization does not fit."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with Pruijt's article?","o":["In the US, squatting is treated as criminal trespass","Amsterdam's squatters kept a disruptive repertoire after legalization","European activists helped bring a squatters' identity to New York","Amsterdam's housing associations grew out of the squatters' movement"],"a":3,"e":"Pruijt stresses the opposite: the third-sector housing associations did not originate in the movement, which is why he finds hardly any cooptation in Amsterdam. The other three statements are in the text."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course reading would endorse this claim? 'A movement's pre-existing oppositional identity, partly imported from abroad, can to some extent override the local opportunity structure.'","o":["Rydin (2021), on growth coalitions","Pruijt (2003)","Sorensen, on path dependence","Raco and Imrie (2000)"],"a":1,"e":"Pruijt uses the role of European activists on the Lower East Side to argue that an oppositional identity can partly override the opportunity structure. Sorensen stresses constrained developmental pathways, not identity overriding structure."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: New York squatters mostly targeted city-owned buildings. Statement II: Evicting squatters is easier for private owners, and city-owned buildings without redevelopment plans carry lower risk.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are in the text, and II explains I: the squatters' manual recommended city-owned buildings because private owners evict more easily and risk is lower where no redevelopment is planned."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Pruijt argues that squatting is itself a form of gentrification. Statement II: Squatters share some traits with pioneering newcomers who are rich in cultural capital.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Pruijt notes overlaps such as cultural capital (II is true), but he denies that squatting is gentrification: it creates no luxury housing and squatters often sided with residents against developers. So I is false."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about the opportunity structure are consistent with Pruijt? I. The 1971 Dutch ruling extended house right to squatters. II. New York's squattable buildings were in better condition than Amsterdam's. III. The erosion of legal protection after 1986 coincided with the Amsterdam movement's decline.","o":["I and III only","I and II only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and III are in the text. II is reversed: disinvestment left New York's tenements in far worse condition, with holes in roofs and missing stairs."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of Pruijt's identity-based explanation of the squatters' movement proper would a rational choice scholar in Olson's line most plausibly make?","o":["Squatting gives a private, excludable benefit, a home, so mobilisation reflects a selective incentive","Squatters act as pioneers for capital, so local conflict leaves big real estate and banks untouched","Squatters' identity became taken for granted through habit, so it is an institution, not a choice","Squatting violates property rights and the rule of law, so the state should never tolerate it"],"a":0,"e":"For Olson, squatting is unusual because the collective good comes with a private, excludable benefit (a home), which explains mobilisation without appeal to identity. The 'pioneers for capital' critique is Marxist, and the 'taken for granted' one is sociological institutionalism."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs how New York tenements became derelict, as Pruijt describes it?","o":["Rent control froze income, so the city bought buildings, so it let them decay through neglect","Owners could not let profitably and maintain, so banks redlined, so milkers and abandonment followed","Squatters occupied buildings, so owners fled the city, so the buildings were left to decay","Speculators bought buildings to renovate them, so rents rose, so tenants left the empty buildings"],"a":1,"e":"Owners found they could not let profitably while maintaining buildings, banks' redlining blocked investment, 'milkers' extracted rent without upkeep, tenants fled, and the city took over for tax arrears. Squatting came after abandonment, not before it."},{"t":"raco","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"What did the Single Regeneration Budget replace when it was set up in April 1994?","o":["The Regional Development Agencies created under the Thatcher governments","Many separately funded urban schemes of the 1980s and early 1990s","A national programme of council house building on deprived estates","The planning powers of local authorities over regeneration areas"],"a":1,"e":"SRB brought many separately funded 1980s and early 1990s schemes together into one competitive funding source. The RDAs were not replaced: they were New Labour's regional bodies that later assessed SRB bids."},{"t":"raco","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whose quotation opens the article, stating that how rights and duties are specified structures power relations and governance practices?","o":["Mulgan, the government adviser on community","Giddens, on the extension of obligations","Healey, writing on collaborative planning","Rose, on advanced liberal government"],"a":2,"e":"The epigraph is from Healey (1997): the way rights and duties are specified and redeemed reveals the form and culture of a political community. Giddens is cited later for 'an extension of individual obligations', not in the epigraph."},{"t":"raco","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"SRB bids had to devote ten per cent of project funding to one activity. Which?","o":["Independent external evaluation of each scheme's outcomes and impacts","Matching funds raised from private sector partners","Compensation for residents displaced by redevelopment","Community capacity building, such as training representatives"],"a":3,"e":"Ten per cent had to go to building community 'capacities', for example training representatives or giving them administrative resources, and bids had to make capacity building a key objective. Evaluation was done by the partnerships themselves through monitoring returns."},{"t":"raco","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"How do Raco and Imrie, following Jones (2000), describe the role of the Regional Development Agencies in SRB?","o":["As autonomous regional governments with elected assemblies and tax powers","As sub-contractors of central government, bound by strict contractual rules","As community-led bodies elected by the local SRB partnerships","As private consultancies hired to train community representatives"],"a":1,"e":"Footnote 3: RDAs look like regional devolution but are tightly controlled from the centre, 'enacting a less-than-glamourous role as sub-contractor to central government'. They assess bids with central criteria; they are not autonomous governments."},{"t":"raco","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Which label did critics such as Foley et al. give SRB: distributing reduced resources through competition with strong managerial controls?","o":["Entrepreneurial governance","Managerial localism","Roll-back neoliberalism","Third Way localism"],"a":1,"e":"Foley et al. (1998) call it 'managerial localism'. 'Entrepreneurial governance' recalls Harvey's urban entrepreneurialism, which is about inter-urban competition, not this critique of SRB."},{"t":"raco","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Who described empowerment as 'a strategy or technique for the transformation of subjectivity from powerlessness to active citizen'?","o":["Cruikshank, on technologies of citizenship","Dean, on the new prudentialism","Donzelot, on the transformation of responsibility","Mulgan, on generative rules for communities"],"a":0,"e":"Cruikshank (1994) calls empowerment a 'technology of citizenship'. Dean is cited for the new prudentialism and Donzelot for the 'invitation to autonomy', both related but different points."},{"t":"raco","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Which development best illustrates what Raco and Imrie mean by giving the local an 'ontological status'?","o":["A council merges small districts into a larger tier for efficiency","A region gains an elected assembly with tax-raising powers","A ministry treats neighbourhoods as the natural site of government","A national agency sets uniform service standards for all localities"],"a":2,"e":"SRB rescales government from society and nation to community and locality, which becomes 'the locus' of effective government. Merging districts for efficiency is the opposite logic: postwar territories of government reflected functionalism and efficiency."},{"t":"raco","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How do Raco and Imrie position their Foucauldian view of the state against neo-Gramscian and neo-Marxist state theory?","o":["They note parallels but stress mentalities of government, the state being one form of it","They reject those theories because they ignore the economy and see the state as purely cultural","They adopt those theories fully and treat the state as a unified set of class relations","They argue that those theories make the state too fragmented and plural to be analysed"],"a":0,"e":"They note parallels with Jessop and neo-Gramscian work, but the Foucauldian approach differs in emphasis: the state is 'no more than a specific form of government'. Treating the state as a unified actor is what they reject in older Marxist and pluralist work."},{"t":"raco","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT a way in which SRB made community knowledge calculable, according to the authors?","o":["Partnerships set their own baselines, milestones and outputs","Representatives were trained by professionals in monitoring","Bidders supplied their own statistics to justify their case","Locally elected auditors judged bids on residents' behalf"],"a":3,"e":"The authors stress the opposite: the documents do little to identify local electoral control over SRB decisions. Baselines, professional training and self-supplied statistics are all named as ways expertise was codified."},{"t":"raco","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"What do Raco and Imrie mean by 'directed self-government' under SRB?","o":["Elected councils direct the communities and keep all decision powers","Self-government is phased in until the state withdraws completely","Central directives replace self-government when communities fail","Communities govern themselves, within duties defined from above"],"a":3,"e":"Local agents are encouraged to take responsibility for their own governance, but the forms, roles and responsibilities are set by central guidance. The phasing-out option misreads the argument: the state does not withdraw, it governs at a distance."},{"t":"raco","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Best Value was promoted as a 'nonideological' tool of empowerment for residents as consumers. How do Raco and Imrie treat this claim?","o":["As accurate: a neutral technique that leaves politics to councils","As political: it involves choices about what counts as efficiency","As a cover for returning services to direct ministerial control","As proof that consumer choice has replaced local citizenship"],"a":1,"e":"Footnote 4: such discourses are 'fundamentally political', because they are part of the machinery of government and involve choices about what counts as efficiency or best practice. Best Value works as disciplinary power, not as a return to direct central provision."},{"t":"raco","lv":2,"fmt":"example","q":"SRB partnerships had to establish an 'accountable body'. What was it responsible for?","o":["Electing community representatives and running local hearings","Training residents to prepare baselines and monitoring returns","The partnership's debts and the conditions on public money","Assessing rival bids from other partnerships in the region"],"a":2,"e":"The accountable body becomes 'responsible in law for meeting the debts and other liabilities of the partnership and the conditions attached to public money'. Training representatives is part of capacity building, a separate requirement."},{"t":"raco","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Under the rights and responsibilities agenda the presence of state agencies is ostensibly reduced. What happens to policymakers' power, according to Raco and Imrie?","o":["It declines, as communities now set the policy agendas","It moves to private firms that run the partnerships","It is enhanced: they shape communities' actions more","It stays the same; only the language of policy changes"],"a":2,"e":"Citing Henkel, they argue that the presence of government is reduced but 'the power of policymakers to shape the actions of policy communities is enhanced'. The 'declines' option is the official empowerment story the article unmasks."},{"t":"raco","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Why, following Dean, do the authors argue that demands to expand community representatives' rights do not simply shrink the state?","o":["Rights claims are always rejected by central government in the end, so nothing changes","Such rights need governmental practices to secure them, so state domains multiply","Communities lack money to use these rights without loans from the state","Courts rather than ministries decide which community rights are valid"],"a":1,"e":"Claims to community rights require new practices that 'secure, defend, protect, and foster' them, which 'only serve to multiply the domains and capacities of the state'. Nothing in the text says rights claims are simply rejected; they are absorbed into government."},{"t":"raco","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Raco and Imrie, which statements are true? I. Advanced liberalism governs through the regulated choices of autonomous actors. II. SRB transferred a measurable quantity of power from the state to deprived communities. III. SRB monitoring data were mainly used to check targets, not to improve policy. IV. Their argument rests on interviews they held with SRB residents.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"I and III are in the text (Rose; Foley et al.). II is false: empowerment brings no 'quantitative increases in power' but a qualitative change in subjectivity. IV is false: the evidence is policy documents and secondary evaluations, with no own fieldwork."},{"t":"raco","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Raco and Imrie, which statements are true? I. The active citizen was an entirely new invention of New Labour. II. Government through community transforms pre-existing bonds and affiliates them to expertise. III. Best Practice guidance made bidders adjust to the scrutiny of their peers. IV. If the art of government is constructed, it can also be reconstructed.","o":["I F · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I is false: the active citizen was already part of Thatcherite ideology; what is new is its central place in state and civil society relations. II (Rose), III (peer review as government at a distance) and IV (the conclusion on resistance) are all true."},{"t":"raco","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Raco and Imrie read SRB's quarterly reporting and capacity building as technologies that transform community subjectivity. How would a rational choice institutionalist most plausibly critique this reading?","o":["Partnerships may just comply strategically with funding rules, so no change in subjectivity is needed","The reporting serves capital's need to cut social spending, so accumulation, not mentalities, drives it","Capacity building may build genuine shared understanding, which the reading cannot tell from control","SRB's forms reflect choices locked in by earlier schemes, so path dependence explains them better"],"a":0,"e":"A rational choice institutionalist explains behaviour by incentives: managers who use monitoring only to check targets, and treat involvement as 'a necessary requirement (for funders)', look like strategic compliance. The accumulation critique is Marxist, the shared-understanding one communicative, the path-dependence one historical institutionalist."},{"t":"raco","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"How would a Marxist political economist most plausibly critique Raco and Imrie's account of SRB?","o":["It reads strategic compliance with funding incentives as an inner change in the subjects themselves","It cannot separate real institution-building through dialogue from control, so all participation looks like power","It neglects how local cultural norms and scripts shape the way partnerships interpret central rules","It stays with mentalities of government and leaves out the fiscal pressures and class interests behind cuts"],"a":3,"e":"Raco and Imrie deliberately refuse a unified state and class analysis, yet their own sources point to cost-cutting and 'abandonment'; a Marxist would ground SRB in fiscal crisis and uneven development. The strategic-compliance critique belongs to rational choice, the dialogue one to communicative planning."},{"t":"raco","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality gives a tenants' association a formal say in estate renewal. The agreement states that the association must observe public-service rules on financial propriety, act 'responsibly', and accept the council's final responsibility for strategic decisions. Which concept fits most precisely?","o":["Calculable spaces: the estate is made governable through quantified targets","Communities of obligation: the right to take part comes bundled with duties","Managerial localism: reduced budgets are allocated through competitive bids","Disciplinary peer review: tenants adjust their conduct to rival associations"],"a":1,"e":"This mirrors the DETR tenant participation guidance: a 'proper balance of rights and responsibilities', where gaining input means accepting centrally defined duties. Calculable spaces would need baselines and quantified monitoring, which the case does not mention."},{"t":"raco","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about expertise in Raco and Imrie are correct? I. Policy rhetoric gives the knowledge of policy recipients a privileged status. II. Training of community representatives is run by professionals and experts. III. Professional experts are freed from performance mechanisms as communities take over.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are both in the section on expertise: local knowledge is rhetorically privileged, yet representatives are trained by professionals. III is wrong: professionals' activities 'also become subordinated to performance-related mechanisms'."},{"t":"raco","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A government white paper states: 'Every household has a duty to manage its own health and plan for its own risks; public services will help people make the right choices by educating them and preventing problems early.' Which idea in Raco and Imrie does this passage illustrate?","o":["Managerial localism: reduced resources allocated through competition","Communities of obligation: rights granted on condition of local duties","Calculable spaces: domains made governable through quantification","Dean's new prudentialism: risk minimisation tied to self-care"],"a":3,"e":"Dean's 'new prudentialism' links risk minimisation to strategies of self-care, with active citizens monitoring their own health and welfare and professionals taking educative and preventative roles. Communities of obligation concern community rights conditional on duties, not individual self-care."},{"t":"raco","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Raco and Imrie present the Regional Development Agencies as genuine regional devolution away from Whitehall. Statement II: The RDAs assess SRB bids using central government criteria.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"II is true (footnote 3). I is false: the authors say RDAs are 'tightly controlled and coordinated from the centre', a form of 'strong governing from a distance'. II is in fact evidence against I."},{"t":"raco","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs how Best Value disciplines local authorities, in Raco and Imrie's reading?","o":["Residents vote out failing councils, so councils compete for votes, so services improve through elections","Centre sets value-for-money norms, so councils gain new tax powers, so they become independent of it","Centre sets value-for-money norms, so 'irresponsible' conduct triggers action, so councils self-police","Centre sets value-for-money norms, so councils lose their functions, so private firms run local services"],"a":2,"e":"Best Value sets new disciplines; failure to act 'appropriately' or 'responsibly' triggers central action, so councils internalise the norms: discipline 'sustains itself by its own mechanism'. The electoral chain is the official consumer-empowerment story, not the authors' reading."},{"t":"raco","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs how expertise is redrawn under SRB, according to the authors?","o":["Local knowledge is privileged, so professionals are excluded, so communities set policy free of expert rules","Experts distrust local knowledge, so communities are ignored, so consultants write the bids for them","Local knowledge is privileged, but representatives are trained, so communities gain more power over the centre","Local knowledge is privileged, but communities are trained in procedures, so their aims become calculable"],"a":3,"e":"Rhetoric privileges local knowledge, but community skills are seen as limited, so professionals train representatives, who then 'shape, predict, and make calculable' community aspirations. The third chain breaks at the last link: training brings no quantitative gain in power."},{"t":"raco","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Hypothetical: a squatters' group accepts a city contract to run a self-help housing service in a district that both the market and the council have left, with targets and quarterly reports. Combining Pruijt (2003) and Raco and Imrie, which reading is most accurate?","o":["Pruijt: cooptation into service delivery; Raco and Imrie: self-help after abandonment, governed from afar","Pruijt: terminal institutionalization ends the movement; Raco and Imrie: a real transfer of power to residents","Pruijt: flexible institutionalization keeps disruption; Raco and Imrie: a regime of truth set by developers","Pruijt: a free-rider problem solved by contracts; Raco and Imrie: horizontal accountability towards residents"],"a":0,"e":"Pruijt's cooptation turns activists into service providers; Raco and Imrie see self-help where services are 'unattractive to the private sector' as 'public and private sector abandonment' governed from a distance through targets. Accountability under such contracts is vertical, and power is not transferred."},{"t":"raco","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combining Rydin's urban politics chapter with Raco and Imrie, which statement about community involvement in regeneration is most accurate?","o":["Rydin sees community networks as consensus tools; Raco and Imrie see them as real transfers of power","Both argue that involving organised communities settles conflict and secures just outcomes for all","Rydin asks who counts as 'the community'; Raco and Imrie show how policy constructs communities","Rydin explains low involvement by free-riding; Raco and Imrie by a lack of selective incentives"],"a":2,"e":"Rydin questions who is recognised as 'the community' in governance networks (others are labelled 'hard to reach'); Raco and Imrie show SRB 'identifies and institutionalises communities of action'. Free-riding and selective incentives are Olson's vocabulary, used by neither."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Rydin mentions Love Canal as a trigger case. For which strand of urban politics?","o":["The Right to the City protests that followed the 2008 crisis","The environmental justice movement in the United States","Castells' movements over the means of collective consumption","The tactical coalitions between Tea Party and progressive groups"],"a":1,"e":"Love Canal, a low-income development built on an old landfill, triggered US environmental justice activism about toxic sites near communities of colour. The Right to the City is a separate justice theme (Occupy after 2008), not linked to Love Canal."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Who introduced the urban regime concept, according to Rydin?","o":["Molotch, in his 1970s work on growth coalitions","Pierre, in his analysis of business's systemic power in cities","Castells, in his theory of urban social movements","Stone, in his study of governing coalitions in Atlanta"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin credits Stone's Atlanta study with introducing urban regime theory as a response to critiques of the growth coalition. Molotch coined the growth coalition, the concept regime theory moved away from."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"In Uysal's Sulukule study, what did the Sulukule Platform put forward against the state's scheme?","o":["An alternative plan (STOP) prepared with outside academics","A court claim for collective ownership of the Roma quarter","A tourism and heritage strategy written with the council","A residents' cooperative that bought the land from the state"],"a":0,"e":"The Platform (NGOs, a Roma association, activists and academics) used meetings, festivals and an alternative STOP plan. It did not win property rights; unclear property rights were one reason it failed."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which actors formed the 'great alliance' behind Turin's post-industrial agenda (Ravazzi and Belligni)?","o":["Trade unions, the Catholic Church and the regional government","Fiat's management, national ministers and property developers","Centre-left politicians, liberal business and Politecnico academics","Neighbourhood committees, housing cooperatives and the municipal council"],"a":2,"e":"The alliance joined centre-left politicians, liberal business (the Group of 70, led by a banker) and academics from the Politecnico. Fiat was only involved early on and national ministers were not the core; this was a local regime."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"What was the 'Bioblitz' in Fougère and Bond's Denniston Plateau study?","o":["A blockade of the mine's access road by local climate protesters","A community exercise collecting ecological data on the site","A formal Environment Court hearing on the mining consent","An online petition run by national environmental NGOs"],"a":1,"e":"The Bioblitz was a community-based exercise in collecting ecological data on the plateau. It was one of the places where agonistic, adversarial rather than hostile, exchange became possible; the court hearings were where antagonism peaked."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"How, according to Rydin, are sectors of society that do not self-organise often labelled by planners?","o":["As 'NIMBYs' or 'objectors' who block local development","As 'free riders' or 'non-joiners' in collective action","As 'the precariat' or 'the excluded' in urban society","As 'hard to reach' or 'silent', as if recalcitrant"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin notes that labelling them 'hard to reach' or 'silent' suggests they are recalcitrant in the face of offers of involvement. 'Free riders' is Olson's vocabulary, not the chapter's."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Which situation is an example of Agyeman's 'just exercise of procedures'?","o":["A city recognises a Roma community as a group with its own claims","A council runs an open-ended visioning process before it drafts a plan","An agency enforces noise rules as strictly in poor as in rich areas","Pollution burdens end up spread evenly across a city's neighbourhoods"],"a":2,"e":"Just procedures concern how regulation and implementation are exercised, so even-handed enforcement fits. The visioning option is 'fair process', a different aspect; the others are recognition and fair outcomes."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT among Dowding et al.'s criteria for identifying an urban regime, as listed by Rydin?","o":["The agenda is distinctive and long-lived","The coalition crosses organisational boundaries","Partners are bound by a formal legal contract","The coalition survives changes in personnel"],"a":2,"e":"A regime is by definition informal yet relatively stable, so a formal legal contract is not a criterion. Surviving personnel changes and a long-lived, distinctive agenda sustained by a boundary-crossing coalition are all on the list."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Which situation best illustrates a conflict over 'collective consumption' in Castells' sense?","o":["Tenants protest cuts to public transport and social housing","Shoppers boycott a supermarket chain over rising food prices","Landowners lobby for a rezoning that raises their land values","Villagers oppose a wind farm because it spoils their view"],"a":0,"e":"Collective consumption means public services needed to reproduce labour, such as housing and transport, which households cannot provide alone. A consumer boycott is about private goods, the classic misreading of the term."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to Rydin, Molotch's growth coalition idea was originally a reaction against which view of planning?","o":["Technocratic views of planning as neutral expertise","Marxist views of planning as a tool of capital accumulation","Pluralist views of groups forming on demand","Communicative views of planning as consensus"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin says the growth coalition was a reaction against technocratic views, stressing class and local elites instead. The Marxist link runs the other way: political economists later criticised the concept as too agent-based."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Rydin explains why pluralism came to be seen as naïve. Which reason is NOT one she gives?","o":["Groups differ hugely in their power and their resources","Some issues never become a focus of activism","It legitimated a focus on middle-class groups","Large groups fail to organise as members free-ride"],"a":3,"e":"Free-riding in large groups is Olson's rational-choice argument against pluralism, not Rydin's. Her reasons are unequal power and resources, non-issues and the legitimation of middle-class pressure groups."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"What does Pierre's notion of business's 'systemic power' imply for urban regimes?","o":["Regimes dominate business because councils control planning permissions","Leaders must cater to corporate interests, giving regimes a corporate bias","Business power is mainly visible in the formal votes of the elected city council","Regimes are neutral arenas where all interests carry the same weight"],"a":1,"e":"To access the systemic power of the corporate sector, political leaders must cater to corporate interests, so regimes keep a bias towards business. Regime power works largely outside formal council votes, so the 'formal votes' option inverts the idea."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Tea Party activists and progressive groups jointly fight a transport tax but never agree on climate change. Why does this illustrate agonism?","o":["They reached full consensus on infrastructure after long deliberation","One side co-opted the other into a single, shared political programme for the city","Planners created the coalition to give marginal groups a formal voice","They cooperate on points of convergence and agree to disagree on the rest"],"a":3,"e":"Trapenberg Frick's tactical coalitions show adversaries working together pragmatically while their fundamental disagreement remains, which is agonism. Full consensus is the governance promise that agonism rejects."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"How does a 'movement' differ from a pressure group in Rydin's chapter?","o":["A movement represents a fundamental social cleavage, not just one issue","A movement is formally registered, while a pressure group is informal","A movement works only through petitions, meetings and formal lobbying of officials","A movement has one leader who speaks on behalf of all its members"],"a":0,"e":"Movements are socially more significant because they represent a social cleavage, a fundamental aspect of how society is structured. Petitions and lobbying are the institutionalised channels that urban politics looks beyond."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: for years, tenants from several districts of a Dutch city, joined by migrant associations, organise against the sale of social housing and cuts to local bus lines. They frame it as renters against owners and investors and hold assemblies in their own neighbourhoods. Which concept fits most precisely?","o":["A pluralist pressure group that formed simply because demand for housing rose","A tactical coalition of polarised adversaries who agree to disagree","An urban social movement around collective consumption, rooted in place","A growth coalition of actors with a stake in land and development"],"a":2,"e":"A lasting mobilisation over public services (housing, transport), expressing a social cleavage and based in particular places, is an urban social movement. The pluralist reading misses the cleavage and is exactly the view Rydin calls naïve."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase most precisely states Fougère and Bond's conclusion on the Denniston Plateau, as Rydin reports it?","o":["Antagonistic conflict can open up space in which agonism becomes possible","Antagonism always prevents agonism from emerging in consent processes","Agonism only arises once experts take over mediation of the conflict","Antagonism must first be suppressed by planners before any agonism can emerge"],"a":0,"e":"They found that antagonism dominated, especially in formal hearings, but that antagonistic conflict can still create space for agonism. Experts did the opposite of enabling agonism: technocratic knowledge helped keep the process post-political."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"excerpt","q":"A municipal memo argues: 'We cannot build the new district alone. Our land, the corporations' capital and the university's knowledge only achieve anything when combined; nobody commands anyone here, together we simply become able to act.' Which idea grounds this passage?","o":["Molotch's growth coalition, because elites use local government for growth","Mouffe's agonism, because the partners accept that they will keep on disagreeing","Castells' movement theory, since actors mobilise over collective services","Stone's regime 'power to', since interdependent actors pool resources to act"],"a":3,"e":"Pooling interdependent resources to gain the capacity to act, without domination, is Stone's 'power to' rather than 'power over'. The growth coalition fits less precisely: it stresses elites steering local government for property-led growth, not resource interdependence."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about urban social movements in Rydin's chapter is NOT consistent with the text?","o":["Mayer's last phase links them to globalisation and financialisation","Critics note neglect of gender and a North Atlantic bias in this work","Castells tied them mainly to workplace conflicts over wages","Their distinctive feature is a physical base in a particular place"],"a":2,"e":"Castells tied urban social movements to the means of collective consumption, such as housing and transport, not to wage conflict in the workplace. Mayer's phases, the gender and North Atlantic critiques and the place base are all in the chapter."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"who","q":"Which course reading would endorse this claim? 'Research cannot assume that conflicts will be resolved towards agreement; it must ask how planning handles power and inequality, and how to plan justly.'","o":["Raco and Imrie (2000), on government through community","Moroni, on reforming planning towards kosmos","Rydin (2021), on the urban politics frame","Olson, on the logic of collective action"],"a":2,"e":"This is the core of Rydin's urban politics framing against the governance frame: conflict is unavoidable and justice is the research task. Raco and Imrie analyse how communities are governed, not how to plan justly."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Li and Liu supplemented regime theory with 'fragmented authoritarianism' and 'strategic selectivity' to study Guangzhou's Three Old Redevelopment. Statement II: They saw China's private sector as relatively less influential than in the USA and its civil society as rather weak.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are in Rydin's account, and II is the stated reason for I: because business and civil society differ from the US case, the regime concept had to be supplemented. This is an example of the transferability problem."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about justice in Rydin's chapter are correct? I. Recognition means accepting groups as self-determined, not just giving them a voice. II. Environmental justice grew from US cases of toxic sites near communities of colour. III. Rydin argues that planning on its own can secure all four of Agyeman's aspects.","o":["II and III only","I and II only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I and II are in the text. III is wrong: because conflicts are linked to wider power and inequality, Rydin says planning alone can achieve little and justice needs wider political change."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Ravazzi and Belligni explain Turin's turn to a market-centred agenda through a regime's naming, blaming and claiming. Which criticism would a Marxist political economist most plausibly make?","o":["The study ignores that the regime is an informal institution whose path dependence shapes its options","The shift reflects capital's push for new accumulation, which the study recasts as coalition choice","The study fails to explain why members joined, since collective action needs selective incentives","The study misses how naming and claiming act as technologies that shape citizens' conduct"],"a":1,"e":"A Marxist reads the agenda shift as driven by structural pressures of accumulation and inter-urban competition, so regime analysis is too agent-based, a critique Rydin herself reports. The selective-incentive critique belongs to Olson, the path-dependence one to historical institutionalism."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs how an urban regime forms in Stone's account?","o":["Council has all needed resources, so it invites business only for legitimacy, so a formal partnership forms","Business wants growth, so it captures the council by contract, so the regime replaces elected government","The strongest actor needs allies, so it takes 'power over' the others, so a stable hierarchy of control arises","Council cannot deliver its agenda alone, so interdependent actors pool resources, so they gain 'power to' act"],"a":3,"e":"Regimes arise because local government cannot implement its agenda alone; actors with interdependent resources cooperate and so gain the capacity, 'power to', govern. The 'power over' chain swaps in exactly the kind of power Stone excludes."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs how REDWatch gained influence in Sydney (Rogers, in Rydin)?","o":["It joined state consultation, reached consensus with officials, so its plan was adopted","It preserved vanished plans as a memory, so planners used its monitory status internally","It won a court case on disclosure, so the state was forced to publish its plans in full","It organised mass protests, so the state cancelled the redevelopment and its partnerships"],"a":1,"e":"REDWatch kept documents that disappeared from state websites, an 'inconvenient corporate memory'; government planners then used its monitory status to argue internally for other outcomes. It deliberately did not seek consensus, so the first chain inverts its agonistic stance."},{"t":"alex","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why does Alexander open the chapter by calling for 'a more modest approach' to planning theory?","o":["Because empirical tests have shown that planners never decide in a rational way","Because several authors doubt whether a general theory of planning is possible","Because Habermas proved that no planning theory can be free of strategic distortion","Because planning practice has dropped rational methods in favour of intuition"],"a":1,"e":"Alexander notes that the requirements for theory are well stated but no coherent body of thought meets them, and that Mandelbaum, Rittel and Webber and others question whether a 'general theory of planning' is even possible; hence a contingency framework. The last option is wrong: he stresses that rationality is still the prevailing paradigm in practice."},{"t":"alex","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"In which cell of his framework does Alexander place optimisation, linear programming and multi-objective decision-making?","o":["Real, individual, accomplishment-oriented, next to satisficing and incrementalism","Real, collective, accomplishment-oriented, next to bargaining and negotiation","Ideal, interactive, accomplishment-oriented, next to game theory and coalitions","Ideal, individual, accomplishment-oriented, with formal utilitarian rationality"],"a":3,"e":"Cell (d) holds the formal models of instrumental and substantive rationality and the normative-analytic tools related to them, such as linear programming. Satisficing and incrementalism (cell a) describe how individuals actually decide, so they are real, not ideal, models."},{"t":"alex","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"Whose 'transactive' or 'radical' planning does Alexander list among the alternatives that cannot replace the rational paradigm?","o":["John Friedmann","Charles Lindblom","Herbert Simon","Donald Schön"],"a":0,"e":"Alexander says Friedmann's transactive or radical planning modifies some procedures of the rational model and adds substantive assumptions about ethics and society, but none of these alternatives can replace the paradigm. Lindblom is discussed too, but for disjointed incrementalism, an avoidance response."},{"t":"alex","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"On which two independent dimensions does Alexander say any discourse on planning can be mapped?","o":["Ideal versus real theory, and individual versus collective domain of action","Means versus ends, and having reasons versus giving reasons to others","Procedural versus structural focus, and concrete versus abstract level","Strategic versus communicative purpose, and private versus public interest"],"a":2,"e":"To defuse the procedural versus substantive debate, Alexander maps theories by focus of conceptualisation (totally procedural to totally structural) and level of abstraction (case-specific to universal). The first option names two of the three dimensions of his contingency framework, a different figure."},{"t":"alex","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"In the Habermasian scheme Alexander summarises, how does strategic action coordinate the behaviour of participants?","o":["Through the force of the better argument, until all participants freely agree","Through power, influence and manipulation, in pursuit of the actor's own goals","Through formal optimisation of a welfare function set by elected politicians","Through shared cultural scripts that tell each participant what is appropriate"],"a":1,"e":"Strategic action, open or covert, uses power, influence and manipulation (or distorted communication) to pursue each actor's objectives, unlike communicative action, which persuades through rational argument toward consensus. The first option describes communicative action."},{"t":"alex","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"How does Alexander say his contingency framework should be tested?","o":["By asking practitioners in a survey which planning models they prefer to use","By checking that each cell is logically derived from utilitarian principles","By showing that it predicts planning outcomes better than any rival model","Theoretically, by its fit with real factors, and pragmatically, in practice"],"a":3,"e":"Alexander says the test is both theoretical (are its dimensions a valid reflection of critical real-world factors that help describe and explain?) and pragmatic (does it answer practical dilemmas better than intuition alone?). He says no model can be perfect, so he does not claim superior prediction."},{"t":"alex","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the reasons Alexander gives why Habermas's theory of communicative action (TCA) cannot be a general paradigm for planning?","o":["It is normative: it does not claim to describe how people actually behave","It is entirely procedural, as divorced from substance as classic rationality","It is too empirical: it only records how participants actually talk together","It covers interaction but not how actors choose a course before interacting"],"a":2,"e":"Alexander criticises TCA for being normative not descriptive, procedural not substantive, and for leaving out pre-interactive deliberation. Calling it 'too empirical' reverses his point: TCA is an idealisation that Habermas himself says does not aim to characterise behaviour empirically."},{"t":"alex","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Before talks with the province, a municipal team ranks three possible routes for a new road with a multi-criteria analysis, to decide which route it will push. Which part of the universe of action is this, in Alexander's terms?","o":["Pre-interactive deliberation, which classic rationality covers but TCA cannot","Communicative action, because the team will later justify its choice to others","Strategic action, because the team bargains with the province over the route","The ritual response, because the team uses a rational tool with token doubts"],"a":0,"e":"Alexander says TCA 'cannot account for pre-interactive decision making': how a unit decides which course to pursue before entering the arena. That internal ranking is the domain of classic rationality. The bargaining with the province would be strategic action, but it comes after this step."},{"t":"alex","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why does Alexander call cases in the real, collective, consensus-oriented cell (c) 'obviously rare'?","o":["Because planning law rarely permits mediation in conflicts over land use","Because consensus exists only in ideal models and never in observed behaviour","Because public bodies seldom have the budget to hire professional mediators","Because they need neutral participants or a collectivity seeking consensus"],"a":3,"e":"Models in cell (c), such as mediation or arbitration, count only when performed by a neutral participant or a consensus-oriented collectivity; once actors seek their own goals, the process is strategic action (cell b). The second option is wrong: cell (c) explicitly contains communicative action that 'can be observed and described'."},{"t":"alex","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why does Alexander reject Simon's satisficing and Lindblom's disjointed incrementalism as a new paradigm?","o":["They abandon process models in favour of a substantive social ideology","They rest on limiting assumptions and face many of the critiques they made","They only describe collective bargaining and ignore individual decisions","They were derived from Habermas's ideal of undistorted communication"],"a":1,"e":"Alexander says these descriptive models 'have been subject to many of the same critiques that they advanced against the rational model': they too rest on limiting assumptions. They are avoidance responses, not abandonment ones, and they describe individual decision-making (cell a), not collective bargaining."},{"t":"alex","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why, according to Alexander, do earlier contingency models such as Hudson's SITAR fail to provide a new paradigm?","o":["They reject rationality altogether and rely only on practitioners' experience","They are too concrete, describing single cases without any generalisation","They combine classic and modified rationality but ignore social interaction","They borrow game theory and so assume that every actor behaves strategically"],"a":2,"e":"Existing contingency models 'limit themselves to integrating classic rationality with a few of the modified rationality models' and so ignore critical theory's valid point that rational models miss the social and interactive side of decision and action. They do not reject rationality: they build on it."},{"t":"alex","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Why does Alexander conclude that substantive paradigms such as neo-Marxism, systems or ecology approaches and public choice cannot replace rationality?","o":["They are limited to specific contexts or need an improbable act of faith","They are purely procedural and so say nothing about real cities and land","They have never been used by practitioners and so lack a professional base","They deny that actors pursue goals and so cannot explain strategic action"],"a":0,"e":"Alexander concludes that all such alternatives 'are either limited to specific contexts or, if universalized, require an improbable act of faith in their underlying ideological assumptions'. The second option reverses the critique: these are substantive, not procedural, models."},{"t":"alex","lv":2,"fmt":"inverse","q":"Alexander says rationality remains 'the normative basis' for planning methods and for planners' claims to expertise. Which demand does he therefore make of any successor paradigm?","o":["It must be built on a substantive theory of cities rather than on procedure","It must prove more accurate than rationality as a description of behaviour","It must be accepted by a free and open consensus of all affected planners","It must fill the same range of functions, alone or as a set of theories"],"a":3,"e":"Because rationality plays two roles (descriptive model and normative paradigm, both analytic and prescriptive), a successor 'must consist of a theory or model, or a set of related theories, that can fulfill the same range of functions'. Being a better description alone would cover only one role."},{"t":"alex","lv":2,"fmt":"recall","q":"Alexander quotes Habermas: 'It is not my aim to characterize behavioral dispositions empirically'. What does Alexander use this quote to show?","o":["That TCA is a descriptive theory of how planners and residents really interact","That TCA is normative: an ideal abstracted from behaviour, not a description","That Habermas rejects strategic action as an irrelevant category for planning","That communicative action can only be studied with qualitative field methods"],"a":1,"e":"The quote supports Alexander's first objection: TCA 'is abstracted from the real world and does not purport to describe real behavior'. Habermas does not reject strategic action: he includes it for completeness, and Alexander argues it describes most real interaction."},{"t":"alex","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Alexander's contingency framework, which statements are true? I: Game theory and formal coalition models belong to an ideal, interactive, accomplishment-oriented cell. II: Habermas's communicative action is placed among the real (descriptive) theories. III: Bargaining, negotiation and political 'games' are forms of strategic action in a real, collective cell. IV: An individual deliberating alone can, by definition, be consensus-oriented.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV T"],"a":1,"e":"I and III are true: game theory sits in cell (e), bargaining and negotiation in cell (b). II is false: Habermas's communicative action is ideal (cell f); only observable communicative action, such as neutral mediation, sits in the real cell (c). IV is false: that cell is empty by definition, because consensus is a property of interaction."},{"t":"alex","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Alexander, which statements about the breakdown of rationality are true? I: Rationality has not yet been superseded as the prevailing paradigm for theorists and practitioners. II: A successor may consist of a set of related theories rather than one theory. III: The ritual response replaces the rational model with practitioners' intuition and experience. IV: Contingency models share a weakness with ideological substantive models: the choice of contingencies is arbitrary.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"III is false: relying on intuition and experience is one version of the abandonment response; the ritual response keeps the rational model with only token acknowledgement of its anomalies. The rest is true: rationality 'has yet to be superseded', a successor can be 'a set of related theories', and the selection of contingencies is 'essentially arbitrary', as with ideological models."},{"t":"alex","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Alexander argues that TCA cannot account for 'pre-interactive' deliberation, how an actor decides which course to pursue before entering the arena; he leaves that to classic rationality. How would a sociological institutionalist (week 4) critique this picture of the actor?","o":["Actors do not choose goals in a vacuum: institutions supply the scripts through which they discover what they want","Actors' menu of options is narrowed by earlier policy choices that lock in routines until a critical juncture","Actors deliberate rationally, but only enter the arena if selective incentives make taking part worth their while","Actors' goals reflect their position in the relations of production, so deliberation expresses class interests"],"a":0,"e":"Sociological institutionalism holds that institutions 'establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences', so the deliberation Alexander treats as individual rational choice is itself institutionally shaped. The locked-in routines option is the historical institutionalist critique, the selective incentives option an Olsonian rational choice point, and the class option a Marxist one."},{"t":"alex","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Alexander (2005), quoted in the week 4 lecture, holds that if planning aims to transform society, 'institutional transformation must be a critical aspect of planning'. How would a historical institutionalist such as Sorensen critique this ambition?","o":["Rules only change behaviour once their meaning is taken up in people's mental maps and shared symbols","Institutions embody dispersed knowledge and evolve unintentionally, so reform should stay modest","Arrangements lock in through path dependence; major change tends to come at critical junctures","Planners are self-interested too, so new institutions will mainly serve the officials who design them"],"a":2,"e":"Historical institutionalism stresses path dependence: earlier choices create developmental pathways, and major change comes at critical junctures (Sorensen's Toronto case) followed by contestation within the pathway. The dispersed-knowledge option is Moroni's evolutionary critique, the mental-maps option Dembski and Salet's, and the last a rational choice (state failure) point."},{"t":"alex","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"A hypothetical planning faculty drops all teaching of process models. Students are told to rely on the experience and intuition they will build up on the job, and planners' roles are kept deliberately narrow. Which of Alexander's responses to the breakdown of rationality is this?","o":["The ritual response: the rational model is still followed, with token doubts","The abandonment response, in its version at a lower level of abstraction","The avoidance response: descriptive decision models replace rationality","The search response: a radically different model is offered instead"],"a":1,"e":"Alexander's abandonment response holds that any process model is unnecessary; one version substitutes, at a lower level of abstraction, 'the practitioner's intuition and experience' or 'a pragmatic limitation of planners' roles'. Avoidance would swap in other decision models such as satisficing, which is not what happens here."},{"t":"alex","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Alexander considers the long debate between 'procedural' and 'substantive' planning theory essentially sterile. Statement II: Focus (procedural to structural) and level of abstraction are independent dimensions, and any theory that generalises across cases must abstract, whatever its focus.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are Alexander's claims, and II is his reason for I: critics equate procedural with abstract and substantive with real, but abstraction is a separate dimension, so the debate was really about the right level of abstraction, a 'contrived conflict between essentially complementary concepts'."},{"t":"alex","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Alexander accepts Habermas's communicative action as the best description of how interaction in planning actually takes place. Statement II: In Habermas's scheme, strategic action coordinates participants through power, influence and manipulation rather than through rational argument.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"I is false: Alexander argues that 'it is strategic action that describes interactive behavior most accurately', while communicative action is a normative ideal. II is true: that is how he summarises Habermas's distinction between strategic and communicative action."},{"t":"alex","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs Alexander's argument that 'one paradigm cannot supplant another'?","o":["Rationality covers interaction, not deliberation; TCA covers deliberation, not interaction; so each needs the other","Rationality covers deliberation; TCA covers interaction; since TCA is more realistic, it should replace rationality","Rationality covers deliberation; TCA covers interaction; the lifeworld spans both, so a paradigm must include both","Rationality covers deliberation; TCA covers interaction; both are normative, so a descriptive model must replace both"],"a":2,"e":"Alexander's chain: classic rationality ignores the interactive, collective side; TCA cannot account for pre-interactive deliberation; the lifeworld comprises both, so the two are complementary and any new paradigm 'must include both'. The first chain swaps the domains; the second wrongly calls TCA realistic, while he calls it normative."},{"t":"alex","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Combine Alexander with Rydin's governmental model (week 3), in which politicians set the goals and expert planners choose the best means. Where would Alexander's framework place this model, and what would he say it misses?","o":["In the individual, accomplishment-oriented cells of rationality; it misses the interactive, strategic side","In the consensus-oriented cells, since goals are set democratically; it misses pre-interactive deliberation","In the real, collective cell of strategic action; it misses the formal optimisation of ideal rational models","Outside the framework, as an abandoned model; it misses the substantive knowledge that neo-Marxists offer"],"a":0,"e":"The governmental model is instrumental rationality: one unit (the planner) links means to given ends, which fits Alexander's individual, accomplishment-oriented cells (d as an ideal, a once bounded). His critique of rationality then applies: it ignores interaction, such as the bargaining in cell (b). Political goal-setting is not consensus-oriented communicative action."},{"t":"alex","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"A province opens 'dialogue tables' to agree a regional wind plan with two energy firms and thousands of scattered residents. Combining Alexander with Olson (Hindmoor), what should a planner expect?","o":["Communicative action: open dialogue lets the better argument produce a fair consensus for all","Strategic interaction in which the two firms organise well while dispersed residents free-ride","A ritual response: the province consults for show while optimising the plan with one CBA","Residents will organise more easily than the firms, because their numbers give them weight"],"a":1,"e":"Alexander expects most real interaction to be strategic (cell b), not communicative; Olson adds that small groups with concentrated stakes organise more easily, while large dispersed groups face a collective action problem (a tendency, Hindmoor warns). The first option takes the normative ideal of cell (f) as a description, which Alexander rejects."},{"t":"rc","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"How does the HC6 lecture, following Ricardo, define the 'rent' that landowners try to capture?","o":["The monthly payment a tenant makes to a landlord for the use of a dwelling or plot","The profit a developer earns by building on land after paying all construction costs","Income from owning land or natural resources, over and above the cost of production","The tax a municipality levies on land value gains that follow from its own rezoning"],"a":2,"e":"The slide defines rent with Ricardo as income from owning land or natural resources over and above the cost of production: 'the unearned value of land', which landowners act rationally to capture. A developer's building profit is a return on production, not unearned value, so the second option is wrong."},{"t":"rc","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"Which planning decision is an example of what the lecture calls a 'regulatory taking'?","o":["Downzoning a plot, so that the owner may build much less than before","Rezoning farmland to housing, so that the owner can sell it as building land","Building a new tram line, so that homes near the stops become more valuable","Taxing tourists per night, so that hosts can pass the cost on to their guests"],"a":0,"e":"The slide lists excluding land from zoning, conservation or heritage status, downzoning and infrastructure decisions as regulatory takings: public decisions that lower the value of private property, often followed by compensation for 'planning damage'. Rezoning to housing does the opposite: it creates betterment."},{"t":"rc","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"According to Coase (1960), as presented in the lecture, which two conditions must hold for bargaining to produce an efficient outcome?","o":["Property rights must be clearly defined, and transaction costs must be zero","A neutral planner must mediate, and all parties must share the same goals","The state must hold all land rights, and it must tax every externality","The injured party must hold the right, and the number of parties must be two"],"a":0,"e":"Coase's claim: if property rights are clearly defined (who has the right to what) and transaction costs are zero, bargaining leads to an efficient outcome regardless of who holds the initial right. That last clause is why the 'injured party must hold the right' option is wrong."},{"t":"rc","lv":1,"fmt":"recall","q":"On which source does the lecture base its rational choice reading of Airbnb?","o":["Gil (2024), on the financialisation and assetisation of housing","Yates (2023), on corporate grassroots lobbying by online platforms","Von Briel and Dolnicar (2021), on how Airbnb regulation evolved","Gurran et al. (2018), on Coase, property rights and regulation"],"a":3,"e":"The slide names other lenses (Gil on financialisation, Von Briel and Dolnicar on regulation over time, Yates on platform lobbying) but says 'Today: rational choice-based theories (based on Gurran et al. 2018)', whose paper links Airbnb to Coase and property rights. Yates is tempting because lobbying also appears in the Olson part, but it is not the base text."},{"t":"rc","lv":1,"fmt":"define","q":"Why does the lecture say landowners have a de facto 'veto right' in land-use planning?","o":["Because Dutch law requires the written consent of every owner before a plan is adopted","Because owners elect the municipal council that has to approve the land-use plan","Because owners can always claim full compensation for any change in a zoning plan","Because a plan only leads to building if owners decide to act on it with their land"],"a":3,"e":"Planning mostly permits rather than forces development: if owners do not want to develop, a plan alone changes little, hence Needham's 'Land-use planning will only succeed if it can influence private decisions'. Compensation is only claimed for planning damage, not for every change, so the third option overstates."},{"t":"rc","lv":1,"fmt":"example","q":"In the lecture's Airbnb case, which of these is an externality?","o":["The extra income a host earns compared with renting to a permanent tenant","The cheaper and more 'authentic' stay that guests get than in a hotel","Guests' noise that neighbours bear but the host never pays for","The tourist tax that guests pay to the city on top of the nightly room price"],"a":2,"e":"An externality is an effect of one actor's action on others that is not reflected in prices: neighbours bear noise and disruption that the host's price does not include. The host's extra income and the guests' cheaper stay are the private incentives of the parties to the deal itself."},{"t":"rc","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Change the lecture's noisy factory example: quiet is still worth €1,000 to the homeowner, but insulation now costs €1,200. Assuming zero transaction costs, what does Coase predict?","o":["No insulation either way; if the homeowner has the right, the factory compensates","Insulation is installed if the homeowner holds the right to quiet, but not otherwise","Insulation is installed either way, because the homeowner's quiet must be protected","No insulation either way, and since rights no longer matter, nobody pays anybody"],"a":0,"e":"Insulating now costs more than quiet is worth, so the efficient outcome is no insulation, whoever holds the right; only who pays whom differs. If the homeowner holds the right, the factory pays compensation (up to €1,000), which is cheaper than insulating. The option that ties insulation to the homeowner's right denies Coase's point that the initial right does not decide the outcome."},{"t":"rc","lv":2,"fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the problems that rational choice theorists, according to the lecture, see in 'one-size-fits-all' Airbnb rules?","o":["Illegal rentals are hard to detect, so the rules are difficult to enforce","Uniform rules ignore residents' shared norms about what a good street is","Inspections, enforcement and bureaucracy bring high transaction costs","The problem may shift elsewhere, an unintended waterbed effect of the rule"],"a":1,"e":"The slide lists enforcement difficulties, platforms withholding data, transaction costs, legal challenges ('regulatory takings') and unintended outcomes such as the waterbed effect. Shared norms and cognitive scripts are a sociological institutionalist concern, not part of the rational choice list."},{"t":"rc","lv":2,"fmt":"contrast","q":"Coasean-inspired tools either better define rights or reduce transaction costs. Which pairing fits the lecture's logic?","o":["Platform monitoring defines rights; tradable permits cut transaction costs","Tradable permits cut transaction costs; a 60-night cap defines a right","A hosting tax defines a right; collective VvE talks define a right as well","Tradable permits define a right; platform monitoring cuts transaction costs"],"a":3,"e":"Tradable permits turn hosting nights into a clearly defined, tradable right (a market for nights); digital monitoring of platforms lowers information and enforcement costs. A 60-night cap is the restrictive one-size-fits-all rule the Coasean approach wants to move beyond."},{"t":"rc","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"Owners of large villas on a village edge lobby the province to keep a strict green belt around the village, so that their views and property values stay intact. How does the lecture, following Taylor (2016), read this?","o":["As irrational behaviour, since rezoning would raise their land values","As altruism, since the green belt protects nature for all residents","As rent-seeking: preserving growth limits is rational for these owners","As free-riding, since the villa owners let others pay for the lobby"],"a":2,"e":"Taylor distinguishes owners who aim to preserve restrictions on growth from those who try to avoid controls on future development; the lecture stresses that both are rational from a rent-seeking perspective. Calling the villa owners irrational misses that their amenity and value stability are interests too."},{"t":"rc","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A student wants to study short-stay rental from a rational choice perspective, as the lecture presents it. Which research design fits that knowledge stance best?","o":["Life stories of five hosts, to show how each makes sense of hosting","A discourse analysis of how council debates frame 'the tourist'","An archive study of how one city's rules grew out of local history","A model of hosts' costs and benefits, tested across many cities"],"a":3,"e":"The lecture says rational choice generally takes a positivist stance: it assumes universal rational behaviour and aims at generalisable explanations through game theory, modelling and cost-benefit analysis. Life stories and discourse analysis look for situated meaning, and an archive study of one city's path fits historical institutionalism."},{"t":"rc","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A national government changes property law so that unused building rights expire after five years. Owners who had been holding on to building land start to develop it. How does the lecture say rational choice explains this?","o":["As reproduction: insiders use their strategic position to keep the status quo","As transformation: actors deliberately redesign rights, changing incentives","As an emergent order that no actor intended or could have designed at all","As a critical juncture, after which the old pathway simply cannot return"],"a":1,"e":"The lecture says rational choice can explain both: reproduction when actors use strategic positions to protect interests, and transformation when actors deliberately redesign rules and incentives, as in Needham's 'property rights can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use'. A critical juncture is historical institutionalist vocabulary."},{"t":"rc","lv":2,"fmt":"define","q":"Which statement best matches the lecture's core assumption about property rights and other institutions?","o":["They mirror a natural order of ownership, so planners should leave them as they are","They express cultural scripts, so they change only as slowly as shared meanings do","They shape incentives and so strategies; they can be redesigned, with side-effects","They are set once by the constitution, so rational actors take them as fixed givens"],"a":2,"e":"The core assumption on the slide: property rights and other institutions shape incentives and therefore the strategies actors pursue; they can be deliberately changed, though this may lead to unexpected side-effects. The cultural scripts option is the sociological institutionalist view."},{"t":"rc","lv":2,"fmt":"apply","q":"A city bans short-stay rental outright in its historic centre. Why do rational choice theorists warn that such a rule risks legal challenges as a 'regulatory taking'?","o":["Because owners lose value they expected from their rights and seek redress","Because the ban is set by the council instead of by the national parliament","Because neighbours may sue the city for failing to enforce the ban strictly","Because platforms are foreign firms that do not fall under Dutch planning law"],"a":0,"e":"A regulatory taking is public regulation that lowers the value of private property; owners who bought homes to rent to tourists lose expected income and value, so they contest the rule or claim compensation. The lecture lists this risk among the problems of one-size-fits-all rules; neighbours suing over weak enforcement is a different issue."},{"t":"rc","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"Indicate True or False for each statement according to rational choice theory, as presented in the lecture. I: The self-interest that actors maximise is not limited to money. II: With zero transaction costs, who holds the initial right decides whether the efficient outcome is reached. III: Owners who lobby to keep growth limits around their land can be as rational as owners pushing for rezoning. IV: Because the theory is agency-focused, it can explain only how arrangements are reproduced, not how they change.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: core assumption 1 says self-interest is 'not only economic'. II is false: under Coase the initial right only decides who pays whom. III is true: Taylor shows that both preserving restrictions and avoiding controls are rational from a rent-seeking perspective. IV is false: rational choice can explain transformation too, when actors deliberately redesign rules and incentives."},{"t":"rc","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"Indicate True or False for each statement according to rational choice theory. I: Each neighbour suffering from short-stay rentals has an incentive to leave the lobbying to others. II: A Coasean planner's first choice is a uniform ban on short-stay rental in all residential zones. III: Municipalities and planners are themselves self-interested actors, so the state can fail as well as the market. IV: Digital monitoring of platforms can bring outcomes closer to efficiency by lowering information and enforcement costs.","o":["I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: neighbours are a large, diffuse group whose members can free-ride (Olson). II is false: a Coasean approach seeks to clarify rights and reduce transaction costs rather than restrict across the board. III is true: rational choice applies to all actors and looks at state failure as well as market failure. IV is true: monitoring is one of the lecture's Coasean-inspired examples."},{"t":"rc","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"The lecture's Swiss hypothesis explains why densification works in Switzerland: the BGBB removes outsiders' speculative interest, so insiders rationally accept inward growth. How would a historical institutionalist critique this explanation?","o":["Owners' stance on growth reflects shared norms and cognitive scripts about land, not just their calculation of rents","It takes the BGBB as given; the real question is how earlier choices locked that law in and why it has persisted","It treats land rents as neutral incentives, while they are a way in which capital extracts value from urban space","No designer could have planned such an order: the BGBB works because it evolved slowly as a general, abstract rule"],"a":1,"e":"Historical institutionalism stresses path dependence: rules are the legacy of earlier choices that create a developmental pathway, so it would ask how the BGBB came about and persisted, and why other countries reversed their limits. The norms and scripts option is the sociological institutionalist critique, the capital option a Marxist one and the evolved-rule option Moroni's evolutionary view."},{"t":"rc","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"A core assumption in the lecture is that property rights and other institutions shape incentives and can be deliberately changed to steer behaviour. How would a sociological institutionalist critique this assumption?","o":["New rules change behaviour only when their meaning is taken up in actors' shared norms and mental maps","Rules cannot be redesigned at will: earlier choices lock them in until a critical juncture opens change","Redesigning property rights within capitalism will mainly serve owners, since rights express class power","Rules embody dispersed knowledge no reformer has, so changes should be modest, general and gradual"],"a":0,"e":"Sociological institutionalism treats institutions as cultural norms and cognitive scripts: actors follow what is appropriate, so a redesigned right works only if its meaning is embedded, as Dembski and Salet argue for symbolic markers. Lock-in until a critical juncture is the historical institutionalist critique; dispersed knowledge and modest reform is Moroni's evolutionary one."},{"t":"rc","lv":3,"fmt":"case","q":"A hypothetical Dutch city bans short-stay rental in its canal district. An investor who bought three apartments there to let to tourists sees their market value fall and takes the city to court. Which concept from the lecture fits the investor's situation most precisely?","o":["An externality: an effect of one actor on others that is not reflected in prices","A regulatory taking: a public rule that lowers the value of private property","Rent-seeking: lobbying the political process to capture an unearned land value","A collective action problem: a large, diffuse group fails to act on its interest"],"a":1,"e":"The loss comes from public regulation that reduces the value of the investor's property, which is a regulatory taking; the lecture expects such rules to face legal challenges. An externality is tempting because value falls, but externalities arise from private actors' actions (such as guests' noise), not from a public rule."},{"t":"rc","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: In the lecture's noisy factory example, Coase expects insulation to be installed even when the factory has the right to make noise. Statement II: With zero transaction costs, the party that values an outcome most can pay the other party to bring it about.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true, and II is the reason for I: quiet is worth €1,000 to the homeowner and insulation costs €700, so the homeowner pays the factory €700 and the factory insulates. If the homeowner held the right, the factory would insulate rather than pay €1,000; the outcome is the same either way."},{"t":"rc","lv":4,"fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rational choice theorists expect a 60-night cap on short-stay rental to deliver an efficient outcome once it has been written into law. Statement II: Because rules shape incentives, actors may respond with strategies that move the problem elsewhere.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"I is false: the lecture lists enforcement difficulties, withheld platform data, transaction costs, legal challenges and unintended outcomes as problems of such one-size-fits-all rules. II is true: the waterbed effect is exactly a strategic response that shifts the problem, and it is a reason against I, not for it."},{"t":"rc","lv":4,"fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs why, for Coase, the initial allocation of rights does not decide the outcome?","o":["Rights are clear and bargaining is free; the right-holder can refuse any offer; so the holder's preferred use results; the initial right decides the use","Rights are clear and bargaining is free; the party valuing the outcome most pays the other; so the efficient use results; the right only decides who pays","Rights are clear but bargaining is costly; parties still bargain without any loss; so the efficient use results; the right only decides who pays whom","Rights are clear and bargaining is free; the state sets a price on the externality; so the efficient use results; the right only decides who pays"],"a":1,"e":"Coase's chain: clear rights and zero transaction costs let the party that values the outcome most buy it from the other, so the efficient use results and the initial right only settles who pays whom. The third chain breaks the zero-cost condition; the fourth replaces bargaining with a state-set price, a tax logic rather than Coase's."},{"t":"rc","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"Needham (week 3) notes that interests in clean air are protected by public law, not by property rights that holders enforce in court. Combining Needham with the lecture's account of Coase, why?","o":["Clean air is not a real interest, so it cannot become a property right in court","Polluters always hold the initial right, so victims have nothing they could trade","Many diffuse victims make bargaining and court action too costly to organise","Courts cannot measure pollution, so only experts can set an efficient level"],"a":2,"e":"Needham says the choice between property rights and public law is partly technical: with diffuse effects and hundreds of victims who cannot organise a lawsuit, public law is used. In Coase's terms, information costs, the multiple-parties problem and free-riding make transaction costs far from zero. The second option is wrong: for Coase the initial right does not block bargaining."},{"t":"rc","lv":4,"fmt":"multi","q":"In Taylor's Melbourne case, landowners got a betterment tax (the GAIC) shifted onto future buyers. Combining Taylor with Olson (Hindmoor) and the lecture, why did the landowners win?","o":["Buyers organised a counter-lobby, but planners sided with the more expert party","A few owners had large concentrated stakes; future buyers were diffuse and latent","Landowners are a privileged group, since each one paid the entire cost of the tax","Containment made land plentiful, so the tax had little value for the government"],"a":1,"e":"Taylor shows insiders rent-seeking successfully while costs pass to housing consumers, the outsiders; Olson explains why: concentrated stakes make organising worthwhile, whereas a large, dispersed group of future buyers stays latent. A privileged group is one where a member pays the full cost of the collective good, which is not what happened."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin (2021), which of these statements about theory are true? I. Theory is best seen as a law connecting variables, tested by searching for confirming or refuting evidence. II. Theory is a heuristic aid: a way to try to know what is essentially unknowable in its completeness. III. Every theory carries an ontology, normative positions and an epistemology, even when some of these stay unstated. IV. Choosing a theory brings value judgements into research, which Rydin calls often profoundly satisfying.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II F · III F · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV F"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin rejects the law-like, natural-science model of theory (following Flyvbjerg et al.: complexity and the double hermeneutic) and adopts theory as a heuristic aid and coherent narrative (p. 9). Each theory carries ontology, normative positions and epistemology, so choosing one makes value judgements part of research, which she finds 'often profoundly satisfying' (pp. 9–10). The tempting pattern that marks I as true confuses the model she describes with the one she adopts."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin (2021), which of these statements about empiricism and phronesis are true? I. Empiricism fails partly because nobody can collect all data, so choosing which data to collect already involves values. II. Pure empiricism produces a disparate field in which detailed findings are hard to connect to each other. III. Rydin rejects phronesis mainly because it pays no attention to power in planning. IV. Rydin concludes that phronesis cannot be combined with an explicit theoretical framing.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are Rydin's two objections to the 'modernist settlement' of letting facts speak (p. 10). III is false: phronesis explicitly attends to 'the ineliminable presence of power'; her objections are that it attacks a straw man of theory, treats good research as a virtue of character, and hides its own theoretical choices (p. 11). IV is the tempting trap: she says phronesis can be combined with theory, but should go with an explicit theoretical framing."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to the course framework (HC1 and the HC8 midterm Q&A), which of these statements are true? I. People exercise agency only when they act against existing structures. II. Monism and pluralism concern how many ways of knowing count as legitimate; universal and situated concern how broadly a claim applies. III. Normative commitments enter a theory mainly through its evaluative and prescriptive claims. IV. A theory that treats knowledge as monist must also treat it as universal.","o":["I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":1,"e":"HC8 stresses that agency also covers choices made within existing structures, so I is false. II is the HC8 distinction between the two knowledge axes, and III follows the HC1 slides that highlight 'are fair', 'should be strive[n] for' and 'should good planners'. IV is the tempting trap: HC8 shows the axes are independent (pluralist plus universal is possible, and monist plus situated, perhaps critical realism)."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Rydin (2021) argues that a theory is a heuristic aid: a coherent narrative judged partly by how persuasively it illuminates empirical research, not a law to be tested. How would a positivist in the rational choice tradition critique this?","o":["Any general framework abstracts from context; good research is practical wisdom about how to act in one specific setting, aware of power.","Treating theory as a freely chosen set of ideas hides that ideas reflect the material relations of capitalist production.","No researcher can gather the dispersed, local knowledge of countless actors, so one coherent account of planning oversteps.","A narrative judged by persuasiveness cannot be falsified; theory should yield general, testable hypotheses about incentives."],"a":3,"e":"A positivist rational choice scholar wants theory as general, testable propositions (Olson's logic of collective action is built that way), so persuasive narratives look unfalsifiable. The context-bound practical wisdom critique belongs to Flyvbjerg's phronesis, the material-relations critique to Marxism, and the dispersed-knowledge critique to Hayek."},{"t":"fw","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Rydin (2021) argues that planning research needs an explicit theoretical framework, because data never speak for themselves. How would a phronetic researcher in Flyvbjerg's line critique this?","o":["Frameworks must be stated as laws linking variables and tested against data, not chosen according to the researcher's values.","Which framework seems persuasive is shaped by class interests, so the researcher's free choice of theory masks ideology.","Searching for one coherent framework repeats central planning's error of assuming that a single mind can grasp the whole of society.","General frameworks matter less than situated judgement on where a problem is going, who gains and loses, and what should be done."],"a":3,"e":"Flyvbjerg's phronesis asks four value-rational questions (where are we going, who gains and loses by which mechanisms of power, is it desirable, what should we do) and doubts the value of general theory for social science. Rydin's reply is that phronesis hides its own theory, but the critique itself fits phronesis. The law-testing option is positivist, the class-interest one Marxist, the one-mind error Hayekian."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin (2007), which of these statements about knowledge claims are true? I. Knowledge differs from information and data because it specifies a causal relationship. II. Calling residents' claims 'knowledge' is wrong because lay experience has no value in planning. III. Knowledge claims rank above ethical claims, so they should weigh more heavily in planning decisions. IV. Engaging different knowledges is fundamentally different from engaging different voices.","o":["I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV F"],"a":1,"e":"I is Rydin's definition (MS p. 4) and IV her verdict on deliberation (MS p. 8). II is false: local experience can be an important basis for knowledge claims, it is just not a sufficient condition on its own. III is the tempting trap: Rydin explicitly says this is not a ranking; knowledge claims are not more important than ethical claims, only different, and a process must be able to tell them apart."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin's (2007) typology of knowledge claims, which of these statements are true? I. Normative knowledge of the desired State B1 still counts as knowledge, because a goal must engage with possible realities. II. A planned State B1 should be judged against the predicted State B, not only against the current State A. III. Empirical claims about the current State A come only from experts, so lay input belongs in normative debate. IV. With careful framing, an empirical description of an area can be made free of value choices.","o":["I F · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"Rydin keeps normative claims as knowledge because 'not any imaginings will serve' (MS p. 17), and adds type 2 (predicted State B) because B1 must be judged against what would happen anyway. III is false: empirical claims come in lay and expert forms, hence 'experiential knowledge' and forums where lay and expert meet. IV is the tempting trap: none of the types is value-free; describing an area by market prices or by child poverty already makes a difference."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin (2007), which of these statements about her own position are true? I. Her position is pluralist: actors other than certified experts can hold valid knowledge. II. Because knowledge is socially constructed, no claim can be judged better than another. III. Contemporary planning theory has been better at opening-up than at closing-down. IV. Her stance can be called realist constructivism: knowledge is socially constructed yet engages material reality.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin accepts many knowledges and actors (pluralism), says theory is better at opening-up than closing-down (MS p. 13), and follows Wynne's 'realist constructivism'. The tempting trap is II: social construction does not mean anything goes; she wants arenas where claims are tested and recognised, which the lecture calls soft relativism rather than strong relativism."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Rydin (2007) argues that planners should give voice to many claims, then test them and recognise only some as knowledge. How would a radical planning theorist in Sandercock's line critique this?","o":["Testing arenas leave untouched the class interests of landowners and developers, and it is these that decide what actually gets built.","Knowledge relevant to land use is dispersed and tacit, so no arena can gather and test enough of it to guide a plan.","Planners who run testing arenas pursue their own interests and will recognise the claims that expand their own budgets.","Testing claims against validity criteria privileges expert ways of knowing and silences the stories of the most disadvantaged."],"a":3,"e":"Sandercock calls for an 'epistemology of multiplicity' (dialogue, experience, symbolic and non-verbal evidence) and the empowerment of the most disadvantaged, so closing-down looks like a return of the expert as 'the knower'. Rydin answers that it is patronising to assume lay people only offer stories, but the critique fits Sandercock. The class-interest option is Marxist, the dispersed-knowledge one Hayekian, the budget-maximising one public choice."},{"t":"know","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Rydin (2007) argues that knowledge is socially constructed, yet claims can still be tested and recognised in hybrid forums that include lay participants. How would a positivist planner of the systems era critique this?","o":["The criteria used to test claims are themselves effects of power, so closing-down only reproduces the truths of dominant groups.","Treating knowledge as competing claims ignores that whose knowledge counts follows from who owns the land and the capital in a city.","A focus on testing truth claims neglects value-rational deliberation about whether a project is desirable and who gains.","Validity comes from sound scientific method, not negotiation, so forums with lay participants dilute objective expert knowledge."],"a":3,"e":"Systems planning rested on the modernist consensus that experts mirror nature through method, so a positivist sees hybrid forums as a loss of objectivity. The power-effect critique is Foucauldian, the ownership critique Marxist, and the value-rational critique belongs to Flyvbjerg's phronesis."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hayek's 'Planning and democracy', which of these statements are true? I. Hayek's individualism rests on the assumption that people are selfish. II. 'Social ends' are identical ends of many individuals, so common action belongs where individual ends coincide. III. The probability of agreement falls as the scope of state action widens. IV. Delegating the making of general rules, even to local authorities, can be sensible.","o":["I F · II F · III F · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"Hayek says individualism 'does not assume ... that man is egoistic or selfish'; it rests on the limits of any mind's view of needs (p. 44), so I is false. II and III come from pp. 44–45. IV is the tempting trap: Hayek objects only to delegating discretion over particular cases, not to delegating general rule-making (p. 49)."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hayek's 'Planning and democracy', which of these statements about democracy are true? I. Democracy is the highest political end and must be defended whatever it costs. II. A homogeneous, doctrinaire majority could be as oppressive as a dictatorship. III. Democratically conferred power cannot become arbitrary, because its source is the people. IV. Only within a competitive system based on free disposal over private property is democracy possible.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"Hayek calls democracy 'essentially a means, a utilitarian device', not the highest end (p. 52), and warns that a doctrinaire majority could be oppressive. III is the tempting trap: his final lesson is that 'it is not the source but the limitation of power which prevents it from being arbitrary' (p. 53). IV restates his claim on pp. 51–52."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hayek, which of these statements about why parliaments fail at planning are true? I. The failure lies mainly in the poor quality of individual representatives. II. Majority voting suits choices between limited alternatives, but there need not be a majority view on everything. III. A coherent economic plan can be built clause by clause through compromise in parliament. IV. Planning must end in dictatorship because dictators always destroy all personal freedom.","o":["I F · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":0,"e":"Hayek blames the task, not the representatives (pp. 47–48), and says 'it is a superstition to believe that there must be a majority view on everything'. A plan needs 'a unitary conception', so it cannot be voted clause by clause. IV is the tempting trap: he explicitly does not claim dictatorship must extirpate freedom; planning leads to dictatorship because dictatorship is the most effective instrument of coercion."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Hayek argues: 'It is the price of democracy that the possibilities of conscious control are restricted to the fields where true agreement exists, and that in some fields things must be left to chance.' How would a defender of the governmental (rational planning) model critique this?","o":["Politicians can set goals democratically while experts choose the means through evidence, so delegation need not be arbitrary.","Leaving outcomes to the market is not chance at all; it reproduces the power of owners over all those who must sell their labour.","Agreement is not a fixed stock to be discovered; stakeholders can build shared understanding through open deliberation.","Individual ends are not formed in isolated minds but shaped by shared norms and taken-for-granted cultural scripts."],"a":0,"e":"The governmental model separates politics (setting goals) from rational, expert choice of means, so it denies that delegating to planners must slide into arbitrary discretion. The market-power critique is Marxist, the deliberation critique communicative planning, and the cultural-scripts critique sociological institutionalism."},{"t":"hayek","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Hayek holds that scales of value exist only in individual minds, so the individual should be 'the ultimate judge of his ends'. How would a sociological institutionalist critique this?","o":["Under capitalism people's ends are distorted by estranged labour, so what they choose reflects alienation rather than freedom.","Preferences are not formed in isolated minds; norms, cognitive scripts and a logic of appropriateness shape what people want.","Even when individuals agree on a common end, each has an incentive to free-ride, so agreement alone does not secure any collective good.","Experts can weigh many competing needs through systematic analysis, so no single mind has to survey them all."],"a":1,"e":"Sociological institutionalists (as in Dembski & Salet) see institutions as internalised and shaping basic preferences, so Hayek's starting point of given individual ends is itself questioned. Estranged labour is Marx's critique, free-riding Olson's rational choice point, and expert analysis the governmental model's answer."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Needham (2012), which of these statements are true? I. Land-use planning succeeds only if it can influence the voluntary decisions of owners. II. Property rights are natural rights that exist before the state recognises them. III. A property right is a way of using land that is recognised and protected by the courts. IV. Planning is a command-and-control system in which the authority decides how land is used.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T"],"a":0,"e":"Needham writes that 'Land-use planning will succeed only if it can influence the private decisions' (p. 24) and defines a property right as a use 'recognized by, and protected by, the courts' (p. 27). II is false: against Locke, rights are created by the state (Bromley). IV is the tempting trap: Needham says planning is not really command and control, since owners still decide unless the authority owns the land."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Needham (2012), which of these statements about how interests in land are protected are true? I. Whether an interest is protected at all is partly a political decision. II. Clean air is protected by public law partly because its many affected victims would struggle to co-operate in court. III. A right of support suits private law because the cause and culprit are clear and few parties are involved. IV. Once the split between private-law and public-law protection is made, it cannot be revised.","o":["I T · II F · III F · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"The door-colour example shows the political side, and the right of support versus clean air shows the technical side (p. 28). IV is the tempting trap: because rights are created, they are a 'policy variable' that 'can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use' (pp. 28–29), even if ideology and the ECHR slow change."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Needham (2012), which of these statements about planning and markets are true? I. 'Planning or the market?' is the wrong question; the relevant question is the mix of the two. II. Needham evaluates instruments by whether they maximise economic efficiency. III. His interest in property rights matches libertarian attacks that deny external effects. IV. Following Coase, harm is reciprocal: restraining A to protect B harms A.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"Since most land-use decisions are voluntary exchanges, Needham (with Lindblom and Buitelaar) says only the mix of planning and market is interesting (p. 29), and he uses Coase's reciprocity (p. 30). III is false: he explicitly separates his approach from libertarian attacks on planning. II is the tempting trap: he follows Williamson's 'remediability', comparing how effectively instruments reach politically set goals, not assumed maximum efficiency."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Needham argues that planning does not serve an abstract 'public interest' but protects 'the identifiable interests of many identifiable private persons'. How would a Marxist political economist critique this?","o":["Planning should rest on expert analysis of external effects in the public interest, not on adding up individual interests.","Treating planning as protection of private interests hides how it secures capital accumulation and the power of property owners.","Any authority weighing many people's interests in land needs a scale of values that nobody has, so it must end in arbitrary discretion.","What counts as an 'interest' in land is set by cultural norms, which is why a neighbour's door colour seems trivial."],"a":1,"e":"For Marxist political economy, planning is structurally tied to capital accumulation and class power over land, so describing it as neutral protection of identifiable interests obscures whose interests prevail. The external-effects option is the governmental, welfare-economics view Needham rejects, the scale-of-values option is Hayekian, and the cultural-norms option sociological institutionalist."},{"t":"needham","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Needham argues that because property rights are created by the state, they are a policy variable that can be deliberately redesigned to steer land use. How would a historical institutionalist critique this?","o":["Rights are embedded in cultural norms and taken-for-granted scripts, so any redesign meets meanings that planners themselves share.","Insiders who gain from the current rights will lobby to capture any redesign, so reform turns into a new source of rent-seeking.","Rights are locked in by past choices and the interests built on them, so they change mostly at critical junctures, not by design.","Rules should be few, general and abstract and evolve by trial and error, not be engineered towards specific land-use outcomes."],"a":2,"e":"Historical institutionalism stresses path dependence: once rights are established, increasing returns and vested interests make them durable, with change concentrated at critical junctures. The cultural-scripts option is sociological institutionalism (the HC8 contrast), rent-seeking by insiders is Taylor's rational choice argument, and the evolutionary rules option is Moroni's."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hindmoor's account of Olson, which of these statements are true? I. Olson argues that free-riding makes collective action impossible. II. Because Olson defines public goods by non-excludability alone, common-pool goods face the collective action problem too. III. The link between large group size and latency is at most an empirical tendency. IV. In the assurance game, staying home is the dominant strategy, so cooperation is hard to reach.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":3,"e":"II follows from Box 5.1 (p. 103) and III is Hindmoor's own qualification (p. 121). IV is false: the assurance game has no dominant strategy and cooperation should be easily achieved. I is the tempting trap: Hindmoor insists that reading Olson as saying collective action is impossible 'is simply not the case' (p. 102); Olson says groups face a problem they may overcome."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hindmoor's account of the solutions to collective action problems, which of these statements are true? I. With process benefits the problem 'simply dissolves', because the reward is tied to taking part itself. II. Free-market economists answer the tragedy of the commons by calling for a coercive leviathan. III. Conditional cooperation is more likely when leaving the group is costly and contributions can be monitored cheaply. IV. Successful collective action usually requires everyone to give up self-interest.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"Process benefits (Chong, kibbutz gossip) cannot be enjoyed without joining (pp. 117–118), and conditional cooperation thrives with long-term interaction, costly exit, cheap monitoring and tight-knit groups (pp. 120–121). II is false: environmentalists invoked the leviathan, free-market economists proposed property rights and tradeable permits. IV is the tempting trap: Hindmoor says it does not usually require that, though in its early stages 'it may however require that a few are prepared to do so' (p. 127)."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"For each statement, decide whether it is true according to Olson's logic as Hindmoor presents it. I. Neighbours bothered by many holiday rentals in their street will organise, because they share a common interest. II. A few large rental investors may organise more easily than thousands of dispersed neighbours. III. A small group is certain to organise, because its members can see who contributes. IV. A residents' association that offers members free legal advice on planning appeals uses a selective incentive.","o":["I F · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"Shared interest alone does not produce collective action, so I is false, while a few actors with concentrated stakes face a smaller problem (II). Free legal advice for members only is the textbook positive selective incentive (IV; compare union legal aid). III is the tempting trap: Hindmoor calls the size effect 'at most, an empirical tendency', so small size makes organising more likely, not certain."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Olson assumes rational, self-interested individuals who will free-ride on collective goods unless coercion or selective incentives make them contribute. How would a communicative planning theorist critique this?","o":["In open dialogue people come to understand each other's claims and build shared commitments; interests are not fixed in advance.","Whether groups organise depends on institutions laid down earlier, which give some groups lasting advantages of access and resources.","The calculating, self-interested individual is itself a subject produced by market-based rationalities of government.","Rules that let groups cooperate evolve through trial and error over time; they need not be imposed by a coercive leviathan."],"a":0,"e":"Communicative planning, building on Habermas, treats preferences and interests as formed and transformed in deliberation aimed at mutual understanding, so Olson's fixed-preference calculation misses what dialogue can do. The earlier-institutions critique is historical institutionalist, the produced-subject critique Foucauldian governmentality, and the evolving-rules critique evolutionary (Moroni)."},{"t":"olson","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Olson's logic implies that small groups with concentrated interests organise more easily than large, dispersed groups, whatever the setting. How would a historical institutionalist critique this?","o":["People join groups because norms and identities tell them what is appropriate, not because of a cost-benefit calculation.","The model is static: it ignores how earlier institutional choices lock in some groups' organisation and access over decades.","Group interests follow from class position in the relations of production, so free-riding is secondary to the struggle between classes.","Influence depends less on organising numbers than on holding the resources a governing coalition needs to act."],"a":1,"e":"Historical institutionalists explain group power through path dependence: past policies and institutions create organisations, access points and feedback effects that persist. Norms of appropriateness are the sociological institutionalist critique, class position the Marxist one, and resources for a governing coalition Stone's regime theory."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Dembski & Salet (2010), which of these statements are true? I. The success of a symbolic marker depends mainly on the quality of its design. II. Institutions must be validated again and again in practice, so their meaning is never fixed. III. Institutions are internalised in people's mental maps rather than external constraints on choice. IV. Successful spatial concepts such as the Green Heart mainly aimed at preservation rather than transformation.","o":["I F · II F · III F · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"The authors place themselves in the internalised, validated-in-action quadrant (II and III) and note that successful concepts like the London Green Belt and Green Heart aim at preservation (IV, p. 612). I is the tempting trap: their thesis is that success lies 'in the institutional embedding in the mental maps of humankind', not in the symbol itself, which is why redesigning a failed symbol misses the point."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Dembski & Salet (2010), which of these statements about how institutions are made and remade are true? I. Following Berger & Luckmann, codes gain institutional depth as a reciprocity of expectations grows among norm holders. II. Bourdieu's habitus means that practice is a mechanical reaction to social structure. III. Following Paasi, regional identity has an internal and an external side. IV. Norms usually change gradually because, once fixed, they cannot be reinterpreted.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV T"],"a":0,"e":"I and III are on pp. 618 and 620. II is false: habitus makes practice neither a mechanical reaction to structure nor independent of it, as the card-game image shows. IV is the tempting trap: the authors say norms change gradually not because they are fixed, but because it is unlikely that everyone changes their interpretation at once (p. 617)."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Dembski & Salet (2010), which of these statements about symbols and rites are true? I. A rite of institution produces discontinuity out of continuity. II. A rite works by itself, whether or not the audience has inculcated its meaning. III. Planners can use symbolic markers voluntaristically, as long as the symbol is well chosen. IV. The art of planning is to find niches of under-institutionalised space.","o":["I F · II F · III F · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV T"],"a":1,"e":"Bourdieu's rite of institution is 'social magic' that turns continuity into a sharp difference (p. 620), and the authors end with the art of finding under-institutionalised niches (p. 622). II is false: inculcation by the audience is the Achilles heel without which the rite stays powerless. III is the tempting trap: 'Symbolic markers cannot be used in a voluntaristic manner' (p. 621), however well chosen."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Dembski & Salet argue that 'institutions are not carved in stone': they must be validated in everyday practice, which opens room for deliberate innovation. How would a historical institutionalist critique this?","o":["Actors follow rules because rules reduce uncertainty and serve their interests, so a symbol matters only if it changes the pay-offs.","Whether a region's new image takes hold depends on capital's search for new spaces of accumulation, not on audiences' mental maps.","It underestimates how earlier choices create self-reinforcing paths, so real change comes mostly at critical junctures.","A symbol's meaning should be built in open dialogue among stakeholders instead of being instituted by planners for an audience."],"a":2,"e":"Historical institutionalism stresses path dependence and treats innovation as a historical particularity, which is exactly the view Dembski & Salet reject (pp. 616–617); from that side their stress on constant revalidation underplays persistence. The pay-off option is rational choice institutionalism, capital's search for accumulation is Marxist political economy, and open dialogue is communicative planning."},{"t":"dembski","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Dembski & Salet treat the success of symbolic markers such as Guggenheim Bilbao as a question of whether their meaning is inculcated in the audience. How would a political economist in Harvey's line critique this?","o":["Building a regional identity is a collective good, so each municipality will free-ride on the branding efforts of its neighbours.","Flagship symbols are tools of inter-urban competition for mobile capital, and their image can mask decline as social control.","Planners cannot deliberately institute new meanings; useful rules and identities emerge spontaneously through trial and error.","A symbol's meaning should come from open dialogue with residents rather than from planners deciding what an audience must absorb."],"a":1,"e":"Harvey (1989) reads place-marketing, spectacle and flagship projects as features of urban entrepreneurialism driven by inter-urban competition, with image working like 'bread and circuses'. Free-riding on branding is Olson's logic, spontaneous emergence fits Moroni's evolutionary view (against the authors' 'deliberate change'), and open dialogue is communicative planning."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Harvey (1989), which of these statements are true? I. Urban governance means much more than urban government: the power to reorganise urban life lies in broader coalitions. II. What sets the new entrepreneurialism apart from earlier boosterism is risk absorption by the national public sector. III. Projects focused on place may benefit an area wider or narrower than the jurisdiction and divert attention from regional problems. IV. Harvey treats cities as active agents that choose their strategies in a market of places.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV T"],"a":0,"e":"I is Harvey's point on governance and class alliances (p. 6), III his contrast of place and territory (pp. 7–8). IV is false: he warns against reifying cities, which seem active agents 'when they are mere things'. II is the tempting trap: what he suspects is new is risk absorption by the local, not the national, public sector (p. 7)."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Harvey (1989), which of these statements about the consequences of urban entrepreneurialism are true? I. Public risk absorption makes multinational capital more mobile, not less. II. Much US public-private partnership subsidises affluent consumers and corporations at the expense of collective consumption for the poor. III. Imitation between cities makes any competitive advantage ephemeral. IV. Socialism within one city is a feasible project, provided the right progressive coalition takes power.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F"],"a":0,"e":"I, II and III summarise Section 4 (pp. 11–13): lower relocation costs for firms, regressive redistribution, and serial reproduction that erodes advantage. IV is the tempting trap: Harvey writes that 'Socialism within one city is not, of course, a feasible project even under the best of circumstances' (p. 16); his hope lies in alliances across space."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Harvey (1989), which of these statements about the shift to entrepreneurialism are true? I. The 1985 Orleans colloquium showed that only conservative city governments wanted to become entrepreneurial. II. Harvey traces the shift to a single cause: the recession of 1973. III. By the mid-1980s the shift was complete in all large US cities. IV. Under the right circumstances, inter-urban competition may open the way to a non zero-sum pattern of development.","o":["I F · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III F · IV T","I F · II F · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV F"],"a":2,"e":"Only IV is true (p. 5). The Orleans consensus held 'across national boundaries and even across political parties and ideologies', so I is false, and the shift was 'checkered' (New Orleans stayed a ward of federal government), so III is false. II is the tempting trap: 1973 is part of a backdrop that also includes deindustrialisation, structural unemployment, fiscal austerity, neoconservatism and the declining power of the nation state."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Harvey argues that inter-urban competition works as an 'external coercive law' that pushes cities into the serial reproduction of the same projects. How would a historical institutionalist critique this?","o":["Cities respond to competition through institutions built up over time, so their paths diverge rather than converge on one model.","Competition between jurisdictions is healthy, because mobile residents and firms can choose the package of services they prefer.","Outcomes depend on whether local actors can assemble a governing coalition that pools their resources, so local agency matters most.","Entrepreneurial cities are produced by discourses that teach officials and residents to see themselves as competitors."],"a":0,"e":"Historical institutionalists (for example Sorensen on Toronto) explain outcomes through path-dependent national and local institutions, which predicts divergence rather than one coercive logic producing the same forms everywhere. Welcoming competition is a public choice view, assembling a governing coalition is Stone's regime theory, and discourse-produced competitors is a Foucauldian reading."},{"t":"harvey","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Harvey argues that image-led projects such as Baltimore's Harborplace create civic pride that works as social control: 'The circus succeeds even if the bread is lacking.' How would a rational choice theorist critique this?","o":["Civic identity rests on shared norms that must be inculcated, so a new image only works if it fits meanings people already hold.","An image need not be manipulation: one shaped in open dialogue with residents can express a genuinely shared vision of the city.","Residents and officials back flagship projects because they expect individual gains, not because an urban image manipulates them.","Residents are not passive spectators: urban movements contest flagship projects and can win real concessions from the city council."],"a":2,"e":"Rational choice assumes self-interested, calculating actors, so support for spectacle is explained by expected pay-offs (jobs, property values, re-election) rather than false consciousness. Inculcated norms is the sociological institutionalist view of Dembski & Salet, open dialogue communicative planning, and contesting movements the urban politics perspective."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hyötyläinen & Haila (2018), which of these statements are true? I. Entrepreneurial public real estate policy is rent-maximising land policy carried out by municipalities and the state. II. In Finland it was mainly private developers, not the state, that pushed cities towards competition and new public management. III. The Land Use and Building Act (MRL, 1999) ended state ratification of local plans and legalised development contracts. IV. Leasing land had helped Helsinki keep planning power with its town planners.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":2,"e":"I is the core concept (p. 137), III the MRL (p. 139) and IV the authors' account of leasing (p. 138). II is the tempting trap: the authors stress that in Finland it was the state that pushed cities towards new public management, through urban programmes, subsidy rules and its own corporatised real estate."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hyötyläinen & Haila (2018), which of these statements about the Eiranranta sale are true? I. The investment company Apollo outbid the established household developers. II. The Real Estate Department preferred to sell to Skanska, even though Skanska bid lower. III. REC members saw concentrations of wealth as a segregation risk equal to concentrations of social housing. IV. Eiranranta includes a share of social housing, in line with the metropolitan social-mix policy.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV F"],"a":3,"e":"Apollo bid three million euros more and the department wanted Skanska instead (p. 141), so I and II are true. IV is false: Eiranranta has no social housing. III is the tempting trap: members opposed concentrations of social housing in areas like Jakomäki but not concentrations of wealth ('Not every place needs to be mixed', p. 142)."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Hyötyläinen & Haila (2018), which of these statements about how the new land policy was justified are true? I. The 'necessity to save' worked as a strong discourse that made market rule appear natural. II. The authors also criticise urban scholars whose celebration of diversity ends up accepting the privatisation of public land. III. REC members cited competition with Espoo and Kauniainen for 'good taxpayers' as a motive. IV. The area was renamed Eiranranta with marketing in mind.","o":["I T · II F · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"All four are true. The authors use Bourdieu's 'strong discourse' (pp. 139–140), quote a scholar who says a good city can have both luxury and rough areas, report the competition for taxpayers (p. 140) and quote that 'Even the name was changed to Eiranranta with marketing in mind'. The tempting pattern marking II as false overlooks that the authors explicitly criticise fellow academics."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Hyötyläinen & Haila argue that Helsinki's turn to selling land at the highest bid let a market logic capture public land and served the wealthy. How would a rational choice theorist critique this?","o":["Selling to the highest bidder curbs rent-seeking by insiders who captured undervalued public land, so prices can serve the public purse.","The 'necessity to save' is a rationality that leads officials to govern themselves as entrepreneurs, beyond any state imposition.","The problem is not market logic as such but a closed committee process; the area's future should be set through stakeholder dialogue.","Public land should be decommodified through collective self-management by its residents, not just kept in municipal hands."],"a":0,"e":"Rational choice draws attention to rent-seeking: the authors themselves suggest established developers were used to capturing land rent, so a public choice scholar would read open bidding as a cure rather than the disease. Self-governing officials is governmentality, stakeholder dialogue communicative planning, and decommodified self-management the commons view of Bossuyt & D'Ottaviano."},{"t":"hh","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Hyötyläinen & Haila explain why Real Estate Committee members did not see Eiranranta as a warning sign of segregation by their focus on real estate business. How would a sociological institutionalist critique this?","o":["Members acted on taken-for-granted norms of what a city does today ('the rules of the game'), so the cause lies in cultural scripts.","Members' views hardly matter: the coercive law of inter-urban competition would push any committee to sell to the highest bidder anyway.","Members responded rationally to incentives, as budget sales targets and tax competition made selling the land the best choice.","The corporatisation of state real estate and the 1999 planning act set a path that later committee members could hardly leave."],"a":0,"e":"Sociological institutionalism explains behaviour through internalised norms and a logic of appropriateness, which fits the members' unanimous sense that market rules had become common sense. The coercive-law option is Harvey's political economy, the incentives option rational choice, and the earlier path set by laws and corporatisation historical institutionalism (the same contrast as the HC8 example)."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin's chapter on urban politics, which of these statements are true? I. Like governance theory, urban politics assumes conflicts can be handled towards agreed outcomes. II. In this frame planners cannot be neutral and should act more like advocates than technocrats. III. Pluralism came to be seen as naïve partly because some issues never became the focus of activism. IV. Planning on its own can secure justice, as long as its procedures are fair.","o":["I T · II F · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II F · III F · IV F"],"a":2,"e":"Urban politics differs from governance by seeing conflict as unavoidable (p. 105), so I is false; II and III are on pp. 105–106. IV is the tempting trap: because conflicts are linked to wider power and inequality, the perspective is modest about what planning alone can do, and justice may require wider political change (p. 107)."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin's account of regime theory, which of these statements are true? I. Regime power is mainly 'power over': one actor dominating others. II. A regime emerges because local government cannot carry out its agenda alone. III. Critics argue regime theory reflects US conditions, such as cities' reliance on local property taxes. IV. Stone's four regime types include a lower-class opportunity expansion regime.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II F · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV F"],"a":1,"e":"Stone's regime rests on resource interdependence because local government cannot act alone (II); its transferability is questioned because of US property taxes and local autonomy (III); and the four types are maintenance, development, middle-class progressive and lower-class opportunity expansion (IV). I is the tempting trap: regime power is 'power to', the capacity to achieve goals by combining resources, not 'power over'."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Rydin's chapter, which of these statements about social movements and agonism are true? I. Castells linked urban social movements to conflicts over the means of collective consumption. II. Agonism aims to turn enemies into adversaries who are willing to agree to disagree. III. Mouffe sees deliberation towards consensus as the most democratic way to handle conflict. IV. Critics note that urban social movement research has neglected gender and leaned towards North America and Northern Europe.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II F · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV T"],"a":1,"e":"I (p. 110), II (p. 112) and IV (p. 111) are in the chapter. III is the tempting trap: Mouffe rejects the governance promise of deliberative consensus as misleading and sees agonistic co-existence of adversaries as more democratic than the 'false promises' of deliberation."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"The urban politics perspective holds that conflict is unavoidable in planning, so planners cannot be neutral and should act as advocates for disadvantaged groups. How would a communicative planning theorist critique this?","o":["Planners should remain neutral experts who supply evidence to elected politicians, since politics alone legitimately sets the goals.","Conflicts in planning derive from class relations rooted in capital accumulation, so local advocacy cannot change the outcomes.","Well-designed dialogue lets stakeholders reach mutual understanding, so planners should facilitate deliberation, not take sides.","Which groups win depends on who overcomes free-riding; advocacy cannot undo the edge that small, concentrated interests enjoy."],"a":2,"e":"Communicative planning (Healey, Innes) treats conflict as something deliberation can work through towards mutual understanding and even consensus, with the planner as facilitator, which is exactly the governance-side view urban politics rejects. Neutral experts serving politicians is the governmental model, class relations is Marxist political economy, and free-riding is Olson's rational choice logic."},{"t":"rydin6","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Stone's regime theory, as Rydin presents it, sees governing capacity arising from informal coalitions that pool resources: 'power to' rather than 'power over'. How would a Foucauldian governmentality scholar critique this?","o":["Coalitions only mediate the structural imperatives of capital accumulation, which set the urban agenda before any coalition is formed.","Power is not a resource partners pool; it works through rationalities that shape how partners see themselves and their duties.","Regimes are shaped by national institutions laid down long ago, like US reliance on property taxes, which lock cities into fixed paths.","Partners join a coalition only when selective incentives outweigh their own costs, so cooperation remains a collective action problem."],"a":1,"e":"Governmentality (Raco & Imrie) treats power as dispersed and productive, working through discourses and technologies that constitute subjects, so treating power as a resource held and pooled by actors misses how partnerships govern conduct. Structural imperatives is Marxist political economy, national institutions and paths historical institutionalism, and selective incentives rational choice."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Bossuyt & D'Ottaviano (2025), which of these statements are true? I. Housing is a commons by nature, whatever practices and rules govern it. II. Marginalisation is a form of enclosure in which a commons shuts out outsiders. III. Cooptation is a form of enclosure in which the state undermines self-governance. IV. Dardot and Laval argue that some degree of closure is needed to protect commons.","o":["I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II F · III F · IV T"],"a":1,"e":"II and III are two of the three forms of enclosure, next to commodification (p. 1023). I is false: housing is not a commons in itself but becomes one through practices and rules (p. 1022). IV is the tempting trap: it is Harvey who sees 'some degree of closure' as necessary; Dardot and Laval argue that commons cannot be objects of property at all."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Bossuyt & D'Ottaviano (2025), which of these statements about the patterns of protection are true? I. Regularisation protects against eviction, but it can open new routes to enclosure. II. Ideation means that commons ideas formed in one place can materialise later somewhere else. III. Federation operates at the level of the housing development itself. IV. Integration embeds residents in the movement's wider housing struggle.","o":["I T · II F · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III F · IV F","I T · II T · III F · IV T"],"a":3,"e":"Regularisation turns collective use claims into individual titles and mortgages (pp. 1033, 1035); ideation (Varvarousis) and integration operate between development and movement (pp. 1034–1035). III is the tempting trap: federation is the pattern at the interface between movements and the state, which helped insert autogestão into MCMV-Entidades; at the level of the development itself the patterns are regularisation and restoration."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Bossuyt & D'Ottaviano (2025), which of these statements are true? I. Occupations exploit a contradiction between the social function of property and the right to private property. II. Movements legitimise occupations by targeting vacant, tax-defaulting or public buildings. III. Institutionalist approaches to the commons suffice once their design principles are properly applied. IV. Only public law, not private law, has progressive potential for housing commons.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III T · IV F"],"a":1,"e":"I and II describe the legal contradiction and how the UMM picks buildings (p. 1027). III is false: the authors call institutionalist approaches 'only of partial use' because they miss the political dimension (p. 1036). IV is the tempting trap: against work that stresses the City Statute, the authors highlight the mediating role and progressive potential of private law."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Bossuyt & D'Ottaviano show how housing commons in São Paulo are protected through regularisation, restoration, integration, ideation and federation. How would a rational choice theorist critique this?","o":["Isolated commons cannot escape a capitalist land market; without a wider struggle against commodification they will be drawn back into it.","Without enforceable sanctions, each resident gains from subletting or selling, so the commons erodes unless incentives are realigned.","Residents' wish to own a home reflects deep cultural scripts of citizenship, which patterns of protection cannot easily override.","Allocating central buildings to households by movement points replaces the price system with the discretion of a few administrators."],"a":1,"e":"Rational choice focuses on individual incentives and free-riding: the points system works like a selective incentive, but once residents hold titles and mortgages, unenforceable bans on subletting leave strong individual incentives to defect. The land-market option is Marxist political economy, cultural scripts sociological institutionalism, and the price-system complaint Hayekian."},{"t":"bossuyt","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Bossuyt & D'Ottaviano conclude that protection is partial because regularisation turns collective claims into individual titles, and they call for legal reform enabling collective property. How would a historical institutionalist critique this?","o":["Decades of policy favouring homeownership lock in individual titles, so legal reform is likely only at a critical juncture.","Protection depends on managing networks of movements, agencies and funders, which calls for skilled partnership working more than law.","Rights are policy variables: the state can attach binding covenants against subletting, so individual titles need not mean enclosure.","As long as urban land remains a commodity, any form of title will in the end be drawn back into the circuit of exchange value."],"a":0,"e":"Historical institutionalism stresses path dependence: decades of policy favouring homeownership make individual titles self-reinforcing, so the authors' call for reform faces a sticky path that tends to change only at critical junctures. Network management is governance theory, rights as policy variables is Needham, and land as commodity Marxist political economy."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Pruijt (2003), which of these statements are true? I. A housing shortage combined with abundant empty property is the basic precondition for squatting. II. Post-Fordist theory expects a market-oriented regime to co-opt movement groups as service providers while also increasing repression. III. Castells (1983) held that urban movements can avoid institutionalisation as long as they stay radical. IV. Pruijt judges cooptation to be harmful to movements in every case.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV F","I T · II F · III F · IV T","I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":2,"e":"I opens the article (p. 1) and II is the post-Fordist hypothesis (pp. 5–6): cooptation as a pull factor, repression driven by competition for a clean, safe image. IV is false: he takes a neutral stance and says costs and benefits must be judged case by case. III is the tempting trap: Castells (1983) held that institutionalisation, with its loss of identity, is inevitable, which is precisely the view Pruijt's title question challenges."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Pruijt (2003), which of these statements about Amsterdam and New York are true? I. Amsterdam's squatters' groups became providers of services that the retreating state neglected. II. In New York, ACORN received buildings only after promising to give up squatting. III. New York's official homesteaders gradually came to see themselves as homeowners opposed to squatters. IV. In Amsterdam, squatters in old working-class districts usually left voluntarily when new social housing was scheduled.","o":["I T · II T · III T · IV T","I T · II T · III T · IV F","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II F · III F · IV F"],"a":2,"e":"II, III and IV are in the New York and Amsterdam sections (pp. 9–10, 20). I is the tempting trap: contrary to post-Fordist expectations, Amsterdam saw hardly any cooptation; squatters' groups did not become service providers, and the housing associations did not come from the movement (p. 8)."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"tf","q":"According to Pruijt (2003), which of these statements are true? I. A market-oriented regime offers more opportunities for squatting than a redistributive one. II. For a squatters' movement proper, squatting is both means and primary goal. III. Cooptation of a housing movement works when officials can claim to share its ultimate goal of housing the poor. IV. The 2002 legalisation of eleven Lower East Side squats was a case of flexible institutionalisation.","o":["I T · II T · III F · IV F","I F · II F · III F · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV T","I F · II T · III T · IV F"],"a":3,"e":"Pruijt's 'perhaps paradoxical' conclusion is the reverse of I: a market-oriented regime offers fewer opportunities (pp. 22–23). II and III explain why a squatters' movement proper is immune to cooptation (pp. 12–13). IV is the tempting trap: legalisation came once no squattable buildings were left and ended the movement, which Pruijt classifies as terminal institutionalisation."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Pruijt explains why New York co-opted its housing movement while Amsterdam did not by the difference between a market-oriented and a redistributive urban regime. How would a Marxist political economist critique this?","o":["A movement survives when activists receive selective incentives, so its fate depends on rewards for members, not on the regime.","Squatting lasts only where its meaning is inculcated in the everyday practice and norms of the wider public, not only of activists.","In both cities, squatting and its legalisation stay local fixes that leave the land market and capital accumulation untouched.","Legalisation and self-help schemes are technologies that turn squatters into responsible citizens who govern their own conduct."],"a":2,"e":"A Marxist would see both regimes as variants within capitalist urbanisation: legalised squats and co-opted service providers alike leave property markets and accumulation intact (Pruijt himself notes conflict stays local, shielding banks and big real estate). Selective incentives is rational choice, inculcated meaning sociological institutionalism (Dembski & Salet), and self-governing citizens governmentality."},{"t":"pruijt","lv":3,"fmt":"critique","q":"Pruijt argues that a squatters' movement proper is immune to cooptation because squatting is both its means and its goal. How would an urban regime theorist in Stone's line critique this?","o":["Refusing any place in a governing coalition leaves squatters without resources to shape policy, so immunity also means marginality.","Identity cannot shield a movement for long, because property markets will absorb squatted buildings once land values rise enough.","Individual squatters weigh the personal gains of legal tenure against the costs of conflict, so a shared identity will not hold them.","The 1971 house right and its later erosion show that legal paths set earlier, not identity, decided the fate of the movement."],"a":0,"e":"Regime theory sees influence as 'power to' gained by pooling resources in an informal governing coalition; a movement that refuses negotiation, as the Lower East Side squatters did, gains no governing capacity. Absorption by property markets is Marxist, weighing personal gains is rational choice, and earlier legal paths historical institutionalism."}],"GQX":[{"t":"buit","type":"quote","q":"Buitelaar ends with: 'we must realize that politics is divisive by definition: so should be The Just City!' What does he mean?","o":["Choosing a conception means taking sides in value conflicts instead of quick consensus","Justice is a matter for party politics, so planners should leave it to elected councils","All four conceptions are equally valid, so a city should combine them in equal measure","Conflict between groups shows that the city's institutions have already become unjust"],"a":0,"e":"Justice literacy requires being explicit, making choices and possibly entering into conflict over values rather than rushing to common ground. Combining all four conceptions is exactly what 'justice is not à la carte' warns against."},{"t":"buit","type":"position","q":"On the course's deliberate ↔ emergent axis, how do the Equal City and the Free City differ?","o":["Both are deliberate: each of them designs a desired material pattern for the city","Equal City emergent: patterns grow from mixing; Free City deliberate: rules","Equal City deliberate: a designed pattern; Free City emergent: spontaneous order","Both are emergent: neither conception sets any goal at all for the urban outcome"],"a":2,"e":"The goal-based Equal City aims at a preferred end-state that planners pattern deliberately; the Free City judges only the rules and accepts whatever order emerges (cosmos). Saying both design a pattern ignores that the Free City explicitly does not."},{"t":"buit","type":"consequence","q":"If only institutions can be just, as Buitelaar assumes, what follows for a planner alarmed by rapidly rising rents in one district?","o":["The rent rise is itself unjust and must be reversed to restore the old pattern","The rent rise signals possible injustice and should prompt debate on the rules","The rent rise is irrelevant, since outcomes never say anything about justice","The rent rise proves the market works, so the rules are just by definition"],"a":1,"e":"Outcomes cannot be just or unjust in themselves, but price rises have a signalling function that raises concern and starts discussion about the (in)justice of institutions. Calling outcomes irrelevant overlooks that signalling role."},{"t":"buit","type":"contrast","q":"Some critics replace willingness to pay with surveys of how happy or satisfied people are. How does Buitelaar classify this change?","o":["A shift from the Happy City to the Supportive City, with a happiness threshold","A shift to the Equal City, since happiness is spread more evenly than money","Another value measure, but still utilitarian: overall collective well-being","A move to the capability approach: what people are actually able to be and do"],"a":2,"e":"Measuring subjective happiness changes how the central value is operationalised, but it is 'equally utilitarian in nature', still striving for overall collective well-being. The capability approach rejects utility as a mental reaction, so that option is wrong."},{"t":"buit","type":"apply","q":"A province sets a maximum commuting time between every residential area and a minimum number of jobs. Which idea in Buitelaar does this apply?","o":["Agglomeration economies: concentrating jobs to maximise productivity","Moroni's spatial primary goods: accessibility to jobs for everyone","Social mixing: spreading income groups evenly across job markets","Entitlement theory: legitimate transfers of land between employers"],"a":1,"e":"Moroni's four spatial primary goods include accessibility to jobs, defined as a maximum acceptable commuting distance to a minimum number of jobs: a Supportive City threshold. Agglomeration economies maximise total efficiency and do not guarantee a minimum for every area."},{"t":"buit","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT an example Buitelaar gives for the Free City?","o":["Premodern cities as spontaneous orders under a few rules","Refraining from growth controls and from rent control","Almere Oosterwold, where households develop public land","Large-scale social housing built by the city of Vienna"],"a":3,"e":"Vienna's social housing appeared in the lecture as a Supportive City example (affordable housing as a primary good). Premodern spontaneous orders (Cozzolino), refraining from growth controls and rent control, and Oosterwold are all Free City examples."},{"t":"fw","type":"quote","q":"Rydin writes that 'all theoretical endeavours in the social sciences are inevitably value-laden, and a rigorous approach should seek to make this clear and explicit'. What does she conclude?","o":["Researchers should avoid normative topics so their analysis stays rigorous","Researchers should state their theory's values rather than claim neutrality","Researchers should adopt practitioners' values so their work stays relevant","Researchers should combine all theories so no single value set can dominate"],"a":1,"e":"Rigour, for Rydin, means making the value-ladenness of theory explicit, not escaping it. Avoiding normative topics would not help, since even analytic research is 'imbued with value judgements'."},{"t":"fw","type":"contrast","q":"How does Rydin's notion of theory differ from Keeble's (1952), as discussed in the knowledge lecture?","o":["Keeble: a desirable end state for a town; Rydin: coherent general statements about the world","Keeble: coherent general statements about the world; Rydin: a desirable end state for a town","Keeble: law-like hypotheses tested on data; Rydin: practical wisdom about action in context","Keeble: a body of explicit assumptions; Rydin: a design diagram showing an ideal urban form"],"a":0,"e":"Keeble discusses diagrams of town form as 'theory' in the sense of a desirable end state, the craft view; Rydin's theory is 'a set of ideas that fit together coherently and make general statements about the world'. The second option simply swaps the two."},{"t":"fw","type":"apply","q":"A study of a stalled area development finds that officials pursued their own career incentives rather than the public interest. Which approach in Rydin's Table 1.1 does this finding draw on?","o":["New institutionalism: organisational culture and path dependency","Governance theory: stakeholder networks in policy delivery","Rational choice: state failure and state actors' self-interest","Urban politics: wider political forces, power and conflict"],"a":2,"e":"Table 1.1 says rational choice adds state failure and the influence of state actors' self-interest to the governmental model. Culture and path dependency would point to new institutionalism instead."},{"t":"fw","type":"position","q":"A theory holds that planning outcomes emerge from many interacting actions and that existing arrangements tend to be reproduced. Where does it sit on dimension 2?","o":["Deliberate and transformation-oriented","Emergent and transformation-oriented","Deliberate and reproduction-oriented","Emergent and reproduction-oriented"],"a":3,"e":"Outcomes arising from interacting actions are emergent, and an emphasis on stability is reproduction. Dimension 2 combines both axes, so each must be read separately."},{"t":"fw","type":"consequence","q":"If Rydin is right that data never 'speak for themselves', what follows for a report that presents housing statistics as neutral facts?","o":["The report is worthless, because data cannot support any narrative at all","The report is neutral as long as its statistics are accurate and complete","The report has already chosen a framing, which should be made explicit","The report should add statistics until a single narrative is proven true"],"a":2,"e":"For Rydin data must be interpreted into a narrative, and the same data can support several; a report always embeds a framing and its values. She does not say data are useless, only that they need a theoretical frame."},{"t":"fw","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT among the difficulties of planning research that Rydin (2021) names?","o":["Worries about the amount and the quality of the available data","The lack of any planning theory that researchers could draw on","Getting beyond description to an understanding of dynamics","Balancing rigour with the pragmatism needed to finish at all"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin's problem is the opposite: there are many competing theories, so the researcher must choose. Data worries, getting beyond description and balancing rigour with pragmatism are all on her list."},{"t":"hall","type":"quote","q":"Hall writes that planning was 'slipping into insignificance, not because it was wrong or misplaced, but because it lacked the intellectual wherewithal to defend itself'. What is his point?","o":["Practice had dropped theory, so it could not argue for its own value any more","Planning was wrong in its aims, so its decline was deserved by the profession","Planning theory was too weak to win elections, so politicians abandoned it","The profession had grown too intellectual, so the public no longer trusted it"],"a":0,"e":"Hall blames the anti-theoretical style of 1980s practice and the RTPI's 'intellectual abdication': without theory, planning could not defend itself against the New Right. The first option contradicts his explicit 'not because it was wrong'."},{"t":"hall","type":"position","q":"Hall reports Lindblom's 'muddling through'. Where does this view sit on the course's monist ↔ pluralist axis?","o":["Monist: one rational method yields the best decision","Monist: experts' models are the only valid knowledge","Pluralist: knowledge dispersed, every knower limited","Pluralist: every claim is equally valid as knowledge"],"a":2,"e":"The lecture summarised the right-wing critique as 'Knowledge is dispersed, and every knower is limited', with decisions emerging from bargaining between groups: a pluralist view. Lindblom does not claim that every claim is equally valid; that is relativism."},{"t":"hall","type":"contrast","q":"Hall says the systems planner was in practice doing two very different things. Which two?","o":["Designing new buildings and drafting land-use regulations","Observing reality as scientist and acting on it as designer","Advising politicians and organising local community groups","Collecting statistics and programming the city's computers"],"a":1,"e":"As a social scientist the planner passively observed and analysed; as a designer the planner acted on reality, which is less certain and depends on objectives set in messy dealings with politicians and the public. Advising and organising mixes up later roles."},{"t":"hall","type":"consequence","q":"If planners, as Hall reports for 1965 to 1975, lost their claim to 'any unique and useful expertise', what followed for the profession?","o":["It gained authority, since the public trusted its neutrality","It returned to design, the only skill no one else could claim","It merged with engineering, which took over its main tasks","It became hard to say what planners knew that others did not"],"a":3,"e":"Hall compares the lost claim with the expertise of a doctor or lawyer; planning extended so thinly that it seemed almost meaningless (Wildavsky). A return to design is not what he describes: planners moved further into the social and economic."},{"t":"hall","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT a criticism Hall makes of postmodern planning theory?","o":["It is written in a hermetic style for a small coterie","It is uninterested in what happens where, and why","It denies meta-theory, so it cannot explain much","It relies too heavily on mathematical city models"],"a":3,"e":"Hall criticises its hermetic style, its aspatial character and its inability to explain socio-economic relationships. Reliance on mathematical models is the opposite of postmodernism; it characterises the 1960s systems planners."},{"t":"hall","type":"apply","q":"A Dutch ministry commissions a traffic model and a social cost-benefit analysis (MKBA) to identify the 'optimal' route for a new motorway. Which legacy in Hall's history does this continue?","o":["The communicative turn: planning as dialogue","The advocacy tradition: plans for unheard groups","The 1960s systems and rational planning tradition","The pre-1955 craft of designing an ideal town form"],"a":2,"e":"Modelling, forecasting and evaluating alternatives to find the best option is the rational and systems approach that came from the US transportation studies. A participatory omgevingsdialoog would be the communicative legacy."},{"t":"marx","type":"quote","q":"Marx writes: 'He feels at home when he is not working, and when he is working he does not feel at home.' What does this sentence show?","o":["The worker is lazy because his wage is too low to motivate him","Labour is external to the worker, not his own self-expression","The worker prefers family life to the company of colleagues","Work is unpleasant by nature, so it must be shared out fairly"],"a":1,"e":"The sentence illustrates estrangement from the act of production: labour is external, not the worker's own, a means to needs outside it. Marx does not say work is unpleasant by nature; free conscious activity is what makes us human."},{"t":"marx","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'For Marx, the problem of capitalism is simply that workers are paid too little.' Why is this a misreading of 'Estranged Labour'?","o":["Marx thinks wages are fair, but that working hours are too long for workers","Marx blames the problem on greedy owners rather than on the wage system","Marx thinks the main problem is the lack of democracy inside each factory","Marx locates the problem in the estranged relation of labour itself"],"a":3,"e":"Higher wages would be 'better payment for the slave': the wage is itself a consequence of estranged labour. Blaming greed is also wrong; Marx criticises political economy for treating greed as the only driving force."},{"t":"marx","type":"contrast","q":"How do Marx and Cozzolino differ in how they treat ownership?","o":["Marx: ownership spreads design control; Cozzolino: it follows from estranged labour","Marx: ownership should pass to experts; Cozzolino: it should pass to the city","Marx: property results from estranged labour; Cozzolino: it spreads design control","Marx: ownership is a cultural script; Cozzolino: it is the main driver of history"],"a":2,"e":"For Marx private property is the product of estranged labour and a form of servitude; Cozzolino values ownership as the legal title that distributes design control and so enables incremental adaptation. The first option simply swaps the two."},{"t":"marx","type":"position","q":"How does 'Estranged Labour', read with the HC4 lecture, relate to reproduction ↔ transformation?","o":["It explains how capitalism reproduces itself and aims at transforming the base","It explains only reproduction and sees no possible route to any transformation","It explains how planners transform society through gradual reforms of the law","It treats both poles as equal, since history moves in cycles without direction"],"a":0,"e":"The more workers produce, the stronger capital becomes: the text explains reproduction. Yet the goal is transformation, by abolishing private property. Gradual legal reform is what the structural argument doubts, since law sits in the superstructure."},{"t":"marx","type":"consequence","q":"If, as the lecture says, the base is generally dominant over the superstructure, what follows for a progressive new planning law?","o":["It will transform property relations, because law is the motor of history","It will be ignored, since the superstructure has no effect on social life","It will produce spontaneous order, as long as it consists of general rules","It will tend to maintain property relations unless the base changes"],"a":3,"e":"Law and planning sit in the superstructure, which tends to maintain the base; real change must come from the base. Saying the superstructure has no effect overstates the model, which is two-way. General rules and spontaneous order are Hayek's vocabulary."},{"t":"marx","type":"apply","q":"In 2021 a majority of Berlin voters backed socialising housing firms that own more than 3,000 flats. How does a Marxist reading place this?","o":["It tweaks rents within the market, so it remains a superstructure measure","It is spontaneous order, since the outcome emerged from many voters' choices","It targets ownership in the base, yet within capitalism, with compensation","It is a free-rider problem, since most voters gained nothing from the change"],"a":2,"e":"The campaign targets who owns the means of producing housing (the base) rather than rents, aiming at decommodification. But owners would be compensated and the rest of the market stays private: socialisation of one sector, not Marx's abolition of private property."},{"t":"cozz","type":"quote","q":"Which quotation comes from Cozzolino (2020)?","o":["'should not reflect the image of a stringent technocratic order'","'The goals themselves stand outside the planning process'","'it is a superstition to believe that there must be a majority view on everything'","'not any imaginings will serve as a planning goal'"],"a":0,"e":"Cozzolino writes that in rule making 'the built environment should not reflect the image of a stringent technocratic order imposed from the top'. The others come from Rydin (2021), Hayek (1944) and Rydin (2007)."},{"t":"cozz","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT a recommendation Cozzolino makes for designing NEW neighbourhoods?","o":["Favour demand-driven development by many builders","Abandon the political use of large urban projects","Separate public assets from privately owned lots","Copy the streets and facades of historic quarters"],"a":3,"e":"Cozzolino says emulating the physical form and aesthetics of the traditional neighbourhood cannot reproduce its complexity; the institutional framework matters, not the look. The other three are his recommendations for avoiding new AANs."},{"t":"cozz","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'Cozzolino condemns anti-adaptive neighbourhoods and wants them replaced by traditional-style quarters.' What is wrong with this?","o":["He wants AANs replaced, but by modernist high-rise rather than by traditional forms","He does not blame AANs and wants conditions for adaptation, not a copy of old forms","He defends AANs as fully adaptive and sees no need for any change in their rules","He wants to keep AANs as they are and to build only traditional quarters elsewhere"],"a":1,"e":"Cozzolino says it is not his aim to blame or judge AANs, which matter to many residents; the challenge is creating conditions for more adaptive environments, and he explicitly rejects nostalgia. He does not see AANs as fully adaptive: they are locked in."},{"t":"cozz","type":"contrast","q":"How does Cozzolino (2020) differ from Hayek's 'Planning and democracy' (1944)?","o":["Cozzolino defends central planning; Hayek rejects it for being undemocratic","Cozzolino rejects ownership; Hayek sees private property as democracy's basis","Cozzolino studies design control in neighbourhoods; Hayek national planning","Cozzolino argues from value pluralism; Hayek from the physical form of cities"],"a":2,"e":"Hayek's chapter is about democratic assemblies and the ends of central economic planning; Cozzolino applies a related liberal, emergent logic to design control and ownership in the built environment. Neither defends central planning, and Cozzolino welcomes multiple ownerships."},{"t":"cozz","type":"position","q":"Where does Cozzolino sit on the structure ↔ agency dimension?","o":["Structural: residents' actions are fully set by capital and class relations","Agency-leaning, but rules and ownership decide how much room action gets","Structural: path-dependent institutions lock all neighbourhoods into decline","Pure agency: individual choices explain outcomes and institutions do not matter"],"a":1,"e":"Action is 'the main source of urban change', so he leans to agency, yet rules and ownership enable or constrain that action, which is why he wants to change them. Pure agency is the trap: his whole argument is about institutional framework conditions."},{"t":"cozz","type":"consequence","q":"If Dutch municipalities let owners split homes and add storeys without a permit, bound only by rules against nuisance, what would Cozzolino expect?","o":["Little change, since adaptation requires a comprehensive municipal plan first","Quick decline, since unplanned changes cause synchronous decay of the stock","More owner-led incremental change, except where ownership is communal","Change only after residents agree on one shared design in a public forum"],"a":2,"e":"Proscriptive rules redistribute design control to individual owners and make incremental adaptation more likely; but where large majorities are needed (communal ownership), rule changes alone may stay ineffective. Synchronous decay is linked to building all at once, not to incremental change."},{"t":"gov","type":"quote","q":"Which quotation comes from Rydin's governmental models chapter (2021)?","o":["'planning redresses problems that market processes alone cannot solve'","'Adaptability is indeed a relative concept'","'not any imaginings will serve as a planning goal'","'it is a superstition to believe that there must be a majority view on everything'"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin concludes the market-failure argument with 'planning redresses problems that market processes alone cannot solve'. The others come from Cozzolino (2020), Rydin (2007) and Hayek (1944)."},{"t":"gov","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'Rydin dismisses the governmental model as an outdated theory that no planner uses any more.' What is wrong with this?","o":["Rydin calls it the only valid model and asks research to adopt it fully","Rydin treats it as an ideal many planners aspire to and research shares","Rydin says it was never used in practice, only in planning textbooks","Rydin says it was replaced by the communicative model in the 1970s"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin calls the model an ideal that planning policy makers aspire to, found in public documentation and professional guidance, and shows seven recent studies that share it. She does not endorse it as the only valid model either: she shows what it leaves out."},{"t":"gov","type":"apply","q":"A researcher follows the paper trail of a contested permit and shows where local officials and a national commission bargained, stalled and finally refused. Which theme in Rydin's chapter does this fit?","o":["Supporting decision-making with new tools","Assessing integration across policy fields","Discretion and negotiation in implementation","Mapping power relations in growth coalitions"],"a":2,"e":"This mirrors Pinho's study of the Ria de Aveiro development: following the steps to find points of discretion and negotiation within implementation. Growth coalitions belong to urban politics, outside this frame."},{"t":"gov","type":"contrast","q":"How does Scott's high-modernist city relate to Rydin's governmental model?","o":["Scott shows rational planning can succeed when civil society is strong","Scott shows the expert-led model when backed by an authoritarian state","Scott rejects the model by arguing for more market-led urban growth","Scott shows the model works only for small, incremental local steps"],"a":1,"e":"Scott shows what happens when expert-led, rationalist planning combines with high-modernist ideology and an authoritarian state facing a weak civil society. He does not argue for market-led growth; his alternative stresses mētis, practical local knowledge."},{"t":"gov","type":"consequence","q":"If a thesis adopts the governmental frame without reflection, what is most likely missing from its analysis?","o":["Data on the situation before and after the plan","An assessment of which policy instruments worked","A step-by-step account of the planning process","Markets, power and politics outside the process"],"a":3,"e":"Research in this frame stays within the model: goals as given, steps and instruments, before-and-after data. What goes missing is the context outside the planning process: markets, power and politics. The other three are exactly what such a thesis does contain."},{"t":"gov","type":"position","q":"Where does the governmental model sit on the reproduction ↔ transformation dimension?","o":["Transformative: it aims to overturn existing institutions","Reformist: it improves planning within existing institutions","Reproductive: it argues planning only serves the ruling class","Neutral: it says institutions play no role in planning at all"],"a":1,"e":"Failures are fixed inside the planning process, for example by new tools such as GIS, so the model is reformist within existing institutions. The reproduction claim about the ruling class is a Marxist reading of planning, not the governmental model's own position."},{"t":"hayek","type":"quote","q":"Which quotation comes from Hayek's 'Planning and democracy'?","o":["'planning is a synthesising activity, looking across all relevant issues'","'it is a superstition to believe that there must be a majority view on everything'","'engaging different knowledges is fundamentally different to engaging different voices'","'the more one neighbourhood is designed from the top, the less space for complexity'"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek writes that majority decision works for limited alternatives, 'but it is a superstition to believe that there must be a majority view on everything'. The others are Rydin (2021), Rydin (2007) and Cozzolino (2020)."},{"t":"hayek","type":"contrast","q":"Hayek (1944) and Scott (1998) both distrust comprehensive state planning. How do their arguments differ?","o":["Hayek: no agreement on ends pushes power to experts; Scott: legibility ignores mētis","Hayek: planners lack data but could get it; Scott: planners have data but no will","Hayek: planning fails because markets fail; Scott: planning fails due to capital","Hayek: planning is undemocratic by design; Scott: it is too democratic to work"],"a":0,"e":"Hayek argues from value pluralism and the political mechanics of majority rule and delegation; Scott shows how high-modernist states simplify society to make it legible, ignoring practical local knowledge (mētis). Neither blames a lack of data or political will."},{"t":"hayek","type":"position","q":"Where does Hayek's chapter lean on the structure ↔ agency dimension, and why?","o":["Structure: the economic base determines what individuals can choose","Agency: it starts from individuals as the judges of their own ends","Structure: democratic institutions fully determine political outcomes","Agency: politicians freely choose to become dictators out of ambition"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek starts from individuals and their ends, so he leans to agency, though his slide from planning to dictatorship shows institutions shaping outcomes. He does not explain dictatorship by personal ambition: the task, not the people, is the cause."},{"t":"hayek","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'Hayek rejects democracy because majorities are always wrong.' What is wrong with this?","o":["Hayek values democracy only as long as it produces liberal outcomes","Hayek values democracy as a means; it fails when asked for too much agreement","Hayek rejects democracy but accepts majority rule for economic plans","Hayek says democracy is the highest end, above individual liberty"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek calls democracy 'a utilitarian device for safeguarding internal peace and individual freedom'; it breaks down when planning requires more agreement than exists. He does not say democracy is the highest end; that is the trap in the last option."},{"t":"hayek","type":"consequence","q":"If Hayek is right that 'many separate plans do not make a planned whole', what follows for a democracy that plans sector by sector?","o":["The sectoral plans will balance out through negotiation between ministries","Pressure grows to give one person or body the power to integrate them","Parliament can safely leave each sector to its own expert agency","Each sector will gradually develop its own complete ethical code"],"a":1,"e":"Since separate plans do not add up and parliament cannot produce the comprehensive plan, demands grow to give the government or 'some single individual' power to act: the cry for an economic dictator. Leaving sectors to separate agencies is only the first step on that road."},{"t":"hayek","type":"not","q":"Which idea do the HC4 slides NOT attribute to Hayek?","o":["Spontaneous order","Indeterminism","Complex phenomena","Base and superstructure"],"a":3,"e":"Base and superstructure is Marx's model. The slides attribute spontaneous order, indeterminism and the theory of complex phenomena to Hayek, even though none of these terms appears in 'Planning and democracy' itself."},{"t":"know","type":"contrast","q":"How does Rydin (2007) differ from the governmental model in Rydin (2021) on knowledge?","o":["2007: many actors make claims tested in arenas; 2021: experts gather it positively","2007: experts alone hold valid knowledge; 2021: residents' knowledge is the main base","2007: knowledge is purely relative; 2021: knowledge is constructed through deliberation","2007: values replace knowledge; 2021: knowledge replaces political goal-setting"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin 2007 is pluralist: lay and expert claims are heard and tested. The governmental model assumes knowledge 'can be gathered and made available to the planning process in a positive way' by expert planners. Rydin 2007 is not purely relativist: claims can be tested."},{"t":"know","type":"quote","q":"Which quotation comes from Rydin (2007)?","o":["'Many separate plans do not make a planned whole'","'not any imaginings will serve as a planning goal'","'Adaptability is indeed a relative concept'","'The goals themselves stand outside the planning process'"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin writes that normative knowledge of B1 is still knowledge because 'not any imaginings will serve as a planning goal'. The others are from Hayek (1944), Cozzolino (2020) and Rydin's governmental chapter (2021)."},{"t":"know","type":"position","q":"Where does Rydin (2007) sit on the monist ↔ pluralist and realism ↔ relativism questions?","o":["Monist and realist: only experts produce knowledge that mirrors reality","Pluralist and strongly relativist: no claim can be judged better than another","Pluralist and soft relativist: many actors know, yet claims can be tested","Monist and soft relativist: one source, but frameworks cannot be compared"],"a":2,"e":"Rydin accepts that many actors hold knowledge (pluralist) and that knowledge is socially constructed, yet claims can be tested and recognised, which the lecture calls soft relativism. Strong relativism is the trap: she explicitly wants closing down."},{"t":"know","type":"apply","q":"A province wants to know whether its policy of densifying around stations really reduces car use. Following Rydin, how should this claim be tested?","o":["By a resident survey on how much they support the station policy","By engaging rival causal models in research and policy evaluation","By public-sphere debate on whether car use ought to be reduced","By asking the province's planners to rely on their professional view"],"a":1,"e":"Densification reducing car use is a process claim about causal mechanisms; Table 2 tests these by engaging rival causal models in academic research or evaluations until robust enough to act on. Public debate on whether car use ought to fall concerns a normative claim, not this causal one."},{"t":"know","type":"consequence","q":"According to Rydin, what happens to knowledge claims if planning has no specific forums to test them?","o":["They become more robust because more voices are heard","They are automatically resolved through expert synthesis","They lose their specific character and dissolve into other claims","They turn into normative claims that the public sphere can settle"],"a":2,"e":"Rydin warns that without testing forums knowledge claims lose their specific character and dissolve into other claims. More voices alone do not make knowledge robust: robustness requires testing."},{"t":"know","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'Rydin (2007) wants to replace collaborative planning with expert-led knowledge production.' What is wrong with this?","o":["Rydin wants deliberation to drop knowledge and focus only on values","Rydin keeps many voices but adds arenas to test knowledge claims","Rydin says lay knowledge should always outrank expert knowledge","Rydin rejects any distinction between experts and lay participants"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin wants to bridge modernist and postmodern planning theory: hear many voices for democracy and empowerment, and also create spaces to test knowledge claims. She does not rank lay over expert knowledge either: both are claims to be tested."},{"t":"davoudi","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT among the reasons Davoudi gives why evidence fails to steer policy in the real world?","o":["Policy-making is bricolage, built from borrowed bits and compromises","More information can confuse choices instead of clarifying them","Much commissioned research simply sits unused on the shelf","Researchers routinely falsify their data to please their funders"],"a":3,"e":"Davoudi never claims researchers falsify data; her problem is with how evidence is used, not faked. Bricolage (Ball), confusion from more information (Young et al., Keynes) and unused research (Weiss's 'problem of little effect') are all in the article."},{"t":"davoudi","type":"contrast","q":"How do Davoudi's instrumental and enlightenment models differ on how research reaches policy?","o":["Instrumental: via public debate; enlightenment: via direct expert advice","Instrumental: direct and quick; enlightenment: indirect, via debate","Instrumental: through courts; enlightenment: through planning inspectors","Instrumental: two-way exchange; enlightenment: one-way from science"],"a":1,"e":"The instrumental model assumes a direct, linear and fast transfer; under the enlightenment model research has indirect benefits that take longer and works by illuminating the landscape and informing public debate. The first option swaps the two."},{"t":"davoudi","type":"apply","q":"For twenty years, cost-benefit studies of a hypothetical rail line show benefits far below costs, yet regional leaders keep pushing it in the name of national cohesion. Which idea from Davoudi fits best?","o":["Evidence competing with interests and ideology","The expert-on-top model, with research in charge","The enlightenment model shaping long-run debate","The soundness test turning into a tick-box ritual"],"a":0,"e":"Evidence is only one contender: regional interests and a value of cohesion (ideology) keep the project alive whatever the studies show, Weiss's four I's at work. The expert-on-top model would predict the opposite, since research would lead policy."},{"t":"davoudi","type":"quote","q":"Davoudi concludes that two things share 'ambitious and naïve assumptions that the complex political and socio-economic processes could be technicized, commanded and controlled through a scientific process'. Which two?","o":["Geddes's survey method and communicative planning","Weiss's four I's and the enlightenment model","Evidence-based policy and 1970s positivist planning","High modernism and market-led planning of the 1980s"],"a":2,"e":"The concluding sentence links the current enthusiasm for evidence-based policy to the positivist approach to planning of the 1960s and 1970s. Communicative planning is criticised for a different reason: focusing on knowing how rather than knowing what."},{"t":"davoudi","type":"position","q":"Where does Davoudi's account of real-world policy-making sit on the deliberate versus emergent dimension?","o":["Fully deliberate: good evidence lets policy follow a planned path","Closer to emergent: bricolage and muddling through, not a plan","Fully emergent: research and planning make no difference at all","Closer to deliberate: experts steer while politics stays outside"],"a":1,"e":"With Ball's bricolage and Lindblom's 'disjointed, incremental' decisions, Davoudi describes policy as messy and partly emergent. She is not fully on the emergent side, since she still wants research and planning to inform policy."},{"t":"davoudi","type":"consequence","q":"If Davoudi is right that 1980s and 1990s planning focused on process (knowing how) at the expense of knowing what, what should planning education do?","o":["Train planners mainly as mediators of conflicts between parties","Teach procedural law so plans pass the soundness test more often","Leave substantive questions to economists and other specialists","Rebuild substantive knowledge of how cities and places work"],"a":3,"e":"Davoudi cites the RTPI's 2003 call for critical thinking about space and place to rebuild the substantive knowledge base of planning. Mediation and procedure are exactly the 'knowing how' focus she says crowded it out."},{"t":"scott","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Scott's account of Chandigarh?","o":["Le Corbusier kept the bazaars but widened the streets that ran around them","Small street traders and hawkers were banned from the city centre","Le Corbusier replaced the central housing with an 'acropolis of monuments'","As in Brasília, an unplanned city grew at the periphery and margins"],"a":0,"e":"In place of crowded bazaars Le Corbusier substituted huge squares that today stand largely empty; he did not keep the bazaars. The other three are in Scott's account, including the unplanned periphery that contradicted the order at the centre."},{"t":"scott","type":"contrast","q":"How do Le Corbusier and Jacobs, as Scott presents them, differ on what makes something 'orderly'?","o":["For Le Corbusier order is set by residents' tastes; for Jacobs by rules","For Le Corbusier order is efficient traffic; for Jacobs it is neat lawns","For Le Corbusier order is geometric form; for Jacobs the function served","For Le Corbusier order means mixed use; for Jacobs it means single use"],"a":2,"e":"Le Corbusier believed the most efficient forms would always have a classical, geometric clarity; Jacobs held that the order of a thing is set by the purpose it serves, not its surface regularity. The last option reverses their views on use."},{"t":"scott","type":"apply","q":"A hypothetical Dutch municipality designs a new district by calculating square metres, daylight and parking per resident, and asks residents nothing because 'the norms already cover their needs'. Which idea from Scott fits best?","o":["The plan's abstract, schematic citizen with stipulated needs","The symbiosis of the planned and the unplanned city","Jacobs's eyes on the street that keep the sidewalks safe","The clean tablecloth of building on a cleared, empty site"],"a":0,"e":"Scott shows Le Corbusier established needs by stipulating an abstract, simplified subject with so many square metres, air and light, never admitting residents might have something valuable to say. The case says nothing about a cleared site or unplanned settlements."},{"t":"scott","type":"quote","q":"'The despot is not a man. It is the Plan.' Who wrote this, and how does Scott use it?","o":["Jacobs, mocking planners who treat cities as works of art","Holston, quoting a Brasília official on the city's master plan","Scott himself, summing up the danger of every planning system","Le Corbusier; Scott shows the plan overriding law and voters"],"a":3,"e":"Scott quotes Le Corbusier at length and concludes that the plan's wisdom sweeps away elected authorities, voters, the constitution and the law: at least a dictatorship of the planner. Scott does not condemn every planning system; his target is authoritarian high modernism."},{"t":"scott","type":"consequence","q":"If Scott is right that Brasília's planned centre virtually required an unplanned Brasília at the margins, what follows for judging a new-town master plan?","o":["Judge the plan only on the area inside its own official boundaries","Also ask where the people who build and serve the town will live","Assume squatting will disappear once the planned housing is finished","Prefer plans that leave no land at all outside the designed districts"],"a":1,"e":"Scott's point is that the order at the centre depends on the unplanned city that houses construction workers and the poor, so an evaluation that stops at the plan's boundary misses its real effects. In Brasília squatting did not disappear; the squatters organised and stayed."},{"t":"scott","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT a feature Scott attributes to Le Corbusier's high-modernist planning?","o":["A refusal of any concession to the existing city and its history","Plans designed to be seen at a glance from a great distance or the air","Adjusting the plan step by step in response to residents' use of it","Standardised building parts and international building standards"],"a":2,"e":"Incremental adjustment to feedback is the opposite of Le Corbusier's doctrine: the plan was to be laid out from start to finish, with 'a clean tablecloth'. Refusing concessions, the view from the air and standardisation (even via the League of Nations) are all in the chapter."},{"t":"needham","type":"quote","q":"Needham writes: 'The decision remains free, but is influenced or constrained.' What is he describing?","o":["How permits, levies, subsidies, persuasion and infrastructure steer owners without taking their decision","How expropriation leaves the owner free to choose a new location for her activities after the land is taken","How a property right lets its holder decide freely without any influence from neighbours or the authority","How the court decides freely between plaintiff and defendant, constrained only by the rules of procedure"],"a":0,"e":"After listing development control, subsidies and levies, persuasion and infrastructure, Needham says the decision 'remains free, but is influenced or constrained'; it 'ceases to be free if ownership is taken away' (p. 25). So expropriation is the exception, not the example."},{"t":"needham","type":"position","q":"Where does Needham sit on the deliberate ↔ emergent dimension of change, and why?","o":["Emergent: he holds that property rights evolve through unintended trial and error that no state designs","Deliberate: property rights can be purposefully changed by the state to steer land use","Emergent: he argues that planning goals should be left entirely to spontaneous market exchange","Neither: he treats rights as natural and fixed, so the question of change does not arise for him"],"a":1,"e":"Since rights are created, 'property rights can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use' (pp. 28-29): change by design. Emergent, unintended evolution of rules is Moroni's evolutionary view, not Needham's."},{"t":"needham","type":"contrast","q":"How do Needham and Moroni differ on property rights and institutional change?","o":["Needham sees rights as natural; Moroni sees them as created by the state for specific policy purposes","Both see rights as fixed by national constitutions, so neither expects any real change in property law","Needham: rights are a policy variable to redesign; Moroni: institutions mostly evolve unintended","Needham wants to abolish property rights; Moroni wants planners to design all of them from scratch"],"a":2,"e":"Needham calls rights a 'policy variable' that can be purposefully changed; Moroni argues most institutions are the 'unintentional outcome of a slow and lengthy evolution' that cannot be designed like organisations. The first option swaps their positions in a misleading way."},{"t":"needham","type":"misread","q":"A classmate says: 'Needham wants to abolish zoning and let markets in property rights decide land use.' What is wrong with this?","o":["Nothing: he argues that markets always reach planning goals more efficiently than permits","He wants the opposite: to replace all private property in land with public land ownership","He only says markets are desirable where they are feasible, which covers most land uses","He only tests feasibility, finds it limited, and does not recommend replacing planning"],"a":3,"e":"The chapter 'goes no further than an investigation of feasibility: it does not recommend replacing land-use planning by markets in property rights' (p. 23), and the conclusion is 'not very radical'. He also says feasibility is not desirability, which rules out the third option."},{"t":"needham","type":"consequence","q":"If rights are created by states rather than natural, as Needham argues, what follows?","o":["All countries must end up with the same set of property rights over the long run","Courts may ignore property rights whenever a planner finds them inconvenient","One planning problem may have a market solution in one country but not in another","Property rights lose all value, since the state can withdraw them at any moment"],"a":2,"e":"Because 'different state governments can create different property rights' (p. 27), US covenants and English landlord-run estates work, while Dutch property law blocks the same solutions (pp. 31-32). Rights are still protected by courts, so they do not lose their value."},{"t":"needham","type":"apply","q":"In a Dutch apartment building, all owners are by law members of the owners' association (VvE), which can enforce house rules against an owner who lets her flat to tourists. In Needham's terms this is...","o":["public-law protection: the municipality enforces the house rules through its permits","private-law protection: rights and duties among owners, enforced by their association","an interest protected neither way, since the state has decided it is less important","a libertarian denial of external effects, since the owners simply regulate one another"],"a":1,"e":"The neighbours' interest in quiet is turned into enforceable rights and duties among owners, which the association uses in civil law: the private-law route. Compulsory membership is exactly what Needham says is missing for Dutch housing estates, which is why covenants do not work there."},{"t":"ni","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREADS the lecture's distinction between institutions and organisations?","o":["The European Broadcasting Union is an organisation; its statutes are an institution","Informal norms can be institutions just as much as written laws and statutes can be","A municipality is an institution, since it is the body that makes and enforces rules","A housing corporation is an organisation, even though it works within institutions"],"a":2,"e":"A municipality is a concrete entity with a purpose, so it is an organisation; the rules it makes and works within are institutions. The EBU example and the inclusion of informal norms are straight from the lecture."},{"t":"ni","type":"contrast","q":"How do sociological and evolutionary institutionalism differ on how institutions change?","o":["SI: change is deliberate but must be embedded in symbols and habits; EI: change is emergent trial and error","SI: change follows from a rational redesign of the payoffs; EI: change follows from critical junctures in crises","SI: change comes from slow, unintended evolution; EI: change comes from new symbols that planners create","SI: institutions never change once internalised; EI: institutions change whenever planners design them"],"a":0,"e":"On the lecture's table, sociological change is deliberate but must be embedded (symbols, habitus), while evolutionary change is emergent trial and error with only modest intervention. The third option swaps the two strands."},{"t":"ni","type":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: in a Dutch conservation area, owners may only use certain roof tiles and window frames when renovating. In Ostrom's IAD terms, this is mainly a…","o":["Position rule, defining which roles exist in the conservation area","Choice rule, defining which actions are allowed or forbidden","Information rule, defining what owners must report to the city","Aggregation rule, defining how owners decide on things together"],"a":1,"e":"Choice rules define what an actor in a position is allowed, obliged or forbidden to do; the slide's example is that fishers may only use certain gear. Restricting materials is the same kind of rule."},{"t":"ni","type":"quote","q":"The lecture quotes Sorensen: critical juncture analysis explains 'the processes through which political actors take advantage of conjunctural crises to further particular institutional changes'. What does this imply?","o":["Historical institutionalism leaves no room for agency, since paths fully determine outcomes","Crises change institutions automatically, whatever the actors involved happen to want","In historical institutionalism agency matters most when crises open room for change","Actors can redesign institutions at any time if they understand the crisis well enough"],"a":2,"e":"Historical institutionalism stresses structure, but critical junctures are moments of heightened agency in which actors use crises to push particular changes. So it is not agency-free, and change is neither automatic nor available at any time."},{"t":"ni","type":"consequence","q":"If sociological institutionalism is right that institutions shape preferences, what follows for a survey of residents' housing preferences?","o":["The answers can be read as fixed, independent inputs that planners simply add up","The answers will be strategic, since residents misstate preferences to gain benefits","The answers are irrelevant, since only experts know what residents truly need","The answers partly reflect existing institutions, so they are not neutral givens"],"a":3,"e":"If institutions 'establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences', stated preferences are partly products of existing norms (for example homeownership as normal). Strategic misstatement is a rational choice worry; dismissing residents is not implied."},{"t":"ni","type":"position","q":"Where does sociological institutionalism sit on the structure and agency dimension?","o":["Pure agency: individuals freely choose which norms they want to follow","Pure structure: actors are passive bearers of the economic base","Internalised structure, re-enacted and slowly changed through practice","Agency within external rules that actors calculate their way around"],"a":2,"e":"Sociological institutionalism leans to structure, but a structure that is internalised (scripts, templates) and reproduced or changed in practice. Calculating around external rules is the rational choice position; a passive base is Marxist determinism."},{"t":"olson","type":"quote","q":"Hindmoor quotes Hardin: 'Each man is locked into a system that compels him to increase his herd without limit in a world that is limited.' What does this express?","o":["Individually rational use of a shared, non-excludable resource leads to its collective ruin","Herders cooperate out of a shared moral norm, which shows the limits of self-interest models","A dominant herder pays for the whole pasture and lets the others graze without paying","Herders who expect to meet for a long time develop tit-for-tat rules for grazing"],"a":0,"e":"Each farmer gains by adding animals whether or not others hold back, so the pasture is ruined: a collective action problem over a common-pool good (Box 5.3). Tit-for-tat rules are the Ostrom answer Hindmoor presents later, not Hardin's claim."},{"t":"olson","type":"contrast","q":"Taylor's future homebuyers ('outsiders') and Olson's latent groups both fail to make their interests count. What separates them?","o":["Latent groups free-ride although members exist; future buyers are not yet present to take part","Latent groups are always small; future buyers form a large privileged group that pays for it all","Latent groups are coerced by the state; future buyers are protected by their property rights","Nothing: both are defined by the same mechanism of selective incentives that fail to work"],"a":0,"e":"In a latent group existing members free-ride on each other; Taylor's outsiders (via Evans) are future residents who 'have no voice' in participation at all. Both leave concentrated insiders or organised members dominant, but for different reasons."},{"t":"olson","type":"position","q":"Where does Hindmoor's account of Olson sit on the structure ↔ agency dimension?","o":["Structure: class position fully determines whether a group can act together","Structure: inherited routines and paths decide which groups are able to form","Agency: collective outcomes are built up from individuals' self-interested choices","Agency: shared meanings formed in dialogue decide which groups will be able to act together"],"a":2,"e":"Olson explains group outcomes from rational individuals' choices whether to contribute (methodological individualism). The solutions Hindmoor reviews bring in structures such as the state, rules and norms, but the starting point is individual agency."},{"t":"olson","type":"misread","q":"A student essay claims: 'Olson's selective incentives explain why people join Amnesty International: members get a magazine and a pin.' What is wrong with this?","o":["Nothing: Olson treats all groups as driven by excludable rewards of this kind","Olson concedes his analysis cannot explain 'non-economic' groups that serve others","Olson says Amnesty is a privileged group, because a few rich donors pay for everything","Olson shows that groups like Amnesty never form, since everyone free-rides"],"a":1,"e":"Olson 'eventually acknowledges' that his analysis 'cannot be used to explain collective action in non-economic groups like Greenpeace or Amnesty International' which people do not join for self-interested reasons (p. 123). That is one of the limits of the self-interest assumption."},{"t":"olson","type":"consequence","q":"Box 5.1 says that 'in most cases excludability is a matter of cost'. What follows from this?","o":["Whether a good invites free-riding can change when excluding non-payers becomes cheaper","No good can ever become excludable, so every good invites free-riding to the same degree","Excludable goods are always provided by the state, because markets cannot set a price","Cost only affects rivalness, so the collective action problem stays exactly the same"],"a":0,"e":"Most goods can in principle be made excludable (black-out ships for the lighthouse) but at a cost; if exclusion becomes cheap, a good can move from public to toll good and the free-rider problem weakens. The second option contradicts 'in principle most goods can be rendered excludable'."},{"t":"olson","type":"apply","q":"Three residents organise a street campaign for traffic calming and promise those who help a free parking sticker and an invitation to a residents' dinner. In Hindmoor's terms, the three are...","o":["a privileged group, because they pay the whole cost of the traffic calming","free-riders, because they benefit from the work of the other residents","a latent group, because nobody bears a share of the cost of the campaign","entrepreneurs offering positive selective incentives to overcome free-riding"],"a":3,"e":"Entrepreneurs can facilitate collective action 'by providing selective incentives' (p. 117), here excludable rewards for contributors. They do not bear the full cost of the good itself, so this is not a privileged group."},{"t":"taylor","type":"quote","q":"Submissions called the GAIC 'unbelievable, unethical, outright robbery'. How does Taylor treat such statements?","o":["As proof that the charge was legally invalid and should have been dropped","As evidence that owners framed the charge as a tax on value they already had","As irrelevant emotion, which she excludes from her coding of the submissions","As a sign that the government had failed to consult owners before the bill"],"a":1,"e":"Taylor uses such framings to show that objectors treated the GAIC as a tax on their existing land value, not on a future windfall, often in emotional terms about years of hard work (p. 269). She codes them rather than excluding them."},{"t":"taylor","type":"contrast","q":"How would Taylor and Dembski and Salet differ in reading landowners' stories about their 'rural way of life'?","o":["Taylor sees them as institutions emerging from practice; Dembski and Salet as lobbying for rents","Both see them as strategic manipulation aimed at protecting existing land values on the fringe","Taylor reads them as partly driven by self-interest; Dembski and Salet as shared meaning","Both see them as evidence of path dependence in the regional planning system over decades"],"a":2,"e":"Taylor tests whether amenity claims align with property interests (rent-seeking, happenstance); Dembski and Salet's sociological institutionalism treats symbols and meanings as constitutive of institutions. The first option swaps the two readings."},{"t":"taylor","type":"apply","q":"A year after telling a Dutch inquiry that rezoning would not change her land's value, a farmer sells the rezoned plot for twenty times the farm price. Which part of Taylor's argument does this mirror?","o":["The finding that owners with development plans only rarely objected to the charge","The minister's claim that deferral protected hobby farmers and lifestyle owners","The contrast between discredited windfalls and the large sales reported soon after","The bias in participation towards insiders whenever an urban extension is planned"],"a":2,"e":"Taylor sets landowners' successful denial of betterment against press reports of sales such as a 10-hectare site bought for $50,000 that sold for $7.54 million (p. 273). The case is about a denied gain, not about which owners objected, so the Table 4 finding is not the point."},{"t":"taylor","type":"consequence","q":"If the redesigned GAIC is in practice largely paid by homebuyers, what follows for Taylor's argument?","o":["Betterment is captured for the public, so the rent-seeking problem has been solved","Insiders keep most of the value gain while outsiders pay, which hurts affordability","Developers bear the cost, so they will lobby to have the boundary moved back again","Landowners lose the windfall, which confirms that all their objections were sincere"],"a":1,"e":"Taylor's closing cycle: containment creates premiums, these are redistributed through insiders' lobbying, and costs are likely passed on to housing consumers as outsiders (p. 274), a concern in a city with affordability problems. Developers tend to pass such charges on rather than bear them (p. 271)."},{"t":"taylor","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT part of the rent-seeking lens Taylor applies to the submissions?","o":["Planning allocates rights with economic value, which invites capture","Owners may pursue gains through political pressure, not productive effort","Altruistic arguments can unintentionally align with property interests","Landowners' claims must be assumed to be deliberately dishonest"],"a":3,"e":"Taylor does not claim all landowners were lying; she tests whether part of their claims reflected perceived self-interest, and allows for happenstance (p. 260). The other options are core elements: capture of allocated rights (p. 264), rent-seeking and Clingermayer's routes."},{"t":"taylor","type":"position","q":"Where does Taylor's study sit on the universal versus situated knowledge dimension?","o":["Situated in one Melbourne episode, though it draws a wider lesson on insiders","Fully universal: it derives a law of land markets valid in every planning system","Purely situated: it explicitly denies that its findings matter beyond Melbourne","Universal, because it tests a model with price data from many growth boundaries"],"a":0,"e":"The article is a single-case content analysis of one Melbourne episode, but Taylor draws a broader implication about insiders and outsiders and compares Melbourne with the UK (p. 274). It neither claims a universal law nor rules out wider relevance."},{"t":"dembski","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'Dembski & Salet show that institutions explain why cities cannot change.' What is wrong with this summary?","o":["Nothing: they agree that institutions are rusty fixtures that block change","They use institutions to explain change: norms must be revalidated in practice","They argue institutions are irrelevant, since only symbols explain change","They argue change comes only from external shocks at critical junctures"],"a":1,"e":"Their title is 'the transformative potential of institutions': because norms must be validated again and again, institutions are 'not carved in stone' and leave room for innovation. The critical-juncture option describes historical institutionalism, which they find weak at explaining change."},{"t":"dembski","type":"contrast","q":"Scott and Dembski & Salet both doubt that planners can impose meaning from above. What separates their reasons?","o":["Scott thinks the state can impose meaning; Dembski & Salet deny planning any role","Scott studies symbols and rituals; Dembski & Salet study state maps and simplification","Scott stresses lost local know-how (mētis); Dembski & Salet stress meaning the audience must absorb","Scott sees norms as validated in action; Dembski & Salet see norms as fixed before action"],"a":2,"e":"Scott's high-modernist schemes fail because they erase practical local knowledge; Dembski & Salet's symbols fail when their meaning is not inculcated. The second option swaps the two authors' objects of study."},{"t":"dembski","type":"apply","q":"A municipality plans a yearly festival to rename a district. Which step of Dembski & Salet's research model asks what happens to people who ignore or mock the new name, and who reacts?","o":["Step 8, the sanctions for misrecognition","Step 5, the audience being addressed","Step 6, the articulation over time","Step 1, the symbolic markers used"],"a":0,"e":"Step 8 analyses sanctions in case of misrecognition and who the sanctioners are (the believers are expected to act as sanctioners). Step 6 covers conflicting interpretations over time, but not the response to misrecognition."},{"t":"dembski","type":"quote","q":"Dembski & Salet write: 'Institutional innovation does not just mean turning the latch of the door. Rather, it is deliberate change.' What do they mean?","o":["Change happens by itself once an old norm loses its formal legal basis","Planners can replace old norms at will if they act deliberately enough","Changing meaning requires a sound grasp of the norms that already exist","Innovation must wait for a crisis that opens the door to a new path"],"a":2,"e":"The sentence follows their claim that changing perceptions 'requires a very sound notion of existing social norms and symbols'. 'Deliberate' does not mean voluntaristic, so the second option misreads it."},{"t":"dembski","type":"position","q":"Where do Dembski & Salet sit on the universal ↔ situated knowledge dimension, and why?","o":["Universal: good symbol design works in any region if communicated well","Universal: their eight-step model yields one general law of symbolic success","Situated: only residents, never planners, may legitimately create symbols","Situated: a symbol's meaning depends on audience, place and existing norms"],"a":3,"e":"Meaning must be inculcated by particular audiences with their own norms and habitus, so it is situated. The third option overshoots: planners can act, provided they connect to existing norms and are seen as legitimate."},{"t":"dembski","type":"consequence","q":"If Dembski & Salet are right, how should a city-branding campaign be evaluated?","o":["By its design quality and the reach of its media campaign","By whether a law makes the new name compulsory in documents","By whether audiences absorb its meaning and act on it in practice","By its cost per resident compared with rival regional campaigns"],"a":2,"e":"Success lies in institutional embedding: people must believe and reproduce the meaning in practice. Design quality and reach are exactly the 'pearls of rhetoric' the authors call not decisive."},{"t":"dembski","type":"not","q":"Which of these is NOT one of the four quadrants in Dembski & Salet's Table 1?","o":["Rational choice institutionalism","Historical institutionalism","Evolutionary institutionalism","Actor-centred institutionalism"],"a":2,"e":"Table 1 has rational choice, historical, political or actor-centred, and sociological institutionalism. Evolutionary institutionalism is Moroni's strand and does not appear in their map."},{"t":"moroni","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'Moroni says institutions are natural phenomena that have nothing to do with human action.' What is wrong with this?","o":["Nothing: he places institutions with rivers and the weather","Artificial but undesigned: results of action, not of design","They are artificial and designed, just like any organisation","They are natural only in the Global South, not in Europe"],"a":1,"e":"Moroni's third category is exactly this: artificial phenomena that are the unintended result of interplay among intentional actions. The third option is the opposite error, collapsing institutions into organisations."},{"t":"moroni","type":"contrast","q":"Alexander and Moroni both write about 'institutional design'. What separates them?","o":["Alexander rejects design; Moroni sees design at macro, meso and micro levels","Alexander: design at all three levels; Moroni: design for organisations only","Both reject design, but Alexander bases this on power and Moroni on symbols","Both accept design, but Moroni limits it to the macro level of whole societies"],"a":1,"e":"Alexander defines institutional design across macro, meso and micro levels; Moroni sees a breach between the macro level and the others and keeps 'design' for organisations. The last option reverses Moroni: the macro level is exactly where he rejects design."},{"t":"moroni","type":"apply","q":"A town has an old, unwritten custom that neighbours consult each other before building an extension, and it works well. The council wants to replace it with a detailed new permit procedure. Which principle would Moroni invoke first?","o":["Modesty: recognise what has proved useful","Openness: make rules for many future cases","Knowability: publish all rules in advance","Motivational realism: assume non-virtue"],"a":0,"e":"Modesty asks reformers to recognise and implement institutions that have evolved and proved beneficial rather than invent new ones. Knowability is tempting, but nothing in the case says the custom is unclear or unknown."},{"t":"moroni","type":"quote","q":"Moroni cites Hodgson: institutions are both structures 'outside' individuals and ideas that live 'inside' their heads. Which principle does this support?","o":["Sense-making: meaning cannot be made by fiat","Robustness: rules should rarely be changed","Peculiarity: rules have meta-ends, not goals","Gradualism: change should go step by step"],"a":0,"e":"Under sense-making Moroni argues that institutions rely on the meaning given to them, which an administrative fiat cannot create, so reformers face 'the limits of potential control over meaning'. Peculiarity concerns the difference between organisations and institutions."},{"t":"moroni","type":"position","q":"Where does Moroni sit on the reproduction ↔ transformation dimension, and why?","o":["Pure reproduction: institutions never change once they have evolved","Pure transformation: planners should rebuild institutions from scratch","In between: evolved rules persist, yet evolution and reform renew them","Transformation only through crises that open short windows for choice"],"a":2,"e":"Evolution produces qualitative novelty and Moroni allows gradual, modest reform, but always within a consolidated framework. The last option is Sorensen's critical-juncture logic, not Moroni's."},{"t":"moroni","type":"consequence","q":"If Moroni is right, by what standard should a planner judge a proposed land-use rule?","o":["Whether it delivers the housing target that the current cabinet has set","Whether it shifts land value uplift from owners to the public purse","Whether the local audience has inculcated the meaning of the rule","Whether it is general, stable, known in advance and open to discovery"],"a":3,"e":"Openness, robustness, knowability and adaptive efficiency are Moroni's tests; subordinating rules to a set target is the second-order taxis error. The third option is Sorensen's equity standard."},{"t":"moroni","type":"not","q":"Which of these authors does Moroni NOT draw on as a source of the evolutionary view of institutions?","o":["Friedrich von Hayek","Adam Ferguson","Viktor J. Vanberg","Evgeny Pashukanis"],"a":3,"e":"Moroni cites Pashukanis as an example of early 20th-century planning thought that was hostile to stable general rules and favoured case-by-case intervention: the opposite of his position. Ferguson, Hayek and the Neo-Austrian Vanberg are his sources."},{"t":"sorensen","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'Sorensen shows that Toronto's value capture was locked in by the 1946 Planning Act and has hardly changed since.' What is wrong with this?","o":["Nothing: he argues the 1946 rules have stayed fixed ever since","Contest and incremental change never stopped, and 1989 was a new juncture","He argues the 1946 Act had no lasting effect on later value capture","He shows that only federal laws, not provincial ones, shaped the rules"],"a":1,"e":"Sorensen rejects lock-in: lot levies spread, the 1989 DC Act was a second critical juncture, and contest continues today, all within a developmental pathway set in 1946. The third option overcorrects: 1946 did set the terrain of debate."},{"t":"sorensen","type":"contrast","q":"Olson (via Hindmoor) and Sorensen both discuss organised interests. What separates their focus?","o":["Olson: why groups manage to organise; Sorensen: how organised actors contest rules over time","Olson: how rules are contested over time; Sorensen: why groups manage to organise at all","Both study symbols, but Olson studies national and Sorensen local symbolic markers","Both reject the role of interests and explain change by evolution and trial and error"],"a":0,"e":"Olson's logic of collective action explains which groups organise (small, concentrated ones); Sorensen traces how organised municipalities and developers fight over formal rules through junctures and reactive sequences. The second option swaps the two."},{"t":"sorensen","type":"apply","q":"A Dutch city asks why a similar city nearby captures far more of the value uplift from rezoning under the same national law. What would Sorensen advise it to examine?","o":["The local institutional history and timing that set each city's practice","Which city has evolved the most efficient rules by trial and error","Whether residents in each city believe in the symbols of the plan","How much each city's developers spend on lobbying national ministers"],"a":0,"e":"For Sorensen, variation in value capture comes from contingent, locally specific institutional histories and the timing of change, as in Toronto versus Vancouver. The evolutionary option assumes a benign selection of efficient rules, which he does not."},{"t":"sorensen","type":"quote","q":"Sorensen calls path dependence 'a pattern more often assumed than it is explained'. What does he mean?","o":["Path dependence does not exist in cities, so it should be dropped","Scholars invoke it without showing the mechanisms behind it","Path dependence is fully explained by global ideological trends","Only quantitative models can explain path dependence well"],"a":1,"e":"His point is that path dependence is often asserted rather than traced; the Toronto case shows its mechanisms: junctures set the terrain, the rules and the ambiguities within which contest unfolds. He does not deny path dependence, so the first option is wrong."},{"t":"sorensen","type":"position","q":"Where does Sorensen sit on the deliberate ↔ emergent dimension, and why?","o":["Fully deliberate: planners design value capture rules as they choose","Fully emergent: rules evolve by trial and error, beyond anyone's control","Mixed: each battle is deliberate, but the long pathway emerges over time","Mixed: change is emergent at junctures but deliberate in between them"],"a":2,"e":"Actors deliberately fight over specific rules, but the pathway that results from junctures and reactive sequences is not anyone's design. The last option reverses the logic: junctures are when deliberate choice matters most."},{"t":"sorensen","type":"consequence","q":"If Sorensen is right, what follows for comparing land value capture across countries?","o":["Expect convergence, since global ideologies set the same rules everywhere","Expect variation, and compare the junctures and conditions behind it","Expect the most efficient rules to be selected in every country","Expect no change anywhere, since every country is locked in"],"a":1,"e":"He stresses locally contingent outcomes and offers HI as a comparative method: identify similar junctures and the antecedent and productive conditions that made outcomes diverge. The first option ignores his point that global currents are mediated locally."},{"t":"sorensen","type":"not","q":"Which of these is NOT a source of evidence Sorensen uses for the Toronto case?","o":["Ontario parliamentary debates and City reports","Financial data on value capture and Section 37","Eleven interviews with planners and councillors","A survey of homebuyers' willingness to pay more"],"a":3,"e":"He combines secondary literature, parliamentary debates and City reports, two sets of financial data and eleven interviews, in a process-tracing narrative. No survey of homebuyers is used."},{"t":"fog","type":"contrast","q":"Foglesong and Raco & Imrie both study how the state relates to urban development. What is the key difference?","o":["Foglesong: the state serves capital's collective needs; Raco & Imrie: power works through discourses and self-governing subjects","Foglesong: power works through discourses and self-governing subjects; Raco & Imrie: the state serves capital's collective needs","Foglesong: the state is a neutral arbiter among groups; Raco & Imrie: the state is a tool directly controlled by capitalists","Foglesong: the state is a unitary actor pursuing set goals; Raco & Imrie: the state is a relatively autonomous servant of capital"],"a":0,"e":"The course contrasts Marxist readings (Harvey, Foglesong), in which the state serves capital, with Raco & Imrie's governmentality, in which power is not held by one sovereign state but works through discourses, techniques and subjects. The second option simply swaps the two."},{"t":"fog","type":"position","q":"Where does Foglesong's knowledge claim sit on the universal ↔ situated dimension, according to the course material?","o":["Fully situated: it only describes one US city in the 1920s","Fairly general about capitalist cities, built on US history","Fully universal: a law valid for all societies and all eras","Situated: it describes the Nordic welfare-state land model"],"a":1,"e":"The course says his knowledge claim is general about capitalist cities but grounded in US planning history from the colonial era to the 1920s. It is not a law for all societies: it is explicitly about capitalist urbanisation."},{"t":"fog","type":"consequence","q":"If Foglesong is right that planning must not undermine private property itself, what follows for a city that tries to capture all land value increases for public use?","o":["Capital will support it, as long-term collective interests require it","Owners will accept it, as land's value is socially produced","It will meet resistance and end up limited or compromised","It will spread, as democratic control over land grows freely"],"a":2,"e":"Planning can coordinate land use only so far as it does not undermine private property; full value capture would challenge property itself and meet resistance from owners. The 'collective interest' option forgets that capital needs only some socialisation of control, not its full extension."},{"t":"fog","type":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: in a booming business district each office owner adds floors, streets jam and values start to fall. The owners' association asks the city for strict zoning, yet each owner lobbies for an exemption for his own plot. What does this show in Foglesong's terms?","o":["Planning is legitimised by presenting it as the public interest","The state is a tool that the owners' association simply directs","Democratic majorities push control over land too far for capital","Capital needs socialised control, yet the single owners resist it"],"a":3,"e":"This is the property contradiction in miniature: problems no single owner can solve call for socialised control, which owners collectively want and individually resist. The instrumentalist reading fails because the owners do not act as one coherent class controlling the state."},{"t":"fog","type":"not","q":"Which case is NOT linked to Foglesong's property contradiction in the course material?","o":["Taylor: Melbourne landowners fighting a betterment levy","Sorensen: Toronto's struggle over who pays for growth","Hyötyläinen & Haila and Savini & Aalbers: Week 6 cases","Dembski & Salet: symbolic markers in a city-region"],"a":3,"e":"The course reads Taylor, Sorensen, Eiranranta and Falck as clashes between the social character of land value and private or financial ownership. Dembski & Salet study whether symbolic markers gain meaning through institutional embedding, which has nothing to do with land ownership."},{"t":"fog","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS the course reading of Foglesong?","o":["Planning is needed to socialise some control over land for capital","Planning is a neutral correction of failures in the land market","Planning is constrained by fear of democratic control over property","Planning's form reflects the balance of political forces at a time"],"a":1,"e":"Foglesong is dissatisfied with mainstream accounts that treat planning as neutral public-interest expertise or as a correction of market failure, because they ignore whose interests planning serves. The other statements match the course summary."},{"t":"harvey","type":"quote","q":"Harvey writes that under capitalism the 'forcing' of competition 'occurs after rather than before'. What does he mean?","o":["Cities are told in advance by capital which projects to build","Cities experiment freely and competition then sorts the results","Cities are free from competition until national rules change","Cities only compete after the welfare state has collapsed fully"],"a":1,"e":"Like firms, cities explore paths speculatively without knowing what will succeed; inter-urban competition disciplines them afterwards (p. 15). The first option describes a captive local state, which Harvey rejects."},{"t":"harvey","type":"contrast","q":"Harvey (1989) and Foglesong both write in a Marxist tradition. How do the questions they ask differ?","o":["Harvey asks why planning exists at all; Foglesong asks why governance changed after 1973","Harvey asks how land is financialised; Foglesong asks how symbols create new meaning","Harvey asks why governance shifted after the 1970s; Foglesong asks why planning exists at all","Both ask the same question, but Harvey uses US cases and Foglesong uses British cases"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey explains the shift from managerialism to entrepreneurialism since the early 1970s; Foglesong asks why planning exists in capitalist societies and why its form is limited. The first option swaps the two questions."},{"t":"harvey","type":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: a national government pays for housing, rail links and training to keep a chip-machine firm in its region, with only an expectation, not a binding promise, that the firm will stay. How does this read through Harvey?","o":["The public sector absorbs costs to anchor mobile capital in the production strategy","The firm is a growth coalition that captures the planning system for its own rent","The state sells public land to the firm to maximise land rent for public budgets","The region follows the consumption strategy by investing in spectacle and image"],"a":0,"e":"Harvey's competition as 'external coercive law' makes public bodies absorb risk and costs to lure or keep mobile capital; here the lever is production in the international division of labour. Selling land for rent is Hyötyläinen & Haila's entrepreneurial public real estate policy, which does not happen here."},{"t":"harvey","type":"position","q":"Where does Harvey (1989) sit on the deliberate ↔ emergent dimension of change?","o":["Deliberate: national governments designed the entrepreneurial shift step by step","Deliberate: each mayor rationally chose entrepreneurialism as the best strategy","Neither: Harvey argues that urban governance did not change in the 1970s at all","Mostly emergent: many speculative local moves are filtered by competition"],"a":3,"e":"No one designed the global shift: cities experiment speculatively and competition sorts them after the event, so the change is largely emergent. Mayors do make choices, but for Harvey it is the competitive filtering, not rational design, that explains convergence."},{"t":"harvey","type":"consequence","q":"If Harvey is right that entrepreneurialism focuses on place rather than territory, what follows for problems that affect the whole region?","o":["They are solved faster, because flagship places spread wealth evenly","They are neglected, as flagship projects draw attention and resources","They move to national government, which takes over all local tasks","They disappear, since place projects always serve the whole region"],"a":1,"e":"Place-specific projects tend to become such a focus of public and political attention that they divert concern and even resources from the broader problems of the region (p. 8). The 'always serve the whole region' option ignores that benefits may be narrower than the jurisdiction."},{"t":"harvey","type":"not","q":"Harvey lists who may take part in the coalitions of urban governance. Which is NOT in his account?","o":["Chambers of commerce, financiers and property developers","Universities, religious institutions and trade unions","Elected officials only, since governance equals government","Social movements, political parties and local state branches"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey's point is that governance is much broader than government: it includes business, developers ('growth machine' politics), universities, unions, religious bodies, parties, social movements and parts of the local state (p. 6). 'Elected officials only' collapses governance back into government."},{"t":"hh","type":"contrast","q":"Foglesong and Hyötyläinen & Haila both deal with conflict over who controls the value of urban land. What is the key difference?","o":["Foglesong studies private owners against socialised control; in Helsinki the public owner itself maximises rent","Foglesong studies financial investors and SPVs; in Helsinki private landowners resist value capture","Foglesong studies symbolic markers; in Helsinki the state defends its impartial mediating role","Foglesong studies Nordic welfare cities; in Helsinki the state abolishes all public land ownership"],"a":0,"e":"Foglesong explains planning as a response to private ownership of socially produced land; Hyötyläinen & Haila show a public landowner (state and city) acting like a private rent maximiser. The SPV option describes Savini & Aalbers, not Foglesong."},{"t":"hh","type":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: a state property company sells surplus barracks land to a city, but demands the price of serviced building land even though the city will make the plan. How do Hyötyläinen & Haila read such a move?","o":["As value capture, which returns unearned gains to the community","As a state real estate player that has lost its impartial role","As a development contract between two public landowners","As the welfare city, which shifts care tasks to municipalities"],"a":1,"e":"When a state company demanded developed-land prices from Helsinki, the city defended its planning monopoly; the authors read this as the state becoming one rent-seeking player among others, unusual because it claims municipal rights (pp. 138, 143). It is not value capture: the gain belongs to the city that assigns development rights."},{"t":"hh","type":"quote","q":"An REC member said that 'Land ownership has become a secondary thing'. What does this reveal, according to the authors?","o":["The city had decided to lease all land for 99 years","The REC wanted to give land free to social housing","Entrepreneurial land policy had become common sense","Developers no longer wanted to own land in Helsinki"],"a":2,"e":"Members unanimously backed the sale, only one councillor opposed it, and selling to the highest bidder was repeated as 'the rules of the game': the new policy was already 'accepted and viewed as natural' (p. 141). The leasing option reverses what happened."},{"t":"hh","type":"position","q":"Where does Hyötyläinen & Haila's knowledge claim sit on the universal ↔ situated dimension?","o":["Universal: it states a law of land markets valid in all societies","Universal: it models land sales as rational choice by individuals","Situated: it shows that Nordic welfare cities are immune to markets","Situated: a single Helsinki case read within a Nordic welfare city"],"a":3,"e":"The study is one case (Eiranranta), explicitly framed as new for a Nordic welfare city and shaped by Finnish institutions such as the planning monopoly and MRL. The 'immune to markets' option is wrong: the whole point is that neoliberal policy reached Helsinki."},{"t":"hh","type":"consequence","q":"Critics of the MRL recalled what development contracts produced in the 1960s and 1970s. If they are right, what follows from relying on such contracts again?","o":["Poor-quality, fragmented housing on the city's outskirts","Higher social housing shares in prestigious neighbourhoods","A stronger planning monopoly for the city's town planners","Lower land prices because developers compete more openly"],"a":0,"e":"Critics pointed out that earlier development contracts led to poor-quality housing and a fragmented urban fabric, often on the outskirts (p. 139); the authors add that selling land gave developers more power in planning. A stronger planning monopoly is the opposite of that shift."},{"t":"hh","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS the controversy over Apollo's bid?","o":["Apollo, an investment company new to Helsinki, made the highest bid","The other bids were surprisingly close, and one member suspected a cartel","The Real Estate Department backed Apollo because it offered the most money","The Deputy Mayor proposed selling to Apollo, against the department's advice"],"a":2,"e":"The Real Estate Department wanted to reject Apollo and sell to the household developer Skanska, despite Skanska's lower bid (p. 141). The other statements match the text."},{"t":"savini","type":"quote","q":"Savini & Aalbers quote Harvey (1982): 'the more open the land market is, the more recklessly can surplus money capital build pyramids of debt claims'. What do they use this to support?","o":["Land is used as fictitious capital and leverage for credit","Municipalities should open their land markets to more investors","Land prices fall when land markets are opened to finance","Debt-financed projects are safer than equity-based ones"],"a":0,"e":"The quote supports their point that land is increasingly used as collateral, leveraged to create credit that can be put to use elsewhere (p. 6). Opening land markets is precisely what Harvey warns against, so the second option inverts the quote."},{"t":"savini","type":"quote","q":"After the 2009 bankruptcy procedure, the authors write that 'these dynamics were completely alien to the land use planning taking place locally'. Which dynamics?","o":["Residents' legal action against the regional health campus","The European programmes that funded the site's early reconversion","Sesto's negotiations with the Deputy Mayor about local jobs","The restructuring of Risanamento's holding and its creditors"],"a":3,"e":"The reorganisation and restructuring of the holding, including recapitalisation by creditors and forced sales, directly changed the ownership of the Falck site, yet had nothing to do with local planning (p. 12). Negotiations about jobs belong to the earlier, locally embedded phase."},{"t":"savini","type":"position","q":"Where do Savini & Aalbers sit on the structure ↔ agency dimension, according to the course material?","o":["Agency: the Deputy Mayor's personal choices drive outcomes","Structure, fully determinist: finance makes gridlock certain","Structure-leaning, but public choices still shape outcomes","Agency: each investor freely picks the best use for the land"],"a":2,"e":"Financial structures push, but the authors reject inevitability and name the public authorities' regulative versus proactive role as a key variable, which the course reads as more balanced than Harvey. The determinist option contradicts their explicit statement that gridlock is not inevitable (p. 15)."},{"t":"savini","type":"consequence","q":"Under financialised conditions, what do Savini & Aalbers expect if a city makes excessive public demands on a project?","o":["Investors simply accept them because land prices rise","The financial viability of the project is jeopardised","The region steps in and pays for the demanded facilities","The SPV becomes a public company owned by the city"],"a":1,"e":"The local government faces a trade-off: it wants to capture as much value as possible, but excessive public demands risk jeopardising the project's financial sustainability, so cities tend to rely on strong regulation (p. 15). The regional campus in Falck boosted negotiations but is not presented as a general consequence."},{"t":"savini","type":"contrast","q":"How do Savini & Aalbers' conclusions differ from Weber's (2010) view of local governments and financial markets?","o":["Weber: cities shape and are shaped by finance; Sesto was shaped without shaping it","Weber: cities are passive recipients of finance; Sesto actively steered its own agenda","Weber: finance dominates every city alike; Sesto shows cities can resist it with ease","Weber: only regions can steer finance; Sesto shows that municipalities can do so too"],"a":0,"e":"Weber argues that local governments may be 'shaping and being shaped by financial markets'; the Falck case shows a local government 'shaped and constrained by finance without being able to give shape to financial markets' (p. 15). The second option reverses Weber, who explicitly rejects seeing cities as passive recipients."},{"t":"savini","type":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: a fiscally stressed town on a metropolitan edge, with a quarter of its area vacant industrial land, courts a large investor and relaxes its plan in the hope that public benefits will trickle down. What do Savini & Aalbers predict?","o":["A strong position, as it holds attractive undeveloped land","A stable land value, kept up by competition between towns","Dependence on incoming capital and profit-oriented planning","Quick agreement, as the investors want a fast and certain plan"],"a":2,"e":"Fiscal stress and concentrated social problems can make local governments too dependent on incoming capital, inducing profit-oriented planning that hopes to trickle down public benefits (p. 4). Attractive undeveloped land normally gives a strong negotiating position, but the authors say fiscal stress undercuts it."},{"t":"bossuyt","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'All five patterns of protection operate inside the housing development itself.' What is wrong?","o":["There are three patterns, all of them located in the state","The patterns sit at three levels, including movement and state","Only regularization protects; the others are forms of enclosure","The patterns operate in the market rather than in the building"],"a":1,"e":"Regularization and restoration operate at the development; integration and ideation between development and movement; federation at the movement-state interface. None of the patterns is a form of enclosure."},{"t":"bossuyt","type":"contrast","q":"Ostrom and Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano both study how commons last. How do they differ?","o":["Ostrom studies housing in cities; the authors study forests and fisheries","Ostrom rejects any rules for commons; the authors stress rules and boundaries","Ostrom studies rules for natural resources; the authors add political claims","Ostrom wants the state to own commons; the authors want the market to own them"],"a":2,"e":"Ostrom's work studied stability of common-pool resources such as fisheries through self-made rules; the authors argue housing commons also need attention to political claims and enclosure by capital and state. Ostrom does not reject rules; rules are central to her work."},{"t":"bossuyt","type":"apply","q":"A Dutch housing cooperative adapts its statutes to the rules of a national loan fund and accepts a municipal ground lease, to secure its future. Which concept from the article fits best?","o":["Ideation, because ideas from the cooperative later appear in other projects","Commodification, because members sell their homes for private profit","Marginalization, because the cooperative closes itself to outsiders","Structural coupling, aligning a commons with state systems to stabilise it"],"a":3,"e":"Structural coupling (de Angelis) is strategic alignment with legal and financial state systems to stabilise claims and safeguard autonomy. Nothing in the case involves sale or exclusion, so the enclosure options do not fit."},{"t":"bossuyt","type":"quote","q":"The authors conclude: 'Social rights help to create and reclaim housing as commons, but may not be enough to sustain them.' What does this point to?","o":["The mediating role of private law, and the need for collective property forms","The failure of all movements, so housing should return to the free market","The irrelevance of law, since only collective labour sustains a commons","The success of the City Statute, which already secures collective titles for residents"],"a":0,"e":"Against work stressing public law (the City Statute), the authors highlight how private law shapes outcomes, and they call for legal reform enabling collective property. The City Statute does not provide collective titles; that is precisely the gap."},{"t":"bossuyt","type":"consequence","q":"Regularization turned collective claims into individual titles and mortgages. What follows for collective action, according to the authors?","o":["It erodes, as households become indebted subjects less tied to the movement","It grows, since secure owners have more time and money for the movement","It stays the same, because the movements can enforce their own ban on resale and subletting","It moves to the state, which now organises residents' committees itself"],"a":0,"e":"Individual titles and mortgages turn households into 'indebted subjects' and convert collective political claims into personal debt relations, so collective action erodes. Movements ban sale and subletting but cannot enforce it, as a unit on a subletting platform showed."},{"t":"bossuyt","type":"position","q":"Where does the protection of housing commons sit on the deliberate-emergent dimension?","o":["Purely deliberate: planners design every pattern of protection in advance","Purely emergent: protection arises by chance without any organisation","Mixed: points systems and federations are deliberate, ideation is emergent","Neither: protection is fixed entirely by law and involves no human choices at all"],"a":2,"e":"Protection is partly deliberate (points systems, committees, federations) and partly emergent (ideation, ideas maturing until an opportune political moment). Planners are not the designers here; movements and residents are."},{"t":"gurran","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'Gurran and Ruming treat discourse as a rational exchange of arguments in which the best evidence wins.' What is the misreading?","o":["They treat discourse as the messy medium of power conflicts between groups","They treat discourse as irrelevant, since only material interests count","They treat discourse as a set of state rules that bind all the actors","They treat discourse as private belief, with no effect on legislation"],"a":0,"e":"The authors write that discourse 'is not simply the product of rational and deliberative processes, but the messy and complex medium of power conflicts'. They do not dismiss discourse: it is their whole method."},{"t":"gurran","type":"contrast","q":"Hayek and the NSW development industry both oppose heavy-handed planning. What separates Hayek's case from the industry discourse as Gurran and Ruming present it?","o":["Hayek wants all infrastructure privatised; the industry wants developers to pay for it","Hayek argues from knowledge and freedom; the industry frames rules as costs to its growth","Hayek defends local resident control; the industry defends strong ministerial planning","Hayek supports inclusionary zoning; the industry rejects any affordable housing levy"],"a":1,"e":"Hayek's argument is principled: central planning cannot use dispersed knowledge and threatens freedom. The industry discourse frames regulation as red tape that costs growth, and is selectively anti-intervention: it asked for infrastructure from general taxation, not developer charges."},{"t":"gurran","type":"apply","q":"A Dutch advisory group (STOER) proposes scrapping 'contradictory and superfluous' rules so that homes can be built faster and more affordably. What would Gurran and Ruming ask first?","o":["Whether selective incentives exist to get builders to cooperate on the rules","Whether residents have been made responsible for monitoring building output","Whether affordability is the aim or a justification, and what happens to social rent","Whether the rules were set at a critical juncture that now locks in their form"],"a":2,"e":"Their NSW study shows affordability used as a 'semantic justification' for deregulation while direct affordable housing measures were cut, so they would ask whether this package actually supports low-income renters. The critical-juncture question is historical institutionalist."},{"t":"gurran","type":"quote","q":"The authors write that it 'would be an over-simplification to suggest that ideology or even particular economic interests, are the only drivers of planning and policy reform processes.' What do they mean?","o":["Planners' professional expertise, not politics, finally decided the reform's content","Contingent politics such as resident backlash and a minority in parliament also mattered","The Bill's content was decided by the courts, which overruled ideology and interests","Global markets, not local actors, set Australia's planning reform agenda in the end"],"a":1,"e":"Industry discourse dominated the formal process, yet the Bill stalled in a parliament where the government lacked a majority, after a backlash by more than 300 resident groups. Planners' expertise played no deciding role in their account."},{"t":"gurran","type":"consequence","q":"If housing affordability is only a 'semantic justification' in a planning reform, what would you expect to find in the final legislation?","o":["New direct measures for low-income renters next to modest deregulation","Deregulation for development while direct affordable housing tools weaken","Stronger social and environmental objects alongside faster approvals","A national rent cap, financed by levies on value uplift from rezoning"],"a":1,"e":"That is what happened in NSW: rezoning was eased and 'onerous' controls banned, while affordable housing contribution provisions were wound back. The first option describes what a genuine affordability agenda would do."},{"t":"gurran","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the rhetorical strategies that Gurran and Ruming identify in the NSW reform?","o":["Unevidenced cost figures such as '40%'","Relegation through 'while' and 'having regard to'","Affordability used as a semantic justification","Independent cost-benefit studies of each rule"],"a":3,"e":"The point is the absence of such evidence: 'no evidence of actual costs or savings is ever provided'. Unevidenced figures, relegating wording and affordability as justification are the strategies the authors name."},{"t":"pruijt","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'In Amsterdam, legalization in the early 1980s marked the end of the squatting cycle.' Why does this misread Pruijt?","o":["Legalization never happened in Amsterdam, only mass evictions by police","Squatting went on for two decades, so legalization did not end the cycle","Legalization ended squatting, but only after the 1980 coronation riots","The cycle ended because housing associations took over the movement"],"a":1,"e":"Pruijt stresses that legalization was not an end-of-cycle event: squatting continued two decades later, with disruption alongside convention (flexible institutionalization). The housing associations did not take over the movement."},{"t":"pruijt","type":"contrast","q":"Pruijt and Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano both use 'cooptation'. How do their meanings differ?","o":["Pruijt: a group's goals are adopted while problems are redefined; Bossuyt: the state undermines self-governance","Pruijt: the state undermines a commons' self-governance; Bossuyt: a movement adopts the state's goals","Pruijt: residents sell their homes for profit; Bossuyt: the state buys out the occupiers' buildings","Pruijt: cooptation is always good for movements; Bossuyt: cooptation always strengthens a commons"],"a":0,"e":"For Pruijt (after Selznick), the co-opting body embraces some movement ideas while redefining problems so solving them does not threaten its stability. For Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano, cooptation is a form of enclosure in which the state undermines self-governance."},{"t":"pruijt","type":"apply","q":"A country's highest court rules that owners must go to court before removing occupiers from an unused building. Which element of Pruijt's analysis does this change?","o":["The urban regime, which shifts from market-oriented to redistributive","The movement's identity, which turns from housing to squatting proper","The protection of property, which enables open and long-lived squats","The building stock, which improves in technical condition after renewal"],"a":2,"e":"Like the 1971 Dutch house-right ruling, legal protection for squatters changes the property-protection part of the opportunity structure, making long-lived, open squats possible. It does not change the city's regime or the movement's identity."},{"t":"pruijt","type":"quote","q":"Pruijt writes that 'a pre-existing oppositional identity can to some extent override the opportunity structure.' What does this sentence claim?","o":["Structure is irrelevant, since identity alone decides whether movements succeed","Identity is fixed by the opportunity structure, so local context explains all","Agency partly counters structure: imported identity let a movement emerge anyway","Opportunity structures are created by movements, so they can always be overridden"],"a":2,"e":"European activists brought a squatters' identity to New York, so a squatters' movement arose against an unfavourable structure. 'To some extent' matters: Pruijt explicitly does not claim the opportunity structure becomes irrelevant."},{"t":"pruijt","type":"consequence","q":"If Pruijt is right, what should be expected when a city moves from redistributive to market-oriented housing policy?","o":["More room for open squatting, because the state withdraws from housing","Fewer chances for squatting and more pull to co-opt groups as service providers","No change, since squatting depends only on the movement's own identity","An end to all conflict, since non-profits take over low-income housing"],"a":1,"e":"His paradoxical finding is that a market-oriented regime offers fewer opportunities for squatting, and post-Fordist logic pulls groups into service roles; the one leeway is the disorganised low-income renovation sector. Identity matters, but not to the exclusion of the regime."},{"t":"pruijt","type":"position","q":"Where does Pruijt's account sit on the reproduction-transformation dimension?","o":["Pure transformation: squatting always overturns the city's housing market","Pure reproduction: every movement ends up co-opted by the retreating state","Mixed: cooptation reproduces the order, but squatting proper keeps an oppositional edge","Neither: squatting is a private housing choice without any political meaning"],"a":2,"e":"Cooptation turns groups into service providers for a retreating state (reproduction), while a squatters' movement proper keeps an oppositional identity; yet Pruijt concedes conflict stays local, leaving big real estate and politics untouched, so transformation is limited."},{"t":"raco","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'Raco and Imrie's interviews with SRB residents prove that people internalised the new responsibilities.' What is the misreading?","o":["They found residents resisted, so the agenda failed in practice on the ground","They analysed documents and secondary evaluations, not lived experience","They interviewed only national ministers and not the residents of the areas","They used a survey of all English councils, not interviews with any residents"],"a":1,"e":"The evidence is a critical reading of policy documents plus secondary evaluations such as Foley et al.; there is no primary fieldwork. The article shows how texts construct subjects, not how people experienced or resisted them."},{"t":"raco","type":"contrast","q":"Harvey (1989) and Raco and Imrie both discuss public–private partnerships in urban policy. What separates their explanations?","o":["Harvey sees partnerships as freely chosen by residents; Raco and Imrie as forced on them by capital","Harvey links partnerships to inter-urban competition; Raco and Imrie to governing through subjects","Harvey sees partnerships as a form of welfare; Raco and Imrie as a return to direct state provision","Harvey studies only national policy; Raco and Imrie only the private sector partners in the schemes"],"a":1,"e":"For Harvey, partnerships express urban entrepreneurialism under inter-urban competition for mobile capital; for Raco and Imrie, they are technologies of an advanced liberal art of government. The capital-forcing option misplaces Harvey's structural argument onto Raco and Imrie."},{"t":"raco","type":"apply","q":"A new regional agency is announced as 'devolution', but it may only fund projects that meet ministry criteria fixed in its contract. How would Raco and Imrie read it?","o":["As genuine devolution that moves real powers away from the national ministry","As a growth coalition in which regional business elites capture the agency","As governing from a distance: the agency acts as a central sub-contractor","As a market solution in which the agency allocates funds by competitive price"],"a":2,"e":"This mirrors their reading of the RDAs: ostensibly regional, but bound by contractual and financial rules to central directives, 'sub-contractor to central government'. The devolution reading is the official rhetoric that the article unmasks."},{"t":"raco","type":"quote","q":"Raco and Imrie write that 'Responsibility, therefore, becomes a discourse of control'. What do they mean?","o":["Talk of shared responsibility shapes conduct and ties individuals to communities","Responsible councils are given legal control over their communities' spending decisions","Ministers take responsibility for failures, so control returns to the national state","Residents who act responsibly gain control over the partnership's budget and targets"],"a":0,"e":"Blair-style talk of social responsibility as 'a value shared' works as a technology: it shapes 'the conduct of conduct' and links individual action to community. It does not give anyone formal control over budgets, which stay tied to central targets."},{"t":"raco","type":"position","q":"Where does Raco and Imrie's account sit on the structure–agency dimension?","o":["Agency-centred: communities freely choose to take on new duties","Fully structural: class forces determine every policy outcome","Structure-leaning: agency is real but produced and channelled","Undecided: they argue the dimension does not apply to policy"],"a":2,"e":"Active citizens do act, but their subjectivity is formed through rationalities, programmes and technologies of government. It is not fully structural in a Marxist sense: power is dispersed and the conclusion leaves room for resistance."},{"t":"raco","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT presented in the article as a technology of government in SRB?","o":["Quarterly quantitative progress reports","Best Value disciplines for local councils","Peer review through Best Practice guides","Local electoral control of SRB decisions"],"a":3,"e":"The authors note that the documents 'do little to identify' institutionalised local accountability or 'direct local electoral control' over SRB. Reporting, Best Value and peer review are all analysed as technologies of government."},{"t":"rydin6","type":"misread","q":"A student writes: 'For Stone, an urban regime is the formal majority coalition in the city council after an election.' What is the misreading?","o":["A regime is formal but includes the opposition parties too","A regime is informal, crosses sectors and works alongside formal politics","A regime is set up by national law rather than by a local election","A regime consists only of business leaders and excludes all elected politicians"],"a":1,"e":"Stone's regime is an informal yet relatively stable group with access to institutional resources, crossing state, market and civil society and operating alongside formal politics. It is not a council coalition and it does include political leaders."},{"t":"rydin6","type":"contrast","q":"Molotch's growth coalition (in Rydin) and Harvey's urban entrepreneurialism both describe pro-growth alliances. What separates them?","o":["Molotch treats the alliance as structurally forced by capital; Harvey treats it as a free choice","Molotch studies national states; Harvey only studies neighbourhood associations and residents","Molotch focuses on agents with land stakes; Harvey ties the alliance to inter-urban competition","Molotch's alliances are always fragmented; Harvey's are formally contracted public bodies"],"a":2,"e":"The growth coalition is agent-centred: actors with most to gain from land and property coalesce with local government. Harvey subordinates such partnerships to structural inter-urban competition, which is why political economists found growth coalitions too agent-based."},{"t":"rydin6","type":"apply","q":"Provincial planners invite only well-organised residents' associations into a consultation network and call everyone else 'hard to reach'. Which problem from Rydin's chapter does this raise?","o":["Who represents 'the community', as non-organised groups are seen as recalcitrant","Why large groups fail to organise, since each resident prefers to free-ride on others","How the regime's 'power to' depends on the resources of business partners","How the procedures of the network were exercised unevenly across districts"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin asks who counts as the community in governance networks: favouring self-organised groups labels others as 'hard to reach' or 'silent'. Free-riding is Olson's explanation, not the problem Rydin raises."},{"t":"rydin6","type":"quote","q":"Rydin writes that for urban social movement theorists 'politics was not reduced to being a handmaiden of these socioeconomic structures.' What idea does this express?","o":["Politics has some autonomy, so change can come short of crisis and revolution","Politics is fully independent of capitalism, so structure plays no role at all","Politics is decided by elites, so movements are a mere reflection of their aims","Politics is driven by individual choice, so structural analysis is unnecessary"],"a":0,"e":"Inspired by Gramsci, these theorists kept capitalist dynamics in view but gave politics a degree of autonomy, allowing incremental progressive change. The first option overstates it: endemic conflict shaped by capitalism remained part of the analysis."},{"t":"rydin6","type":"position","q":"Where does Rydin place the researcher in urban politics research?","o":["As a detached analyst who avoids taking sides in order to stay neutral and objective","As a policy adviser who measures which instruments raise housing output","As a mediator who helps conflicting parties reach a durable consensus","As engaged, taking the emic view of the marginalised, often in action research"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin concludes the perspective is normative: researchers adopt the emic perspective of disempowered groups and often move from detached analysis to supporting them. Consensus-building is the governance frame's aim, which urban politics questions."},{"t":"rydin6","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of Holman's reasons why urban regime analysis travels poorly as a comparative framework?","o":["A growing diversity of actors in urban politics","Cross-national differences in local autonomy","Differences in incentives for business to engage","The absence of social movements outside the US"],"a":3,"e":"Holman's four factors are actor diversity, differences in local autonomy, the institutional configuration of the economy (business incentives) and globalisation. Social movements exist widely; Rydin's examples include Istanbul and Sydney."},{"t":"alex","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Alexander's view of his own contingency framework?","o":["It is developed only at the highest level of abstraction, as a point of departure","At lower levels it cannot be complete, and his examples are partial illustrations","It may offer the only hope, perhaps no more than a hope, of an adaptable paradigm","It solves the problem of contingency models by deriving its dimensions logically"],"a":3,"e":"Alexander admits the intrinsic problem remains: the selection of contingencies is 'essentially arbitrary', and any attempt to justify it starts an infinite regress; he relies on Habermas's partition and an inductive approach, not logical derivation. The other three paraphrase his own modest conclusions."},{"t":"alex","type":"contrast","q":"Alexander and Davoudi (week 3) both doubt that rational analysis alone can guide planning. How do their remedies differ?","o":["Alexander wants better evidence; Davoudi wants planners to rely on intuition","Alexander adds models of interaction; Davoudi seeks evidence-informed policy","Alexander drops rationality; Davoudi wants to restore the instrumental model","Alexander adopts TCA as the new paradigm; Davoudi adopts the tactical model"],"a":1,"e":"Alexander keeps rationality for deliberation and combines it with models of interaction in a contingency framework; Davoudi rejects the linear, instrumental view of evidence and argues for an enlightenment model in which evidence competes with ideology, interests, institutions and information. Davoudi criticises tactical use of evidence rather than adopting it."},{"t":"alex","type":"apply","q":"A housing association's board runs a year of sessions in which its departments argue and trade off claims to set the association's priorities for the next decade. Where does Alexander's framework put this?","o":["Real, collective, accomplishment-oriented: strategic planning as interaction","Ideal, individual, accomplishment-oriented: formal optimisation of objectives","Real, collective, consensus-oriented: neutral mediation for mutual understanding","Ideal, interactive, consensus-oriented: Habermas's undistorted communication"],"a":0,"e":"Alexander places strategic planning, 'a process through which organizations determine their aims and priorities' (Bryson), in cell (b) as an interactive exercise in substantive rationality. Departments pressing their own claims are not neutral participants, so this is not cell (c)."},{"t":"alex","type":"quote","q":"Alexander writes that certain alternatives to rationality, if universalised, 'require an improbable act of faith in their underlying ideological assumptions'. Which alternatives is he judging?","o":["Formal optimisation models such as linear programming and decision analysis","Habermas's communicative action and its principle of universalization","Substantive paradigms such as neo-Marxism, systems theory and public choice","The ritual use of the rational model in planning education and practice"],"a":2,"e":"The phrase closes his review of substantive and other proposed paradigms (neo-Marxists, systems and ecology approaches, Poulton's public choice, Friedmann, Sillince). He treats critical theory separately and says it has many, though not all, of the attributes of a paradigm."},{"t":"alex","type":"position","q":"Where does Alexander stand on whether one model of decision-making should govern planning?","o":["One rational model, refined by bounded rationality, should govern all choices","Communicative action should replace rationality as the single paradigm","He rejects all general models and relies on case-by-case practical judgement","Several models, each valid in its domain, within one overarching framework"],"a":3,"e":"Alexander says 'one paradigm cannot supplant another' and gives rationality, strategic and communicative models their own domains. Yet he does not retreat to case-by-case judgement: at its highest level his framework 'aspires to be comprehensive and universal'."},{"t":"alex","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT a limitation Alexander himself admits about his framework?","o":["The choice of contingencies that partition the universe is essentially arbitrary","Its cells only apply to Western planning systems with strong local government","Its lower levels of abstraction are incomplete and still have to be developed","His examples for the cells are partial illustrations, not a full inventory"],"a":1,"e":"Alexander concedes arbitrariness, incompleteness at lower levels and partial examples. He never limits the framework to Western systems; on the contrary, at its highest level it aspires to be comprehensive and universal."},{"t":"rc","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Coase's theorem as the lecture uses it?","o":["It starts from externalities: one actor's actions affect others","It holds only if rights are clear and transaction costs are zero","It shows bargaining always beats regulation in real-world cities","It inspires tools that clarify rights and lower transaction costs"],"a":2,"e":"The lecture stresses that transaction costs are never zero (information, bargaining, enforcement, holdouts, many parties), so Coase does not show that bargaining always wins in practice; it inspires interventions that clarify rights and cut transaction costs. The other three statements match the slides."},{"t":"rc","type":"contrast","q":"Hayek (week 2) and rational choice theory both doubt that the state can simply plan well. How do their reasons differ?","o":["Hayek: the state lacks the dispersed knowledge it needs; RC: officials act in their own interest","Hayek: officials act in their own interest; RC: the state lacks the dispersed knowledge it needs","Hayek: capital controls the state; RC: planners cannot measure the costs and benefits of plans","Hayek: only experts can plan; RC: democracy cannot set the goals of a comprehensive plan"],"a":0,"e":"Hayek's argument is about knowledge and democracy: no planner can gather dispersed knowledge, and parliaments cannot agree on a complete ranking of ends. Rational choice adds state failure: politicians, planners and municipalities are self-interested actors too. The second option swaps the two."},{"t":"rc","type":"apply","q":"A municipality introduces a levy on the value gain that owners make when farmland is rezoned for housing. What does the lecture's rational choice lens predict?","o":["Owners accept the levy, since the gain was created by public action","Owners lobby against it, downplay the gain or push it onto buyers","Owners stop selling land at all, so the housing plan is abandoned","Owners rely on the council to share the gain fairly among residents"],"a":1,"e":"Landowners act rationally to capture rent: when planning raises values they try to keep the betterment, so a value capture levy invites rent-seeking (as in Taylor's Melbourne case, where owners disputed the gain and had the tax shifted to purchasers). Accepting it because the gain is public in origin is a normative argument, not a rational choice prediction."},{"t":"rc","type":"quote","q":"The lecture quotes Gurran et al. (2018): societies 'manage externalities arising from private developments and land use activities through regulation or taxation'. What does the lecture set against this standard approach?","o":["A full ban on platforms, since only prohibition can be enforced at reasonable cost","Neighbourhood-led cultural campaigns that change residents' norms about hosting","Comprehensive plans drawn up by expert planners who optimise social welfare","Coasean tools that clarify rights and lower costs, so that parties can bargain"],"a":3,"e":"After listing the problems of one-size-fits-all regulation, the lecture asks what a rational choice scholar would propose and answers with Coase: better define rights and reduce transaction costs (tradable permits, VvE negotiation, hosting taxes into a fund, platform monitoring). Comprehensive expert plans belong to the rational-comprehensive model, not to rational choice."},{"t":"rc","type":"position","q":"Where does rational choice theory, as the lecture presents it, sit on the universal versus situated knowledge dimension?","o":["Situated: each city's rules reflect its own culture, so findings do not travel","Neither: it deals with values only and makes no claims about knowledge","Universal by default, though the lecture warns authors apply it inconsistently","Situated: it trusts local practical knowledge (metis) above formal models"],"a":2,"e":"The core assumptions say knowledge is generally approached from a positivist stance: assume universal rational behaviour and aim at generalisable explanations. The slide adds that assumptions differ per author and are not always used consistently, so read each paper critically. Metis is Scott's term."},{"t":"rc","type":"consequence","q":"According to the lecture, what follows for planners from the fact that transaction costs are never zero?","o":["Coase becomes irrelevant, so uniform restrictions are the only option left","Planners should design tools that clarify rights and lower bargaining costs","Planners should wait until the market itself removes all transaction costs","The initial allocation of rights stops mattering for who gains or who loses"],"a":1,"e":"The slide says that even if transaction costs are not zero, 'we can still design Coasean-inspired interventions' that clarify rights and enable negotiation, rather than only restrict. With positive transaction costs the initial allocation of rights matters more, not less, so the last option reverses the logic."},{"t":"p1","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Næss (2001)?","o":["Greener new buildings are only relatively better as long as the total building stock keeps on growing.","The right planning procedure depends on whether local lifestyles fit the sustainability goals or not.","The compact city is a sufficient answer, because densification makes extra floor space harmless.","Scenarios contrasting a trend future with an environmental one make long-term goals debatable."],"a":2,"e":"Næss supports densification but insists it is not enough: as long as floor space per person keeps growing, efficiency gains are cancelled out, so per-capita growth of the stock must stop. The other three are accurate (relative gains, goal-dependent procedures, the NAMIT scenarios)."},{"t":"p1","type":"contrast","q":"The L1 slides put Oslo's 'most sustainable city' ranking next to Norway's oil fund. Which reading of this pairing fits WEAK sustainability?","o":["Turning oil (natural capital) into financial capital is acceptable if the total capital stock is kept up.","Natural capital cannot be swapped for money, so the oil extraction breaches a planetary boundary.","Oslo shows sufficiency: the city has chosen to seek enough when more consumption is possible.","Oslo shows consistency: oil revenues remodel the industrial metabolism after natural cycles."],"a":0,"e":"Weak sustainability (neo-classical, Solow and Hartwick) assumes capitals are substitutable, so converting oil into a financial fund can count as 'sustainable'. The second option is the strong-sustainability reading, which treats natural capital as complementary and non-substitutable."},{"t":"p1","type":"apply","q":"A municipality replaces its street lights with LEDs; because running costs fall, it then lights many more cycle paths and squares all night. Which lecture 1 idea does this illustrate?","o":["Sufficiency: the municipality limits its lighting to what residents truly need at night.","Consistency: lighting is redesigned to mimic natural cycles of the urban metabolism.","Strong sustainability: technology cannot replace the natural capital lost to lighting.","The rebound effect: efficiency savings are spent on more use, so total energy may not fall."],"a":3,"e":"The 'efficiency paradox' slide shows direct rebound: cheaper use per unit leads to more use, which is why the lecture adds sufficiency to efficiency. Sufficiency would mean self-limiting the lighting, the opposite of what happens here."},{"t":"p1","type":"quote","q":"Næss writes: 'It has become politically impossible not to be a supporter of a sustainable development, so there is a clear danger that the concept will be watered out.' What is his point?","o":["Because consensus on sustainability exists, communicative planning can now deliver it locally.","Everyone stretches the concept to cover their own concerns, so it risks losing its radical content.","Sustainability has become a technical matter that experts should settle outside of politics.","Opposition to sustainability has vanished, so the remaining barrier is a lack of public money."],"a":1,"e":"Næss reads Brundtland as a counter-discourse that a dominant growth discourse can de-radicalise by stretching it, so he sticks to a narrow, Brundtland-based reading. He does not conclude that consensus makes communicative planning work; he criticises consensus-seeking later in the paper."},{"t":"p1","type":"position","q":"In a note, Næss criticises Campbell's (1996) planner's triangle. What is his objection?","o":["It ignores nature and treats the environment as a mere backdrop to social and economic conflict.","It puts sustainability at the centre, whereas Næss sees sustainability as unreachable in principle.","It relies too much on alliances, whereas Næss prefers a consensus that includes all stakeholders.","It puts economic growth on a par with equity and environment; Brundtland saw growth only as a means."],"a":3,"e":"Næss notes that Campbell's triangle places economic growth as one pole of sustainability, while Brundtland treated growth only as a possible means, and only if its content changes. The alliance option reverses Næss's own position: he rejects all-stakeholder consensus in favour of alliances."},{"t":"p1","type":"consequence","q":"According to Næss, what happens when planners step back from substantive goals and expert knowledge?","o":["Residents' everyday knowledge fills the gap and steers land use towards sustainable outcomes.","Experts of banks, developers and road builders fill the gap, so expertise serves those who can pay.","Market prices fill the gap and signal environmental limits better than any plan could do.","Higher-level authorities fill the gap and impose national goals on every local plan instead."],"a":1,"e":"Næss argues the vacuum is not filled by residents' lay knowledge but by the experts of powerful interests, which is why he wants expert and lay knowledge combined and goal-oriented planning. The residents option is the communicative-planning hope he rejects."},{"t":"p2","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Angelo and Wachsmuth (2020)?","o":["They want the 'city as solution' view scrutinised rather than accepted at face value.","They set out to prove that cities emit less per person than suburbs and rural areas.","Each problem first received a non-city solution and only later a city solution.","They note that environmentalists also turned to the city, not only urbanists to nature."],"a":1,"e":"The authors explicitly do not try to prove or disprove that cities are sustainable; their aim is historical and critical, explaining how the 'city as solution' imaginary arose. The other options are accurate summaries of their argument and their own admission that the story is two-sided."},{"t":"p2","type":"contrast","q":"Which argument belongs to the OPTIMISTIC view of cities on the lecture 2 slides?","o":["Planning geared to growth corridors and inter-city competition locks in unsustainability.","Climate gentrification shows that injustice persists even in cities that adapt to climate change.","An implementation gap separates ambitious city climate plans from results on the ground.","Mayors and city networks such as C40 can bypass blockages in national climate policy."],"a":3,"e":"The optimistic view lists political and organisational arguments: mayors and networks like C40 bypassing national blockages, alongside agglomeration and cheap emission cuts. Growth-geared planning, climate gentrification (Miami) and the implementation gap are on the pessimistic side."},{"t":"p2","type":"apply","q":"An architecture biennale celebrates a dense Mumbai settlement for its walkability, recycling and self-built incremental homes, as lessons for Northern eco-districts. Where does this fit in Angelo and Wachsmuth's account?","o":["The informal-settlement root: a former environmental problem recast as a model.","The sprawl root: density promoted against decades of car-based suburbs.","The climate root: mayors cast as protagonists after treaties stalled.","The resilience wave: coastal cities investing after extreme weather."],"a":0,"e":"The informal-settlement root runs from slums as environmental problems (Habitat I, The Challenge of Slums) to Brand's 'How slums can save the planet', Dharavi and Aravena: the same features become models. The sprawl root also praises density, but it concerns Northern suburbs, not informal settlements."},{"t":"p2","type":"consequence","q":"Why, according to Angelo and Wachsmuth, did climate change offer no obvious 'less city' solution?","o":["Climate change was framed as a Southern slum problem, so Northern cities were never seen as part of it.","Retreating from affluent coastal cities meant an unthinkable loss of assets, and the urban-age idea was set.","Treaties such as Kyoto had already cut emissions, so only adaptation was left for cities to handle.","Anti-urban movements had won the policy debate, so cities first had to prove their value to states."],"a":1,"e":"Sprawl and slums could first be answered with 'less city', but leaving affluent coastal cities was unthinkable and the 'urban age' ideology was already established, so anti-urban visions remained 'paths not taken'. Treaties had not succeeded: their failure is why cities were cast as climate protagonists."},{"t":"p2","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT one of the measurement problems of urban sustainability discussed in lecture 2?","o":["Definitions of urban sustainability are overly broad and vary between authors.","There is no standard classification of sustainable development indicators.","Cities withhold their emission data because it would harm their international ranking.","Consistently measured CO2 estimates exist for only about 80 cities worldwide."],"a":2,"e":"Tanguay et al. name broad definitions, no standard classification and data constraints; the World Bank adds that only about 80 cities have consistent CO2 estimates, mostly modelled rather than measured. Deliberate withholding of data by cities is not among the reasons given."},{"t":"p2","type":"position","q":"In their political research agenda, what shift in environmental thinking do Angelo and Wachsmuth observe?","o":["From urban growth limits towards a degrowth consensus in which cities cap development.","From Northern planning models towards a Southern-led agenda in which informality sets norms.","From the 'limits to growth' tradition to 'sustainability as the sustainability of growth'.","From mayor-led networks towards technocratic treaties that took cities out of climate policy."],"a":2,"e":"The authors note a move away from the anti-capitalist 'limits to growth' tradition towards sustainability as the sustainability of growth, with planning focused on wealthy residents' liveability. The degrowth option inverts their diagnosis: they find mainstream urban sustainability 'system affirmative'."},{"t":"p3","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Campbell (1996)?","o":["Nature is socially constructed for Campbell, in the sense that groups conceive and use it differently.","A holistic master plan balancing all three goals can reach sustainable development directly.","Campbell warns that steady-state, no-growth economics could keep the poor in poverty.","Planners must choose to act as mediator or as advocate, and Campbell says both roles are needed."],"a":1,"e":"Campbell says the centre cannot be reached 'in a single, holistic leap to a pre-ordained balance' and warns that Sustainable Development Master Plans risk becoming glib wish lists. The other three statements are accurate, including his social but non-relativist view of nature."},{"t":"p3","type":"contrast","q":"Campbell notes that industry must pay a subsistence wage so labour can reproduce itself, and must leave enough forest for it to regrow. What parallel is he drawing?","o":["The development conflict is unique, since only there do the poor and nature share an interest.","Sustained yield shows that markets solve the resource conflict without any public regulation.","Property and resource conflicts share a structure: business resists intervention yet needs it.","Wages and forests are both natural capital, so Campbell adopts weak sustainability's view."],"a":2,"e":"Campbell shows that in both the property conflict (labour) and the resource conflict (nature) the private sector resists regulation but depends on it for its own reproduction, and how much is 'enough' is contested. Sustained yield does not mean markets need no regulation; regulation is precisely what conserves the resource."},{"t":"p3","type":"consequence","q":"Barcelona's 18 new parks were followed by gentrification in the old town but not in isolated peripheral districts. What lesson does lecture 3 draw?","o":["Peripheral parks were too small to attract investment, so park size is the decisive factor.","Green gentrification is a North American pattern that rarely appears in European cities.","Greening always displaces residents in the end; lag times only postpone the outcome.","Green gentrification depends on location and market pressure, so it is a planning choice."],"a":3,"e":"Anguelovski et al. (2018) found that outcomes depended on location and pre-existing market pressure, 'which is precisely why it remains a planning choice rather than an inevitability'. The lecture says the phenomenon is more prevalent in North America but still evident in Europe, so the second option overstates."},{"t":"p3","type":"apply","q":"A minister argues that only a richer economy can afford nature protection, so growth must come first. How would Campbell label this, and when would he accept it?","o":["As bioregionalism, acceptable only if the economy is rescaled to its ecological region.","As trickle-down environmentalism, acceptable only if the growth is fairly distributed.","As sustained yield, acceptable only if forests regrow as fast as they are harvested.","As ecocentrism, acceptable only if nature is valued apart from any human use of it."],"a":1,"e":"Campbell calls the logic 'trickle-down environmentalism' (visible in Brundtland's call to revitalise growth) and accepts part of it, but only fairly distributed growth lets the poor protect their environment: the development conflict is solved only with the property conflict."},{"t":"p3","type":"quote","q":"Campbell writes: 'Pretending at all times to be at the center of the planner's triangle will only make sustainability a hollow term.' What follows for planners?","o":["Each planner must locate themselves in the triangle and choose to mediate or to advocate.","Planners should abandon sustainability and serve whichever corner their employer represents.","Planners should avoid conflict and leave all trade-offs in the triangle to elected politicians.","Planners should reach the centre with a holistic Sustainable Development Master Plan."],"a":0,"e":"Because allegiances and bureaucracies constrain planners, Campbell asks each to orient themselves in the triangle and decide whether to be a mediator outside the conflict or an advocate within it; both roles are needed. The master-plan option is exactly what he warns against."},{"t":"p3","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT a limit Campbell names for the paths towards the centre of the triangle?","o":["Negotiation fails in all-or-nothing disputes, such as whether a dam is built or not.","Technological gains per unit are often cancelled out by rising total demand.","Market mechanisms such as pollution credits tend to neglect questions of equity.","Translation fails because planners cannot learn the languages of ecology and justice."],"a":3,"e":"Campbell sees translation between economics, ecology and social justice as a useful planner skill; its limit is that the economic language may dominate and real clashes remain, not that planners cannot learn the languages. The other three limits are named in his procedural and substantive paths."},{"t":"p4","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Witte, Wiegmans and Louw (2025)?","o":["Net port area in Amsterdam and Rotterdam stayed fairly stable, but with large internal dynamics.","The net claims already exceed the available port land, which is why conflicts will intensify.","Vacant port land shrank in both regions, so less slack is left to absorb new land claims.","Rotterdam's coastal extension gives it more room to rearrange uses than confined Amsterdam."],"a":1,"e":"The abstract says the opposite: 'even though the net claims do not yet outnumber the available land, the cumulative effects of these diverging interests do'. The 'puzzling space' option is accurate: Rotterdam can even consider a third coastal extension, while Amsterdam is squeezed by Haven-Stad."},{"t":"p4","type":"contrast","q":"A municipality buys farmland, services it and sells building plots to developers. In Witte's lecture 4 scheme, what is it doing and what is the typical risk?","o":["Acting inside the system with public land instruments; the risk is rigidity and lengthy procedures.","Acting alongside the system through open planning; the risk is tokenism and unequal power.","Acting outside the system as a private stakeholder; the risk is a 'double hat' and financial loss.","Acting inside the system through pre-emption; the risk is that NIMBY groups block the allocation."],"a":2,"e":"Buying, servicing and selling land is active land policy (VINEX to NOVEX): the municipality operates 'outside' as a private stakeholder, which creates the double hat problem (regulator and developer) and only works in rising markets, as the 2008 to 2010 losses showed. Pre-emption is a public instrument used 'inside' the system."},{"t":"p4","type":"apply","q":"A province uses a project decision (projectbesluit) to site a high-voltage station directly, overriding a reluctant municipality. Which Omgevingswet idea from lecture 4 does this reflect?","o":["The primacy of the informal trajectory, with planning law codifying earlier agreements.","Art. 21's care for habitability, which obliges 'everyone' to protect the living environment.","Thorbecke's partial autonomy of lower governments, protected by the principle of equivalence.","Complementary task allocation: tasks go to 'the most suitable level', including direct intervention."],"a":3,"e":"Under the Omgevingswet, the Thorbecke roles remain, but levels may act 'complementary' and province or state can intervene locally through a project decision ('they can make use of this'). Thorbecke's partial autonomy points the other way: it explains why such overriding is sensitive."},{"t":"p4","type":"quote","q":"Witte quotes Ricardo: 'The price of corn is not high because the land rent paid is high, but the high rent is paid because the price of corn is high.' What does this support in lecture 4?","o":["Land value follows its function, so rezoning to a valuable use creates the surplus land policy targets.","High land prices push up house prices, so municipalities should cap land rents to make homes affordable.","Farmland is worth protecting because its rent reflects the long-term value of food production.","Leasehold is better than sale, because annual rents track the changing value of the land use."],"a":0,"e":"'Price follows the product': the use determines the land value, not the other way round. The residual value figure shows that re-zoning creates a surplus (land rent) that active land policy tries to capture. The house-price option reverses Ricardo's causal direction."},{"t":"p4","type":"position","q":"Where does the formal, legal planning process sit in Dutch open planning ('polderen') according to Witte?","o":["At the start: the Omgevingsplan sets a binding frame within which stakeholders may then negotiate.","Towards the end: planning law mostly codifies agreements already reached in the informal trajectory.","In parallel: legal and informal tracks run separately and are only compared at the council vote.","Nowhere: covenants and agreements have replaced the land-use plan in most municipalities."],"a":1,"e":"The slides speak of the 'primacy of the informal trajectory' and a paradox with the heavily legalised system: the formal process comes 'towards the end' as 'codification' of earlier informal agreements. Extra-legal arrangements are 'not illegal, but also not codified in law'; they have not replaced the plan."},{"t":"p4","type":"consequence","q":"In Amsterdam's Westpoort some vacant port plots could not be allocated to firms. What explains this in Witte et al. (2025)?","o":["The coal terminals still held long leaseholds on them, which the port could not end early.","Haarlemmermeer contested the plots, which froze any allocation until a court ruling came.","The port had used up its environmental space: there was no noise capacity left for new users.","The plots were reserved for Haven-Stad housing, so industry was no longer allowed on them."],"a":2,"e":"Noise became a binding constraint: plots could not be allocated for lack of 'noise capacity', and the 2016 annual report warned of insufficient environmental space. Land can be physically empty yet unusable. Haarlemmermeer's dispute concerns the Houtrakpolder expansion site, not existing Westpoort plots."},{"t":"p5","type":"contrast","q":"On Witte's slide, Weiwerd, Oterdum and Heveskes near Delfzijl are listed as examples of which governance approach?","o":["Fight: the village holds its ground and port and village must learn to co-exist.","Flight: the village is moved as a whole to a new site, as happened in Kiruna.","Compensation: the village stays, with buffer zones and liveability investments.","Freeze: the village disappears as port and industrial development take over."],"a":3,"e":"The slide's own table puts these Delfzijl villages under 'Freeze (village disappears)', with Nieuwesluis, Blankenburg, the Antwerp villages and Lützerath. 'Flight (village goes)' holds only Kiruna and Immerath, where the settlement is relocated."},{"t":"p5","type":"apply","q":"After an industrial accident, a government funds noise buffers and liveability projects for a village beside a port, instead of deciding where port and village should go. What does lecture 5 call this?","o":["Compensation planning: offsetting harm to residents rather than settling the land-use conflict.","Strategic planning: a long-term port vision that sets where industry may go in the future.","Pro-active positive zoning: deliberately designating where industrial activity is allowed.","Flight: preparing the residents to leave so that the port can expand onto the village site."],"a":0,"e":"Moerdijk moved 'from strategic planning ('Port Vision 2030') to compensation planning ('Moerdijk regulation')', with investments after the Committee Nijpels advice. Strategic planning is exactly what was left behind: compensation accepts port growth and remedies harm."},{"t":"p5","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS lecture 5 on Moerdijk?","o":["The 2011 Chemie-Pack fire put the co-existence of village and port on the planning agenda.","Kiruna is a 'freeze' case: the town was left to empty slowly while the mine kept growing.","From 2025 the energy transition (PowerPort) reopened the question of the village's future.","Witte asks whether residents' resistance is just NIMBYism or whether more is at stake."],"a":1,"e":"Kiruna is the slide's example of 'flight': the town is moved, with houses transported on trucks. Freeze is the pattern of villages like Weiwerd or Lützerath that disappear. The other statements follow the timeline and the discussion slide."},{"t":"p5","type":"consequence","q":"Why does Witte frame Moerdijk as 'village vs. the greater good'?","o":["Because residents ask for more compensation than the port's annual profits could ever cover.","Because the EU requires villages to give way whenever an energy project serves several countries.","Because port and energy growth serve regional or national interests, while the village bears the costs.","Because the village council refuses to take part in the participation process for the port vision."],"a":2,"e":"Port regionalisation and energy infrastructure are justified by wider interests (jobs, energy supply, competitiveness), while their spatial externalities land on a small community. That is why Witte asks who should decide and what a just outcome is. No EU rule of that kind is mentioned."},{"t":"p6","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS de Satgé and Watson (2018)?","o":["Imported planning concepts tend to 'land' with little preliminary investigation of their fit.","Northern theory assumed strong civil societies, capable institutions and formal economies.","A conflict of rationalities, not a shared public interest, is the realistic starting point.","'Southern' refers to a geographical region: the cities that lie in the global South."],"a":3,"e":"The authors insist that 'southern' refers less to a geographical South than to 'a perspective or orientation'; the southern project is 'a conceptual rather than a physical location'. The other three statements are their claims (pp. 2, 30)."},{"t":"p6","type":"contrast","q":"How does Watson's 'conflicting rationalities' differ from Habermas-inspired communicative planning?","o":["Communicative planning expects dialogue to yield consensus; Watson says planners must work with deep difference.","Communicative planning ignores power entirely; Watson says power only matters where the state is weak.","Communicative planning relies on experts; Watson wants local experts to replace foreign consultants.","Communicative planning is a Southern idea; Watson shows it fails when it is applied in the North."],"a":0,"e":"Watson (2003) challenged the then-dominant theory that the right dialogue can produce consensus: planners 'needed to understand how to accept and work with deep difference and ongoing conflict'. The power option is wrong: for the authors power is pervasive, defining what counts as rational everywhere."},{"t":"p6","type":"apply","q":"A city presents its plan to clear a riverside settlement as a 'purely technical' flood-safety measure, and dismisses residents' objections as uninformed. Which idea from the reading fits best?","o":["Informality as a mode of urbanisation, through which the urban poor secure shelter and income.","Rationality as power: framing knowledge as technical or expert is itself an exercise of power.","Best-practice landing: a Northern model is copied without any check of its local preconditions.","Meso-level theory: concepts are built inductively from a case and tested in other contexts."],"a":1,"e":"Following Foucault and Flyvbjerg, power defines 'what is counted as rationality and so what is counted as reality'; labelling a choice 'technical' shuts out other rationalities. Best-practice landing would need an imported model, which the case does not mention."},{"t":"p6","type":"quote","q":"The authors conclude that state-society engagement in Southern cities 'is shaped more often by a deep conflict of rationalities ... than by some kind of public interest'. What follows for participation?","o":["Participation should be dropped, because informal residents have no shared interests at all.","Participation only works once land titles have formalised the rights of informal dwellers.","Consensus-seeking participation cannot take a shared public interest as its starting point.","Participation should be left to NGOs, since the state is always one unified actor."],"a":2,"e":"The proposition rejects a 'public interest' that 'could provide a starting point for participatory and consensus-seeking processes'; planning must begin from conflict and deep contextual understanding. The 'unified state' option contradicts the finding that the state itself holds many wills to govern."},{"t":"p6","type":"position","q":"Where do de Satgé and Watson place their southern theorising project between universal theory and pure particularism?","o":["Close to universalism: one general theory, with Southern cities as empirical variation.","Pure particularism: every city needs its own theory, so generalising should be avoided.","Anti-theory: concepts from Marx, Foucault or Habermas are rejected as Northern and useless.","In between: meso-level concepts built from cases, tied to context, yet testable elsewhere."],"a":3,"e":"They defend 'meso-level' generalisation: midlevel concepts developed inductively, tied to the contexts that produced them and open to testing elsewhere. Scott and Storper's 'empirical variation' is the universalism they reject; they also prefer critical appropriation of Northern lenses over rejecting them."},{"t":"p6","type":"not","q":"De Satgé and Watson discuss four objections to a 'southern theorising project'. Which is NOT one of them?","o":["Whether Southern cities need formal planning at all, given widespread informality.","Whether it simply creates a new binary between North and South.","Whether theory rooted in place can still generalise beyond one case.","Which conceptual lenses to use, since most come from the global North."],"a":0,"e":"The four issues are a new North/South binary, generalisation, conceptual lenses and which norms (pp. 20 to 24). The authors never question the need for planning; they ask that it start from context and conflict."},{"t":"p7","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Liu and Zhou (2021) on China's old planning system?","o":["Four main plan types sat with different ministries and had planning periods of 5 to 20 years.","A superior spatial planning law existed, but local governments routinely ignored its rules.","The same plot could be classified differently, since plans used different land classes.","In 2018 the Ministry of Natural Resources took over the main spatial planning functions."],"a":1,"e":"The core problem was the absence of a superior planning law: the legal framework was scattered and main functional zone planning had no law at all, which is why an NTSP law is called a milestone. The other statements match the article (Table 1, pp. 3 to 4)."},{"t":"p7","type":"contrast","q":"How does lecture 7 contrast China's two kinds of land?","o":["Urban land is privately owned by residents; rural land is owned by the state and leased to farmers.","Both are state-owned, but only rural land can be sold freely once a village committee agrees.","Urban state land has transferable use rights; collective land must be expropriated before urban use.","Urban land is owned by city governments; rural land belongs to provinces under the hukou system."],"a":2,"e":"The dual land system: state-owned urban land with transferable use rights versus village collective land whose transfer is severely restricted; non-agricultural use requires state expropriation first. Villagers hold use rights, not ownership. Hukou is household registration, not land ownership."},{"t":"p7","type":"apply","q":"A county sells industrial land cheaply to attract a factory and sells residential land at high prices to fund its budget. Which lecture 7 mechanism is this?","o":["Hukou reform: migrants gain urban registration so that new factories find enough workers.","Authoritarian deliberation: the state consults residents but keeps the final decision itself.","'Multiple plans in one': land-use and urban plans are merged into one territorial plan.","Land finance after the 1994 fiscal reform, fuelling a land-driven growth coalition."],"a":3,"e":"The 1994 fiscal reform left local governments with a small share of tax, so they raised revenue from land: cheap industrial land attracts investment, dear residential land maximises revenue, and planning becomes the tool to convert farmland. 'Multiple plans in one' is the later NTSP answer to fragmentation."},{"t":"p7","type":"quote","q":"Liu and Zhou locate the root of China's planning problems in 'the game of interests between the central and local governments, between different departments'. What does lecture 7 add that the article leaves unexplained?","o":["Land finance after the 1994 fiscal reform, which gave localities a stake in converting farmland.","The 2019 'Opinions', which set out the three steps towards a unified planning system by 2035.","The ecological red line, which protects ecosystems from expansion by local governments.","The European Spatial Development Perspective, which inspired China's turn to spatial planning."],"a":0,"e":"The lecture's land-driven growth machine explains why local interests clashed with farmland and ecological protection: their budgets depended on land sales. The 2019 'Opinions' and the red line are in the article itself, as part of the reform, not an explanation of the conflict of interests."},{"t":"p7","type":"position","q":"How does lecture 7 characterise the co-creation committee at Enning Road, Guangzhou?","o":["A full collaborative turn: residents co-decided the plan from the start as equal partners.","Authoritarian deliberation: conflict fell, but the committee came late, with unequal power.","Insurgent planning: residents built invented spaces against the state's regeneration plan.","Pure tokenism: the committee met but had no effect on developers or government at all."],"a":1,"e":"Positives: participants could urge the developer or government to act, social conflict fell, and there was moral responsibility. Limits: set up only at the implementation stage, limited influence, unequal power. It was not pure tokenism, since it had real effects, but it was state-bounded."},{"t":"p7","type":"consequence","q":"Why do Liu and Zhou argue that urban development boundaries must plan for shrinkage too?","o":["Because eco-cities attract so many people that other cities must shrink to free up land quotas.","Because the hukou reform will send most urban migrants back to their villages by 2035.","Because thousands of towns lost people in 2000 to 2010 and villages vanish at over 100 a day.","Because the three control lines forbid any further growth of cities in the eastern provinces."],"a":2,"e":"More than 10,000 towns and street offices lost population between 2000 and 2010, and natural villages fell from 3.77 million (1990) to 2.61 million (2016). Plans that assume growth everywhere would waste land and infrastructure. The control lines limit growth but do not forbid it."},{"t":"p8","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Hodson, Geels and McMeekin (2017)?","o":["Innovation lies in the particular form of reconfiguration rather than in individual technologies.","How strongly a transport system corresponds to the city varies from automobility to cycling.","The authors test their framework with new ethnographic fieldwork in several UK cities.","What counts as sustainable mobility is contested: less travel, modal shift, shorter trips, efficiency."],"a":2,"e":"The paper is conceptual and 'propositional and generative': examples come from existing mobility studies, not new fieldwork, and the authors call for theoretically informed ethnography as future work. The other statements are central claims of the paper."},{"t":"p8","type":"contrast","q":"What do geographers criticise in the multi-level perspective (MLP), according to Hodson et al.?","o":["The MLP ignores technology and reduces transitions to changes in cultural values and lifestyles.","The MLP focuses on single cities and so cannot explain national energy and transport systems.","The MLP sees transitions as quick switches, while geographers show they always take centuries.","MLP cases were mostly national and treated space as given; the same niche differs by place."],"a":3,"e":"Geographers argued that space and scale are actively and relationally produced, so regimes can be territorially uneven and 'the same' niche looks different in different places. The MLP's bias was national, not urban, which is the reverse of the second option."},{"t":"p8","type":"apply","q":"A city adds e-scooters, protected bike lanes and a new tram line, while car traffic continues as before; the three new systems barely interact. In Hodson et al.'s framework, how do these arrangements relate?","o":["Co-existing: parallel, largely independent arrangements beside the existing car system.","Complementary: productive fusions in which new and old systems reinforce each other.","Competing: a struggle of new against old in which the car regime is being displaced.","A niche breakthrough: landscape pressure has opened a window for one radical innovation."],"a":0,"e":"Table 1 distinguishes competing (struggles new vs new or new vs old), co-existing (parallel, largely independent) and complementary (productive fusions). Nothing here displaces cars or fuses systems. The niche-breakthrough option is the classic MLP story the authors find too simple for cities."},{"t":"p8","type":"quote","q":"Hodson et al. write: 'There is nothing particularly novel and innovative about a bike or a credit card-sized piece of plastic.' What point are they making about London's bike hire?","o":["The scheme failed, because it only copied Paris and added no new technology of its own.","The innovation lies in embedding generic technology in a city's existing systems and policies.","Cycling cannot be a real transition, because bikes are an old technology from the 19th century.","Corporate sponsors such as Barclays matter more for transitions than municipal policies do."],"a":1,"e":"Bike sharing is a 'generic' intervention; what matters is the process of embedding it in a longer trajectory (2001 cycling strategy, 2003 congestion charge, bus lanes, budgets). The scheme did learn from Paris, but the authors present it as a success of embedding, not a failure."},{"t":"p8","type":"position","q":"Where do Hodson et al. stand on deliberate versus emergent change?","o":["Purely deliberate: a city coalition designs the transition and implements it step by step.","Purely emergent: experiments are accidents, so governance and planning play no real role.","Both: experiments are purposive, but the forms of reconfiguration are 'by definition emergent'.","Neither: transitions are determined by the global landscape, outside any actor's influence."],"a":2,"e":"Experiments are purposive acts of governance, yet reconfiguration combines old and new in ways that are 'by definition emergent' and uneven between places. Wider flows such as neoliberal restructuring shape but do not determine local outcomes, which rules out the last option."},{"t":"p8","type":"consequence","q":"Automobility has weak 'correspondence' with the city in Hodson et al.'s analysis. What follows for urban transitions?","o":["Cities should focus their policy on cars, since that is where the largest emissions are.","National governments should hand over car regulation to cities so that correspondence rises.","Car-based systems will disappear first, since cities have the least interest in keeping them.","Cities can shape cycling and buses much more than cars, which global firms and states govern."],"a":3,"e":"Automobility is governed by global car manufacturers, their alliances with national governments and oil interests; buses, light rail and cycling correspond more strongly to the city. So a city can shape some systems far more than others. Nothing in the paper predicts cars will vanish first."},{"t":"p9","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Bulkeley et al.'s (2019) findings on 40 European living labs?","o":["In the Netherlands most of the urban living labs studied were of the civic type.","The civic demonstration was the most common combination of design and disposition.","The platform disposition appeared across strategic, civic and organic labs alike.","Most labs were controlled trials, since living labs began as corporate product tests."],"a":3,"e":"Trials were rare, even though living labs originated in corporate technology testing; the UK sample had none at all. The labs leaned towards contingent dispositions (demonstrations and platforms). The other three statements match Table 2 and the discussion (pp. 10 to 12)."},{"t":"p9","type":"contrast","q":"What separates an 'enclave' from a 'demonstration' in Bulkeley et al.'s typology?","o":["An enclave bounds a district as a microcosm set apart; a demonstration showcases a possible city.","An enclave is led by community groups; a demonstration is always led by national agencies.","An enclave tests one product under control; a demonstration curates links between many actors.","An enclave is temporary; a demonstration is a permanent part of the city's statutory plan."],"a":0,"e":"The enclave draws a spatial boundary around an experimental district, a microcosm of the whole yet set apart, whose boundary becomes an 'obligatory passage point'. The demonstration exhibits what the city could resemble under the right conditions. Testing one product is the trial; curating links is the platform."},{"t":"p9","type":"apply","q":"An NGO runs a cultural maker house in a city district. Funding is patched together from municipal premises, foundations and fees, and the house links many one-off activities into a running programme. How would Bulkeley et al. classify it?","o":["A strategic demonstration showcasing corporate technology to national sponsors.","An organic platform: community-led, creating conditions for many new relations.","A civic trial that tests a municipal service under controlled real-world conditions.","A civic enclave that seals off a district so lessons pass through one gateway."],"a":1,"e":"This is Stapeln (STPLN) in Malmö: civil society leads (organic design) and the house works as a platform, letting a resource-poor initiative connect one-off activities into a programme. The enclave option misreads the openness: platforms loosen boundaries instead of guarding them."},{"t":"p9","type":"quote","q":"Bulkeley et al. 'suggest that such goals for urban improvement are not necessarily radical in nature and indeed can seek to reinforce the status quo'. Whom are they disagreeing with?","o":["John Dewey, who saw all experimentation as a form of open-ended inquiry.","Keller Easterling, who defined a disposition as a propensity within a context.","Karvonen and van Heur, who see urban laboratories as intentionally radical.","Michel Foucault, who analysed laboratories as forms of disciplinary power."],"a":2,"e":"On 'change orientation' the authors part ways with Karvonen and van Heur, who treat labs as intentionally radical, and study the 'politics of improvement' instead. Dewey and Easterling are sources they build on, not opponents."},{"t":"p9","type":"position","q":"Where do urban living labs lean on the spectrum from control to contingency, according to Bulkeley et al.?","o":["Towards control: most are trials and enclaves that limit indeterminacy in space and time.","Evenly spread: each of the four dispositions is about equally common in every country.","Towards control in strategic labs and towards contingency only in organic, grassroots labs.","Towards contingency: demonstrations and platforms, reflecting fragmented governing capacity."],"a":3,"e":"The first conclusion is that ULLs favour contingent dispositions, because municipalities, universities and community groups have limited autonomy and resources, and transformations are open-ended. Strategic labs were mostly demonstrations or platforms too, so control is not typical of them either."},{"t":"p9","type":"consequence","q":"What happened to Rotterdam's Concept House Village in Heijplaat once the property market recovered?","o":["The municipality moved the site back into mainstream development: 'You got it for five years'.","The lab was scaled up into a national programme for sustainable retrofitting of social housing.","Residents took over the site and turned it into a community-run organic platform.","The universities bought the land, so the test bed became a permanent research campus."],"a":0,"e":"The lab got its site only because the crisis had stalled Heijplaat's redevelopment; when the economy recovered, the municipality reclaimed it. The authors conclude that ULLs often achieve other things than planned and that 'new thinking about their failure will be needed'."},{"t":"p10","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS the editorial by Lin, Geertman, Witte and Pinto (2025)?","o":["Digital planning is just a newer label for the same field as Planning Support Science.","Planning support systems are tools that can be used within digital planning practice.","The smart city debate was driven mainly by business and is criticised as technocratic.","Digital planning is more government-led and goal-guided than platform urbanism is."],"a":0,"e":"The editorial separates them: Planning Support Science is scientific (how instruments fit governance and context), digital planning is application-oriented (how they make planning inclusive and effective); one is a prerequisite for the other. The other statements are accurate."},{"t":"p10","type":"contrast","q":"How does the editorial distinguish digital planning from the smart city concept?","o":["Smart cities focus on citizens' needs; digital planning focuses on technology suppliers.","Smart cities are business-driven; digital planning is government-led and guided by policy goals.","Smart cities are a Chinese idea; digital planning grew from European land-use regulation.","Smart cities use data; digital planning avoids big data in favour of face-to-face deliberation."],"a":1,"e":"The smart city debate was driven mainly by business (whole ecosystems such as Songdo) and criticised as technocratic with a weak link to citizens' problems. Digital planning includes such tools but is strategic, procedural and operational, more government-led and guided by sustainable, inclusive goals."},{"t":"p10","type":"apply","q":"A municipality buys an advanced 3D city model, but planners rarely open it because it cannot answer the questions they face in their projects. Which problem from the editorial is this?","o":["The digital divide: older citizens lack the devices needed to view the model at home.","Platform urbanism: a private platform has captured the city's data and its services.","A supply-demand mismatch: the tool starts from the technology, not the planning task.","Cybersecurity risk: the model exposes sensitive infrastructure data to outside attackers."],"a":2,"e":"The editorial lists a mismatch between technology supply and planning demand, and endorses Klosterman's view that planning support should 'start from the peculiarities of the planning activity itself, and not from the characteristics of the technology'. The digital divide concerns citizens, not planners' use."},{"t":"p10","type":"quote","q":"Which field is built on Klosterman's point that planning support should start from 'the peculiarities of the planning activity itself, and not from the characteristics of the technology'?","o":["Platform urbanism, as defined by Barns for smartphone-based services.","The smart city, following Giffinger et al.'s six smart city domains.","Agentic AI, which acts autonomously on behalf of planning departments.","Planning Support Science, as Geertman and Stillwell reframed PSS."],"a":3,"e":"As the PSS field matured, Geertman and Stillwell proposed reading PSS as Planning Support Science, echoing Klosterman: the science of fitting instruments to the governance and application at hand. Smart city work was business-driven and technology-led, the opposite orientation."},{"t":"p10","type":"position","q":"Where does the editorial sit on the course dimension of deliberate versus emergent change?","o":["Strongly deliberate: government-led planning consciously guided by sustainability and inclusion.","Strongly emergent: platforms evolve by themselves, so planners can only watch and adapt.","Neutral: digital tools are value-free, so the question of direction does not arise.","Sceptical of both: AI will take over, so neither deliberate nor emergent change matters."],"a":0,"e":"Digital planning is defined as government-led and 'consciously guided by policy goals', with efficiency, effectiveness and inclusivity as aims. The editorial warns that smart city apps and platforms develop without planning intervention, which is why it calls for alignment and data governance."},{"t":"p10","type":"consequence","q":"Batty and Yang propose a cyclic system with two loops, which the editorial cites. What follows from it for digital planning?","o":["Data analytics should replace political debate, since evidence makes argument redundant.","An evidence loop driven by data must connect to a democratic loop of argument and politics.","Planners should alternate between digital and analog years to keep both skills alive.","Citizens run the data loop and planners the decision loop, so the roles never overlap."],"a":1,"e":"Batty and Yang pair an evidence analytics loop (data-driven) with a democratic decision-making loop (argument, discussion, design, politics). Analytics alone cannot decide, which fits the editorial's warning that digitalisation 'is not just the straightforward implementation and use of new technologies'."},{"t":"p11","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Li and Dang (2025)?","o":["Generative AI extends AI's reach from numbers to policy texts, design and sentiment analysis.","Market forces and politicians seeking an 'innovative' city push AI into planning practice.","Like CAD and GIS before it, AI mainly changes how planners visualise and analyse data.","Agencies often lack the resources and skills to carry out rigorous algorithmic audits."],"a":2,"e":"The contrast is the point: CAD and GIS left interpretation and negotiation to humans, while AI 'stands apart by automating critical thinking tasks that were once exclusive to human professionals'. The other three statements are claims the authors make."},{"t":"p11","type":"contrast","q":"Li and Dang discuss two counter-arguments: AI only augments planners, and AI can be regulated. What is their reply?","o":["Both are wrong, so planning agencies should ban AI tools until the law catches up.","Both are right, so the profession can adopt AI as long as it publishes its algorithms.","Regulation works but augmentation fails, because planners resist learning new software.","Both have merit but understate disruption: support tools tend to drift into replacement."],"a":3,"e":"In journalism and manufacturing, tools introduced as support evolved towards partial or full replacement, speed and cost savings can override social goals, and agencies lack capacity for audits; so balanced augmentation is 'precarious'. They do not call for a ban: the aim is deliberate integration on the profession's terms."},{"t":"p11","type":"apply","q":"An AI tool selects a site for a new transit hub; the area then gentrifies quickly. Residents ask who is responsible, and the planners point to the software vendor. Which risk do Li and Dang name?","o":["Blurred accountability: AI distances a recommendation from its human overseers.","Deskilling: planners lose the judgement built up by reconciling community values.","The digital divide: residents lacked the skills to take part in the AI's design.","Algorithmic bias: training data carried historic inequities such as redlining."],"a":0,"e":"The authors use this very example: if an automated site selection triggers gentrification, who bears responsibility? Without transparency, open data and a clear chain of accountability, errors are hard to trace. Bias concerns what goes into the model; here the problem is who answers for the outcome."},{"t":"p11","type":"quote","q":"According to Li and Dang, what 'threatens to erode public trust and professional legitimacy'?","o":["Public participation that is too slow to keep up with fast-changing data in growing cities.","AI reducing the planner's role in negotiating interests and obscuring who is responsible.","Private developers who use their own AI to outbid municipalities in the land market.","Planning schools that keep teaching GIS instead of moving straight to machine learning."],"a":1,"e":"The opening argument: by reducing the planner's role in negotiating complex interests and obscuring responsibility for outcomes, AI threatens to erode trust and legitimacy. The authors defend slow deliberation, so the first option inverts their position."},{"t":"p11","type":"position","q":"Is AI's disruption of planning avoidable, according to Li and Dang?","o":["Yes: if planners refuse to use AI, the technology will stay outside public planning.","Yes: strict regulation and ethics boards can keep AI out of core planning decisions.","No: markets, political branding and generative AI push it in, so planners must set the terms.","No, and it does not matter, because AI will make planning both faster and fairer."],"a":2,"e":"Even if planners resist, developers, transport firms and politicians wanting an 'innovative' city push AI forward, and agencies that lag risk becoming uncompetitive. The authors conclude the profession should set the terms of integration, not that AI is harmless."},{"t":"p11","type":"consequence","q":"Li and Dang propose 'community-in-the-loop'. Which risk is this mainly meant to address?","o":["Blurred accountability between vendors, politicians and planning departments.","The high cost of AI licences for small municipalities with tight budgets.","Slow decisions caused by too many stakeholders taking part in deliberation.","Biased data and lost voices: marginalised groups help collect, build and validate."],"a":3,"e":"Embedding community values means involving diverse stakeholders from the start, with marginalised groups in data collection, model building and validation, which targets algorithmic bias and the exclusion of voices that numbers miss. Accountability is addressed by audits, oversight and the planner as 'sense-maker'."},{"t":"p12","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Durrant, Lamker and Rydin (2023)?","o":["Planning instruments grew up to cope with and foster growth, and success is often measured in money.","Land value capture is their preferred tool, because it makes developers fund social housing.","There is little evidence that absolute decoupling is happening or even possible in a pro-growth world.","New infrastructure should be coordinated with the planned replacement or shrinkage of old networks."],"a":1,"e":"Land value capture is explicitly excluded: it depends on creating more monetised development value, often in expensive locations, and is therefore inherently pro-growth (p. 290). The other three statements are core claims of the comment."},{"t":"p12","type":"contrast","q":"How do Durrant et al. distinguish degrowth from post-growth?","o":["Degrowth wants planned recession; post-growth wants green growth through eco-modern technology.","Degrowth is a planning practice; post-growth is an economic theory without spatial application.","Degrowth is structural and economic, voicing North-South inequality; post-growth is pragmatic re-tooling.","Degrowth applies to the Global South only; post-growth applies to wealthy cities in the North."],"a":2,"e":"Degrowth is more rooted in economics, structural and theoretical, and gives voice to North-South inequality; post-growth is an interdisciplinary, pragmatic search for radical alternatives within existing structures, which suits an analysis of today's planning tools. Neither label means green growth, which they criticise."},{"t":"p12","type":"apply","q":"A ministry justifies a new motorway by comparing it with a 'do nothing' scenario of rising congestion. What would Durrant et al. ask of planners?","o":["Accept the comparison, since congestion costs are a valid measure of public benefit.","Fund the motorway through land value capture along its route to make it pay for itself.","Approve it if the motorway is built with low-carbon concrete and electric charging points.","Scrutinise the 'do nothing' baseline and plan road phase-outs alongside any new build."],"a":3,"e":"The authors call for critical scrutiny of the 'do nothing' scenarios that often justify new projects, and for coordinating new infrastructure with planned shrinkage of existing networks, such as cycling space taken from roads. Low-carbon materials alone would be the eco-modernist labelling they criticise."},{"t":"p12","type":"quote","q":"Durrant et al. write: 'It is particularly worrying to see how the pursuit of sustainability has been subsumed to the pro-growth agenda.' What do they mean?","o":["Eco-modernism makes it almost always possible to label more development as sustainable.","Sustainability has been dropped from planning law in favour of explicit growth targets.","Growth has become so green that sustainability goals are no longer needed in planning.","Environmental groups now support growth because it funds nature protection programmes."],"a":0,"e":"Planning embraced eco-modernism (eco-cities, carbon-neutral developments), so it became 'almost always possible' to label more development as sustainable (Krähmer), and trade-offs hid the deeper resource conflicts Campbell identified. Sustainability was absorbed into growth, not dropped."},{"t":"p12","type":"position","q":"On what do Durrant et al. ground the planner's legitimacy to challenge growth-based plans?","o":["On their personal values, since planners should act as activists for degrowth.","On expertise about consequences, since most planners are employees under politicians.","On consensus, since a challenge is only legitimate once all stakeholders agree.","On the law, since national rules already forbid plans that assume growth."],"a":1,"e":"Because most planners are employees, often under elected politicians, the authors question how far they can promote a new agenda on values; instead they ground the role in expertise: spelling out medium- to long-term consequences and the infeasibility of growth-based plans, and voicing 'uncomfortable truths'."},{"t":"p12","type":"not","q":"Which is NOT a reason Durrant et al. give for doubting absolute decoupling?","o":["Rebound effects: efficiency gains do not reduce total consumption (the Jevons paradox).","Technological change misses high-consumption areas or shifts the problem elsewhere.","Renewable energy cannot be scaled up fast enough to power a growing economy.","The potential of recycling and the circular economy has been overestimated."],"a":2,"e":"Their reasons are rebound, problem-shifting technology, overestimated recycling and circularity, and underestimated impacts of the service economy and of consumption shifted across the globe (p. 289). A limit on scaling renewables is not among them."},{"t":"p13","type":"misread","q":"Which statement MISREPRESENTS Hansen et al. (2022)?","o":["Most of the eleven cities transformed their green space planning radically after COVID-19.","Participation efforts and cross-departmental cooperation increased in most of the cities.","Climate adaptation sparked the most new policies, partly after floods and heat waves.","Awareness of social cohesion grew, but specific actions for disadvantaged areas were few."],"a":0,"e":"Only Barcelona reported concrete acceleration after COVID-19, and the verdict is that 'these championing cases ... cannot be classified as radical urban transformations'. The other statements are reported results (pp. 2409 to 2413)."},{"t":"p13","type":"contrast","q":"In the Wolfram et al. outcomes used by Hansen et al., how does exnovation differ from innovation?","o":["Exnovation imports ideas from abroad; innovation develops them inside the municipality.","Exnovation exposes and dismantles path dependencies; innovation creates and anchors novelties.","Exnovation is led by citizens; innovation is led by officials in the green space department.","Exnovation concerns social justice; innovation concerns ecological and climate measures."],"a":1,"e":"Innovation means creating, nurturing and anchoring novelties; exnovation means exposing and dismantling path dependencies ('unlocking'). Exnovation was rare in the cities, with Aarhus's unit for participation and cultural change as the clearest case. Social justice is a separate fourth outcome."},{"t":"p13","type":"apply","q":"A city creates a sub-department for citizenship and cultural change to break old routines in how its green space staff work with residents. Which outcome does this best represent?","o":["Social justice: redistributing resources and decision power to disadvantaged groups.","Innovation: a new strategy anchored in policy, such as a green area factor.","Exnovation: deliberately dismantling lock-in practices through change management.","Self-governance: residents take over the planning of their own green spaces."],"a":2,"e":"Aarhus's unit for participation and cultural change is the paper's clearest exnovation case: change management aimed at practices locked in by past routines. It supports co-governance, but its distinctive contribution is unlocking the organisation, not handing control to residents."},{"t":"p13","type":"quote","q":"Hansen et al. use the phrase 'intentional, actively pursued, radical and systemic'. What does it describe?","o":["Incremental change that may seed future transformation through citizen interest.","Co-governance, in which diverse actors are enabled and aligned across sectors.","Path dependency, in which past investments and routines constrain planning choices.","Transformative change, which must break through path dependencies in institutions."],"a":3,"e":"Drawing on transformation theory, transformative change is 'intentional, actively pursued, radical and systemic' and requires breaking through path dependencies and changing institutions, structures and practices. Incremental change is the opposite pole of the title's 'transformative or piecemeal'."},{"t":"p13","type":"position","q":"Where does Hansen et al.'s study land on structure versus agency?","o":["Structure dominates: officials see what is needed but lack resources, staff and capacity.","Agency dominates: committed officials transformed their cities despite weak structures.","Neither: change came mainly from disruptive events such as COVID-19 and floods.","Agency of citizens dominates: residents now steer green space through self-governance."],"a":0,"e":"The fundamental problem is limited resources and staff capacity caused by system-level conditions beyond green space departments or even municipalities (p. 2419); cities struggled to use windows of opportunity. Disruptive events produced only short-term change, so the third option overstates them."},{"t":"p13","type":"consequence","q":"What did Hansen et al. find on social justice in green space planning?","o":["It was the strongest lens: most cities targeted green space at disadvantaged areas first.","Awareness rose but specific approaches were rare: a 'considerable disparity' with ecology.","It was explicitly rejected by officials as outside the remit of green space departments.","It was fully covered by participatory budgeting, which all eleven cities had introduced."],"a":1,"e":"Social justice was the weakest lens: awareness grew, cultural diversity was rarely addressed, and even where city policy centred on equality it had not reached green space planning, a 'considerable disparity' compared with the many ecological responses (p. 2418). Participatory budgeting was mentioned for Helsinki, not all cities."}],"LENS":[{"t":"buit","w":1,"s":"A city introduces a congestion charge on its ring road after a cost-benefit analysis shows clear net welfare gains. The analysis also shows that low-income commuters from outer districts lose the most.","q":"How would Buitelaar (2026) read this decision?","o":["The charge shifts the costs of mobility onto workers so that capital can keep accumulating","The CBA figures do not speak for themselves; the report hides the framing it has chosen","Pricing road space overrides individual plans and requires a central authority's coercion","A Happy City choice: it maximises total utility but ignores how gains and losses fall"],"a":3,"e":"CBA is the Happy City's evaluation method, and Buitelaar notes the standard critique that utilitarianism neglects the skewness of distributions. The other options are Marxist, Rydin's (2021) and Hayek's readings."},{"t":"buit","w":1,"s":"A new coalition agreement in a Dutch city opens with: 'We want a just city for everyone.' The parties have very different views on rent control, social mixing and private development, but the agreement does not say which view prevails.","q":"How would Buitelaar (2026) respond?","o":["The vague wording creates superficial consensus; the conflicts stay hidden and may surface later","Talk of justice belongs to the superstructure and masks the class interests behind the coalition","The definition of 'just' frames what future research on the city will be able to find out","Planning theory has become divorced from practice, so politicians use its words without meaning"],"a":0,"e":"Keeping justice vague to avoid controversy is his first problem: short-term, superficial consensus while conflicts remain obfuscated. The other options are readings by a Marxist, Rydin (2021) and Hall."},{"t":"buit","w":1,"s":"A municipality sold farmland plots to households, who built their own homes, roads and green space under a few general rules. Ten years later, plot values and household wealth in the area differ widely, and a newspaper calls the outcome 'unfair'.","q":"How would Buitelaar (2026) analyse this?","o":["Private ownership of land lets some owners appropriate value that others' labour produced","From the Free City view the pattern is just if plots were acquired and traded legitimately","The case shows the post-1960 shift from designing physical form to managing a process","The newspaper's judgement is just one narrative; the same data could support a different one"],"a":1,"e":"This is an Oosterwold-type spontaneous order; under Nozick's entitlement theory the resulting pattern is just if acquisition and transfer were legitimate, whereas an Equal City view would object. The other options reflect Marx, Hall and Rydin (2021)."},{"t":"buit","w":1,"s":"After a wealthy household buys and renovates several homes in a working-class Utrecht street, prices rise and long-time residents hang banners saying 'this is unjust'. The alderman asks a planner whether the residents are right.","q":"How would Buitelaar (2026) advise the planner?","o":["Gentrification shows capital extracting value from the living space of the working class","The protest shows that theory has lost touch with the physical design of real places","The outrage signals possible injustice; the verdict depends on the rules and conception","The planner should report only the price data, which settle the question on their own"],"a":2,"e":"For Buitelaar outcomes signal but do not prove injustice; the verdict depends on whether the institutions are just and on which conception you hold (the Equal City condemns it, the Free City asks how homes were acquired). The other options are a Marxist reading, Hall's lament and the empiricism Rydin rejects."},{"t":"fw","w":1,"s":"A provincial report on why housebuilding fell short of its target lists high interest rates, building costs and the stacking of municipal rules. It concludes: 'The figures speak for themselves: deregulate.'","q":"How would Rydin (2021) read this report?","o":["Rightly so: stacked rules block the price signal, and removing them restores spontaneous coordination","The figures fit several narratives; the report has picked one framing and its values without saying so","The shortfall shows housing is built only when profit is secured, so deregulation mainly serves capital","Better models of the housing system would reveal the optimal level of regulation for each municipality"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin's point is that data are always interpreted into a narrative and the same figures can support different ones, so 'the figures speak for themselves' hides a framing. The other options are how a Hayekian, a Marxist and a 1960s systems planner would read it."},{"t":"fw","w":1,"s":"A municipality hires a research bureau to evaluate its new participation process. The bureau says it will count only the number of participants per evening, 'because that is objective and needs no theory'.","q":"How would Rydin (2021) respond?","o":["Choosing what to measure already carries values; theory is needed to define good participation","The bureau shows the divorce of theory and practice: practice has become unreflective procedure","The bureau should first decide which conception of justice it uses, since justice is not à la carte","The evaluation should be dropped, since a participation evening is merely part of the superstructure"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin argues that selecting data is a value-laden choice and that theory focuses research and links it to a broader narrative. The distractors are readings by Hall (New Proceduralism), Buitelaar (justice literacy) and a Marxist (superstructure)."},{"t":"fw","w":1,"s":"A master's student studying a Rotterdam neighbourhood is asked by her supervisor what 'planning' means in her thesis. She replies: 'Whatever the municipal planning department intends and does.'","q":"How would Rydin (2021) respond to this definition?","o":["It shows there is theory in planning but no theory of planning, as in the pre-1955 craft era","It is fine: planning is simply what planners do, so a definition has no theoretical consequences","It confirms that planning is part of the superstructure that protects existing property relations","It frames planning as instrumental and takes the department's intentions at face value"],"a":3,"e":"For Rydin any definition frames the object of research; an instrumental, department-based definition downplays politics and aligns research with practice's professed values, as she says of Næss and Saglie. The first option borrows Hall's phrase, the last a Marxist reading."},{"t":"fw","w":1,"s":"Two consultancies evaluate the same Amsterdam transformation area. One uses governance theory and stresses the stakeholder network; the other uses political economy and stresses land values and capital. The alderman asks which report is 'true'.","q":"How would Rydin (2021) answer the alderman?","o":["Test both against the data and keep the one whose predictions are confirmed, as in natural science","Both are equally valid stories, so choosing between them can only be a matter of personal taste","Both are partial narratives with different ontologies and values; judge them by persuasiveness","Only the political economy report is true, since the economic base determines the superstructure"],"a":2,"e":"Rydin sees theory as a heuristic, partial narrative carrying ontological and normative assumptions; theories are not arbitrary, and their persuasiveness in illuminating the case matters. Law-like testing is the model she rejects, and pure relativism ignores that theories can be argued for."},{"t":"hall","w":2,"s":"A Dutch municipality reorganises its planning department around standard checklists. Planners now spend their days checking permit applications against rules; nobody in the department can explain what kind of city the rules are meant to produce.","q":"How would Hall (2014) read this situation?","o":["Checklists are general rules that keep discretion limited; this is how a free society should plan","The checklists form part of the superstructure that protects existing property relations","As 'New Proceduralism': practice cut off from theory, unable to defend why planning matters","Knowledge claims are being reduced to procedure; arenas to test them are what is missing here"],"a":2,"e":"Hall describes 1980s and 1990s practice as codified 'New Proceduralism' and 'rampant technocracy' that lost the 'vision thing' and the intellectual means to defend itself. The other options are Hayekian, Marxist and Rydin (2007) readings."},{"t":"hall","w":2,"s":"A national agency launches a 'digital twin' of a Dutch city: real-time data on traffic, energy and housing feed models that will recommend the optimal interventions to the council. The project leader says politics will simply follow the evidence.","q":"How would Hall (2014) read this project?","o":["It repeats 1960s systems planning, with its naive view of politics and the city","It is impossible, because the knowledge it needs is dispersed over many individuals","It will mainly serve capital by raising land values where investment is steered","It needs arenas where residents' knowledge claims can be voiced and then tested"],"a":0,"e":"Hall's systems planners assumed politics was benign and receptive to expert advice, while the urban system sits inside its own system of control. The distractors are the Hayekian (dispersed knowledge), Marxist and Rydin (2007) readings."},{"t":"hall","w":2,"s":"A planning consultancy rebrands itself: 'We no longer design plans. We host evening sessions with residents, make sure every voice is heard, involve groups that are rarely organised, and share all information openly.'","q":"How would Hall (2014) comment?","o":["Engaging different voices is not yet engaging different knowledges, which need testing","Hosting sessions leaves the ownership of land untouched, so the results stay the same","An evening session is a heuristic: it frames which findings the consultancy will reach","This is good old-fashioned democratic common sense: Davidoff's advocacy planning again"],"a":3,"e":"Hall's verdict on communicative planning is that, stripped of its philosophy, it is 'good old-fashioned democratic common sense', no different from Davidoff 15 years earlier. The other options are Rydin (2007), Marxist and Rydin (2021) readings."},{"t":"hall","w":2,"s":"An activist collective argues that official city planning has always secretly served the powerful, pointing to exclusionary zoning in 1920s American suburbs. It calls for all planning to be handed to community groups.","q":"How would Hall (2014) respond?","o":["Planning law belongs to the superstructure, so community planning will change little","They have a point, but equating all official planning with its noir side is unfair","Handing planning to communities is right: knowledge is dispersed among many people","The groups should first state which conception of justice their critique assumes"],"a":1,"e":"Hall grants Sandercock a point but objects that implying all official planning is secretly malign equates 1920s exclusionary zoning with 1960s Swedish social democrats, and that redefining planning robs the word of meaning. The other options are Marxist, Hayekian and Buitelaar readings."},{"t":"marx","w":2,"s":"Amsterdam's council introduces a strict rent cap for mid-priced homes. The alderman calls it 'the end of speculation'; investors respond by selling homes to owner-occupiers, and the stock of rental homes falls.","q":"How would a Marxist (Marx and the HC4 lecture) read this?","o":["A price control that overrides market signals and gives officials power to judge each case","A rule that freezes the stock, so the area loses its capacity for incremental adaptation","A measure that tweaks outcomes while homes remain capital, so the base is left untouched","A cap that needs clearer evidence on supply effects before the council can judge it fairly"],"a":2,"e":"For a Marxist, capping rents is like 'better payment for the slave': it softens outcomes while housing stays a commodity owned as capital, and the investors' exit shows the base still dominates. The other readings are Hayek's, Cozzolino's and an evidence-based planning view."},{"t":"marx","w":2,"s":"A tech firm builds a showpiece campus near Eindhoven with free gyms, gourmet canteens and nap rooms. Staff work long hours on narrowly defined tasks, and many say they only feel like themselves at weekends.","q":"How would Marx read this?","o":["Comforts outside the task do not change that the work is external and coerced","The firm has found the right incentives, so free-riding among staff is avoided","The campus is a self-organised order that adapts to what employees want","The firm's culture gives staff scripts that make the long hours feel normal"],"a":0,"e":"Marx would see estrangement from the activity: the worker feels at home only outside work, whatever the perks. The other options read the case through rational choice, a Hayekian or Cozzolino lens, and sociological institutionalism."},{"t":"marx","w":2,"s":"A Dutch housing association sells hundreds of social rental homes to an investment fund to finance new construction. The fund renovates them and lets them at market rents to new tenants.","q":"How would a Marxist (Marx and Madden and Marcuse) read this?","o":["Voluntary exchange moves homes to those who value them most, so welfare rises","Homes are recommodified: they now serve exchange value instead of use value","The sale shows the association's path dependency, locked in by earlier choices","The deal is a rational response to transaction costs of managing scattered stock"],"a":1,"e":"Selling social homes to a fund turns housing back into a commodity produced and traded for exchange value; the need of low-income households no longer registers as demand. The other options are a market-liberal, a historical institutionalist and a transaction-cost reading."},{"t":"marx","w":2,"s":"A province plans a large new town on farmland, designed all at once by one public-private consortium. The land was bought cheaply by developers years ago and its value will multiply once the plan is approved.","q":"How would a Marxist read this?","o":["A neighbourhood built at once will be anti-adaptive, since ownership is concentrated","A central plan like this lacks the dispersed knowledge that a market would provide","A legible grid imposed from above will erase the practical knowledge of residents","The plan serves the owners of land, turning a public decision into private gain"],"a":3,"e":"A Marxist asks who owns the means of production, here the land, and sees planning as part of the superstructure that secures accumulation. The other readings are Cozzolino's (anti-adaptive neighbourhoods), Hayek's and Scott's."},{"t":"cozz","w":2,"s":"A Dutch municipality plans a new district of 3,000 homes on former farmland. To deliver quickly, it sells all land to one developer consortium, which builds every home within five years to one design code; the zoning plan copies the final design exactly.","q":"How would Cozzolino (2020) read this plan?","o":["A new AAN in the making: one designer, all-at-once building and frozen rules leave no room to adapt","A rational plan: one integrated design and clear goals let experts choose the most efficient means","A growth coalition: landowners and developers capture the land rent that rezoning creates for them","A legitimacy problem: the knowledge claims behind the design were never tested in public forums"],"a":0,"e":"Single designer, all-at-once construction and rules that coincide with the built form are core AAN characteristics. Cozzolino would split land into many plots and builders and leave room beyond the built form. The first option is the governmental model's reading, which he rejects."},{"t":"cozz","w":2,"s":"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality allows splitting a home only if the original home is at least 140 m², split homes are 100 metres apart and parking is provided on the plot. Few homes are split, despite the housing shortage.","q":"How would Cozzolino (2020) read these requirements?","o":["As evidence-based standards that experts have set to secure the public interest in housing quality","As rules that let landlords profit from scarcity while tenants bear the costs of the housing shortage","As institutional scripts that only shift once a crisis creates a critical juncture for real reform","As prescriptive rules that block owner-led incremental change and keep the stock from adapting"],"a":3,"e":"Such requirements predefine outcomes (size, spacing, parking) instead of only preventing externalities, so they block the small-scale, owner-led changes that keep areas adaptive. He would accept rules against real nuisance, but not these prescriptions. The landlord reading is Marxist."},{"t":"cozz","w":2,"s":"A historic city centre in Italy is protected by strict heritage rules: facades, uses and floor plans may not change. Shops stand empty, young families leave, and the council asks for a new regeneration programme funded by the state.","q":"How would Cozzolino (2020) respond?","o":["Accept the decline: preservation has value in itself, so the state must fund the centre indefinitely","Restrictive preservation rules can make even a historic quarter behave like an AAN and need top-down care","Treat this as a wicked problem with no definitive formulation, best handled by small incremental steps","The empty shops show that heritage rules mainly protect property values for owners, not use for residents"],"a":1,"e":"Cozzolino notes that old quarters sometimes present the typical problems of AANs because of preservation policies and restrictive rules, and asks whether preservation is reconcilable with long-term adaptability. Anti-adaptiveness comes from rules and design control, not from age."},{"t":"gov","w":2,"s":"A Dutch province faces competing claims on scarce land: solar fields, 20,000 homes, nature restoration and dairy farming. The provincial council has adopted goals for each; officials must now propose where everything should go.","q":"How would the governmental model (Rydin 2021) approach this?","o":["Let land prices allocate the land, with rules only against harm to neighbours","Treat the goals as given and integrate all claims in one balanced plan","Ask which coalition of landowners and developers benefits from each location","Open a forum where every group's knowledge claims are heard and then tested"],"a":1,"e":"This is comprehensive synthesis: officials integrate all dimensions into a reasoned, balanced view to meet politically set goals, using tools such as MCA and GIS. Letting land prices decide is a liberal reading; the coalition question is political economy."},{"t":"gov","w":2,"s":"A wind farm is proposed two kilometres from a village. Residents worry about noise and shadow flicker; ecologists warn about bird strikes; the council wants to know whether to grant the permit.","q":"How would the governmental model (Rydin 2021) handle this?","o":["Gather evidence on noise, flicker and birds for the competent body","Treat residents' worries as the real knowledge and let the village decide","See the dispute as a clash of class interests over who profits from energy","Leave the choice to the developer and residents, who know their own needs"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin uses wind farms to illustrate evidence-based planning: knowledge of impacts on birds and residents (noise, flicker) is gathered 'in a positive way' to support the decision. Treating residents' worries as decisive gives up the expert-led model."},{"t":"gov","w":2,"s":"Hypothetical: a London borough set a target that 35% of new homes in large schemes be affordable, but after viability negotiations with developers only 18% was delivered. The council commissions research on the gap.","q":"How would research framed by the governmental model (Rydin 2021) study this gap?","o":["Show that developers' power over land rent makes the targets unreachable","Model each actor's self-interest to show how developers capture the rent","Ask how residents could co-produce the knowledge on viability themselves","Take the 35% as given and trace where discretion and instruments failed"],"a":3,"e":"Within the frame, research becomes policy evaluation: the goal is taken as given, and the study follows the steps, the instruments and the negotiation to find where implementation fell short. Developers' power over land rent is a political economy reading."},{"t":"gov","w":2,"s":"A city council adopts the goal that by 2035 every resident should live within 300 metres of green shade. The planning department receives a budget to deliver it.","q":"How would the governmental model (Rydin 2021) expect the department to proceed?","o":["Let residents plant trees on their own plots and see what order emerges","Question whether shade is the right goal, given whose interests it serves","Collect data, design and test options, choose the best, then monitor","Wait for a crisis that opens a critical juncture for institutional change"],"a":2,"e":"With the goal set politically, the department follows the rational steps: data and forecasting, design and testing of alternatives, choice, implementation and review. Questioning the goal itself falls outside the model, where goals stand outside the planning process."},{"t":"hayek","w":2,"s":"A government proposes that all planning applications that comply with the adopted local plan are decided by officers, while councillors' committees keep only departures from the plan and a few sensitive cases.","q":"How would Hayek (1944) read this proposal?","o":["As acceptable if officers apply an agreed general rule, worrying only where discretion remains","As a gain in efficiency, since experts implement the goals that politicians have already set","As a loss of the deliberation through which residents and developers could build agreement","As a shift that serves developers, whose property gives them power over the local plan"],"a":0,"e":"Hayek accepts delegation of the application of general rules; what he fears is delegation of discretion 'on the merits'. The efficiency reading is the governmental model's; the developer reading is Marxist."},{"t":"hayek","w":2,"s":"A ministry wants one national spatial plan that allocates all scarce land by scoring every option on a 'broad welfare' index, with weights for housing, nature, jobs, culture and mobility set by an expert bureau.","q":"How would Hayek (1944) read this plan?","o":["As a sound comprehensive synthesis that weighs every dimension in one balanced view","As a call to test the index's causal claims in forums open to lay and expert input","As needing a complete ethical code: the experts' weights become everyone's values","As a threat to adaptability, since one plan leaves no room for incremental change"],"a":2,"e":"One index for all land presupposes a ranking of all values, which Hayek says nobody has; the experts setting the weights impose their scale of preferences. Praising it as comprehensive synthesis is the governmental model's reading."},{"t":"hayek","w":2,"s":"Coalition parties agree a national mobility plan is needed but cannot agree on its aims. Three ministers who do agree draft the whole plan, and parliament can only vote on it as one package.","q":"How would Hayek (1944) read this situation?","o":["As legitimate, since the ministers are accountable to parliament for the plan","As a correct use of expert knowledge to choose means for an agreed set of goals","As a minority's will imposed, with parliament left to accept or reject the whole","As a chance to build consensus by letting stakeholders deliberate on the plan"],"a":2,"e":"Hayek predicts that the largest group able to agree, possibly a small minority, imposes its plan, while parliament can only accept or reject it as a whole and cannot direct it. Formal accountability does not help, since 'it is not the source but the limitation of power' that matters."},{"t":"know","w":2,"s":"In a provincial area process on nitrogen reduction, farmers argue that the official deposition model overestimates their emissions and present measurements from their own barns. Environmental groups reply that only the national model counts.","q":"How would Rydin (2007) respond?","o":["Treat the national model as the evidence base and use farmers' input mainly to build support for the plan","Treat the barn data as empirical claims: hear them, then test them with the model in a lay-expert forum","Accept that no central model can grasp local conditions and leave emission choices to individual farmers","Treat the barn data and the model as equally valid stories and look for a compromise both sides accept"],"a":1,"e":"Rydin would open up (hear the farmers' empirical claims) and close down (test them against the model, with procedures to resolve claims). Using input only to build support is the governmental model; 'equally valid stories' is the relativism she rejects."},{"t":"know","w":2,"s":"A region runs a scenario study for 2050. The consultant drops a car-free scenario early as 'unrealistic' without showing why, and the alderman wants to debate only the two growth scenarios that fit the coalition agreement.","q":"How would Rydin (2007) read this?","o":["Politicians set the goals, so narrowing the scenarios to the coalition's choices is legitimate","Only small adjustments from the current situation should be compared, so two options suffice","The planner should check if the scenario is genuinely possible and keep possible futures open","Scenarios are pointless, since no planner can foresee or steer the future direction of a region"],"a":2,"e":"For normative claims Rydin wants informed public debate in which the planner checks whether futures are warranted as possible and prevents any possible future from being ruled out. Accepting the coalition's narrowing is the governmental model's goal-setting logic, not Rydin's."},{"t":"know","w":2,"s":"A national bureau forecasts housing demand in a city to 2035. A residents' group says the forecast underestimates demand from young people leaving home, based on a survey in its own neighbourhood.","q":"How would Rydin (2007) handle these predictive claims?","o":["Treat the residents' survey as equally valid local knowledge and average the two figures","Accept the bureau's forecast as objective evidence and note the group's view as an opinion","Assume the forecast was chosen to back existing policy, so discard it and start a new study","Keep forecasting expert-led but stay alert to lay input; test the models and methods"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin sees predictive claims as mostly expert-led, with the planner as client of researchers but alert to lay input, and testing that challenges the causal model and prediction techniques. Averaging treats knowledges as additive, which she rejects."},{"t":"know","w":2,"s":"A citizen-science network measures air quality with low-cost sensors and finds higher levels near a primary school than the official station shows. The municipality ignores the data because the sensors are not certified.","q":"How would Rydin (2007) read the municipality's response?","o":["It fails to open up: the readings are claims that deserve a hearing and a joint test of methods","It is right: only certified expert data can count as knowledge in a rational planning process","It shows that whoever owns the measuring tools decides which version of reality gets recognised","It is wrong because residents' experience of their own street should outweigh official data"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin would see the readings as empirical claims to be heard and then tested, for example by comparing methods in a lay-expert forum. Letting residents' data simply outweigh official data skips closing down: experience is not of itself sufficient to be knowledge."},{"t":"davoudi","w":3,"s":"Residents of a flood-prone polder know from decades of experience which ditches overflow first after heavy rain. The water board only accepts hydrological model outputs as evidence, and residents' observations are filed as 'complaints'.","q":"How would Davoudi read the water board's stance?","o":["As a sound division of labour in which experts supply the technical means","As a narrow view of evidence that excludes experiential and tacit knowledge","As residents strategically exaggerating risks to gain public investment","As a board serving the property interests of the large landowners"],"a":1,"e":"Davoudi argues evidence includes formal and informal, expert and experiential, systematic and tacit knowledge; treating only model output as evidence repeats the positivist misconception. The division-of-labour reading is Rydin's governmental model, the strategic reading rational choice, the landowner reading Marxist."},{"t":"davoudi","w":3,"s":"A national research foundation funds a ten-year programme on how housing markets work. It gives no direct policy advice, but its findings slowly change how newspapers, parties and civil servants frame the housing question.","q":"How would Davoudi judge this programme?","o":["Positively, as research that illuminates the landscape and informs debate","Negatively, as research that fails to evaluate the steps of a given policy","Negatively, as a way for researchers to secure funding for their own careers","Positively, as research that will finally let experts decide housing policy"],"a":0,"e":"This is the enlightenment model Davoudi favours: indirect, slow benefits that inform public debate rather than 'punchy policy messages'. The 'evaluate the steps' criticism is the governmental model's critical friend, the careers reading rational choice; expert rule is the expert-on-top model she rejects."},{"t":"davoudi","w":3,"s":"In a dispute over a regional road plan, the province's traffic model counts as 'the evidence', while a farmers' association's own survey of road use is dismissed as anecdotal. The province alone decides which data are admissible in the hearing.","q":"How would Davoudi read this situation?","o":["As experts rightly supplying the best means for goals the province has set","As a collective action problem the farmers could solve with selective incentives","As power deciding what counts as knowledge, beyond any neutral test of truth","As an institutional void in which new meanings for the region can take root"],"a":2,"e":"Davoudi follows Flyvbjerg: power determines what counts as knowledge, so the question of whose data are admitted is itself political. The 'best means' reading is the governmental model, selective incentives come from Olson, institutional voids from Dembski and Salet."},{"t":"davoudi","w":3,"s":"A new government announces that all spatial decisions will be based on a national dashboard of indicators, promising that 'the numbers will decide, not politics'.","q":"How would Davoudi read this promise?","o":["As a legible simplification that will erase residents' informal practices","As a welcome end to the rent-seeking of groups that lobby for planning favours","As a sound basis for welfare optimisation if the indicators are complete","As the naive hope that political processes can be technicized and controlled"],"a":3,"e":"Davoudi's conclusion targets exactly the 'ambitious and naïve assumptions' that political and socio-economic processes can be technicized and controlled through a scientific process. The legibility reading is Scott's, rent-seeking comes from Taylor and rational choice, welfare optimisation from the governmental model."},{"t":"scott","w":3,"s":"A national agency plans the renewal of a dense neighbourhood using a data dashboard of traffic counts, floor space and crime statistics. A Saturday market in a car park and the informal repair shops along one street do not show up in the data, and the plan replaces them with a car-free plaza and an office block.","q":"How would Scott read this plan?","o":["As a legible simplification that erases the informal, practical order the data cannot see","As a sound division of labour, since experts optimise the means for goals set by politicians","As a rational response to transaction costs that keep the market traders from bargaining","As a way of turning the neighbourhood into a source of profit for the owners of capital"],"a":0,"e":"Scott would see the state making the area legible through simplified indicators and so destroying the complex order that lives in practice (mētis, Jacobs's mixed use). The division-of-labour reading is Rydin's governmental model, the transaction-cost reading rational choice, the profit reading Marxist."},{"t":"scott","w":3,"s":"A hypothetical Dutch post-war district has high-rise slabs, separate levels for cars, cyclists and pedestrians, and shops grouped in one centre. Residents avoid the elevated walkways after dark. The municipality now proposes to demolish some slabs, bring roads back to ground level and add ground-floor shops and homes along them.","q":"How would Scott (with Jacobs) read the proposal?","o":["As unwise, since the original design reflected a rational, expert-made consensus","As a correction of functional segregation that brings back use and eyes on the street","As proof that only a critical juncture can break a path set at the district's founding","As evidence that the original plan rested on an expert-on-top view of research"],"a":1,"e":"The district is a textbook case of functional segregation and the death of the street; mixing uses at ground level restores the continual use that Jacobs links to safe sidewalks. The critical-juncture reading is historical institutionalism and the expert-on-top reading is Davoudi's vocabulary."},{"t":"scott","w":3,"s":"A housing corporation builds a new estate with identical facades, no balconies and strict rules against changing doors, colours or front gardens, to keep the estate 'visually calm'. Residents say they cannot make their homes their own, and many move out within a few years.","q":"How would Scott read this situation?","o":["As a free-rider problem: no single resident gains enough to fight the rules","As the result of unclear property rights between the corporation and tenants","As tenants' alienation from the product of the labour they sell to employers","As a uniform visual order that resists personal touches, like Brasília"],"a":3,"e":"Scott describes Brasília's identical facades without even balconies, which resisted the stamp of individuality and fed brasilite. The free-rider reading is Olson, the property-rights reading Needham and Coase, the alienation reading Marx's estranged labour."},{"t":"scott","w":3,"s":"A government builds a new administrative town for 50,000 people. The construction workers, expected to leave when the work is done, instead settle on land at the edge, organise, and demand water, schools and title. The planning agency treats the settlement as a temporary nuisance outside its plan.","q":"How would Scott read the agency's stance?","o":["As justified, since a large group of workers will rarely manage to organise itself","As wise, since a spontaneous order should be left alone by any public authority","As short-sighted, since the planned town depends on the unplanned one at its edges","As correct, since the plan's goals were set by elected politicians beforehand"],"a":2,"e":"This mirrors the candangos: Scott argues the two Brasílias were symbiotic, so ignoring the settlement misreads how the town actually works. The 'large groups rarely organise' reading comes from Olson, which the case contradicts; the hands-off reading is Hayekian; the elected-goals reading is the governmental model."},{"t":"needham","w":3,"s":"An alderman defends a large logistics hall next to a village: 'The public interest requires this jobs hub.' Residents reply that they will face lorries on their street and lose the view over open fields.","q":"How would Needham (2012) read this dispute?","o":["'Public interest' talk depersonalises those affected; weigh the identifiable interests of residents and others instead","The residents will not organise, because each one expects the others to do the work, so the hall will be built anyway","Landowners near the site will lobby to capture the value increase, and costs will be shifted to future residents","Only experts can define the public interest, so the alderman should commission an objective cost-benefit analysis"],"a":0,"e":"Needham rejects 'the public interest' because it turns people into an amorphous public ('But we too are the public!') and looks instead at identifiable interests: traffic in my street, an open landscape. Free-riding is Olson, value capture by insiders is Taylor, and expert-defined public interest is Rydin's governmental model."},{"t":"needham","w":3,"s":"A Dutch housing association considers giving tenants of its social rented homes a long, tradable right of use, short of full ownership, so they gain more control over their homes and can sell the right on when they move.","q":"How would Needham (2012) read this proposal?","o":["As a trap: rights in property always reflect class power, so the tenants will end up worse off than before","As rent-seeking: the sitting tenants become insiders who capture value at the expense of future renters","As a possible way to strengthen poorer people by giving them rights that need not be full ownership","As pointless: only a public authority can protect tenants' interests, since tenants cannot go to court"],"a":2,"e":"Needham supports 'the (rather old-fashioned) idea that the power of poorer people can be strengthened by giving them rights ... in landed property' (pp. 34-35), and says the rights route is neither left nor right. The class reading is Marxist; the insider reading is Taylor's."},{"t":"needham","w":3,"s":"A covered shopping passage owned by a pension fund doubles as the main walking route between a station and a neighbourhood. When the owner starts closing it at 8 p.m., residents ask the municipality to step in.","q":"How would Needham (2012) read this situation?","o":["As proof that public space must always be publicly owned, because private owners never serve the public","As a sign that residents lack a shared identity with the place, which must be built through symbols","As the entrepreneurial city at work: public space is sold off to attract mobile investment capital","As a privately owned public space whose access depends on agreed rights and permit conditions"],"a":3,"e":"Needham describes malls where the owner 'allows free access to everyone, but has the right to prohibit access under certain circumstances, such as when the shops are closed', with planning conditions specifying public access (p. 33). Whether the municipality can act depends on how those rights and conditions were set."},{"t":"ni","w":4,"s":"In January 2025 a Dutch court ordered the state to meet its statutory nitrogen target by 2030, on pain of a 10 million euro penalty. The court also noted that an earlier permitting approach had violated EU law.","q":"How would a historical institutionalist read this?","o":["The penalty changes the state's payoffs, so a rational state will now comply with the target","Shared meanings of farming and nature explain resistance, so new narratives must come first","Evolved rural practices cannot be quickly reshaped, so a top-down target is likely to fail","The question is whether the ruling becomes a critical juncture that breaks a long path"],"a":3,"e":"Historical institutionalism looks for path dependence (years of permits granted under earlier rules) and asks whether a moment like a court ruling opens a critical juncture. The payoff reading is rational choice, the meanings reading sociological, and the evolved-practices reading evolutionary."},{"t":"ni","w":4,"s":"A Dutch city turns a former harbour into a new district. Old cranes are restored, festivals celebrate 'from shipbuilding to creative making', and investors and residents rally behind the plan.","q":"How would a sociological institutionalist read this?","o":["Symbols linking past and future build consensus and help institute new meaning","The festivals cut investors' information costs and so make deals more credible","A crisis in shipbuilding opened a juncture that set the district on a new path","Branding sells the city to mobile capital and hides who profits from the plan"],"a":0,"e":"This mirrors Savini and Dembski's Amsterdam-Noord: symbolic narratives, acts and artefacts mobilise consensus and enable institutional change; planning is creating meaning. The information-cost reading is rational choice, the juncture reading historical, and the branding reading Harvey's entrepreneurial city."},{"t":"ni","w":4,"s":"In an informal settlement, residents trade plots through handwritten agreements witnessed by a local committee. Newcomers must be introduced by a member, and disputes are settled by an elders' vote.","q":"How would an Ostrom-style rational choice institutionalist read this?","o":["As a path set at the settlement's founding, which later residents cannot escape","As self-organised rules that create credible commitment among self-interested users","As an expression of shared cultural templates that residents have internalised","As exploitation in which committee members extract rents from the poorest residents"],"a":1,"e":"Boundary rules (introduction by a member), information rules (witnessed agreements) and aggregation rules (elders' vote) are self-crafted institutions that overcome the problem of commitment, as in Bouwmeester and Hartmann (2021). The other readings are historical, sociological and Marxist."},{"t":"ni","w":4,"s":"Facing unaffordable housing, a minister proposes one sweeping law that replaces all existing housing, tax and rental rules at once with a newly designed system.","q":"How would an evolutionary institutionalist read this?","o":["As a sound plan, since self-interested actors will adapt to new incentives quickly","As workable only if a deep crisis first opens a window for such a radical change","As a risky taxis-style design of rules that evolved and store dispersed knowledge","As futile unless new meanings of home and tenure are first embedded in practice"],"a":2,"e":"Moroni's evolutionary view holds that most institutions are unintended outcomes of slow evolution and cannot be designed like organisations; he favours modest, gradual change of general rules rather than taxis-oriented engineering. The other readings are rational choice, historical and sociological."},{"t":"olson","w":3,"s":"In an Amsterdam housing block of 400 flats with a neglectful landlord, only three tenants run the tenants' committee. When the committee starts offering free legal advice on rent disputes to members only, membership rises sharply.","q":"How would Hindmoor's account of Olson read this?","o":["Tenants finally became conscious of their shared class position against the landlord","The tenants' interests were protected by public law, so the committee became redundant","Members came to see joining as the appropriate thing that good tenants simply do","A positive selective incentive, offered by a few entrepreneurs, overcame free-riding"],"a":3,"e":"Free legal representation is Olson's own example of a positive selective incentive, and the three organisers act as entrepreneurs who solve the second-order problem (pp. 116–117). Class consciousness is a Marxist reading; appropriateness is sociological institutionalism."},{"t":"olson","w":3,"s":"Organisers of a protest against a bridge closure fear that nobody will come. A well-known local baker and her friends say they will march whatever happens; once about a third of the town joins, others follow and stay.","q":"How would Hindmoor read this?","o":["As proof that Olson was wrong, because people do act for their group interest","As a privileged group, because the baker pays the full cost of the bridge","As a few unconditional cooperators helping the group pass a stable threshold","As coercion, since the baker's friends punish everyone who stays at home"],"a":2,"e":"Reaching the threshold from zero is hard; 'the existence of just a few unconditional cooperators might make a significant difference', after which limited cooperation can be stable (pp. 126–127). Hindmoor stresses that successful collective action does not refute Olson."},{"t":"taylor","w":3,"s":"A municipality holds three evenings on a planned urban extension. Almost everyone present owns a home or land nearby; they secure a lower density and a later start. Young households who would buy the future homes were not there.","q":"How would Taylor (2016) read this?","o":["A large latent group failed to organise: each resident free-rode on the others' efforts","The plan followed a path set decades ago; only a real crisis could have changed its direction","Residents re-enacted shared meanings of village life that were taken for granted by all","Insiders with a stake shaped the plan, while outsiders who will bear the costs had no voice"],"a":3,"e":"Participation biased towards existing owners while future residents have no voice is Taylor's insider-outsider problem (p. 260), and lower densities pass costs on to outsiders. Free-riding is Olson, shared meanings sociological institutionalism, and path dependence historical institutionalism."},{"t":"taylor","w":3,"s":"Objections to a town's expansion arrive from two sides. Environmental groups and inner-city residents cite the compact city. Owners of large plots on the edge cite birdsong, dark skies and their rural quiet.","q":"How would Taylor (2016) read the second group?","o":["As evidence that rural owners hold an internalised template of what a village should look like","As people who may be sincere, but whose amenity arguments also align with their property interests","As a class fraction defending the use value of their land against capital's drive for exchange value","As holders of local knowledge that a central plan can never capture, so their view should prevail"],"a":1,"e":"Taylor finds landowner objectors cited local amenity and rural lifestyle, and stresses they may argue in good faith while their altruistic arguments unavoidably align with property interests (p. 266): homevoters and happenstance. The template reading is sociological, the class reading Marxist, the local-knowledge reading Hayekian."},{"t":"dembski","w":4,"s":"A coastal region with a century of shipbuilding and dredging firms markets itself as 'Maritime Delta'. Firms, vocational schools and local festivals already use maritime imagery, and within a few years residents and outsiders use the label as a matter of course.","q":"How would Dembski & Salet explain the label's success?","o":["A housing crisis opened a critical juncture in which a brand coalition chose this label","Firms adopted the label because it cut their marketing and transaction costs","The marker connects to embedded practices and meanings its audience already holds","The label is an unplanned outcome that no actor designed, launched or promoted"],"a":2,"e":"For Dembski & Salet a marker works when its meaning is institutionally embedded and inculcated; here it bends an existing past towards the future. The juncture reading is historical-institutionalist and the cost reading is rational choice."},{"t":"dembski","w":4,"s":"After a factory closes, its site lies empty for years: no group claims it and nobody has settled ideas about what it should be. A collective starts temporary workshops and markets there, which slowly become a recognised meeting place for the whole town.","q":"How would Dembski & Salet read this?","o":["New practices take root in an institutional void and gradually become institutionalised","Trial and error selected a successful use, a spontaneous order that holds dispersed knowledge","The owners free-ride on the collective's work and will capture the uplift in land value","A crisis created permissive conditions, and the collective's ideas were the productive ones"],"a":0,"e":"An under-institutionalised space not subject to struggles over the norm is where 'the art of planning' finds room for new cultural practices to institutionalise. The evolutionary reading is Moroni's; it explains the order by selection, not by new meaning being instituted."},{"t":"dembski","w":4,"s":"A national government merges five villages into one municipality, decrees that it will be called 'Groot-Vechtstreek' and has all signs changed within a month. Years later, residents still introduce themselves by their village names.","q":"How would Dembski & Salet read the slow uptake of the new name?","o":["The old names are locked in by positive feedback from earlier investments","A name imposed by decree lacks inculcation and legitimate 'knowers' locally","Residents free-ride on the old names to avoid the costs of switching identity","The renaming hides the class interests of developers behind a neutral label"],"a":1,"e":"Symbolic markers cannot be used voluntaristically: the meaning must be inculcated by the audience and transmitted by recognised knowers. Positive feedback and lock-in belong to historical institutionalism, not to a meaning-based account."},{"t":"dembski","w":4,"s":"A city stages a large opening event for its new 'Knowledge Quarter', then stops all communication about it. Ten years later the name has faded, and few people use it in daily life.","q":"How would Dembski & Salet explain the fading name?","o":["The rules were changed too often, destroying the information they carried","Too few actors had organised interests to keep lobbying for the name","Without everyday, often unconscious reaffirmation, regional meaning fades","The crisis that opened a window for change had passed before the event"],"a":2,"e":"Following Billig and Paasi, regions must be reaffirmed through conscious and especially unconscious remembrance, or their meaning and mobilising effect disappear. The first option is Moroni's robustness principle, which concerns formal rules, not symbols."},{"t":"dembski","w":4,"s":"A hypothetical state builds a futuristic city from scratch in the desert, promoted with a slogan about a post-oil future. There are no residents yet, and no existing urban practices or local institutions on the site.","q":"How would Dembski & Salet read this project's symbolic ambition?","o":["A high-modernist scheme that ignores local know-how and imposes legibility","A designed order that cannot gather the dispersed knowledge it needs to work","A meaning designed rather than embedded, with no audience practices to carry it","A spectacle in the competition between cities for mobile global capital"],"a":2,"e":"With no audience and no practices to inculcate the meaning, the marker resembles the failed landmarks in the paper. The other readings are Scott's, Moroni's and Harvey's, each plausible from their own perspective."},{"t":"moroni","w":4,"s":"In a city's allotment gardens, rules on watering rotas, plot swaps and shared tools grew up over decades without anyone drafting them, and disputes are rare. A new council wants to replace them with a detailed regulation linked to new targets for food production.","q":"How would Moroni respond to the council's plan?","o":["The rules carry tested knowledge; recognise and improve them instead of inventing new ones","The rules reflect the power of established gardeners, so a crisis is needed to change them","The new rules will work only if gardeners come to believe in their meaning in daily practice","The rules exist because they cut the costs of cooperation, so keep them while they stay cheap"],"a":0,"e":"Evolved rules are a distillate of experience; Moroni's modesty and peculiarity principles warn against subordinating them to set targets. The other readings are historical-institutionalist, sociological and rational choice."},{"t":"moroni","w":4,"s":"Country A has simple, stable building rules on height, nuisance and safety, and over thirty years many unexpected uses appear, such as offices turned into homes and garages into workshops. Country B fixes the use of every plot in detailed plans based on current forecasts.","q":"How would Moroni explain why Country A adapts better?","o":["Country A had a critical juncture whose choices set a more flexible pathway","General rules allow a discovery process that no forecast can replace","Owners in Country A share a culture that accepts informal reuse as normal","Developers in Country A lobby less, so they capture fewer rents from rules"],"a":1,"e":"This is adaptive efficiency: rules that frame regulated competition let the best uses be discovered, since nobody can know them beforehand. The juncture reading is Sorensen's; it explains where a path came from, not why general rules perform better."},{"t":"moroni","w":4,"s":"A national government sets up a new Housing Delivery Agency with a director, a budget and a target of 50,000 homes. Critics in a planning journal call it 'a textbook case of institutional design'.","q":"How would Moroni classify the agency?","o":["Institutional design: the state is redesigning society's basic rules","An institution that will only work once people internalise its meaning","Organisational design: building an arrangement of resources for a goal","A reactive sequence that refines rules set at an earlier critical juncture"],"a":2,"e":"An agency with resources and an explicit goal is an organisation, so designing it is legitimate organisational design. Moroni would object only if the government tried to reshape basic rules such as property or contract around its target."},{"t":"moroni","w":4,"s":"After a scandal, parliament passes a hastily drafted, very detailed building law full of exceptions for named projects. It is amended four times in three years, and developers and residents say they no longer know which rules apply.","q":"How would Moroni read this?","o":["It shows landowners capturing rents by lobbying for rules","It is a reactive sequence of contest after a critical juncture","It fails because the rules lack symbolic meaning for people","It violates openness, knowability and robustness at once"],"a":3,"e":"Case-specific rules break openness, unclear rules break knowability and frequent change breaks robustness. The rent-seeking reading is a rational choice one (as in Taylor): plausible, but not Moroni's explanation."},{"t":"moroni","w":4,"s":"In a fast-growing city, an informal land market has developed its own rules for buying, selling and witnessing plot sales. A donor proposes to replace them in one step with a new cadastre and titling system designed by foreign experts.","q":"How would Moroni assess the donor's proposal?","o":["Titling turns land into a commodity that serves capital, not residents","Reform should build successor institutions gradually on rules that work","A crisis is needed first, to open a window in which titling can be chosen","Titling fails unless residents see the donor as a legitimate knower"],"a":1,"e":"Non-tabula-rasa, modesty and gradualism all point the same way: the existing rules carry knowledge, and reform should repair them step by step. The first option is a Marxist reading and the last a sociological one."},{"t":"sorensen","w":4,"s":"A provincial government elected on a deregulation platform caps developer contributions during an affordability crisis. Developers say this will lower house prices; the city says it will only raise developers' profits and land values.","q":"How would Sorensen read this dispute?","o":["Lower costs will raise supply, so prices fall once developers respond","Frequent rule changes undermine the stable information rules provide","A contest over the rules of the game that redistributes costs and benefits","The cap will work only if developers internalise its meaning in practice"],"a":2,"e":"For Sorensen value capture rules shift costs and benefits between actors, which is why they are fought over; whether cuts lower prices or raise profits is an open empirical question. The first reading is a market (rational choice) argument, the second Moroni's."},{"t":"sorensen","w":4,"s":"Two cities in the same country work under the same planning law. One routinely captures about 60 percent of the uplift from rezoning; the other negotiates about 10 percent.","q":"How would Sorensen explain the difference?","o":["One city evolved more efficient rules through trial and error","Locally contingent institutional histories and practices diverged","Developers in one city are a smaller group and organise better","Residents in one city share a culture that accepts paying more"],"a":1,"e":"As in Toronto versus Vancouver, variation reflects contingent, locally specific institutional histories and the timing of change. The third option is Olson's collective action logic; the first is evolutionary."},{"t":"sorensen","w":4,"s":"After a corruption scandal, a mayor promises to 'scrap the whole planning system'. Ten years later the city still uses density bonuses and infrastructure charges, now under new names and with lower rates.","q":"How would Sorensen read this outcome?","o":["Bounded change within a developmental pathway: names and amounts change","Proof of lock-in: earlier choices made any change at all impossible","Evolution kept the useful rules and quietly discarded the useless ones","The old symbols stayed because residents still believed in their meaning"],"a":0,"e":"Like Ontario's recent laws, the reform changes amounts, timing and names while the basic tools remain: path dependence without lock-in. The lock-in option is tempting but ignores that real change did happen."},{"t":"sorensen","w":4,"s":"Municipalities in a federal country complain that the provincial government rewrites their planning powers almost every year, often without consulting them, and they lobby hard against each new bill.","q":"How would Sorensen interpret this situation?","o":["Municipalities fail to organise because their interests are too diffuse","Rules changed this often cannot embody the knowledge of generations","Multilevel governance lets senior governments trigger institutional change","The province is building a shared meaning that municipalities must absorb"],"a":2,"e":"Municipalities as 'creatures of the province' sit in a multilevel system where senior governments can change the rules by law, which makes urban institutions prone to critical junctures and constant contest. The second option is Moroni's robustness concern."},{"t":"sorensen","w":4,"s":"After an earthquake in a hypothetical country, expectations of sweeping reform are high. A newly elected majority passes a modest law that no party had proposed, with an ambiguous clause on who pays for new infrastructure.","q":"How would Sorensen read this law?","o":["A designed order that cannot gather the dispersed knowledge it needs","A critical juncture whose contingent, ambiguous choice will be contested","A rite of institution that consecrates a new boundary for the region","A small group capturing rents at the expense of a large diffuse public"],"a":1,"e":"Crisis gives permissive conditions, the majority's unexpected choice reflects productive conditions and contingency, and the ambiguity will fuel a reactive sequence of contest, as with Ontario's 1946 Act. The first option is Moroni's reading."},{"t":"fog","w":6,"s":"A growing city wants to pay for a new tram line by claiming part of the value increase on land next to the future stops. Landowners along the route, who stand to gain most, launch a well-funded campaign against the levy, while business associations quietly agree that the tram is needed.","q":"How would Foglesong read this conflict?","o":["As the property contradiction: land value is socially produced by the tram, yet private owners resist sharing it.","As rent-seeking by insiders: a small, concentrated group of owners lobbies to keep windfalls at outsiders' cost.","As a critical juncture: the tram debate opens a brief window that will set a long pathway for land value capture.","As an evolved institution: secure property rights embody dispersed knowledge and should not be redesigned at will."],"a":0,"e":"Foglesong would see socially produced value (from public transit) colliding with private ownership, with capital collectively needing the tram but individual owners resisting the claim. The rent-seeking option is Taylor's rational choice reading of a very similar case."},{"t":"fog","w":6,"s":"After a referendum in which a majority voted to take large rental portfolios into public ownership, the government appoints a panel of legal and economic experts. The panel takes two years, and its report is then followed by a framework law with further procedural steps before anything happens.","q":"How would Foglesong read the government's response?","o":["As the danger Hayek saw: assemblies agree a plan is needed but not on its ends, so power slides to experts.","As a collective action problem: tenants are many and dispersed, so they free-ride and cannot sustain pressure.","As containment of democracy: when majorities push control over property too far, procedure slows them down.","As governmentality: tenants are turned into responsible, self-governing subjects audited through targets."],"a":2,"e":"This is the capitalism–democracy contradiction: democratic control over property went further than capital accepts, and the state contained it through expert bodies and procedure. The Hayek option also involves experts, but Hayek sees expert rule as the road to arbitrary power, not as protection of property against majorities."},{"t":"fog","w":6,"s":"In an early twentieth-century American city, leading merchants and bankers fund a grand civic centre plan with boulevards and public buildings. The plan is presented as a gift to all citizens, but it also clears slums near the business district and raises property values there.","q":"How would Foglesong read this plan?","o":["As interurban competition: the city builds an attractive image to lure mobile capital away from rival cities.","As planning serving capital's collective needs while legitimising them as the interest of all citizens.","As a symbolic marker whose new meaning depends on whether it becomes embedded in residents' mental maps.","As high modernism: a legible, geometric order imposed from above that ignores the city's lived complexity."],"a":1,"e":"Foglesong's book traces episodes such as the City Beautiful movement through the contradictions of capitalist urbanisation; read that way, the plan coordinates land for capital while presenting it as the public interest (legitimation). Interurban competition for mobile capital is Harvey's 1989 entrepreneurialism, a later and different logic."},{"t":"fog","w":6,"s":"A city that owns most of its land and leases it out faces pressure from homeowners who want to buy their plots outright. City officials reply that the lease system lets the public recoup value increases that the city's own investments have created.","q":"How would Foglesong read this debate?","o":["As two ways the state protects private interests in land: planning rules versus rights enforced in court.","As a small, concentrated group of homeowners who organise more easily than the dispersed public interest.","As an efficient property institution evolving gradually that planners should leave to spontaneous adjustment.","As a struggle over the social character of land: public control of created value versus private ownership."],"a":3,"e":"The officials defend public capture of socially produced value, the owners want private ownership and control: the property contradiction. The first option is Needham's view of planning and property rights as two ways of protecting private interests, a different (institutional-economic) lens."},{"t":"harvey","w":6,"s":"A national government announces billions for housing, rail and talent programmes in one high-tech region. The money is meant to keep a large chip-machine maker and its suppliers in the country; the firm gives no binding commitment, but ministers say the country must stay 'an attractive location'.","q":"How would Harvey (1989) read this?","o":["As entrepreneurial public real estate policy: the state sells public land to the firm to raise maximum revenue for its budget.","As competition acting as a coercive law: the public sector absorbs costs and risks to anchor mobile capital in the region.","As a rational governmental model: experts set clear goals, compare alternatives and choose the option with the highest net benefit.","As de-contextualisation: layered financial investors far away decide on land use without regard for local needs in the region."],"a":1,"e":"For Harvey, inter-urban (and inter-regional) competition for mobile capital forces public bodies to offer packages and absorb risk, with returns that are expected rather than guaranteed. The rational governmental reading treats this as neutral optimisation and misses the competitive pressure behind it."},{"t":"harvey","w":6,"s":"A medium-sized city hosts a large international music contest and a year of festivals. City marketing reports record numbers of visitors and residents say they feel proud of their city. In the same year, food bank use in the outer districts rises sharply.","q":"How would Harvey (1989) respond to the city's success story?","o":["The festivals are symbolic markers that can institute new social meaning and so slowly change the city's institutional rules.","The city should let visitors and residents choose freely; as long as everyone has equal rights, the outcome is just by definition.","The spectacle reinvents bread and circuses: image builds civic pride and social control while the underlying problems deepen.","The festivals show that collaborative deliberation among stakeholders can build a shared consensus about the city's future."],"a":2,"e":"Harvey reads urban spectacle as image over substance: it creates civic pride and place-bound identity and opens mechanisms of social control, while a 'dual city' of regeneration and impoverishment grows (pp. 13–16). The symbolic-marker reading belongs to Dembski & Salet and misses his critique of social control."},{"t":"harvey","w":6,"s":"The mayor of a declining port city builds a coalition with the university, unions and housing associations. She proposes a pact with five other port cities: none of them will offer tax breaks to lure firms from the others, and they will lobby the national government together.","q":"How would Harvey (1989) assess this initiative?","o":["It cannot work, because each city gains by breaking the pact secretly, so free-riding will undermine any shared agreement.","It is a step towards progressive urban corporatism: inter-urban linkage that mitigates competition and widens political horizons.","It is irrelevant, because the coercive laws of competition leave cities no room at all to act in their own interest.","It is a typical growth coalition that will use the pact mainly to raise land values for developers and financiers."],"a":1,"e":"Harvey hopes for a geopolitical strategy of inter-urban linkage that mitigates competition and shifts horizons beyond the locality, building on cities as 'important power bases' (p. 16). The 'no room at all' option overstates his structuralism: he explicitly sees potential for progressive corporatism."},{"t":"harvey","w":6,"s":"To win a large logistics centre, a city council, the regional hospital and the local university agree on local wage deals below the national sector agreements for new support staff. The council calls it a 'smart regional labour package'.","q":"How would Harvey (1989) interpret this package?","o":["As a sign that the region's high-quality labour supply is a public good that the firm will inevitably under-provide.","As a necessary step towards the national Keynesian compromise that protects workers in every region equally.","As a critical juncture in which an external shock opens a window for lasting reform of local labour institutions.","As the locality becoming a site of labour regulation: local bargaining replaces national bargaining to attract capital."],"a":3,"e":"Harvey notes that local costs are most easily controlled when local replaces national collective bargaining and public institutions lead with wage restraint, which supports flexible accumulation (pp. 9, 12). The critical-juncture reading borrows historical institutionalism and ignores the competitive logic he stresses."},{"t":"hh","w":6,"s":"A national government turns its building agency into a commercial state real estate company. When a growing city wants to buy surplus state land for housing, the company demands the price of serviced building land, while the city insists on paying the price of raw land because it will make the plan.","q":"How would Hyötyläinen & Haila read this conflict?","o":["As a collective action problem: the many small buyers cannot organise against one large, concentrated seller.","As an evolved institution: the price conflict is a spontaneous adjustment that will select efficient property rules.","As de-contextualisation: distant shareholders of the state company decide on land without regard for local needs.","As a state turned real estate player: it chases land rent, loses impartiality and claims value belonging to the city."],"a":3,"e":"This mirrors the Finnish case: a state company demanded developed-land prices and Helsinki defended its planning monopoly; the state 'lost its impartiality' (pp. 138, 143). The evolved-institution reading is Moroni's, which would not see a loss of public impartiality as the core problem."},{"t":"hh","w":6,"s":"An alderman defends selling a prime waterfront to the highest bidder for luxury apartments without social housing. 'A good city has different kinds of areas,' she says, 'luxury ones and rough ones. Residents should be free to choose; that is real diversity.'","q":"How would Hyötyläinen & Haila respond?","o":["They would endorse it as a rights-based Free City: equal rights to choose are enough, and patterns of outcome do not matter.","They would see 'diversity' justifying a two-sided policy: let some areas get rough and privatise public land for the wealthy.","They would call it rent-seeking: the alderman is captured by a small, concentrated group of developers lobbying for gains.","They would read it as a symbolic marker that will gradually institute new shared meaning about mixing in the city."],"a":1,"e":"The authors criticise scholars who say 'a good city can have different types of areas, luxury ones and rough ones', arguing such statements justify a two-sided policy of rough areas plus owner-occupied housing on privatised land (pp. 139–140). The Free City reading comes from Buitelaar's framework, not from the authors' critique."},{"t":"hh","w":6,"s":"In a municipal tender for valuable building land, a newcomer investment fund outbids three long-established local builders by several million euros. The builders' bids are suspiciously close to each other, and the municipal land department argues for selling to a familiar builder 'with more experience'.","q":"How would Hyötyläinen & Haila interpret the stakes?","o":["The real question is who captures the land rent and value increase created by public development rights.","The fund will set up special-purpose vehicles whose gains flow to distant shareholders far from the site.","The builders face a free-rider problem: each would gain from a cartel but none can enforce it on the others.","The department is a bureau maximising its own budget, so it prefers the bidder that keeps its staff busy."],"a":0,"e":"In the Apollo controversy the authors suggest established developers were used to undervaluing land to extract the value increase belonging to the city, so the fight was over land rent, a question 'sidelined' by talk of experience (pp. 141, 143). The SPV reading belongs to Savini & Aalbers."},{"t":"hh","w":6,"s":"After a deep recession a national government cuts its spending and shifts youth care and elderly care to municipalities. Politicians and newspapers repeat that 'there is simply no money', and several cities start selling municipal buildings and land, saying they have no alternative.","q":"How would Hyötyläinen & Haila read this situation?","o":["Responsibilisation: residents are made to govern themselves through community targets and performance audits.","A critical juncture: the crisis opened a short window in which a few actors locked in a lasting policy pathway.","Welfare state to welfare cities, with the necessity to save as a strong discourse that makes market rule seem natural.","Rational choice: each city sells because the market price signals the true opportunity cost of holding the land."],"a":2,"e":"The Finnish state cut spending and shifted services to municipalities, so 'the welfare state began turning into welfare cities', while 'the necessity to save' became a Bourdieuian 'strong discourse' naturalising market rule (p. 139). Responsibilisation is Raco & Imrie's governmentality lens, focused on citizens rather than public land."},{"t":"savini","w":6,"s":"Construction of a landmark tower in a German port city stops halfway because its developer, the subsidiary of a distant, heavily indebted property group, stops paying the builder after interest rates rise. The city now negotiates over a half-built asset whose future depends on the group's creditors.","q":"How would Savini & Aalbers read this situation?","o":["As interurban competition: the city built a flagship to attract mobile capital and now carries the public risk.","As de-contextualisation: a local landmark's fate is decided by distant financial layers, not by local needs.","As a critical juncture: the crisis opens a short window that will lock in a new pathway for the waterfront.","As a symbolic marker that failed because its meaning never became embedded in residents' mental maps."],"a":1,"e":"Decisions made in a distant, layered investment structure, driven by debt and interest rates, determine what happens to a local project: de-contextualisation, as with Risanamento's bankruptcy in Falck. Harvey's interurban competition fits flagship strategies, but nothing here says the city took on the investment risk."},{"t":"savini","w":6,"s":"A Dutch city with a long tradition of active land policy considers selling a large public site to an international fund that would develop it through a chain of project companies. Officials argue that the city lacks the money to develop the land itself.","q":"What would Savini & Aalbers advise?","o":["Leave property rules general and stable, and let the use of the site evolve through decentralised adjustment.","Recognise that the fund and city share an interest, but dispersed residents will free-ride on any opposition.","Focus on the site's symbolic meaning, since a strong landmark only works if residents take it to heart.","Combine strong regulation with proactive public intervention, to keep investment tied to local needs."],"a":3,"e":"The authors warn that layered ownership detaches decisions from local needs and recommend strong regulation plus proactive capacity to intervene in land development. The first option is Moroni's evolutionary, kosmos-oriented advice, which trusts general rules over active public steering."},{"t":"savini","w":6,"s":"A small municipality cannot assess a developer's financial model for a brownfield. A consultant hired by the developer says the programme must be twice as dense to be viable, and the council, afraid of another decade of vacancy, approves extra floors.","q":"How would Savini & Aalbers interpret this?","o":["Evidence competes with interests, so the consultant's figures are one claim among many in a political arena.","Concentrated landowners rent-seek as insiders and shift the costs of development onto future homebuyers.","Detached calculations leave a weak council unable to judge viability, so planning bends to unlock capital.","Planners deliberately design new institutions to reach politically defined goals for the brownfield site."],"a":2,"e":"Cities that do not grasp detached economic calculations cannot define a viable programme, and a yield-dependent council approves higher volumes: planning becomes 'functional to the urgent need to unlock capital' (p. 14). The first option is Davoudi's critique of evidence-based policy, a different question about knowledge in policy."},{"t":"savini","w":6,"s":"A stalled private redevelopment on a former factory site only moves forward after the regional government commits several hundred million euros to build a hospital and research campus on part of the site. The council then approves a plan with more retail and offices than before.","q":"How would Savini & Aalbers read the regional investment?","o":["As public investment used to unlock capital tied up in a financialised project, not to answer local needs.","As a reactive sequence after a critical juncture, contesting the pathway of value capture set decades ago.","As advanced liberal government at a distance, steering communities through targets, audits and bids.","As planning protecting identifiable private interests in land, one of two ways the state can protect them."],"a":0,"e":"In Falck the region's €323 million health campus boosted negotiations and helped the council approve a plan with more retail and offices (p. 13): instrumental planning serving the release of capital. The reactive-sequence option is Sorensen's historical institutionalism, which looks at long pathways rather than financial layering."},{"t":"bossuyt","w":7,"s":"A group of Amsterdam renters wants to form a housing cooperative in a vacant municipal building. The city offers the building only if each household buys its own flat with an individual mortgage, which is the only legal form on offer.","q":"How would Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano read the city's offer?","o":["As welcome security: individual titles protect households and give them the freedom that only private property can offer","As integration: households are drawn into the wider movement's struggle by joining protests to earn allocation points","As protection that is only partial: secure tenure, but individual titles and debt open new routes to enclosure","As cooptation of a squatters' movement proper, which is immune because squatting is both its means and its end"],"a":2,"e":"The authors would see regularization through individual titles as protective against eviction yet a route to commodification, since collective claims become private property and debt; they would call for legal forms for collective property. The 'welcome security' option is Hayek's reading."},{"t":"bossuyt","w":7,"s":"Activists occupy an empty office block in Rotterdam. They immediately change the locks, clean the floors, set rules on who may enter and take night shifts to guard the building, while telling the press they are 'occupying, not invading'.","q":"How would Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano interpret this behaviour?","o":["As appropriation with care: maintaining the building builds legitimacy, a resource against eviction","As terminal institutionalization: convention has replaced disruption, so the movement is ending","As a technology of government: the state trains activists to monitor themselves against targets","As rent-seeking: the activists capture a valuable asset for private use at the expense of the owner"],"a":0,"e":"In the appropriation stage occupiers care for the building and set rules of access; this care is strategic, since legitimacy, following de Angelis, protects against eviction. Terminal institutionalization is Pruijt's concept, and here disruption has not ended."},{"t":"bossuyt","w":7,"s":"Years after completion, a self-managed housing project installs gates and a doorman, stops admitting new members from the movement and fills vacancies with relatives of residents.","q":"How would Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano read this development?","o":["As successful restoration: physical barriers keep the building safe, so the commons is now fully protected","As commodification: residents sell their units at market prices, which turns the homes into investments","As cooptation: the state takes over the project's self-governance through municipal administrative rules","As a slide towards marginalization: protecting the commons turns into excluding outsiders from it"],"a":3,"e":"Marginalization is a self-selecting commons shutting outsiders out; the authors note that integrating new members counters it, so protection can tip into exclusion. Gates alone are restoration, but the shift in membership is the key point, and no sale or state takeover is described."},{"t":"bossuyt","w":7,"s":"In a Dutch collective self-build project, households earn priority for choosing their flat by putting in building hours and attending meetings. Members say they knew each other well by the time they moved in.","q":"How would Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano read this allocation system?","o":["As proof that only private rewards drive participation, so solidarity is an illusion","As an effort-based points system that curbs free-riding and builds a social fabric","As a growth coalition in which landowners pool resources to steer local development","As a regime of truth that makes deregulated development appear as plain common sense"],"a":1,"e":"The effort-based points system rewards participation, prevents free-riding and helps build the 'social fabric' residents describe, cultivating solidarity. The first option is a hard-line Olsonian reading that the authors do not share."},{"t":"gurran","w":7,"s":"A province's consultation on a new housing strategy receives 3,000 responses from residents and about 20 from developers. The final strategy adopts the developers' vocabulary throughout: 'unlocking' sites, removing 'barriers' and protecting 'viability'.","q":"How would Gurran and Ruming (2016) read this?","o":["Collective action: small, concentrated groups organise more easily than large diffuse ones, so they win","Communicative failure: the process lacked good facilitation, so the better argument could not prevail","Influence is not counted in submissions: one framing became a regime of truth fixing acceptable solutions","Capital accumulation: the state serves capital's need to invest surplus in the built environment of cities"],"a":2,"e":"In NSW the community made 72% of submissions and the industry 1%, yet the industry framing was adopted; Gurran and Ruming explain this through regimes of truth that define acceptable problems and solutions. The collective-action reading is Olson's and counts organisation, not discourse."},{"t":"gurran","w":7,"s":"Residents' groups from many towns join forces, stage a striking media stunt and lobby swing MPs. A deregulation bill stalls in parliament, even though developers' views dominated the formal consultation.","q":"How would Gurran and Ruming read this?","o":["An urban social movement: a lasting mobilisation over collective consumption that expresses class cleavage","Cooptation: the groups are drawn into formal service delivery and so lose their disruptive edge over time","Communities of obligation: the groups win a right to be heard only by accepting duties set from the centre","Influence outside the formal process: homeowners' latent electoral power can still block a dominant discourse"],"a":3,"e":"This mirrors the Better Planning Network: its influence 'was exerted outside the government's contained and formal policy-making processes', and conservative homeowners' latent electoral power preserved the status quo. Cooptation (Pruijt) would mean the groups were absorbed, not that they blocked the bill."},{"t":"gurran","w":7,"s":"A city council boasts record numbers of building permits, yet rents and house prices keep rising. The alderman concludes that even more permits and fewer rules will solve the problem.","q":"How would Gurran and Ruming respond?","o":["As in NSW, more supply need not ease affordability; ask what else drives prices","Prices signal scarcity, so the market should be freed to add still more supply","Containment of land creates dual predictability that rewards land speculators","The city has become entrepreneurial and competes for mobile global capital"],"a":0,"e":"In NSW approvals almost doubled under the old system while Sydney prices rose sharply: 'increased supply did not ease affordability pressures', so they would question the supply narrative and point to demand, investment and social housing. The price-signal reply is Hayekian."},{"t":"pruijt","w":7,"s":"A Dutch city cuts its neighbourhood budgets. It then offers a protest group of tenants a small subsidy to run housing advice and a repair service, on condition that the group stops organising occupations of empty homes.","q":"How would Pruijt read this offer?","o":["As cooptation under a market logic: the group becomes a service provider and drops its disruptive means","As a technology of citizenship that turns passive tenants into self-auditing, responsible active citizens","As fair process in Agyeman's sense, because the tenants now gain a formal voice in planning decisions","As a growth coalition in which the city and tenants pool land and capital to steer development jointly"],"a":0,"e":"Pruijt would see the privatization of poverty: a retreating state co-opts a movement group as a service provider in exchange for abandoning squatting. The 'technology of citizenship' reading is Raco and Imrie's governmentality."},{"t":"pruijt","w":7,"s":"Hypothetical: a country first protects squatters by law and runs a redistributive housing policy, and a large, open squatting scene develops. Later a law makes squatting a criminal offence, after which the scene shrinks and the remaining squatters keep a low profile.","q":"How would Pruijt explain the change?","o":["As proof that squatters were only rational actors who quit once their private gains were taken away","As the end of a growth regime, once landowners lost the power they needed to steer local development","As a shift in the opportunity structure: losing legal protection pushes squatting into covert, isolated forms","As successful agonism, since the opposing sides now accept each other as legitimate adversaries in debate"],"a":2,"e":"Pruijt shows that property protection shapes what squatting can be: house right enabled open, long-lived squats in Amsterdam, while absence of rights in New York pushed squatters into covert action that isolated them. The rational-actor reading is Olsonian."},{"t":"pruijt","w":7,"s":"In a gentrifying district, critics accuse a long-standing squat of attracting artists and cafés that push up rents. The squatters reply that they create no luxury housing and have supported tenants against developers.","q":"How would Pruijt weigh this dispute?","o":["Squatting is a form of gentrification, since squatters are the first wave of capital investment","Squatting has nothing to do with change in a neighbourhood, so the critics are simply mistaken","Squatting causes speculation, so cities should evict squats to protect existing tenants","Squatting is not gentrification, though squatters share traits with pioneers and can slow it at best"],"a":3,"e":"Pruijt sees overlaps (pioneering newcomers rich in cultural capital) but holds that squatting is not gentrification; squatters often sided with residents, and a squatter's verdict was 'you can only slow it down'. Saying squatting has nothing to do with neighbourhood change overcorrects."},{"t":"pruijt","w":7,"s":"Squatters occupy a building that a non-profit developer has claimed for 'affordable' housing. The squatters point out that the planned rents are beyond local incomes, that the developer partners with for-profit firms, and that earlier plans never materialised.","q":"How would Pruijt read this conflict?","o":["As proof that radical squatters always block social housing, so cities should side with developers","As agonism that turns into consensus once both sides deliberate long enough in a formal arena","As a market regime's disorganised poverty industry, where radicals act as watchdogs, though locally","As a tactic by landowners to raise land values by blocking the construction of new housing"],"a":2,"e":"In New York, opaque non-profits in partnerships with for-profit firms competed with squatters for buildings; this gave radicals a watchdog role against co-opted groups, but kept conflict local and left big real estate and politics unaffected. Amsterdam's consensus made such conflicts rare."},{"t":"raco","w":7,"s":"Using the new Dutch 'right to challenge' (uitdaagrecht), a residents' cooperative takes over the maintenance of a local park together with part of its budget. It must report in the municipality's format, and the council decides each year whether the cooperative performed well enough to keep the task.","q":"How would Raco and Imrie (2000) read this?","o":["Spontaneous order: residents coordinate through their own local knowledge, which planners should let evolve freely","Responsibilisation: a right to act comes bundled with duties and reporting, so the council governs at a distance","Collective action: the cooperative needs selective incentives to stop its members free-riding on others' work","Collaborative planning: dialogue between residents and officials builds shared capacity and mutual trust"],"a":1,"e":"Raco and Imrie would see a 'community of obligation': rights to act on condition of duties, with the council setting the reporting format and judging performance, so conduct is shaped from a distance. The spontaneous-order reading is Hayek's or Moroni's, the free-rider one Olson's."},{"t":"raco","w":7,"s":"After budget cuts, a municipality asks residents of a deprived district to set up volunteer neighbourhood care and a cooperative shop, because commercial firms have left and the council will fund only start-up training. Officials call it 'giving the neighbourhood back to its residents'.","q":"How would Raco and Imrie read this?","o":["Uneven development: capital leaves the district to seek higher returns elsewhere in the urban system","A housing commons: residents put use value above exchange value through collective self-governance","An urban regime: council and residents pool resources and so gain the 'power to' govern together","Abandonment presented as inclusion: self-help is required where public and private provision left"],"a":3,"e":"Like SRB's credit unions and community enterprises in areas 'unattractive to the private sector', this is 'public and private sector abandonment' presented as empowerment. The uneven-development reading is Marxist; it explains capital's exit but not how residents are made responsible."},{"t":"rydin6","w":7,"s":"Over several years a province, a tech company and a land-owning agricultural cooperative agree in informal meetings on a large data centre. Residents learn of it only after the zoning is fixed, and their objections about noise and water use are dismissed as NIMBY reactions.","q":"How would Rydin's urban politics frame read this?","o":["As a market failure: the province should have left the site choice to the price system instead of planning it centrally","As a technology of government: the consultation trained residents to act as responsible, self-governing citizens","As a free-rider problem: residents only organise late because each one hopes that the others will bear the costs","As power and inequality: a growth-oriented coalition shaped the decision, while residents were misrecognised and excluded"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin would stress conflict, power in the process and inequality in outcomes: an informal pro-growth coalition with 'power to' act, residents' place attachment recast as NIMBY, and failures of recognition and fair process. The market-failure and free-rider readings are Hayekian and Olsonian."},{"t":"rydin6","w":7,"s":"A city runs an inclusive, open-ended visioning process for climate adaptation. Afterwards, flood rules are enforced strictly in a low-income district but loosely in a wealthy one, and most new pumping capacity ends up in the wealthy area.","q":"How would Rydin, using Agyeman's justice framework, read this?","o":["Justice was done, since the inclusive visioning process gave every group a voice","The outcome reflects path dependence of earlier water institutions, not justice","Fair process alone is not enough: procedures and outcomes were plainly unjust","The case shows the rule of law at work, since the same rules applied to all"],"a":2,"e":"Agyeman's four aspects are separate: a fair visioning process does not guarantee just exercise of procedures or fair outcomes. Treating voice as sufficient is the error, and the rules were not in fact applied evenly."},{"t":"rydin6","w":7,"s":"Tenants in several post-war districts of a Dutch city form a lasting network against demolition and sale of social housing. They occupy empty homes, link up with students and migrant groups, and say the struggle is about who the city is for.","q":"How would Rydin's urban politics frame read this network?","o":["As an urban social movement expressing a social cleavage over housing, with the occupations seen as legitimate protest","As a pluralist interest group that formed because demand for housing rose, so its activism simply follows that rising demand","As illegal disruption that undermines the rule of law and the property rights on which a free society depends","As a group that will soon collapse, because members gain the benefits of its success whether or not they take part"],"a":0,"e":"Urban politics attends to the disempowered, treats non-violent occupations as legitimate protest and reads movements as expressing a cleavage over collective consumption. The pluralist reading is the one Rydin calls naïve; the others are Hayek and Olson."},{"t":"rydin6","w":7,"s":"In a dispute over enlarging a national park, farmers and nature organisations clash bitterly at formal hearings. During a joint field count of meadow birds, however, they exchange views respectfully, though the official decision still rests mainly on expert reports.","q":"How would Rydin, drawing on Fougère and Bond, read this?","o":["The field count shows consensus is near, so planners should now design a deliberative process to settle the conflict","Antagonism can open space for agonism, while relying on expert knowledge keeps the decision largely post-political","The hearings show that conflict harms planning, so planners should keep opponents apart and decide on technical grounds","The farmers act rationally to protect their income, so the conflict will end once they are paid enough compensation"],"a":1,"e":"Like the Denniston Plateau case (antagonistic hearings, agonistic moments around the Bioblitz, expert knowledge depoliticising), this shows antagonism creating room for agonism. The consensus option repeats the governance promise that agonism rejects."},{"t":"alex","w":3,"s":"A Dutch province invites municipalities to 'dialogue tables' on the route of a new rail link. In practice each municipality uses the sessions to push its own station location, and two threaten to withdraw co-funding if they lose.","q":"How would Alexander read this process?","o":["Rydin's governmental model: politicians have set the goal and experts must now pick the optimal route.","Davoudi: the province will use evidence tactically, citing only studies that back a decision already taken.","Strategic action under a communicative label: actors pursue their own goals, as most interaction does.","Hayek: the parties cannot agree on the ends of the plan, so decisions will slide to expert discretion."],"a":2,"e":"Alexander argues that most observed interaction is 'strategic, not communicative': bargaining, threats and exit belong in cell (b), even when the process is framed as dialogue. The governmental reading ignores the interaction itself, which is exactly Alexander's critique of classic rationality."},{"t":"alex","w":3,"s":"A city council decides that every decision, from its internal budget split to a conflict between residents and a festival organiser, must be settled with one cost-benefit analysis, 'so that we are always rational'.","q":"How would Alexander respond?","o":["CBA suits a unit's own deliberation, but conflicts between parties call for models of interaction.","CBA is fine in principle, but evidence will always compete with ideology, interests and institutions.","One plan for all decisions assumes a complete ethical code that no council will ever possess.","A single method seen from above simplifies the city and ignores residents' practical knowledge."],"a":0,"e":"For Alexander no single model covers the whole universe of decision and action: formal rational tools fit individual deliberation (cell d), while a dispute between parties is interaction, strategic or (rarely) consensus-oriented. The second option is Davoudi's critique, the third Hayek's and the fourth Scott's."},{"t":"alex","w":3,"s":"A planning department is told that its rational planning manual no longer matches how decisions are really made. It keeps the manual and its step-by-step model unchanged, but adds a short preface admitting that 'reality is messier'.","q":"How would Alexander classify this reaction?","o":["Hall: planning has lost its priestly clothing, and the manual is a relic of the systems era.","Davoudi: the manual reflects the naive, linear model of research use of 1960s planning.","Rydin (2007): the manual mixes knowledge claims with ethical claims that need other arenas.","The ritual response: keeping the rational model with only token acknowledgement of anomalies."],"a":3,"e":"Alexander's 'ritual' response, widespread in planning education and practice, keeps the rational model 'with only token acknowledgment of its anomalies', exactly like the short preface. Hall describes the historical trajectory of the field rather than this type of response."},{"t":"alex","w":3,"s":"Several municipalities, a province and two developers set up a regional land bank with its own rules on buying, holding and releasing land, so that they stop outbidding each other on the urban fringe.","q":"How would Alexander read this initiative?","o":["Moroni: a taxis-style intervention; reform should rather be modest, general and kosmos-oriented.","Institutional design: rules devised through interaction among parties to reach held objectives.","Dembski and Salet: the land bank works only if its meaning is inculcated in actors' mental maps.","Sorensen: a critical juncture that opens a new developmental pathway for land value capture."],"a":1,"e":"Alexander treats institutional design, devising rules, procedures and organisational structures to achieve held objectives, as part of planning, and places it with coordinative planning in the real, collective, accomplishment-oriented cell (b). The other options are week 4 readings that question or qualify deliberate design."},{"t":"rc","w":3,"s":"A Dutch city plans 600 flats on a car park next to a low-rise neighbourhood. About 200 homeowners form an action group and hire a lawyer to fight the plan, while the thousands of future residents who would benefit are not represented at the hearings.","q":"How would a rational choice theorist read this situation?","o":["Scott: a top-down, legible plan that ignores the practical knowledge of the residents on the ground.","Concentrated owners organise to protect their values, while diffuse future residents stay latent.","Rydin's governmental model: the council should weigh all interests and set the public interest itself.","Marx: the plan turns housing into a commodity, and residents are alienated from the city they make."],"a":1,"e":"Rational choice expects self-interested insiders with concentrated stakes (property values, amenity) to organise, while future residents, a large, dispersed and not yet present group, face a collective action problem. The governmental reading assumes a public interest above actors, which rational choice rejects: the public interest is an aggregate of private ones."},{"t":"rc","w":3,"s":"Six months before municipal elections, an alderman pushes through an expensive new square in the city centre, although officials had advised spending the money on sewer maintenance that voters rarely notice.","q":"How would a rational choice theorist read this decision?","o":["Rydin's governmental model: a neutral administration has chosen the best means for a goal set by politics.","Davoudi: officials' evidence was one input among ideology, interests and institutions in the decision.","Alexander: a pre-interactive deliberation in which one unit ranks options before entering the arena.","State failure: the alderman maximises his own interest, here visible projects that win votes."],"a":3,"e":"Rational choice applies the self-interest assumption to all actors, including politicians and municipalities, and so looks at state failure as well as market failure: a visible square brings more electoral benefit than invisible sewers. Davoudi's reading is plausible too, but it concerns how evidence informs policy, not the alderman's incentives."},{"t":"rc","w":3,"s":"A new metro line raises house and land prices around its stations by 20 to 30 percent. Owners near the stations did not contribute to the line, and the city debates a levy on these gains to help pay for it.","q":"How would a rational choice theorist expect the owners to respond?","o":["They keep the betterment and lobby against the levy, as rent-seekers would.","They accept the levy once its fairness is embedded in shared local norms.","They act as a class whose rents are drawn from labour elsewhere in the city.","They wait for a critical juncture, as land policy follows a set pathway."],"a":0,"e":"The lecture's asymmetry: when planning or infrastructure raises land values, owners act rationally to capture the rent and keep the betterment, so a value capture levy invites lobbying. The critical juncture option reads the case through Sorensen's historical institutionalism, which explains value capture by pathways, not owners' calculations."},{"t":"p1","w":1,"s":"A wealthy Dutch suburb runs a broad consensus process on its new housing plan. Residents, developers and the council agree on spacious energy-neutral villas on farmland at the town edge, and everyone praises the harmony of the process.","q":"How would Næss (2001) read this plan?","o":["A success: the plan balances growth, equity and environment, so it nears the triangle's centre by negotiation.","Consensus proves nothing: if lifestyles are the problem, an agreed plan can still raise land take and floor space.","A case for mapping how the new villas compete, co-exist or complement the existing urban systems.","A civic demonstration that shows other municipalities what a future suburb could look like."],"a":1,"e":"Næss stresses that bottom-up consensus works only when local lifestyles fit sustainability goals; spacious greenfield villas raise land take and per-capita floor space whatever the process. The first option is a Campbell-style reading, the third Hodson et al.'s, the fourth Bulkeley et al.'s."},{"t":"p1","w":1,"s":"A city promotes a new office district built to passive-house standards as proof of its climate leadership, while total office floor space in the region keeps rising every year.","q":"How would Næss (2001) assess this?","o":["A product of the urban turn: the district mainly shows that cities came to be imagined as solutions.","A strategic demonstration whose value lies in displaying technologies to visitors and investors.","Only relatively better: unless new buildings replace worse ones, a growing stock raises total use.","A resolved resource conflict: efficiency per unit reconciles economic growth with protecting nature."],"a":2,"e":"Næss argues that new green buildings are favourable only in a relative sense unless they replace worse ones; as the stock grows, there is more volume to heat and more material used. The resource-conflict reading reflects Campbell's technological path, which Næss finds insufficient."},{"t":"p1","w":1,"s":"In a Dutch province, an environmental NGO, a tenants' union and a cycling federation form a coalition to stop a motorway widening. The road lobby refuses to join any talks.","q":"How would Næss (2001) view the coalition's strategy?","o":["Sensible: build an alliance of groups that share equity and environmental values, not consensus with all.","The planner should mediate between all three corners to find a compromise the road lobby can accept.","A clash of rationalities at an interface where state logic meets the survival logic of the poor.","A struggle between competing governance arrangements that may later co-exist or complement each other."],"a":0,"e":"Næss calls consensus among all stakeholders 'futile' and proposes alliance-building among groups that support the basic equity and environmental values. Mediation between all corners is Campbell's dilemma-based role; the interface reading belongs to de Satgé and Watson."},{"t":"p1","w":1,"s":"A planner is told by the alderman to drop the sustainability chapter from a growth-oriented spatial strategy, because it 'does not fit political priorities'. The municipality has an official climate goal.","q":"What would Næss (2001) advise the planner?","o":["Seek a binding higher-level law with control lines so local politicians cannot alter the plan at will.","Turn the chapter into a living-lab experiment, so sustainability can be tested without formal approval.","Accept it for now, since no planner can stay at the centre of the triangle at all times anyway.","Use the official goals to legitimise impact analysis of alternatives, and push for public monitoring."],"a":3,"e":"Næss advises planners to assess alternatives against sustainability criteria, use official municipal or national goals as legitimation, and wants legislation requiring impact analysis and public monitoring because planners who expose powerful groups risk being called disloyal. The control-line option reflects Liu and Zhou's top-down legal approach."},{"t":"p2","w":1,"s":"A Dutch city wins a 'Green Capital' award for its dense, cycle-friendly centre. The council says the city is 'part of the solution to the climate crisis' and plans a branding campaign around it.","q":"How would Angelo and Wachsmuth (2020) respond?","o":["Ask how the 'city as solution' idea became common sense and whom it serves, not take the claim as given.","Check whether floor space and consumption per person still grow, since density alone is not enough.","See a resolved resource conflict in which growth and environment now complement one another.","Treat the centre as a civic demonstration whose value lies in showing others a possible future."],"a":0,"e":"Angelo and Wachsmuth aim to prevent the shift from 'city as problem' to 'city as solution' from being naturalised, and ask historical and political questions about it. The second option is Næss's per-capita argument, the third a Campbell-style reading, the fourth Bulkeley et al.'s."},{"t":"p2","w":1,"s":"A UN report praises dense informal settlements as 'unexpectedly green' because residents walk, recycle and live in little space, and proposes them as models for European eco-districts.","q":"How would Angelo and Wachsmuth (2020) read this report?","o":["A development conflict in which nature protection clashes with poverty reduction and needs a compromise.","A reconfiguration where informal and formal systems co-exist, to be mapped city by city.","A recent reversal: settlements long framed as environmental problems are recast as models, which needs scrutiny.","Romanticising slum life as vibrant and entrepreneurial hides how the state decides what is legitimate."],"a":2,"e":"The informal-settlement root shows exactly this turn, from Habitat I's problem framing to Brand's 'How slums can save the planet'; the authors want such framings studied, not celebrated. The last option is Roy's critique from lecture 6, the first Campbell's development conflict."},{"t":"p2","w":1,"s":"After a national government withdraws from a climate agreement, forty mayors sign a charter pledging to meet the agreement's targets in their own cities anyway.","q":"How would Angelo and Wachsmuth (2020) interpret this?","o":["Fragmentation: without a superior spatial planning law, local plans conflict with national goals.","The climate root at work: as multilateral treaties floundered, mayors were cast as climate protagonists.","A pro-growth move: city pledges let more development be labelled sustainable while avoiding limits.","Multi-level governance: national arrangements shape urban experiments in ways cities cannot control."],"a":1,"e":"The authors use the Chicago Climate Charter as an example of the climate root: as UNFCCC, Kyoto and Paris faltered, cities and mayors became the hope (ICLEI, C40, Covenant of Mayors). The multi-level option is Hodson et al.'s angle; the pro-growth option is Durrant et al.'s."},{"t":"p2","w":1,"s":"A city's sustainability index ranks a wealthy downtown district as the 'greenest' because of low car use, although its residents fly and consume far more than households in the suburbs.","q":"What would Angelo and Wachsmuth (2020) highlight?","o":["A property conflict: the district's wealth comes at tenants' expense and calls for housing redistribution.","Competing accounts of sustainability co-exist; the task is to map which one becomes dominant.","A data problem: better digital twins and real-time analytics would produce a fairer, more inclusive index.","Dominant urban sustainability is narrow and serves the rich, whose consumption may cancel density gains."],"a":3,"e":"Their political research dimension stresses that mainstream sustainability planning is socially narrow, focused on wealthy residents whose consumption-heavy lifestyles may cancel the carbon savings of efficient downtowns. The mapping option is Hodson et al.'s, the data option Lin et al.'s."},{"t":"p3","w":1,"s":"Utrecht plans a large new park in a low-income district. Residents welcome the greenery but fear rising rents; an alderman says the health and climate benefits justify accepting some displacement.","q":"How would Campbell (1996) frame this situation?","o":["A matter of substance: per-capita resource use matters most, so the park is secondary to curbing floor space.","A sign of how cities came to be cast as sustainability solutions; the task is to historicise that framing.","A clash of deep rationalities with no shared public interest, so negotiation cannot be the starting point.","A conflict between equity and environment, to be confronted and negotiated rather than wished away."],"a":3,"e":"Greening that displaces the poor sits on the equity-environment side (the development conflict, fed by the growth corner), and Campbell insists conflicts be confronted rather than hidden under a harmonious vision. The deep-rationalities option is de Satgé and Watson, who doubt such negotiation is possible."},{"t":"p3","w":1,"s":"A forestry region must choose between protecting old-growth forest that shelters a rare bird and keeping sawmill jobs. Unions and environmental groups frame it as 'jobs versus environment'.","q":"What would Campbell (1996) point out?","o":["The frame hides equity: workers, firms and communities are all lumped together as 'economy'.","Competing accounts of sustainability are at play; the analyst should map which becomes dominant.","Growth-dependence must end: planners should voice that expanding the sawmill is infeasible.","An ecological red line should be drawn by decree so the forest is strictly protected."],"a":0,"e":"Campbell argues the popular 'jobs versus environment' (spotted owl versus timber) framing hides the third dimension, social equity, because it lumps workers, corporations and communities together under 'economy'. The red-line option reflects Liu and Zhou's top-down control lines."},{"t":"p3","w":1,"s":"A regional council adopts a 'Sustainable Development Master Plan' with ambitious goals for growth, nature and social justice, but says nothing about how conflicts between these goals will be handled.","q":"How would Campbell (1996) judge this plan?","o":["A good start, provided its goals are substantive and backed by an alliance of like-minded groups.","Useful once a superior law prevents later changes and a single map unifies all the plan data.","Likely a glib wish list: the centre is reached only by confronting conflicts, not by one leap.","A civic demonstration that will show other councils what a sustainable future could look like."],"a":2,"e":"Campbell predicts that 'Sustainable Development Master Plans' risk becoming glib wish lists, like 1950s comprehensive planning for one public interest; the centre is approached only indirectly, through negotiation over land use, transport and housing. The alliance option is Næss's strategy."},{"t":"p3","w":1,"s":"Tenant groups and an environmental justice network jointly campaign against an incinerator planned in a poor neighbourhood. The operator offers 200 local jobs in return.","q":"How would Campbell (1996) read this case?","o":["A port-city interface claim, to be structured by level and by type of claim: urban, environmental, economic.","An example of 'more city' thinking replacing 'less city' solutions in the history of sustainability.","A case for an online participation platform that makes the siting decision efficient and inclusive.","The development conflict, where environmental racism sits; equity and environment planners should ally."],"a":3,"e":"Campbell uses exactly this no-win choice between jobs and noxious land uses to show that environmental racism lies at the heart of the development conflict, and urges community development and environmental planners to work together. The first option is Witte et al.'s framework, the third Lin et al.'s."},{"t":"p4","w":2,"s":"A Dutch port city plans 5,000 homes on an active inner-city port site. The port authority wants the same quays for hydrogen, and the water board wants part of the area for a retention basin. Each plan fits on paper if the others are ignored.","q":"How would Witte, Wiegmans and Louw (2025) read this situation?","o":["Several systems are being reconfigured at once, and their experiments may compete, co-exist or complement.","A growth-dependent plan: set ceilings on new homes and divert building to lower-priced, high-need areas.","Proof that the city has become the self-evident scale at which global sustainability is to be solved.","Cumulative claims exceed the land even if each fits alone; map them by level and by type of claim."],"a":3,"e":"This is 'more claims than land': net claims may fit, but cumulative urban, environmental and economic claims do not, so conflicts should be structured by level (region, interface, waterfront) and type. The first option is Hodson et al.'s reconfiguration lens, the second Durrant et al.'s post-growth tools."},{"t":"p4","w":2,"s":"A Dutch municipality invites residents, housing associations and shop owners to co-design a neighbourhood plan long before any legal procedure. Months later the council adopts the outcome in its Omgevingsplan with hardly any changes.","q":"How would Witte (lecture 4) read this approach?","o":["An 'alongside' strategy: informal first, law codifies later; it promises co-creation but risks tokenism.","An interface where a techno-managerial state and residents' survival rationalities meet and clash.","A civic demonstration lab that shows what the neighbourhood could look like under the right conditions.","A property conflict between growth and equity, to be confronted by negotiation over land use."],"a":0,"e":"Open planning ('polderen') involves stakeholders early to build draagvlak; the formal plan comes 'towards the end' as codification. Witte's balancing act names its promise (co-creation, less conflict) and risk (tokenism, unequal power). The interface reading is de Satgé and Watson's, which doubts that consensus is the starting point."},{"t":"p4","w":2,"s":"Council members in a port city complain that they cannot steer decisions on noise and land leases. The port authority is a company fully owned by the municipality and the state, and it leases out the port land.","q":"How would Witte et al. (2025) and lecture 4 read this complaint?","o":["A failure of digital transparency that a shared data platform and online participation could fix.","Port authorities are public companies leasing land, not decision bodies, so accountability goes grey.","An example of authoritarian deliberation: participation is legalised but comes late and has little effect.","A rebound effect: efficiency gains in port operations are spent on more activity and more noise."],"a":1,"e":"The article notes both port authorities are publicly owned companies that lease land, not public decision-making bodies, so the council has little direct influence; lecture 4 warns about 'grey accountability' and meta-governance. Authoritarian deliberation is Lin's term for state-bounded participation in China."},{"t":"p4","w":2,"s":"Port A is hemmed in by the city and decides to phase out fossil fuel terminals to free land for hydrogen. Port B, with room on reclaimed land at the coast, adds new energy firms next to its modernised petrochemical cluster.","q":"How would Witte et al. (2025) explain the difference?","o":["Different conceptions of sustainability compete, and each port picks the account its coalition promotes.","Port A shows strong sustainability and Port B weak sustainability, since B substitutes capital for nature.","'Puzzling space' differs: confined ports must replace uses, roomier ones can follow an 'and-and' approach.","Port B is a strategic living lab that tests technologies, while Port A follows a civic, municipal agenda."],"a":2,"e":"This mirrors Amsterdam (replace: fossil phase-out frees about 370 ha) and Rotterdam ('and-and': new energy beside petrochemicals). Ports with more 'puzzling space' can adapt more easily than confined ones. The first option is Hodson et al.'s competing sustainabilities, which ignores the land constraint the article stresses."},{"t":"p5","w":2,"s":"For years the government postpones a decision on a hamlet next to an expanding industrial zone. No one formally orders residents to leave, but young families move away, shops close and empty houses are demolished one by one.","q":"How would Witte's lecture 5 typology read this?","o":["Freeze: no clear choice is made, and the village slowly disappears through uncertainty and decline.","A development conflict between equity and environment that planners must confront by negotiation.","An interface where the state's techno-economic rationality clashes with residents' place attachment.","A 'majority tyranny' risk, where a sustainability goal is used to override a local minority."],"a":0,"e":"Under 'freeze' the village disappears without being moved as a whole; indecision and uncertainty hollow it out, which is what the Moerdijk news of 2025 to 2026 showed. The interface reading would be de Satgé and Watson's, the majority-tyranny worry is Næss's."},{"t":"p5","w":2,"s":"A national programme needs about 700 hectares around a port village for cable routes, a high-voltage station and new industry. Residents hang posters saying 'Save our village', and an official calls them NIMBYs.","q":"How would Witte (lecture 5) frame the debate?","o":["Set a national ceiling on energy infrastructure and redistribute it to regions with lower demand.","Use the three control lines so that farmland and ecology are protected before industry expands.","Treat it as evidence that cities, not villages, are now the scale at which sustainability is solved.","Ask if more than NIMBYism is at stake: who decides, and is staying, moving or compensation just?"],"a":3,"e":"The discussion slide asks exactly this: 'A typical case of NIMBYism, or is there more at stake? With whom should be the initiative and final decision?' and what a just outcome would be. The control lines belong to China's territorial spatial planning (Liu and Zhou)."},{"t":"p6","w":2,"s":"A European consultancy sells a fast-growing African city a ready-made package: a water-square design and a polder-style participation toolkit that worked in Rotterdam. The ministry adopts it citywide within a year, without local pilots.","q":"How would de Satgé and Watson (2018) read this?","o":["An 'alongside' strategy that builds draagvlak by involving stakeholders early, as Dutch planning does.","A best practice 'landing' with little check of fit; ask for its origin narrative and preconditions.","A resource conflict between economic growth and environmental protection, to be negotiated.","Growth-dependent infrastructure that should come with a planned phase-out of older networks."],"a":1,"e":"Concepts 'tend to land ... with little preliminary investigation of their potential fit', often with negative outcomes; the authors endorse Healey's 'origin narratives' that state the context in which an idea worked. The first option is Witte's description of Dutch practice, the very thing being exported."},{"t":"p6","w":2,"s":"Officials running a housing megaproject skip community engagement, saying an elected government already knows what residents need. On site, long-time residents, backyard tenants, hostel dwellers and newcomers fight over who gets the new units.","q":"How would de Satgé and Watson (2018) read this case?","o":["An alongside process whose co-creation slid into tokenism and unequal power relations.","A property conflict between growth and equity that a skilled planner could negotiate.","State techno-managerial rationality meets fragmented resident rationalities at the interface.","Co-governance stuck at the lower rungs of information and consultation, as in many cities."],"a":2,"e":"This is the N2 Gateway pattern: officials discouraged engagement because a democratic government 'knows what the people want', and residents' claims were granular and conflicting, which the state's megaproject logic failed to read. Campbell's property conflict assumes negotiable interests; here the clash is between world views."},{"t":"p6","w":2,"s":"A planning school in an African country still teaches European zoning codes. Its graduates write master plans that assume households own cars, hold formal land titles and pay property tax, and most of these plans are never implemented.","q":"How would de Satgé and Watson (2018) explain the failure?","o":["Planning is 'not a view from nowhere': plans built on wrong assumptions about households and land fail.","A digital skills gap: planners need training in data analytics before plans can be implemented.","Weak correspondence: national systems such as automobility cannot be shaped at the city scale.","Deskilling: reliance on standard outputs has displaced the planners' accumulated judgement."],"a":0,"e":"Because planning must act, bad theory has direct effects: African cities are 'littered' with failed plans based on wrong assumptions about household strategies, car ownership, land and institutional capacity (p. 16); Porter's phrase makes the same point. Deskilling is Li and Dang's argument about AI."},{"t":"p7","w":2,"s":"In a Chinese county, the land-use plan marks a field as protected farmland, the urban plan marks it for construction, and the transport plan uses yet another base map. Each plan has its own ministry and planning period.","q":"How would Liu and Zhou (2021) read this situation?","o":["Different rationalities of state and residents meet at an interface where issues are rendered technical.","Several governance arrangements co-exist, loosely coupled, without one of them being dominant.","Cumulative claims exceed the land even if net claims fit; classify them by level and type of claim.","Fragmentation without a superior law: fix it with 'multiple plans in one', One Map and control lines."],"a":3,"e":"Different classifications made the same plot carry multiple uses; the NTSP answers with one ministry, one system, a single 'One Map' and three control lines. The 'more claims than land' option is Witte et al.'s port-city lens, which assumes land scarcity rather than plan fragmentation."},{"t":"p7","w":2,"s":"A new Chinese planning law requires draft plans to be published and public hearings to be held. Plans are still drawn up from the top down within national control lines, and residents are consulted once the draft is finished.","q":"How would Liu and Zhou (2021) evaluate this law?","o":["A milestone: governing space by law ends constant revisions and requires public participation.","Authoritarian deliberation: participation is legalised but late, limited and marked by unequal power.","A best practice imported with little check of its fit, likely to harm those who cannot conform.","A pre-figurative display that showcases a sustainable future without any route to scaling up."],"a":0,"e":"The authors call the law 'epoch-making': it fills the lack of top-level design, stops plans changing without legal procedure and emphasises participation through publication and hearings. The second option is lecture 7's more critical reading (Lin), which Liu and Zhou, writing from inside the policy world, do not make."},{"t":"p7","w":2,"s":"A fast-growing Chinese city wants to expand onto wetlands and high-quality farmland at its edge. Local officials argue the land sales would fund new metro lines.","q":"How would Liu and Zhou (2021) respond?","o":["Set absolute ceilings on growth and redirect building to cheaper areas with higher housing need.","The red line and permanent farmland bar it; the urban boundary limits where the city may grow.","Treat it as a resource conflict between growth and environment, to be negotiated case by case.","Exnovate the lock-in practices of siloed departments that block green space planning."],"a":1,"e":"The three control lines are the heart of the NTSP: the ecological protection red line, permanent basic farmland and the urban development boundary, protecting ecology and food security and preventing sprawl. Campbell's negotiation of the resource conflict is the opposite style: here the conflict is settled by binding lines."},{"t":"p7","w":2,"s":"A European scholar argues that China should simply copy the European Spatial Development Perspective and the Dutch planning system to fix its spatial planning problems.","q":"How would Liu and Zhou (2021) answer?","o":["Agree fully: a universal theory of planning applies to all countries, so copying is efficient.","Reject all foreign input: planning ideas from the West are tools of colonial domination.","Learn from abroad, but fit plans to China's times, laws and local conditions; China may become a model.","Treat it as a demonstration that only shows what a city could look like under the right conditions."],"a":2,"e":"The authors use Europe as a reference point and say China should learn from international experience, but stress that formulation and implementation must fit the times, laws and local conditions, and that China could become a model for other developing countries. The 'colonial domination' reading is closer to the postcolonial critique in lecture 6."},{"t":"p8","w":2,"s":"Two Dutch cities receive the same national e-bike subsidy. In one, cycling to work booms, helped by fast bike routes and parking at stations; in the other, use barely changes because the routes and parking are missing.","q":"How would Hodson, Geels and McMeekin (2017) read this?","o":["The same generic innovation is embedded in different systems and governance: place-specific reconfiguration.","The cities run different living lab dispositions: one a platform, the other a controlled trial.","Both cities treat the city as the self-evident scale of action, which hides the national role.","The digital divide explains it: residents in the second city lack the skills to use e-bikes."],"a":0,"e":"Innovations radical in one city are normal in another (Oxford electric, Brighton cycling); outcomes depend on how a generic intervention is embedded in existing infrastructures and policies. The third option borrows Angelo and Wachsmuth's 'methodological cityism', which Hodson et al. cite but use to stress wider flows, not local difference."},{"t":"p8","w":2,"s":"A city's mobility plan aims both to cut the need to travel through remote work hubs and to electrify its entire car fleet. Officials present both as 'sustainable mobility' and see no tension between them.","q":"How would Hodson et al. (2017) analyse this plan?","o":["Efficiency gains in electric cars will be eaten by rebound, so plan phase-outs of roads instead.","Competing conceptions of sustainable mobility; ask which account dominates and who promotes it.","The compact city is the answer, as long as floor space per person stops growing in rich countries.","A resource conflict between growth and environment, approached only by resolving the conflict."],"a":1,"e":"Following Banister, sustainable mobility can mean reducing the need to travel, modal shift, shorter trips or efficiency; these are contested, and the key question is which accounts become dominant in shaping reconfiguration, and who promotes them. The rebound option is Durrant et al.'s post-growth view, which takes a side instead of mapping."},{"t":"p8","w":2,"s":"A national government gives grants directly to households for heat pumps and home batteries, bypassing city councils. Each city ends up with scattered pilot projects that are hardly connected to each other.","q":"How would Hodson et al. (2017) read this?","o":["Complementary task allocation: the most suitable government level takes the task over.","Organic living labs: civil society leads projects that respond to specific community needs.","Marketised individualisation: the state bypasses city institutions, producing atomised experiments.","Exnovation: lock-in practices are being dismantled by deliberate national intervention."],"a":2,"e":"Among five modes through which the national state enrols cities, 'marketised individualisation' targets individuals and communities directly, bypassing city institutions; the result can be atomised, fragmented experimentation. Complementary task allocation is Witte's Omgevingswet principle about government levels."},{"t":"p8","w":2,"s":"A researcher wants to judge how far a city's mobility transition has come and proposes simply counting the number of pilot projects the city has run in the past ten years.","q":"How would Hodson et al. (2017) advise the researcher?","o":["Classify each pilot by type and disposition, for instance as a civic demonstration or platform.","Score each pilot on efficiency, effectiveness and inclusivity, the three goals of digital planning.","Check each pilot against innovation, exnovation, co-governance and social justice outcomes.","Ask what the experiments add up to: how they connect to each other and to existing systems."],"a":3,"e":"For socio-technical arrangements the key question is 'what do all these experiments add up to in a city?': how experiments connect with each other (UK evidence suggests many are episodic) and with existing systems, as competing, co-existing or complementary. The other options are the frameworks of Bulkeley et al., Lin et al. and Hansen et al."},{"t":"p9","w":3,"s":"An energy company fits out a new apartment block with smart meters, home batteries and a building energy system. It recruits residents to use them for two years to judge whether the technology is ready to sell at a larger scale.","q":"How would Bulkeley et al. (2019) classify this?","o":["A place-specific reconfiguration in which old and new energy systems compete and co-exist.","A strategic demonstration: a corporate-led showcase of specific technologies, like Hållbarheten.","A conflict of rationalities in which the firm's techno-managerial logic meets residents' needs.","An efficiency gain that will be eaten up by rebound unless total energy use is capped as well."],"a":1,"e":"E.ON's Hållbarheten in Malmö is the paper's example: strategic design (a large corporate partner, testing for wider rollout) with a demonstration disposition. The reconfiguration reading is Hodson et al.'s, which looks at systems in a city rather than at the lab's design and disposition."},{"t":"p9","w":3,"s":"Under austerity, a council and a local university set up a shared data hub that bundles separate energy, transport and ICT projects, plus an online ideas competition that funds a handful of citizen projects.","q":"How would Bulkeley et al. (2019) read this?","o":["Quadruple-helix digital planning that serves efficiency, effectiveness and inclusivity goals.","A governance experiment in which the local state is restructured to claim new authority.","A civic platform, formed partly as a tactic to gain resources and capacity under austerity.","Co-governance stuck at the lower rungs of participation: information and consultation."],"a":2,"e":"This is MK: Smart in Milton Keynes: municipality and civic university (civic design), with a Data Hub that curates connectivity between projects (platform). Lead shifted to the Open University as a tactical move under austerity. The digital planning reading is Lin et al.'s, which focuses on tools rather than lab type."},{"t":"p9","w":3,"s":"Residents of a low-income neighbourhood start a repair café and a project against fuel poverty. They rely on small grants and volunteer time, and their activities are a series of one-off events in a borrowed community hall.","q":"How would Bulkeley et al. (2019) classify this initiative?","o":["An organic lab: civil society responds to local needs with bids and volunteer time.","An invented space of insurgent planning, created by the marginalised against the state.","Self-build housing that a post-growth planner should support with protected land.","A trial: a controlled test of products under real-world conditions in one location."],"a":0,"e":"Organic (grassroots) ULLs respond to community needs such as fuel poverty, are led by civil society and have limited budgets matched by volunteer time, so activities tend to be one-off projects. The invented-spaces option is Miraftab's insurgent planning from lecture 6, which stresses resistance rather than experimentation."},{"t":"p10","w":3,"s":"A municipality launches an app for reporting problems in public space. After a year, almost no reports come from older residents or people with disabilities, although they use the streets most.","q":"How would Lin, Geertman, Witte and Pinto (2025) respond?","o":["Algorithmic outputs erode democratic engagement, so return to slow, face-to-face planning.","The app reveals a conflict of rationalities between the state and marginalised residents.","Inclusive digital planning needs suitable hardware, settings and co-design with the users.","Participation is stuck at information and consultation; embed co-creation in top policy."],"a":2,"e":"The Zwolle study co-designed a mapping tool with people with disabilities and found that inclusion depends on hardware and setting as well as software; the editorial lists the digital divide as a key risk. The slow-planning option is Li and Dang's more sceptical response to AI."},{"t":"p10","w":3,"s":"A technology firm offers a city a ready-made smart-city platform for traffic and parking data. The city adopts it quickly, but no planning goals or data agreements are attached and the planning department is not involved.","q":"How would Lin et al. (2025) read this?","o":["A strategic living lab: the city becomes a test-bed for a corporate partner's priorities.","A generic innovation whose meaning depends on how it is embedded in existing systems.","An unavoidable market push that will deskill planners whatever the city decides to do.","Platforms built without planning input risk poor alignment; data governance is critical."],"a":3,"e":"The editorial notes that smart city applications and platforms often develop without planning intervention, and poor alignment can hinder digital planning, so data governance becomes critical; digital planning should be guided by policy goals. The deskilling option is Li and Dang's, who see the push as unavoidable."},{"t":"p10","w":3,"s":"A planning department runs VR workshops in which residents co-design a healthier square. Participants are enthusiastic, but the software allows only one user at a time and its visuals look unrealistic. The pilot ends after one project.","q":"How would Lin et al. (2025) assess this pilot?","o":["Promising co-design with usability gaps; improve the tool and study how to scale co-design.","Too fast for democracy: automated design short-circuits slow, face-to-face deliberation.","A reconfiguration in which old and new systems co-exist without changing each other.","An efficiency gain that should be paired with a ceiling on new development in the area."],"a":0,"e":"This mirrors the Eindhoven VR study: mostly positive participants, yet better visuals and multi-user functions were recommended. The editorial asks that tools be co-developed with users and that research explore scaling co-design from experiments to practice. The 'too fast' option is Li and Dang's critique of automated decisions."},{"t":"p10","w":3,"s":"Facing budget cuts, a council proposes an AI agent that screens permit applications and drafts decisions, so that two planning officer posts can be cut. A councillor asks how this fits good digital planning.","q":"How would Lin et al. (2025) respond?","o":["Augmentation will not hold under budget pressure; the profession faces its starkest crossroads.","Beware agentic AI replacing human-centred processes; keep it transparent and train officials.","Exnovate the old permit routines first, since they are a lock-in practice of the department.","Treat it as a civic platform that links the council's projects under one shared data hub."],"a":1,"e":"The editorial warns that AI is shifting towards an autonomous, 'agentic' decision-maker and that politicians with tight budgets may substitute human-centred processes with AI; governance should keep AI transparent, inclusive and human-centred, with trained officials. The 'starkest crossroads' option is Li and Dang's more pessimistic view."},{"t":"p11","w":3,"s":"A city uses a machine-learning model to rank neighbourhoods for public investment. The model is trained on twenty years of past investment data, and the districts that were neglected before again score lowest.","q":"How would Li and Dang (2025) read this?","o":["Algorithmic bias: historic inequities in the data are reproduced, as with redlining.","A supply-demand mismatch that co-design with planners and users would resolve.","A property conflict between growth and equity, to be negotiated by planners.","Competing sustainabilities, where the dominant account is promoted by data firms."],"a":0,"e":"AI is sold as impartial, but datasets carry social and historical biases; deployed uncritically it can reinforce redlining and marginalise under-represented voices. The co-design option is closer to Lin et al.'s editorial, which is more confident that better tool design solves such problems."},{"t":"p11","w":3,"s":"To save teaching hours, a planning school replaces studio exercises in negotiating between residents and developers with an AI tool that generates 'optimal' land-use plans for students to present.","q":"How would Li and Dang (2025) respond?","o":["Fine, as long as tools are co-developed with planners and scaled up from small pilots.","Risky: judgement built by reconciling values would be lost; teach AI and ethics as well.","A demonstration lab whose lessons will not scale beyond the school's own studio.","A technocratic best practice that lands without checking its fit with the local context."],"a":1,"e":"Deskilling: once outputs are treated as authoritative, 'accumulated practitioner wisdom' is displaced. The authors want education redefined with digital literacy, AI basics and ethics alongside policy, design and engagement skills, not instead of them. The first option reflects Lin et al.'s co-design agenda."},{"t":"p11","w":3,"s":"A software vendor assures a city that its planning AI will only 'augment' staff, and that an ethics board will audit the algorithm every year. The city's planning department has no data scientists.","q":"How would Li and Dang (2025) assess these assurances?","o":["Sound: transparent, human-centred governance and staff training will keep AI in check.","Irrelevant: the real issue is whether the AI is part of a strategic or civic living lab.","Helpful: an ethics board turns the AI tool into a form of co-governance with citizens.","Precarious: support tools drift towards replacement and audits need skills the city lacks."],"a":3,"e":"Both counter-arguments 'have merit but understate AI's disruptive force': augmentation drifts towards replacement under pressure for speed and savings, and agencies often lack resources and skills for rigorous audits. The first option is the more optimistic governance stance of Lin et al.'s editorial."},{"t":"p12","w":3,"s":"A Dutch municipality rezones farmland for expensive owner-occupied homes and uses the land value uplift to pay for a park and a share of social rental housing in the new district.","q":"How would Durrant, Lamker and Rydin (2023) read this?","o":["Active land policy with a double hat, which only works as long as the market keeps rising.","Exchange agreements whose link between urban growth and green space is ambiguous.","A property conflict between economic growth and social equity, to be negotiated over land.","Land value capture ties public goals to more monetised development; delink and protect land."],"a":3,"e":"Land value capture is excluded because it depends on creating more monetised value, often in expensive places, to fund other goals; post-growth planning would protect land for public, social and community housing directly. The double-hat option is Witte's lecture 4 reading, which worries about financial risk, not growth-dependence."},{"t":"p12","w":3,"s":"A city markets itself as a sustainability beacon, with green towers, solar roofs and falling local emissions. A study of its residents' flights and consumption shows a footprint far above the national average.","q":"How would Durrant et al. (2023) read this?","o":["At best relative decoupling: counting consumption emissions shows how easily growth is labelled green.","Evidence that the city has become seen as the solution to global environmental problems.","A civic demonstration whose transformative potential is more modest than its claims.","Competing accounts of sustainability, to be mapped without choosing between them."],"a":0,"e":"Cities that market themselves as beacons may look worse once residents' consumption emissions are counted (Krähmer); local efficiency is relative decoupling, not absolute. The Angelo and Wachsmuth option explains why cities are seen as solutions, not whether this one decouples."},{"t":"p12","w":3,"s":"A national government invests in high-speed rail to nearby capitals and, in the same budget, approves extra runway capacity at its main airport to 'keep growth going'.","q":"How would Durrant et al. (2023) respond?","o":["Rail and air regimes will compete, co-exist or complement; map which pattern emerges.","Coordinate the rail investment with reduced air capacity: plan the phase-out alongside it.","Use control lines to protect farmland and ecology around the new airport runway.","Treat it as a development conflict between equity and environment, to be negotiated."],"a":1,"e":"Investment in long-distance rail should be coordinated with reduced air travel capacity; new infrastructure should come with planned shrinkage of existing infrastructure (p. 292). The first option is Hodson et al.'s, who map relations between systems instead of prescribing phase-outs."},{"t":"p12","w":3,"s":"A planner is told to write a housing plan that assumes 3% economic growth every year for 20 years. From impact assessments she knows the plan would exceed the region's carbon budget, but her alderman wants an upbeat story.","q":"How would Durrant et al. (2023) advise her?","o":["Build an alliance with groups that share equity and environmental values to win the vote.","Reclaim interpretive agency from algorithms by acting as a sense-maker of their outputs.","Voice uncomfortable truths: set out the long-term consequences and infeasibility of the plan.","Keep negotiations informal first, so that the formal plan later codifies a broad agreement."],"a":2,"e":"Just as it is unethical to propose an unimplementable plan, it is unethical to advise action known to be disastrous: planners should 'voice uncomfortable truths' grounded in expertise on consequences. Alliance-building is Næss's (L1) strategy, which rests on shared values rather than expertise."},{"t":"p13","w":3,"s":"A European city declares a climate emergency, adopts a green roof strategy and launches a tree-planting programme. In the same year, the maintenance budget for existing parks is cut and two green space officers are not replaced.","q":"How would Hansen et al. (2022) read this?","o":["Old and new arrangements co-exist, as reconfiguration is by definition emergent.","Incremental change: new strategies, but maintenance and capacity keep it piecemeal.","A growth-dependent agenda that labels more development as sustainable and green.","A demonstration that shows a sustainable future without a pathway to scaling up."],"a":1,"e":"New non-statutory strategies and action plans are typical of the cities studied, but long-term maintenance, funding and staff capacity keep change incremental. The co-existence reading is Hodson et al.'s, which would not focus on the administrative capacity Hansen et al. stress."},{"t":"p13","w":3,"s":"A city redesigns a large park. It holds two information evenings and runs an online survey, but the design choices are made by the parks department, and residents see the final plan only when it is published.","q":"How would Hansen et al. (2022) characterise this participation?","o":["An 'alongside' strategy in which early involvement builds draagvlak for the formal plan.","A digital planning tool that makes the process more efficient, effective and inclusive.","Between tokenism and co-governance: participation stuck at information and consultation.","A conflict of rationalities between a techno-managerial state and residents' survival logic."],"a":2,"e":"Co-governance increased in most cities, but participation mostly stayed at the lower rungs of information and consultation, partly for lack of capacity and flexibility; only where co-creation was embedded in high-level policy did perspectives change. Witte's 'alongside' would require stakeholders to shape solutions early, which did not happen here."},{"t":"p13","w":3,"s":"In a growing city, developers who want to build more homes agree to create and pay for new public green spaces in return for extra building rights. The council presents this as a win for both housing and nature.","q":"How would Hansen et al. (2022) read this deal?","o":["Exclude it: value capture depends on more monetised development, which is pro-growth.","Green gentrification: greening raises land values and displaces existing residents.","Active land policy, in which the council acts as a private stakeholder with a double hat.","Urban growth plays an ambiguous role: it funds greening but ties it to densification."],"a":3,"e":"Aarhus's 'exchange agreements' show this: developers contribute to the common good, for example new green space, in return for economic gain; growth brought resources but also conflicts with affordable housing. Durrant et al. would exclude such tools outright, which is a different, normative post-growth stance."},{"t":"p13","w":3,"s":"A city's new greening programme targets heat stress and biodiversity with detailed ecological criteria. It says nothing about which neighbourhoods benefit first, and low-income districts are not prioritised.","q":"How would Hansen et al. (2022) respond?","o":["Put social and environmental justice at the heart of green planning to avoid unjust outcomes.","Treat it as a resource conflict between growth and environment, approached by negotiation.","Recognise that the city has become the self-evident scale for solving climate problems.","Fix it with control lines that bar urban growth on ecologically sensitive land at the edge."],"a":0,"e":"The authors call for putting social and environmental justice at the heart of green space planning, since technocratic decisions often unintentionally reinforce inequities; justice was the weakest lens, a 'considerable disparity' with ecological responses. Campbell would frame equity versus environment as the development conflict, not the resource conflict."}],"WHO":[{"t":"buit","kind":"quote","txt":"First, it is important to be aware that The Just City is actually a contradiction in terms: in itself, the city cannot be just or unjust."},{"t":"buit","kind":"quote","txt":"Pursuing both at the same time for the same place is likely to lead to not achieving either of them"},{"t":"buit","kind":"quote","txt":"two people can be equal but poor, or very unequal but rich. Most would argue that the latter is to be preferred over the former."},{"t":"buit","kind":"quote","txt":"Although it is tempting to rush to the common ground in order to avoid any collision, we must realize that politics is divisive by definition"},{"t":"buit","kind":"quote","txt":"The word ‘just’ in the idea of The Just City has such a positive connotation that it is hard to refute"},{"t":"buit","kind":"idea","txt":"A football result can never be unfair; only particular actions in the game, like a serious foul, can be. Likewise a spatial pattern is not unjust if it arose from legitimate actions."},{"t":"buit","kind":"idea","txt":"A 30% housing-cost ratio says little about affordability on its own: whether a household runs a payment risk depends on its absolute income and basic household budget."},{"t":"buit","kind":"idea","txt":"Urban justice requires explicit choices between competing conceptions, at both the city-wide strategic level and the site level, rather than lumping them together."},{"t":"fw","kind":"quote","txt":"But any definition of planning adopted by a researcher implies a framing of that object of research and that has theoretical implications."},{"t":"fw","kind":"quote","txt":"However, it is the role of the academy to do the best job it can of the research task first."},{"t":"fw","kind":"quote","txt":"all theoretical endeavours in the social sciences are inevitably value-laden, and a rigorous approach should seek to make this clear and explicit"},{"t":"fw","kind":"quote","txt":"Choosing between theories is a way of making these value judgements an integral element of one’s research, and that is often profoundly satisfying."},{"t":"fw","kind":"idea","txt":"Practical-wisdom research dismisses theory by attacking a straw man and treats good research as a virtue of character, whereas research is a skill that can be learnt."},{"t":"fw","kind":"idea","txt":"Every approach to planning research can be read as a reaction to the limits of the governmental model, the one closest to planning's own image as a rational, expert-led process."},{"t":"fw","kind":"idea","txt":"Letting the facts speak for themselves leaves a disparate field: each situation is researched in detail, but the findings are hard to connect to one another."},{"t":"fw","kind":"idea","txt":"Social research should not copy the natural-science idea of theory as laws between variables, because the social world is complex and people interpret it themselves."},{"t":"hall","kind":"quote","txt":"but in doing so, it began to sow the seeds of its own destruction"},{"t":"hall","kind":"quote","txt":"honeymoon was followed in quick succession during the 1970s by tiffs and temporary reconciliations, in the 1980s by divorce"},{"t":"hall","kind":"quote","txt":"the urban-regional system was inside its own system of control"},{"t":"hall","kind":"quote","txt":"planning was slipping into insignificance, not because it was wrong or misplaced, but because it lacked the intellectual wherewithal to defend itself"},{"t":"hall","kind":"quote","txt":"The 1950s were never like that; then, the academics were the coaches, down there with the team."},{"t":"hall","kind":"quote","txt":"planning in actuality was a very long way indeed from the cool, rational, Olympian style envisaged in the systems texts"},{"t":"hall","kind":"idea","txt":"Before 1955 the planner, sure of his expertise and free of political interference, was expected to get the plan right first time, with no learning or feedback built in."},{"t":"hall","kind":"idea","txt":"Stripped of its German philosophical basis, communicative planning theory prescribes nothing that advocacy planning had not already said fifteen years earlier."},{"t":"marx","kind":"quote","txt":"Political economy starts with the fact of private property; it does not explain it to us."},{"t":"marx","kind":"quote","txt":"He feels at home when he is not working, and when he is working he does not feel at home."},{"t":"marx","kind":"quote","txt":"Conscious life activity distinguishes man immediately from animal life activity."},{"t":"marx","kind":"quote","txt":"The animal is immediately one with its life activity."},{"t":"marx","kind":"idea","txt":"Raising or equalising wages leaves the root untouched: the wage itself follows from the estranged relation of the worker to his labour."},{"t":"marx","kind":"idea","txt":"The economists describe the laws of wages, profit and rent, but treat competition as accidental and greed as the only driving force, without grasping their inner connection."},{"t":"marx","kind":"idea","txt":"Humans also produce when free from physical need, and only truly produce in that freedom; they even form objects according to the laws of beauty."},{"t":"marx","kind":"idea","txt":"If work is a torment to the one who performs it, it must be a pleasure to someone else: a person outside production who becomes its master."},{"t":"cozz","kind":"quote","txt":"An adaptive built environment has as many designers as the number of users who over time shape it."},{"t":"cozz","kind":"quote","txt":"Adaptability is indeed a relative concept."},{"t":"cozz","kind":"quote","txt":"public spaces should not be venerated in an uncritical fashion but instead questioned for their concrete utility"},{"t":"cozz","kind":"quote","txt":"Multiple and independent ownerships allow the built environment to involve the dispersed creativity of society"},{"t":"cozz","kind":"idea","txt":"Old building codes only prohibited harm to neighbours, such as noise or pollution, and could not impose any predetermined spatial configuration on the city."},{"t":"cozz","kind":"idea","txt":"Neighbourhoods built in one go tend to decay all at the same time, while areas built incrementally keep a mix of values, maintenance states and users."},{"t":"cozz","kind":"idea","txt":"Participatory design and city gaming tend to produce more complex designs, but not spaces where self-organising complexity can emerge."},{"t":"cozz","kind":"idea","txt":"When the planning rules of a neighbourhood simply copy its initial design, the knowledge that comes from continual neighbouring adaptation is lost."},{"t":"gov","kind":"quote","txt":"The goals themselves stand outside the planning process"},{"t":"gov","kind":"quote","txt":"planning redresses problems that market processes alone cannot solve"},{"t":"gov","kind":"quote","txt":"the gold standard for planning is being seen to be rational"},{"t":"gov","kind":"quote","txt":"Planning can struggle with both too little knowledge (of the right kind) and too much (to process)."},{"t":"gov","kind":"idea","txt":"Planning involves stakeholders, but only in an instrumental way: to make planning more effective, not as the source of its legitimacy."},{"t":"gov","kind":"idea","txt":"Tools such as cost-benefit analysis, multi-criteria analysis and GIS do not take decisions; they offer information to where decisions legitimately lie."},{"t":"gov","kind":"idea","txt":"Investment, spatial coordination, regulation, fiscal measures, tradeable permits and nudges together are a measure of the power of the state planner."},{"t":"gov","kind":"idea","txt":"Research in this frame mirrors the planning model itself: goals are taken as given and data are collected before and after the planning action."},{"t":"hayek","kind":"quote","txt":"it is a superstition to believe that there must be a majority view on everything"},{"t":"hayek","kind":"quote","txt":"Many separate plans do not make a planned whole"},{"t":"hayek","kind":"quote","txt":"It is inevitable that they should impose their scale of preferences on the community for which they plan."},{"t":"hayek","kind":"quote","txt":"We have no intention, however, of making a fetish of democracy."},{"t":"hayek","kind":"idea","txt":"A single plan would need every need of every person ranked in one order of values, and no such moral code exists in anyone's mind."},{"t":"hayek","kind":"idea","txt":"The probability that people agree on what the state should do falls as the range of things the state tries to do grows."},{"t":"hayek","kind":"idea","txt":"Civil law is as technical as economic planning, yet nobody proposes delegating it, because it consists of general rules a majority can agree on."},{"t":"hayek","kind":"idea","txt":"People can agree that there should be a plan while agreeing only on the mechanism, hiding their disagreement behind a label like 'common welfare'."},{"t":"know","kind":"quote","txt":"Knowledge differs from information and data in that the specification of a causal relationship is central to knowledge."},{"t":"know","kind":"quote","txt":"Not all claims within planning can be recast as knowledge claims just to promote the status of the claim-maker."},{"t":"know","kind":"quote","txt":"not any imaginings will serve as a planning goal"},{"t":"know","kind":"quote","txt":"it is patronising to assume that knowledge debates cannot be ventured openly in a variety of languages"},{"t":"know","kind":"idea","txt":"Planners co-produce knowledge in two ways: they give voice to claims from many actors and then actively test and recognise which of them count as knowledge."},{"t":"know","kind":"idea","txt":"Comparing the current state, the future without planning, the planned future and the actual outcome yields seven types of knowledge planning needs."},{"t":"know","kind":"idea","txt":"Consensus building works where information is fully shared or uncertainty is rampant, but most planning disputes lie between these two extremes."},{"t":"know","kind":"idea","txt":"Describing an area's affluence by market prices or by child poverty makes a difference: even descriptive knowledge is framed by values and carries a causal story."},{"t":"davoudi","kind":"quote","txt":"ambitious and naïve assumptions that the complex political and socio-economic processes could be technicized, commanded and controlled through a scientific process"},{"t":"davoudi","kind":"idea","txt":"Today's enthusiasm for evidence-based policy rests on the same assumptions as the positivist, rational planning of the 1960s and 1970s."},{"t":"davoudi","kind":"idea","txt":"Research should not spoon-feed politicians but illuminate the landscape in which decisions are made, informing public debate over time."},{"t":"davoudi","kind":"idea","txt":"Facts are not evidence in themselves; they become evidence when used to support or refute a proposition, and tacit and experiential knowledge can count too."},{"t":"davoudi","kind":"idea","txt":"Evidence is only one contender for influence on policy: ideology, interests, institutional habits and prior information all compete with it."},{"t":"davoudi","kind":"idea","txt":"A test of whether plans rest on a robust and credible evidence base soon turned into a procedural, tick-box exercise about the process followed."},{"t":"davoudi","kind":"idea","txt":"Planning debates of the 1980s and 1990s focused so much on process, knowing how, that substantive knowledge of how cities work, knowing what, was neglected."},{"t":"davoudi","kind":"idea","txt":"Research for policy's sake is selective: fields such as strategic waste planning that are not seen as policy problems attract hardly any research money."},{"t":"scott","kind":"quote","txt":"Brasília is about the closest thing we have to a high-modernist city"},{"t":"scott","kind":"quote","txt":"Technocracy, in this instance, is the belief that the human problem of urban design has a unique solution, which an expert can discover and execute."},{"t":"scott","kind":"quote","txt":"Here is a case where what seems like rationality and legibility to those working in administration and urban services seems like mystifying disorder for the ordinary residents who must navigate the city."},{"t":"scott","kind":"quote","txt":"The real Brasília, as opposed to the hypothetical Brasília in the planning documents, was greatly marked by resistance, subversion, and political calculation."},{"t":"scott","kind":"idea","txt":"Where the modernist planner first sees the city from the air, the critic sees it as a pedestrian on daily rounds would, starting from the micro-order of sidewalks and intersections."},{"t":"scott","kind":"idea","txt":"The love of straight lines and right angles had no backing in machines, science or nature; it was an aesthetic ideology that also produced a grid legible at a glance."},{"t":"scott","kind":"idea","txt":"A capital built on state land designed out street corners and the square as a public visiting room, leaving only official public spaces and vast dead voids."},{"t":"scott","kind":"idea","txt":"Construction workers expected to leave a new capital squatted, organised and won land and services, so most residents ended up in settlements the plan never foresaw."},{"t":"needham","kind":"quote","txt":"Achieving the goals of land-use planning depends on how land is used, not on who owns it."},{"t":"needham","kind":"quote","txt":"The market cannot be excluded from influencing land use."},{"t":"needham","kind":"quote","txt":"The dog is not allowed to sleep in the manger, even if he owns it, if the horse has no other source of food."},{"t":"needham","kind":"quote","txt":"This chapter goes no further than an investigation of feasibility: it does not recommend replacing land-use planning by markets in property rights."},{"t":"needham","kind":"quote","txt":"But we too are the public!"},{"t":"needham","kind":"idea","txt":"Dutch industrial estates decay because owners cannot be obliged to join a maintenance association and unexpired leases cannot be sold on, so nobody holds the rights needed to keep the estate in good shape."},{"t":"needham","kind":"idea","txt":"My interest in the colour of my neighbour's front door is judged less important than hers, so neither a property right nor a public authority protects it."},{"t":"needham","kind":"idea","txt":"Shifting the protection of an interest from regulation to a property right amounts to creating a market, because it creates new structures within which people exchange freely."},{"t":"ni","kind":"quote","txt":"Institutions are a system of established rules and norms, which guide, constrain, regulate behavior of actors"},{"t":"ni","kind":"quote","txt":"Organizations are concrete entities with a specific purpose"},{"t":"ni","kind":"quote","txt":"Institutions form a way of answering and reconciling the relationship between structure and agency"},{"t":"ni","kind":"quote","txt":"Coordinate effects, provide certainty, information and credible commitment"},{"t":"ni","kind":"quote","txt":"Internalized rather than externalized understanding of institutions"},{"t":"ni","kind":"idea","txt":"The European Broadcasting Union is the organisation; its statutes and the formal and informal rules of the song contest are the institutions."},{"t":"ni","kind":"idea","txt":"Institutionalisms disagree on three questions: what institutions are, what they do, and how they change, including whether they can be designed."},{"t":"ni","kind":"idea","txt":"Rational choice, sociological, historical and evolutionary institutionalism rest on different assumptions about structure, agency and how institutions change."},{"t":"olson","kind":"quote","txt":"Olson is often interpreted as arguing that free-riding renders collective action impossible or irrational. This is simply not the case."},{"t":"olson","kind":"quote","txt":"In most cases excludability is a matter of cost."},{"t":"olson","kind":"quote","txt":"Revolutions are, for example, rarely spontaneous events."},{"t":"olson","kind":"quote","txt":"Gossiping is an enjoyable activity."},{"t":"olson","kind":"quote","txt":"It is when it is judged in terms of its average performance against all other strategies that tit-for-tat does well."},{"t":"olson","kind":"idea","txt":"A flatmate who hates squalor may clean the whole flat alone and tolerate the others' free-riding, so the collective good is still provided."},{"t":"olson","kind":"idea","txt":"Civil rights activists joined partly for the adrenaline rush and the sense of being part of history, so taking part was itself the reward."},{"t":"olson","kind":"idea","txt":"Soldiers facing each other for long periods developed truces, until commanders rotated troops to erode the trust that repeated contact had built."},{"t":"taylor","kind":"idea","txt":"Fringe landowners argued that being brought inside the growth boundary would not raise their land values, yet sales soon afterwards showed huge gains."},{"t":"taylor","kind":"idea","txt":"Changes to a betterment charge, including moving liability from existing owners to purchasers, are best read as a response to pressure from insiders."},{"t":"taylor","kind":"idea","txt":"Owners who already had plans to develop barely objected to the infrastructure charge, while owners without such plans objected in large numbers."},{"t":"taylor","kind":"idea","txt":"Through the political discourse around planning, self-interested claims by property owners can come to be accepted at face value as the more equitable position."},{"t":"taylor","kind":"idea","txt":"Urban containment creates land price premiums, these are redistributed through the lobbying of existing owners, and the costs pass to future homebuyers."},{"t":"taylor","kind":"idea","txt":"Fringe owners can rent-seek in two opposite ways: by pushing to be included and unlock development value, or by defending restrictions that protect their amenity."},{"t":"taylor","kind":"idea","txt":"Housing developers tend to accept infrastructure charges that are transparent and can be passed on to buyers; the resistance came from landowners."},{"t":"taylor","kind":"idea","txt":"Perceived property rights can gain political traction even when they contradict the legal basis of property, and taxing owner-occupation is politically toxic."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"quote","txt":"Cultural symbols used by planners are often detached from institutionalised practices and, thus, are destined to fall on fallow ground rather than take root and develop a mobilising effect."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"quote","txt":"In fact, institutions are symbolic systems. Concrete social practices serve not only to achieve certain ends but also to reproduce the symbolic system and, in so doing, make life meaningful."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"quote","txt":"Institutional symbols unfold their social magic only if the meaning of the symbol is inculcated into, and is thus guaranteed by, the audience."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"quote","txt":"The art of planning is to find niches of underinstitutionalised space (social as well as spatial) that are not subject to institutional power struggles over the domination of the social norm."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"quote","txt":"Institutional embedding makes people believe in the meaning of symbols in social interaction."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"idea","txt":"Ageing is gradual, yet ceremonies that mark adulthood consecrate an arbitrary boundary: a kind of social magic that produces discontinuity out of continuity."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"idea","txt":"A region's identity is a two-sided coin: being recognised as different by outsiders matters as much as being recognised from inside."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"idea","txt":"When a planning metaphor or landmark fails, redesigning the symbol misses the point: success depends on whether its meaning is embedded in people's minds."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"idea","txt":"Institutional approaches can be mapped on two choices: external constraint versus internalised in agency, and fixed prior to action versus validated in action."},{"t":"dembski","kind":"idea","txt":"Spectators at a Balinese cockfight cheer not for the birds but for their own status group, showing that symbols say something about something else."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"quote","txt":"An institution cannot simply be 'produced' and made available as other human artefacts."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"quote","txt":"Institutions perform two basic roles: in the first place, they ensure a pattern-coordination among individual actions; second, they are a fund of knowledge accrued over the centuries, and available to all."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"quote","txt":"To strongly recognize the role of institutions invites us to scale down our ability to design and shape institutions."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"quote","txt":"When it comes to institutions, we are never actually intervening in a tabula rasa situation, but inevitably within a long-term, consolidated framework."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"quote","txt":"A frequent shake-up of the urban institutional setup creates confusion for both developers and citizens."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"quote","txt":"Open institutions are set of universal rules that apply to an indeterminate number of future cases and situations."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"quote","txt":"Nobody can know beforehand what the most efficient combination of all the factors is."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"idea","txt":"Calling both the building of an organisation and the reform of society's basic rules 'institutional design' blurs two entirely different enterprises."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"idea","txt":"Many institutionalist planners reject end-state land-use plans, yet repeat the same engineering mindset one level up when they set out to redesign society's basic rules."},{"t":"moroni","kind":"idea","txt":"People are better described as rule-following animals than as rational maximisers: rules let individually ignorant people use more knowledge than any of them holds."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"quote","txt":"A housing crisis contributed to 'permissive conditions' in each case, but differing 'productive conditions' determined the outcome."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"quote","txt":"Critical juncture analysis in this way helps to explain the processes through which political actors take advantage of conjunctural crises to further particular institutional changes."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"quote","txt":"This case is also revealing about path dependence in cities and planning, a pattern more often assumed than it is explained."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"quote","txt":"Historical institutionalism frames institutions as unequally distributive, produced by and constitutive of unequal power relations, and contested."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"quote","txt":"Even the radical changes of the last few years work to change the amounts, timing, and names of LVC instruments, while leaving in place the basic structure of (much reduced) density bonuses and development charges."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"quote","txt":"The fact that these institutions have been intensely and continually contested in Toronto is evidence that they are substantively important."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"idea","txt":"Whether lower developer contributions will cut house prices or simply raise land values and developers' profits is an open question for future research."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"idea","txt":"Global ideological currents matter, but their effects on the ground are mediated through local institutions and battles over specific sets of rules."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"idea","txt":"If new development contributes nothing to growth-related infrastructure, lower-income residents end up subsidising wealthier newcomers or developers' profits."},{"t":"sorensen","kind":"idea","txt":"A planning law passed in a crisis often leaves key details ambiguous, and that ambiguity drives decades of contest in courts, tribunals and parliament."},{"t":"fog","kind":"idea","txt":"Urban land is collectively produced and collectively needed, yet privately owned and controlled; this tension is what calls planning into being under capitalism."},{"t":"fog","kind":"idea","txt":"Handing control over land to the state is risky for property owners, because in a democracy popular majorities might push that control much further than capital accepts."},{"t":"fog","kind":"idea","txt":"Early planning bodies were often run by expert commissions and independent boards, which kept land-use decisions away from direct popular pressure."},{"t":"fog","kind":"idea","txt":"Planning is neither neutral public-interest expertise nor a simple tool of capitalists, but a contradictory response to tensions built into capitalist urbanisation."},{"t":"fog","kind":"idea","txt":"Capital as a whole needs some public control over land use, yet that same control threatens the rights and profits of individual owners, who therefore resist it."},{"t":"fog","kind":"idea","txt":"The history of US planning from colonial times to the 1920s, from housing reform to zoning, can be explained through the contradictions of capitalist urbanisation."},{"t":"fog","kind":"idea","txt":"Accounts that treat planning as a tool of capitalists cannot explain why capitalists and landowners so often resisted it."},{"t":"fog","kind":"idea","txt":"The form planning takes at a given moment reflects struggles between fractions of capital, property owners, reformers and working-class movements."},{"t":"harvey","kind":"quote","txt":"It makes it seem as if \"cities\" can be active agents when they are mere things."},{"t":"harvey","kind":"quote","txt":"Above all, the city has to appear as an innovative, exciting, creative, and safe place to live or to visit, to play and consume in."},{"t":"harvey","kind":"quote","txt":"Bread and circuses was the famous Roman formula that now stands to be reinvented and revived"},{"t":"harvey","kind":"quote","txt":"a dual city of inner city regeneration and a surrounding sea of increasing impoverishment"},{"t":"harvey","kind":"quote","txt":"even the most resolute and avantgarde municipal socialists will find themselves, in the end, playing the capitalist game"},{"t":"harvey","kind":"idea","txt":"Public absorption of risk and public provision of infrastructure lower the cost of relocation for multinational firms, making capital more rather than less geographically mobile."},{"t":"harvey","kind":"idea","txt":"The real problem is not civic boosterism as such but the generality of inter-urban competition, which lets bad projects drive out good ones and makes well-meaning coalitions 'realistic'."},{"t":"harvey","kind":"idea","txt":"In the 1960s US cities regained social control after urban unrest through redistribution of real income to the poor, and the local state's role as facilitator of capitalist development declined."},{"t":"hh","kind":"quote","txt":"Keeping the planning power in the hands of town planners has helped Finnish cities to develop a socially sustainable built environment and heterogeneous urban fabric without glaring inequalities"},{"t":"hh","kind":"quote","txt":"This was as if playing and getting used to the market game."},{"t":"hh","kind":"quote","txt":"urban scholars should be aware of and take responsibility for the consequences of their beliefs."},{"t":"hh","kind":"quote","txt":"privatising the municipal land can also mean privatising development rights."},{"t":"hh","kind":"quote","txt":"Not every place needs to be mixed; there can also be that kind of diversity."},{"t":"hh","kind":"quote","txt":"Anglo-American companies tend to follow the policy that they will not move to a city if they are not allowed to own the land."},{"t":"hh","kind":"idea","txt":"Metropolitan cities agreed to curb harmful inter-city competition by building social housing in every municipality and neighbourhood, yet one shoreline sale was developed for a single social class."},{"t":"hh","kind":"idea","txt":"In the UK the state enabled land to be treated as a financial asset by selling it to others; here the state itself became an actor treating land according to the rent it yields."},{"t":"savini","kind":"quote","txt":"Real estate is a commodity in tension: the risk of investment in the production of urban space is related to the fact that this commodity is immobile but today tradable on global markets as a liquid asset"},{"t":"savini","kind":"quote","txt":"the financialisation of land facilitates the globalisation of land."},{"t":"savini","kind":"quote","txt":"Today, project developers can be completely extraneous to the local context."},{"t":"savini","kind":"quote","txt":"Land thus becomes used as collateral that can be leveraged to create credit that can be put to use elsewhere."},{"t":"savini","kind":"quote","txt":"The land became an asset through company bonds to generate further credit for the project, but no brick had been laid."},{"t":"savini","kind":"quote","txt":"Under financialised capitalism, the full meaning of land as a form of fictitious capital becomes apparent."},{"t":"savini","kind":"quote","txt":"Pressed by the risk of long-term vacancy, the city regulated and waited instead of proactively stimulating the project."},{"t":"savini","kind":"idea","txt":"The more complex a project's property structure and the less proactive the public authorities, the further decisions about land drift away from local conditions and needs."},{"t":"bossuyt","kind":"quote","txt":"Social rights help to create and reclaim housing as commons, but may not be enough to sustain them."},{"t":"bossuyt","kind":"quote","txt":"Private ownership, along with individual financing, turns households into indebted subjects"},{"t":"bossuyt","kind":"quote","txt":"there is a need to further theorize and map out the possibilities for an archipelago of the commons."},{"t":"bossuyt","kind":"idea","txt":"Five patterns protect housing commons at three levels: regularization and restoration, integration and ideation, and federation linking movements to the state."},{"t":"bossuyt","kind":"idea","txt":"Occupations of vacant buildings exploit a legal contradiction between the social function of property and the right to private property."},{"t":"bossuyt","kind":"idea","txt":"Protection of a commons is both legal and physical: recognition guards against eviction, while night vigils and barriers guard the building itself."},{"t":"bossuyt","kind":"idea","txt":"Commons research has focused on how resources are reclaimed; the neglected question is how a housing commons reproduces itself over time against enclosure."},{"t":"bossuyt","kind":"idea","txt":"Enclosure of housing commons takes three forms: commodification through sale, marginalization through excluding outsiders, and cooptation by the state."},{"t":"gurran","kind":"quote","txt":"discourse is not simply the product of rational and deliberative processes, but the messy and complex medium of power conflicts"},{"t":"gurran","kind":"quote","txt":"By claiming that the cost of 'red tape' amounts to '40%', the benefits of deregulation appear obvious"},{"t":"gurran","kind":"quote","txt":"Thus the powerful interests which had infused planning discourses and regulatory reform proposals expressed through the Green and White Papers, were initially thwarted by fickle state politics."},{"t":"gurran","kind":"idea","txt":"Housing development is framed as the engine of economic growth, while the planning system is cast as the constraint that holds back investment and supply."},{"t":"gurran","kind":"idea","txt":"The national dwelling shortfall of 2008 would have been a modest surplus if social housing output had stayed at its 1996 level."},{"t":"gurran","kind":"idea","txt":"The development industry is selectively anti-intervention: it wants planning controls cut, but asks for infrastructure to be paid from general taxation."},{"t":"gurran","kind":"idea","txt":"Planning is pictured either as a way of 'bulldozing the beautiful' or as a brake on supply, and both images give developers new ways to contest policy."},{"t":"gurran","kind":"idea","txt":"The key question is whether debates on planning reform and supply distract from the real pressures on low-income renters and aspiring home owners."},{"t":"pruijt","kind":"quote","txt":"Both are examples of a squatters' movement proper, which means that squatting itself is means and primary goal at the same time."},{"t":"pruijt","kind":"quote","txt":"a pre-existing oppositional identity can to some extent override the opportunity structure."},{"t":"pruijt","kind":"quote","txt":"existing neighborhood groups can be co-opted to function as plugs for holes left by the retreating state."},{"t":"pruijt","kind":"idea","txt":"Perhaps paradoxically, a market-oriented city with little commitment to low-income housing offers fewer chances for squatting than one based on redistribution and planning."},{"t":"pruijt","kind":"idea","txt":"Legalization need not end a movement: conventional tactics can complement disruptive ones rather than replace them."},{"t":"pruijt","kind":"idea","txt":"Without legal rights, squatters act covertly, which isolates them and makes cooperation and self-organisation less likely."},{"t":"pruijt","kind":"idea","txt":"Squatting is not gentrification, even though squatters share traits with pioneering newcomers; at most, resistance can slow gentrification down."},{"t":"pruijt","kind":"idea","txt":"Radicals can act as watchdogs when co-opted neighbourhood groups drift from movement goals towards managerial self-interest."},{"t":"raco","kind":"quote","txt":"The local is given an ontological status as the locus in and through which effective forms of government can be established, regulated, and implemented."},{"t":"raco","kind":"quote","txt":"In practice, community involvement in SRB is not optimal but a necessary requirement (for funders)."},{"t":"raco","kind":"quote","txt":"This represents forms of governance where the presence of government institutions and state agencies is ostensibly reduced but the power of policymakers to shape the actions of policy communities is enhanced."},{"t":"raco","kind":"quote","txt":"Transparency, through auditing and monitoring, creates new calculable spaces for experts to monitor and leaves partnerships open to quantitative assessment and funding discipline from above"},{"t":"raco","kind":"idea","txt":"Requiring bids to add value to national programmes puts responsibility for the coherence of urban policy on its recipients rather than on policymakers."},{"t":"raco","kind":"idea","txt":"Regeneration funding rescales the territory of government from society and the nation-state to the community and the locality."},{"t":"raco","kind":"idea","txt":"A tool sold as nonideological empowerment of residents as consumers of local services in fact works as disciplinary power over local councils."},{"t":"raco","kind":"idea","txt":"Training community representatives to run bureaucratic procedures and monitoring makes the aspirations of local people calculable for government."},{"t":"rydin6","kind":"quote","txt":"Seeing planning processes as potentially disadvantaging the dispossessed and disempowered means that planners cannot be neutral technocrats or bureaucrats but need to behave more like advocates."},{"t":"rydin6","kind":"quote","txt":"These sectors are described as 'hard to reach' or 'silent', language which suggests that they are being recalcitrant in the face of offers of involvement in key networks."},{"t":"rydin6","kind":"quote","txt":"Urban regimes are about generating power, but this is 'power to' achieve change in line with certain goals rather than 'power over' (i.e. by one actor over another)."},{"t":"rydin6","kind":"idea","txt":"Justice has four aspects: recognising groups as self-determined, fair processes such as visioning, just exercise of procedures, and fair outcomes."},{"t":"rydin6","kind":"idea","txt":"Theorists disheartened by the message that change needs crisis and revolution turned to Gramsci: the disempowered can grasp their own interests and win change without a vanguard."},{"t":"rydin6","kind":"idea","txt":"Turin's turn from a pro-welfare to a market-centred agenda was built by a local alliance of politicians, liberal business and academics through naming, blaming and claiming."},{"t":"rydin6","kind":"idea","txt":"Research on planning conflict should take the emic view of disempowered groups, and its normative stance often moves the researcher from detached analysis to supporting them."},{"t":"rydin6","kind":"idea","txt":"Speaking of 'place attachment' rather than NIMBYism is a way to resist the pejorative label when residents defend the place that shapes who they are."},{"t":"alex","kind":"quote","txt":"Planning theoreticians are in a state of turmoil. Nothing is accepted, everything is questioned."},{"t":"alex","kind":"quote","txt":"Each of these models is almost a mirror image of the other, focusing exactly on what its counterpart neglects."},{"t":"alex","kind":"quote","txt":"It does not take an extreme degree of cynicism to recognize that it is strategic action that describes interactive behavior most accurately"},{"t":"alex","kind":"quote","txt":"Yet in any contingency model this selection is essentially arbitrary, and any attempt to justify it is the beginning of an infinite regress."},{"t":"alex","kind":"quote","txt":"A contingency framework offers the only hope, perhaps no more than a hope, of a paradigm that can incorporate complexity"},{"t":"alex","kind":"idea","txt":"Planning's reactions to the collapse of the rational paradigm can be sorted into four kinds: ritual, avoidance, abandonment and search."},{"t":"alex","kind":"idea","txt":"The quarrel between procedural and substantive theory was really about the right level of abstraction: a contrived conflict between complementary concepts."},{"t":"alex","kind":"idea","txt":"Mediation counts as consensus-oriented action only with a neutral participant; once the parties pursue their own goals it is strategic action."},{"t":"rc","kind":"quote","txt":"Rational decision-making by ALL actors involved in planning"},{"t":"rc","kind":"quote","txt":"Insider landowners benefit from urban scarcity rents when growth is focused inward."},{"t":"rc","kind":"quote","txt":"Don't just include theories as an after-thought. Use them to formulate a hypothesis about a specific relationship you could study"},{"t":"rc","kind":"idea","txt":"Rather than only restricting short-stay rentals, a city can clarify rights and cut bargaining costs: tradable hosting-night permits, homeowners' association talks, a hosting tax paid into a neighbourhood fund."},{"t":"rc","kind":"idea","txt":"Uniform caps on short-stay rental are hard to enforce: rentals are hard to detect, platforms withhold data, inspections are costly and the problem may simply move elsewhere."},{"t":"rc","kind":"idea","txt":"A farmland law that caps sale prices and bars non-farmers from buying farmland leaves no speculative lobby for urban expansion, so densification becomes the rational strategy."},{"t":"rc","kind":"idea","txt":"Unlike rational-comprehensive planning, which sets an ideal standard for decisions, this is an analytical framework for how planning actually works, with market and state failure."},{"t":"rc","kind":"idea","txt":"Whether the factory may make noise or the homeowner may have quiet, the insulation gets installed; the only difference is who pays whom."},{"t":"p1","kind":"quote","txt":"In my opinion, aiming to achieve consensus among all stakeholders is futile"},{"t":"p1","kind":"quote","txt":"It has become politically impossible not to be a supporter of a sustainable development, so there is a clear danger that the concept will be watered out"},{"t":"p1","kind":"quote","txt":"If the members of a local planning committee agree on the statement that car traffic does not represent any environmental problem, this conclusion will not for that reason be true!"},{"t":"p1","kind":"idea","txt":"Norwegians and Danes now have over 50 m2 of floor area each, twice as much as a generation ago; raising this further is hard to defend while poor countries must never reach it."},{"t":"p1","kind":"idea","txt":"Planners must add the 'city in ecology' view (the city's footprint far beyond its hinterland) to the 'ecology within the city' view of parks and clean air."},{"t":"p1","kind":"idea","txt":"Incrementalism fails for sustainability: if current practice is unsustainable, small steps never change course and radical alternatives are never discussed."},{"t":"p2","kind":"quote","txt":"from the city as a sustainability problem to the city as a sustainability solution"},{"t":"p2","kind":"quote","txt":"not simply a matter of urbanists turning to the environment but also of environmentalists turning to the city"},{"t":"p2","kind":"idea","txt":"The sustainability conversation was not always urban; it became urban, an outcome that was neither necessary nor inevitable."},{"t":"p2","kind":"idea","txt":"Sprawl, informal settlements and climate change each first got a non-city answer and later a city answer, moving policy from 'less city' to 'more city'."},{"t":"p2","kind":"idea","txt":"Early critics of suburban sprawl blamed economic growth itself; their critique was often anti-capitalist rather than just anti-suburban."},{"t":"p2","kind":"idea","txt":"Urban sustainability thinking should be studied historically, multi-spatially, politically and representationally, including the images that make policies look like common sense."},{"t":"p3","kind":"quote","txt":"Nothing inherent in the discipline steers planners either toward environmental protection or toward economic development"},{"t":"p3","kind":"quote","txt":"We are all unavoidably anthropocentric; the question is which anthropomorphic values and priorities we will apply to the natural and the social world around us."},{"t":"p3","kind":"quote","txt":"the development conflict is where the real action for planners will be"},{"t":"p3","kind":"idea","txt":"Pre-industrial societies had no choice but to be sustainable; modern societies must choose it voluntarily, so the path to sustainability lies forward, not behind us."},{"t":"p3","kind":"idea","txt":"A lumberjack is not less 'close to nature' than a weekend hiker: different groups hold different conceptions and uses of nature."},{"t":"p3","kind":"idea","txt":"Planners should translate between the languages of economics, ecology and social justice in both directions, so the economic language does not dominate like a colonial lingua franca."},{"t":"p4","kind":"quote","txt":"the battle for space is about to intensify as there are more claims than land"},{"t":"p4","kind":"quote","txt":"the port authority seems to 'mind its own business' more"},{"t":"p4","kind":"idea","txt":"Moving port activity to peripheral downstream sites used to defuse conflict, but whole port regions are now so intertwined with the city that this escape is no longer viable."},{"t":"p4","kind":"idea","txt":"Land can be physically vacant yet unusable once a port has used up its environmental space, such as its noise capacity."},{"t":"p4","kind":"idea","txt":"In Dutch open planning the informal trajectory comes first; the formal procedure arrives towards the end and mainly codifies earlier agreements."},{"t":"p4","kind":"idea","txt":"Active land policy turns the municipality into regulator and land developer at once, and it only works well in rising markets."},{"t":"p5","kind":"idea","txt":"After the 2011 chemical fire, planning for one Dutch port village shifted from a strategic port vision to compensation planning with a special regulation and extra investments."},{"t":"p5","kind":"idea","txt":"A village next to a growing port can stay and hold its ground, slowly disappear, or be moved as a whole: three governance approaches with European examples."},{"t":"p5","kind":"idea","txt":"Whether villagers resisting port and energy expansion are simply NIMBYs, and who should take the final decision, are open questions of a just outcome."},{"t":"p6","kind":"quote","txt":"it is a conceptual rather than a physical location"},{"t":"p6","kind":"quote","txt":"power is used (by actors in a particular context) to define what is counted as rationality and so what is counted as reality"},{"t":"p6","kind":"quote","txt":"a democratic government 'knows what the people want'"},{"t":"p6","kind":"idea","txt":"Apartheid planners used Garden Cities, neighbourhood units and Radburn layouts as 'best practice' to justify racial segregation and forced removals."},{"t":"p6","kind":"idea","txt":"Every planning idea that travels should come with the story of the context and preconditions in which it once worked, instead of being copied as a best practice."},{"t":"p6","kind":"idea","txt":"The state is not a single actor: officials hold many wills to govern and diverse improvement agendas, and they conflict within the state itself."},{"t":"p7","kind":"idea","txt":"Since 1949 the country had no basic or superior plan for its territorial space, a root cause of disordered urban expansion, farmland loss and ecological decline."},{"t":"p7","kind":"idea","txt":"Spatial planning has moved from a single actor to multiple actors, from one target to many, and from top-down control towards more decentralised local approaches."},{"t":"p7","kind":"idea","txt":"Ecological red lines must keep ecosystems connected so that protected areas do not end up as isolated 'ecological islands'."},{"t":"p7","kind":"idea","txt":"Plans were spread over more than ten national departments, with different land classes and horizons, producing planning 'dislocation, offside and vacancy'."},{"t":"p7","kind":"idea","txt":"Legalised participation in urban regeneration became 'authoritarian deliberation': it reduced conflict but came late and left power unequal."},{"t":"p7","kind":"idea","txt":"Eco-cities combine three trends: an eco-state regulating carbon, local entrepreneurialism using them for growth, and policy mobility of foreign technologies."},{"t":"p8","kind":"quote","txt":"Cities are not bounded containers but embedded, territorial contexts within wider relational networks of flows"},{"t":"p8","kind":"quote","txt":"Sustainable urban mobility provides orientation and a suite of possibilities rather than a prescribed route map"},{"t":"p8","kind":"quote","txt":"Innovation is in the particular form of reconfiguration rather than individual technologies"},{"t":"p8","kind":"idea","txt":"In one city several regimes, such as car, bus and cycling systems, compete, coexist and complement each other, rather than one niche fighting one regime."},{"t":"p8","kind":"idea","txt":"Experiments do not happen in sealed laboratories: what matters is how new elements relate materially to infrastructure that already exists."},{"t":"p8","kind":"idea","txt":"Choosing the unit of analysis in an urban transition is a strategic, partly normative choice for which the researcher is responsible."},{"t":"p9","kind":"quote","txt":"It is at heart a form of disciplinary power."},{"t":"p9","kind":"quote","txt":"new thinking about their 'failure' will be needed"},{"t":"p9","kind":"quote","txt":"such goals for urban improvement are not necessarily radical in nature and indeed can seek to reinforce the status quo"},{"t":"p9","kind":"idea","txt":"Experimentation is a form of Deweyan inquiry: an undetermined situation is problematised and then given a determination and a solution."},{"t":"p9","kind":"idea","txt":"What makes a laboratory is the combination of intervention with observation in an 'improved' environment, balanced between control and contingency."},{"t":"p9","kind":"idea","txt":"Living labs lean towards open, contingent forms because municipalities, universities and community groups have limited autonomy and resources."},{"t":"p10","kind":"quote","txt":"Digitalization of planning is not just the straightforward implementation and use of new technologies."},{"t":"p10","kind":"quote","txt":"Any typology should also encompass more than the narrow operational objectivity of digital planning as a set of tools."},{"t":"p10","kind":"idea","txt":"Planning support systems are tools used within digital planning, while Planning Support Science is its scientific prerequisite."},{"t":"p10","kind":"idea","txt":"Unlike smart city and platform urbanism, digital planning is government-led and consciously steered by goals of sustainable and inclusive development."},{"t":"p10","kind":"idea","txt":"Politicians with tight budgets may be tempted to replace human-centred planning processes with AI tools, which threatens representation and justice."},{"t":"p10","kind":"idea","txt":"A review of definitions is needed so the new field does not suffer the conceptual fragmentation that weakened the smart city concept."},{"t":"p11","kind":"quote","txt":"threatens to erode public trust and professional legitimacy"},{"t":"p11","kind":"quote","txt":"In effect, the profession risks deskilling as reliance on algorithmic outputs displaces accumulated practitioner wisdom"},{"t":"p11","kind":"quote","txt":"AI disrupts these foundations by recasting city-making as a purely technical optimization problem"},{"t":"p11","kind":"idea","txt":"Earlier tools such as CAD and GIS left interpretation and negotiation to humans; the new tools automate the critical thinking that defined the planner's craft."},{"t":"p11","kind":"idea","txt":"Planners should act as 'sense-makers' of algorithmic output, keeping final responsibility as doctors do with clinical decision-support systems."},{"t":"p11","kind":"idea","txt":"'Slow planning' based on face-to-face deliberation should serve as a deliberate counterweight to computational speed."},{"t":"p12","kind":"quote","txt":"planning has bound itself into a pro-growth agenda that is mentally and institutionally hard to overcome"},{"t":"p12","kind":"quote","txt":"It is particularly worrying to see how the pursuit of sustainability has been subsumed to the pro-growth agenda"},{"t":"p12","kind":"quote","txt":"Planners should consider part of their role to voice uncomfortable truths and to avoid the easier path or discourse of growth-dependence"},{"t":"p12","kind":"idea","txt":"Land value capture should be left out of the planner's toolbox, because it depends on creating ever more monetised development value."},{"t":"p12","kind":"idea","txt":"Plans should set ceilings on development, not only floors or targets, and divert building from high-price areas to areas of high need."},{"t":"p12","kind":"idea","txt":"Shrinking areas could serve as testbeds for new ideas, but shrinkage is not success in itself, and fast-growing areas may be better placed to lead."},{"t":"p13","kind":"quote","txt":"these championing cases of sustainable urban development in Europe cannot be classified as radical urban transformations"},{"t":"p13","kind":"quote","txt":"this lack of consideration of social issues in spatial planning is a considerable disparity"},{"t":"p13","kind":"idea","txt":"Even a pandemic produced concrete acceleration of green space planning in only one of the eleven cities studied."},{"t":"p13","kind":"idea","txt":"The root problem is limited resources and staff capacity, caused by system-level conditions beyond green space departments or even municipalities."},{"t":"p13","kind":"idea","txt":"Interviewed officials may be preservers of the status quo or agents of change, so future research should also hear active citizen groups."},{"t":"p13","kind":"idea","txt":"Integrating green space into wider planning brings influence and resources, but also trade-offs and heavier workloads for staff."}],"PAIRS":[{"t":"buit","term":"Prioritarianism","def":"Below the sufficiency threshold, priority goes to those furthest below it, the worst off (Crisp's compassion principle)."},{"t":"buit","term":"Limitarianism","def":"Adds a ceiling on wealth to the sufficiency floor, defended instrumentally by Robeyns; Raworth's doughnut is a version."},{"t":"buit","term":"Budget approach","def":"Affordability test: is disposable income minus housing costs below the budget needed to run a household? Captures payment risk."},{"t":"buit","term":"Negative test of universality","def":"Hayek's test for just rules: general, about interaction between individuals, and forbidding unjust conduct rather than enforcing outcomes."},{"t":"buit","term":"Spatial primary goods","def":"Moroni's list: adequate and affordable housing, access to jobs, nearby greenery, a safe living environment."},{"t":"buit","term":"Background justice","def":"Justice of the basic structure of society (Rawls), as opposed to allocative 'who gets what' decisions at city level."},{"t":"fw","term":"Heuristic","def":"A tool that helps you find things out without guaranteeing a complete answer; Rydin's view of what theory is."},{"t":"fw","term":"Modernist settlement","def":"Latour's label for the view that the world is 'out there' and only needs looking at to reveal itself."},{"t":"fw","term":"Ontology","def":"Assumptions about how the world is; together with epistemology and values it makes up a theory."},{"t":"fw","term":"Epistemology","def":"A position on how knowledge can be generated through research; one of the three parts of any theory."},{"t":"fw","term":"Monist vs pluralist","def":"Whether only one form of knowledge counts, or many forms and many knowers should play a role in planning."},{"t":"fw","term":"Praxis","def":"The aim of phronetic research: action that changes practice, aware of 'the ineliminable presence of power'."},{"t":"hall","term":"Advocacy planning","def":"Davidoff (1965): planners work bottom-up for diverse groups, make goals explicit and present alternatives."},{"t":"hall","term":"Disjointed incrementalism","def":"Decisions in a pluralist world made in small steps through bargaining, since no one has total knowledge or power."},{"t":"hall","term":"New Proceduralism","def":"Hall's label for 1980s and 1990s practice reduced to a codified, bureaucratised regulatory routine."},{"t":"hall","term":"Insurgent planning histories","def":"Sandercock's counter to the 'official story': community-based planning, often by groups excluded from the profession."},{"t":"hall","term":"Paradigm crisis","def":"Mid-1970s state of planning theory: by separating process from substance it undermined its own legitimacy."},{"t":"hall","term":"Collective consumption","def":"Castells: state-provided housing, schools and transport that reproduce the labour force and dampen class conflict."},{"t":"marx","term":"Objectification","def":"Labour embodied in an object; under estrangement it appears as loss of the object and bondage to it."},{"t":"marx","term":"Self-estrangement","def":"Marx's name for estrangement within the act of production itself, as opposed to estrangement of the thing."},{"t":"marx","term":"Inorganic body","def":"Marx's term for nature as far as it is not itself the human body: humans live from it in continuous exchange."},{"t":"marx","term":"Primordial condition","def":"The fictitious starting point economists invent to explain things, which only pushes the question back."},{"t":"marx","term":"Progressive planner","def":"HC4 figure (Madden and Marcuse): plans for the working class and poor through decommodified housing."},{"t":"marx","term":"Better payment for the slave","def":"Marx's verdict on an enforced wage rise: it would not restore human status and dignity to labour."},{"t":"cozz","term":"Synchronous degradation","def":"Buildings constructed all at once age and run down at the same time, a typical risk of large estates"},{"t":"cozz","term":"Intricacy (Jacobs)","def":"A variety of reasons for which people come to a neighbourhood; a sign of lasting attractiveness"},{"t":"cozz","term":"Anti-commons","def":"Communal ownership in which significant change needs the consent of large majorities, so adaptation stalls"},{"t":"cozz","term":"Demand-driven development","def":"Small developments initiated by future users, such as self-building, which spread design control"},{"t":"cozz","term":"Robust skeleton","def":"Main infrastructure and public spaces designed top-down, on which small private changes can occur over time"},{"t":"cozz","term":"Civitas (Pirenne)","def":"11th-century city in which owning a dwelling made one a citizen, driving incremental growth"},{"t":"gov","term":"Merit good","def":"Something society collectively decides people should have, such as good housing, which markets under-supply"},{"t":"gov","term":"Public good","def":"Once supplied to some, others cannot be excluded from it; street lighting is the classic example"},{"t":"gov","term":"Missing market","def":"No price rations use, so resources such as beautiful views or information are over-used or under-supplied"},{"t":"gov","term":"Triple bottom line","def":"Social, environmental and economic goals used to check how comprehensive a planning exercise is"},{"t":"gov","term":"Plan implementation","def":"Smith (2018): evaluating whether the stated goals and objectives of a plan were actually achieved"},{"t":"hayek","term":"Social ends","def":"Identical ends of many individuals, or ends people will help others pursue in return for help with their own"},{"t":"hayek","term":"Talking shop","def":"How parliaments come to be seen under planning: ineffective assemblies unable to carry out their tasks"},{"t":"hayek","term":"Cry for an economic dictator","def":"The demand to give one person full powers once parliament cannot produce a comprehensive plan"},{"t":"hayek","term":"Unitary conception","def":"What a real economic plan needs, so that it cannot be assembled by voting on it clause by clause"},{"t":"hayek","term":"Judging the case on its merits","def":"The usual description of arbitrary decisions made by an authority with discretion over particular cases"},{"t":"hayek","term":"Totalitarian","def":"Organising all of society for one end and refusing any autonomous sphere where individual ends are supreme"},{"t":"know","term":"Hybrid forums","def":"Rip's arenas for agonistic, collective learning, where lay and expert knowledge claims are engaged and tested"},{"t":"know","term":"Socially robust knowledge","def":"Nowotny: knowledge tested for validity, involving an extended group of experts, and repeatedly tested and modified"},{"t":"know","term":"Realist constructivism","def":"Wynne's label for knowledge that is socially constructed yet engages with a material reality"},{"t":"know","term":"Epistemology of multiplicity","def":"Sandercock: knowing through dialogue, experience, local, symbolic and contemplative ways, not only science"},{"t":"know","term":"State B2","def":"In Figure 1: the actual outcome after planning, which differs from both the planned and the unplanned future"},{"t":"know","term":"Mode 2 knowledge production","def":"Gibbons et al.: practitioners and users actively take part in producing knowledge, alongside researchers"},{"t":"davoudi","term":"Bricolage (Ball)","def":"Policy-making as borrowing and copying bits of ideas, producing ramshackle compromises reworked in practice."},{"t":"davoudi","term":"Problem of little effect","def":"Weiss: research, even when commissioned by government, often sits on the shelf and is rarely used."},{"t":"davoudi","term":"Paradox of policy analysis","def":"Shulock: the mismatch between how the policy process should work and its messy, political reality."},{"t":"davoudi","term":"Survey-analysis-plan","def":"Geddes's linear sequence for making plans, criticised for skipping problem definition and ending at the plan."},{"t":"davoudi","term":"Knowing what vs knowing how","def":"Substantive knowledge of how cities work versus knowledge of the planning process itself."},{"t":"davoudi","term":"Evidence-informed society","def":"Davoudi's aim (after Smith): broad capacity to use available evidence, instead of policy spoon-fed by research."},{"t":"scott","term":"Death of the street","def":"Le Corbusier's second principle: full separation of pedestrians from vehicles, and of slow from fast traffic."},{"t":"scott","term":"Eyes on the street","def":"Jacobs: shopkeepers, passers-by and residents at windows who informally watch a busy sidewalk and keep it safe."},{"t":"scott","term":"Sidewalk terms","def":"Jacobs: casual acquaintance among people who meet in the street, enough for small favours without friendship."},{"t":"scott","term":"Monocephalic city","def":"Le Corbusier's centralised city whose single core, like a brain, issues commands to the rest of the country."},{"t":"scott","term":"Public visiting room","def":"Holston's image of the old Brazilian square: open to all classes and used for play, shopping, meeting and politics."},{"t":"scott","term":"Superquadra","def":"Brasília's uniform residential block of about 1,500 to 2,500 people, each with its own nursery and primary school."},{"t":"needham","term":"Right of support","def":"A neighbour's right to take to court someone whose digging endangers the foundations of her house"},{"t":"needham","term":"Covenant (housing estate)","def":"Buyers' voluntary self-restriction against acts that harm the estate's amenity, enforced by a residents' association"},{"t":"needham","term":"Conservation agreement","def":"A trust buys a farmer's development rights and never uses them, so the land stays open while the farmer keeps ownership"},{"t":"needham","term":"Privately owned public space","def":"A space owned and maintained privately but open to all, with access and upkeep secured by planning conditions"},{"t":"needham","term":"Takings","def":"Restrictions under public law that remove an owner's rights, for which the state may have to pay compensation"},{"t":"needham","term":"Regulation","def":"Making building and development conditional on a permission granted only if the initiative conforms to a plan"},{"t":"ni","term":"Boundary rule","def":"IAD rule specifying how actors enter or leave a position, such as membership of a fishing cooperative"},{"t":"ni","term":"Aggregation rule","def":"IAD rule determining how individual actions combine into a collective decision, such as majority voting"},{"t":"ni","term":"Payoff rule","def":"IAD rule assigning rewards or sanctions to actions and outcomes, such as fines for breaking quotas"},{"t":"ni","term":"Goodness-of-fit","def":"Reproduction of arrangements seen as legitimate because they match established templates (DiMaggio and Powell)"},{"t":"ni","term":"Critical juncture","def":"A short period in which agents' choices have a heightened probability of affecting the outcome"},{"t":"ni","term":"Problem of commitment","def":"Ostrom's puzzle: how users can be sure others will also hold back, which self-made institutions solve"},{"t":"olson","term":"Dominant strategy","def":"A choice that leaves a player better off whatever the other player decides to do"},{"t":"olson","term":"Nash equilibrium","def":"Outcome in which each player picks the best reply, assuming the other also plays its best reply"},{"t":"olson","term":"Focal point","def":"An obvious shared reference, such as a usual meeting place, that lets people coordinate without talking"},{"t":"olson","term":"Pareto-superior","def":"An outcome that is better for everyone involved than the outcome it is compared with"},{"t":"olson","term":"Hegemonic stability","def":"A dominant power bears the costs of an open world order because it gains so much from it"},{"t":"olson","term":"Unconditional cooperator","def":"Someone who contributes even when others do not, helping a group reach the threshold of stable cooperation"},{"t":"taylor","term":"Heresthetics","def":"Clingermayer's route to rent-seeking through the intentional manipulation of an issue"},{"t":"taylor","term":"Happenstance","def":"Clingermayer's route in which altruistic goals unintentionally line up with rent-seeking ones"},{"t":"taylor","term":"Hypothecated tax","def":"A charge whose revenue is earmarked for one purpose, such as infrastructure in new suburbs"},{"t":"taylor","term":"Purchaser pays","def":"The model, presented as fairer, that moved the GAIC's formal liability from current owners to buyers"},{"t":"taylor","term":"Taxed Out!","def":"Landowner group that campaigned in 2009 and 2010 against Melbourne's new infrastructure charge"},{"t":"taylor","term":"Incidence (of a charge)","def":"Who ultimately bears the cost, whoever formally pays; for betterment taxes it is disputed"},{"t":"dembski","term":"Habitualisation","def":"Berger & Luckmann's ongoing process of normalisation through which social codes gain institutional depth"},{"t":"dembski","term":"Reciprocity of expectations","def":"Norm holders share typical expectations that everyone will act in the customary way"},{"t":"dembski","term":"Logic of appropriateness","def":"Acting on what is seen as fitting and legitimate, ranked higher than efficiency (March & Olsen)"},{"t":"dembski","term":"Banal nationalism (Billig)","def":"Everyday, mostly unconscious reminders, like a flag on display, that keep a collective identity alive"},{"t":"dembski","term":"Social magic","def":"Imposing a new social reality on a person or region by representation, though little has materially changed"},{"t":"dembski","term":"Encoded message (step 2)","def":"The social pattern a marker symbolises: the past, a transformation, or the past bent towards a new future"},{"t":"dembski","term":"Zwischenstadt (Sieverts)","def":"The in-between city: neither compact city, nor countryside, nor classic suburb"},{"t":"moroni","term":"Principle of peculiarity","def":"Organisations serve specific ends through commands; institutions are rules with meta-ends such as peaceful coexistence"},{"t":"moroni","term":"Principle of knowability","def":"Rules must be clear and known in advance so people can act on them; cryptic or short-lived law fails"},{"t":"moroni","term":"Principle of sense-making","def":"Rules must mean something to actors; their meaning cannot be created by administrative fiat"},{"t":"moroni","term":"Principle of gradualism","def":"Reform step by step, remedying recognisable defects in rules whose development we can reconstruct"},{"t":"moroni","term":"Successor institutions","def":"What reformed rules almost always are, since nobody intervenes on a blank slate but within a consolidated framework"},{"t":"moroni","term":"Pattern-predictability","def":"What open rules should guarantee: behaviour that can be foreseen in standard situations, not in every detail"},{"t":"moroni","term":"Institutional intervention","def":"Changing a society's basic rules of conduct, which needs a different approach from building an organisation"},{"t":"sorensen","term":"Lot levies","def":"Per-lot charges on subdivision developers to pay for off-site infrastructure such as sewage treatment plants"},{"t":"sorensen","term":"Antecedent conditions","def":"Existing institutions, shared norms on feasible change, capacity and actors' power before a juncture"},{"t":"sorensen","term":"Path dependence","def":"Once an option is chosen, returning to the point where other alternatives were open becomes ever harder"},{"t":"sorensen","term":"Positive feedback","def":"Self-reinforcing effects that make some institutions especially hard to change over time"},{"t":"sorensen","term":"Process tracing","def":"Method of following causal chains through a sequence of events, used to narrate Ontario's planning history"},{"t":"sorensen","term":"Creatures of the province","def":"Status of Canadian municipalities: little legal autonomy, with powers a province can change unilaterally"},{"t":"sorensen","term":"Community Benefits Charge","def":"Limited levy that Bill 108 (2019) introduced in place of negotiated Section 37 density-bonus agreements"},{"t":"fog","term":"Socialisation of land control","def":"Shifting some say over land use from individual owners to public bodies, through zoning, public works and planning"},{"t":"fog","term":"Functionalism","def":"Flaw of explaining why planning exists simply by its usefulness to capital; a common critique of Foglesong"},{"t":"fog","term":"Insulation of planning","def":"Placing land-use decisions in expert commissions, technical procedures and independent boards, away from voters"},{"t":"fog","term":"Fractions of capital","def":"Groups of capitalists with diverging interests whose conflicts, with owners and workers, shape planning outcomes"},{"t":"fog","term":"Liberal/pluralist view","def":"Account in which planning serves the public interest or fixes market failure, with the state as neutral arbiter"},{"t":"fog","term":"Planning the Capitalist City","def":"Foglesong's 1986 book tracing US urban planning from the colonial era to the 1920s"},{"t":"harvey","term":"Flexible accumulation","def":"Post-Fordist regime of more mobile, malleable production and labour; the entrepreneurial turn may have eased the shift to it"},{"t":"harvey","term":"Boosterism","def":"Aggressive local promotion to attract investment; a long US tradition revived after federal cuts in 1972"},{"t":"harvey","term":"Growth machine","def":"Molotch's label for the coalition of developers, financiers and business that pushes local growth"},{"t":"harvey","term":"Reification of cities","def":"Talking of 'cities' as active agents when they are mere things, which hides the actors behind the process"},{"t":"harvey","term":"Dual city","def":"Regenerated inner city surrounded by a sea of increasing impoverishment, masked by successful projects"},{"t":"harvey","term":"Bread and circuses","def":"Roman formula revived through urban spectacle: image buys civic pride and social control even when welfare is lacking"},{"t":"hh","term":"Shadow rents","def":"Rents calculated but never paid for city services and administration; a way of getting used to the market game"},{"t":"hh","term":"Premises Centre","def":"Helsinki body founded in 2005 to manage all city real estate and charge its own departments market rent"},{"t":"hh","term":"Real Estate Committee","def":"Politically elected body deciding on city land; it unanimously backed selling the shoreline sites in 2004–05"},{"t":"hh","term":"Development contract","def":"Agreement between a municipality and a private landowner about a plan and its implementation, legalised by the 1999 act"},{"t":"hh","term":"Household developers","def":"Established Helsinki builders such as SRV, NCC and Skanska, with long ties to the city's land department"},{"t":"hh","term":"Social-mix policy","def":"Joint metropolitan aim to prevent segregation by building social housing in all municipalities and neighbourhoods"},{"t":"savini","term":"Generator company","def":"Development consortium or holding that sets up a project vehicle and collects the project's gains"},{"t":"savini","term":"Supply-driven programming","def":"A 'calculate-build and then sell' logic that reduces the variety of local markets and social demands"},{"t":"savini","term":"Tax Increment Financing","def":"Fiscal tool that guarantees project yields or funds works from expected rises in a zone's property tax"},{"t":"savini","term":"Betterment values","def":"Unearned land value increases from private initiative that cities may try to capture through regulation"},{"t":"savini","term":"Immobiliare Cascina Rubina","def":"The SPV Gruppo Pasini set up for the Falck site in 2000, later sold to Risanamento along with its debt"},{"t":"savini","term":"Agenzia Sviluppo Nord Milano","def":"Renamed public-private agency (PSTA), 80% municipal, that pursued reindustrialisation of the Falck site"},{"t":"bossuyt","term":"Decommodification","def":"Holding housing for its use value rather than its exchange value: a home to live in, not an asset for profit"},{"t":"bossuyt","term":"Use-right concession","def":"Dandara's unusual tenure form: residents receive a right to use the building instead of individual private titles"},{"t":"bossuyt","term":"Porosity and proliferation","def":"De Angelis and Stavrides: commons survive through open, permeable boundaries and by spreading beyond themselves"},{"t":"bossuyt","term":"Extensão","def":"Brazilian model in which public universities collaborate with societal actors such as social movements"},{"t":"bossuyt","term":"Indebted subjects","def":"What individual titles plus mortgages make of households, turning collective political claims into personal debt"},{"t":"bossuyt","term":"Archipelago of the commons","def":"Linked commons that support each other instead of standing alone as islands in commodified waters"},{"t":"gurran","term":"Green Paper (NSW, 2012)","def":"96-page discussion document of July 2012 on a new planning system, which drew over 1,200 submissions"},{"t":"gurran","term":"Better Planning Network","def":"Coalition of 300+ resident groups and councils whose campaign outside formal consultation helped stall the Bill"},{"t":"gurran","term":"Economic dividend","def":"What the Property Council said a new planning system should be geared towards delivering for NSW"},{"t":"gurran","term":"Antidevelopment culture","def":"Australand's label for the community attitude it said must shift towards a 'progressive' one"},{"t":"gurran","term":"Section 106","def":"English value capture tool that let councils keep securing land for social housing within market schemes"},{"t":"gurran","term":"Development contributions","def":"Australian infrastructure charges on developers under a user-pays logic; industry wanted broad-based funding instead"},{"t":"pruijt","term":"Licensed squatters","def":"UK label for people renting short-life property; their groups ran such housing and had to stop organising squatting"},{"t":"pruijt","term":"Sweat equity","def":"Homesteaders' own renovation work on abandoned city buildings, given in return for eventual ownership"},{"t":"pruijt","term":"Tenant Interim Lease","def":"New York programme letting tenants self-manage tax-foreclosed city buildings with a route to cooperative ownership"},{"t":"pruijt","term":"Slightly repressive tolerance","def":"Soja's label for Amsterdam's policy of buying squats and handing them to housing associations"},{"t":"pruijt","term":"Disinvestment cycle","def":"US spiral of milking, bank redlining, arson and abandonment that left New York tenements in ruins"},{"t":"pruijt","term":"Poverty pimps","def":"Squatters' name for non-profits accused of channelling low-income housing money into executives' salaries"},{"t":"raco","term":"Managerial localism","def":"Critics' label for SRB: reduced resources distributed through competition, with strong central controls"},{"t":"raco","term":"Capacity building","def":"Use of 10% of SRB funds to train community representatives and give them administrative resources"},{"t":"raco","term":"New prudentialism","def":"Dean: risk minimisation tied to self-care, with citizens monitoring their own health and welfare"},{"t":"raco","term":"Directed self-government","def":"Communities govern themselves, but in forms, roles and duties laid down from above by central guidance"},{"t":"raco","term":"Accountable body","def":"Body each SRB partnership had to set up, legally liable for its debts and the conditions on public money"},{"t":"raco","term":"Vertical accountability","def":"Answering upwards to funders and targets, instead of to local communities judging spending from below"},{"t":"rydin6","term":"Environmental justice","def":"Frame born in US struggles against toxic waste sites placed near communities of colour, with Love Canal as a trigger case"},{"t":"rydin6","term":"Fragmented authoritarianism","def":"Li and Liu: an authoritarian policy style used in China to overcome the scattering of resources among many actors"},{"t":"rydin6","term":"Strategic selectivity","def":"Li and Liu: the state spreads resources unevenly between towns and villages, so a full regime forms only in some places"},{"t":"rydin6","term":"Tactical coalition","def":"Trapenberg Frick: a pragmatic alliance of polarised groups, typed as ephemeral, emergent or established"},{"t":"rydin6","term":"Naming, blaming and claiming","def":"How Turin's regime built its agenda: shared problem perception, a story of the path not taken, then core purposes"},{"t":"rydin6","term":"Monitory civil society","def":"REDWatch's stance in Sydney: watching and publicising state plans instead of seeking consensus, which gave it informal influence"},{"t":"alex","term":"Ritual response","def":"Keeping the rational model in teaching and practice with only token acknowledgement of its anomalies"},{"t":"alex","term":"Avoidance response","def":"Swapping in descriptive decision models such as satisficing, or modifying rationality without following through"},{"t":"alex","term":"Abandonment response","def":"Dropping general process models for intuition and experience, or for a substantive political ideology"},{"t":"alex","term":"Pre-interactive deliberation","def":"How an actor decides which course to pursue before entering the arena of discussion: the gap in TCA"},{"t":"alex","term":"Reflexive consciousness","def":"Habermas: the good is how participants would choose to act if they communicated openly and without coercion"},{"t":"alex","term":"Universalization principle","def":"A norm can be valid only if it is unconditionally and freely accepted by all affected parties"},{"t":"rc","term":"Waterbed effect","def":"Unintended outcome in which a rule does not solve a problem but pushes it to another place or form"},{"t":"rc","term":"Holdout","def":"A party that delays agreeing in order to extract a bigger share, a strategic cost of striking bargains"},{"t":"rc","term":"Dual role of the state","def":"It creates and protects private property rights, and it also makes public policies aimed at those same owners"},{"t":"rc","term":"Landowner 'veto right'","def":"Owners' de facto power to block a plan by not developing, since planning must sway private decisions"},{"t":"rc","term":"Value capture","def":"Mechanisms by which the public recovers part of the land value rise that its own planning decisions create"},{"t":"rc","term":"Tradable permits (Airbnb)","def":"A Coasean tool that creates a market for short-stay nights: total nights are capped, but hosts can trade them"},{"t":"p1","term":"Weak sustainability","def":"Natural and human-made capital are treated as substitutable, so losing nature is fine if total capital is kept (Solow)."},{"t":"p1","term":"Strong sustainability","def":"Natural capital is complementary to, not interchangeable with, other capital; planetary boundaries set the limits."},{"t":"p1","term":"Sufficiency","def":"'Seeking enough when more is possible': self-limitation of material needs plus an egalitarian distribution."},{"t":"p1","term":"Consistency (Huber)","def":"Qualitative change in the industrial metabolism through fundamental innovations that mimic natural cycles."},{"t":"p1","term":"Bridge sustainability","def":"Vallance et al.: changing social behaviour in order to reach bio-physical environmental goals."},{"t":"p1","term":"Post-political sustainability","def":"Swyngedouw: a frame impossible not to support, which hides the political choice of which natures we want."},{"t":"p2","term":"Paths not taken","def":"Alternatives in the history of urban sustainability that mainstream policy ignored, such as linking poverty and environment."},{"t":"p2","term":"System-affirmative","def":"Accepting the existing economic system, as mainstream city-centric sustainability policy does."},{"t":"p2","term":"Dystopian Anthropocene","def":"McPhearson et al.: a future mirroring today's status quo, with inequality, growth-focus and worsening environmental ills."},{"t":"p2","term":"SDG 11","def":"The standalone urban goal that made the urban agenda universal after the Millennium Development Goals."},{"t":"p2","term":"Externalisation (Krähmer)","def":"Counting only emissions inside city limits while residents' consumption-based emissions stay high."},{"t":"p2","term":"Operational landscapes","def":"Hidden spaces of energy, extraction, logistics and waste beyond the city that keep it running."},{"t":"p3","term":"Trickle-down environmentalism","def":"Growing the economy first so that society can afford environmental protection later."},{"t":"p3","term":"Green gentrification","def":"Greening raises land values and displaces the residents it was meant to benefit."},{"t":"p3","term":"Just green enough","def":"Modest, community-directed greening scaled to existing residents' needs instead of flagship projects."},{"t":"p3","term":"Wicked problem","def":"An issue with no definitive formulation or stopping rule, entangled with values and other issues (Rittel and Webber)."},{"t":"p3","term":"Mess (Ackoff)","def":"A system of interrelated problems that cannot be solved by solving each part separately."},{"t":"p3","term":"Bioregionalism","def":"Rescaling economy and community to ecological regions; risks a utopian spatial fix and underdevelopment."},{"t":"p4","term":"Double hat problem","def":"A municipality acting both as regulator of land use and as a land developer seeking returns."},{"t":"p4","term":"Voorkeursrecht","def":"Pre-emption right: the municipality gets the first right to buy when an owner sells land."},{"t":"p4","term":"Erfpacht","def":"Leasehold: the public owner keeps the land and grants long-term use rights; a softer instrument."},{"t":"p4","term":"Extra-legal coordination","def":"Plans, agreements and covenants that are not illegal but not codified in law ('buitenwettelijk')."},{"t":"p4","term":"Complementary task allocation","def":"Omgevingswet principle: the most suitable government level acts, e.g. via a project decision."},{"t":"p4","term":"House of Thorbecke","def":"Coordination structure of the decentralised unitary state linking national, provincial and local levels."},{"t":"p5","term":"Port regionalisation","def":"Ports expanding beyond the original harbour into a wider region, with side effects on nearby places."},{"t":"p5","term":"Spatial externalities","def":"Side effects of port growth borne by surrounding places and people who share little of its benefits."},{"t":"p5","term":"Pro-active stance (Moerdijk)","def":"Positive zoning of industrial activity: deliberately designating where industry may go."},{"t":"p5","term":"PowerPort","def":"Energy-transition and industry expansion around Moerdijk needing about 700 ha, reopening the village's future."},{"t":"p6","term":"Meso-level theory","def":"Mid-level concepts built inductively from cases, tied to their context, between universal and particular."},{"t":"p6","term":"Origin narrative (Healey)","def":"The story of context and preconditions that should travel with any transferred planning idea."},{"t":"p6","term":"Paradigmatic case","def":"A single case with metaphorical, prototypical value that reveals general features of a society."},{"t":"p6","term":"Invited vs invented spaces","def":"Participation offered by the powerful versus arenas that marginalised groups create themselves (Miraftab)."},{"t":"p6","term":"Will to improve vs will to survive","def":"Li's contrast between the urge to govern and improve and the poor's urge to survive and thrive."},{"t":"p6","term":"Shadow state","def":"Brokers and contractors operating around the official state in some postcolonial contexts."},{"t":"p7","term":"Hukou","def":"Household registration by birthplace that ties access to urban public services to urban status."},{"t":"p7","term":"Dual land system","def":"Split between state-owned urban land with tradeable use rights and village collective land."},{"t":"p7","term":"Urban village (Shenzhen)","def":"Former village absorbed by city growth on collective land, housing many migrants."},{"t":"p7","term":"Work unit (danwei)","def":"Self-contained state enterprise or institution that organised housing and services in the socialist city."},{"t":"p7","term":"One Map","def":"Unified digital base of territorial development and protection data underpinning the NTSP."},{"t":"p7","term":"Micro-regeneration","def":"Small-scale renewal of historic areas and urban villages instead of wholesale demolition."},{"t":"p8","term":"Correspondence","def":"How closely an infrastructure system is organised at the city scale: strong for buses, weak for cars."},{"t":"p8","term":"Regime (MLP)","def":"Rules, routines and standards that structure, but do not determine, action in an existing system."},{"t":"p8","term":"Niche (MLP)","def":"A protected space where radical innovations are nurtured before they can compete."},{"t":"p8","term":"Socio-technical landscape","def":"The broad, usually slow-changing outside context: demographics, ideology, climate, macro-economy."},{"t":"p8","term":"Marketised individualisation","def":"State measures that bypass city institutions and target individuals and communities directly."},{"t":"p8","term":"Governance experiment","def":"Restructuring the local state to tackle urgent problems, claim resources or expand authority."},{"t":"p9","term":"Disposition (Easterling)","def":"A propensity within a context, lying in the relationships between parts rather than in the parts."},{"t":"p9","term":"Experimental city","def":"The idea that urban sustainability is increasingly governed through experiments and pilots."},{"t":"p9","term":"Inquiry (Dewey)","def":"Problematising an undetermined situation and then giving it a determination and solution."},{"t":"p9","term":"Obligatory passage point","def":"The enclave's boundary that any lesson must cross before it can be replicated elsewhere."},{"t":"p9","term":"Politics of improvement","def":"How lab priorities are set, which techniques of change dominate and how it is all financed."},{"t":"p9","term":"Pre-figurative display","def":"Showing a sustainable future in one building or district, with little attention to scaling up."},{"t":"p10","term":"Platform urbanism","def":"Urbanisation deeply shaped by the conditions and affordances of digital platforms (Barns)."},{"t":"p10","term":"PPGIS","def":"Public participation GIS: map-based tools that collect citizens' spatial knowledge and views."},{"t":"p10","term":"Smart city","def":"A mostly business-driven concept of ICT-enabled cities, criticised as technocratic."},{"t":"p10","term":"Quadruple helix","def":"Collaboration of government, market parties, societal organisations and education and research."},{"t":"p10","term":"Evidence analytics loop","def":"Batty and Yang's data-driven cycle, paired with a democratic decision-making loop."},{"t":"p10","term":"Agentic AI","def":"AI acting as an autonomous decision-maker rather than as a tool that supports humans."},{"t":"p11","term":"Sense-maker","def":"A planner who interprets AI output in light of local histories, cultures and values."},{"t":"p11","term":"Community-in-the-loop","def":"Marginalised groups taking part in data collection, model building and validation of AI tools."},{"t":"p11","term":"Slow planning","def":"Face-to-face deliberation used as a deliberate counterweight to computational speed."},{"t":"p11","term":"Augmentation argument","def":"The claim that AI frees planners for strategy and value judgements instead of replacing them."},{"t":"p11","term":"Algorithmic audit","def":"Independent checking and certification of AI tools, as is done in finance and healthcare."},{"t":"p12","term":"Eco-modernism","def":"Belief that green technology lets development keep growing while becoming sustainable."},{"t":"p12","term":"Maximum space standards","def":"Upper limits on floor space per dwelling: a contested sufficiency tool next to minimums."},{"t":"p12","term":"Cumulative impacts","def":"Combined effects of individually 'good' projects that together may breach ecological limits."},{"t":"p12","term":"'Do nothing' scenario","def":"Baseline often used to justify new infrastructure, which planners should scrutinise critically."},{"t":"p12","term":"Phase-out planning","def":"Coordinating new infrastructure with planned shrinkage of old, e.g. rail with less air capacity."},{"t":"p12","term":"Hacks","def":"Changes from within existing planning tools, pending a broader new vision for planning."},{"t":"p13","term":"Green space governance","def":"Multi-actor, often decentralised and participatory steering of urban green and blue space."},{"t":"p13","term":"Self-governance","def":"End of the continuum where non-government actors steer green space themselves."},{"t":"p13","term":"Green Area Factor","def":"Helsinki rule requiring enough green space for ecosystem services in new developments."},{"t":"p13","term":"Exchange agreements","def":"Aarhus deals in which developers contribute green space to the common good for their gain."},{"t":"p13","term":"Guilds (gildes)","def":"Utrecht's cross-sector teams linking urban development and green space planning in new areas."},{"t":"p13","term":"GREEN SURGE","def":"EU project that studied green space planning in 20 cities in 2014: the study's baseline."}],"ODD":[{"w":1,"items":["Prioritarianism","Limitarianism","Doctrine of sufficiency","Entitlement theory"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three belong to the Supportive City family (sufficiency and its two variants); Nozick's entitlement theory grounds the Free City."},{"w":1,"items":["Value: what is distributed","Principle: how it is allocated","Ground: why that allocation is just","Scope: which data are collected"],"odd":3,"e":"Value, principle and ground are correctly defined materials of justice; scope is to whom or where the principle applies (e.g. the inhabitants of the city, future generations), not a data choice."},{"w":1,"items":["Make the values behind a theory choice explicit","Choose openly between competing justice conceptions","Avoid rushing to common ground on justice","Let the facts speak for themselves"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three express week 1's shared call for explicit value choices (Rydin 2021 on theory, Buitelaar on justice); letting the facts speak is the empiricism Rydin rejects."},{"w":1,"items":["Reproduction ↔ transformation","Deliberate ↔ emergent","Universal ↔ situated","Goal-based ↔ rights-based"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are axes of the course framework (dimensions 2 and 3); goal-based ↔ rights-based is Buitelaar's 'basis' distinction between conceptions of the just city."},{"w":1,"items":["Focus attention under time and money limits","Link findings to a broader narrative","Build and revise theory through research","Take planners' intentions at face value"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Rydin's three advantages of theory; taking planners' intentions at face value is what she criticises in Næss and Saglie's instrumental definition of planning."},{"w":1,"items":["Flyvbjerg's phronesis","Empiricism: let the facts speak","Theory as law between variables","Justice literacy"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are positions Rydin (2021) discusses and criticises; justice literacy is Buitelaar's aim of knowing the vocabulary and grammar of justice."},{"w":2,"items":["Lindblom's muddling through","Meyerson & Banfield's Chicago housing study","Altshuler's Minneapolis-St Paul study","Davidoff's advocacy planning"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are the pluralist critique 'from the right' that Hall reports; Davidoff's advocacy planning came from the left."},{"w":2,"items":["Written in a hermetic style for a small coterie","Infuriatingly aspatial","Cannot explain socio-economic relationships","Strangely quietist"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Hall's charges against postmodern theory; 'strangely quietist' is his verdict on the Marxian logic of the 1970s."},{"w":2,"items":["Castells: collective consumption","Lojkine: the state underpins private investment","Scott & Roweis: no transcendent norms","Forester: planning as communicative action"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Marxist positions in Hall's account of the 1970s; Forester draws on Habermas to found communicative planning."},{"w":2,"items":["The product confronts the worker as an alien power","Labour is felt as coerced rather than voluntary","Species-life becomes a means to physical existence","Particular decisions are delegated to experts' discretion"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are forms of estrangement in Marx's text; the delegation of particular decisions to experts is Hayek's account of how democratic planning slides towards arbitrary power."},{"w":2,"items":["Markets lead to material inequality by design","Plan for the working class and the poor","Decommodified (public, cooperative) housing","Plan for a level field rather than limit supply"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are the progressive planner (Madden and Marcuse) on the HC4 slide; planning for a level field rather than limiting supply is the liberal planner (Glaeser and Gyourko)."},{"w":2,"items":["Owners versus the disowned of the means of production","Class struggle as the motor of history","Base shapes and is maintained by superstructure","Action, ownership and time as drivers of adaptation"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three belong to the lecture's account of Marx's historical materialism; action, ownership and time are Cozzolino's three neglected factors behind adaptive neighbourhoods."},{"w":2,"items":["Construction all at once, from scratch","Mono-functional 'dormitories'","Large open spaces managed by one agency","Many independent owners with their own lots"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are characteristics of anti-adaptive neighbourhoods in Cozzolino's Figure 3; many independent owners is a feature of adaptive environments."},{"w":2,"items":["Tenants cannot adapt their homes","Markets are power structures","The traditional city is romanticised","Rules should leave room beyond the built form"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are the Tutorial 1 critiques of Cozzolino's agency assumption (hint: Marx); leaving room beyond the built form is Cozzolino's own recommendation."},{"w":2,"items":["Action as the main source of urban change","Ownership as the title that distributes design control","Time as the condition for incremental adaptation","Rational analysis as the source of a plan's legitimacy"],"odd":3,"e":"Action, ownership and time are Cozzolino's three under-estimated factors; legitimacy from the rationality of the analysis is Rydin's (2021) governmental model."},{"w":2,"items":["Direct public investment, incl. landownership","Tradeable market-based permits","Information and persuasion, incl. nudges","Distributed design control among many owners"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are policy instruments that, for Rydin (2021), measure the power of the state planner; distributed design control among many owners is Cozzolino's alternative to central design."},{"w":2,"items":["Complete, accurate information","Clear goals agreed by all","Objective, value-free decisions","Ends and means chosen together"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are the ideal-typical assumptions of the rational (governmental) model from the lecture; choosing ends and means together is Lindblom's incrementalist challenge to them."},{"w":2,"items":["Hettinga et al.: Geodesign in Escamp","Kumar et al.: Bangalore vulnerability index","Ma et al.: Chongming Eco Island","Rip: hybrid forums for collective learning"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are studies Rydin (2021) uses to show research in the governmental frame; Rip's hybrid forums are borrowed by Rydin (2007) for arenas that test knowledge claims."},{"w":2,"items":["Social ends as identical ends of many individuals","Planning shifts decisions to experts","General rules instead of case-by-case discretion","Material equality via shared means of production"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Hayek's claims in 'Planning and democracy'; material equality through shared means of production is Marx's implicit value on the HC4 slides."},{"w":2,"items":["Proscriptive rules that only prevent externalities","General rules that apply equally to all","Design control spread over many owners","Prescriptive plans that fix spatial outcomes"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three belong to the liberal line of Hayek and Cozzolino: general, mainly prohibitive rules and dispersed control. Prescriptive plans that predefine outcomes are what Cozzolino blames for anti-adaptive neighbourhoods."},{"w":2,"items":["Complete ethical code","Plebiscitarian dictatorship","Price of democracy","Spontaneous order (kosmos)"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are concepts from the chapter itself. Spontaneous order is attributed to Hayek in the lecture but comes from his later work (Law, Legislation and Liberty), not from 'Planning and democracy'."},{"w":2,"items":["Opening up and closing down","Socially robust knowledge","Hybrid forums for testing claims","Locality knowledge used by dwellers themselves"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three belong to Rydin's (2007) proposal for hearing and testing knowledge claims in arenas; locality knowledge used by dwellers in their own adaptations is Cozzolino's (after Jacobs)."},{"w":2,"items":["Sandercock's epistemology of multiplicity","Healey's collaborative planning","Innes's consensus building","McLoughlin's systems planning"],"odd":3,"e":"Rydin groups Sandercock, Healey and Innes as versions of the communicative 'new orthodoxy' that places knowledge with many stakeholders; McLoughlin's systems planning is the modernist expert model that Hall describes."},{"w":2,"items":["Truth (constatives)","Normative legitimacy (regulatives)","Truthfulness (expressives)","Efficiency claims"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Habermas's validity claims as Rydin presents them. Efficiency is one of the other claim types Rydin lists next to ethical, pragmatic and aesthetic claims, not a Habermasian validity claim."},{"w":3,"items":["Expert on top: research leads policy","Expert on tap: research serves policy demands","'What matters is what works'","Research illuminates the landscape of debate"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three belong to Davoudi's instrumental, linear view of the research-policy link. Illuminating the landscape and informing debate is the enlightenment model, the alternative she prefers."},{"w":3,"items":["Policy-making is a rational process","Evidence comes only from positive science","Experts are apolitical and value-free","Policy-making is bricolage"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are the misconceptions Davoudi says underlie the instrumental view. Bricolage (Ball) is her description of how policy is really made, which contradicts the first misconception."},{"w":3,"items":["Lindblom's 'muddling through'","Ball's policy as bricolage","Rittel and Webber's wicked problems","Lichfield's eleven-step rational process"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three challenge the idea of a linear, rational planning process (Lindblom and Ball are cited by Davoudi; wicked problems appear in the governmental-model lecture). Lichfield's eleven steps are the ideal process of Rydin's governmental model itself."},{"w":3,"items":["Politicians set the goals, experts choose the means","The Plan overrides elected authorities and the constitution","Urban design has one correct solution an expert can find","Politics and bargaining can only produce the wrong answer"],"odd":0,"e":"The last three describe Le Corbusier's technocracy in Scott's chapter. Keeping politicians in charge of goals is Rydin's governmental model, which is rational but not authoritarian; Le Corbusier wanted the plan to override politics altogether."},{"w":3,"items":["Mētis: practical knowledge built up by local experience","Experiential and tacit knowledge counting as evidence","Shopkeepers' informal knowledge of who uses the street","Falsifiable hypotheses tested in spatial interaction models"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three value situated, practical knowledge (Scott, Davoudi's widened notion of evidence, Jacobs via Scott). Testable hypotheses and spatial models belong to the positivist systems planning of the 1960s that Davoudi criticises."},{"w":3,"items":["A city without crowds or street corners","Residents' mock illness of anonymity","Most people living in unplanned settlements by 1980","Rampant land speculation in the planned centre"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are outcomes Scott reports for Brasília. Land speculation is what the plan avoided: the state planning agency owned all the land, so speculation and rent gouging were circumvented."},{"w":3,"items":["Right of support for a house's foundations","Covenant enforced by a residents' association","Conservation agreement buying development rights","Emission limits set in a factory's permit"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three protect interests through private law (rights enforced by their holders); emission limits are Needham's example of public-law protection by an authority."},{"w":3,"items":["Interests protected by private or public law","Property rights as a policy variable","Remediability: compare instruments by effectiveness","Selective incentives for those who contribute"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Needham's concepts; selective incentives are Olson's route to overcoming collective action problems (Hindmoor)."},{"w":3,"items":["Coase: should A harm B or B harm A?","Hodgson's three conditions for a market","Lindblom: the market cannot be excluded","Hardin: a leviathan must save the commons"],"odd":3,"e":"Needham builds on Coase, Hodgson and Lindblom; Hardin's call for a coercive leviathan appears in Hindmoor's Box 5.3 on Olson."},{"w":4,"items":["Boundary rules","Payoff rules","Aggregation rules","Cognitive scripts"],"odd":3,"e":"Boundary, payoff and aggregation rules are types in Ostrom's IAD framework (rational choice institutionalism); cognitive scripts belong to sociological institutionalism (Hall and Taylor)."},{"w":4,"items":["Holsen: weak participation in Norway","Growing US mortgage interest deduction","Critical junctures, then bounded pathways","Amsterdam-Noord's shipyard symbols"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three illustrate historical institutionalism (path dependence and junctures); Amsterdam-Noord's symbols are the lecture's sociological institutionalist example."},{"w":4,"items":["North: humanly devised constraints","Users craft rules to save the commons","Institutions give credible commitment","Kosmos: modest rule-keeping, not design"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are rational choice institutionalism (North, Ostrom); the kosmos approach of modest rule-keeping is Moroni's evolutionary institutionalism."},{"w":3,"items":["Prisoners' dilemma","Assurance game","Chicken game","Remediability"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are the game forms Hindmoor uses for collective action problems; remediability (Williamson) is Needham's way of comparing planning instruments by effectiveness."},{"w":3,"items":["A flatmate who cleans alone","A hegemon keeping world trade open","A union offering members free legal aid","A rich resident paying for the whole pool roof"],"odd":2,"e":"The others are privileged groups (one member bears the whole cost); free legal aid is a positive selective incentive for contributors."},{"w":3,"items":["Kibbutz gossip against shirkers","Truces in First World War trenches","Valencia's irrigation turns","Melbourne's 'Taxed Out!' campaign"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Hindmoor's examples of how groups sustain cooperation; Taxed Out! is the landowner campaign against the GAIC in Taylor's rent-seeking study."},{"w":3,"items":["Premium-seeking owners lobbying to be included","Homevoters defending growth restrictions","Owners discrediting the expected betterment","A large latent group failing to organise"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Taylor's forms of landowner rent-seeking around Melbourne's boundary; a large latent group failing to organise is Olson's logic of collective action (Hindmoor)."},{"w":3,"items":["Insiders capture gains, outsiders pay","Heresthetics: deliberate framing of an issue","Containment fuels speculation at the fringe","Remediability: compare instruments by effectiveness"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three belong to Taylor's rent-seeking analysis; remediability, comparing policy instruments by how effectively they reach a goal, is Needham's criterion."},{"w":3,"items":["Owners lobby to keep an unearned land rent","Self-interested actors bend the rules of the game","Policies survive if they survive stakeholder conflict","A crisis opens a short window for new rules"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three express Taylor's rational choice reading of rules contested by self-interested owners; a crisis opening a short window is a critical juncture, the historical institutionalist mechanism Sorensen uses."},{"w":4,"items":["Rites of institution","Inculcation by the audience","Banal nationalism","Adaptive efficiency"],"odd":3,"e":"Rites of institution, inculcation and banal nationalism are Dembski & Salet's ideas about how symbols gain meaning; adaptive efficiency (North) is one of Moroni's kosmos principles."},{"w":4,"items":["Pearl River Delta metaphor","Section 37 density bonus","Guggenheim Bilbao","Flemish Diamond"],"odd":1,"e":"The Pearl River Delta, Guggenheim Bilbao and the Flemish Diamond are Dembski & Salet's examples of symbolic markers that failed to (yet) create new meaning; Section 37 is a Toronto value-capture tool in Sorensen."},{"w":4,"items":["Geertz's interpretive anthropology","Ostrom's actor-centred institutionalism","Scharpf's actor-centred institutionalism","Rational choice institutionalism"],"odd":0,"e":"In Dembski & Salet's Table 1, rational choice and actor-centred institutionalism (Ostrom, Scharpf) treat institutions as external constraints; Geertz belongs to the sociological quadrant where institutions are internalised."},{"w":4,"items":["The audience addressed","The subjective context","Permissive conditions","Sanctions for misrecognition"],"odd":2,"e":"Audience, subjective context and sanctions are steps in Dembski & Salet's eight-step research model; permissive conditions belong to Sorensen's critical juncture framework (after Soifer)."},{"w":4,"items":["Peculiarity","Robustness","Openness","Permissive conditions"],"odd":3,"e":"Peculiarity, robustness and openness are among Moroni's ten kosmos principles; permissive conditions (after Soifer) are part of Sorensen's critical-juncture framework."},{"w":4,"items":["Adam Ferguson","David Hume","Stuart Hall","Carl Menger"],"odd":2,"e":"Ferguson, Hume and Menger are Moroni's sources for the evolutionary view of institutions; Stuart Hall is the source of the conjunctural analysis that Sorensen links to historical institutionalism."},{"w":4,"items":["Order without an ordinator","An element of surprise","A process story over time","A logic of appropriateness"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are features of invisible-hand explanations that Moroni takes from Koppl; the logic of appropriateness (March & Olsen) belongs to the sociological institutionalism of Dembski & Salet."},{"w":4,"items":["Institutions: the rules of the game","Institutions: symbolic systems that make life meaningful","Organisations: the players of the game","Institutions: rules with meta-ends like peaceful coexistence"],"odd":1,"e":"Three items follow Moroni (with North): rules versus players, and institutions as rules with meta-ends. Institutions as symbolic systems reproduced in practice is Dembski & Salet's sociological view."},{"w":4,"items":["Variation, selection and propagation of rules","Critical juncture","Reactive sequence","Developmental pathway"],"odd":0,"e":"Critical juncture, reactive sequence and developmental pathway are Sorensen's historical-institutionalist concepts of change; variation, selection and propagation are Moroni's three steps of institutional evolution."},{"w":4,"items":["Development charges","Section 37 density bonuses","Ontario's greenbelt","Lot levies"],"odd":2,"e":"Development charges, Section 37 bonuses and lot levies are value capture instruments in Sorensen's Toronto history; the greenbelt was a smart-growth policy that supported intensification but captures no land value."},{"w":4,"items":["Stuart Hall","Jamie Peck","Inch & Shepherd","Hillel Soifer"],"odd":3,"e":"Hall, Peck and Inch & Shepherd represent conjunctural analysis in critical urban and cultural studies; Soifer belongs to the historical-institutionalist literature on critical junctures that Sorensen adds to it."},{"w":4,"items":["Permissive conditions","Rites of institution","Productive conditions","Antecedent conditions"],"odd":1,"e":"Permissive, productive and antecedent conditions belong to Sorensen's critical-juncture method; rites of institution (after Bourdieu) are Dembski & Salet's account of how new meaning is instituted."},{"w":6,"items":["Property contradiction","Capitalism–democracy contradiction","Entrepreneurial public real estate policy","Relative autonomy of the state"],"odd":2,"e":"Three are Foglesong's building blocks (two contradictions and a structuralist theory of the state); entrepreneurial public real estate policy is Hyötyläinen & Haila's concept for Helsinki's land sales."},{"w":6,"items":["Critical junctures and reactive sequences","Expert commissions shielded from voters","Legitimation as the public interest","Socialised control over land use"],"odd":0,"e":"Shielded commissions, legitimation and socialised control all belong to Foglesong's account of planning under capitalism; critical junctures and reactive sequences are Sorensen's historical institutionalism."},{"w":6,"items":["Social character of land vs private ownership","Planning as neutral arbiter among groups","Speculative public-private partnerships","Selling public land to maximise revenue"],"odd":1,"e":"Foglesong, Harvey (1989) and Hyötyläinen & Haila all work in a political-economy frame that asks whose interests planning serves; planning as a neutral arbiter among groups is the liberal/pluralist view they reject."},{"w":6,"items":["Competing in the international division of labour","Competing in the spatial division of consumption","Competing for control and command functions","Selling public land to the highest bidder"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are among Harvey's four basic options for entrepreneurial cities (the fourth is redistribution via central government); selling public land to maximise rent is Hyötyläinen & Haila's entrepreneurial public real estate policy."},{"w":6,"items":["The 1973 recession and deindustrialisation","Fiscal austerity at national and local levels","Neoconservative appeal to market rationality","The 2009 credit crunch and SPV bankruptcies"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Harvey's backdrop for the shift to entrepreneurialism (p. 5); the credit crunch and the bankruptcy of a development company belong to Savini & Aalbers' Falck case."},{"w":0,"items":["Helsinki replacing managerial with entrepreneurial strategies","Capital switching into the built environment","Land treated as an asset 'according to the rent it yields'","Symbolic markers that institute new social meaning"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three draw directly on Harvey's work (Hyötyläinen & Haila borrow his 1989 terms and his 1982 view of land; Savini & Aalbers start from capital switching); symbolic markers come from Dembski & Salet's sociological institutionalism."},{"w":6,"items":["Put public assets to work","Make land use efficient","Increase transparency","Protect the city's planning monopoly"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are among the four justifications for entrepreneurial public real estate policy (the fourth is creating diversity); protecting the planning monopoly is what leasing achieved and what selling undermined."},{"w":6,"items":["New state urban policy with competitive urban programmes","A redistribution shift under 'the necessity to save'","The Land Use and Building Act (MRL, 1999)","The PII regime of close developer negotiation"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are the changes that 'scaled down' land-use decisions to Finnish cities; PII (Programmi Integrati d'Intervento) is the Italian framework in Savini & Aalbers' Falck case."},{"w":6,"items":["Blocks bordered by 1.2 m fences with gated entrances","Adverts promising that no common laundries are needed","A Finnish record land price of €2,621 per m²","Land sold four times while no brick was laid"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three describe Eiranranta in Helsinki; land changing hands four times at rising prices without construction is the Falck site in Sesto San Giovanni (Savini & Aalbers)."},{"w":6,"items":["Gruppo Pasini","Skanska","Risanamento (Gruppo Zunino)","Sesto Immobiliare (Bizzi & Partners)"],"odd":1,"e":"Pasini, Risanamento and Sesto Immobiliare were successive owners of the Falck site in Savini & Aalbers; Skanska is one of Helsinki's 'household developers' in Hyötyläinen & Haila."},{"w":6,"items":["Symbolic markers such as landmarks","Strict regulation and betterment levies","Public-led land readjustment","Project bonds and Tax Increment Financing"],"odd":0,"e":"Regulation and betterment levies, land readjustment and project bonds or TIF are the value capture tools Savini & Aalbers list (pp. 6–7); symbolic markers are Dembski & Salet's means of giving new meaning to a city-region."},{"w":6,"items":["Falck Corporation and PSTA in the 1990s","Weber's Chicago","Renzo Piano's masterplan","A large loan from Banca Intesa"],"odd":1,"e":"Three belong to the history of the Falck site; Weber's Chicago is the counter-case of a city using financial markets for its own agenda, which the authors fear is the exception."},{"w":7,"items":["Use value over exchange value","Self-governance by residents","Solidarity through cohabitation","Communities of obligation"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano's dimensions of a housing commons; communities of obligation (rights conditional on duties) is Rose's term used by Raco and Imrie."},{"w":7,"items":["Commons cannot be objects of property","Some degree of closure is needed","Commons survive through porosity","Cooptation absorbs new elements to avert threats"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are the commons theorists' positions on property cited by Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano (Dardot and Laval, Harvey, de Angelis and Stavrides); cooptation after Selznick is the basis of Pruijt's framework."},{"w":7,"items":["Mutirão (collective labour)","Autogestão (self-management)","Effort-based points system","Sweat equity homesteading"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three come from the São Paulo cases in Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano; sweat-equity homesteading is New York's official programme to 'tame' squatting in Pruijt."},{"w":7,"items":["Regime of truth","Semantic justification","Rhetorical relegation","Technologies of performance"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Gurran and Ruming's discourse concepts; technologies of performance (audit, targets, devolved budgets) belong to Raco and Imrie's governmentality analysis."},{"w":7,"items":["Property Council of Australia","Urban Taskforce","Australand","Shelter NSW"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are development industry submitters in Gurran and Ruming's sample; Shelter NSW is the affordable housing advocacy group whose inclusionary proposal was ignored."},{"w":7,"items":["Planning as 'red tape' that blocks supply","Affordability as the reason to deregulate","Growth as the 'main purpose' of planning","Value uplift levied for affordable homes"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three belong to the industry discourse that the NSW government largely adopted; levying value uplift for affordable housing was Shelter NSW's and the planners' alternative."},{"w":7,"items":["Flexible institutionalization","Terminal institutionalization","Cooptation into service provision","Structural coupling with state systems"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Pruijt's forms of integration that complement repression; structural coupling is de Angelis' concept used by Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano for commons aligning with state systems."},{"w":7,"items":["House right for squatters (1971)","Redlining and disinvestment of tenements","Covert squatting in city-owned buildings","A federal funding cap per housing unit"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are parts of the opportunity structure in Pruijt's comparison (property protection, building condition); the federal cap per unit belongs to MCMV-Entidades in Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano."},{"w":7,"items":["ACORN gives up squatting to obtain 58 buildings","Banana Kelly turns into a service provider","Tenant Interim Lease absorbs organisers' energy","REDWatch becomes an 'inconvenient corporate memory'"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are New York examples of cooptation and the privatization of poverty in Pruijt; REDWatch is Rogers' Sydney case of agonistic monitoring in Rydin's chapter."},{"w":7,"items":["Communities of obligation","Directed self-government","Calculable spaces of action","Semantic justification"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are governmentality concepts in Raco and Imrie; semantic justification (affordability used to warrant deregulation) is Gurran and Ruming's."},{"w":7,"items":["Quarterly progress reports against a priori targets","Best Value disciplines for local councils","Peer review through Best Practice guides","A participation charter for strategic plan making"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are SRB-era technologies of government in Raco and Imrie; the participation charter is the NSW reform promise discussed by Gurran and Ruming."},{"w":7,"items":["Empowerment as a qualitative change in subjectivity","Self-help where services are 'unattractive to the private sector'","Rights 'as much about duties' as entitlements","Agonism: turning enemies into adversaries"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three belong to Raco and Imrie's reading of SRB; agonism (Mouffe) is one of the dynamics of conflict in Rydin's urban politics chapter."},{"w":7,"items":["'Power to' rather than 'power over'","Resource interdependence of council and business","An informal yet relatively stable coalition","Government at a distance through funding conditions"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three define Stone's urban regime as Rydin presents it; government at a distance is Raco and Imrie's governmentality concept."},{"w":7,"items":["Recognition of groups as self-determined","Fair process, such as open visioning","Just exercise of procedures","Use value prioritised over exchange value"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Agyeman's aspects of justice in Rydin's chapter; use value over exchange value is a dimension of housing commons in Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano."},{"w":7,"items":["Mobilising over the means of collective consumption","Expressing a fundamental social cleavage","A physical base in a particular place","Squatting as both the means and the end"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three characterise urban social movements in Rydin's chapter (Castells, social cleavage, place base); squatting as means and end is Pruijt's definition of a squatters' movement proper."},{"w":3,"items":["Bargaining and negotiation","Political 'games'","Strategic planning by an organisation","Linear programming"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three sit in Alexander's real, collective, accomplishment-oriented cell (b), strategic action. Linear programming is a formal optimisation model in the ideal, individual cell (d)."},{"w":3,"items":["Ritual response","Enlightenment model","Avoidance response","Search response"],"odd":1,"e":"Ritual, avoidance and search are three of Alexander's four responses to the breakdown of rationality. The enlightenment model is Davoudi's (after Weiss) account of how research informs policy indirectly."},{"w":3,"items":["Normative, not descriptive of behaviour","Entirely procedural, divorced from substance","Assumes a linear path from research to policy","Blind to deliberation before interaction"],"odd":2,"e":"Three are Alexander's objections to Habermas's TCA as a planning paradigm. A linear path from research to policy is Davoudi's critique of evidence-based policy, aimed at the instrumental model, not at TCA."},{"w":3,"items":["Tradable permits for hosting nights","VvE negotiations on house rules","Hosting tax paid into a neighbourhood fund","A 60-night cap per year for every host"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are the lecture's Coasean-inspired tools, which define rights or cut transaction costs so actors can bargain. A uniform 60-night cap is the one-size-fits-all restriction (Utrecht 2018) that rational choice theorists find hard to enforce."},{"w":3,"items":["Information costs","Holdouts and misrepresentation","Enforcement costs","Scarcity rents for insider owners"],"odd":3,"e":"Information costs, holdouts and misrepresentation, and enforcement costs are among the lecture's transaction costs that block Coasean bargaining. Scarcity rents belong to the Swiss densification hypothesis: they are what insider owners gain, not a cost of bargaining."},{"w":3,"items":["Oversimplified model of human behaviour","Ignores power and inequality","Too focused on individual agency","Ignores knowledge dispersed among many people"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are among the lecture's critiques of rational choice theory. Dispersed knowledge is Hayek's argument against central planning; it is not on the lecture's list, which adds limiting assumptions and the nirvana fallacy in reverse."},{"w":1,"items":["Methodological cityism","Per-capita degrowth of the building stock","Alliance-building instead of all-stakeholder consensus","Scenarios contrasting a trend future and an environmental one"],"odd":0,"e":"The last three are Næss's (2001) substantive and procedural proposals; methodological cityism (treating the city as the self-evident scale) is Angelo and Wachsmuth's critique (L2).","psc":1},{"w":1,"items":["Property conflict: economy versus equity","Resource conflict: economy versus environment","Efficiency, sufficiency and consistency","Development conflict: equity versus environment"],"odd":2,"e":"Three are the conflicts of Campbell's planner's triangle (L3); efficiency, sufficiency and consistency are Huber's sustainability strategies from lecture 1.","psc":1},{"w":1,"items":["Reaction to sprawl in the global North","Environmental deficits of informal settlements","Climate change as the concern linking North and South","Green gentrification as a green growth machine"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three are Angelo and Wachsmuth's three roots of the 'city as solution' consensus (L2); green gentrification (Gould and Lewis) is the core dilemma of lecture 3.","psc":1},{"w":2,"items":["Pre-emption right (voorkeursrecht)","Three control lines","Active land policy, from VINEX to NOVEX","Early stakeholder involvement ('polderen')"],"odd":1,"e":"Three are Dutch municipal strategies (inside, outside, alongside) from Witte's lecture 4; the three control lines belong to China's territorial spatial planning (L7).","psc":1},{"w":2,"items":["Invented spaces of insurgent planning","Hukou registration by birthplace","Land finance after the 1994 fiscal reform","Dual land system: state and collective land"],"odd":0,"e":"Hukou, land finance and the dual land system are Chinese planning legacies (L7); invented versus invited spaces is Miraftab's insurgent planning, discussed for African cities (L6).","psc":1},{"w":2,"items":["Reconfiguration of multiple systems","Competing, co-existing or complementary relations","Correspondence between infrastructure systems and the city","The interface where rationalities meet and clash"],"odd":3,"e":"The first three come from Hodson, Geels and McMeekin (L8); the interface (after Li) is de Satgé and Watson's concept (L6).","psc":1},{"w":3,"items":["Trial","Exnovation","Enclave","Platform"],"odd":1,"e":"Trial, enclave and platform are three of Bulkeley et al.'s four living lab dispositions (L9); exnovation is one of the transformation outcomes used by Hansen et al. (L13).","psc":1},{"w":3,"items":["Mismatch between technology supply and planning demand","Deskilling of planners' judgement","Algorithmic bias reproducing past inequity","Blurred accountability for automated decisions"],"odd":0,"e":"Deskilling, bias and blurred accountability are three of Li and Dang's four disruptive factors of urban AI (L11); the supply-demand mismatch is a risk named in Lin et al.'s digital planning editorial (L10).","psc":1},{"w":3,"items":["Ceilings on development, not only targets","Minimum and maximum space standards","Exchange agreements: developers fund new green space","Restricting second homes and short-term lets"],"odd":2,"e":"Three are post-growth tools proposed by Durrant, Lamker and Rydin (L12); Aarhus's exchange agreements are a growth-linked mechanism reported by Hansen et al. (L13).","psc":1}],"SEQ":[{"t":"buit","w":1,"title":"Buitelaar's argument, step by step","steps":["Everyone backs the concept of the Just City, but conceptions differ","Debates suffer from vagueness, narrowness and inconsistency","Four conceptions are decomposed into materials of justice","Their internal and external congruence is assessed","Conclusion: justice literacy needs explicit, possibly conflictual choices"],"e":"The article moves from the concept/conception distinction and the three problems (pp. 352–353), through the four cities and their materials (pp. 354–362), to congruence (pp. 362–365) and the conclusion that 'politics is divisive by definition'."},{"t":"buit","w":1,"title":"Sen's bike: from good to standard of living","steps":["Commodity: the bike as a good","Characteristic: it can be set in motion and transport someone","Capability: being able to ride it, or to choose not to","Utility: the pleasure or satisfaction of riding it"],"e":"Sen separates the commodity, its characteristics, the capability to use it and the utility it gives (p. 362); the capability approach locates the standard of living in the third step, not in the good or the mental reaction."},{"t":"fw","w":1,"title":"Rydin's arc through planning theory (the book's chapters)","steps":["Governmental model: planning as a linear rational activity","Actor-centred approaches: rational choice, institutions, governance, urban politics","Structural account: Marxian political economy","Relational perspectives: Foucault, then assemblage and material entities"],"e":"Rydin (pp. 13–14) says the book moves from the rational ideal, through actor-centred approaches stressing agency, to a structural account, and ends with relational perspectives that unsettle the very idea of the actor."},{"t":"fw","w":1,"title":"Rydin's three framings of planning, from least to most politicised","steps":["Goal-directed decision-making: an instrumental means to ends","State action ordering the environment in the collective interest","Engagement with different interests, judged by who benefits most","Maintaining (or not) systemic features of society"],"e":"Each small shift in the definition reframes planning: from neutral public administration, to corrective state intervention, to a political process judged by its beneficiaries and its role in society's systemic features (pp. 4–5)."},{"t":"hall","w":2,"title":"The US transportation-study procedure that became the systems model","steps":["Set goals","Take inventories of the existing situation","Build mathematical models","Forecast future change","Design and evaluate alternatives","Implement and monitor"],"e":"Hall says the model came from the Detroit (1955) and Chicago (1956) transportation studies: goals, inventories, models, forecasts, alternatives and evaluation, then implementation and monitoring (p. 394)."},{"t":"hall","w":2,"title":"Hall's marriage metaphor for theory and practice","steps":["Late 1950s and 1960s: honeymoon, a seemingly perfect link","1970s: tiffs and temporary reconciliations","1980s: divorce","1980s to 1990s: New Proceduralism; the relationship has 'gone sour'"],"e":"Hall frames the chapter with this metaphor (p. 386) and ends on the unresolved question of a relationship between planning and the academy that 'had gone sour' (p. 413)."},{"t":"marx","w":2,"title":"The steps of Marx's argument in 'Estranged Labour'","steps":["Accept the premises and terms of political economy","Start from a fact: the worker grows poorer as wealth grows","The product confronts the worker as an alien power","Labour itself becomes external and forced","Species-life becomes a means to physical existence","Man is estranged from man; private property is the consequence"],"e":"Marx first takes political economy's own premises, then derives the four estrangements in order (product, activity, species-being, other people) and concludes that private property is the product of estranged labour rather than its cause."},{"t":"marx","w":2,"title":"From Marx's history to his political ideology (HC4 slides)","steps":["Class struggle now: worker versus bourgeois","Revolution","Proletarian appropriation","Communism","Anarchy: a stateless society"],"e":"The slide continues the dialectic spiral as a political programme: class struggle leads to revolution, appropriation by the proletariat and communism, ending in a stateless society built upon material equality."},{"t":"cozz","w":2,"title":"Cozzolino (2020): from the adaptive city to AANs","steps":["Civitas: owning a dwelling makes one a citizen","Incremental growth under codes that only forbid harm","Modern planning turns to prescriptive rules and standards","Post-war demand: total design and large master plans","AANs built all at once become locked in","Top-down regeneration creates paternalistic circles"],"e":"Cozzolino traces the shift from the traditional city, built by many owners under proscriptive codes, to modern prescriptive planning and large all-at-once estates, which cannot self-regenerate and therefore call for repeated top-down interventions."},{"t":"cozz","w":2,"title":"Tutorial 1: reconstructing Cozzolino's argument","steps":["Problem: AANs resist incremental change","Cause: single developer, all-at-once, prescriptive rules","Mechanism: nobody can make small adjustments","Alternative: many owners under proscriptive rules","Implication: include time, ownership and locality knowledge"],"e":"The tutorial slide reconstructs the argument as problem, cause, mechanism, alternative and implication, ending in Cozzolino's planning advice (e.g. Oosterwold)."},{"t":"gov","w":2,"title":"Why plan? The welfare-economics chain","steps":["Markets allocate resources efficiently","Except when they fail: externalities, public goods, monopoly","So the state may intervene","Public interest = greatest net benefit to society","Planning becomes the optimisation of welfare"],"e":"The lecture (after Rydin pp. 19–20 and Klosterman) summarises the justification: markets work except when they fail, which allows state intervention aimed at the public interest understood as net welfare."},{"t":"gov","w":2,"title":"The linear process behind plan evaluation","steps":["Deliberate","Predict","Plan","Formulate","Implement","Evaluate"],"e":"Rydin says evaluation of planning bodies rests on the governmental view of a linear process: deliberate, predict, plan, formulate, implement, evaluate (p. 25)."},{"t":"hayek","w":2,"title":"Hayek: from value pluralism to limited common action","steps":["A single plan needs a complete ethical code","No such code exists: people hold partial, conflicting values","No mind can survey more than a limited field of needs","So the individual is the ultimate judge of his own ends","So common action is limited to fields where ends coincide"],"e":"Hayek's argument runs from the impossibility of a complete ethical code, via the limits of any mind, to individualism and the conclusion that collective action belongs only where individual ends coincide."},{"t":"know","w":2,"title":"Rydin (2007): the argument step by step","steps":["Modernist consensus breaks down: knowledge is socially constructed","New orthodoxy: many knowledges handled through deliberation","Deliberation cannot tell knowledge apart from other claims","Recast knowledge as claims about causal relations","Planners co-produce knowledge: open up, then close down in arenas"],"e":"Rydin moves from Latour's breakdown of the modernist consensus, via the communicative new orthodoxy and its limits, to recasting knowledge as claims and proposing arenas where planners give voice to and test them."},{"t":"davoudi","w":3,"title":"Davoudi: evidence in British planning over time","steps":["Booth's 1887 social survey of London","Geddes's 'survey before plan'","Schuster Committee widens the profession (1948)","Systems and rational planning of the 1960s and 1970s","Modernising Government White Paper (1999)","The 2004 'soundness' test for plans"],"e":"Davoudi traces descriptive surveys (Booth, Geddes), the broadening of planning's knowledge base after Schuster, the positivist systems and rational planning of the 1960s and 1970s, and then the New Labour evidence drive that culminated in the soundness test."},{"t":"davoudi","w":3,"title":"The rational planning process as Davoudi describes it","steps":["Define problems and goals","Identify alternative policies or plans","Evaluate the alternatives","Implement the preferred option","Monitor and review"],"e":"Davoudi describes the rational process as a cycle of five logical steps, and then notes (with Lindblom) that it hardly matched the disjointed, incremental way decisions were actually made."},{"t":"scott","w":3,"title":"Scott: from plan to unplanned Brasília","steps":["Kubitschek promises 'fifty years of progress in five'","Costa wins the 1957 competition; building starts on an empty plateau","Over 60,000 workers outgrow their temporary housing and squat","The candangos organise for land, services and title","By 1980, 75 percent live in settlements the plan never anticipated"],"e":"Kubitschek's project led to Costa's plan and rapid construction on state land; the workforce, never planned for, squatted, used Kubitschek's own heroic rhetoric to claim land and services, and the unplanned Brasília became home to most of the population."},{"t":"scott","w":3,"title":"Scott's chapter: the argument step by step","steps":["Le Corbusier's doctrine: total planning, geometry, segregation, rule by the Plan","Brasília as the high-modernist city built, almost","Living there: no street corners, brasilite and disorientation","An unplanned Brasília at the margins sustains the planned centre","Jacobs's street-level critique: visual order is not experienced order"],"e":"Scott first sets out the doctrine through its most extreme spokesman, then tests it on its nearest built case, shows how residents experienced it and what grew outside the plan, and closes with Jacobs's critique from the street."},{"t":"needham","w":3,"title":"Needham: from 'the public interest' to markets in property rights","steps":["Reject 'the public interest' as the basis of planning","Look at identifiable people's interests in how land is used","The state protects interests by private law (rights) or public law (planning)","The allocation is partly political, partly technical, and revisable","Shifting protection to rights creates markets in property rights","Conclusion: feasible only in limited circumstances"],"e":"Needham moves from rejecting the public-interest framing, via the two routes of protection and their revisability, to a modest conclusion: markets in rights are feasible only under limited circumstances, and feasibility is not desirability."},{"t":"ni","w":4,"title":"Part A of the lecture: from dilemma to Ostrom's rules","steps":["Prisoners' dilemma: rational choices leave both worse off","Hardin: rational users overuse and deplete a shared resource","The lecture asks: solution?","Ostrom: users self-organise rules to overcome the problem of commitment","IAD framework: seven rule types shape the action situation"],"e":"The lecture moves from the prisoners' dilemma (slide 13) via Hardin's tragedy and the open question 'Solution?' (14-15) to Ostrom's self-organised institutions and her IAD framework with seven rule types (16-17)."},{"t":"ni","w":4,"title":"Historical institutionalism: how change happens","steps":["Rules are reproduced along a path for a long time","A crisis opens a critical juncture","Actors use the crisis to push particular changes","A new developmental pathway is set","Contestation continues within a constrained range of options"],"e":"Following the Sorensen quotes in the lecture: critical junctures are short periods in which choices weigh heavily, actors use conjunctural crises to further changes, and afterwards path dependence is not lock-in but continuous contestation within a constrained pathway."},{"t":"olson","w":3,"title":"Hindmoor: from zero to stable cooperation (Figure 5.5)","steps":["Nobody cooperates; each cooperator would be worse off than if all defected","A few unconditional cooperators start anyway, acting on norms","Cooperation grows towards threshold A","Beyond A, cooperators do at least as well as if all defected","Limited cooperation becomes stable even if some defect"],"e":"Reaching the threshold from zero is hard, which is why a few people willing to set self-interest aside matter early on; beyond the threshold, conditional cooperation can hold at limited levels (pp. 126–127)."},{"t":"taylor","w":3,"title":"Taylor's Figure 1: how a growth boundary creates betterment","steps":["Without a boundary, urban and rural land prices meet at one point","A binding boundary is drawn before that point","Prices split at the line: higher urban, lower rural","The gap between them is the betterment","Moving the boundary unlocks the gap for included parcels","The GAIC aims to tax part of this gain"],"e":"Taylor's Figure 1 (p. 263): a binding boundary splits the price at the line into a higher urban and a lower rural price; the gap is betterment, unlocked for any parcel the boundary is moved to include, which is what the GAIC was meant to tax."},{"t":"dembski","w":4,"title":"Dembski & Salet: how the argument of the paper unfolds","steps":["Puzzle: why do some symbols of urban change mobilise while others fail?","Problem: institutions are usually seen as 'rusty old fixtures' explaining continuity","Choice: institutions as internalised and validated in action (sociological quadrant)","Theory of instituting: habitualisation, habitus, symbols and rites of institution","Research model: eight steps for studying symbolic markers","Advice: find niches of under-institutionalised space"],"e":"The paper moves from an empirical puzzle to a theoretical repositioning of institutions, builds a theory of how meaning is instituted, turns it into a research model and ends with advice for planning."},{"t":"dembski","w":4,"title":"Dembski & Salet (after Bourdieu): how a rite of institution creates new meaning","steps":["Reality changes gradually, like ageing or a region slowly growing","A rite marks an arbitrary boundary and consecrates the difference","Others start to treat the person or region according to the new representation","The person or region adopts the new self-image ('Become what you are')","Discontinuity is produced out of continuity, if the audience has inculcated the meaning"],"e":"Social magic works through representation: others act on the consecrated difference and the bearer lives up to it. The last step carries the condition the authors stress: without inculcation the rite stays powerless."},{"t":"moroni","w":4,"title":"Moroni: how the argument of the paper unfolds","steps":["Welcome the institutional turn in planning theory","Warn that taxis thinking may resurface at the institutional level","Distinguish institutions (rules) from organisations (players)","Descriptive side: institutions mostly evolve unintentionally","Prescriptive side: organisational design vs institutional intervention","Kosmos-oriented reform: ten principles; no planning of institutions"],"e":"The paper first frames the risk, then fixes its key distinction, builds the evolutionary (descriptive) theory and only then draws the prescriptive conclusions about how to reform institutions."},{"t":"moroni","w":4,"title":"Moroni (after Hume): how a rule of stable possession emerges","steps":["Each person sees an interest in leaving others' goods alone if they do the same","Each acts on the supposition that the other will act likewise, without any promise","Repeated experience of the inconvenience of breaking the rule gives it force","The rule arises gradually, like a language, as a basic institution of society"],"e":"Hume's account shows the evolutionary view: a key institution emerges from interdependent, intentional actions and slow experience, not from anyone's design."},{"t":"sorensen","w":4,"title":"Sorensen: Ontario's value capture rules over time","steps":["1917 Act: cities may only comment on subdivision plans","1946 Planning Act: subdivision and zoning powers, ambiguous on who pays","1959 amendment confirms lot levies retroactively","1989 Development Charges Act gives charges a statutory basis","2001: the amalgamated City of Toronto starts charging development charges","2019 to 2023: Bills 108, 23 and 97 cut value capture revenue"],"e":"Two critical junctures (1946, 1989) and a possible third (from 2019) are separated by reactive sequences of incremental change, such as the legalisation of lot levies and Toronto's new practice after amalgamation."},{"t":"sorensen","w":4,"title":"Sorensen: how the argument of the paper unfolds","steps":["Define value capture as a purposeful shift of development costs and benefits","Note that conjunctural analysis says little about specific institutional change","Add HI concepts: critical junctures, permissive and productive conditions","Trace Toronto's history by process tracing: two, possibly three junctures","Conclude: path dependence without lock-in; productive conditions decide outcomes"],"e":"The paper moves from a normative definition of value capture to a theoretical gap, fills it with HI concepts, tests them on the Toronto narrative and draws conclusions about path dependence and divergent outcomes."},{"t":"fog","w":6,"title":"Foglesong's argument: why planning exists and why it is limited","steps":["Urban land has a social character: its value is collectively produced","It is privately owned, so congestion and bad housing go unsolved","Capital as a whole needs some socialised control over land","Socialised control threatens owners and opens the door to voters","Planning takes a limited, insulated form shaped by struggle"],"e":"The property contradiction creates the need for planning; the resistance of owners and the capitalism–democracy contradiction then explain why planning stays limited and shielded, with its exact form set by political struggle."},{"t":"harvey","w":6,"title":"Harvey (1989): how the article builds its argument","steps":["Conceptual issues: avoid reifying cities; governance is wider than government","Three assertions from Baltimore: partnership, speculation, place over territory","Four basic options for competing cities","Macro-economic consequences: mobility, polarisation, instability, image","Critical perspective: dual city and a progressive urban corporatism"],"e":"Harvey first clears up concepts, then characterises entrepreneurialism, maps the strategies, traces their consequences and ends with a critique and a political alternative."},{"t":"harvey","w":6,"title":"Harvey's Baltimore: from 'armpit' to 'renaissance city'","steps":["Early 1970s: dismal reputation as 'the armpit of the east coast'","1978: narrow referendum allows city land for private development","Harborplace and the harbour become the partnership's flagship","Tourist employment expands from under one to over fifteen thousand jobs","Hailed as a 'renaissance city' while studies find 'rot beneath the glitter'"],"e":"The referendum gave partnership a mandate, the harbour project built the image, and the image then masked continuing decline: 'the triumph of image over substance'."},{"t":"hh","w":6,"title":"Helsinki's turn to entrepreneurial land policy","steps":["1993: the City begins selling land","1994: the Real Estate Department calculates 'shadow rents'","1995: state real estate companies replace the National Board","1999: the MRL ends state ratification of local plans","2005: the Premises Centre charges departments market rent"],"e":"The city started selling, rehearsed market logic with shadow rents, followed a state that had corporatised its property, gained autonomy through the MRL and finally put market rent at the heart of its real estate management."},{"t":"hh","w":6,"title":"Eiranranta: from depot to enclave","steps":["1930s–2000s: light industry, waste water site and city depot","2001: rezoned as a residential area","March 2004: the REC decides to sell, highest offer wins","2005: record bids; controversy over Apollo","Fenced flats sold at €10,000–16,000 per m², no social housing"],"e":"Rezoning created the value, the sale decision applied the highest-bidder rule, the bids revealed a fight over land rent, and the result was an exclusive enclave."},{"t":"savini","w":6,"title":"Savini & Aalbers: how land capital is de-contextualised","steps":["Land becomes a liquid (quasi-)financial asset","It is managed in portfolios with assets elsewhere","Its value rests on simplified yield expectations","Investment decisions split from land-use programming","Planning becomes instrumental to unlocking capital"],"e":"Liquidity lets land enter global portfolios, where it is valued by yield projections; investment decisions then separate from local programming, and a dependent municipality ends up bending planning to release capital."},{"t":"savini","w":6,"title":"Falck 2009–2011: from bankruptcy to approval","steps":["Credit crunch: Milan's prosecutor opens bankruptcy procedure against Risanamento","Risanamento must sell the Falck site and stop major projects","Sesto Immobiliare buys for €405M, largely by transferring land to banks","The deal requires approval of at least 962,000 m² within two years","The SPV presses for a quick deal; the programme grows to almost 1.2M m²","September 2011: council approves, boosted by the region's €323M campus"],"e":"Financial distress forced a sale on conditions set by creditors, the purchase tied land value to swift municipal approval, and the pressure to cover debt inflated the programme until the council approved it with regional money."},{"t":"bossuyt","w":7,"title":"Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano: the layers of federation","steps":["Floor committees","Resident committees","Entidades (residents' organisations)","Housing movements","Federations lobbying the state"],"e":"Commoning is layered: floor and resident committees feed into the entidades, which belong to movements, which join federations that lobby for political change; this layering inserted autogestão into the MCMV programme."},{"t":"gurran","w":7,"title":"The NSW planning reform, 2011–2013","steps":["Change of government; review announced (2011)","Panel holds 100+ public meetings","Green Paper released (July 2012)","Over 1,200 submissions received","White Paper and draft Bill (April 2013)","Bill stalls in parliament (end 2013)"],"e":"After the March 2011 change of government a review panel consulted widely; the Green Paper drew over 1,200 submissions; the White Paper and draft Planning Bill followed in April 2013; by the end of 2013 the Bill had stalled in the Legislative Assembly after community backlash."},{"t":"gurran","w":7,"title":"Australian housing provision, as the authors trace it","steps":["Public housing about 18% of postwar output","Most of it sold off by 1981","Mortgage markets deregulated in the 1980s","Rapid price growth 1996–2004","NHSC estimates an 85,000-dwelling shortfall (2008)","Social housing under 2% of NSW approvals (2014)"],"e":"Postwar public housing (1945–1981) was largely sold through low-cost ownership and right-to-buy schemes; 1980s mortgage deregulation preceded the 1996–2004 price boom; the NHSC's 2008 estimate followed; by July 2014 social housing was under two per cent of NSW approvals."},{"t":"pruijt","w":7,"title":"Pruijt: the steps of the argument","steps":["Castells: institutionalization is inevitable","Distinguish institutionalization from cooptation","Post-Fordist hypothesis: market regimes co-opt","Amsterdam flexible, New York co-opted","Lower East Side anomaly: squatting as an end","Paradox: market regimes offer less room"],"e":"Pruijt starts from Castells' claim, separates terminal and flexible institutionalization from cooptation, tests the post-Fordist hypothesis in two cities, explains the Lower East Side anomaly by the type of movement, and concludes that market regimes offer fewer opportunities for squatting."},{"t":"pruijt","w":7,"title":"Banana Kelly's trajectory (Pruijt)","steps":["Squatting buildings in the Bronx","Renovating the buildings","Award-winning service provider","Scandal and poor maintenance"],"e":"Banana Kelly moved from squatting to renovation to becoming an award-winning service provider, before collapsing in scandal and poor maintenance: a case of cooptation consistent with post-Fordist theory."},{"t":"raco","w":7,"title":"Raco and Imrie: how SRB governs through community","steps":["Rationality: rights and responsibilities, active citizens","Programme: SRB funds local partnerships competitively","Technologies: capacity building, targets and audit","Subjects: communities that police themselves","Result: a little extra freedom, more surveillance"],"e":"The article moves from the political rationality of advanced liberalism, through the SRB programme and its technologies, to the formation of self-governing subjects; the outcome is Foucault's paradox of a little more freedom in return for more monitoring."},{"t":"raco","w":7,"title":"Structure of Raco and Imrie's argument","steps":["Policy turn to 'rights and responsibilities'","Framework: governmentality and advanced liberalism","Rationality and programme: government through community","Technologies: subjectivity and expertise","Conclusion: control, yet room for resistance"],"e":"The authors first justify the Foucauldian framework, then analyse SRB in two parts (government through community; subjectivity and expertise), and conclude that the art of government, being constructed, can also be reconstructed."},{"t":"rydin6","w":7,"title":"Rydin: from pluralism to adapting regime theory","steps":["Pluralism: groups form when there is demand","Critique: pluralism is naïve about unequal power","Molotch: growth coalitions of local elites","Critique: too agent-based and too simple","Stone: urban regimes with 'power to' govern","Critique and amendment: corporate bias, US-specific"],"e":"Rydin presents each concept as a response to critiques of the previous one: pluralism gives way to growth coalitions, whose flaws lead to Stone's regime theory, which in turn is criticised for corporate bias and poor transferability and amended abroad (e.g. Li and Liu)."},{"t":"rydin6","w":7,"title":"Turin's urban regime (Ravazzi and Belligni)","steps":["Incubation: local elite shapes a common frame","Naming: a shared view of the city's problems","Blaming: story of the path not taken to modernise","Claiming: core beliefs and purposes for the city","Narrow election win reveals weak support","Regime opens up to a wider 'city dialogue'"],"e":"The researchers trace the incubation of a common frame, then naming, blaming and claiming as the agenda formed; after the mayor's narrow election victory the coalition widened into a 'city dialogue' with more civil society leaders."},{"t":"alex","w":3,"title":"Alexander's argument in 'After rationality'","steps":["Rationality breaks down as planning's paradigm, yet is not superseded","The field responds with ritual, avoidance, abandonment and search","Substantive and earlier contingency alternatives fall short","Critical theory adds interaction but misses pre-interactive deliberation","One paradigm cannot supplant another: the two are complementary","A contingency framework assigns each model its own domain"],"e":"Alexander moves from diagnosis (rationality broken but not replaced), through the four responses and the failed candidates, to the juxtaposition of rationality and TCA, which leads to his conclusion that both are needed and to the six-cell contingency framework."},{"t":"rc","w":3,"title":"The lecture's rational choice argument on Airbnb","steps":["Short-stay rental imposes externalities on neighbours","Regulation lags: hosts organise, neighbours free-ride","One-size-fits-all rules are hard to enforce and backfire","Coase: clear rights and zero transaction costs give efficiency","Transaction costs are never zero in practice","Design tools that clarify rights and cut transaction costs"],"e":"The lecture moves from the problem (externalities), through why regulation lags (Olson's collective action problem) and why standard regulation struggles, to Coase's theorem, its limiting condition, and finally Coasean-inspired tools such as tradable permits and VvE negotiations."},{"t":"rc","w":3,"title":"Rijnenburg (Utrecht) as presented in the lecture","steps":["Developers negotiate with the municipality about housing plans","The financial crisis makes developers' business case unviable","An alternative vision emerges: Rijnenburg as an energy landscape","Landowners respond with framing, a consortium, lobbying and negotiation"],"e":"Before 2008 developers were negotiating housing plans; the crisis made their business case unviable and the energy landscape vision took over; Buitelaar et al. (2025) then describe the landowners' strategies of framing ('low-hanging fruit'), collaborating in a consortium, lobbying and negotiating."},{"t":"p1","w":1,"title":"From 'limits' to planetary boundaries","steps":["Silent Spring opens the foundational decade","The Population Bomb warns of overpopulation","The Limits to Growth sets out the 'limits' framing","Brundtland softens limits to those of technology and social organisation","Rockström et al. quantify planetary boundaries"],"e":"Lecture 1 timeline: Carson 1962, Ehrlich 1968, Meadows et al. 1972 (with Blueprint for Survival), Brundtland 1987 ('not absolute limits'), then planetary boundaries from 2009 (Rockström), updated 2015 and 2023, which reintroduce global biophysical limits."},{"t":"p2","w":1,"title":"The sprawl root: from 'less city' to 'more city'","steps":["Suburbs are promoted as the cure for dense, polluted tenements","Environmentalists inspired by Silent Spring attack sprawl and growth","The oil crisis starts a shift towards the city as solution","Calthorpe calls the city the most environmentally benign settlement","The Sierra Club launches its 'Challenge to Sprawl'","'Density is green' is popularised and enters climate plans"],"e":"Angelo and Wachsmuth trace the sprawl root: suburbs first as the cure, 1960s critics (Silent Spring, Limits to Growth), the 1970s oil crisis, Calthorpe in 1985, the Sierra Club in 1998, and Owen's 2004 popularisation, after which density became the basis of climate plans."},{"t":"p3","w":1,"title":"How greening becomes green gentrification","steps":["A city creates or upgrades a green amenity in an underserved area","The area becomes attractive to investors and better-off buyers","Land values, rents and property taxes rise","Low-income residents are directly or indirectly displaced","Clean, green space ends up concentrated among those who can afford it"],"e":"Lecture 3's mechanism (Gould and Lewis's 'green growth machine'): the metrics that make greening look successful, rising property values and new investment, are the same ones that predict displacement, producing environmental privilege. Barcelona shows the chain depends on location and market pressure."},{"t":"p4","w":2,"title":"How re-zoning creates land rent (lecture 4)","steps":["Land is in its initial or alternative use, such as farmland","Re-zoning turns it into developable land","Land development (servicing) produces developed land","Construction delivers new real estate","Real estate value minus construction and land costs leaves a surplus: land rent"],"e":"Witte's 'stages in land and real estate development' figure: the value gained mainly by re-zoning is the surplus that active land policy tries to capture. It also explains why active land policy fails in falling markets: when real estate values drop, the surplus disappears and municipalities carry the loss."},{"t":"p5","w":2,"title":"Moerdijk: from fire to an open future","steps":["2011: chemical fire at Chemie-Pack","2013: port development strategy ('Port Vision 2030')","June 2025: about 700 ha needed for PowerPort and the energy transition","November 2025: municipality says the village must make way","May 2026: the village 'may be able to stay after all'"],"e":"The timeline from the slides and news items shows safety, then energy transition, as successive 'greater good' claims on the same space. The back and forth since 2025 is what the typology calls freeze in practice: no decision, while uncertainty pushes residents to leave."},{"t":"p6","w":2,"title":"Where 'conflicting rationalities' came from","steps":["Cape Town residents reject an informal-settlement upgrade plan and protest","A municipal Commission of Enquiry records clashing world views","Watson (2003) coins 'conflicting rationalities', challenging consensus planning","Built on one case and secondary sources, the concept needs in-depth testing","De Satgé and Watson (2018) test it in Langa and the N2 Gateway"],"e":"Chapter 1 tells this origin story (pp. 3 to 4): an inquiry report's verbatim evidence revealed the gap between the state's modernising ambitions and shack dwellers' world views. The authors admit a concept based on one case is hard to defend, so the book tests it retroductively in a paradigmatic case."},{"t":"p7","w":2,"title":"China's planning path to one territorial system","steps":["Socialist planning: work units and Soviet-aided industry (1950s to 1970s)","1989 City Planning Act: 'one report, two permits' for urban land","1994 fiscal reform pushes local governments into land finance","2018 reform creates the Ministry of Natural Resources","2019 'Opinions' set up the territorial spatial planning system"],"e":"Lecture 7 and Liu and Zhou trace this arc: planning first served the state economy, then urban growth (with sprawl and land finance), and finally an integrated NTSP with control lines and 'One Map'. Each step explains the next: fragmented, growth-driven local planning is what the 2018 and 2019 reforms try to fix."},{"t":"p8","w":2,"title":"How Hodson et al. build their argument","steps":["Puzzle: many plans and innovations, but uneven sustainable infrastructure on the ground","Review the MLP and geographers' critique that it neglects space and place","From the experimentation literature, draw three issues: materiality, governance, sustainabilities","Extend them with mobility examples such as London's bike hire","Propose a framework: reconfiguration as competing, co-existing or complementary","Call for theoretically informed ethnography and comparative research"],"e":"The paper moves from a puzzle through two literatures to propositions, illustrations, a preliminary framework (Table 1) and a research agenda. It is conceptual throughout, which is why it ends with a call for empirical work rather than findings."},{"t":"p9","w":3,"title":"Living lab dispositions: from control to contingency","steps":["Trial: a confined test of products or processes in 'real world' conditions","Enclave: a bounded district treated as a microcosm, set apart from the city","Demonstration: a showcase of what the city could become under the right conditions","Platform: open conditions where many new relations and experiments can form"],"e":"Bulkeley et al. order the four ideal-type dispositions from tightly controlled to highly contingent (pp. 7 to 9). Power shifts along the way from discipline (the trial) to 'government' or the conduct of conduct (the demonstration) to curated connectivity (the platform)."},{"t":"p10","w":3,"title":"From planning tools to digital planning outcomes","steps":["Planning support systems: computer tools for scenarios, forecasting and participation","Planning Support Science: how such tools fit the governance and context at hand","Digital planning: how they make planning efficient, effective and inclusive in practice","Outcomes: better social, economic and environmental results for a sustainable future"],"e":"The editorial layers the concepts: PSS are tools, PSScience studies their fit and is a prerequisite for digital planning, which is application-oriented and aims at outcomes defined in its definition (p. 3). Skipping the middle steps is the technology-first mistake the editorial warns against."},{"t":"p11","w":3,"title":"How urban AI deskills planning (Li and Dang)","steps":["An AI tool proposes an 'optimal' zoning mix","Once validated, the algorithm gains authority","Human intervention is sidelined in the name of efficiency","Accumulated practitioner wisdom is displaced: deskilling","Public trust and professional legitimacy erode"],"e":"Li and Dang's first disruptive factor: algorithms that are validated tend to become authorities, so human judgement is sidelined, and the profession loses the skills built by reconciling values and context. Combined with blurred accountability, this produces the legitimacy crisis the comment warns about."},{"t":"p12","w":3,"title":"The post-growth planning argument","steps":["Planning tools were developed to cope with and foster growth","Sustainability was subsumed into pro-growth eco-modernism","There is little evidence of absolute decoupling","So planning should delink public goals from growth and new development","Re-tool housing and infrastructure instruments: ceilings, reuse, phase-outs","Planners voice uncomfortable truths as supportive holders of shared leadership"],"e":"Durrant et al. move from diagnosis (planning's bond with growth, green growth labelling) via the empirical core (decoupling fails) to the aim of post-growth planning (p. 290), concrete tools (pp. 290 to 292) and a new professional role (pp. 293 to 294)."},{"t":"p13","w":3,"title":"How Hansen et al. built their study","steps":["2014: the GREEN SURGE project studies green space planning in 20 cities","2020 to 2021: officials in eleven of these cities are interviewed again","Closed answers give an overview of the extent of change","Open answers are coded inductively into four trends","Findings are discussed against four transformation outcomes"],"e":"The longitudinal design (pp. 2403 to 2407) compares the 2014 'case study portraits' with new structured interviews, then moves from extent of change, to trends (global events, urban growth, integrated planning, governance), to the Wolfram et al. lenses: innovation, exnovation, co-governance and social justice."}]};
